/* AQA GCSE Geography (8035) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Opus 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8035', KITS: {"3.1.1.1":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.1.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Question 1 of Paper 1 opens on The challenge of natural hazards, and this room holds the three things it asks for first: what a natural hazard actually is, what types there are, and why the same size of event kills 185 people in one country and tens of thousands in another. Every later room in this section — tectonic hazards, tropical storms, extreme UK weather and climate change — is marked on the reasoning built here, because the extended question that closes the section is answered not by listing what happened but by weighing the strength of the event against the vulnerability of the people it reached.","idea":[{"h":"A natural hazard has two halves, and the second half is the one students forget","t":"A natural hazard is a natural event or process that threatens people or property. Both halves have to be present. An earthquake under the middle of the Pacific, far from any island or shipping lane, is a natural event but not a natural hazard, because there is nothing for it to harm; the same earthquake beneath a city is a serious hazard. When a hazard event does cause major loss of life or damage, it is called a natural disaster — the same event is a hazard before it happens and a disaster afterwards. A top-level answer uses the definition as a tool rather than an opening line: because a hazard exists only where a natural process meets people, anything that moves people towards the process, such as a city spreading onto a floodplain, creates hazard without changing the physics of the event at all.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (definition)"},{"h":"Four types, grouped by the force doing the damage","t":"The specification's aims name tectonic, geomorphological, biological and meteorological processes, and that is how hazards are grouped — by the force doing the damage, not by the place it happens in. Tectonic (geological) hazards come from movement of the Earth's crust: earthquakes, volcanic eruptions and the tsunami an undersea earthquake can launch. Meteorological (atmospheric) hazards come from the weather: tropical storms, tornadoes, heatwaves, droughts, blizzards and extreme rainfall. Geomorphological hazards come from material moving at the surface: landslides, mudflows, rockfalls and avalanches. Biological hazards come from living things: disease epidemics and insect infestations such as locust swarms. A top-level answer also knows the joins between the groups, because real events run in chains: an earthquake triggers landslides and a tsunami, a tropical storm triggers flooding, and days of rain trigger a landslide on a slope that has been cleared of trees.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (types)"},{"h":"Hazard risk is a chance, and it has more than one dial","t":"Hazard risk is the probability that people will be harmed by a hazard event. It rises if the event becomes more likely or more powerful, but it also rises if more people are exposed to it or if those people are more easily harmed. Exposure is how many people and how much property lie within reach; vulnerability is how badly they are likely to suffer when it strikes. The factors an examiner expects are urbanisation, which crowds people onto the steep slopes and floodplains left over in fast-growing cities; poverty, which forces people into unsafe housing on unsafe land and leaves them nothing to recover with; farming, which draws dense populations onto fertile volcanic soils and deltas; and climate change, which is loading the weather-related hazards. Add the magnitude and frequency of the event and a country's level of development, monitoring and preparedness. A top-level answer never lists these: it weighs them, deciding which dial was turned in the case it is arguing about.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Is it a hazard? Applying the definition to four events","setup":"Four natural events are described. (a) An earthquake of magnitude 6.8 occurs under the middle of the Pacific Ocean, far from any inhabited island and away from shipping lanes. (b) A volcano erupts on an uninhabited island in the Southern Ocean. (c) Heavy rain falls for three days on a city built on a river floodplain. (d) A landslide blocks a mountain road used every day by a school bus. Which of these are natural hazards, and how would you justify the decision in a single sentence?","steps":["Put the two-part test to each case in turn: is the process natural in origin, and is there anybody or anything within reach that it could harm?","Cases (a) and (b) fail the second half. Both are natural processes, but nothing and nobody is exposed to them, so they are natural events and not natural hazards.","Cases (c) and (d) pass both halves: the rainfall threatens homes and lives on the floodplain, and the falling material threatens the bus, the people in it and the road itself.","Justify it in one sentence that carries the test, so that it decides all four cases at once: a natural hazard is a natural event or process that threatens people or property, so an event with nobody in reach of it is not a hazard."],"cues":["What two conditions does the word 'hazard' place on a natural event?","Which of the four have nothing and nobody within reach?","For the two that are left, what exactly is threatened in each?","How would you word the rule so that it settles all four cases in one sentence?"]},{"title":"Sorting six events into the four types","setup":"A revision list contains six events: an avalanche in the Alps; a tsunami in the Indian Ocean; a drought in East Africa; a locust swarm in Kenya; a landslide after heavy rain on a hillside in Rio de Janeiro; a heatwave in western Europe. Sort them into the four groups of natural hazard — tectonic, meteorological, geomorphological and biological — and identify the one event that belongs to two groups at once, explaining why.","steps":["Ask what force is doing the damage in each case, because the grouping follows the process, not the country it happens in.","The tsunami is tectonic: the wave is generated by crustal movement, normally a large undersea earthquake.","The drought and the heatwave are meteorological: each is a prolonged, extreme condition of the atmosphere.","The avalanche and the hillside failure are geomorphological: in both, material on a slope loses its grip and moves downhill.","The locust swarm is biological: the damage is done by living organisms stripping the crops.","The hillside failure is the event that sits across two groups, because the movement of material is geomorphological but the trigger is meteorological — rain soaking into the slope — and that join is how hazard chains begin."],"cues":["What decides which group an event goes in — where it happens, or what does the damage?","Which listed event is generated by movement of the crust?","Which two are conditions of the atmosphere?","Which two involve material moving down a slope?","Which one does its damage through living organisms?","Which event has a trigger from a different group than the damage itself?"]},{"title":"Reading Figure 1: why magnitude alone does not predict the death toll","setup":"Figure 1 gives three earthquakes. Haiti, 12 January 2010: magnitude 7.0, focus about 13 km deep, epicentre about 25 km from Port-au-Prince, low-income country; the death toll is disputed, with Haitian government figures running from over 200,000 to over 300,000 while some independent studies put it in the tens of thousands. Christchurch, New Zealand, 22 February 2011: magnitude 6.3, focus about 5 km deep, epicentre about 10 km from the city centre, high-income country; 185 people died. Darfield, New Zealand, 4 September 2010: magnitude 7.1, focus about 10 km deep, epicentre about 40 km from Christchurch, high-income country; nobody was killed directly. Using the table, explain why magnitude alone does not predict the death toll.","steps":["Rank them by magnitude first, since that is the claim being tested: Darfield at 7.1 is the strongest, Haiti at 7.0 is next and Christchurch at 6.3 is the weakest.","Now rank them by deaths: Haiti is far the worst, Christchurch killed 185 and Darfield killed nobody, so the two rankings do not match.","Deal with the New Zealand pair first: the weaker event was the deadly one because its focus was shallower and its epicentre lay beside the city, so the shaking at the surface was far more violent where people actually were.","Explain the gap between Haiti and Christchurch from the income column: codes, enforcement, insurance and emergency services in a high-income country held the death toll down, while in the poorest country in the Americas dense, unreinforced concrete housing collapsed onto the people inside it.","State the conclusion the table supports: magnitude sets the size of the event, but depth, distance and above all vulnerability set the size of the disaster."],"cues":["Which of the three released the most energy?","Does the order of the death tolls match the order of the magnitudes?","Two of them are in the same country — so what, apart from magnitude, differs between them?","What does the income column let you say about the buildings and the rescue?","In one sentence, what does the table prove about magnitude as a predictor?"]},{"title":"Turning three notes into a 'to what extent' judgement","setup":"A student has three notes for the question 'To what extent does hazard risk depend more on a country's level of development than on the strength of the hazard itself?': (i) Haiti, 2010, magnitude 7.0, low-income country, deaths in the tens of thousands at the very least; (ii) Darfield, New Zealand, 2010, magnitude 7.1, high-income country, nobody killed directly; (iii) Tohoku, Japan, 11 March 2011, magnitude 9.0 offshore earthquake and tsunami, high-income country, around 18,000 dead or missing. Put the notes into an order that builds an argument, and say what the conclusion has to do.","steps":["Open with the pair that supports the statement most strongly, because two events of almost the same size gave opposite outcomes: 7.0 in Haiti killed tens of thousands and 7.1 at Darfield killed nobody.","Explain what development actually did there — enforced building codes, trained emergency services, insurance and a state able to pay for recovery — so the same shaking produced two different disasters.","Turn the argument on Japan: wealth, codes, drills and sea walls did not prevent around 18,000 deaths, because the magnitude 9.0 event and the tsunami it launched went beyond what the defences were designed for.","Name the limit this comparison exposes: development reduces risk within a range of event sizes, and beyond that range the magnitude takes the argument back.","Write the conclusion as a qualified position rather than a yes or a no, since 'to what extent' is answered by how far and under what conditions the statement holds."],"cues":["Which two notes are nearly identical in magnitude but opposite in outcome?","What specific things does a high income buy that change the outcome?","Which note works against the statement, and why is it the strongest counter?","What limit on the statement do these three cases together expose?","What must the last sentence do that a simple 'I agree' would not?"]},{"title":"Using Figure 3: the hazard stayed, the risk fell","setup":"Figure 3 states that the Bhola cyclone of November 1970 killed an estimated 300,000 to 500,000 people on the coast of Bangladesh, that a cyclone in April 1991 killed about 138,000, and that Cyclone Sidr, an intense storm crossing the same coast in November 2007, killed far fewer: about 3,400 by the Bangladesh government count and over 4,000 in commonly cited totals. It also states that thousands of raised concrete cyclone shelters have been built, mangroves planted along parts of the shore, and a Cyclone Preparedness Programme run since 1972 in which tens of thousands of trained volunteers carry warnings into the villages. Using Figure 3, explain how the risk was reduced even though the hazard was not.","steps":["Take the trend out of the three storms first: the death tolls drop from hundreds of thousands, to about 138,000, to a few thousand across roughly forty years.","Test the hazard side of risk for a change that could explain it — the coast is the same low-lying delta and Sidr was itself an intense storm, so the fall is not because the storms weakened.","Attribute the fall to the vulnerability side, using the resource: shelters give people somewhere above the surge to reach, and the volunteer network delivers the warning early enough for them to walk there.","Add the physical defence the resource names: mangroves along the shore take some of the energy out of the surge before it reaches the villages behind them.","Close on the idea being tested: risk combines the hazard with the vulnerability of the people, so cutting vulnerability cuts the risk without touching the hazard at all."],"cues":["What happens to the death tolls across the three storms?","Is there anything in the resource suggesting the storms themselves got weaker?","Which two measures in the resource act on the people rather than on the storm?","Which measure in the resource acts on the water itself?","What does this case show about the two sides of hazard risk?"]}],"check":[{"q":"An earthquake of magnitude 7.5 occurs beneath an uninhabited part of the Sahara. Is it a natural hazard?","a":"No. It is a natural event, but nothing and nobody is exposed to it, so it threatens no people or property. It would become a hazard the moment people lived, worked or travelled within reach of the shaking."},{"q":"Which group does a tsunami belong to, and what normally triggers one?","a":"Tectonic (geological). A tsunami is a wave set off by movement of the crust, usually a large undersea earthquake, so it is the second link in a hazard chain that begins with the earthquake."},{"q":"Two cities face the same chance of a magnitude 6 earthquake. Why might their hazard risk still be very different?","a":"Because risk depends on exposure and vulnerability as well as on the event: how many people and buildings lie within reach, how the buildings are built and enforced, how wealthy people are, and how good the warnings, drills and emergency services are."},{"q":"Which factor affecting hazard risk explains why the lower slopes of an active volcano are often densely farmed?","a":"Farming. Weathered volcanic ash makes unusually fertile soil, so people accept the hazard in exchange for high yields — as on the slopes of Vesuvius above Naples."}]},"room":{"facts":["A natural hazard is a natural event or process that threatens people or property; a natural disaster is a hazard event that has actually caused major loss of life or damage.","Hazard risk is the probability that people will be harmed by a hazard event: it combines the hazard itself with the exposure and the vulnerability of the people in its reach.","The four groups of hazard: tectonic (geological), meteorological (atmospheric), geomorphological and biological.","The factors to weigh under hazard risk: urbanisation, poverty, farming and climate change, plus the magnitude and frequency of the event and the country's level of development, monitoring and preparedness.","The extended question that closes Section A of Paper 1 is worth 9 marks with 3 further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology; its levels are 1 (Basic, 1–3), 2 (Clear, 4–6) and 3 (Detailed, 7–9), and about a minute a mark leaves time to write it properly."],"questions":[{"q":"Define the term 'natural hazard'. [1 mark]","a":"AO1 = 1 mark. One mark for a definition holding both halves: a natural event or process, and the threat of harm to people or property, eg 'a natural event that threatens or causes loss of life, injury or damage to property'. No credit for naming an example alone.","sol":"A natural hazard is a natural event or process — an earthquake, a tropical storm, a landslide — that has the potential to cause loss of life, injury or damage to property. Both halves matter. The event has to be natural in origin, and there has to be something within its reach for it to harm: a volcanic eruption on an uninhabited island is a natural event, not a natural hazard. When such an event does cause major loss of life or damage, it is called a natural disaster.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The word 'natural' is only half of the definition — think about who or what has to be nearby before the word 'hazard' applies at all.","Test your wording on an eruption in the middle of an empty ocean: if your definition calls that a hazard, it is missing something.","One sentence, two clauses: the kind of event in the first, what it could do and to whom in the second."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (definition)"]},{"q":"State two different types of natural hazard. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks. One mark for each type correctly named, eg tectonic (geological), meteorological (atmospheric), geomorphological, biological. The question asks for types, so two events from the same group (eg earthquake and tsunami) earn one mark only.","sol":"Tectonic (geological) hazards and meteorological (atmospheric) hazards. Tectonic hazards come from movement of the Earth's crust — earthquakes, volcanic eruptions and tsunami. Meteorological hazards come from the atmosphere — tropical storms, droughts, heatwaves and extreme rainfall. The other two groups are geomorphological hazards, where material moves at the surface, such as landslides and avalanches, and biological hazards, which come from living things, such as disease epidemics and locust swarms. Because the question asks for types and not events, 'earthquake and tsunami' would only count once, as both are tectonic.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The groups are named after the process that does the damage, not after individual events.","Sort by where the hazard comes from rather than by what it does: name the natural system it starts in and you have named its type.","Two words or short phrases is all this needs; an example after each is optional and earns nothing extra."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (types)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, compare the Christchurch earthquake of February 2011 with the Darfield earthquake of September 2010. [2 marks]","a":"AO4 = 2 marks. One mark for a comparison taken from the table, eg Darfield was the stronger at 7.1 against Christchurch's 6.3; second mark for a developed comparison using further data, eg but Darfield's focus was deeper, about 10 km against about 5 km, and its epicentre about four times further from the city, and it killed nobody while Christchurch killed 185. No credit for describing one event on its own.","sol":"Darfield was the stronger earthquake of the two, at magnitude 7.1 against Christchurch's 6.3, and yet it was the weaker one that was deadly: Christchurch killed 185 people while Darfield killed nobody directly. The table shows why the comparison runs that way. Christchurch's focus was the shallower, at about 5 km against about 10 km, and its epicentre lay about 10 km from the city centre against Darfield's 40 km, so the energy reached the built-up area far less weakened.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A comparison holds the two events in the same sentence; two separate descriptions score as one.","Work along the columns of the table one at a time and note which event is larger or smaller in each.","Use the words 'whereas' or 'while' and put a figure from the table on each side of them."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, suggest three reasons why the people living on the hillside are more at risk from a landslide than the people living in the apartment blocks below. [3 marks]","a":"AO3 = 3 marks. One mark for each valid reason read from the resource, eg the houses sit on a steep slope while the blocks stand on flat land; the trees at the top have been cleared, so roots no longer bind the soil; there is no drainage channel, only a bare gully, so runoff concentrates and cuts into the slope; the houses are self-built on posts with no engineered foundations; the alleyways are flights of steps, so emergency vehicles cannot reach them. No credit for reasons that cannot be seen in the resource.","sol":"First, the houses are built directly on a steep slope while the apartment blocks stand on flat land at the foot of it, and it is steep ground that fails. Second, the trees at the top have been cleared for a new row of houses on a cut edge, so there are no roots to bind the soil and no leaves to intercept the rain, which means more water soaks in and the slope becomes heavier and weaker. Third, there is no concrete drainage channel, only a bare gully full of rubbish, so runoff concentrates in one line down the hill and cuts into the ground beside the houses.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Three separate reasons, each of which a reader could point to in the description.","Work down the scene from the cleared ground at the top to the alleyways between the houses.","Write each reason as its own short sentence rather than one long one, so the examiner can count them."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)"]},{"q":"Explain one reason why the number of people at risk from natural hazards is increasing. [2 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks. Credit one reason only. One mark for a valid reason (urbanisation, population growth, poverty pushing settlement onto unsafe land, climate change); second mark for developing it through to the risk, eg cities in LICs and NEEs grow fastest onto the land nobody else wants, such as steep slopes and floodplains, so more people are exposed to landslides and floods.","sol":"Urbanisation. The world's fastest-growing cities are in LICs and NEEs, and the housing that goes up to receive the new arrivals is often built on the land nobody else wanted — steep hillsides, floodplains and low-lying coastal ground. That puts a large number of people and their homes inside the reach of landslides, river floods and storm surges, where a single event can now harm thousands of people at once, even though the events themselves are no more frequent than before.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The number of hazard events is not what is changing here; something about where people are is.","Pick one reason and develop it, because a second reason earns nothing on this question.","Two sentences: the reason, then a 'so that means…' sentence carrying it through to people being in reach of the hazard."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, suggest how the way this hillside has been built on increases the risk to the people living there. [4 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear interpretation of the scene with sound application of understanding of slope stability and vulnerability, eg clearance at the top and the lack of drainage linked through to slope failure, and the stepped alleyways linked to rescue. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): basic interpretation, generic statements about poor housing not tied to the resource.","sol":"The houses in Figure 2 are built directly onto a steep hillside, and it is on steep ground that soil and rock fail, so the ground beneath them is the least stable in the scene. At the top of the slope the trees have been cleared for a new row of houses on a cut edge; without roots to bind the soil and leaves to intercept the rain, more water reaches the surface and soaks in, adding weight to the slope and reducing the friction holding it together. There is no concrete drainage channel, only a bare gully filled with rubbish running straight down the hill, so runoff concentrates in that single line, cutting into the soil and undermining the houses beside it. The houses themselves are self-built in brick and blockwork, some standing on concrete posts over the steepest ground, so they have no engineered foundations to resist any movement of the slope. Finally, the alleyways are narrow flights of steps rather than roads, so an ambulance or a fire engine could not reach the top of the slope if the hillside did give way — the injuries would be worse because help would arrive on foot.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Two things are being built here: the houses, and the slope they stand on. Both have been changed.","For each feature you pick out of the resource, ask what it does to the water, to the soil or to the rescue.","Two or three developed points, each anchored in something the resource actually shows, beat a list of five."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, suggest why the Haiti earthquake killed so many more people than the Christchurch earthquake. [4 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear interpretation of the table through sound application of understanding of vulnerability, eg the income column linked to building quality, enforcement and rescue capacity, with clear and effective use of the depth and distance data to support the answer. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): limited interpretation, eg 'Haiti was poorer' with no chain through to why people died, and limited, piecemeal use of the table.","sol":"Figure 1 shows Haiti at magnitude 7.0 and Christchurch at 6.3, so Haiti did release considerably more energy; but magnitude alone cannot explain a gap of that size, and the column that does explain it is income. New Zealand is a high-income country: building regulations require structures that can flex and hold together in shaking, there is public money to enforce them and to inspect, and emergency services reached the rubble within minutes, so 185 people died, 115 of them in the collapse of one office building. Haiti was the poorest country in the Americas. Rapid urban growth in Port-au-Prince had produced dense, self-built housing in unreinforced concrete with heavy floors and no seismic design, so buildings pancaked onto the people inside them, and a state with almost no equipment or trained rescuers could not reach them in time. The table adds one more point in the same direction: Haiti's focus was the deeper of the two, at about 13 km against about 5 km, so the shaking should have been less severe at the surface, which makes the difference in deaths a difference in vulnerability rather than in the events.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the magnitudes, then ask whether that difference is anything like big enough to explain the difference in deaths.","Take the income column and turn it into specific things: what gets built, who enforces it, who digs people out.","Use the depth figures too — they point the opposite way to the death tolls, which strengthens your argument."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)"]},{"q":"Explain why people continue to live in places at risk from natural hazards. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): reasons developed into chains and supported by specific places, eg fertile volcanic soils on the slopes of Vesuvius, delta farmland in Bangladesh, jobs and family ties in a coastal city, the low frequency of large events, and having no affordable alternative. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): accurate reasons with some development. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): a list of undeveloped statements.","sol":"Some people stay because the hazard is also the resource. Volcanic ash weathers into unusually fertile soil, so the lower slopes of Vesuvius are farmed intensively for vines and vegetables even though about three million people live in the metropolitan area around a volcano that destroyed Pompeii and Herculaneum in AD 79 and last erupted in 1944. In the same way, the delta of Bangladesh floods because it is a delta, and it is those floods that lay down the silt which keeps the land fertile enough to feed the millions who farm it. Others stay for work and for each other. Hazardous sites are often the best economic sites — flat land beside a river, a natural harbour, a coastline with a tourist trade — so the jobs, the schools, the family and the community are all there, and leaving would mean giving up an income for an event that may not come. That points to a third reason: frequency. A large eruption or earthquake may not occur in a lifetime, so people discount a risk they have never seen, particularly where they trust the defences, the building codes and the warning system to protect them. Finally, many people have no choice at all. The poorest urban households cannot afford safe land, so they build on the steep or flooded ground that nobody else wants, and the risk they live with is a consequence of their poverty rather than a decision about hazard.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Some people choose the site, and some people have no alternative — the answer needs both kinds of reason.","For each reason, name a place where you can see it happening, then push the reason through to why it outweighs the danger.","Three developed reasons, each with its own example, is the right size for this tariff."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain how preparation and planning can reduce the risk from a natural hazard. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): full use of the resource, with the fall in death tolls explained through shelters, the volunteer warning network and mangroves, and the point made that the hazard itself is unchanged. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear use of the resource with some explanation. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): describes the resource without explaining how risk fell. Max Level 1 for an answer that ignores Figure 3.","sol":"Figure 3 shows the death tolls on the same stretch of coast falling from an estimated 300,000 to 500,000 in 1970, to about 138,000 in 1991, to a toll in the thousands when Cyclone Sidr crossed in 2007 — about 3,400 by the Bangladesh government count and over 4,000 in commonly cited totals. Nothing in the resource suggests the storms have weakened — Sidr was an intense storm striking the same low-lying delta — so what changed is the vulnerability of the people, and that is what preparation and planning act on. The Cyclone Preparedness Programme, running since 1972, uses tens of thousands of trained volunteers to carry the warning into villages that may have no other way of receiving it; a warning is only useful if it arrives early enough to be acted on, and volunteers on bicycles with megaphones close the last gap between a forecast and a household. That warning then needs somewhere to send people, which is why thousands of raised concrete cyclone shelters matter: they put families above the height of the storm surge, which is what kills in a delta, and they are close enough to walk to in the hours a warning buys. The mangroves planted along parts of the shore work on the water instead, taking energy out of the surge before it reaches the embankments and the villages behind them. Together these measures cut the risk without altering the hazard at all: the cyclones still come, but far fewer people are now in their reach when they do.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Risk has two sides. Check the resource for evidence that the storms changed before you assume they did.","Sort the measures in the resource into those that move people and those that weaken the water.","Quote the death tolls from the resource as your evidence, then explain each measure through to the life it saves."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)"]},{"q":"'The risk from a natural hazard depends more on a country's level of development than on the strength of the hazard itself.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use named examples in your answer. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. The command phrase is 'to what extent', so the answer must weigh development against magnitude and frequency rather than argue one side, and must reach a judgement. Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): detailed knowledge of named events, thorough understanding of the interrelationships between the event, the place and the people, and a coherent, reasoned evaluation. Credit any well-supported position, including a qualified disagreement.","sol":"The evidence for the statement is strong, and the clearest form of it is a pair of earthquakes of almost the same size with opposite outcomes. The Haiti earthquake of 12 January 2010 measured 7.0 and struck about 25 km from Port-au-Prince, the capital of the poorest country in the Americas; even the lowest credible estimates put the dead in the tens of thousands, and over a million people lost their homes. The Darfield earthquake in New Zealand on 4 September 2010 was slightly stronger at 7.1 and killed nobody directly. The difference was not the crust but the country. New Zealand's building regulations require structures to flex without collapsing and are enforced, its emergency services are trained and equipped, its households are insured and its government could fund a rebuild; in Port-au-Prince, rapid urban growth had filled the hillsides with self-built, unreinforced concrete housing that pancaked onto its occupants, and the state had almost nothing with which to dig them out. Development also reaches the aftermath: a cholera epidemic that began in Haiti later in 2010 killed thousands more, which a functioning water and health system would have prevented.\n\nThe statement has limits, though, and they matter. The Tohoku earthquake and tsunami of 11 March 2011 struck one of the most prepared countries on Earth — codes, drills, sea walls, warning systems — and around 18,000 people still died or went missing, because a magnitude 9.0 event and the tsunami it launched exceeded what the defences had been designed for. Magnitude, depth and distance also decide a great deal within one country: the Christchurch earthquake of 22 February 2011 was weaker than Darfield at 6.3, yet it killed 185 people because its focus was shallow and lay beside the city.\n\nOn balance I agree with the statement, but only within a range. For the ordinary run of events, development is the stronger variable, because it decides whether a building stands and whether help arrives; the same magnitude produced tens of thousands of deaths in Haiti and none at Darfield. Beyond that range, at extreme magnitudes or where the event strikes directly beneath a city, the physical event reasserts itself. Development sets how much hazard a society can absorb; the hazard decides whether that is enough.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The strongest evidence for the statement is two events of nearly the same magnitude in different countries — find that pair before you write anything.","Then look for the case that embarrasses the statement, so that your answer has something to weigh rather than a list of agreements.","Structure: support, counter, then a conclusion that says how far and under what conditions the statement holds."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (definition)"]},{"q":"Assess the importance of urbanisation as a factor increasing hazard risk. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. The command word is 'assess', so urbanisation must be weighed against the other factors — poverty, farming, climate change, magnitude and frequency, level of development — and an informed overall judgement given. Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): detailed knowledge of named places, thorough understanding of how urban growth changes exposure and vulnerability, and a substantiated judgement.","sol":"Urbanisation increases hazard risk mainly by changing exposure: it moves a very large number of people into a small area, and in the fastest-growing cities it moves them onto the ground nobody else wanted. In Rio de Janeiro, self-built housing climbs steep hillsides where the trees have been cleared and there are no drainage channels, so heavy rain runs straight into bare soil and slopes fail beneath whole rows of houses. Port-au-Prince had grown rapidly before 2010, and the housing built to receive that growth was dense, unreinforced concrete without seismic design, which is why a magnitude 7.0 earthquake killed people in the tens of thousands at the very least. Urban growth also concentrates the cost: shops, workplaces, hospitals, water pipes and power lines sit in the same few square kilometres, so one event can remove a city's ability to look after itself, and narrow stepped alleyways can stop emergency vehicles reaching the people who need them.\n\nUrbanisation is not the whole story, however, and it is not even independent of the other factors. What made Port-au-Prince deadly was less the fact of urban growth than the poverty and weak governance that decided how the city was built: Christchurch is also urban, and a shallow magnitude 6.3 earthquake beneath it killed 185 people rather than tens of thousands, because the buildings and the rescue were of a different order. Meanwhile Bangladesh's worst hazard risk is largely rural and coastal, and the fall in its cyclone death tolls from hundreds of thousands in 1970 to a few thousand by 2007 came from shelters, warnings and volunteers rather than from anything to do with urbanisation. Climate change is separately loading the weather-related hazards through warmer seas, heavier rainfall and higher sea levels, and magnitude still decides the extremes, as Tohoku showed in 2011.\n\nMy judgement is that urbanisation is the most important factor in explaining why the number of people exposed to natural hazards keeps rising, since more than half the world now lives in towns and cities and the fastest growth is in hazard-prone LICs and NEEs. It is not, on its own, the factor that decides how many of them die. That depends on poverty and the level of development, which determine what gets built on the new land and who comes when it falls down.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the two questions urbanisation answers: how many people are in reach of a hazard, and how badly they suffer when it arrives.","You cannot assess one factor without the others — line up poverty, farming, climate change and magnitude as the comparison.","Look for an urban case with a low death toll and a rural case with a high one; those two cases discipline the judgement."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)"]},{"q":"'Climate change is now the most important factor affecting hazard risk.' Discuss this statement. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. 'Discuss' requires a balanced treatment developed on both sides: how a warming atmosphere and ocean load meteorological and hydrological hazards, set against the fact that climate change does not touch tectonic hazards and that poverty, urbanisation and preparedness still decide the death tolls. Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): well-developed ideas on both sides, supported by named examples, with a substantiated conclusion.","sol":"There is a real case for the statement, and it rests on the physics of the hazards that come out of the atmosphere. Tropical storms draw their energy from warm ocean water, so as sea surface temperatures rise the conditions that allow the most intense storms to form occur over a wider area and for more of the year. Warmer air holds more water vapour, which loads the heaviest rainfall events and the river and flash flooding that follow them, while rising sea level raises the platform every storm surge is launched from, so the same storm pushes water further inland than it would have done a century ago. Heat itself has become a hazard in its own right in the form of longer and more severe heatwaves and droughts, and drought lengthens the fire season. On this reasoning climate change is unique among the factors: it changes the hazard, not just the number of people standing in front of it.\n\nAgainst that, climate change is silent on an entire group of hazards. It has no effect whatever on the movement of the crust, so the earthquakes that killed tens of thousands in Haiti in 2010, 185 people in Christchurch in 2011 and around 18,000 in Japan in 2011 owe nothing to a warming atmosphere. Even among weather hazards, the death toll is decided mostly elsewhere. Bangladesh sits on a low delta that is more exposed to storm surges than it was, and yet its cyclone deaths fell from an estimated 300,000 to 500,000 in 1970 to a few thousand by 2007, because it built shelters and embankments and trained tens of thousands of volunteers to deliver warnings. Over the same period urbanisation and poverty pushed far more people onto steep slopes and floodplains, which is why a landslide or a flood now finds a crowd where it once found a field.\n\nI would conclude that climate change is the most important factor changing the hazards themselves, particularly for storms, floods, droughts and heatwaves, and that its importance is rising. It is not yet the most important factor affecting hazard risk overall, because risk also depends on who is in the way and how well protected they are, and there poverty, urbanisation and the level of preparedness still do most of the work. The danger is that the two combine: heavier rain falling on unplanned hillside settlements in growing cities is a larger increase in risk than either factor alone.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Ask which side of risk climate change acts on — the hazard, or the vulnerability of the people.","Name a group of hazards that climate change cannot affect at all; that is the strongest limit on the statement.","Finish by separating the claim into the part you accept and the part you do not, rather than agreeing or disagreeing outright."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (types)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What two things must be present before a natural event counts as a natural hazard?","a":"A natural event or process, and people or property within its reach that it could harm."},{"q":"Which type of natural hazard is a tropical storm?","a":"Meteorological (atmospheric)."},{"q":"What is hazard risk?","a":"The probability, or chance, that people will be harmed by a natural hazard event."},{"q":"Which factor affecting hazard risk explains why fertile floodplains and volcanic slopes carry dense populations?","a":"Farming — fertile soils draw people onto hazardous land."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Natural hazard","back":"A natural event or process that threatens people or property. It needs both halves: a natural process, and people or property within its reach.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (definition)","open":false},{"front":"Natural disaster","back":"A natural hazard event that has actually caused major loss of life or damage to property. The same event is a hazard before it happens and a disaster afterwards.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (definition)","open":false},{"front":"Hazard risk","back":"The probability that people will be harmed by a hazard event. It rises with the strength and frequency of the event and with the exposure and vulnerability of the people.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","open":false},{"front":"Exposure","back":"How many people and how much property lie within reach of a hazard. Urban growth onto floodplains and steep slopes raises exposure without changing the event at all.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","open":false},{"front":"Vulnerability","back":"How badly people are likely to be harmed when a hazard strikes: weak housing, poverty, age, ill health, poor warnings and thin emergency services all raise it.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","open":false},{"front":"Tectonic (geological) hazards","back":"Hazards caused by movement of the Earth's crust, mostly at plate margins: earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, and the tsunami an undersea earthquake can launch.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (types)","open":false},{"front":"Meteorological (atmospheric) hazards","back":"Hazards caused by the weather and the atmosphere: tropical storms, tornadoes, heatwaves, droughts, blizzards and the flooding that follows extreme rainfall.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (types)","open":false},{"front":"Geomorphological hazards","back":"Hazards caused by material moving at the Earth's surface: landslides, mudflows, rockfalls and avalanches, often triggered by heavy rain or by an earthquake.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (types)","open":false},{"front":"Biological hazards","back":"Hazards caused by living things: disease epidemics such as cholera, and infestations such as locust swarms that strip crops from farmland.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (types)","open":false},{"front":"Hazard chain","back":"One hazard setting off another: an undersea earthquake launching a tsunami, shaking triggering landslides, a tropical storm bringing a storm surge and river flooding.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (types)","open":false},{"front":"Urbanisation as a factor in hazard risk","back":"Cities concentrate people and property, and the fastest-growing cities spread onto the land nobody else wants — steep slopes and floodplains — so one event can harm thousands at once.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","open":false},{"front":"Poverty as a factor in hazard risk","back":"Poverty forces people onto unsafe land and into self-built housing that cannot resist shaking or flooding, and leaves them no savings, insurance or health care to recover with.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","open":false},{"front":"Farming as a factor in hazard risk","back":"Weathered volcanic ash and river silt make the most fertile soils on Earth, so farmers accept the hazard for the harvest: the slopes of Vesuvius and the delta of Bangladesh are farmed intensively.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","open":false},{"front":"Climate change as a factor in hazard risk","back":"A warmer ocean gives tropical storms more energy, warmer air holds more moisture for intense rainfall, and rising sea level carries storm surges further inland; droughts and heatwaves lengthen.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","open":false},{"front":"An example of preparation cutting hazard risk","back":"Bangladesh's cyclone shelters, embankments and volunteer warning network; New Zealand's enforced building codes, under which a magnitude 7.1 earthquake at Darfield in 2010 killed nobody directly; Japan's drills, sea walls and quake-resistant construction.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Natural hazards pose major risks to people and property (hazard risk)","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 (written for this room) — Three earthquakes: a data table. Every figure here is real and is quoted only to the precision it can be vouched for.\nEvent | Date | Magnitude | Depth of focus | Epicentre | Country income level | Deaths\nHaiti | 12 January 2010 | 7.0 | about 13 km | about 25 km from Port-au-Prince | low income | disputed: Haitian government figures ran from over 200,000 to over 300,000, while some independent studies put the toll in the tens of thousands\nChristchurch, New Zealand | 22 February 2011 | 6.3 | about 5 km | about 10 km from the city centre | high income | 185\nDarfield (Canterbury), New Zealand | 4 September 2010 | 7.1 | about 10 km | about 40 km from Christchurch | high income | nobody killed directly","Figure 2 (written for this room) — A photograph described in words: a hillside settlement above the São Conrado district in the south of Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The description names only what such a scene shows; it carries no figures.\nThe camera looks up a steep hillside. Self-built brick and blockwork houses, two and three storeys high, are packed onto the slope with no space between them, and several stand on concrete posts over the steepest ground. Bare soil and rubbish fill a gully that runs straight down the slope, and there is no concrete drainage channel beside it. Blue plastic water pipes and a tangle of electricity cables run along the alleyways, which are narrow flights of steps rather than roads. At the top of the slope the trees have been cleared and a new row of houses is being built on the cut edge. On the flat land at the foot of the hill stand high-rise apartment blocks with gardens, a wide tarmac road and, beyond them, the beach.","Figure 3 (written for this room) — A short text extract: cyclone risk on the coast of Bangladesh. The events and figures in it are real; the wording was written for this room.\nMost of coastal Bangladesh is delta, a flat plain lying only a few metres above sea level, and the country holds more than 160 million people, giving it one of the highest population densities on Earth. In November 1970 the Bhola cyclone drove a storm surge across this coast and killed an estimated 300,000 to 500,000 people, the deadliest tropical cyclone ever recorded. A cyclone in April 1991 killed about 138,000. Since then Bangladesh has built thousands of raised concrete cyclone shelters, strengthened coastal embankments, planted mangroves along parts of the shore, and run a Cyclone Preparedness Programme, begun in 1972, in which tens of thousands of trained volunteers carry warnings into the villages by bicycle, megaphone and radio. When Cyclone Sidr, an intense storm, crossed the same coast in November 2007, the death toll was far lower: about 3,400 by the Bangladesh government count and over 4,000 in commonly cited totals. The hazard had not gone away; what the country could do about it had changed."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (the 9-mark question that closes Paper 1 Section A, 9 marks + 3 SPaG)","items":["Question: 'The risk from a natural hazard depends more on a country's level of development than on the strength of the hazard itself.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","Point: For events of comparable magnitude, the level of development does more to decide the death toll than the earthquake does.","Evidence: The Haiti earthquake of 12 January 2010 measured 7.0, with a focus about 13 km deep some 25 km from Port-au-Prince, and killed people in the tens of thousands at the very least; the Darfield earthquake in New Zealand on 4 September 2010 was slightly stronger at 7.1 and killed nobody directly.","Developed explanation: New Zealand enforces building regulations that require structures to flex rather than shear, funds trained and equipped emergency services, and insures its households, so shaking damaged buildings without burying the people in them. Port-au-Prince had grown rapidly into dense, self-built housing of unreinforced concrete with heavy floors and no seismic design, so floors pancaked onto their occupants, and a state that was the poorest in the Americas had almost no machinery or trained rescuers to reach them within the hours that matter.","Link to the question: the two events were of almost the same magnitude, so the variable that changed between them was not the hazard but the society it struck, which is exactly what the statement claims.","Mini-judgement: on this evidence the statement holds strongly for ordinary events, though it is a claim about what happens to people, not about the physical event itself.","Why this is Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) on the 9-mark grid: it carries detailed and accurate knowledge of named events with dates and magnitudes (AO1), shows thorough understanding of the interrelationships between the event, the place and the people (AO2), and evaluates the relative significance of development and magnitude in a coherent, reasoned way (AO3). Its spelling, punctuation and grammar are accurate and it uses specialist terms — magnitude, focus, epicentre, unreinforced, vulnerability — which is High performance for the 3 SPaG marks."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: 'The risk from a natural hazard depends more on a country's level of development than on the strength of the hazard itself.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","The weaker paragraph: 'I agree because poor countries suffer more from hazards. In Haiti there was a big earthquake and loads of people died because the houses were bad. In rich countries like New Zealand the houses are better so not as many people die. This shows that being poor is worse when there is a hazard.'","What it meets, in the words of the grid: Level 1 (Basic, 1–3) — 'demonstrates limited knowledge', 'shows slight geographical understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes' and 'demonstrates limited application of knowledge and understanding in evaluating'.","What it misses: there is no date, no magnitude and no named place beyond the country, so AO1 stays at limited knowledge; 'the houses were bad' is an assertion rather than a chain of reasoning about unreinforced concrete, enforcement and rescue, so AO2 never reaches 'some geographical understanding'; and there is no comparison of two events of similar magnitude, so nothing is actually weighed and AO3 cannot rise out of Level 1. The command phrase 'to what extent' is not answered at all, because no limit on the claim is considered.","The single change that would lift it most: replace 'a big earthquake' with the magnitude and the date, and set it beside an event of the same magnitude elsewhere, so that the paragraph has two things to weigh instead of one thing to assert."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the terms and processes this room is marked on","items":["Natural hazard: a natural event or process that threatens people or property. Both halves are needed — a natural process, and people or property within its reach.","Natural disaster: a hazard event that has actually caused major loss of life or damage. The same event is a hazard before it happens and a disaster afterwards.","Hazard risk: the probability that people will be harmed by a hazard event.","Exposure: the number of people and the amount of property lying within reach of the hazard.","Vulnerability: how badly those people are likely to be harmed when it strikes — housing, wealth, health, warnings, emergency services, and the state's ability to fund recovery.","Magnitude: the size or strength of the event. Frequency: how often events of that size occur. A rare, large event is often the more dangerous, because people have not prepared for something they have never seen.","The four groups of hazard, named after the process that does the damage: tectonic (geological), meteorological (atmospheric), geomorphological and biological.","Hazard chain: one hazard setting off another — an undersea earthquake launching a tsunami, shaking triggering landslides, a tropical storm bringing both a storm surge and river flooding.","The factors an examiner expects under hazard risk: urbanisation, poverty, farming and climate change, plus the magnitude and frequency of the event and the country's level of development, monitoring and preparedness.","The distinction that earns marks: the hazard is the event, the risk is what the event can do to people. Measures like shelters, codes and warnings cut the risk without changing the hazard at all."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Haiti 2010 and the Canterbury earthquakes, New Zealand 2010–11","items":["Haiti, 12 January 2010: magnitude 7.0, focus about 13 km deep, epicentre about 25 km from the capital, Port-au-Prince, to the west-south-west, near Léogâne.","Haiti's death toll is disputed and should be quoted honestly: Haitian government figures ranged from over 200,000 to over 300,000, while some independent studies put it in the tens of thousands. Well over a million people were left homeless.","Haiti was the poorest country in the Americas, with rapid urban growth, self-built unreinforced concrete housing and no effective enforcement of building codes.","A cholera epidemic broke out in Haiti later in 2010 and killed thousands more over the following years — a secondary effect of a destroyed water and sanitation system.","Darfield (Canterbury), New Zealand, 4 September 2010: magnitude 7.1, focus about 10 km deep, epicentre about 40 km west of Christchurch, striking at about half past four in the morning. It caused serious damage but killed nobody directly.","Christchurch, New Zealand, 22 February 2011: magnitude 6.3, focus about 5 km deep, epicentre about 10 km from the city centre, during the working day. It killed 185 people — the shallow focus and the short distance made the shaking far more violent than the magnitude alone suggests.","115 of the 185 deaths occurred in the collapse of one office building, the CTV building; widespread liquefaction wrecked roads, pipes and house foundations in the eastern suburbs.","New Zealand is a high-income country with enforced seismic building codes, a national insurance scheme, trained emergency services and the public funds to rebuild — the reason the same country could take a magnitude 7.1 earthquake with no direct deaths.","The pair is the single most useful comparison in this room: 7.0 in a low-income country against 7.1 in a high-income country, five months apart."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — cyclones and preparation in Bangladesh, 1970 to 2007","items":["Most of coastal Bangladesh is delta only a few metres above sea level; the country holds more than 160 million people and has one of the highest population densities on Earth.","Bhola cyclone, November 1970: a storm surge killed an estimated 300,000 to 500,000 people, the deadliest tropical cyclone ever recorded.","April 1991 cyclone: about 138,000 people killed on the same coast.","Cyclone Sidr, November 2007: an intense storm crossing the same coast, with a death toll of about 3,400 by the Bangladesh government count and over 4,000 in commonly cited totals.","The Cyclone Preparedness Programme has run since 1972, using tens of thousands of trained volunteers to carry warnings into villages by bicycle, megaphone and radio.","Thousands of raised concrete cyclone shelters have been built along the coast, together with strengthened embankments and mangrove planting on parts of the shore.","The argument this case makes: the hazard did not weaken, the delta did not rise, and the population grew — yet the death toll fell by orders of magnitude, because preparation cut vulnerability."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — figures for weighing the argument","items":["Tohoku, Japan, 11 March 2011: a magnitude 9.0 offshore earthquake and the tsunami it launched left around 18,000 people dead or missing, almost all of them drowned — proof that at extreme magnitudes even codes, drills and sea walls are not enough.","Nepal, 25 April 2015: a magnitude 7.8 earthquake killed nearly 9,000 people; mountainous terrain slowed rescue and much of the housing was stone and brick built without seismic design.","Typhoon Haiyan, Philippines, 8 November 2013: one of the most powerful tropical cyclones ever recorded at landfall; the official death toll was over 6,000, most of them in and around Tacloban, where a storm surge several metres deep swept through a low-lying coastal city.","Naples, Italy: about three million people live in the metropolitan area around Mount Vesuvius, which buried Pompeii and Herculaneum in AD 79 and last erupted in 1944; the volcanic soils on its lower slopes are farmed intensively for vines and vegetables.","More than half of the world's people now live in urban areas, and the fastest urban growth is in LICs and NEEs, where new housing often goes up on steep slopes, floodplains and low-lying coasts."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'The risk from a natural hazard depends more on a country's level of development than on the strength of the hazard itself.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","items":["Decoding the command phrase: 'to what extent' asks how far the statement holds, not whether you like it. The answer must weigh development against magnitude, frequency, depth and distance, and finish with a position that says how far and under what conditions — a straight 'I agree' cannot reach Level 3.","Opening move (two sentences, no long introduction): define hazard risk as the probability of people being harmed, and split it into the event on one side and exposure and vulnerability on the other. That split is the argument's spine.","Paragraph 1 — the strongest support: Haiti, 12 January 2010, magnitude 7.0, against Darfield, New Zealand, 4 September 2010, magnitude 7.1. Almost identical magnitudes, five months apart; tens of thousands dead in one and nobody directly killed in the other.","Paragraph 2 — the mechanism, so that the comparison is explained and not merely stated: enforced building codes and inspection, trained and equipped emergency services, insurance and the public funds to rebuild in New Zealand; rapid urban growth, unreinforced concrete and an unenforced code in Port-au-Prince, plus the cholera epidemic that followed the loss of clean water.","Paragraph 3 — the counter-argument: Tohoku, Japan, 11 March 2011, magnitude 9.0, around 18,000 dead or missing in a country as prepared as any on Earth; and, within one country, Christchurch on 22 February 2011 at magnitude 6.3 killing 185 people because the focus was shallow and lay beside the city. Magnitude, depth and distance still decide a great deal.","The evaluation thread that runs through every paragraph: each time you give a fact, say whether it strengthens or limits the statement, so the judgement is built as you go rather than announced at the end.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly ten minutes of writing, leaving a minute to read it back for spelling, punctuation and the specialist terms that carry the 3 SPaG marks — magnitude, focus, epicentre, vulnerability, exposure, unreinforced.","What the conclusion has to say: agree within a range. For the ordinary run of events development is the stronger variable, because it decides whether buildings stand and whether help arrives; beyond that range — extreme magnitudes, or an event directly beneath a city — the physical event takes the argument back. Development sets how much hazard a society can absorb; the hazard decides whether that is enough."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:54:48.379057Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.87,"notes":"Factually clean and exam-true: every date, magnitude, depth, distance and death toll (Haiti 2010, Darfield 2010, Christchurch 2011 with 115 of the 185 deaths in the CTV building, Bhola 1970, the 1991 cyclone, Sidr 2007, the Cyclone Preparedness Programme from 1972, Tohoku 2011, Nepal 2015, Haiyan 2013, Vesuvius) checks out against USGS, GeoNet, NDRRMC and UNDRR/CPP sources, every countable claim recounts correctly against Figures 1-3, tariffs are 1/2/3/4/6/9 with Levels 1-3 only, and the three SPaG-bearing questions are all 9-markers in the natural hazards section, which is where Paper 1 puts them (01.9) - fix the AO4 omission on question 6 and the give-away hint on question 1 and it is ready to publish."}}},"3.1.1.2":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.1.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Tectonic hazards are where Paper 1 starts, and AQA has already shown what it does with them: the specimen paper closes Section A with a nine-mark question asking to what extent the primary effects of a tectonic hazard are more significant than the secondary effects, worth nine marks plus three for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology. Its mark scheme credits only effects — not causes and not responses — and it expects a named example with real data behind it. Around that sit the shorter questions: describing the global distribution of earthquakes and volcanoes from a world map, explaining the physical processes at a constructive, destructive or conservative margin from a diagram, and explaining how monitoring, prediction, protection and planning cut the risk. So this room has two jobs. It has to give you the physics, so that you can explain why one margin builds a gentle shield of runny lava and another builds a steep, violent cone. And it has to give you two real earthquakes in countries of very different wealth, with their dates, magnitudes, depths, death tolls and responses at your fingertips, because \"lots of people died\" is a Level 1 sentence and \"185 people died in Christchurch, 115 of them in the collapse of a single office block\" is a Level 3 one.","idea":[{"code":"3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes","h":"Why the plates move at all","t":"The Earth's crust is cracked into plates that float on the hot, slowly moving rock of the mantle beneath. Two kinds of crust sit on them: continental crust, which is thick (around 35 km), old and relatively light, and oceanic crust, which is thin (around 8 km), young and denser. The plates creep at only a few centimetres a year, roughly the speed your fingernails grow, dragged by heat-driven movement in the mantle and by the weight of cold, dense oceanic plate sinking back into it. Everything else in this room follows from two facts: the plates move, and oceanic crust is denser than continental crust. A top-level answer never just says \"convection currents move the plates\"; it uses the density difference to explain which plate goes underneath, and uses the slowness of the movement to explain why stress has to build for years before it is released in seconds."},{"code":"3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes","h":"Three margins, three different hazards","t":"At a constructive margin two plates move apart. Pressure on the mantle below falls, rock melts, and magma rises into the gap and cools to make new ocean floor. The magma is basaltic — runny and low in gas — so eruptions are frequent but gentle, building gently sloping volcanoes such as those of Iceland on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, and the earthquakes are shallow and usually weak. At a destructive margin the denser oceanic plate is subducted beneath the continental plate into a deep trench, as the Nazca plate is beneath South America. Friction locks the plates until the rock snaps, giving powerful earthquakes whose foci get deeper away from the trench; the sinking plate also drives water into the mantle above, which melts to form thick, gas-rich andesitic magma and violent eruptions from steep-sided cones. At a conservative margin, such as the San Andreas Fault in California, plates slide past each other, so no crust is made or destroyed and there are no volcanoes at all — but the friction still produces major earthquakes. A top-level answer keeps the three chains separate and never puts a volcano on a conservative margin."},{"code":"3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes","h":"Reading the distribution off a map","t":"Because hazards are made at plate margins, they appear on a world map as narrow lines rather than as an even scatter. The densest belt encircles the Pacific Ocean — about three-quarters of the world's active volcanoes and around nine-tenths of its earthquakes lie along it. A second, much thinner line of earthquakes runs down the middle of the Atlantic, marking a constructive margin where most of the volcanoes are underwater and Iceland is the largest and best-known of the few places where the ridge rises above the sea. A third belt runs from the Mediterranean through Turkey and Iran along the Himalayas; along the Himalayan stretch there are earthquakes but almost no volcanoes, because two continental plates are pushing together and nothing is being subducted and melted. AQA marks distribution questions by point, and its mark schemes say plainly that there is no credit for simply listing names of countries. A top-level answer therefore describes the shape of the pattern first, develops one statement with detail from the map, and stops there: on a two-mark question an exception such as Hawaii, sitting alone in mid-Pacific, shows the map has been read but earns no further mark."},{"code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","h":"Primary and secondary effects: the difference is causation, not timing","t":"A primary effect is damage done directly by the shaking or by the eruption itself: buildings collapsing, roads cracked and twisted, people killed and injured, land buried under lava or ash. A secondary effect happens as a result of a primary effect: fire from a fractured gas main, a tsunami from the displaced sea floor, a landslide on a weakened slope, disease from broken water pipes, jobs lost because the business premises have gone. Students lose marks by sorting effects by how quickly they happened rather than by what caused them — a tsunami arriving twenty minutes later is still secondary, and a business destroyed by the shaking itself is primary. It also helps to split each effect into social, economic and environmental. A top-level answer names the effect, attaches a real figure to it, and then says explicitly which kind it is and why, because the nine-mark question is won on the distinction, not on the list."},{"code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","h":"Immediate and long-term responses","t":"Immediate responses come in the first hours and days: search and rescue, treating the injured, recovering the dead, putting up emergency shelter, getting clean water, food and medicine in, restoring power, and accepting foreign urban search and rescue teams and aid. Long-term responses run for months and years: rehousing people permanently, rebuilding roads, ports, hospitals and schools, repairing the economy and the tourist industry, improving and — crucially — enforcing building codes, and preparing for the next event. The line between them is not fixed, but an examiner wants to see you know which is which. A top-level answer shows that responses depend on what survived: in Haiti the port and the hospitals were themselves destroyed, so the immediate response was crippled by the primary effects, whereas in Christchurch a working airport, working roads and a solvent government meant help arrived within the hour."},{"code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","h":"Two earthquakes, two levels of wealth","t":"The specification asks for named examples from two areas of contrasting wealth, and Haiti 2010 and Christchurch 2011 make the comparison unusually clean. Haiti, 12 January 2010: magnitude 7.0, focus 13 km deep, epicentre 25 km west of Port-au-Prince, striking at 16:53 when buildings were full. The Haitian government put the dead above 200,000, roughly 300,000 were injured and about 1.3 million were made homeless. Christchurch, 22 February 2011: magnitude 6.3, focus only 5 km deep and the epicentre only 10 km from the city centre — physically a nastier combination at the surface — yet 185 people died, 115 of them in the collapse of one office block. The difference is not the earthquake, it is the country: enforced building standards, emergency services on the scene within the hour, and household cover through the government-backed Earthquake Commission. A top-level answer makes that comparison explicitly in the same sentence, rather than describing Haiti and then describing Christchurch."},{"code":"3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard","h":"Why people stay","t":"People remain in tectonically active places partly because the hazard pays. Volcanic ash weathers into exceptionally fertile soil, which is why Java is farmed so intensively and why vines, lemons and pistachios do well on Etna. Volcanic areas offer geothermal heat and power — around nine in ten Icelandic homes are heated with geothermal water — as well as minerals and a tourist industry. They also stay because the alternatives are worse or impossible: family, work, land and community are there; many people cannot afford to move; huge cities such as Tokyo, Naples and San Francisco were built on these margins long ago and cannot be relocated; and in richer places people genuinely trust the monitoring and the building codes to protect them. A top-level answer separates the reasons that are pull factors, such as soil and geothermal energy, from the reasons that are really constraints, such as poverty and inertia, and says which of them applies in the example being used."},{"code":"3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard","h":"Monitoring, prediction, protection, planning","t":"Monitoring means instrumenting a volcano: seismometers to pick up the tremors made by moving magma, tiltmeters and satellite positioning to detect the ground bulging, and sensors for rising sulfur dioxide. Prediction is what monitoring buys you, and it works very differently for the two hazards. Volcanic eruptions can often be forecast days ahead — at Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines tens of thousands of people were evacuated before the climactic eruption of 15 June 1991, and the evacuation is widely credited with saving thousands of lives. Earthquakes cannot yet be reliably predicted; the best available is a warning system such as Japan's, which detects the first, faster seismic waves and gives seconds — enough to brake a bullet train or shut a gas valve, not enough to empty a city. Protection therefore carries the load for earthquakes: cross-braced steel frames, deep foundations, base isolation on rubber bearings, automatic shut-off valves, and, for lava, barriers and the seawater sprayed on the flow at Heimaey in Iceland in 1973. Planning ties it together: hazard maps, land-use zoning, emergency supplies, and drills such as Japan's on 1 September. A top-level answer judges these methods conditionally: they are highly effective where a government can afford to fund and enforce them, and close to useless where it cannot."}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing a distribution from Figure 2 without listing countries","setup":"The question: \"Using Figure 2, describe the global distribution of active volcanoes. [2 marks]\". Figure 2 is the world map in this room's resources: active volcanoes are black triangles and magnitude 6.0+ earthquakes are red dots, the symbols lie in narrow belts round the rim of the Pacific, down the middle of the Atlantic, and from the Mediterranean along the Himalayas; Hawaii sits alone in mid-Pacific, and Australia, northern Asia, Canada and Greenland are blank. AQA gives one mark for a basic statement about the distribution shown and a second for a developed point using detail from the map, and its mark schemes say there is no credit for simply listing names of countries.","steps":["Open by naming the shape of the whole pattern rather than a place: they sit in long, thin belts and are nowhere near evenly scattered.","Earn the developed mark by identifying which belt is biggest and tracing where it goes: the broadest, most continuous one encircles the entire Pacific Ocean.","Anchor that belt with ocean names and compass directions instead of a country list, because directions are map detail and a list is not.","Finish with what breaks the rule, which proves you read the whole map: an isolated cluster sits far out in mid-ocean, nowhere near a margin."],"cues":["Should your first sentence name a country, or say what shape the pattern has?","Which of the belts on Figure 2 is the widest and the longest?","Why would writing \"Chile, Japan, Indonesia\" score nothing on this question?","Which symbols on Figure 2 do not fit inside any belt?"]},{"title":"Turning Figure 3 into a chain of reasoning about a destructive margin","setup":"The question: \"Using Figure 3, explain the processes at a destructive plate margin that cause earthquakes and volcanic eruptions. [6 marks]\". Section B of Figure 3 shows a thin oceanic plate labelled 8 km thick with an arrow pointing right; a much thicker continental plate labelled 35 km thick with an arrow pointing left; the oceanic plate bending down beneath the continental plate into a deep ocean trench and continuing into the mantle, where shading shows it heating and melting; a steep-sided volcano standing above the melting with fold mountains behind it; and star symbols for earthquake foci lying along the sinking plate and getting deeper the further they are from the trench. Level 3 wants detailed explanation of formation with evidence taken from the resource; describing the diagram alone is capped at Level 1.","steps":["Begin from the property of the rock that decides everything else: the thinner plate is also the denser one, which is why it goes underneath rather than over the top.","Name the process and the landform it makes in the same sentence: this is subduction, and the bending of the descending plate is what opens the deep trench drawn at the margin.","Explain the earthquakes using the diagram's own evidence: the two plates lock together by friction, stress builds until the rock fractures, and the energy released shakes the surface — which is exactly why the foci track deeper away from the trench as the plate goes down.","Explain the eruptions as a separate chain: water carried down with the sinking plate lowers the melting point of the mantle above it, magma forms, and being less dense it rises through the plate overhead.","Close by saying why the cone is steep and the eruption violent: the magma is thick and full of gas, so it cannot flow away, pressure builds until it bursts out, and the sticky lava piles up around the vent instead of spreading."],"cues":["Which of the two plates in Section B goes underneath, and what property of the rock decides that?","What is that process called, and which landform on the diagram does it create?","The foci get deeper away from the trench — what does that tell you about where the earthquakes are happening?","Melting needs more than heat. What else does the sinking plate carry down with it?","Why is the cone here steep, when the volcano in Section A is gently sloping?"]},{"title":"Reading Figure 1 for a \"suggest why\" answer","setup":"The question: \"Using Figure 1, suggest why the effects of the Haiti earthquake were so much greater than those of the Christchurch earthquake. [6 marks]\". Figure 1 is the table in this room's resources. It gives Haiti, 12 January 2010: magnitude 7.0, focus 13 km deep, epicentre 25 km from the city centre, 16:53 local time, over 200,000 deaths on the Haitian government estimate, about 300,000 injured, about 1.3 million made homeless, a low-income country whose building regulations were almost never enforced and where household earthquake insurance was very rare. And Christchurch, 22 February 2011: magnitude 6.3, focus 5 km deep, epicentre 10 km from the city centre, 12:51 local time, 185 deaths, several thousand injured, a high-income country with long-established, enforced national building standards and standard household cover through the government-backed Earthquake Commission. The marks are AO2 and AO3, so every point has to come off the table.","steps":["Deal with the physical difference first, because it is the obvious one and because it is small: the Haitian event was 0.7 higher on the magnitude scale, so it released more energy.","Immediately show that this cannot be the whole answer: the New Zealand focus was the shallower of the two and its epicentre the closer to the city, both of which make shaking at the surface worse, and yet far fewer people were killed.","Move to the row that does the real work — income group — and convert it into a mechanism rather than a label: where there is little money, codes are not funded or enforced, so ordinary buildings pancake onto the people inside them.","Use the insurance row to reach the long-term effects: with almost no household cover, families had nothing to rebuild with, which is why the homelessness in the table was counted in years rather than months.","End on the comparison the table makes possible: a stronger but deeper and more distant earthquake killed more than a thousand times as many people, so wealth rather than magnitude explains the gap."],"cues":["Which single row of Figure 1 is the obvious physical difference between the two events?","Look at depth and distance. Do they support your first point or undermine it?","Which row of the table explains why buildings behaved so differently?","What does a family do after the shaking stops if nothing is insured?","In one sentence, what does Figure 1 prove about magnitude as a predictor of deaths?"]},{"title":"Building one Level 3 paragraph on primary versus secondary effects","setup":"The question: \"‘The primary effects of a tectonic hazard are always more significant than the secondary effects.’ To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use a named example in your answer. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]\". The named example is the Haiti earthquake of 12 January 2010: magnitude 7.0, focus 13 km deep, epicentre 25 km west of Port-au-Prince, striking at 16:53; over 200,000 deaths on the Haitian government estimate, about 300,000 injured, about 1.3 million made homeless; the port and the main hospitals destroyed; more than a million people living in crowded camps; and a cholera epidemic that began in October 2010 and killed roughly 10,000 people over the following decade. The mark scheme credits only effects, not causes or responses, and wants a discursive element on whether primary effects really are the more significant. Build one paragraph that would sit in Level 3.","steps":["Fix the claim the paragraph will argue before you choose any evidence: the killing was done by the primary effects, but the lasting damage was done by the secondary ones.","Anchor the primary side in one or two figures rather than a list, and say what made them primary: buildings came down in the shaking itself, and the government put the dead above 200,000.","Build the bridge that makes the paragraph geographical rather than a list: the destruction of the port and the hospitals was itself a primary effect, and it is what converted homelessness into disease.","Give the secondary effect its own date, because the gap in time is the argument: the cholera arrived in October 2010, nine months after the shaking had stopped.","Finish with the qualified mini-judgement the command phrase needs: over days the primary effects were the more significant, over a decade the secondary ones were, so the word in the statement that fails is \"always\"."],"cues":["What is this paragraph going to argue — that the statement is right, wrong, or right only for a while?","Which of the figures in the setup were caused directly by the ground shaking?","Why does the destroyed port belong on both sides of this argument?","How long after the earthquake did the cholera epidemic begin?","Which single word in the statement is the easiest one to disagree with?"]},{"title":"Reaching a judgement on risk reduction across two different hazards","setup":"The question: \"Evaluate the effectiveness of the ways in which the risks from tectonic hazards can be reduced. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]\". The evidence available to you: volcanoes can be monitored with seismometers, tiltmeters, satellite positioning and gas sensors, and at Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines tens of thousands of people were evacuated in the days before the climactic eruption of 15 June 1991, an evacuation widely credited with saving thousands of lives; earthquakes cannot yet be reliably predicted, only located, and Japan’s warning system detects the first, faster seismic waves and gives seconds of warning, enough to brake a train or shut a gas valve; enforced building standards and a fast emergency response held the Christchurch death toll to 185 at magnitude 6.3, while unenforced codes in Haiti contributed to over 200,000 deaths at magnitude 7.0. Plan an evaluation that reaches a judgement.","steps":["Split the question before you answer it, because the four methods do not work equally on the two hazards: forecasting works on volcanoes and does not yet work on earthquakes.","Prove the volcanic half with the strongest case you have, and say what the instruments actually detected: rising tremors and gas bought enough warning at Pinatubo for a mass evacuation before the climactic eruption.","Concede the limit honestly, because an evaluation that only praises cannot reach Level 3: the warning for an earthquake is measured in seconds rather than days, so it can stop a train but cannot empty a city.","Show that the weight therefore shifts onto protection and planning for earthquakes, and prove it by comparison: the smaller New Zealand earthquake killed 185 where the Haitian one killed far more, and enforced building standards are the difference.","Reach the judgement the command word demands, and make it conditional rather than flat: these methods are highly effective where a government can fund and enforce them and close to useless where it cannot, so wealth decides how well risk reduction works."],"cues":["Do all four methods work the same way on an earthquake as on a volcano?","What did the instruments on Pinatubo actually pick up before the eruption?","How much warning does an earthquake warning system give — days, hours or seconds?","Two earthquakes: one bigger, one far deadlier. What separates them?","Effective for whom? What does every one of these methods need before it works at all?"]}],"check":[{"q":"At which type of plate margin is new crust made, and what kind of eruption does it give?","a":"A constructive margin. Runny, low-gas basaltic magma gives frequent but gentle eruptions that build gently sloping volcanoes, together with shallow and usually weak earthquakes."},{"q":"A tsunami drowns a coastal town twenty minutes after an earthquake. Is that a primary or a secondary effect, and why?","a":"Secondary. It was not caused by the shaking of that town’s own ground; it followed on from a primary effect, the sudden displacement of the sea floor."},{"q":"Why did a magnitude 6.3 earthquake at Christchurch kill 185 people when a magnitude 7.0 earthquake in Haiti killed vastly more, even though the Christchurch focus was shallower and closer to the city?","a":"Because New Zealand is a high-income country with enforced earthquake-resistant building standards, emergency services on the scene within the hour and widespread insurance, while Haiti is a low-income country where codes existed on paper but were not enforced."},{"q":"Which of monitoring, prediction, protection and planning is least use against an earthquake, and why?","a":"Prediction. There is still no reliable way of saying when an earthquake will happen, only where earthquakes are likely; the best warning systems give seconds, not days."}]},"room":{"facts":["The three plate margins: constructive (plates move apart, magma rises, new crust forms, gentle eruptions, weak shallow earthquakes), destructive (denser oceanic plate is subducted into a trench and destroyed, violent eruptions, powerful earthquakes) and conservative (plates slide past each other, no crust made or destroyed, no volcanoes, powerful earthquakes).","A primary effect is caused directly by the shaking or the eruption itself; a secondary effect follows on from a primary effect. Immediate responses come in the first hours and days; long-term responses run for months and years.","Haiti, 12 January 2010: magnitude 7.0, focus 13 km deep, epicentre 25 km west of Port-au-Prince, 16:53 local time. Over 200,000 deaths on the Haitian government estimate, about 300,000 injured, about 1.3 million made homeless, and a cholera epidemic from October 2010 that killed roughly 10,000 people.","Christchurch, New Zealand, 22 February 2011: magnitude 6.3, focus 5 km deep, epicentre 10 km from the city centre, 12:51 local time. 185 deaths, 115 of them in the collapse of the Canterbury Television building, and an estimated 400,000 tonnes of liquefaction silt brought to the surface.","The four ways of reducing risk are monitoring, prediction, protection and planning. Volcanic eruptions can often be predicted days ahead from monitoring data; earthquakes cannot yet be reliably predicted at all.","The nine-mark question that closes Section A of Paper 1 — question 01.9, the only question on that paper assessed for SPaG — carries three further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology, and is marked in three levels: Level 1 Basic 1–3, Level 2 Clear 4–6, Level 3 Detailed 7–9.","Work at about a minute a mark, so a nine-mark answer plus its three SPaG marks deserves roughly eleven of Paper 1’s ninety minutes; a six-mark question deserves about seven."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following describes what happens at a conservative plate margin? Shade one circle only.\nA  Two plates move apart and new crust is formed.\nB  One plate is forced beneath another and crust is destroyed.\nC  Two plates slide past each other and no crust is made or destroyed.\nD  Two plates move apart and a deep ocean trench is formed.\n[1 mark]","a":"One mark for C. No credit if two or more answers are shaded. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"C. At a conservative margin the plates move past one another, either in opposite directions or in the same direction at different speeds. Nothing is added to the crust and nothing is subducted, which is why conservative margins have no volcanoes; the hazard is the earthquake produced when the friction holding the plates finally gives way.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Two of these four options are about crust being created or destroyed. A conservative margin is about neither.","Sort the options by what happens to the crust: made, destroyed, or neither.","Ask which option leaves the total amount of crust unchanged."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, describe the global distribution of active volcanoes. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic description relating to the distribution shown on the map, eg volcanoes lie in narrow belts rather than being spread evenly. Second mark for a developed point using detail from the map, eg the widest belt runs right round the rim of the Pacific Ocean. No credit for simply listing names of countries. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Active volcanoes on Figure 2 are not spread evenly over the map; they lie in narrow belts. The widest and most continuous of these belts runs right round the rim of the Pacific Ocean, from the far north Pacific down the western side of the Americas and back up the eastern edge of Asia. That is all the tariff will pay for: one basic statement of the distribution and one point developed with detail read off the map. Anything further — the Atlantic line, the empty interiors, Hawaii — earns nothing here and costs time that the nine-mark question needs.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The map is asking about a pattern, not about places. What word describes the overall shape of that pattern?","One mark is for a general statement about the distribution, the other for developing it with detail read off the map.","Decide first whether the symbols are evenly spread or concentrated, then say where the biggest concentration is."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes"]},{"q":"State what is meant by the term primary effect of a tectonic hazard. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for the idea of damage caused directly and immediately by the event itself, eg buildings destroyed by the ground shaking, land buried by lava. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"A primary effect is damage caused directly by the hazard itself — by the ground shaking, or by the lava, ash or pyroclastic flow of an eruption. Examples include buildings and bridges collapsing in the shaking, roads cracked and twisted, and people killed or injured by falling masonry.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the word \"primary\" is being contrasted with.","The test is what caused the damage, not how quickly it appeared.","Begin your definition with the word \"directly\"."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, calculate the difference between the depths of focus of the two earthquakes. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for 8 km. Must include units. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"13 km − 5 km = 8 km. The Haiti focus was 8 km deeper than the Christchurch focus. The single mark is for that figure with its unit, so the answer stops there; save what focal depth does to the shaking for a question that asks for it.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Find the row of Figure 1 that gives depth, and read both columns.","A difference means one value subtracted from the other.","The mark scheme will not accept a bare number here — check what the row is measured in."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes","3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why people continue to live in areas at risk from a tectonic hazard. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each valid reason, eg fertile soils weathered from volcanic ash; geothermal energy; minerals; tourism and the jobs it brings; family, work and roots; too poor to move; trust in monitoring and building codes. Credit two reasons only. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"One reason is that volcanic ash weathers into unusually fertile soil, so farming on volcanic slopes is more productive and more profitable than farming the land below. A second is that many people simply cannot afford to leave: their home, their job and their family are there, and moving costs money they do not have.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Some reasons are advantages of the place; others are barriers to leaving. You only need two.","Think about what a volcano puts into the ground, and what it brings to the local economy.","Start with what an eruption leaves behind on the land afterwards."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, suggest one reason why the Haiti earthquake caused far more deaths than the Christchurch earthquake. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a reason drawn from the table, eg Haiti was a low-income country. Second mark for developing it with the table, eg so building regulations were almost never enforced and ordinary buildings collapsed onto the people inside. Answers must use Figure 1; credit one reason only. AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Figure 1 shows that Haiti was a low-income country whose earthquake-resistant building regulations existed on paper but were almost never enforced, whereas New Zealand is a high-income country with long-established standards that are enforced. That means Haitian homes, schools and offices were not built to survive shaking, so they collapsed onto the people inside them at 16:53, when they were still full.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["You are asked to suggest, so the reason must come out of a row in the table, not out of memory.","Two rows of Figure 1 are about money and about buildings. They belong together.","Take the row on building regulations and ask what it means for a person standing inside a building."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, explain the processes at a destructive plate margin that cause earthquakes and volcanic eruptions. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Credit a logical chain of reasoning using the diagram: density difference, subduction, the trench, friction and the release of stress, foci deepening away from the trench, melting of the mantle above the sinking plate, gas-rich viscous magma and violent eruption from a steep-sided cone. Max Level 1 for description of the diagram alone.","sol":"In Section B of Figure 3 the oceanic plate is labelled 8 km thick and the continental plate 35 km thick, more than four times thicker; the oceanic plate is also the denser of the two. Because the arrows show them moving towards each other, the denser oceanic plate is forced down beneath the continental plate, a process called subduction, and the bending of the descending plate opens the deep ocean trench drawn at the margin.\nAs the plate descends it drags against the plate above it. The two are locked together by friction, so stress builds up until it becomes greater than the friction holding them. The rock then fractures, the stored energy is released as seismic waves, and the surface shakes. That is why Figure 3 shows the earthquake foci lying along the sinking plate and getting deeper the further they are from the trench: the foci simply mark where the plate has reached.\nDeeper still, the sinking plate heats up, and water driven out of it lowers the melting point of the mantle above, so magma forms. Being less dense than the surrounding rock, it rises through cracks in the continental plate. This magma is thick, sticky and full of gas, so it cannot flow away freely; pressure builds until the volcano erupts violently, and the viscous lava piles up around the vent to build the steep-sided cone shown above the melting zone.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate chains are wanted here, one ending in an earthquake and one ending in an eruption. Do not mix them.","Start each chain from a property of the rock: density decides which plate sinks, and the gas content of the magma decides how the volcano erupts.","Use the labels on Section B — the thicknesses and the depths of the foci — as the evidence for each link."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, explain why volcanoes are found at constructive plate margins but not at conservative plate margins. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Credit: at a constructive margin plates move apart, pressure falls, mantle melts, magma rises into the gap and erupts as runny basaltic lava building a gently sloping volcano; at a conservative margin plates slide past, no gap opens, nothing is subducted or melted, so no magma reaches the surface, although friction still causes earthquakes. Max Level 1 if the resource is not used.","sol":"Section A of Figure 3 shows the two plates with arrows pointing away from each other, moving apart a few centimetres a year. As they separate the pressure on the mantle beneath them falls, which allows the hot rock to melt, and the magma that forms rises into the gap. Some cools there to make new ocean floor, building the long ridge drawn in the diagram, and some reaches the surface. Because this magma is basaltic — runny and low in gas — it flows out easily rather than exploding, which is why Section A shows a gently sloping volcano with thin sheets of lava spreading from it.\nSection C is different in the one way that matters. The arrows both point along the margin, so the plates are sliding past each other rather than separating. No gap opens between them, so there is no drop in pressure and no melting, and because nothing is being pushed downwards nothing is subducted and heated either. With no route and no source for magma, no volcano can form, and Figure 3 shows none.\nThe plates still lock together as they slide, though. Stress builds until the rock snaps, which is why Section C shows a shallow focus and a road and fence broken and offset at the surface. A conservative margin therefore produces earthquakes without volcanoes.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The difference is about whether anything melts. Work out what has to happen to mantle rock before magma can form.","Two separate routes make magma: releasing pressure, and dragging water down with a sinking plate. Ask which of them Section C offers.","Begin with what happens to the pressure on the mantle when two plates pull apart."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, suggest why the effects of the Haiti earthquake were so much greater than those of the Christchurch earthquake. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks. Credit application of the table: the small difference in magnitude against the much shallower, closer Christchurch focus; income group and its consequences for construction, emergency services and insurance; time of day; the scale of homelessness. Answers that ignore Figure 1 are capped at Level 1.","sol":"The obvious difference in Figure 1 is magnitude: at 7.0 the Haiti earthquake was 0.7 higher than the Christchurch earthquake at 6.3, so it released considerably more energy. But the table shows that this cannot be the whole explanation. The Christchurch focus was only 5 km deep against 13 km in Haiti, and its epicentre was 10 km from the city centre against 25 km, so the shaking reaching the New Zealand city was concentrated and close. On the physical evidence alone Christchurch should have suffered badly, and 185 people died there while the Haitian government put its dead above 200,000.\nThe rows that explain the gap are the ones about wealth. Haiti was a low-income country whose building regulations were almost never enforced, so ordinary homes, schools and offices were not designed to survive shaking and collapsed onto the people inside at 16:53, when they were full. New Zealand’s long-established standards were enforced, so most buildings stayed up.\nWealth also shaped what happened afterwards. Figure 1 shows household earthquake insurance was very rare in Haiti but standard in New Zealand through the government-backed Earthquake Commission, so Haitian families had no money to rebuild with. That is why about 1.3 million people were made homeless in Haiti and stayed homeless for years, while the thousands displaced in Christchurch were rehoused.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two rows point one way and several rows point the other. Find the rows that contradict the magnitude row first.","Turn each row you use into a consequence for a real person: what does \"regulations not enforced\" mean for someone at work at 16:53?","Compare the depth and the epicentre distance before you say anything about wealth."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, suggest why people continue to live close to an active volcano. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Credit interpretation of the extract — fertile soil from weathered ash, high-value crops, tourism income, confidence in monitoring and warnings — developed with own understanding, eg geothermal energy, minerals, family and work ties. No credit for generic points that could not be drawn from the extract or from tectonic understanding.","sol":"Figure 4 shows that the hazard itself is what makes the place worth farming. The speaker says ash weathers into soil so rich that vines and lemons from the slopes fetch more than crops grown on the plain below, so the eruptions that threaten the village are also the reason its farms are profitable. The same eruptions bring in tourists to walk on the cooled lava, and the extract says they keep the shops and hotels open all summer, so the volcano supports two local industries rather than one.\nThe extract also explains why the risk feels manageable. Etna’s lava is runny and slow-moving, so the family can literally watch it come, and the observatory measures every tremor and gas emission, allowing the authorities to close the roads and the cable car days in advance. That monitoring turns a hazard into an inconvenience. Beyond the extract, people also stay because four generations of family, land and work are there and because moving costs money; in other volcanic regions the same pull is stronger still, with around nine in ten Icelandic homes heated by geothermal water.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The extract names two industries that depend on the volcano. Find both before you write anything.","Ask what it is about Etna’s lava, and about the monitoring, that makes the family feel safe enough to stay.","Begin with what the falling ash does to the soil over time."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard"]},{"q":"Explain how monitoring and prediction can reduce the risks from a volcanic eruption. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Credit the instruments and what each detects (seismometers and magma movement, tiltmeters and satellite positioning for ground deformation, gas sensors for rising sulfur dioxide, thermal imaging) and the chain through to a warning, an alert level and an evacuation before the eruption; credit a real example such as Mount Pinatubo, 15 June 1991. Max Level 1 for a list of instruments with no chain to reduced risk.","sol":"Monitoring means putting instruments on a volcano and watching them continuously. Seismometers record the small earthquakes made as magma forces its way through cracks beneath the mountain, and a rising number of tremors is the clearest sign that magma is on the move. Tiltmeters and satellite positioning measure the ground swelling as the magma chamber fills, and gas sensors pick up increases in sulfur dioxide escaping from the vent.\nOn their own these readings do nothing. What they buy is prediction: because the warning signs usually build over days or weeks before an eruption, scientists can raise an alert level, tell the civil authorities how much time they have, and draw the area that has to be cleared. Risk is then reduced because people and livestock are moved out of the danger zone before the eruption, roads and airspace are closed, and hospitals are put on standby.\nMount Pinatubo in the Philippines is the standard example. Monitoring in the weeks before the climactic eruption of 15 June 1991 allowed tens of thousands of people to be evacuated from the slopes in advance, and the evacuation is widely credited with saving thousands of lives. Monitoring cannot stop the eruption or protect the buildings, so the crops, homes and roads were still lost — it reduces the risk to people, not the hazard itself.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["An instrument reading is not a reduction in risk. Work out the steps that turn a reading into a life saved.","Take each instrument in turn and say what physical change in the volcano it is detecting.","Start with what happens underground before an eruption, and which instrument would notice it."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard"]},{"q":"‘The primary effects of a tectonic hazard are always more significant than the secondary effects.’ To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 1 and a named example in your answer. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, plus 3 SPaG. The command phrase is \"to what extent\", so the focus is an evaluation of the relative importance of primary and secondary effects, with a discursive element. Credit only effects, not causes or responses; expect a named example with supporting data and reference to Figure 1.","sol":"A primary effect is damage done directly by the shaking or the eruption; a secondary effect follows on from a primary one. Which of the two matters more depends on where the hazard strikes, and Figure 1 shows why: two earthquakes of similar size in countries of very different wealth produced death tolls more than a thousand times apart.\nIn Haiti on 12 January 2010 the primary effects were overwhelming. A magnitude 7.0 earthquake with a focus only 13 km deep struck 25 km from Port-au-Prince at 16:53, when schools, offices and markets were full. Unreinforced concrete buildings pancaked; the Haitian government put the death toll above 200,000, about 300,000 people were injured and roughly 1.3 million were made homeless, almost all of it inside a minute. Nothing that followed killed on that scale so quickly, so in the short term the primary effects were clearly the more significant.\nOver time, though, the balance shifts. Because the port and the main hospitals were themselves destroyed, aid could not be landed or distributed, and more than a million people were left in crowded camps without safe water. A cholera epidemic began in October 2010, nine months after the shaking stopped, and killed roughly 10,000 people over the following decade. Businesses that had lost their premises could not reopen, so incomes collapsed in what was already the poorest country in the Americas. Measured over ten years rather than one day, the secondary effects arguably did Haiti more harm than the earthquake itself.\nChristchurch shows the opposite balance. The magnitude 6.3 earthquake of 22 February 2011 killed 185 people, 115 of them in the collapse of a single office block, and liquefaction forced an estimated 400,000 tonnes of silt to the surface. But because New Zealand is a high-income country with enforced building standards, emergency services on the scene within the hour and household cover through the Earthquake Commission, no epidemic or famine followed. Its secondary effects were economic and psychological rather than fatal.\nOverall I agree only in part. Primary effects almost always cause the deaths and dominate the first days, so the statement holds at that timescale. Over months and years the secondary effects become the more significant, and the poorer the country the more true that becomes. The word in the statement that fails is \"always\".","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["\"To what extent\" is not a yes or no question. Decide what the statement is right about and what it is wrong about before you start.","Timescale is the key that unlocks this question: try judging the two kinds of effect over a day and then over a decade.","Sort your Haiti facts into two lists, one caused by the shaking itself and one caused by something the shaking had already destroyed."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth"]},{"q":"Assess the extent to which the responses to a tectonic hazard depend on the level of wealth of the area affected. Use named examples in your answer. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, plus 3 SPaG. \"Assess\" requires an informed overall judgement. Credit immediate and long-term responses in two contrasting examples (eg Haiti 2010 and Christchurch 2011), the mechanisms linking wealth to response capacity, and counter-arguments such as international aid, geography and governance.","sol":"Responses are usually divided into immediate ones, in the first hours and days, and long-term ones, running for months and years. Comparing Haiti in 2010 with Christchurch in 2011 shows that wealth shapes both, though it is not the only thing that does.\nThe immediate response in Haiti was crippled by the hazard itself. Port-au-Prince port was wrecked, the airport could take only limited traffic, roads were blocked and many hospitals had collapsed, so search and rescue in the first hours was carried out largely by survivors digging with their hands. Even the United Nations mission lost its headquarters and its head of mission. Foreign search and rescue teams and field hospitals arrived, but they arrived into a country with no functioning system to receive them, and more than a million people were living under tarpaulins within days.\nChristchurch on 22 February 2011 was the same kind of event handled by a solvent state. A national state of emergency was declared, emergency services and the army had cordoned the city centre within hours, and international urban search and rescue teams were on the ground within days. Water, chemical toilets and temporary housing were supplied; thousands of students organised themselves by social media into a volunteer army to shovel liquefaction silt out of the eastern suburbs.\nThe long-term gap is wider still. New Zealand set up a dedicated recovery authority, demolished well over a thousand unsafe buildings in the central city, bought out homes in the worst-hit suburbs and rebuilt to stricter standards, funded partly through the government-backed Earthquake Commission. Haiti depended on pledged foreign aid, much of which arrived slowly or was spent through foreign agencies, and hundreds of thousands of people were still in camps years afterwards.\nWealth is not the whole story: Haiti’s weak government, its dense unplanned capital and the destruction of the port would have hampered any response, and international aid can partly substitute for domestic money. But wealth is what buys enforcement, equipment, insurance and a state able to act on the day. I would judge that wealth is the single strongest control on how effective the response is, particularly in the long term, because recovery has to be paid for year after year long after the cameras have gone.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Split the answer by time before you split it by country, and keep immediate and long-term responses apart.","For each response you name, ask what it costs and who pays for it — that is the link back to wealth.","Look for what was destroyed in Haiti that a response would have needed to use."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of the ways in which the risks from tectonic hazards can be reduced. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, plus 3 SPaG. Credit monitoring, prediction, protection and planning, each judged rather than described, with the contrast between volcanic eruptions (predictable) and earthquakes (not), supporting examples, and a substantiated overall judgement. No credit for simply describing the hazards.","sol":"Risk can be reduced in four ways — monitoring, prediction, protection and planning — but they do not work equally well on the two tectonic hazards, and a fair evaluation has to separate them.\nMonitoring and prediction are at their most effective on volcanoes. Seismometers detect the tremors made by magma forcing its way upwards, tiltmeters and satellite positioning record the ground swelling as the magma chamber fills, and gas sensors register rising sulfur dioxide. Because these signs usually build over days or weeks, an eruption can be forecast. At Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines, monitoring before the climactic eruption of 15 June 1991 allowed tens of thousands of people to be evacuated from the slopes in advance, and that evacuation is widely credited with saving thousands of lives. The limitation is that monitoring saves people but not property: the farms, homes and roads were lost anyway.\nFor earthquakes, prediction barely works at all. Geologists can say where earthquakes are likely but not when. The best available is a warning system such as Japan’s, which detects the first and faster seismic waves and issues an alert seconds before the damaging ones arrive — long enough to brake a bullet train or close a gas valve, nowhere near long enough to empty a city.\nThat throws the weight onto protection and planning, and the evidence that they work is strong. Buildings can be cross-braced, given deep foundations, or set on rubber bearings that absorb the shaking; lava has been diverted, most famously by spraying seawater onto the flow that threatened the harbour at Heimaey in Iceland in 1973. Planning adds hazard maps, land-use zoning, emergency supplies and rehearsed drills. The clearest proof is the comparison between Christchurch and Haiti: the magnitude 6.3 earthquake at Christchurch in 2011, shallower and closer to the city than the Haitian one, killed 185 people, while the magnitude 7.0 earthquake in Haiti in 2010 killed vastly more, in a country where codes existed on paper but were not enforced.\nOverall these methods are highly effective, but conditionally so. Every one of them costs money to install, to staff and to enforce, and the countries most exposed are often least able to afford them. My judgement is therefore that protection and planning are the most effective measures against earthquakes and monitoring and prediction the most effective against eruptions, but that in both cases effectiveness is decided less by the technology than by whether a government can pay for it and make people use it.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["\"Evaluate\" means weighing how well each method works, so every method you name needs a verdict attached to it.","The two hazards behave differently under the same technique. Sort your evidence by hazard before you sort it by method.","Ask first what can be forecast days in advance and what cannot."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard"]},{"q":"Discuss the reasons why people continue to live in areas at risk from tectonic hazards. Use Figure 4 and your own understanding. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, plus 3 SPaG. \"Discuss\" requires a balanced, well-developed treatment: the genuine attractions (fertile soil, geothermal energy, minerals, tourism) set against the constraints (poverty, family and work ties, established cities, perception of risk and trust in management), using Figure 4 and a wider range of places.","sol":"People stay in tectonically active places for two very different kinds of reason: because the hazard brings real advantages, and because leaving is not a genuine option. A good answer has to weigh the two.\nThe advantages are substantial. Volcanic ash weathers into exceptionally fertile soil, and Figure 4 shows what that is worth: the farmer on Etna says the vines and lemons grown on the ash-rich slopes sell for more than anything grown on the plain below. The same is true on a far larger scale on Java, where soils weathered from volcanic ash support one of the most densely farmed landscapes on Earth. Volcanic areas also supply heat and power — around nine in ten Icelandic homes are heated with geothermal water — and valuable minerals, and they attract tourists. Figure 4 makes that second industry explicit: visitors who come to walk on the cooled lava flows keep the shops and hotels of the farmer’s town open all summer.\nManagement makes the risk feel acceptable. The farmer points out that Etna’s lava is runny and slow enough to watch approaching, that the observatory measures every tremor and gas emission, and that roads and the cable car are closed days in advance. In richer countries people trust building codes for the same reason, which is part of why Tokyo and San Francisco have gone on growing on active margins.\nBut much of the explanation is constraint rather than choice. The farmer’s family has worked the same slopes for four generations: land, work, school and community are all there, and in poorer countries people often have neither the savings nor anywhere to move to. Whole cities were established on these margins centuries before plate tectonics was understood, and cannot simply be relocated; about 670,000 people live inside the evacuation zone around Vesuvius. Perception matters too — an eruption or an earthquake that has not happened in living memory stops feeling real.\nDiscussing both sides together, the advantages explain why people first settled these places and the constraints explain why they remain, and the balance shifts with wealth. On Etna the decision looks like an informed choice supported by monitoring; in Haiti, staying in Port-au-Prince was not a decision at all. The honest conclusion is that people stay because the benefits are immediate and certain while the hazard is occasional and uncertain — and because, for most of them, moving was never really available.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["\"Discuss\" wants two sides. One of them is about what the place gives people; the other is about what stops them leaving.","Figure 4 supplies two industries and one reason the risk feels controlled. Find all three before adding your own examples.","Work out what falling volcanic ash does to soil over centuries, and what that is worth to a farmer."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard"]}],"exit":[{"q":"At which type of plate margin is crust destroyed?","a":"A destructive margin, where the denser oceanic plate is subducted beneath the continental plate."},{"q":"How many people died in the Christchurch earthquake of 22 February 2011?","a":"185."},{"q":"What is the name of the process in which shaking makes saturated soil behave like a liquid?","a":"Liquefaction."},{"q":"Which can currently be predicted days in advance: a volcanic eruption or an earthquake?","a":"A volcanic eruption. Earthquakes cannot yet be reliably predicted."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Constructive plate margin","back":"Two plates move apart. Magma rises into the gap and cools to form new crust, building a ridge. Gentle eruptions of runny basaltic lava and shallow, weak earthquakes. Example: the Mid-Atlantic Ridge at Iceland.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes","open":false},{"front":"Destructive plate margin","back":"Oceanic and continental plates move together. The denser oceanic plate is subducted into a trench and melts. Violent eruptions of thick, gas-rich lava from steep-sided cones, plus powerful earthquakes. Example: the Nazca plate beneath South America.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes","open":false},{"front":"Conservative plate margin","back":"Two plates slide past each other. No crust is made or destroyed, so there are no volcanoes. Friction locks the plates until the rock snaps, causing earthquakes. Example: the San Andreas Fault, California.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes","open":false},{"front":"Focus and epicentre","back":"The focus is the point underground where the rock fractures and the earthquake starts. The epicentre is the point on the surface directly above it. The shallower the focus, the stronger the shaking at the surface.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes","open":false},{"front":"Density of oceanic and continental crust","back":"Oceanic crust is thinner (around 8 km) but denser than continental crust (around 35 km thick). The denser plate is forced down into the mantle, bending to form a deep ocean trench.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the result of physical processes","open":false},{"front":"Primary effect of a tectonic hazard","back":"Damage caused directly by the hazard itself: buildings and bridges collapsing in the shaking, roads cracked and twisted, people killed or injured, land buried under lava or ash.","code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","open":false},{"front":"Secondary effect of a tectonic hazard","back":"Damage that follows on from a primary effect: fire from broken gas mains, tsunamis, landslides, disease from broken water pipes, lost jobs and trade, collapsed tourism.","code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","open":false},{"front":"Immediate responses to a tectonic hazard","back":"The first hours and days: search and rescue, treating the injured, emergency shelter, clean water, food and medicine, restoring power, foreign aid and urban search and rescue teams.","code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","open":false},{"front":"Long-term responses to a tectonic hazard","back":"Months and years: rehousing people, rebuilding roads, ports, hospitals and schools, repairing the economy and tourism, improving and enforcing building codes, preparing for the next event.","code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","open":false},{"front":"The Haiti earthquake, 12 January 2010","back":"Magnitude 7.0, focus 13 km deep, epicentre 25 km west of Port-au-Prince, at 16:53. Over 200,000 deaths on the government estimate, about 300,000 injured, about 1.3 million made homeless; cholera from October 2010.","code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","open":false},{"front":"The Christchurch earthquake, 22 February 2011","back":"Magnitude 6.3, focus only 5 km deep, epicentre 10 km from the city centre, at 12:51. 185 deaths, 115 of them in one collapsed office block, and widespread liquefaction.","code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","open":false},{"front":"Liquefaction","back":"Shaking makes saturated sandy soil behave like a liquid, so buildings tilt and sink and silt is forced to the surface. An estimated 400,000 tonnes of silt came up in Christchurch.","code":"3.1.1.2 — The effects of, and responses to, a tectonic hazard vary between areas of contrasting levels of wealth","open":false},{"front":"Monitoring a volcano","back":"Watching for warning signs with instruments: seismometers for the tremors made by moving magma, tiltmeters and satellite positioning for ground swelling, and sensors for rising sulfur dioxide.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard","open":false},{"front":"One way a building can be protected against earthquakes","back":"A cross-braced or flexible steel frame; rubber bearings under the foundations that absorb the shaking; a counterweight near the roof; automatic shut-off valves on gas and water pipes.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard","open":true},{"front":"One reason people continue to live in an area at risk from a tectonic hazard","back":"Fertile soils weathered from volcanic ash; geothermal energy, minerals and tourist income; family, work and land they cannot afford to leave; confidence in monitoring, warnings and building codes.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Management can reduce the effects of a tectonic hazard","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Table: two earthquakes in countries of contrasting wealth (written for this room; every figure is real and is quoted at the precision the published sources support)\n\nFeature | Haiti, 12 January 2010 | Christchurch, New Zealand, 22 February 2011\nMagnitude (moment magnitude) | 7.0 | 6.3\nDepth of focus | 13 km | 5 km\nDistance of epicentre from the city centre | 25 km | 10 km\nTime of day (local) | 16:53 | 12:51\nDeaths | over 200,000 (Haitian government estimate; independent estimates vary widely, from tens of thousands to over 300,000) | 185\nInjured | about 300,000 | several thousand\nPeople made homeless | about 1.3 million | thousands, rehoused in temporary accommodation\nIncome group of the country at the time | low income | high income\nEarthquake-resistant building regulations | on paper, but almost never enforced | long-established national building standards, enforced\nHousehold earthquake insurance | very rare | standard, through the government-backed Earthquake Commission","Figure 2 — Map: the world distribution of earthquakes and active volcanoes (written for this room; the pattern described is the real global pattern)\n\nA world map. Every earthquake of magnitude 6.0 or above recorded in recent decades is drawn as a red dot, and every active volcano on land as a black triangle. The symbols do not cover the map evenly; they lie in narrow belts.\nThe widest and most continuous belt runs right round the rim of the Pacific Ocean. Red dots and black triangles run together from Alaska and the Aleutian Islands, down the western side of the Americas through California, Mexico, Peru and Chile, and up the far side of the ocean from New Zealand through Indonesia, the Philippines and Japan back to the Aleutians.\nA second belt of red dots runs almost exactly down the middle of the Atlantic Ocean, from Iceland in the north to the far South Atlantic. It is narrow and the dots are close together. There are black triangles on Iceland, but hardly any elsewhere along this line.\nA third belt of red dots runs roughly west to east from the Mediterranean through Turkey and Iran and along the Himalayas into south-east Asia. There are black triangles in Italy, Greece and Turkey, but almost none along the Himalayan section.\nLarge areas carry no symbols at all: the interior of Australia, the interior of northern Asia, most of Canada and most of Greenland.\nOne group of black triangles breaks the pattern completely: Hawaii, sitting alone in the middle of the Pacific Ocean, thousands of kilometres from any belt.","Figure 3 — Diagram: three types of plate margin in cross-section (written for this room; the processes shown are real)\n\nThree labelled cross-sections drawn side by side, each showing the crust as a solid block with the mantle beneath it.\nSection A, labelled \"constructive\". Two plates lie beneath an ocean with arrows at the surface pointing away from each other and the label \"moving apart a few centimetres a year\". Magma rises into the gap between them and cools to make new ocean floor, building a long ridge. A gently sloping volcano sits on the ridge crest and runny lava spreads out from it in thin sheets. Star symbols marking earthquake foci sit close to the surface along the ridge.\nSection B, labelled \"destructive\". On the left is a thin oceanic plate labelled \"8 km thick\" with an arrow pointing right. On the right is a much thicker continental plate labelled \"35 km thick\" with an arrow pointing left. Where they meet, the oceanic plate bends down beneath the continental plate into a deep ocean trench and carries on down into the mantle, where shading shows it heating and melting. A steep-sided volcano stands on the continental plate above the melting, with fold mountains behind it. Star symbols marking earthquake foci lie along the sinking plate and get deeper the further they are from the trench.\nSection C, labelled \"conservative\". Two plates lie side by side, both arrows pointing the same way along the margin but one arrow drawn twice as long as the other. A vertical fault line runs between them, and a road and a fence drawn across the fault are shown snapped and offset. A star symbol marking an earthquake focus sits close to the surface. There is no volcano anywhere in this section and no magma is drawn.","Figure 4 — Text extract: a farmer on the slopes of Mount Etna, Sicily (written for this room; the speaker is invented, but the conditions described are real — Etna is the most active volcano in Europe and erupts in most years)\n\n\"My family has farmed the eastern slopes of Etna for four generations. The ash that falls after an eruption weathers into soil so rich that our vines and lemons sell for more than anything grown on the plain below, and the tourists who come up to walk on the cooled lava flows keep the shops and the hotels in our town open all summer. Etna erupts most years, but the lava is runny and it moves slowly enough that we can watch it come; the observatory measures every tremor and every puff of gas inside the mountain, and the authorities close the roads and the cable car days before they need to. What frightens us is not the lava. It is the shaking. In December 2018 an earthquake on the flank of the mountain damaged houses in the villages below ours in a few seconds, and there was no warning at all.\""]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 1 Question 01.9, 9 marks): ‘The primary effects of a tectonic hazard are always more significant than the secondary effects.’ To what extent do you agree?","items":["Question: ‘The primary effects of a tectonic hazard are always more significant than the secondary effects.’ To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 1 and a named example in your answer. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","Point: in Haiti the primary effects did the killing, but the secondary effects did the lasting damage, so the statement is true only over the shortest timescale.","Named evidence: the Haiti earthquake of 12 January 2010 — magnitude 7.0, focus 13 km deep, epicentre 25 km west of Port-au-Prince, striking at 16:53; the Haitian government’s death toll of over 200,000, about 300,000 injured and about 1.3 million made homeless; the destroyed port and hospitals; the cholera epidemic that began in October 2010 and killed roughly 10,000 people over the decade that followed.","The developed paragraph: \"The primary effects in Haiti were the deadliest part of the disaster and they were over in under a minute. A magnitude 7.0 earthquake with a focus only 13 km below the surface struck 25 km from Port-au-Prince at 16:53, when schools, offices and markets were still full, and unreinforced concrete buildings pancaked onto the people inside them; the Haitian government put the dead above 200,000 and about 300,000 more were injured. But the same shaking also destroyed the port and the main hospitals, and that is where the argument turns, because destroying the means of responding is a primary effect whose consequences are entirely secondary. With the port wrecked, aid could not be landed; with the hospitals gone, the injured could not be treated; and with about 1.3 million people forced into crowded camps without safe water, a cholera epidemic broke out in October 2010, nine months after the shaking had stopped, and went on to kill roughly 10,000 people over the following decade. Judged over a single day the primary effects were plainly the more significant, since they caused almost every death. Judged over ten years the secondary effects did the greater harm, because they attacked a country that had already lost the buildings, the port and the incomes it would have needed to recover.\"","Link to the question: the paragraph answers \"to what extent\" by supplying a timescale at which the statement is true and one at which it fails, instead of simply agreeing or disagreeing.","Mini-judgement: over days the primary effects were the more significant; over a decade the secondary ones were; the word in the statement that cannot be defended is \"always\".","Why an examiner would place this in Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): AO1 is met by detailed knowledge of both primary and secondary effects with supporting data; AO2 by thorough understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes — the port and the hospitals connect the shaking to the epidemic; AO3 by a coherent and reasoned evaluation of the relative significance of the two, with a judgement qualified by timescale. It also credits only effects, as the mark scheme requires, and would attract High performance SPaG for consistent accuracy and a wide range of specialist terms (focus, epicentre, magnitude, primary, secondary)."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same. ‘The primary effects of a tectonic hazard are always more significant than the secondary effects.’ To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 1 and a named example in your answer. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","The weaker paragraph: \"I agree with the statement because primary effects are worse. In the Haiti earthquake lots of buildings fell down and a lot of people died and were injured. Loads of people were made homeless and had nowhere to live. It happened because Haiti is on a plate margin and the plates moved, which made the ground shake really badly. Afterwards there were secondary effects too, like diseases and people not having jobs, and lots of countries sent aid and rescue teams to help them. So the primary effects were more significant.\"","Which descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic, 1–3). AO1 is \"limited knowledge of the primary and secondary effects\" — \"lots of buildings\", \"a lot of people\" and \"diseases\" are effects, but there is not one date, magnitude, place name or figure anywhere. AO2 is \"slight geographical understanding\": nothing connects one effect to another.","Which descriptor it misses, and exactly how: AO3 at Level 3 asks for evaluation of the \"relative significance\" of primary and secondary effects \"in a coherent and reasoned way\". This answer asserts a verdict in its first and last sentences and never compares the two, so there is no reasoning to reward, and it ignores the word \"always\" that the command phrase is built around.","Two further faults an examiner would mark: it drifts into causes (\"the plates moved\") and into responses (\"countries sent aid\"), and the mark scheme for this question says plainly that only effects are credited, so those sentences earn nothing at all. It also never refers to Figure 1 or to any named example in the sense the mark scheme means — naming the country is not the same as knowing the event.","What one change would move it up a level: attach a real figure to each effect and then set the two kinds against each other over different timescales. Replacing \"a lot of people died\" with \"the government put the dead above 200,000, almost all in the first minute\" and \"diseases\" with \"cholera from October 2010, which killed roughly 10,000 people\" supplies the AO1 detail; adding \"so over a day the primary effects mattered more, but over a decade the secondary ones did\" supplies the AO3 evaluation.","On SPaG: the writing here is accurate enough for Threshold or Intermediate performance, but \"worse\", \"loads of\" and \"really badly\" are not specialist terms. High performance requires \"a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate\" — focus, epicentre, magnitude, liquefaction, primary, secondary, immediate, long-term."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the Haiti earthquake, 12 January 2010 (the lower-income named example)","items":["Date and time: 12 January 2010 at 16:53 local time — late afternoon, with schools, offices and markets still occupied.","Magnitude and focus: magnitude 7.0, with a shallow focus about 13 km below the surface.","Location: the epicentre lay about 25 km west of Port-au-Prince, Haiti’s capital, near the town of Léogâne.","Plate setting: Haiti sits in the boundary zone between the Caribbean and North American plates, on a conservative (strike-slip) fault system — the Enriquillo–Plantain Garden fault zone — so the hazard here is earthquakes, not volcanoes. That is the account AQA credits; the USGS attributes the 2010 rupture itself to a combination of left-lateral strike-slip and reverse movement, partly on a fault that had not been mapped before.","Level of development: Haiti was the poorest country in the Americas before the earthquake, classed by the World Bank as low income.","Primary effects: an official Haitian government death toll of over 200,000 (independent estimates vary very widely, from tens of thousands to over 300,000); about 300,000 people injured; about 1.3 million made homeless; hundreds of thousands of poorly built concrete homes destroyed or damaged, along with the Presidential Palace, the cathedral, the main prison and many government buildings; the port badly damaged.","Secondary effects: aid could not be landed or distributed because the port, the roads and many hospitals were destroyed; more than a million people lived in crowded camps; a cholera epidemic that began in October 2010 and killed roughly 10,000 people over the following decade; businesses lost their premises and incomes collapsed; schooling was interrupted for hundreds of thousands of children.","Immediate responses: search and rescue in the first hours was carried out largely by survivors with hand tools; the United Nations mission in Haiti lost its own headquarters and its head of mission; foreign urban search and rescue teams and field hospitals arrived within days, but into a country whose airport and port could barely receive them.","Long-term responses: billions of US dollars pledged internationally for reconstruction; new building codes drawn up, though enforcement remained weak; camps cleared only slowly, with hundreds of thousands still displaced years afterwards; cholera treatment and water and sanitation programmes.","The exam point this example proves: a shallow, moderate-to-large earthquake beneath a poor, densely built capital produces catastrophic primary effects and secondary effects that outlast them by a decade."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the Christchurch earthquake, 22 February 2011 (the higher-income named example)","items":["Date and time: 22 February 2011 at 12:51 local time — lunchtime on a working day.","Magnitude and focus: magnitude 6.3, with a very shallow focus about 5 km below the surface.","Location: the epicentre lay about 10 km south-east of the centre of Christchurch, on New Zealand’s South Island — closer to the city and shallower than the Haitian earthquake, and therefore physically a severe combination.","Plate setting: New Zealand straddles the boundary between the Pacific and Australian plates; in the South Island this is largely a conservative margin along the Alpine Fault, and the 2011 earthquake ruptured a previously unmapped fault in the crust near the city.","The earlier, larger earthquake (the mainshock of the sequence): a magnitude 7.1 earthquake had struck the Canterbury region on 4 September 2010, and the 22 February 2011 earthquake was one of its aftershocks; the 2010 shock was deeper, further from the city and at 4:35 in the morning, and it killed nobody directly — a useful reminder that magnitude alone does not decide the death toll.","Level of development: New Zealand is a high-income country with long-established, enforced earthquake-resistant building standards.","Primary effects: 185 deaths, 115 of them in the collapse of the Canterbury Television building; several thousand injured; the spire of Christchurch Cathedral brought down; widespread damage across the central business district; most of the city lost power and water supply.","Secondary effects: liquefaction on a huge scale, with an estimated 400,000 tonnes of silt forced to the surface, wrecking roads, water pipes and sewers in the eastern suburbs; businesses shut for months or permanently; a rebuild running to tens of billions of New Zealand dollars; long-term stress and mental-health effects among residents.","Immediate responses: a national state of emergency declared; emergency services and the army cordoning the city centre within hours; international urban search and rescue teams arriving from Australia, Japan, the United States and the United Kingdom; temporary water supplies and chemical toilets; a Student Volunteer Army of thousands organised through social media to shovel silt.","Long-term responses: a dedicated government recovery authority set up for Canterbury; the central-city cordon kept for many months and well over a thousand unsafe buildings demolished; homes in the worst-affected eastern suburbs declared unsuitable to rebuild on and bought out by the government; payouts through the government-backed Earthquake Commission, which most households pay into through their insurance; new buildings designed to stricter standards, many on base isolators.","The exam point this example proves: a shallower, closer earthquake of smaller magnitude in a rich country killed 185 people rather than hundreds of thousands, because the buildings, the emergency services and the insurance system were all in place beforehand."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the processes, terms and places this room is examined on","items":["Plate tectonics: the crust is broken into plates that move a few centimetres a year over the mantle, driven by heat-driven movement in the mantle and by the pull of cold, dense oceanic plate sinking back into it.","Two kinds of crust: continental crust is about 35 km thick, old and less dense and is not destroyed; oceanic crust is about 8 km thick, young and denser and is destroyed by subduction.","Constructive margin: plates move apart; reduced pressure melts the mantle; magma rises into the gap and makes new crust; runny basaltic lava gives frequent, gentle eruptions and gently sloping volcanoes; earthquakes are shallow and usually weak. Example: the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where the North American and Eurasian plates separate beneath Iceland.","Destructive margin: the denser oceanic plate is subducted beneath the continental plate into a deep ocean trench; friction produces powerful earthquakes whose foci deepen away from the trench; water driven from the sinking plate melts the mantle above, making thick, gas-rich magma and violent eruptions from steep-sided cones, with fold mountains behind. Example: the Nazca plate subducting beneath the South American plate to form the Andes.","Conservative margin: plates slide past each other, in opposite directions or in the same direction at different speeds; no crust is made or destroyed and there are no volcanoes; friction locks the plates until the rock fractures, giving powerful shallow earthquakes. Example: the San Andreas Fault in California, between the Pacific and North American plates.","Focus, epicentre, seismic waves: the focus is where the rock fractures underground, the epicentre the point on the surface directly above it, and seismic waves the energy that travels out from the focus. A shallow focus usually means stronger shaking at the surface.","Distribution: about three-quarters of the world’s active volcanoes and around nine-tenths of its earthquakes lie around the rim of the Pacific Ocean. Hawaii is the well-known exception, a volcanic group far from any plate margin.","Key terms for effects and responses: primary effect (caused directly by the hazard), secondary effect (caused by a primary effect), immediate response (first hours and days), long-term response (months and years); liquefaction, tsunami, landslide, pyroclastic flow and lahar are the secondary hazards most often credited.","Monitoring: seismometers for the tremors made by moving magma, tiltmeters and satellite positioning for ground deformation, gas sensors for rising sulfur dioxide, thermal imaging for heat.","Prediction: volcanic eruptions can often be forecast days or weeks ahead — tens of thousands were evacuated before the climactic eruption of Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines on 15 June 1991, saving thousands of lives. Earthquakes cannot yet be reliably predicted; Japan’s warning system detects the first, faster seismic waves and gives only seconds.","Protection: earthquake-resistant design — cross-bracing and flexible steel frames, deep foundations, rubber bearings (base isolation), a counterweight near the roof, automatic shut-off valves. For lava, barriers and water cooling: at Heimaey in Iceland in 1973 seawater was sprayed onto the advancing lava to slow it and save the harbour.","Planning: hazard maps and land-use zoning, emergency supply kits, rehearsed evacuation drills (Japan holds national drills on 1 September), trained emergency services, and insurance such as New Zealand’s government-backed Earthquake Commission.","Why people stay: fertile soils weathered from volcanic ash (Etna in Sicily, the farmland of Java); geothermal energy — around nine in ten Icelandic homes are heated with geothermal water; minerals and tourism; family, work and land; poverty and the cost of moving; huge cities already built on margins, with about 670,000 people living inside the evacuation zone around Vesuvius; and trust in monitoring and building codes."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — ‘The primary effects of a tectonic hazard are always more significant than the secondary effects.’ To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 1 and a named example in your answer. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","items":["Decode the command phrase: \"to what extent\" asks how far the claim holds, not whether it holds. Flat agreement and flat disagreement score the same. The examiner wants a discursive element weighing the relative importance of the two kinds of effect, so you need a variable that makes the statement true in one case and false in another — timescale is the obvious one, and level of wealth is the second.","Decode the restrictions: the mark scheme for this question credits only effects. Every sentence about why the plates moved, and every sentence about aid, rescue teams or rebuilding, earns nothing. Check your plan and delete causes and responses before you start writing.","Define in the first two sentences: primary effects are caused directly by the shaking or the eruption; secondary effects follow on from a primary effect. State your line straight away — the statement holds in the first days and fails over the following years.","Paragraph 1 — the primary case, using Haiti 2010: magnitude 7.0, focus 13 km deep, 25 km from Port-au-Prince, striking at 16:53 when buildings were full; buildings pancaked; the government put the dead above 200,000, about 300,000 injured, about 1.3 million made homeless, almost all inside a minute. Mini-judgement: over the first days nothing else came close.","Paragraph 2 — the secondary case, same example so the comparison is fair: the port and the main hospitals were destroyed, so people stayed in crowded camps without safe water; cholera arrived in October 2010, nine months later, and killed roughly 10,000 people over the decade; businesses lost their premises and incomes collapsed. Mini-judgement: measured over ten years these did the greater harm.","Paragraph 3 — the contrast that stops the answer being one case study, using Figure 1 and Christchurch 2011: magnitude 6.3, focus 5 km deep, epicentre 10 km from the city centre, 185 deaths, 115 of them in a single collapsed office block, an estimated 400,000 tonnes of liquefaction silt — but no epidemic and no famine, because enforced building standards, fast emergency services and Earthquake Commission cover were already in place. Mini-judgement: in a rich country the secondary effects are economic rather than fatal, so the balance itself depends on wealth.","The evaluation thread that runs through all three paragraphs: the significance of an effect depends on the timescale you measure it over and on the ability of the place to absorb it. Say it once at the end of each paragraph rather than saving it all for the conclusion — the AO3 marks are awarded across the answer, not just at the bottom.","Conclusion: it must answer the question rather than summarise. Say that the statement is right for the first hours and days, when primary effects cause almost all the deaths, and wrong over months and years, when secondary effects dominate — and that the poorer the country, the sooner and the harder that switch happens. Name the word that fails: \"always\".","Timing: about a minute a mark, so allow roughly eleven minutes for the nine marks plus the three SPaG marks. Spend one of them planning the three paragraphs, nine writing, and one checking spelling and punctuation, because the SPaG marks require consistent accuracy and a wide range of specialist terms — focus, epicentre, magnitude, liquefaction, primary, secondary."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:56:49Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file, not the stale copy in the judge prompt: all nine fixes are present and correct, the two errors that capped the last verdict are gone (the 4 September 2010 magnitude 7.1 Darfield shock is now correctly the mainshock with 22 February 2011 as its aftershock, and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge sentence now agrees with Figure 2), the pistachios are out of Figure 4 and out of the two solutions that quoted them while the flank-neutral mention in lesson.idea[6] is true and can stay, and the new claim in room.facts[5] that 01.9 is the only SPaG-assessed question on Paper 1 is confirmed by the specimen mark scheme, which carries exactly one SPaG grid and attaches it to 01.9 with AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3. I re-checked every fact, date, figure and process and re-counted every countable claim against the printed resources — 13 km minus 5 km = 8 km, 7.0 minus 6.3 = 0.7, 35 km against 8 km as 'more than four times thicker', over 200,000 against 185 as 'more than a thousand times', and every Figure 1 row, Figure 3 section and Figure 4 quotation reproduced in the questions, worked examples, model paragraphs and plan — and found nothing false. Publishable; the one thing left is a five-minute tidy so the Figure 2 worked example stops asking for the Hawaii point that the trimmed 2-mark solution says earns nothing."}}},"3.1.1.3":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.1.3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Weather hazards is the half of Paper 1 Question 1 that examiners set most often on a figure, and it carries one of the two case-study demands in the whole of the natural hazards section. You have to be able to trace global atmospheric circulation from rising equatorial air out to the trade winds, explain why tropical storms form in some places and nowhere else, carry one named storm with real figures into a judgement about primary and secondary effects, and argue about whether the weather in the UK really is becoming more extreme. Every one of those is a chain of reasoning rather than a list, and the marks go to the student who keeps the chain moving from cause to consequence and then answers the question that was asked.","idea":[{"h":"Three cells, four pressure belts, and the winds between them","code":"3.1.1.3 — Global atmospheric circulation helps to determine patterns of weather and climate","t":"The sun heats the Earth most strongly at the equator, so air there is warmed, becomes less dense and rises. Rising air cools, its water vapour condenses, and the result is towering cloud, daily downpours and a belt of low pressure at 0°. That air spreads out at high altitude, moves polewards and, having lost its moisture, sinks again at about 30° north and south. Sinking air warms, its relative humidity falls and cloud cannot form, so at 30° there is a belt of high pressure, clear skies and the world's band of hot deserts. The loop between them is the Hadley cell. Between 30° and 60° the Ferrel cell drives surface winds polewards; at about 60° warm air from the Ferrel cell meets cold air coming down from the Polar cell, is forced to rise along the polar front, and creates a second belt of low pressure, which is why the UK sits in a wet, changeable westerly airstream. Surface winds always blow from high pressure to low pressure and are deflected by the Coriolis effect: to the right in the northern hemisphere, to the left in the southern. That is why the winds blowing back towards the equator arrive from the north-east in the north and the south-east in the south, and are called the trade winds. A top-level answer never just labels the cells: it uses them as the engine, showing how rising air at one latitude and sinking air at another produce the rainfall, the deserts and the winds that the question is really about."},{"h":"Why a storm needs warm ocean, spin and quiet air above","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions","t":"A tropical storm is a heat engine running on the ocean. It needs sea surface temperatures of at least 27 °C, and that warmth must reach about 60 m down, otherwise the storm churns up cold water and starves itself. It needs air converging near the surface, which the trade winds of the two Hadley cells provide where they meet. It needs the Coriolis effect to be strong enough to make the rising column spin, which is why storms form between roughly 5° and 30° north and south but never on the equator itself, where the Coriolis effect is zero. Finally it needs low wind shear, that is, winds that do not change much with height, so the growing column is not torn apart. The sequence is: warm water evaporates; the moist air rises rapidly in a spiralling column; as it rises it cools, condenses and releases latent heat; that heat warms the surrounding air and makes it rise faster still, drawing in more moist air below. Air pressure at the centre falls, the pressure gradient steepens and the winds accelerate. When sustained winds reach 119 km/h the system is classed as a category 1 storm on the Saffir-Simpson scale; category 5 begins at 252 km/h. Storms drift west with the trade winds, then curve polewards, and die when they cross land or cooler seas because the supply of warm, moist air is cut off and friction slows them. A top-level answer tells that story as a sequence with a cause at every step, and uses the same engine to explain what climate change might do to it."},{"h":"Primary, secondary, immediate, long-term: four boxes that earn marks","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms have significant effects on people and the environment","t":"Examiners want two distinctions held apart. Primary effects are caused directly by the storm itself: buildings flattened by wind, people drowned by the storm surge, crops stripped by rain. Secondary effects follow on from those: no clean water because the pipes broke, disease because the water is contaminated, unemployment because the fishing boats are gone, food prices rising because the harvest went. Responses split the same way. Immediate responses happen in hours and days: evacuation before landfall, search and rescue, emergency shelter, water and medicine flown in. Long-term responses take months and years: rebuilding homes to a stronger standard, replanting mangroves, moving settlements out of surge zones, rebuilding the economy. Risk itself is reduced by four things the specification names. Monitoring uses satellites and aircraft to watch a storm as it grows. Prediction uses computer models to forecast its track, so warnings can be issued days ahead. Protection is physical: reinforced buildings, storm shutters, levees, houses on stilts, restored mangroves that absorb surge energy. Planning is organisational: evacuation routes, shelters, emergency supply kits, drills and education. A top-level answer carries one named storm with real dates and figures through those boxes and, when the command word asks, weighs them against each other rather than listing them."},{"h":"Why a small island gets so many kinds of bad weather","code":"3.1.1.3 — The UK is affected by a number of weather hazards","t":"The UK sits at about 50° to 60° north, under the polar front, where warm tropical air meets cold polar air and the jet stream steers one Atlantic depression after another across it. That is why the dominant hazards are rain and wind. Heavy or prolonged rainfall causes river and surface-water flooding; deep depressions bring gales and severe gales, and the Met Office has named the strongest since 2015 so that warnings travel faster. Cold air arriving from the north or east brings snow, ice and extreme cold, which close roads and railways and burst pipes. In summer, high pressure sitting over the country for days brings heatwaves and, if it lasts, drought, which strains water supply and dries soils; the same heat fuels thunderstorms, lightning and hail. Fog forms on calm, clear nights and disrupts transport. Each hazard has its own impacts: floods damage property and contaminate water, gales bring down power lines and trees, snow closes schools and costs businesses trading days, heat raises death rates among the elderly. A top-level answer names the hazard, names the weather system that causes it, and takes the impact through to the people or the economy affected, rather than stopping at the weather."},{"h":"One event in detail, then the argument about the trend","code":"3.1.1.3 — Extreme weather events in the UK have impacts on human activity","t":"The specification wants a recent UK extreme weather event studied properly: its causes, its social, economic and environmental impacts, and how management reduced the risk. The Somerset Levels floods of winter 2013 to 2014 do all of that. The cause was physical and human together: a jet stream that steered depression after depression across southern England through a winter that was the wettest in the England and Wales rainfall series begun in 1766, falling on flat land that lies barely above sea level, drained by rivers that had not been dredged for years and whose outlets are held shut by the huge tides of the Bristol Channel twice a day. The impacts were social (about 600 homes flooded, villages such as Muchelney reachable only by boat, people out of their houses for months), economic (around 6,900 hectares of farmland under water, livestock moved, roads including the A361 closed) and environmental (stagnant, contaminated water that had to be treated before it could be pumped back into the rivers, and debris left across the fields). The management that followed included dredging 8 km of the Parrett and the Tone, raising roads and river banks, more pumping capacity, a new Somerset Rivers Authority in 2015 and a planned tidal barrier at Bridgwater. The second half of this idea is an argument, not a fact: the evidence that UK weather is becoming more extreme includes 40.3 °C at Coningsby on 19 July 2022, the first UK temperature above 40 °C, and 341.4 mm of rain in 24 hours at Honister Pass during Storm Desmond in December 2015; the evidence against includes the 1976 drought, the 1987 storm and the fact that a run of records is not by itself a long-term trend. A top-level answer gives both sides and then commits to a position."}],"examples":[{"title":"Building the chain of reasoning from Figure A to the world's hot deserts","setup":"Figure A is a cross-section of global atmospheric circulation from the equator to the North Pole. It shows air rising at 0° with the label air rises, low pressure, heavy rainfall; air sinking at 30° N with the label air sinks, high pressure, clear dry skies; air rising again at 60° N; air sinking at 90° N; and a surface arrow from 30° N back to the equator labelled north-east trade winds. The question is worth 6 marks: Using Figure A and your own understanding, explain why the world's major hot deserts are found at about 30° north and south of the equator. Work out the chain of reasoning the mark scheme rewards.","steps":["Take the upward arrow at the equator as the first link: the sun is always high in the sky there, so its energy is concentrated on a small area of surface and heats it intensely, the air above it expands, becomes less dense and rises.","Follow that air along the top of the Hadley cell: as it rises it cools, its water vapour condenses into towering cloud and falls as heavy rainfall, so the air moving polewards at altitude has lost nearly all its moisture.","Now use the downward arrow the figure draws at 30° N: this dry air sinks, and sinking air is compressed and warms, so its relative humidity falls further and cloud cannot form.","Turn that into pressure, because the figure labels it: sinking air presses down on the surface, which is why a belt of high pressure sits at that latitude, and high pressure means clear skies, strong insolation and almost no rain.","Close the chain with the conclusion the question asked for: because this belt of sinking, drying air sits over the same latitudes all year, the Sahara, Arabian, Kalahari and Australian deserts all lie in it, in both hemispheres."],"cues":["What does the arrow drawn at the equator tell you the air is doing, and why?","What happens to the moisture in that air while it is rising and cooling?","What does the figure show the air doing at 30° north, and what does that do to cloud?","What kind of surface pressure does sinking air create, and what weather comes with it?","Which deserts can you name at those latitudes to finish the answer?"]},{"title":"Turning a satellite image into a 24-hour forecast","setup":"Figure B is a satellite image taken at 06:00 on 12 September. It shows a spiral of cloud about 500 km across turning anticlockwise, with a clear circular hole about 40 km across at its centre, a thick unbroken ring of the brightest cloud around that hole, and spiral bands of cloud beyond the ring. The port town of Port Esperanza is marked on a coastline 250 km west of the storm centre, and an arrow drawn from the centre towards the coast is labelled storm moving west at about 20 km per hour. The leading edge of the outermost band has just reached the coast, but over the town the sky is otherwise still clear apart from a few small clouds. The question is worth 4 marks: Using Figure B, suggest how the weather at Port Esperanza will change during the next 24 hours.","steps":["Use the distance and the speed together before anything else: the storm is travelling west and the town lies 250 km west of the centre, so at about 20 km per hour the centre needs roughly 12 to 13 hours to reach the coast, about half way through the forecast period; the outer bands arrive long before that, and because the spiral is 500 km across its rear edge will still be over the town when the 24 hours end.","Deal with the sky over the town first, because the image shows it: for the first few hours expect thickening cloud as the outer bands come in, a wind that freshens from the north as the air spirals anticlockwise in towards the centre, and air pressure beginning to fall.","Read the spiral bands next: as they arrive the cloud becomes towering cumulonimbus, bringing torrential downpours, thunder and lightning, damaging gusts and a rapidly falling pressure.","Read the bright unbroken ring as the eyewall: it brings the strongest winds and the heaviest rain of all, and because the town is on the coast the very low central pressure and the winds driving water ahead of the storm will raise a storm surge and flood low ground.","Finish with the clear hole in the centre: when it passes over, the town gets a short calm, almost cloudless spell with light winds, and then the far side of the ring returns with winds of similar strength blowing from the opposite direction, now out of the south, followed by the rain bands of the rear half of the storm."],"cues":["How long will the centre take to reach the town, and what will reach the town before it?","What should the weather over the town do in the first few hours, as the outer bands arrive?","What weather comes with the spiralling bands of tall cloud?","What is the brightest unbroken ring, and what does it bring to a coastal town?","What happens when the clear hole in the middle passes overhead, and what follows it?"]},{"title":"Reading a transect table and explaining what makes the wind blow","setup":"Figure C is a table of a transect across a tropical storm with three columns: distance from the centre in km, average wind speed in km/h, and air pressure in hPa. The rows read: 0, the eye, 20, 900; then 25, 250, 915; then 50, 210, 935; then 100, 150, 960; then 200, 90, 985; then 300, 45, 1000; then 400, 25, 1010. The question is worth 6 marks: Using Figure C, describe the pattern of air pressure across the storm and explain how it produces the storm winds.","steps":["Read the pressure column downwards and state the pattern: it is lowest at the centre, 900 hPa, and rises steadily outwards to 1010 hPa at the edge of the transect.","Quantify it, because the mark scheme rewards evidence taken from the resource: that is a rise of 110 hPa over 400 km, an extremely steep pressure gradient.","Now pair that with the wind column so the two patterns sit side by side: the wind is only 20 km/h in the eye, peaks at 250 km/h just 25 km out, then falls away to 25 km/h at the outer edge.","Give the mechanism that links them: air always flows from higher to lower pressure, and the steeper the gradient the faster it flows, so the strongest winds sit where pressure changes most sharply, immediately around the eye.","Account for the exception, because an examiner looks for it: in the eye itself the air is sinking rather than rushing inwards, so despite having the lowest pressure of all it has light winds and clear skies.","Name the process that makes the whole thing spiral: the Coriolis effect deflects the inrushing air, so it spirals in anticlockwise in the northern hemisphere instead of blowing straight to the centre."],"cues":["What is the pattern in the pressure column as you move away from the centre?","What is the size of that pressure change, and over what distance?","Where in the wind column is the largest value, and where is the smallest?","Why should a steep pressure gradient produce fast winds?","Why are the winds light at the point with the lowest pressure of all?","What makes the air spiral in rather than travel straight to the centre?"]},{"title":"Planning a to what extent answer on Typhoon Haiyan","setup":"A 9-mark question, with 3 further marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar, reads: To what extent are the primary effects of a tropical storm more significant than its secondary effects? Use a named example in your answer. You have studied Typhoon Haiyan, which made landfall in the Philippines on 8 November 2013 with sustained winds estimated at about 315 km/h and a storm surge of more than 5 m at Tacloban; over 6,300 people were killed, more than 28,000 were injured, more than a million homes were damaged or destroyed and around 4 million people were displaced. Plan the answer before writing it.","steps":["Decode the command phrase first: to what extent means the two sets of effects have to be weighed against each other and a judgement reached, so an answer in two separate lists cannot reach the top level however much detail it holds.","Define the distinction in one sentence the examiner can see: primary effects are caused directly by the wind, rain and surge themselves, while secondary effects follow on from those primary effects.","Sort the Haiyan detail into those two columns. Primary: the surge of more than 5 m drowning much of Tacloban, winds of about 315 km/h damaging more than a million homes and destroying about 550,000 of them outright, over 6,300 dead and more than 28,000 injured on 8 November 2013. Secondary: no clean water or power for weeks, food shortages and looting, around 4 million people displaced, farmers and fishermen left with no income.","Build the case on one side: the primary effects caused almost every death, they happened in a matter of hours and nothing could stop them once the surge arrived, so by scale and immediacy they were the more significant.","Build the case on the other: the secondary effects reached far more people, lasted months and years rather than hours, and in a lower-middle-income country with limited resources they slowed recovery long after the wind had gone.","Commit to a judgement rather than a summary: significant in the first day means the primary effects, because they caused the loss of life; significant over the following years means the secondary effects, because of how many people they touched and for how long."],"cues":["What does the command phrase force the structure of the answer to do?","What single sentence tells the examiner you understand the distinction being tested?","Which of your Haiyan figures belongs in each of the two columns?","What is the strongest argument that the direct effects mattered most?","What is the strongest argument on the other side?","How can a conclusion answer to what extent without simply repeating both sides?"]},{"title":"Using a map to explain why a flood lasted for weeks","setup":"Figure D is a sketch map of the Somerset Levels in February 2014. It shows the River Parrett passing Langport, joined by the River Tone at Burrowbridge, then flowing through Bridgwater into the Bristol Channel. A large shaded area labelled land under water covers the flat ground either side of both rivers, with Muchelney and Moorland inside it marked as reached only by boat and the A361 marked road closed. Spot heights give the shaded ground as 3 m to 8 m above sea level and the surrounding higher ground, including the Polden Hills, as over 50 m. A pumping station symbol at Burrowbridge shows water being pumped from the fields into the river, and a note at the river mouth reads: high tides in the Bristol Channel hold back river water twice a day. The question is worth 6 marks: Using Figure D and your own understanding, suggest why the flooding on the Somerset Levels lasted for several weeks.","steps":["Use the spot heights as the first reason: the flooded ground lies at 3 m to 8 m while the surrounding hills reach over 50 m, so this is a shallow basin that water runs into and cannot run out of under gravity.","Use the note at the river mouth as the second: the Bristol Channel has one of the largest tidal ranges in the world, so twice every day the tide holds the Parrett back and the floodwater has nowhere to discharge to.","Add the physical cause the map cannot show: weeks of rain from one Atlantic depression after another had already saturated the clay and peat soils, so almost all further rainfall ran straight off the surface into an already full river system.","Add the human cause: the Parrett and the Tone had not been dredged for years, so the channels carried less water than they once did and spilled onto the moors sooner and for longer.","Read the pumping station symbol as the conclusion: the only way out for the water was to be lifted artificially into a river that was itself full, and pumping a flood of this size off flat land takes weeks, which is exactly what the question asked you to explain."],"cues":["What do the two sets of spot heights tell you about the shape of this land?","What does the note at the mouth of the Parrett stop the river doing?","What had the weeks of rain already done to the soil before the worst of the flooding?","What had happened to the capacity of the river channels themselves?","What does the pumping station symbol imply about how the water had to leave?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why does a belt of hot desert lie at about 30° north and south rather than on the equator?","a":"Air that rose and rained at the equator sinks at about 30°. Sinking air warms and its relative humidity falls, so cloud cannot form; the result is high pressure, clear skies and very little rain."},{"q":"Why do no tropical storms form within about 5° of the equator?","a":"The Coriolis effect is zero at the equator and too weak just either side of it, so the rising column of air cannot be made to spin around a centre and the storm never organises."},{"q":"What is the difference between a primary and a secondary effect?","a":"A primary effect is caused directly by the storm itself, such as houses destroyed by the wind or people drowned by the storm surge. A secondary effect follows on from a primary one, such as disease from contaminated water or lost income when boats and crops are gone."},{"q":"Which two winds meet at about 60° north, and what weather does that produce?","a":"Warm south-westerlies from the Ferrel cell meet cold polar easterlies from the Polar cell along the polar front. The warm air is forced to rise, creating low pressure, cloud and the wet, changeable weather the UK is known for."},{"q":"Give one piece of evidence that the weather in the UK is becoming more extreme, and one reason to be cautious about it.","a":"Evidence: the UK exceeded 40 °C for the first time on 19 July 2022, reaching 40.3 °C at Coningsby. Caution: single records are weather rather than climate, and severe events such as the 1976 drought show that extremes also happened before recent warming."}]},"room":{"facts":["Tropical storms need sea surface temperatures of at least 27 °C, warm to a depth of about 60 m, and form between roughly 5° and 30° north and south of the equator, never on the equator itself.","On the Saffir-Simpson scale a system becomes a category 1 storm at sustained winds of 119 km/h; category 5 begins at 252 km/h.","Typhoon Haiyan struck the Philippines on 8 November 2013 with sustained winds of about 315 km/h and a storm surge of over 5 m at Tacloban; more than 6,300 people were killed and over a million homes were damaged or destroyed.","Somerset Levels, winter 2013 to 2014: about 600 homes flooded and around 6,900 hectares of farmland stayed under water for weeks, in the wettest winter in the England and Wales rainfall series that began in 1766.","The highest temperature recorded in the UK is 40.3 °C, at Coningsby in Lincolnshire on 19 July 2022, the first time the UK exceeded 40 °C.","Air rises at the equator, giving low pressure and heavy rain, and sinks at about 30° north and south, giving high pressure and the world's hot deserts; the trade winds blow back towards the equator from the north-east in the northern hemisphere."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure A, give the name of the pressure belt found at the equator. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for: low pressure (accept equatorial low, or low-pressure belt). AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"Figure A draws an upward arrow at 0° with the label air rises, low pressure, heavy rainfall. Air that moves upwards leaves fewer air molecules pressing down on the surface, so surface pressure falls. The answer is therefore low pressure, often called the equatorial low. A give command needs nothing more than the name; no explanation is required and none is credited.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Everything you need is written beside the upward arrow at the bottom left of the figure.","Decide what happens to the weight of air pressing on the surface when air moves away upwards.","Two or three words are a complete answer to a one-mark give question."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Global atmospheric circulation helps to determine patterns of weather and climate"]},{"q":"Using Figure A, describe the surface winds shown between 30° north and the equator. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic description of the movement, eg the winds blow from 30° north towards the equator. Second mark for developing it with detail from the figure, eg they arrive from the north-east and are called the trade winds. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Figure A shows a surface arrow running from 30° north back towards the equator, so the winds blow out of the belt of sinking high-pressure air and into the belt of rising low pressure at 0° (1). The arrow is labelled north-east trade winds, so they do not travel due south but arrive from the north-east, because the Coriolis effect deflects them to the right in the northern hemisphere (2). The command word is describe, so no reasons are needed, but using the label from the figure is what turns a basic statement into the developed point that earns the second mark.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The arrow you want is the one drawn along the surface, not the ones drawn in the loop above it.","A description has two parts here: where the wind goes, and the direction it comes from.","Use the wording printed on the arrow to develop your first statement into a second mark."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Global atmospheric circulation helps to determine patterns of weather and climate"]},{"q":"State one condition needed for a tropical storm to form. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for any single precise condition, eg sea surface temperature of at least 27 °C; warm water to a depth of about 60 m; converging surface winds; low wind shear; a latitude of about 5° to 30°. No credit for warm weather or hot temperatures without qualification. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"Only one condition is required, and it has to be precise enough to be measured: warm sea on its own is too vague to score, whereas sea surface temperatures of at least 27 °C is exactly what the mark scheme wants. Other single conditions that score are ocean water warm to a depth of about 60 m, so the storm does not churn up cold water and starve itself; converging surface winds where the trade winds of the two hemispheres meet; low wind shear, so winds that change little with height do not tear the growing column apart; and a latitude between about 5° and 30°, where the Coriolis effect is strong enough to make the column spin. Writing more than one condition wastes time, because credit is capped at one.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the storm actually uses as fuel, and where it gets it from.","The condition has to be something that could be measured, not an adjective such as hot.","One precise condition on one line is the whole answer."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions"]},{"q":"Explain one reason why tropical storms lose strength when they move over land. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for stating the reason, eg the storm is cut off from the warm ocean, or friction with the land surface slows it. Second mark for developing the mechanism, eg without evaporation from water above 27 °C there is no latent heat to drive the rising air. AO2 = 2 marks.","sol":"A tropical storm runs on energy taken from warm ocean water, so once it crosses a coastline that supply is cut off (1). Without rapid evaporation from water above about 27 °C there is no moist air rising to condense, and no latent heat released to keep the column of air rising, so the low pressure at the centre weakens and the winds drop (2). The alternative reason, worth the same two marks, is friction: the land surface is far rougher than the sea, so it slows the surface winds and disrupts the inflowing spiral. What scores nothing is a vague claim such as mountains stop them, because it names no mechanism.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the storm is taking from the ocean that the land cannot give it.","A two-mark explain question wants a statement and then the process that makes it work.","Write one reason and then carry it one step further, to what happens to the winds."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions"]},{"q":"Using Figure C, calculate the difference between the highest wind speed in the table and the wind speed recorded 200 km from the centre of the storm. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer, 160 km/h. One mark for an indication of the calculation, eg 250 minus 90. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Read down the wind speed column of Figure C and find the largest value: 250 km/h, in the row for 25 km from the centre. Then read the row for 200 km: 90 km/h. Subtract one from the other: 250 minus 90 equals 160 km/h. Show that subtraction, because one of the two marks is for the indication of the calculation rather than for the number itself. Two traps are worth noticing. The top row of the table is the eye, where the wind is only 20 km/h, so the fastest wind is not at the centre. And the units are km/h, so the answer is 160 km/h, not 160 hPa.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The fastest winds in a tropical storm are not found at its centre.","Find both values in the table before you do anything with them.","Write the subtraction out, because the working carries one of the two marks."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions"]},{"q":"Give two types of weather hazard experienced in the UK. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for any two of: heavy rainfall and flooding; strong winds or gales; snow and ice; extreme cold; heatwaves; drought; thunderstorms and lightning; hail; fog. No credit for a third answer. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"Any two named hazards score, one mark each, so the safest pair are the two the UK experiences most often: heavy rainfall leading to flooding (1) and strong winds or gales (2). Other acceptable answers are snow and ice, extreme cold, heatwaves, drought, thunderstorms and lightning, hail, and fog. The command word is give, so no explanation is needed and no extra credit is available for adding one; equally, a vague answer such as bad weather or storms without qualification will not score, because it names no particular hazard.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think across the whole year, not only the winter.","Each answer must name a particular kind of weather rather than a season or a mood.","Two short named hazards, one per mark, is the whole answer."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — The UK is affected by a number of weather hazards"]},{"q":"Using Figure B, suggest how the weather at Port Esperanza will change during the next 24 hours. [4 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks. Level 2 (Clear, 3 to 4): accurate interpretation of the sequence of weather conditions, with clear and effective use of the image. Level 1 (Basic, 1 to 2): some interpretation with limited and piecemeal use of the image. Credit any realistic sequence of cloud, rainfall, wind, pressure and surge. Credit use of the figure's scale: the centre is 250 km away and moving at about 20 km/h, so it arrives in about 12 to 13 hours, the outer bands well before that, and the rear of the 500 km spiral is still over the town when the 24 hours end. Accept a wind from the north before the eye and from the south after it.","sol":"The centre of the storm is 250 km east of the town and the arrow shows it moving west at about 20 km per hour, straight towards the coast, so it will take roughly 12 to 13 hours to arrive, about half way through the forecast. The outer bands come first: the image shows the leading edge of the spiral already at the coast, so the next few hours bring thickening cloud, a wind from the north that steadily freshens as air spirals anticlockwise in towards the low-pressure centre, and slowly falling pressure. As the inner spiral bands reach the coast the cloud becomes tall cumulonimbus, bringing torrential downpours, thunder and lightning and damaging gusts, with pressure now dropping fast. The thick unbroken ring around the hole is the eyewall, which reaches the town shortly before the eye does: it will bring the most destructive winds and heaviest rain of all, and because the town is a port the very low central pressure and the winds driving water ahead of the storm will push up a storm surge that floods the harbour. When the clear hole passes over there will be a short calm, almost cloudless interval with light winds, before the far side of the eyewall returns with winds of similar strength but now blowing from the south, the opposite direction. Rain bands follow it: the spiral is 500 km across, so its rear edge is still over the town as the 24 hours end.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A forecast is a sequence, so work out how far away the storm is and how fast it is coming before you describe any weather.","Divide the 250 km by the 20 km per hour to time the centre, then take the parts of the image in the order they will reach the town, from the outside of the spiral inwards.","Start from what the image shows over the town now, and for each part of the storm say what happens to cloud, rain, wind and pressure, remembering the town is on the coast."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions"]},{"q":"Using Figure A and your own understanding, explain how global atmospheric circulation helps to determine where tropical storms form. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 5 to 6): a logical chain of reasoning linking the rising air and converging trade winds of the Hadley cells to the shaded latitudes on the figure, using evidence from the resource. Level 2 (Clear, 3 to 4): accurate knowledge of the circulation and clear understanding, without the full chain. Max Level 1 for a description of the figure alone.","sol":"Figure A shows the trade winds of the Hadley cell blowing back along the surface towards the equator. In the southern hemisphere the same thing happens in reverse, so the two sets of trade winds converge in the tropics. Where they meet, air is forced upwards, which is why the figure labels the equator as a belt of rising air and low pressure. That rising motion is the first requirement of a tropical storm, because it lifts warm, moist air off an ocean surface that in these latitudes is above 27 °C; as the air rises it cools, condenses and releases latent heat, which drives it upwards faster and deepens the low pressure below. That is why the figure shades the band from about 5° to 30° north as the zone where tropical storms form. It leaves the strip between 0° and 5° unshaded for a second reason that the circulation alone cannot supply: the Coriolis effect, which is zero at the equator, is too weak there to make the rising column spin around a centre, so no organised storm can develop. Beyond about 30°, the sinking air of the high-pressure belt suppresses rising air and sea surface temperatures usually fall below the threshold, so new storms rarely form there, although storms that formed nearer the equator often track polewards into higher latitudes before weakening. The circulation therefore sets both the inner and the outer edge of the storm belt.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The figure only draws one hemisphere, but the storm belt depends on what the other hemisphere is doing as well.","Work outwards from the equator: explain why the very lowest latitudes are excluded, then why the band beyond them is included, then why it stops.","Every stage should link a feature of the circulation to a condition a storm needs."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Global atmospheric circulation helps to determine patterns of weather and climate","3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions"]},{"q":"Using a named example, explain the immediate and long-term responses to a tropical storm. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 5 to 6): specific, accurate responses from a named storm, with clear understanding of why each response was needed and what it achieved. Level 2 (Clear, 3 to 4): accurate responses named but the link to need or effect is undeveloped. Max Level 1 for generic responses with no named example.","sol":"Typhoon Haiyan struck the Philippines on 8 November 2013. The immediate responses began before landfall: the government tracked the storm and evacuated hundreds of thousands of people from the coastal provinces in its path, which is a major reason why the death toll was not higher still than the 6,300 recorded. In the days afterwards a state of national calamity was declared, search and rescue teams worked through the wreckage of Tacloban, field hospitals were set up because the local hospitals were destroyed, and international aid arrived by air and by sea, including foreign military aircraft and ships, because the airport terminal and the roads had been wrecked and no aid could travel overland. These responses target survival: water, food, shelter and medical care within days, before disease spreads through contaminated water. The long-term responses were about reducing the next disaster rather than surviving this one. Homes were rebuilt away from the most exposed shoreline and to a stronger standard, mangroves were replanted because they absorb the energy of a storm surge before it reaches settlements, boats and fishing gear were replaced so families could earn again, and warnings were redesigned to give expected storm surge heights and not wind speed alone, since it was the surge that killed most people. The distinction that earns marks is one of purpose as well as timing: immediate responses save lives, long-term responses rebuild livelihoods and reduce future risk.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Fix the example first, with its country and date, because a generic answer is capped.","Split the answer by purpose as well as by time: what keeps people alive this week, and what reduces the damage next time.","For each response, say why it was needed in that particular place."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms have significant effects on people and the environment"]},{"q":"Using Figure D, suggest why the flooding shown lasted for several weeks. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Level 2 (Clear, 3 to 4): clear understanding of drainage, supported by full interpretation of the evidence on the map, eg the spot heights and the tidal note. Level 1 (Basic, 1 to 2): limited understanding with basic use of the map. Credit either one fully developed reason or two less developed ones. No credit for reasons that cannot be seen or inferred from the resource.","sol":"The first reason is the shape of the land. Figure D gives spot heights of 3 m to 8 m for the shaded ground and over 50 m for the surrounding higher ground, including the Polden Hills, so the flooded area is a shallow, almost flat basin. Water running off the higher ground collects in it, and with so little gradient there is almost no force moving it towards the sea, so it simply sits there. The second reason is the tide. The note at the mouth of the Parrett records that high tides in the Bristol Channel hold back river water twice a day, so for a large part of every day the river cannot discharge into the sea at all and its level rises, which in turn prevents water draining off the moors into the river. The pumping station symbol at Burrowbridge confirms the consequence: the only way out for the floodwater was to be lifted artificially into a river that was itself already full, and pumping this volume of water off flat land takes weeks rather than days.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["More than one feature of the map is doing the work: one is about the shape of the land, another about the sea.","Compare the two sets of spot heights before you write anything about drainage.","Use the note printed at the river mouth, and say what it stops the river doing."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Extreme weather events in the UK have impacts on human activity"]},{"q":"Explain how monitoring, prediction, protection and planning can reduce the effects of tropical storms. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 5 to 6): each strategy explained through to the effect it reduces, with a logical chain of reasoning. Level 2 (Clear, 3 to 4): accurate knowledge of the strategies with some understanding of how they work. Max Level 1 for a list of strategies with no explanation.","sol":"Monitoring means watching a storm as it develops. Geostationary satellites photograph the whole ocean basin continuously and aircraft fly into the storm to measure central pressure and wind speed directly, so forecasters know how strong a system is and where it is before it is anywhere near land. Prediction turns that data into a forecast track using computer models, which is what allows a warning to be issued several days ahead; that lead time is the thing that actually saves lives, because evacuating a coastal city takes days, not hours. Protection is physical and reduces damage rather than exposure: buildings are reinforced and fitted with storm shutters, houses in surge zones are raised on stilts, sea walls and levees are built, and mangroves are replanted because their roots absorb much of the energy of a storm surge before it reaches a settlement. Planning is organisational and makes the other three work: evacuation routes and shelters are identified in advance, emergency supplies of water, food and medicine are stockpiled, drills are held and people are taught what a surge warning means, so that when the prediction arrives the population knows what to do with it. The four are a sequence rather than a list: monitoring feeds prediction, prediction triggers the plan, and protection limits what the storm can destroy in the buildings and coastline it reaches.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Each of the four does a different job, so decide which reduce deaths and which reduce damage.","Take them in the order they actually happen when a real storm approaches.","Every strategy needs the effect it reduces written after it, not just a name."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms have significant effects on people and the environment"]},{"q":"Explain how climate change might affect the distribution, frequency and intensity of tropical storms. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 5 to 6): each of the three aspects explained through the mechanism of warmer oceans and a warmer atmosphere, with recognition that confidence differs between them. Level 2 (Clear, 3 to 4): accurate points about warming and storms with partial explanation. Credit recognition that projections are uncertain.","sol":"All three effects follow from the same mechanism: tropical storms run on heat taken from ocean water above about 27 °C, and the oceans are warming. Distribution is expected to change because the area of sea reaching that threshold is growing, extending further from the equator, so storms may form and survive at higher latitudes than at present and may reach coasts that have rarely had to plan for them. Intensity is the change scientists are most confident about: a warmer sea evaporates more water, more condensation releases more latent heat, and that extra energy makes the pressure at the centre fall further and the winds blow harder, so a larger proportion of storms are expected to reach the top of the Saffir-Simpson scale even if the total number does not rise. Warmer air also holds more water vapour, so storms are expected to carry heavier rainfall, and rising sea level makes any given storm surge reach further inland, which means the damage from a storm of unchanged strength still increases. Frequency is the least certain of the three: some projections suggest slightly fewer storms overall, because increased wind shear in some basins would make it harder for a column to organise, even while the strongest storms become more common. A top answer says plainly that intensity and rainfall are the confident predictions and total frequency is not.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["One mechanism, warmer oceans, drives all three of the changes the question names.","Deal with the three words in the question one at a time so nothing is missed.","The three are not equally certain, and saying which is which is worth credit."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions"]},{"q":"To what extent are the primary effects of a tropical storm more significant than its secondary effects? Use a named example in your answer. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 7 to 9): detailed knowledge of both sets of effects from a named storm, thorough understanding of how one leads to the other, and a reasoned evaluation of their relative significance. Credit only effects, not causes or responses. Plus 3 SPaG marks.","sol":"Primary effects are caused directly by the storm itself; secondary effects follow on from them. Typhoon Haiyan made landfall in the Philippines on 8 November 2013 with sustained winds estimated at about 315 km/h, which makes it one of the strongest storms ever recorded to reach land. The primary effects were devastating. A storm surge of more than five metres swept through Tacloban on the island of Leyte, a city built barely above sea level around a bay that funnelled the water inland. The surge and the wind together killed over 6,300 people and injured more than 28,000, and more than a million homes were damaged or destroyed. These effects happened within a few hours, they caused almost all of the deaths, and nothing could be done to stop them once the surge arrived, so measured by loss of life and by the impossibility of avoiding them, the primary effects were clearly the more significant. The secondary effects, however, reached far more people and lasted far longer. Around four million people were displaced. Water pipes and power lines were destroyed, so survivors were without clean water or electricity for weeks, which raised the risk of disease; food ran short and there was looting in Tacloban because roads and the airport were unusable, itself a consequence of the primary damage. Rice and coconut crops were flattened and fishing boats smashed, so hundreds of thousands of families lost their livelihood for years rather than days, and an oil barge driven aground at Estancia leaked oil that contaminated mangroves and fishing grounds. The interrelationship matters here: the secondary effects were so severe partly because the Philippines is a lower-middle-income country, where many homes were lightly built and rebuilding had to come out of limited national resources. Overall, the primary effects were the more significant in the first day, because they caused the deaths that everything else followed from. Over the months and years afterwards the secondary effects were the more significant, because they touched around four million displaced people rather than the thousands directly killed, and because lost water, power and income shaped how long recovery took. The honest judgement is therefore a conditional one: primary effects dominate in scale and immediacy, secondary effects in reach and duration.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The command phrase is asking you to weigh, so decide before you write which measure of significance you are going to weigh them by.","Sort your case-study detail into two columns first, and check that each secondary effect really does follow from a primary one.","Build both sides, then let the conclusion say under what conditions each side wins."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms have significant effects on people and the environment"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of the management strategies used to reduce the risk from an extreme weather event you have studied in the UK. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 7 to 9): detailed knowledge of the named UK event and the strategies used, thorough understanding of how each is meant to work, and a balanced appreciation of advantages and disadvantages leading to a substantiated judgement. No credit for simply describing the event. Plus 3 SPaG marks.","sol":"The Somerset Levels flooded through the winter of 2013 to 2014, when one Atlantic depression after another crossed southern England in what became the wettest winter in the England and Wales rainfall series that began in 1766. About 600 homes were flooded, around 6,900 hectares of farmland stayed under water for weeks, villages such as Muchelney and Moorland could be reached only by boat, and roads including the A361 were closed. Management came in two phases and they were effective in very different ways. The immediate response was largely successful at limiting harm: residents were evacuated, boats ferried people in and out of cut-off villages, livestock were moved to higher ground and the Environment Agency brought in extra high-volume pumps, some from the Netherlands, to lift water off the moors. No one died, which is the strongest evidence that the emergency response worked. Its weakness is that pumping treats the symptom: water was being lifted into rivers that were themselves full and tide-locked twice a day by the Bristol Channel, so it drained the land slowly and at high cost. The long-term strategies are more debatable. Dredging about eight kilometres of the Parrett and the Tone restored channel capacity that had been lost over years without maintenance, and it is popular locally because it visibly does something; but hydrologists point out that even a dredged channel cannot hold the runoff of an entire catchment in a record-breaking winter, and that moving water downstream faster can worsen flooding at Bridgwater. Raising roads and river banks and adding pumping capacity protect particular places rather than the system as a whole. The creation of the Somerset Rivers Authority in 2015 was arguably the most important change, because it gave one body responsibility for a landscape previously managed piecemeal, and a tidal barrier planned at Bridgwater would tackle the real constraint, the tide that holds the rivers shut. Overall, the management has been effective at reducing the impacts of a repeat event but not at removing the risk, because the underlying causes, flat land at three to eight metres above sea level, a huge tidal range and a wetter winter climate, cannot be engineered away. The most defensible judgement is that these strategies buy time and reduce damage, while long-term security would need upstream land management and, eventually, decisions about which land is allowed to flood.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Evaluate means both sides, so for every strategy you name you owe the marker a limitation.","Separate what was done during the flood from what was done in the years afterwards, and judge them by different standards.","Anchor the judgement in whether the strategies deal with the causes or only the symptoms."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Extreme weather events in the UK have impacts on human activity"]},{"q":"Discuss the evidence that weather in the UK is becoming more extreme. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 7 to 9): a balanced discussion with well-developed ideas, specific named UK events and figures, and thorough understanding of what does and does not count as evidence of a trend. Level 1 rewards random statements about weather with little link to the evidence. Plus 3 SPaG marks.","sol":"The case that UK weather is becoming more extreme rests on a run of records in a short period. On 19 July 2022 the temperature reached 40.3 °C at Coningsby in Lincolnshire, the first time the UK had exceeded 40 °C in any record, and 2022 went on to be the warmest year in a national series that begins in 1884. At the wet end, the winter of 2013 to 2014 was the wettest winter in the England and Wales rainfall series that began in 1766, and it was that rainfall which put the Somerset Levels under water for weeks. In December 2015 Storm Desmond dropped 341.4 mm of rain at Honister Pass in Cumbria in twenty-four hours, a UK record, and flooded thousands of homes in Carlisle and the Eden valley. Records of this kind falling in clusters, rather than singly across a century, is what makes the pattern persuasive, and it is consistent with the physics: a warmer atmosphere holds more water vapour, so when rain falls it can fall harder, and a warmer baseline makes extreme heat easier to reach. There is a serious case on the other side. A record is a single day of weather, not a climate; the UK has always had violent extremes, and the 1976 drought and heatwave, the great storm of October 1987, the winter of 1962 to 1963 and the 1953 east coast flood all predate recent warming. Some apparent increases reflect better measurement, with more stations and radar now recording events that would once have gone unnoticed, and more people and property in flood-prone places, which makes the same rainfall far more damaging without the rainfall itself having changed. Familiarity can mislead in the same way: the Met Office and Met Éireann began naming storms in 2015 to make severe weather warnings easier to follow and to raise public awareness, not because windstorms had become more frequent, so named storms in the news are not in themselves evidence that there are more of them. Much UK year-to-year variability is also driven by where the jet stream sits, which shifts naturally. Overall, the evidence is stronger for some extremes than others. Record heat is now clearly outside the historical range, and the clustering of rainfall records is difficult to explain by chance alone; evidence for more frequent severe windstorms is weaker. The defensible conclusion is that UK weather is becoming more extreme at the hot and wet ends, while the impacts of extreme weather have grown faster than the weather itself.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Discuss means the case for and the case against, both developed, before any judgement.","Group your evidence by type of extreme rather than by date, so the discussion has a structure.","Ask of each record whether it shows the weather changing, or only the measuring and the exposure changing."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — The UK is affected by a number of weather hazards","3.1.1.3 — Extreme weather events in the UK have impacts on human activity"]},{"q":"To what extent do the effects of a tropical storm depend more on a country's level of development than on the strength of the storm? [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 7 to 9): detailed knowledge of storm effects in contrasting countries, thorough understanding of the interrelationship between hazard, vulnerability and capacity to respond, and a reasoned judgement that distinguishes deaths from economic cost. Plus 3 SPaG marks.","sol":"Two storms make the comparison. Typhoon Haiyan hit the Philippines, a lower-middle-income country, on 8 November 2013 with sustained winds of about 315 km/h; over 6,300 people died, about 1.1 million homes were damaged, some 550,000 of them destroyed outright, and around four million people were displaced. Hurricane Katrina struck the Gulf coast of the United States on 29 August 2005, weaker at landfall than Haiyan, yet it caused damage estimated at more than 100 billion US dollars, the costliest in the country's history, after levee failures flooded about eighty per cent of New Orleans; the death toll was over 1,800. The pattern in those numbers is the argument. Development shapes the type of effect. In the Philippines, housing in coastal barangays was built of light materials that a five-metre surge could remove entirely, medical care and clean water were quickly beyond reach, and many families depended directly on fishing and farming, so a single day destroyed years of income. Deaths were therefore very high relative to the economic loss. In the United States, buildings and infrastructure were worth far more, so the financial cost was enormous, while warning, evacuation and rescue capacity kept the death toll far lower than the exposure would suggest. Development also shapes recovery: the Philippines depended heavily on international aid and rebuilding took years, whereas the United States could fund its own reconstruction. But strength cannot be dismissed. Haiyan was near the theoretical maximum for a tropical cyclone, and no level of development protects lightly defended low ground from a surge of that size; equally, Katrina shows that a wealthy country can still suffer catastrophic effects when its protection fails and its evacuation leaves behind the poorest, who had no cars. Within a country, it is the poorest districts that suffer most, which suggests wealth matters at the household scale as much as the national one. The judgement is therefore a divided one. Strength sets the ceiling on what a storm can do; development largely decides where the damage falls between lives and money, and how quickly a place recovers. Deaths depend more on development, economic cost depends on wealth being there to lose, and the physical scale of destruction depends on the storm.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["This question needs two contrasting places, so choose them before planning the paragraphs.","Effects are not one quantity: separate deaths, economic cost and speed of recovery, because development affects them differently.","Leave room to argue the other way, because storm strength has to set some limit on what preparation can achieve."],"codes":["3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms have significant effects on people and the environment"]}],"exit":[{"q":"At about which latitudes does air sink to create the belt of high pressure and the world's hot deserts?","a":"About 30° north and 30° south."},{"q":"What is the minimum sea surface temperature a tropical storm needs?","a":"27 °C."},{"q":"Which country did Typhoon Haiyan strike, and in which month and year?","a":"The Philippines, in November 2013."},{"q":"Name the part of a tropical storm with the strongest winds and the heaviest rain.","a":"The eyewall."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Hadley cell","back":"The circulation loop between the equator and about 30° north or south. Air rises at the equator, moves polewards at altitude, sinks at 30° and returns along the surface as the trade winds.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Global atmospheric circulation helps to determine patterns of weather and climate","open":false},{"front":"Ferrel cell","back":"The middle loop, between about 30° and 60°. Surface air moves polewards as south-westerly winds in the northern hemisphere and rises where it meets cold polar air at the polar front.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Global atmospheric circulation helps to determine patterns of weather and climate","open":false},{"front":"Polar cell","back":"The loop between about 60° and the pole. Cold dense air sinks at the pole, giving high pressure, and flows towards 60° along the surface as the polar easterlies.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Global atmospheric circulation helps to determine patterns of weather and climate","open":false},{"front":"Trade winds","back":"The surface winds of the Hadley cell, blowing from the subtropical high pressure at 30° towards the equatorial low. Deflected by the Coriolis effect, so north-easterly in the northern hemisphere and south-easterly in the southern.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Global atmospheric circulation helps to determine patterns of weather and climate","open":false},{"front":"Coriolis effect","back":"The deflection of moving air caused by the rotation of the Earth: to the right in the northern hemisphere, to the left in the southern. It is zero at the equator, which is why tropical storms cannot form there.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions","open":false},{"front":"The conditions a tropical storm needs to form","back":"Sea surface temperature of at least 27 °C, warm to about 60 m depth; converging surface winds; low wind shear; and a latitude of roughly 5° to 30°, where the Coriolis effect can spin the rising column.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions","open":false},{"front":"The eye of a tropical storm","back":"The calm centre, up to about 50 km across, where air is descending. It has the lowest air pressure, light winds, clear skies and the highest temperatures in the storm.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions","open":false},{"front":"The eyewall","back":"The ring of towering cumulonimbus cloud around the eye, where air spirals upwards fastest. It carries the storm's strongest winds and its heaviest rainfall.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions","open":false},{"front":"Saffir-Simpson scale","back":"The scale rating tropical storms 1 to 5 by sustained wind speed. Category 1 begins at 119 km/h and category 5 at 252 km/h. Damage rises far faster than wind speed as the category climbs.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms (hurricanes, cyclones, typhoons) develop as a result of particular physical conditions","open":false},{"front":"Primary and secondary effects of a tropical storm","back":"Primary effects are caused directly by the wind, rain and storm surge, such as homes destroyed or people drowned. Secondary effects follow on from those, such as disease from contaminated water or lost income.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms have significant effects on people and the environment","open":false},{"front":"Typhoon Haiyan, the Philippines, 8 November 2013","back":"Sustained winds of about 315 km/h and a storm surge of more than 5 m at Tacloban. Over 6,300 killed, more than 28,000 injured, over a million homes damaged or destroyed, around 4 million displaced.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms have significant effects on people and the environment","open":false},{"front":"Monitoring, prediction, protection and planning","back":"Monitoring: satellites and aircraft watch the storm. Prediction: models forecast its track days ahead. Protection: reinforced buildings, stilts, sea walls, mangroves. Planning: evacuation routes, shelters, supplies and drills.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Tropical storms have significant effects on people and the environment","open":false},{"front":"Types of weather hazard experienced in the UK","back":"Heavy rainfall and flooding; strong winds and gales; snow and ice; extreme cold; heatwaves and drought; thunderstorms, lightning and hail; fog.","code":"3.1.1.3 — The UK is affected by a number of weather hazards","open":true},{"front":"The Somerset Levels floods, winter 2013 to 2014","back":"The wettest winter in the England and Wales rainfall series begun in 1766. About 600 homes flooded, around 6,900 hectares of farmland under water for weeks, Muchelney and Moorland reachable only by boat.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Extreme weather events in the UK have impacts on human activity","open":false},{"front":"Evidence that UK weather is becoming more extreme","back":"40.3 °C at Coningsby on 19 July 2022, the first UK reading above 40 °C; 2022 the warmest UK year on record; winter 2013 to 2014 the wettest in the England and Wales series since 1766; 341.4 mm of rain in 24 hours at Honister Pass in December 2015.","code":"3.1.1.3 — Extreme weather events in the UK have impacts on human activity","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure A — Global atmospheric circulation: a cross-section from the equator to the North Pole (diagram written for this room; the latitudes and winds are real) The diagram is a slice through the atmosphere. Along the bottom is the Earth's surface, marked 0° (the equator), 30° N, 60° N and 90° N (the North Pole). Three loops of moving air are drawn above it. Loop 1, the Hadley cell, runs between 0° and 30° N. An upward arrow at the equator is labelled: air rises, low pressure, heavy rainfall. At the top of the loop the arrow moves north. At 30° N a downward arrow is labelled: air sinks, high pressure, clear dry skies. Along the surface an arrow points back from 30° N towards the equator, labelled: north-east trade winds. Loop 2, the Ferrel cell, runs between 30° N and 60° N. Along the surface an arrow points from 30° N towards 60° N, labelled: south-westerly winds. At 60° N an upward arrow is labelled: air rises, low pressure, unsettled wet weather. Loop 3, the Polar cell, runs between 60° N and 90° N. A downward arrow at the pole is labelled: air sinks, high pressure, cold and dry. Along the surface an arrow points from 90° N towards 60° N, labelled: polar easterlies. A band shaded along the ocean surface between about 5° N and 30° N is labelled: where tropical storms form. The strip between 0° and 5° is left unshaded and labelled: no storms form here.","Figure B — Satellite image of a tropical storm approaching a coast (description written for this room; the storm and the town are illustrative) A satellite image taken at 06:00 on 12 September. In the centre-right of the image is a spiral of white cloud about 500 km across, turning anticlockwise. At its centre is a small clear circular hole about 40 km across with no cloud in it. Around that hole is a thick unbroken ring of the brightest, tallest cloud in the image. Beyond the ring, bands of cloud spiral outwards, with gaps of clear sky between them. To the left of the storm a coastline runs north to south. The small port town of Port Esperanza is marked on it, 250 km west of the centre of the storm. A white arrow drawn from the storm centre towards the coast is labelled: storm moving west at about 20 km per hour. The leading edge of the outermost cloud band has just reached the coast at the town; over Port Esperanza itself the sky in the image is still clear apart from a few small clouds, and wide gaps of clear sky separate the outer bands from the solid cloud nearer the centre.","Figure C — A transect across a tropical storm: wind speed and air pressure (table written for this room; figures illustrative but typical of a strong storm)\nDistance from the centre (km) | Average wind speed (km/h) | Air pressure (hPa)\n0, the eye | 20 | 900\n25 | 250 | 915\n50 | 210 | 935\n100 | 150 | 960\n200 | 90 | 985\n300 | 45 | 1000\n400 | 25 | 1010","Figure D — Sketch map of the Somerset Levels, February 2014 (map drawn for this room; the places, rivers and tides are real, the shaded flood area is a simplified outline) The map covers part of central Somerset, in south-west England. The Bristol Channel lies along the top, north-west, edge. The River Parrett enters the map from the south-east, passes the small town of Langport, is joined by the River Tone at the village of Burrowbridge, then flows north-west through the town of Bridgwater and out into the Bristol Channel. Taunton is marked in the south-west, on the River Tone. A large grey shaded area, labelled: land under water, covers the low, flat ground on either side of the Parrett and the Tone between Langport and Bridgwater. The villages of Muchelney and Moorland are drawn inside the shaded area, each with a symbol meaning: reached only by boat. Roads crossing the shaded area, including the A361 between Taunton and Glastonbury, carry road closed symbols. Spot heights give the shaded ground as between 3 m and 8 m above sea level; the higher ground drawn around its edge, including the Polden Hills to the north, is given as over 50 m. A pumping station symbol beside the Parrett at Burrowbridge has an arrow showing water being pumped from the fields into the river. At the mouth of the Parrett a note reads: high tides in the Bristol Channel hold back river water twice a day."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7 to 9) on the 9-mark question: To what extent are the primary effects of a tropical storm more significant than its secondary effects?","items":["Point: the secondary effects of Typhoon Haiyan were more significant than the primary effects when significance is measured by how many people were affected and for how long.","Evidence: Haiyan made landfall in the Philippines on 8 November 2013 with sustained winds of about 315 km/h; the primary effects killed over 6,300 people and destroyed or damaged more than a million homes in a few hours, but the secondary effects displaced around four million people and left survivors without clean water or electricity for weeks.","Developed explanation: those secondary effects followed directly from the primary ones, because the surge that flattened Tacloban also broke the water mains and power lines and blocked the roads and airport, which in turn delayed the aid that would have shortened the shortages; with rice and coconut crops flattened and fishing boats smashed, hundreds of thousands of families lost their income for years rather than days, and in a lower-middle-income country that loss could not be absorbed quickly.","Link to the question: the question asks about relative significance, and on every measure except immediate loss of life the secondary effects reached further, lasted longer and cost more.","Mini-judgement: the primary effects were therefore the more significant in the first twenty-four hours, because they caused the deaths that everything else followed from, but over the years afterwards the secondary effects were the more significant."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1 to 3) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["Point: tropical storms have lots of bad effects on people.","Evidence: in the Philippines a big typhoon killed thousands of people and wrecked loads of houses, and afterwards people had no water.","Explanation: this was bad for the people who lived there because they had lost everything and needed help from other countries.","Judgement: so the primary effects were worse than the secondary effects.","Why this sits at Level 1: it meets the descriptor demonstrates limited knowledge of the primary and secondary effects of a hazard, because the storm is not named, there is no date and no figure, and thousands and loads are not data.","What it misses: it fails the Level 3 descriptor demonstrates application of knowledge and understanding in a coherent and reasoned way in evaluating the relative significance, because it never says what significance is being measured by and the final sentence asserts a verdict without weighing anything.","What it misses: it also fails the AO2 strand shows thorough geographical understanding of the interrelationships, because it never shows how the secondary effects grew out of the primary ones."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Typhoon Haiyan, the Philippines, 8 November 2013 (the named tropical storm)","items":["Name: Typhoon Haiyan, known in the Philippines as Yolanda; a typhoon is the name given to a tropical storm in the western Pacific.","Date and place: made landfall at Guiuan in Eastern Samar early on 8 November 2013, then crossed the central Philippine islands, hitting Leyte and Samar hardest.","Strength: sustained winds estimated at about 315 km/h, category 5 equivalent, among the strongest tropical cyclones ever recorded at landfall.","Storm surge: more than 5 m at Tacloban, a coastal city on Leyte built around a bay that funnelled the water inland.","Primary effect, deaths: over 6,300 people killed and more than 28,000 injured, most of them drowned by the surge rather than killed by the wind.","Primary effect, housing: more than a million homes damaged or destroyed; the terminal building at Tacloban airport was wrecked.","Secondary effect, displacement: around 4 million people displaced from their homes.","Secondary effect, services: water pipes and power lines destroyed, leaving survivors without clean water or electricity for weeks and raising the risk of disease.","Secondary effect, order and supply: food and water shortages and looting in Tacloban, made worse because blocked roads and the damaged airport delayed aid.","Secondary effect, economy: rice and coconut crops flattened and fishing boats destroyed, so hundreds of thousands of families lost their livelihoods for years.","Secondary effect, environment: an oil barge driven aground at Estancia leaked oil that contaminated coastal water and mangroves.","Immediate response, before landfall: the government tracked the storm and evacuated hundreds of thousands of people from the provinces in its path.","Immediate response, after landfall: a state of national calamity declared, search and rescue in Tacloban, field hospitals set up, and international aid flown and shipped in, including foreign military aircraft and vessels.","Long-term response: homes rebuilt to a stronger standard and away from the most exposed shoreline, mangroves replanted to absorb surge energy, boats and fishing gear replaced, and warnings redesigned around storm surge height rather than wind speed alone.","Why the effects were so severe: the Philippines is a lower-middle-income country; many coastal homes were lightly built, many families depended directly on fishing and farming, and rebuilding had to be funded from limited national resources with heavy reliance on international aid."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the Somerset Levels floods, winter 2013 to 2014 (the recent UK extreme weather event)","items":["Place: the Somerset Levels and Moors, a large area of low, flat land in south-west England drained by the rivers Parrett and Tone, which reach the sea through Bridgwater into the Bristol Channel.","Dates: flooding began in late December 2013 and much of the land stayed under water into March 2014.","Physical cause, weather: a jet stream positioned across southern England steered one Atlantic depression after another over the region; it was the wettest winter in the England and Wales rainfall series that began in 1766.","Physical cause, land: the Levels lie only a few metres above sea level and are almost flat, so water drains off them very slowly.","Physical cause, soil: weeks of rain saturated the clay and peat soils, so further rainfall ran straight off the surface instead of soaking in.","Physical cause, tides: the Bristol Channel has one of the largest tidal ranges in the world, and high tides hold river water back twice a day, preventing the Parrett from discharging.","Human cause: the Parrett and the Tone had not been dredged for years, so the channels carried less water than they once had and spilled onto the moors sooner.","Social impact: about 600 homes flooded; the villages of Muchelney and Moorland were reachable only by boat for weeks; families were out of their houses for months.","Economic impact: around 6,900 hectares of farmland under water for weeks; livestock moved to higher ground; roads including the A361 closed, cutting journeys and trade; costs of pumping, repair and lost production ran into millions of pounds.","Environmental impact: floodwater became stagnant and contaminated with sewage, silt and oil, and had to be treated before it could safely be pumped back into the rivers; debris was left across fields and roads.","Immediate response: residents evacuated, boats used to reach cut-off villages, livestock moved, sandbags issued, and extra high-volume pumps brought in by the Environment Agency, some of them from the Netherlands.","Long-term response: about 8 km of the rivers Parrett and Tone dredged during 2014; road levels raised; river banks raised and strengthened; permanent pumping capacity increased.","Long-term response, organisation: a Somerset Levels and Moors flood action plan agreed in 2014 and a new Somerset Rivers Authority created in 2015 to coordinate the work; a tidal barrier at Bridgwater planned to keep high tides out of the river.","Evaluation point: dredging restores channel capacity locally but cannot hold the runoff of a whole catchment in a record winter, and moving water downstream faster can increase risk at Bridgwater; upstream land management and a tidal barrier address the causes more directly."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the processes, terms and figures of this room","items":["Global atmospheric circulation: three cells in each hemisphere, Hadley (0° to 30°), Ferrel (30° to 60°) and Polar (60° to 90°).","Pressure belts: low pressure at the equator and at about 60°, where air rises; high pressure at about 30° and at the poles, where air sinks.","Surface winds: trade winds from 30° to the equator (north-easterly in the northern hemisphere); westerlies from 30° to 60°; polar easterlies from the pole to 60°.","Coriolis effect: the deflection of moving air by the rotation of the Earth, to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern; zero at the equator.","Why deserts lie at 30°: air that rose and rained at the equator sinks there, warming and drying as it does, so cloud cannot form.","Names for the same hazard: hurricane in the North Atlantic and eastern Pacific, typhoon in the western Pacific, cyclone in the Indian Ocean and around Australia.","Formation conditions: sea surface temperature of at least 27 °C to a depth of about 60 m, converging surface winds, low wind shear, and a latitude of roughly 5° to 30°.","Sequence of formation: warm ocean water evaporates; moist air rises rapidly in a spiralling column; it cools and condenses, releasing latent heat that drives it higher; surface pressure falls; the pressure gradient steepens and the winds accelerate.","Structure: a calm eye of sinking air up to about 50 km across with the lowest pressure; an eyewall of towering cumulonimbus with the strongest winds and heaviest rain; spiral rain bands beyond it; storms may be several hundred kilometres across and last about a week.","Saffir-Simpson scale: category 1 begins at sustained winds of 119 km/h, category 2 at 154 km/h, category 3 at 178 km/h, category 4 at 209 km/h and category 5 at 252 km/h.","Storm tracks: storms drift westwards with the trade winds, then curve polewards, and weaken over land or cooler seas as the supply of warm moist air is lost and friction slows them.","Climate change and storms: warmer seas extend the area above 27 °C, so distribution may widen; higher sea surface temperatures and a moister atmosphere point to greater intensity and heavier rainfall; total frequency is the least certain of the three and may not rise.","Primary effect: an effect caused directly by the storm itself. Secondary effect: an effect that follows on from a primary effect.","Immediate response: action in the hours and days after the event, such as rescue, shelter, water and medical aid. Long-term response: action over months and years, such as rebuilding, replanting and improving warning systems.","Reducing risk: monitoring (satellites and aircraft), prediction (computer models producing a forecast track days ahead), protection (reinforced buildings, stilts, sea walls, mangroves) and planning (evacuation routes, shelters, supplies, drills).","UK weather hazards: heavy rainfall and flooding, strong winds and gales, snow and ice, extreme cold, heatwaves, drought, thunderstorms, lightning, hail and fog, most of them produced by depressions crossing the country from the Atlantic along the polar front.","UK extremes worth quoting: 40.3 °C at Coningsby, Lincolnshire, on 19 July 2022, the first UK temperature above 40 °C; 341.4 mm of rain in 24 hours at Honister Pass, Cumbria, during Storm Desmond in December 2015; 2022 the warmest year in the UK series that begins in 1884; the Met Office began naming storms in 2015.","Extremes before recent warming, for the other side of the argument: the 1976 drought and heatwave, the great storm of 16 October 1987, the winter of 1962 to 1963 and the east coast flood of 1953."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — To what extent are the primary effects of a tropical storm more significant than its secondary effects? Use a named example in your answer. (9 marks, plus 3 SPaG marks)","items":["Decoded command phrase: to what extent asks you to judge how far a claim holds, not whether it holds. The claim is that one set of effects outweighs the other, so the answer must weigh them against each other on a stated measure and reach a qualified verdict. Effects only: causes and responses earn nothing here.","Timing: about a minute a mark plus time for the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks, so roughly eleven to twelve minutes, of which one minute is planning and one is checking the writing.","Opening, two sentences: define primary effects as those caused directly by the wind, rain and storm surge, and secondary effects as those that follow on from them, then name Typhoon Haiyan, the Philippines, 8 November 2013, winds of about 315 km/h.","Paragraph 1, the primary effects of Haiyan: the storm surge of more than 5 m through Tacloban, over 6,300 deaths and more than 28,000 injured, more than a million homes damaged or destroyed, the airport terminal wrecked. Mini-judgement: on immediate loss of life these are the most significant effects of all.","Paragraph 2, the secondary effects of Haiyan: around 4 million people displaced, weeks without clean water or power and the disease risk that follows, food shortages and looting while blocked roads delayed aid, crops and fishing boats destroyed so livelihoods were lost for years, oil from a grounded barge at Estancia contaminating mangroves. Mini-judgement: these reached far more people and lasted far longer.","Paragraph 3, the interrelationship: show that the secondary effects grew out of the primary ones (the same surge that killed people also broke the water mains and blocked the aid routes), and that they were so severe partly because the Philippines is a lower-middle-income country with lightly built housing and limited resources for rebuilding.","Evaluation thread running through every paragraph: keep returning to the question what does significant mean here, so the answer is measured consistently against number of deaths, number of people affected, duration, and economic cost, rather than drifting into a list.","Conclusion must say: primary effects were more significant in the first day, because they caused almost all the deaths and could not be prevented once the surge arrived; secondary effects were more significant over the following months and years, because they touched around four million people and shaped how long recovery took. The verdict must be conditional and stated, not a restatement of both sides.","SPaG: three further marks ride on this answer, so spell the specialist terms correctly (storm surge, latent heat, primary, secondary, displacement, latitude), punctuate the long sentences properly, and use a wide range of geographical vocabulary."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:59:55.269007Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-judged against the fixed kit file: Figure B is now internally consistent (town and coast 250 km west of the centre on a westward track, outermost band just at the coast) and one arithmetic runs through the figure, question 6's key, sol and hints and worked example 1 - centre in about 12 to 13 hours, rear of the 500 km spiral still overhead at 24 hours, wind northerly before the eye and southerly after it, which is correct for anticlockwise inflow in the northern hemisphere; question 15 and worked example 3 now match NDRRMC (about 1.1 million homes damaged, some 550,000 destroyed); the storm belt reads 5 to 30 degrees in all eight places and question 7 now puts the outer limit at 30 degrees with poleward-tracking storms noted; question 14 states the 2015 Met Office and Met Eireann naming scheme accurately and on the sceptical side of the argument; worked example 0's insolation line and question 9's stem and key are fixed. Everything else re-checked and true - Haiyan 8 November 2013, Guiuan landfall, about 315 km/h (the JTWC one-minute figure standard in GCSE resources), over 5 m surge at Tacloban, 6,300 dead, 28,000 injured, 4 million displaced, the Estancia oil barge; Somerset Levels 600 homes, 6,900 hectares, 8 km dredged, Somerset Rivers Authority 2015, wettest winter in the England and Wales series from 1766; Coningsby 40.3 C on 19 July 2022 and 2022 the warmest year since 1884; Honister Pass 341.4 mm in 24 hours in December 2015; Katrina 29 August 2005, over 1,800 dead, about 80 per cent of New Orleans flooded; the Saffir-Simpson thresholds; and every countable claim in Figures A, C and D. Tariffs (1, 2, 4, 6, 9), AO splits, level labels, sol and card word limits and the family rules all hold. Publish as it is."}}},"3.1.1.4":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.1.4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Climate change is the one physical-geography room that reaches into every other part of the course: it closes the natural hazards section on Paper 1, it explains the weather extremes you meet there, and it reappears in the living world, in resource management and in the Paper 3 decision question. The examiner tests it in a very particular order — first the evidence that the climate has changed, then the natural and human causes, then the effects on people and the environment, then the two responses, mitigation and adaptation. Short questions here are almost always set on a graph or a table of carbon dioxide or temperature, so the skill of quoting data and describing a trend earns marks directly. The long questions are judgement questions: whether human activity is the main cause, whether international agreements work, whether adaptation beats mitigation. None of those can be answered by listing. You need a small stock of real figures and named schemes, and the habit of weighing one side against the other before you conclude.","idea":[{"h":"Evidence for climate change since the start of the Quaternary","t":"The Quaternary period began about 2.6 million years ago and runs to today. Across it the climate has swung repeatedly between cold glacial periods and warmer interglacials, and we know this because the past leaves records. Ice cores drilled from Antarctica and Greenland hold air bubbles that give the carbon dioxide and methane concentration of the atmosphere at the time the snow fell, while the ratio of oxygen isotopes in the ice itself gives the temperature; the Antarctic cores reach back about 800,000 years. Tree rings are wider in warm, wet years, and pollen preserved in peat bogs and lake sediments shows which plants were growing, and therefore how warm it was. For the last few centuries there are written records — harvest dates, paintings of frozen rivers, the frost fairs held on the Thames during the Little Ice Age, the last major one in 1814. From about 1850 there are thermometer records, and since 1958 the continuous measurement of carbon dioxide at Mauna Loa in Hawaii. Since 1979 satellites have measured Arctic sea ice, whose September minimum has shrunk by roughly 13 per cent a decade. A top-level answer does not simply list these sources: it says what each one measures, over what timescale, and uses the ice-core range of about 180 to 280 parts per million to show that today's figure of over 420 ppm is outside anything in the last 800,000 years.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)"},{"h":"Natural causes: orbital changes, volcanic activity and solar output","t":"The specification names three natural factors. Orbital changes, often called Milankovitch cycles, are three slow changes in the Earth's path and posture: the shape of the orbit, which stretches and rounds over about 100,000 years; the tilt of the axis, which moves between roughly 22.1 degrees and 24.5 degrees over about 41,000 years; and precession, the wobble of the axis, over roughly 20,000 to 26,000 years. Together they change how much solar energy reaches particular latitudes in particular seasons, and they set the rhythm of the glacials and interglacials seen in the ice cores. Volcanic activity works the other way and much faster: a large eruption throws sulphur dioxide into the stratosphere, where it forms droplets that reflect sunlight back to space. Global average temperature fell by about 0.5 degrees Celsius in the year or so after Mount Pinatubo erupted in the Philippines in 1991, but the droplets settle out within two or three years. Solar output varies over an 11-year sunspot cycle, and long quiet spells such as the Maunder Minimum of about 1645 to 1715 sit inside the coldest part of the Little Ice Age. A top-level answer uses these to explain change before the industrial era and then states their limits — the timescales are too long, or the effects too short-lived and too small, to explain warming over the last 150 years.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)"},{"h":"Human causes: fossil fuels, agriculture and deforestation","t":"Sunlight passes through the atmosphere as short-wave radiation and warms the ground. The ground radiates heat back as long-wave radiation, and greenhouse gases — water vapour, carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide — absorb some of it and re-radiate it downwards. That natural greenhouse effect keeps the planet habitable; adding gases strengthens it, which is the enhanced greenhouse effect. Burning fossil fuels in power stations, vehicles, homes and industry is the largest human source of carbon dioxide, and cement manufacture adds more. Agriculture is the main human source of methane: cattle and sheep release it as they digest grass, flooded rice paddies release it from the waterlogged soil, and nitrogen fertilisers release nitrous oxide. Deforestation works twice over — felling removes trees that were taking carbon dioxide out of the air by photosynthesis, and burning or rotting the timber returns the carbon they had stored. The evidence that this is what has happened is the match between the timing of industrialisation and the rise in carbon dioxide from about 280 ppm before 1800 to over 420 ppm today. A top-level answer never stops at naming a source: it carries the chain through, from the activity to the gas, to the trapping of long-wave radiation, to the rise in temperature.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)"},{"h":"Effects on people and on the environment","t":"The examiner wants an overview of both, not one case study. Environmental effects: oceans warm and expand and land ice melts, so global mean sea level rose about 0.20 metres between 1901 and 2018 and is now rising by about three to four millimetres a year; Arctic sea ice and mountain glaciers are shrinking; warmer water bleaches coral, as on the Great Barrier Reef in the back-to-back events of 2016 and 2017; as the oceans absorb carbon dioxide they become more acidic; and species shift polewards and uphill, so ecosystems are pulled apart. Effects on people: heatwaves kill, especially the old and the ill; droughts cut crop yields and cause food and water insecurity, which is worst in places such as the Sahel where farming is rain-fed and there is little money to cope; low-lying coasts and deltas such as Bangladesh, and island states such as the Maldives where average land height is about 1.5 metres, lose land and homes to flooding; and people are displaced. Some effects are positive in some places — longer growing seasons and new crops in northern Europe, less winter heating demand — which is exactly what a balanced answer needs. A top-level answer sorts effects by scale and by who bears them, and says why the poorest suffer most.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (effects)"},{"h":"Mitigation: reducing the causes","t":"Mitigation attacks the cause — the greenhouse gases themselves. Alternative energy production replaces coal and gas with wind, solar, hydroelectric, tidal and geothermal power, and with nuclear power, which is low-carbon at the point of generation: in 2020 renewables supplied about 43 per cent of the United Kingdom's electricity, more than fossil fuels over a full year for the first time, and Hornsea One off the Yorkshire coast has a capacity of about 1.2 gigawatts. Carbon capture and storage separates carbon dioxide from power-station or industrial exhaust, compresses it and pumps it into porous rock deep underground; Norway's Sleipner project has injected roughly a million tonnes a year into a sandstone layer beneath the North Sea since 1996. Planting trees creates a carbon sink, because a growing tree removes carbon dioxide by photosynthesis and locks the carbon into its wood. International agreements try to make the cuts add up: the Kyoto Protocol, agreed in 1997 and in force from 2005, set binding targets for industrialised countries, and the Paris Agreement of 2015 committed nearly 200 countries to hold warming well below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels and to pursue efforts to limit it to 1.5 degrees. A top-level answer judges each strategy on cost, time to take effect and who has to agree, rather than describing it.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (mitigation)"},{"h":"Adaptation: living with the change","t":"Adaptation accepts that some warming is already locked in and reduces the harm it does. Agricultural systems change: drought-resistant and salt-tolerant crop varieties, different planting dates, shade and mulching, moving crops to cooler ground, and in Bangladesh raising homes on earth plinths and growing salt-tolerant rice on land reached by saltwater. Water supply is managed by transfer schemes that move water from wet regions to dry ones, by desalination — Thames Water's plant at Beckton in east London, opened in 2010, treats brackish estuary water so the capital has a supply to call on in drought — and by reducing leakage, metering and rainwater harvesting. The risk from rising sea levels is reduced by sea walls and tidal barriers, by restoring salt marshes and mangroves that absorb wave energy, by building on raised ground, and by managed retreat where defence is not worth the cost. The Thames Barrier at Woolwich, with its ten gates across the river, became operational in 1982 and has been closed far more often in recent years than in the 1980s. A top-level answer keeps the distinction sharp: adaptation protects people but does nothing to slow the warming, so it buys time rather than solving the problem, and it costs money that poorer countries do not have.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (adaptation)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading Figure 1 and describing the trend","setup":"Figure 1 gives the annual mean carbon dioxide concentration measured at Mauna Loa Observatory, Hawaii, rounded to the nearest part per million: 1960, 317 ppm; 1970, 326 ppm; 1980, 339 ppm; 1990, 354 ppm; 2000, 370 ppm; 2010, 390 ppm; 2020, 414 ppm. The question is: Using Figure 1, describe the trend in atmospheric carbon dioxide between 1960 and 2020.","steps":["Check the direction before touching any arithmetic: every value in the table is larger than the one above it, so the trend is a continuous rise with no fall anywhere across the six decades.","Test whether the rise is steady by taking the first decade: 326 − 317 = 9 ppm added during the 1960s.","Take the last decade, 2010 to 2020, for comparison: 414 − 390 = 24 ppm, so each decade adds more than the one before and the rise is accelerating.","Write it as one description carrying data: carbon dioxide rose continuously, from 317 ppm in 1960 to 414 ppm in 2020, and the rise sped up — 9 ppm across the 1960s but 24 ppm across the 2010s."],"cues":["Before any numbers: is the line going up, down, or neither?","How much did the concentration change across the first ten years shown?","Does the final decade add the same amount as the first one did?","The description must carry the direction and one more thing — what?"]},{"title":"Turning a natural cause into a chain of reasoning","setup":"A six-mark question asks: Explain how natural factors can cause the Earth's climate to change. A student has written a single sentence — 'Volcanoes can change the climate.' The three natural factors the specification names are orbital changes, volcanic activity and solar output, and the student has been given one real case to use: Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines erupted in 1991 and global average temperature fell by about 0.5 degrees Celsius in the year or so afterwards. Rebuild the sentence as an explanation an examiner would call a logical chain of reasoning.","steps":["Name the mechanism rather than the event: a large eruption throws sulphur dioxide and ash high into the stratosphere, above the level where rain would wash it out.","Say what the material does once it is up there: the sulphur dioxide forms tiny sulphate droplets that reflect incoming solar radiation back to space, so less energy reaches the surface.","Attach the named evidence to the mechanism: after Pinatubo erupted in 1991, global average temperature fell by about 0.5 degrees Celsius for roughly a year.","Close the chain by stating the limit: the droplets settle out within two or three years, so volcanic cooling is short-lived and explains dips in the temperature record rather than a rise lasting a century."],"cues":["Which layer of the atmosphere must the material reach if the effect is to last months?","What do those droplets do to the sunlight arriving at the Earth?","Which real eruption can be attached here, and what happened to global temperature after it?","Why can eruptions not account for a warming trend lasting a hundred years?"]},{"title":"Using Figure 3 to explain an adaptation","setup":"Figure 3 is a photograph of the Thames Barrier at Woolwich in east London, taken from the south bank looking north across the river. Ten steel gates span the river between piers capped with curved stainless-steel hoods. The four central gates are the widest and are shown rotated upright out of the riverbed, so a solid steel wall crosses the channel. Downstream, on the right of the picture, the water is choppy and stands noticeably higher against the piers than the calm water upstream on the left. Behind the barrier on the north bank stand flat-roofed warehouses, blocks of flats and a main road, all on low ground close to river level. The question is: Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain how the structure shown reduces the risk to London from rising sea levels.","steps":["Anchor the answer in what the photograph actually shows: the four central gates have been rotated upright, so the barrier is in use and a steel wall now crosses the whole channel.","Explain what that does to the water: the wall blocks a North Sea storm surge from travelling upstream on the flood tide, which is why the water in the picture stands higher on the downstream side than on the upstream side.","Link it to what is being protected: the low, flat ground behind the barrier — the warehouses, flats and main road in the photograph — lies close to river level and would be under water in a surge tide, so it stays dry.","Finish with the point that makes this adaptation: the barrier does nothing to slow sea-level rise, it only manages the risk from it, and as mean sea level climbs the gates must be closed more often to give the same protection."],"cues":["What in the picture shows the barrier is working rather than resting on the riverbed?","Why is the water on one side of the gates higher than on the other?","What lies behind the barrier, and why is it at risk?","Is this tackling the cause or the consequence — and what follows from that?"]},{"title":"Building one paragraph of a 'to what extent' answer","setup":"The question is: 'Human activity is the main cause of climate change.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? The student has these facts available: Antarctic ice cores show carbon dioxide stayed between about 180 and 280 parts per million for 800,000 years, and it is above 420 ppm today; orbital cycles operate over about 41,000 years for the tilt of the axis and about 100,000 years for the shape of the orbit; industrial burning of fossil fuels began about 200 years ago. Write the paragraph that deals with the natural side and still answers the question.","steps":["Open with the claim the paragraph will defend, not with a fact: the human contribution is the only candidate that acts fast enough to explain the warming of the last 150 years.","Bring the natural rival in fairly: orbital cycles work over about 41,000 and 100,000 years, so they set the timing of the ice ages but cannot alter the climate inside a single human lifetime.","Set the evidence against it: the ice cores hold carbon dioxide between about 180 and 280 ppm for 800,000 years, yet it is above 420 ppm after roughly 200 years of fossil-fuel burning.","End with a mini-judgement that answers the command: natural factors have not stopped working, but on this timescale they are the background noise and not the driver."],"cues":["What job should the first sentence of an evaluative paragraph do?","Why can orbital change not be the main cause of recent warming?","Which pair of figures shows today's carbon dioxide is outside the natural range?","What must the final sentence do so the paragraph answers 'to what extent'?"]},{"title":"Judging between mitigation and adaptation with Figure 4","setup":"Figure 4 is a briefing note written for this room, with illustrative figures. A low-lying English coastal town has 8 million pounds to spend once. Option A: raise the concrete sea wall along the promenade by one metre and renew its timber groynes. Option B: buy 40 hectares of farmland behind the wall, breach the wall and let the land flood to form salt marsh. Option C: fit solar panels and heat pumps to every school, library and council office in the town. Option D: move the doctors' surgery and the primary school to higher ground and fit flood gates to the 300 homes nearest the river. The question is: Using Figure 4, suggest one advantage and one disadvantage of choosing Option C.","steps":["Sort the four options first: A, B and D all change what happens when the sea arrives, so they are adaptation; only C cuts greenhouse gas emissions, so only C is mitigation.","Take the advantage from what Option C actually does: solar panels and heat pumps cut the carbon dioxide released by the town's own buildings, and cut its fuel bills every year afterwards, so it tackles a cause rather than a symptom.","Take the disadvantage from what Option C does not do: the town's problem is flooding from the sea, and nothing in Option C keeps water out of the doctors' surgery or the 300 homes by the river.","Add the scale point that turns this into a reasoned suggestion: one town's emissions are a minute fraction of the global total, so Option C cannot measurably slow the sea-level rise that threatens it — mitigation only works if thousands of places act together."],"cues":["Which one of the four options attacks the cause rather than the consequence?","What does the town gain, year after year, from Option C?","What is the town's actual problem, and does Option C touch it?","Why might one small town's mitigation make no measurable difference to that town?"]}],"check":[{"q":"An ice core is drilled from the Antarctic ice sheet. Name the two things measured in it and say what each one tells you.","a":"The air trapped in the bubbles gives the concentration of carbon dioxide and methane in the atmosphere when that snow fell. The ratio of oxygen isotopes in the ice itself gives the temperature at that time. Together they show that carbon dioxide and temperature rose and fell together through the glacials and interglacials."},{"q":"Volcanic eruptions release carbon dioxide, so why does a large eruption cool the climate rather than warm it?","a":"Because the sulphur dioxide it injects into the stratosphere forms sulphate droplets that reflect sunlight back to space within weeks, and that cooling outweighs the small amount of carbon dioxide released. The cooling lasts two or three years until the droplets settle out."},{"q":"The tilt of the Earth's axis changes over about 41,000 years. Why can that not explain the warming measured since 1900?","a":"Because 41,000 years is far too long a cycle to produce a measurable change in a century; over the last hundred years the tilt has barely moved, while temperature has risen by about 1.1 degrees Celsius."},{"q":"Give one mitigation response and one adaptation response to rising sea levels, and say which tackles the cause.","a":"Mitigation: replacing coal-fired power with offshore wind, which cuts the carbon dioxide emissions causing the warming. Adaptation: building a tidal barrier such as the Thames Barrier, which reduces the harm but leaves the cause untouched. Only mitigation tackles the cause."}]},"room":{"facts":["The Quaternary period began about 2.6 million years ago; Antarctic ice cores give a continuous record of about the last 800,000 years, in which carbon dioxide swung between roughly 180 parts per million in glacials and roughly 280 ppm in interglacials.","Carbon dioxide was about 280 ppm before industrialisation; the annual mean at Mauna Loa passed 400 ppm in 2015 and is now above 420 ppm — higher than any value in the 800,000-year ice-core record.","Global mean surface temperature in 2011 to 2020 was about 1.1 degrees Celsius above the 1850 to 1900 average, and global mean sea level rose about 0.20 metres between 1901 and 2018.","The three natural causes named by the specification are orbital changes (eccentricity about 100,000 years, axial tilt between roughly 22.1 and 24.5 degrees over about 41,000 years, precession roughly every 20,000 to 26,000 years), volcanic activity and solar output (an 11-year sunspot cycle).","The three human causes named by the specification are the use of fossil fuels, agriculture (methane from cattle and rice paddies, nitrous oxide from fertiliser) and deforestation.","The Paris Agreement of 2015 committed nearly 200 countries to hold warming well below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels and to pursue efforts to limit it to 1.5 degrees; the Kyoto Protocol of 1997, in force from 2005, set binding targets for industrialised countries only."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, calculate the increase in the annual mean carbon dioxide concentration at Mauna Loa between 1960 and 2020. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer: 97 ppm. One mark for an indication of the calculation, eg 414 − 317. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Read the 1960 row of Figure 1: 317 ppm. Read the 2020 row: 414 ppm. An increase is the later value minus the earlier one, so 414 − 317 = 97. The answer is an increase of 97 parts per million, and the unit should be given because the table is measured in parts per million.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only two rows of the table matter here — the earliest and the latest.","An increase is found by taking the earlier reading away from the later one.","Show the subtraction, then give the answer with the unit the table uses."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, describe the trend in atmospheric carbon dioxide between 1960 and 2020. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic description of the trend, eg a continuous rise; a bare statement of direction such as 'it increases' earns this first mark. Second mark for developing it with data from the table, or for recognising that the rate of increase itself grows. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Carbon dioxide rises continuously through the whole record, from 317 ppm in 1960 to 414 ppm in 2020, with no decade in which it falls. The rise also gets faster: the 1960s added 9 ppm (317 to 326), while the decade from 2010 to 2020 added 24 ppm (390 to 414). So the trend is an accelerating increase of 97 ppm over sixty years.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look down the column first and decide what the values are doing overall.","A second mark needs figures from the table, not just a direction.","Compare the size of the change in the earliest decade with the size in the latest."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)"]},{"q":"State two ways in which scientists can find out what the climate was like before written records began. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for two valid sources, eg ice cores; tree rings; pollen analysis from peat bogs or lake sediments. The source only has to be named. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"Ice cores drilled from Antarctica or Greenland, whose trapped air bubbles and oxygen isotopes record past greenhouse gas levels and temperature. Tree rings, which are wider in warm, wet growing seasons than in cold, dry ones.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about natural materials that grow or build up year by year and lock in a record.","You need two separate sources, each named — no explanation is required.","Two words each is enough: name the material, not the process."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)"]},{"q":"Outline one way in which changes in the Earth's orbit can cause the climate to change. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for naming an orbital change, eg the shape of the orbit, the tilt of the axis or precession. Second mark for developing it, eg it alters the amount of solar energy reaching a given latitude, so glacials and interglacials follow. AO2 = 2 marks.","sol":"The tilt of the Earth's axis changes between roughly 22.1 and 24.5 degrees over about 41,000 years. A smaller tilt means cooler summers in high latitudes, so winter snow survives the summer and builds into ice sheets, which cools the climate further because the bright ice reflects sunlight.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Three things about the Earth's path and posture change slowly — pick whichever one you can explain.","The second mark comes from saying what the change does to the energy the Earth receives.","Name the change, then carry it one step to its effect on the climate."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)"]},{"q":"Explain how the burning of fossil fuels contributes to the enhanced greenhouse effect. [3 marks]","a":"Credit a chain of reasoning: burning coal, oil or gas releases carbon dioxide (1); the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere rises (1); more of the long-wave radiation leaving the Earth's surface is absorbed and re-radiated back down, so the surface warms (1). AO2 = 3 marks.","sol":"Burning coal, oil and gas in power stations, vehicles and industry releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. Because it is released faster than plants and oceans can absorb it, the concentration builds up, from about 280 ppm before industrialisation to over 420 ppm today. Carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas: it lets short-wave solar radiation through, but absorbs the long-wave radiation the warmed ground emits and re-radiates part of it back to the surface. With more of the gas present, less heat escapes to space and the lower atmosphere and surface become warmer.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The question is about one gas and what it does to radiation, not about which countries burn most fuel.","Three marks means three linked steps, each one caused by the step before it.","Begin at the chimney or exhaust pipe and finish at the temperature of the ground."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, give one reason why scientists describe the recent rise in carbon dioxide as unusual. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a point taken from the graph, eg the present value is higher than any in the record. Second mark for developing it with evidence from the graph, eg it is above 420 ppm when the record never exceeded about 300 ppm in 800,000 years, and the rise took under 200 years rather than thousands. AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Across the whole 800,000 years plotted in Figure 2 the concentration never rises above about 300 ppm, yet at the right-hand edge it climbs above 420 ppm. It is unusual both in size, being far outside the range the saw-tooth has kept to through eight glacial cycles, and in speed, because the graph shows that climb happening almost vertically in under 200 years when previous rises took thousands of years.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Compare the very end of the plotted line with everything to the left of it.","Two marks here means one point and then evidence for it, not two separate points.","Quote a value from the graph rather than describing the shape alone."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)"]},{"q":"What is meant by mitigation of climate change? [1 mark]","a":"One mark for the idea of reducing or removing the causes, ie cutting greenhouse gas emissions or taking greenhouse gases out of the atmosphere. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"Mitigation means action that reduces the causes of climate change, by cutting greenhouse gas emissions or removing greenhouse gases from the atmosphere.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["There are two responses to climate change and they differ in what they aim at.","A one-mark definition needs the action and the thing it acts on: decide which of the two responses this word names, then say what it works on.","One sentence naming what is reduced is enough."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (mitigation)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain how the structure shown reduces the risk to London from rising sea levels. [4 marks]","a":"Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear understanding of how a tidal barrier defends a city, with sound interpretation of the photograph. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): limited understanding, basic interpretation. No credit for simply identifying the structure. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"In the photograph the four widest gates have been rotated upright out of the riverbed, so a solid steel wall now crosses the channel between the piers. This blocks a North Sea storm surge from travelling up the Thames on the flood tide, which is why the water downstream on the right is choppy and stands higher against the piers than the calm water upstream on the left. Behind the barrier the ground is flat and close to river level — the warehouses, flats and main road visible on the north bank would be flooded by a surge tide, and holding the water back keeps them dry and keeps the road open. As mean sea level rises, surge tides start from a higher base, so the same gates protect the same land only if they are closed more often. The barrier therefore reduces the risk from rising sea levels rather than the rise itself: it is an adaptation, buying protection for a very valuable and very low-lying city.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Everything you say must be visible in the photograph or follow directly from it.","Work outwards: what the gates are doing, what that does to the water, what is spared as a result.","Two developed points beat four undeveloped ones, and one of them should say what the structure does not do."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (adaptation)"]},{"q":"Explain how natural factors can cause the Earth's climate to change. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): a logical chain of reasoning for more than one natural factor, with accurate detail and correct timescales. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): accurate knowledge of the factors with clear understanding but limited chains. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): factors named with little or no development. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Orbital changes work over tens of thousands of years. The tilt of the Earth's axis moves between roughly 22.1 and 24.5 degrees over about 41,000 years and the shape of the orbit stretches and rounds over about 100,000 years, which alters how much solar energy reaches high latitudes in summer. When high-latitude summers are cool, winter snow survives, ice sheets grow and the bright surface reflects still more sunlight, deepening the cooling. This is the rhythm of the glacials and interglacials recorded in the ice cores. Volcanic activity works far faster. A large eruption injects sulphur dioxide into the stratosphere, where it forms sulphate droplets that reflect sunlight back to space; global average temperature fell by about 0.5 degrees Celsius in the year after Mount Pinatubo erupted in 1991, but the droplets settled out within two to three years. Solar output varies too, over an 11-year sunspot cycle, and the long quiet spell of the Maunder Minimum from about 1645 to 1715 falls inside the coldest part of the Little Ice Age. Each factor therefore changes the energy balance of the Earth, but on very different timescales.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The specification names three natural factors — use at least two of them.","Each one needs a chain: what changes, what that does to the energy the Earth receives, what happens to temperature.","Timescales separate these factors from one another, so give them."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)"]},{"q":"Suggest why the effects of climate change are more severe for some groups of people than for others. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): thorough application of understanding, linking exposure and ability to cope, with located support. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear reasons with some support. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): generic statements about poor countries suffering more. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Severity depends on two things: how exposed people are, and what they can do about it. Exposure is a matter of geography. People living on low-lying deltas and islands, such as Bangladesh or the Maldives where average land height is about 1.5 metres, are exposed to a sea level that rose about 0.20 metres between 1901 and 2018 and is still rising; people in the Sahel are exposed to longer droughts because their farming is rain-fed and has no reservoir behind it. Ability to cope is a matter of wealth. A high-income country can pay for a tidal barrier such as the Thames Barrier, for desalination plants and for irrigation, and can insure the losses; a low-income farming household cannot, so a single failed harvest becomes hunger, debt and migration. Within any country the same split appears: the old, the ill and those in poor housing die in heatwaves while others sit in air conditioning. The unfairness is sharpened because the countries emitting least tend to be the most exposed and the least able to adapt.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two different things decide how badly someone is hit — one is about where they live.","Sort your points under those two headings rather than listing effects.","Name a place for each heading so the suggestion is anchored rather than generic."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (effects)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, suggest one advantage and one disadvantage of choosing Option C. [4 marks]","a":"Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear interpretation of the resource, with a developed advantage and a developed disadvantage. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): basic interpretation, points not developed. Answers must use Option C as printed. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"The advantage is that Option C is the only option in Figure 4 that tackles the cause. Solar panels and heat pumps on every school, library and council office cut the carbon dioxide those buildings release, and they keep cutting it, and the fuel bills, for as long as they last, so the money buys a permanent reduction rather than a repair. The disadvantage is that it does nothing about the town's actual hazard. The other three options all deal with water arriving: raising the sea wall, letting 40 hectares flood as salt marsh, or moving the surgery and school and fitting flood gates to 300 homes. Option C leaves the surgery, the school and those homes exactly as exposed as they are now. On top of that, one small town's emissions are a minute fraction of the global total, so the carbon saved cannot measurably slow the sea-level rise that threatens it; mitigation on this scale only works if thousands of places do the same.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Start by sorting the four options into the two kinds of response to climate change.","The advantage should come from what Option C does; the disadvantage from what it leaves untouched.","Both points need developing once, and both must refer to what Figure 4 actually offers."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (mitigation)","3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (adaptation)"]},{"q":"Explain how two different mitigation strategies reduce the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): a detailed mechanism for each of two strategies, supported by a named scheme or figure. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): accurate knowledge of both with clear but partial explanation. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): strategies named only. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Alternative energy production reduces carbon dioxide at source. Wind, solar, hydroelectric, tidal and geothermal generation, and nuclear power, produce electricity without burning carbon, so every unit they supply is a unit not generated by a coal or gas station. In 2020 renewables supplied about 43 per cent of the United Kingdom's electricity, more than fossil fuels across a full year for the first time, with large offshore wind farms such as Hornsea One off the Yorkshire coast, whose capacity is about 1.2 gigawatts, doing much of the work. Carbon capture and storage works on the emissions that are still produced. Carbon dioxide is separated from the exhaust gases of a power station or factory, compressed into a dense fluid and pumped down into porous rock deep underground, where an impermeable cap rock traps it; Norway's Sleipner project has injected roughly a million tonnes a year into sandstone beneath the North Sea since 1996. The first strategy prevents emissions, the second intercepts them, and both cut the concentration that drives the enhanced greenhouse effect.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The specification lists four ways of reducing the causes — choose the two you can explain with a real scheme.","For each one, explain the mechanism by which carbon dioxide is kept out of the air, not just what the scheme is.","A named scheme or a figure for each strategy lifts the answer above description."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (mitigation)"]},{"q":"'Human activity is the main cause of climate change.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): detailed knowledge of natural and human causes, thorough understanding of how each alters the energy balance, and a reasoned evaluation of their relative importance on the timescale in question. Level 2 (Clear, 4–6): clear knowledge with reasonable evaluation. Level 1 (Basic, 1–3): limited knowledge, causes listed with slight evaluation. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"The climate has always changed. The Quaternary period, which began about 2.6 million years ago, has swung repeatedly between cold glacials and warm interglacials, and those swings were entirely natural. Changes in the Earth's orbit are the main driver: the shape of the orbit alters over about 100,000 years, the tilt of the axis moves between roughly 22.1 and 24.5 degrees over about 41,000 years, and the axis wobbles over roughly 20,000 to 26,000 years, changing how much solar energy reaches high latitudes in summer and so deciding whether ice sheets grow or melt. Volcanic activity and solar output matter too. After Mount Pinatubo erupted in the Philippines in 1991, sulphate droplets in the stratosphere reflected sunlight and global average temperature fell by about 0.5 degrees Celsius; during the Maunder Minimum of about 1645 to 1715 sunspots were very rare, and that quiet Sun sits inside the coldest part of the Little Ice Age, when the Thames froze hard enough to carry frost fairs.\n\nHowever, none of these factors works fast enough to explain the last 150 years. Orbital cycles operate over tens of thousands of years, so they cannot change the climate within a lifetime. Volcanic cooling fades within two or three years as the droplets settle out, and it cools rather than warms. The sunspot cycle is about 11 years long and its effect on global temperature is small. The human explanation, by contrast, fits both the timing and the size. Antarctic ice cores show carbon dioxide held between about 180 parts per million in glacials and about 280 ppm in interglacials for 800,000 years; the annual mean at Mauna Loa passed 400 ppm in 2015 and is now above 420 ppm, reached in roughly two centuries of burning coal, oil and gas, clearing forest and farming livestock. Extra carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide absorb more of the long-wave radiation leaving the surface and re-radiate it downwards, and global surface temperature in 2011 to 2020 stood about 1.1 degrees Celsius above 1850 to 1900.\n\nTo a very large extent I agree. Natural factors have not switched off: they still produce the year-to-year wobbles in the record and they remain the whole explanation for change before the industrial era. But on the timescale the statement is really about, only human emissions can account for a rise of this speed and this size, so human activity is the driver and natural factors are the background.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The statement is only arguable if you give the natural side its full weight first.","What separates the two sets of causes is not whether they work but how fast and how far.","The judgement has to say how far you agree, and on what timescale that judgement holds."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)","3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)"]},{"q":"Assess the effectiveness of international agreements as a way of managing climate change. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): detailed knowledge of named agreements, thorough understanding of why global action is needed, and a substantiated overall judgement weighing what they achieve against their weaknesses. Level 2 (Clear, 4–6): clear knowledge with reasonable judgement. Level 1 (Basic, 1–3): agreements named with limited evaluation. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Greenhouse gases mix through the whole atmosphere, so carbon dioxide released in one country warms every country. That is why the problem cannot be solved nationally and why international agreements exist. The Kyoto Protocol, agreed in 1997 and in force from 2005, was the first to set binding emission targets, and its strength was that the targets were legally binding and monitored. Its weakness was its coverage: the targets fell only on industrialised countries, the United States never ratified it, and rapidly industrialising economies were not bound at all, so global emissions carried on rising. The Paris Agreement of 2015 answered the coverage problem. Nearly 200 countries signed, and it set a shared goal of holding warming well below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels while pursuing efforts to limit it to 1.5 degrees.\n\nAgainst that, Paris traded strength for breadth. Each country sets its own pledge, and the pledges are reviewed rather than enforced; no country is punished for missing one, and a government can withdraw. The test of effectiveness is the atmosphere, and it has not yet passed: the annual mean carbon dioxide concentration at Mauna Loa passed 400 parts per million in 2015, the year Paris was agreed, and is now above 420 ppm, while global surface temperature in 2011 to 2020 was about 1.1 degrees Celsius above 1850 to 1900. Agreements also struggle with fairness. Low-income countries argue, with justice, that the emissions already in the atmosphere are largely not theirs, yet they face the worst of the flooding and drought, so they want money to adapt as the price of cutting their own emissions.\n\nYet they do work indirectly. Paris set the target that national law is now written against, such as the United Kingdom's legally binding commitment to reach net zero by 2050, and it created the reporting and review that let countries hold each other to account. It has also shifted investment: renewables supplied about 43 per cent of United Kingdom electricity in 2020, and offshore wind on the scale of Hornsea One, at about 1.2 gigawatts, is built because governments guaranteed a market.\n\nOverall, international agreements are necessary but not sufficient. They are the only mechanism that can make the cuts add up globally, and they have changed national policy and investment. Judged on emissions so far, however, they have not yet delivered the reductions their own targets require, so their effectiveness must be rated as partial.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Begin with why the problem needs an international answer at all — that frames the judgement.","Two agreements are worth contrasting, and they fail and succeed in opposite ways.","Judge them against a measurable test, and say what that test shows before you conclude."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (mitigation)"]},{"q":"Evaluate the view that adaptation is a more realistic response to climate change than mitigation. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): detailed and balanced appreciation of the advantages and disadvantages of both responses, supported by named schemes, with a substantiated conclusion. Level 2 (Clear, 4–6): clear knowledge of both with reasonable evaluation. Level 1 (Basic, 1–3): one response described, judgement weak or generic. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Mitigation reduces the causes of climate change; adaptation reduces the harm it does. There is a real case for calling adaptation the more realistic of the two. Warming already in the system cannot be undone, so the flooding, drought and heat are coming whatever is agreed. Adaptation also works at a scale a single authority can manage and delivers protection quickly: the Thames Barrier at Woolwich, with its ten gates, has protected London from North Sea surge tides since 1982; Thames Water's desalination plant at Beckton, opened in 2010, treats brackish estuary water so London has a supply in drought; in Bangladesh homes are raised on earth plinths and salt-tolerant rice is grown on land reached by saltwater. Crucially, the benefit falls on the people who paid for it, which makes adaptation easy to justify politically in a way that mitigation is not.\n\nThe case against is that adaptation treats the symptom. It does nothing to slow a sea level that rose about 0.20 metres between 1901 and 2018 and is still rising by about three to four millimetres a year, so every defence must eventually be raised again and the Thames Barrier must be closed ever more often. It is expensive and recurring, which means the countries most exposed — the Maldives, where average land height is about 1.5 metres, or the Sahel — are the least able to afford it. And some things cannot be adapted to at all: a bleached coral reef, as on the Great Barrier Reef in 2016 and 2017, or a country losing its land.\n\nMitigation is the only response that attacks the driver, and it is not unrealistic. Renewables supplied about 43 per cent of United Kingdom electricity in 2020, overtaking fossil fuels across a full year for the first time; carbon capture and storage has been proven at Sleipner in the Norwegian North Sea since 1996 at roughly a million tonnes a year; planting trees creates a carbon sink. Its weakness is that it only counts globally, so it depends on agreements such as Paris in 2015, and its benefits arrive decades after the cost.\n\nMy judgement is that the view is half right. Adaptation is more realistic in the short term and at a local scale, because it is affordable to some and works immediately. But it is a holding action whose cost rises for as long as emissions continue, so it is only realistic alongside mitigation, not instead of it.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Define both responses in the opening line so the rest of the answer can weigh them.","Judge each one against the same three tests: speed, cost and who benefits.","The word 'realistic' invites a qualified answer — decide over what timescale and at what scale."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (mitigation)","3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (adaptation)"]},{"q":"Discuss the effects of climate change on people and on the environment. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): a balanced discussion with well-developed ideas, detailed knowledge of effects on both people and the environment, understanding of the links between them, and a reasoned view of their relative significance. Level 2 (Clear, 4–6): clear knowledge of both with some development. Level 1 (Basic, 1–3): effects listed, links slight. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"The environmental effects begin with heat itself. Global surface temperature in 2011 to 2020 was about 1.1 degrees Celsius above 1850 to 1900, and warm water expands while land ice melts, so global mean sea level rose about 0.20 metres between 1901 and 2018 and is now rising by about three to four millimetres a year. Arctic sea ice has shrunk, its September minimum falling by roughly 13 per cent a decade since satellite measurement began in 1979, and mountain glaciers are retreating worldwide. Warmer seas bleach coral, as the Great Barrier Reef suffered in the back-to-back events of 2016 and 2017, and as the oceans absorb carbon dioxide they grow more acidic, which weakens shell-building organisms. On land, species shift polewards and uphill and spring events come earlier, which pulls ecosystems apart when a bird arrives after the insects it feeds on have peaked.\n\nThese changes reach people through the same physical systems. Rising sea level and stronger storm surges threaten deltas and low islands: Bangladesh is delta country only a few metres above the sea, and the Maldives has an average land height of about 1.5 metres. Longer droughts cut yields where farming is rain-fed, as in the Sahel, causing food and water insecurity, debt and migration, while heatwaves kill the old and the ill directly. Not every effect is negative: a longer growing season in northern Europe allows new crops, and warmer winters cut heating demand and cold-related deaths.\n\nThe effects are unequal, and that is the heart of the discussion. Exposure depends on where people live, but the damage done depends on wealth. A high-income country can build a tidal barrier, insure losses and irrigate; a subsistence farmer cannot, so the same drought means a lost harvest rather than a higher food price. The countries that have emitted least are often the most exposed and the least able to respond.\n\nOverall the environmental and human effects cannot be separated, because people are harmed through the environment: it is the melting ice and the failing rains that flood the delta and empty the well. The most significant effects are those that are irreversible on a human timescale — lost ice, lost reef and lost land — because adaptation can soften the others but cannot bring these back.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The command asks for both sides of the title, so plan environmental and human effects as separate blocks before you link them.","Each effect needs a mechanism and a figure or a place, not a label.","A discussion has to weigh, so decide which effects matter most and why before you write the conclusion."],"codes":["3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (effects)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"About how far back does the continuous carbon dioxide record from Antarctic ice cores reach?","a":"About 800,000 years."},{"q":"Name the three natural factors the specification gives as possible causes of climate change.","a":"Orbital changes, volcanic activity and solar output."},{"q":"Which response to climate change tackles its causes, and which reduces the harm it does?","a":"Mitigation tackles the causes; adaptation reduces the harm."},{"q":"What temperature limits does the Paris Agreement of 2015 set?","a":"To hold warming well below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels, and to pursue efforts to limit it to 1.5 degrees Celsius."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Quaternary period","back":"The geological period that began about 2.6 million years ago and runs to the present day. Its climate has swung repeatedly between cold glacial periods and warmer interglacials.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)","open":false},{"front":"Ice core","back":"A cylinder of ice drilled from an ice sheet. Bubbles trapped in it give past carbon dioxide and methane levels; oxygen isotopes in the ice give past temperature. Antarctic cores reach back about 800,000 years.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)","open":false},{"front":"Give two sources of evidence for climate change from before 1850","back":"Ice cores from Antarctica or Greenland; tree rings, which are wider in warm, wet years; pollen preserved in peat bogs and lake sediments.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)","open":true},{"front":"The Mauna Loa carbon dioxide record","back":"Continuous measurement of atmospheric carbon dioxide in Hawaii since 1958. The annual mean rises every year, passed 400 parts per million in 2015 and is now above 420 ppm, against about 280 ppm before industrialisation.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (evidence)","open":false},{"front":"Orbital changes (Milankovitch cycles)","back":"Three slow changes: eccentricity, the orbit's shape, about 100,000 years; axial tilt, between roughly 22.1° and 24.5°, about 41,000 years; precession, the axis wobble, roughly every 20,000 to 26,000 years.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)","open":false},{"front":"How volcanic activity changes the climate","back":"A large eruption injects sulphur dioxide into the stratosphere, where it forms sulphate droplets that reflect sunlight. Mount Pinatubo (1991) cooled global temperature by about 0.5 °C for about a year; the effect fades within two to three years.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)","open":false},{"front":"How solar output changes the climate","back":"The Sun's energy output varies over an 11-year sunspot cycle. Long quiet spells reduce it slightly: the Maunder Minimum, about 1645 to 1715, when sunspots were very rare, falls inside the coldest part of the Little Ice Age.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)","open":false},{"front":"The enhanced greenhouse effect","back":"Short-wave solar radiation passes through the atmosphere and warms the surface, which radiates long-wave heat. Greenhouse gases absorb and re-radiate it downwards; adding carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide traps more, so the surface warms.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)","open":false},{"front":"How agriculture and deforestation add to climate change","back":"Cattle release methane as they digest grass and flooded rice paddies release it too, while fertilisers release nitrous oxide. Felling forest removes trees that were absorbing carbon dioxide, and burning the timber returns its carbon to the air.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (causes)","open":false},{"front":"Name two effects of climate change on the environment","back":"Sea level rising as oceans warm and expand and land ice melts; Arctic sea ice and glaciers shrinking; coral bleaching, as on the Great Barrier Reef in 2016 and 2017.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (effects)","open":true},{"front":"Name two effects of climate change on people","back":"Deaths and illness in longer, hotter heatwaves; failed harvests and food insecurity where droughts lengthen, as in the Sahel; homes and farmland lost to flooding on low-lying coasts and deltas.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Climate change is the result of natural and human factors, and has a range of effects (effects)","open":true},{"front":"Mitigation","back":"Action that reduces the causes of climate change by cutting greenhouse gas emissions or removing them from the air: alternative energy production, carbon capture and storage, planting trees, and international agreements.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (mitigation)","open":false},{"front":"Carbon capture and storage","back":"Carbon dioxide is separated from power-station or industrial exhaust gases, compressed and pumped into porous rock deep underground beneath a cap rock. Norway's Sleipner project has injected roughly a million tonnes a year beneath the North Sea since 1996.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (mitigation)","open":false},{"front":"The Paris Agreement, 2015","back":"Nearly 200 countries agreed to hold global warming well below 2 °C above pre-industrial levels and to pursue efforts to limit it to 1.5 °C, through pledges each country sets for itself and reviews.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (mitigation)","open":false},{"front":"Adaptation","back":"Action that reduces the harm climate change does without tackling its causes: drought-resistant and salt-tolerant crops, water transfer and desalination, sea walls and tidal barriers, raising buildings and managed retreat.","code":"3.1.1.4 — Managing climate change involves both mitigation (reducing causes) and adaptation (responding to change) (adaptation)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Atmospheric carbon dioxide at Mauna Loa Observatory, Hawaii. A table written for this room from the real Mauna Loa record; the values are annual means rounded to the nearest part per million.\nYear | Mean carbon dioxide concentration (ppm)\n1960 | 317\n1970 | 326\n1980 | 339\n1990 | 354\n2000 | 370\n2010 | 390\n2020 | 414","Figure 2 — The Antarctic ice-core record of carbon dioxide. A graph described for this room from the real ice-core record. The horizontal axis runs from 800 thousand years before present on the left to the present day on the right. The vertical axis is carbon dioxide concentration in parts per million, scaled from 150 to 450. The plotted line is a repeating saw-tooth: it falls to a low of roughly 180 ppm during each cold glacial period and rises to about 280 ppm in each warm interglacial, and to about 300 ppm in the warmest of them, and this pattern repeats about eight times across the record. At the extreme right-hand edge the line leaves the saw-tooth altogether and climbs almost vertically, in under 200 years, to above 420 ppm — higher than any value anywhere else on the graph.","Figure 3 — The Thames Barrier at Woolwich, east London. A photograph described for this room. It is taken from the south bank of the Thames, looking north across the river. Ten steel gates span the river between piers capped with curved, shining stainless-steel hoods. The four central gates are the widest, and they are shown rotated upright out of the riverbed so that a solid steel wall crosses the channel. Downstream, to the right of the picture, the water is choppy and stands noticeably higher against the piers than the calm water upstream on the left. Behind the barrier on the north bank stand flat-roofed warehouses, blocks of flats and a main road, all on low ground close to river level.","Figure 4 — 'Four ways to spend the money': a briefing note to the councillors of a low-lying English coastal town. This extract is written for this room; the town, its options and all the figures in it are illustrative and not real. The council has 8 million pounds to spend once, and officers have set out four options. Option A: raise the concrete sea wall along the promenade by one metre and renew its timber groynes. Option B: buy 40 hectares of farmland behind the wall, breach the wall and let the land flood to form salt marsh that absorbs wave energy. Option C: fit solar panels and heat pumps to every school, library and council office in the town. Option D: move the doctors' surgery and the primary school to higher ground and fit flood gates to the 300 homes nearest the river."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) on 'Human activity is the main cause of climate change.' To what extent do you agree?","items":["Point: the human contribution is the only cause that acts fast enough to explain the warming of the last 150 years, so natural factors cannot be the main driver of it.","Named evidence: Antarctic ice cores hold carbon dioxide between about 180 parts per million in glacials and about 280 ppm in interglacials across 800,000 years; the annual mean at Mauna Loa passed 400 ppm in 2015 and is now above 420 ppm.","Developed explanation: orbital cycles work over about 41,000 years for the tilt of the axis and about 100,000 years for the shape of the orbit, so they set the timing of the ice ages but cannot alter the climate inside a human lifetime; volcanic cooling fades within two or three years as the sulphate droplets settle out; the sunspot cycle is only about 11 years long and its effect on global temperature is small. Human emissions, by contrast, moved carbon dioxide outside its 800,000-year range in roughly two centuries, and the extra carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide absorb more of the long-wave radiation leaving the surface and re-radiate it downwards.","Link to the question: the statement is really a claim about the recent record, and on that record the size and the speed of the change match the human cause and no natural one.","Mini-judgement: natural factors have not stopped working — they still produce the wobbles in the temperature record and explain everything before the industrial era — but on this timescale they are the background and human activity is the driver.","Why it earns Level 3: it meets 'Demonstrates detailed knowledge' of both sets of causes, 'Shows thorough geographical understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes' by carrying each cause through to the energy balance, and 'Demonstrates application of knowledge and understanding in a coherent and reasoned way in evaluating the relative significance' of the two sets of causes. The specialist terms it actually uses — glacial, interglacial, orbital cycle, sulphate droplets, long-wave radiation — also support High performance SPaG."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–3) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The paragraph as written: 'Climate change is mainly caused by humans. We burn a lot of fossil fuels and cut down trees which is bad for the environment. There are also natural causes like volcanoes and the sun. Volcanoes give out gases. So I agree that humans are the main cause because we do the most damage.'","What it does have: the two sets of causes are both mentioned, and there is a final sentence that takes a side, so it is not zero.","Descriptor it meets: 'Demonstrates limited knowledge of… causes', 'Shows slight geographical understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes' and 'Demonstrates limited application of knowledge and understanding in evaluating'.","Descriptor it misses: 'Demonstrates clear knowledge' and 'reasonable application… in evaluating' at Level 2. Nothing is named — no eruption, no record, no figure — so there is no knowledge to apply.","The specific failures: 'volcanoes give out gases' is wrong-footed, because the climate effect of an eruption is cooling by sulphate droplets reflecting sunlight, not warming by its gases; no mechanism is given for how burning fuel warms anything; no timescale is given, which is the whole basis of the judgement; and 'we do the most damage' is an opinion, not evaluation.","How to lift it to Level 3: attach one figure to each claim (180 to 280 ppm for 800,000 years against over 420 ppm now), replace 'bad for the environment' with the radiation chain, and replace the last sentence with a judgement about timescale."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the processes, terms and figures for climate change","items":["Quaternary period: began about 2.6 million years ago and continues today; repeated glacials and interglacials.","Ice cores: drilled in Antarctica and Greenland; trapped air bubbles give past carbon dioxide and methane; oxygen isotope ratios in the ice give past temperature; the Antarctic record runs about 800,000 years.","Other evidence: tree rings (wider in warm, wet years), pollen in peat and lake sediments, historical records such as harvest dates and the Thames frost fairs of the Little Ice Age (the last major one in 1814), thermometer records from about 1850, satellite measurement of Arctic sea ice since 1979.","Ice-core carbon dioxide range: about 180 parts per million in glacials, about 280 ppm in interglacials, for the whole 800,000 years.","Mauna Loa record: continuous since 1958; the annual mean passed 400 ppm in 2015 and is now above 420 ppm.","Warming so far: global surface temperature in 2011 to 2020 was about 1.1 degrees Celsius above the 1850 to 1900 average.","Sea level: rose about 0.20 metres between 1901 and 2018; now rising by about three to four millimetres a year, from thermal expansion of warming seawater and melting land ice.","Arctic sea ice: the September minimum has shrunk by roughly 13 per cent a decade since satellite records began in 1979.","Orbital changes: eccentricity, the shape of the orbit, about every 100,000 years; axial tilt between roughly 22.1 and 24.5 degrees, about every 41,000 years; precession, the wobble of the axis, roughly every 20,000 to 26,000 years.","Volcanic activity: sulphur dioxide reaching the stratosphere forms sulphate droplets that reflect sunlight; global average temperature fell about 0.5 degrees Celsius in the year after Mount Pinatubo, Philippines, erupted in 1991; the effect fades in two to three years.","Solar output: an 11-year sunspot cycle; the Maunder Minimum of about 1645 to 1715, with very few sunspots, falls inside the coldest part of the Little Ice Age.","The greenhouse effect: short-wave solar radiation passes through the atmosphere; the warmed surface emits long-wave radiation; greenhouse gases (water vapour, carbon dioxide, methane, nitrous oxide) absorb it and re-radiate part of it downwards. Adding gases gives the enhanced greenhouse effect.","Human causes: fossil fuel burning in power stations, transport and industry, plus cement manufacture (carbon dioxide); agriculture — cattle and sheep, flooded rice paddies (methane) and nitrogen fertilisers (nitrous oxide); deforestation, which removes a carbon sink and releases stored carbon when timber is burned or rots.","Mitigation means reducing the causes; adaptation means reducing the harm. Alternative energy, carbon capture and storage, planting trees and international agreements are mitigation; changing agricultural systems, managing water supply and reducing the risk from rising sea levels are adaptation."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — mitigation: the schemes and agreements worth naming","items":["Alternative energy production: wind, solar, hydroelectric, tidal and geothermal generate electricity without burning carbon; nuclear power is low-carbon at the point of generation.","United Kingdom electricity, 2020: renewables supplied about 43 per cent of generation, more than fossil fuels across a full year for the first time.","Hornsea One, off the Yorkshire coast: an offshore wind farm with a capacity of about 1.2 gigawatts, fully operational in 2019.","Carbon capture and storage: carbon dioxide is separated from exhaust gases, compressed and pumped into porous rock deep underground, sealed beneath an impermeable cap rock.","Sleipner, Norwegian North Sea: has injected roughly a million tonnes of carbon dioxide a year into a sandstone layer beneath the seabed since 1996 — the longest-running project of its kind.","Planting trees: a growing tree removes carbon dioxide by photosynthesis and locks the carbon into its wood, creating a carbon sink; the effect is slow and is lost if the trees burn or are felled.","Kyoto Protocol: agreed in 1997, in force from 2005; set legally binding emissions targets, but only for industrialised countries, and the United States never ratified it.","Paris Agreement: agreed at COP21 in 2015 by nearly 200 countries; aims to hold warming well below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels and to pursue efforts to limit it to 1.5 degrees; each country sets its own pledge and the pledges are reviewed rather than enforced.","United Kingdom law: the Climate Change Act of 2008, amended in 2019, commits the country to net zero greenhouse gas emissions by 2050.","The evaluation line to remember: mitigation is the only response that attacks the cause, but it only counts globally, so it depends on agreement, and its benefits arrive decades after the cost is paid."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — adaptation: the schemes and places worth naming","items":["Changing agricultural systems: drought-resistant and salt-tolerant crop varieties, different planting dates, shading and mulching, more efficient irrigation, and moving crops to cooler ground or higher latitudes.","Bangladesh: mostly delta land only a few metres above sea level; homes raised on earth plinths, cyclone shelters built on high ground, and salt-tolerant rice grown where saltwater has reached the fields.","United Kingdom farming: warmer summers lengthen the growing season and have allowed vines and other warm-climate crops to be grown further north, so adaptation is not always defensive.","Managing water supply: transfer schemes moving water from wetter regions to drier ones, reduced leakage, metering, rainwater harvesting, and new reservoirs.","Desalination: Thames Water's plant at Beckton in east London, opened in 2010, treats brackish water from the Thames estuary so London has a supply to call on in drought.","Reducing the risk from rising sea levels: sea walls and tidal barriers, restoring salt marsh and mangrove that absorb wave energy, building on raised ground, and managed retreat where defending the coast is not worth the cost.","The Thames Barrier, Woolwich: ten steel gates across the Thames; became operational in 1982 and was officially opened in 1984; it is closed to stop North Sea storm surges travelling upstream, and closures have become far more frequent than in the 1980s.","The Maldives: the lowest-lying country in the world, with an average land height of about 1.5 metres; the reclaimed island of Hulhumale, beside the capital Male, was built about 2 metres above sea level so that people can be moved onto higher ground.","The evaluation line to remember: adaptation protects people quickly and the benefit falls on whoever pays, but it treats the symptom, costs must be paid again as the sea keeps rising, and the most exposed countries are the least able to afford it."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Evaluate the view that adaptation is a more realistic response to climate change than mitigation. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]'","items":["Decoding the command: 'Evaluate' means judge how well something works with the evidence on both sides, so the answer must weigh advantages against disadvantages and reach a verdict. The mark scheme rewards 'a detailed and balanced appreciation of its advantages and disadvantages'. 'More realistic than' makes it a comparison, so every point about adaptation needs its counterpart about mitigation. Describing the two responses is not evaluating them.","Opening move (about one minute): define both in one sentence each — mitigation reduces the causes by cutting greenhouse gas emissions; adaptation reduces the harm without touching the causes — and signal the line the answer will take, that the view is only half right.","Paragraph 1 — the case for adaptation being realistic: warming already in the system cannot be undone, so harm is coming whatever is agreed; adaptation works at a scale one authority can manage and protects quickly. Examples: the Thames Barrier at Woolwich, ten gates, operational since 1982; the Beckton desalination plant, opened 2010; raised plinths and salt-tolerant rice in Bangladesh. Mini-judgement: the benefit falls on whoever pays, which is why adaptation actually gets built.","Paragraph 2 — the case against adaptation: it treats the symptom. Sea level rose about 0.20 metres between 1901 and 2018 and is still rising by about three to four millimetres a year, so every defence must be raised again and the barrier closed more often; the most exposed places, such as the Maldives with an average land height of about 1.5 metres, are the least able to pay; some losses, such as the Great Barrier Reef bleaching of 2016 and 2017, cannot be adapted to at all. Mini-judgement: adaptation buys time and its bill keeps arriving.","Paragraph 3 — mitigation, and whether it is unrealistic: it is the only response that attacks the driver, and it is being done. Examples: renewables supplied about 43 per cent of United Kingdom electricity in 2020; carbon capture and storage has run at Sleipner in the Norwegian North Sea since 1996 at roughly a million tonnes a year; planting trees creates a carbon sink; the Paris Agreement of 2015 binds nearly 200 countries to a shared target. Its weaknesses are that it only counts globally and pays back decades later. Mini-judgement: unrealistic alone, not unrealistic in principle.","The evaluation thread that runs through every paragraph: judge each response against the same three tests — how fast it works, who pays and who benefits, and whether the benefit lasts. Returning to those three tests in each paragraph is what turns three descriptions into one evaluation.","Conclusion (about a minute and a half): answer the actual view. Say that adaptation is more realistic in the short term and at a local scale, because it is affordable to some and works immediately, but that it is a holding action whose cost rises for as long as emissions continue, so it is realistic only alongside mitigation and not instead of it. Do not introduce a new example here; the conclusion must weigh what has already been argued.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly ten minutes writing plus a minute planning and a final read-through for spelling, punctuation and specialist terms, because three further marks ride on those. Use the terms that earn them: enhanced greenhouse effect, mitigation, adaptation, thermal expansion, carbon sink, managed retreat."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:55:04Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All nine faults are genuinely fixed and nothing else in the kit moved: Hornsea One now reads 'fully operational in 2019', which matches Orsted's own project page and the December 2019 commissioning; 21.5 degrees is gone from all seven places and 22.1 to 24.5 degrees is now correct throughout; the Level 3 SPaG claim now names only terms the paragraph actually uses (glacial, interglacial, orbital cycle, sulphate droplets, long-wave radiation, all five present); the mitigation hint and the Figure 2 hint no longer state the answer; the three open cards carry exactly three acceptable answers each and every back is inside its word limit; question 1's key no longer contradicts itself; and Figure 2 with question 5 now agree at about 300 ppm, which is right for the real EPICA maxima. I re-checked the rest of the kit against the diff and re-verified the unchanged figures - the seven Mauna Loa values, 97 ppm, the 9 and 24 ppm decadal steps, 800,000 years, 1.1 degrees C, 0.20 m, 13 per cent per decade, Pinatubo 1991, 43.1 per cent in 2020, Sleipner 1996, Kyoto, Paris, the Thames Barrier, Beckton 2010, Hulhumale - all hold. Publish once question 4's AO split is put back to AO2."}}},"3.1.2.1":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.2.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Everything else in The living world is built on this room. Before you write a word about rainforests, hot deserts or cold environments the examiner wants to see that you know what an ecosystem actually is: producers, consumers and decomposers joined by food chains and a nutrient cycle, all of it resting on abiotic conditions such as light, temperature, oxygen and nutrients. Two things score here. The first is a named small scale UK ecosystem with real species in it, used to trace one change through several links rather than to list what lives there. The second is accurate knowledge of where the large scale ecosystems lie and what their climate is like, described in bands of latitude and explained by pressure belts. Answers that stay general — \"the plants and animals depend on each other\" — sit at the bottom level however long they are.","idea":[{"h":"A pond as a natural system: producers, consumers, decomposers and the nutrient cycle","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","t":"An ecosystem is the living (biotic) community of an area together with the non-living (abiotic) conditions it depends on, and all the links between them. In a lowland English pond the abiotic parts are sunlight, water temperature, dissolved oxygen, the nitrate and phosphate supply, pH and the mud on the bed. The producers are algae floating in the water, submerged pondweed and the reeds at the margin: they capture sunlight by photosynthesis, so every chain starts with them. Primary consumers such as water fleas, pond snails and tadpoles graze them; secondary consumers such as dragonfly nymphs and three-spined sticklebacks eat the grazers; a grey heron hunts at the top. Because only about a tenth of the energy passes on at each step, chains are short and the food web is wide rather than tall. Decomposers — bacteria and fungi in the mud, helped by detritivores such as freshwater shrimps that shred the leaf litter first — break down everything that dies and release its nitrates and phosphates back into the water, where the producers take them up again. Energy flows one way and is lost as heat; nutrients go round. A top-level answer names its species and its abiotic conditions, uses arrows that mean \"is eaten by\", and shows the two flows — energy through the web, nutrients round the cycle — instead of describing a pond."},{"h":"Balance: what happens when one component changes","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)","t":"A healthy ecosystem sits in a rough balance: predator and prey numbers rise and fall around a level, and the nutrients taken out by growth are returned by decomposition. Because every part is linked, a change to one component travels through the rest. Change an abiotic component and the effect is usually largest: fertiliser washed off a field adds nitrates and phosphates, algae bloom across the surface, the light they block kills the submerged pondweed, bacteria decomposing the dead plants strip the dissolved oxygen out of the water, and fish and invertebrates suffocate — so a change in water chemistry has removed animals three links away. A hot dry summer does something similar, because warm shallow water holds less oxygen and a pond that dries out early destroys that year of frogspawn. Change a biotic component and the web redistributes: remove a grazer and algae spread, remove a predator and its prey increases until the food runs out, and add an invasive species such as the American signal crayfish and native species are eaten or diseased out. Not everything is hit equally — decomposers gain from a bloom, and reeds rooted at the margin photosynthesise in the air whatever happens in the water. A top-level answer follows one change through at least three links, says which parts are barely affected, and then judges how far the whole system is changed and for how long."},{"h":"The large scale ecosystems: bands of latitude, and the climate behind them","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (global ecosystems)","t":"At the global scale ecosystems are called biomes, and they lie in bands that run round the world because they follow climate, and climate follows latitude. Tropical rainforest sits within roughly ten degrees of the Equator — the Amazon Basin, the Congo Basin and the islands of South East Asia — hot all year at about 25 to 27 degrees Celsius with rain in every month, about 1800 to 2000 mm of it. Tropical grassland (savanna) lies just outside it, roughly 5 to 15 degrees north and south, hot but with a marked wet and dry season: East Africa, the Sahel, the Brazilian interior, northern Australia. Hot deserts form two belts centred about 15 to 35 degrees north and south, with under 250 mm of rain a year: the Sahara, Arabian and Thar deserts, the Kalahari, the Namib, the Atacama and central Australia. In the mid-latitudes come temperate grassland in the dry continental interiors (the Prairies, the Steppes) and temperate deciduous forest in wetter maritime areas such as western Europe, including the UK. A belt of coniferous forest, the taiga, runs roughly 50 to 70 degrees north across Canada, Scandinavia and Russia, with tundra beyond it around the Arctic Circle, where permafrost and a growing season of weeks allow only mosses, lichens, sedges and dwarf shrubs. The pattern follows the global circulation: rising air and low pressure at the Equator give the rainforest its downpours, sinking air under high pressure near 30 degrees gives the deserts their clear dry skies, and the seasonal swing of that rain belt gives the savanna its dry season. A top-level answer describes distribution in latitude bands with named regions, never as a list of countries, and then explains it through pressure, air movement and the sun angle."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading the pond food web (Figure 1)","setup":"Figure 1 is the pond food web written for this room. Every arrow means \"is eaten by\". The arrows are: algae → water flea; algae → pond snail; algae → tadpole; pondweed → pond snail; pondweed → mallard; pond snail → mallard; water flea → three-spined stickleback; water flea → dragonfly nymph; tadpole → dragonfly nymph; three-spined stickleback → grey heron; dragonfly nymph → grey heron. Question: using Figure 1, suggest what might happen in the pond if a disease killed all the tadpoles. [4 marks]","steps":["Trace every arrow touching the tadpole before writing: one leads in, from algae, and one leads out, to the dragonfly nymph, so this animal is a single bridge between a producer and a predator.","State the direct effect: the dragonfly nymph has lost one of its two foods, so it must take more water fleas instead.","Develop the knock-on: water fleas are also what the three-spined stickleback eats, so as the nymphs take more of them the stickleback has less food and its numbers can fall.","Follow it to the top and to the producers: the grey heron feeds on sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs, so its food supply changes too, while algae with fewer grazers can spread across the surface.","Close on the web as a whole: the nymph still has water fleas and the heron still has two prey, so the arrows point to numbers shifting around the web rather than a chain breaking, while the algae, now grazed by two species instead of three, are what change most visibly."],"cues":["Which arrows touch the tadpole, and which way do they point?","What does the dragonfly nymph do when one of its two foods disappears?","Who else in the web eats water fleas?","Which organisms near the top and the bottom feel the change next?","Taking the web as a whole, does losing one animal break a chain or only move the numbers about — and which organism changes most?"]},{"title":"Why the sticklebacks disappeared (Figure 2)","setup":"Figure 2 is the pond survey written for this room. Month | Nitrate (mg/l) | Surface covered by algae (%) | Dissolved oxygen (mg/l) | Sticklebacks caught in ten net sweeps. March | 2 | 5 | 10 | 12. April | 6 | 20 | 9 | 11. May | 14 | 55 | 6 | 7. June | 18 | 80 | 3 | 2. July | 12 | 60 | 4 | 3. A note under the table says the farmer spread fertiliser on the field beside the pond in early April. Question: using Figure 2 and your own understanding, explain why the number of sticklebacks caught fell between March and June. [6 marks]","steps":["Quote the two columns that move first, because they carry the cause: nitrate climbs from 2 mg/l to 18 mg/l and the algal cover climbs with it, from 5% to 80%.","Name the process behind those figures: nitrate and phosphate washed off the fertilised field let the algae multiply into a bloom that covers the surface.","Explain the light link: the bloom shades everything below it, so the submerged pondweed cannot photosynthesise and dies.","Explain the oxygen link: bacteria decomposing the dead algae and pondweed use dissolved oxygen, which falls from 10 mg/l to 3 mg/l over the same months.","Close the chain on the fish: sticklebacks take oxygen from the water through their gills, so at that level many suffocate or move out, and the catch drops from 12 to 2 per ten sweeps.","Finish with the point the question is really testing: one abiotic change, the nutrient level, has worked through the producers and the oxygen supply to remove a consumer."],"cues":["Which two columns rise together, and by how much?","What in the note under the table explains that rise?","What does a mat of algae do to the water beneath it?","What uses up oxygen once the plants have died?","How does a fish actually get its oxygen?","Which single component started all of this?"]},{"title":"Following a nutrient round the pond","setup":"In November the oak and willow trees around the pond drop their leaves and many land in the water. Also lying in the mud are dead pondweed, dead water fleas and heron droppings. The pond water is about 5 degrees Celsius in January and about 15 degrees Celsius by May. Question: explain how nutrients are recycled in a pond ecosystem, from the dead leaves of November to new pondweed growth in the spring. [6 marks]","steps":["Open at the store, not the leaf: the material that sinks forms a litter layer on the bed, joining the dead animals and plants already there.","Bring in the shredders: detritivores such as freshwater shrimps and water lice tear the litter into small pieces, giving bacteria and fungi far more surface to attack.","Name the decomposers and what they release: bacteria and fungi break the litter down and free the nitrates and phosphates it holds into the water and the sediment.","Explain the seasonal brake with the temperatures given: decomposition is slow in water at 5 degrees Celsius, so nutrients are released faster as the water warms towards 15 degrees Celsius in spring.","Complete the loop: pondweed and algae absorb those dissolved nutrients and grow, and the snails and water fleas that graze the new growth carry the nutrients back into the food web.","Say what makes it a cycle at all: energy from sunlight passes one way along the chain and is lost as heat, but the same nutrients are used again and again."],"cues":["Where does everything that dies in a pond end up?","What has to happen to a whole leaf before microbes can work on it quickly?","Which organisms release the nutrients, and which nutrients are they?","Why is almost nothing released in January?","Who takes the nutrients up once they are dissolved?","What travels round, and what only travels one way?"]},{"title":"Describing a distribution from the world map (Figure 3)","setup":"Figure 3 is the world map of large scale ecosystems written for this room, with latitude lines marked at the Equator, the Tropics (23.5 degrees north and south) and the Arctic Circle. The desert shading on it covers the Sahara, Arabia and the Thar in the northern hemisphere and the Kalahari, the Namib, the Atacama on the west coast of South America and central Australia in the southern hemisphere, with the deserts of the south-west USA and northern Mexico just north of the Tropic of Cancer. Question: using Figure 3, describe the distribution of hot deserts. [2 marks]","steps":["Lead with the pattern, because that is what a distribution is: the shaded areas form two belts, centred roughly 15 to 35 degrees north and south of the Equator.","Develop it with detail read off the map: the northern belt runs almost continuously from the Sahara across Arabia to the Thar, and reaches across the Tropic of Cancer into the south-west USA.","Add the southern belt in the same style: the Kalahari and Namib in southern Africa, the Atacama on the western coast of South America, and the interior of Australia.","Finish with the exception, because that is what lifts a distribution above a pattern: the Atacama lies along the western side of South America and reaches closer to the Equator than the rest of the southern belt, so the two belts are not perfectly even."],"cues":["What is the shape of the pattern, in latitudes?","Which detail from the map would develop that first sentence?","What is the equivalent statement for the southern hemisphere?","Which shaded area does not sit neatly in the two belts, and what is unusual about where it lies?"]},{"title":"Using a climate table to explain a biome (Figure 4)","setup":"Figure 4 is the climate table written for this room. Tropical rainforest: about 1800 to 2000 mm of rain, in every month; hot all year, about 25 to 27 degrees Celsius; within about 10 degrees of the Equator. Tropical grassland: about 500 to 1500 mm, in one wet season; hot all year, about 25 to 30 degrees Celsius; about 5 to 15 degrees north and south. Hot desert: under 250 mm, and none at all in some years; days over 40 degrees Celsius in summer, cold nights; about 15 to 35 degrees north and south. Tundra: under 250 mm, much of it snow; below freezing for much of the year, with a brief summer a few degrees above; north of about 66 degrees north. Question: using Figure 4 and your own understanding, suggest why no tall trees grow in the tundra. [4 marks]","steps":["Take the temperature column first: below freezing for much of the year leaves a growing season of only a few weeks, far too short for a tree to add enough wood each year.","Use the precipitation column: under 250 mm a year, and much of that arriving as snow, so liquid water is scarce for most of the year even though the ground looks wet in summer.","Add what the table implies and your own knowledge supplies: water frozen into permafrost cannot be drawn up by roots, and the shallow layer that thaws each summer gives no depth to anchor in.","Answer the question that was asked by saying what grows instead: mosses, lichens, sedges and dwarf shrubs, low enough to escape the wind and quick enough to finish growing in the short summer."],"cues":["How long is the growing season, reading the temperature column?","How much usable water does the precipitation column really give?","What is the ground itself doing to the roots?","What does grow there, and what do those plants have in common?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What does an arrow in a food chain or food web mean, and which way does it point?","a":"It means \"is eaten by\", and it points the way the energy travels — from the organism that is eaten to the one that eats it."},{"q":"Algae add oxygen to the water by day, so why does an algal bloom end up killing fish?","a":"The bloom blocks the light, so the submerged plants beneath it die; bacteria decomposing that dead material use dissolved oxygen faster than it is replaced, and the fish suffocate."},{"q":"Between which latitudes do most of the world’s hot deserts lie?","a":"Roughly 15 and 35 degrees, north and south of the Equator."},{"q":"Name the ecosystem that lies between tropical rainforest and hot desert, and the one thing its climate has that the rainforest’s does not.","a":"Tropical grassland (savanna); it has a distinct dry season, whereas the rainforest has rain in every month."}]},"room":{"facts":["An ecosystem is the living (biotic) community of an area together with the non-living (abiotic) conditions it depends on — sunlight, water, temperature, dissolved oxygen, nutrients, pH and soil or sediment — and all the links between them.","A pond food chain to write from memory: algae → water flea → three-spined stickleback → grey heron. Arrows mean \"is eaten by\", and only about 10% of the energy passes to the next level, which is why chains are short.","The nutrient cycle: dead leaves and dead organisms fall to the bed → detritivores shred them → bacteria and fungi decompose them → nitrates and phosphates are released into the water and sediment → producers take them up → grazers pass them along the web.","The eutrophication sequence: fertiliser runoff → nitrates and phosphates in the water → algal bloom → light blocked → submerged plants die → bacteria decompose them → dissolved oxygen falls → fish and invertebrates suffocate.","The latitude bands: tropical rainforest within about 10 degrees of the Equator; tropical grassland about 5 to 15 degrees north and south; hot desert about 15 to 35 degrees north and south; temperate forest and grassland in the mid-latitudes; coniferous forest (taiga) about 50 to 70 degrees north; tundra north of the Arctic Circle at 66.5 degrees north.","Rising air and low pressure at the Equator give the heavy rainfall of the rainforest; sinking air and high pressure near 30 degrees north and south give the clear dry skies of the hot deserts, which receive under 250 mm of rain a year.","The tariffs of Paper 1 Section B — The living world is Question 2 and carries 25 marks. In the specimen paper its parts run 1, 2, 1, 6, 1, 1, 2, 2 and 9 marks; the other tariff used on Paper 1 is the 4-mark question, so expect any of 1, 2, 4, 6 or 9. The 4-mark level questions are marked on Levels 1–2 (3–4 Clear, 1–2 Basic), the 6-mark on Levels 1–3 (5–6 Detailed, 3–4 Clear, 1–2 Basic) and the 9-mark on Levels 1–3 (7–9, 4–6, 1–3).","The timings — about a minute a mark. Paper 1 is 1 hour 30 minutes for 88 marks, so Section B's 25 marks are worth about 25 minutes: a 6-mark question about six, a 9-mark extended question about nine or ten including a minute to plan it. On the specimen paper the 3 SPaG marks sit on question 01.9 in Section A, not on the Living world 9-marker, but AQA indicates the SPaG question afresh on each paper."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, complete the food chain below by naming the missing organism.\nalgae → water flea → three-spined stickleback → ................","a":"One mark for the correct answer: grey heron. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"Only one arrow leaves the three-spined stickleback in Figure 1 and it leads to the grey heron, so the heron completes the chain: algae → water flea → three-spined stickleback → grey heron. The heron is the top consumer of this web, because no arrow leaves it.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A food chain is read in the direction the arrows point.","Find the three-spined stickleback in Figure 1 and look only at the arrows leaving it.","The missing name is the organism at the head of that arrow — copy it exactly as Figure 1 writes it."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, give the number of different organisms that feed on the algae.","a":"One mark for the correct answer: three (3). AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"Three arrows leave the algae in Figure 1 — to the water flea, to the pond snail and to the tadpole — so three organisms feed on it. That is more than feed on the other producer: only two arrows leave the pondweed, to the pond snail and to the mallard.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Every animal that eats the algae is joined to it by one arrow.","Count the arrows that leave the algae, not the ones that arrive anywhere else.","Work along the arrow list in Figure 1 and tally each one whose first named organism is the algae."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, calculate the percentage decrease in the number of sticklebacks caught between March and June.","a":"One mark for an indication of the calculation, eg 12 − 2 = 10 or 10 ÷ 12; one mark for the correct answer 83% (accept 83.3% or 83.33%). AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"March catch = 12, June catch = 2, so the fall is 12 − 2 = 10 sticklebacks. As a percentage of the March figure that is 10 ÷ 12 = 0.8333, which is 83.3%. The catch therefore fell by about 83% between March and June.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A percentage change is always measured against the earlier figure.","Work out the fall in the number caught first, then compare it with the March value.","Read the two stickleback figures out of Figure 2 and subtract, then divide by the first of them and multiply by 100."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)"]},{"q":"State what is meant by the term \"decomposer\".","a":"One mark for a correct definition, eg an organism such as a bacterium or fungus that breaks down dead plant and animal material and releases the nutrients it holds. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"A decomposer is an organism — in a pond, the bacteria and fungi in the mud — that breaks down dead plant and animal material and the waste produced by animals, releasing the nutrients locked in it, such as nitrates and phosphates, back into the water and sediment where producers can take them up again.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what happens to the leaves and bodies that sink to the bottom of a pond.","A definition here needs two halves: what the organism does, and what it puts back.","Write one sentence naming the sort of organism it is and the job it does in the nutrient cycle."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, describe the distribution of hot deserts.","a":"One mark for a basic description of distribution taken from the map, eg most hot deserts lie between about 15 and 35 degrees north and south of the Equator; second mark for a developed point using detail from the map, eg the northern belt is almost continuous from the Sahara across Arabia to the Thar. No credit for simply listing the names of deserts. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The hot deserts on Figure 3 lie in two belts, centred roughly 15 to 35 degrees north and south of the Equator, one either side of the tropical grasslands. The northern belt is almost continuous, running from the Sahara in northern Africa across Arabia to the Thar, and it reaches across the Tropic of Cancer into the south-west USA and northern Mexico. In the southern hemisphere the deserts are separated by ocean: the Kalahari and the Namib in southern Africa, the Atacama on the western coast of South America, and a large block in the interior of Australia.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A distribution is a pattern, not a list of names.","Look at where the shading sits in relation to the latitude lines printed on the map.","Give the pattern in latitudes in your first sentence, then use one detail from the map to develop it."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (global ecosystems)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, explain one way in which the grey heron depends on the algae in the pond.","a":"One mark for stating the link, eg the energy in the heron’s food came originally from the algae; second mark for developing it through the web, eg algae → water flea → three-spined stickleback → grey heron, so fewer algae would mean fewer sticklebacks for the heron. AO2 = 2 marks.","sol":"The heron depends on the algae because the algae are the producer at the base of the chain it feeds on, so all the energy in its food was captured by them first. Figure 1 shows the route: algae are eaten by water fleas, water fleas by three-spined sticklebacks, and the heron eats the sticklebacks. If the algae failed, the numbers at each step beneath the heron would fall and it would have to hunt at another pond.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The heron does not eat algae, so the link has to run through other organisms.","Follow a single route of arrows from the algae up to the heron.","State the link in one sentence, then develop it by naming the organisms the energy passes through on the way."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, suggest what might happen in the pond if all the water fleas were killed by a pollution incident.","a":"Levels 1–2 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4). Level 2 interprets the web to trace consequences through more than one link, eg the sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs lose a food source and compete for the remaining prey, the heron’s food supply falls and the ungrazed algae spread. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Figure 1 shows two arrows leaving the water flea, so both the three-spined stickleback and the dragonfly nymph would lose a food source. The dragonfly nymph could switch to tadpoles, its other prey, so tadpole numbers would be likely to fall, but the stickleback has no other food shown in this web and its numbers would probably drop. That matters higher up: the grey heron feeds on both sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs, so its food supply would become less reliable and it might hunt elsewhere. Lower down, the water flea is one of the three grazers of the algae, so with the fleas gone the algae would be grazed less and could spread across the surface, shading the pondweed beneath. More algae would also mean more dead algae sinking to the mud, so the bacteria and fungi there would have extra material to decompose. A loss at one level would therefore be felt above it, below it and on the pond bed, which is what makes this a system rather than a set of separate species.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Everything that eats water fleas, and everything water fleas eat, will notice their loss.","Deal with the arrows leaving the water flea first, then the arrow arriving at it.","Take one consumer at a time and ask what it eats instead, then say what that switch does to the next organism along."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)"]},{"q":"Explain how nutrients are cycled in a small scale UK ecosystem you have studied.","a":"Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). Level 3 gives a full loop with a named example: litter and dead organisms, detritivores and decomposers, nutrients released into water or soil, uptake by producers, transfer along the food chain, with the role of temperature. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"In the lowland pond I have studied, nutrients move in a closed loop while energy passes through once. Leaves from the oaks and willows around the pond fall in each autumn and sink, joining dead pondweed, dead water fleas and animal waste as a layer of litter on the bed. Detritivores such as freshwater shrimps and water lice shred that litter into small fragments, which gives bacteria and fungi in the mud a much larger surface to work on. Those decomposers break the material down and release the nutrients it holds — mainly nitrates and phosphates — into the water and the sediment. Decomposition is slow in cold winter water, so the nutrients are released faster as the pond warms in spring, which is exactly when algae and pondweed are growing quickly and can absorb them through their leaves and roots. Pond snails, water fleas and tadpoles then graze that new growth, so the nutrients pass into the consumers and, when those animals die, return to the mud. The same atoms are used again and again; the sunlight that entered with photosynthesis leaves as heat and never comes back.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["A cycle has to come back to where it started, so decide what that starting store is.","Work round the loop in order: the dead material, the organisms that break it down, where the nutrients go, and who takes them up.","Name the ecosystem and its species as you go, and finish by contrasting the nutrients going round with the energy passing through."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, suggest how the pond might change if the farmer stopped spreading fertiliser on the field beside it.","a":"Levels 1–3. Level 3 uses the July figures as evidence that recovery has already begun and explains the sequence in reverse: less nitrate, less algae, more light, plants regrow, oxygen rises, invertebrates and fish return; may qualify with nutrients stored in the sediment. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Figure 2 already hints at what would happen. After the June peak the nitrate falls from 18 mg/l to 12 mg/l in July and the algal cover falls from 80% to 60%, while the dissolved oxygen rises from 3 mg/l to 4 mg/l and the catch rises from 2 to 3 sticklebacks. If no more fertiliser were spread, the nitrate supply would keep falling, because what is in the water is taken up by plants or washed out. With fewer nutrients the algal bloom would thin, so more light would reach the water beneath and the submerged pondweed could grow back and photosynthesise, adding oxygen. Less dead algae would mean less decomposition, so bacteria would use less oxygen, and the two effects together would raise the dissolved oxygen towards the 10 mg/l measured in March. Invertebrates such as water fleas and pond snails would recover first because they breed quickly, giving the sticklebacks food again. Recovery might be slow, though: phosphate stored in the pond sediment can keep feeding blooms for years, so the pond might improve over several seasons rather than in one.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Look at what the last row of the table is already doing before you predict anything.","Run the chain that damaged the pond backwards, one link at a time.","Quote figures from Figure 2 as you go, and end by saying what could make recovery slower than expected."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, suggest why there is no belt of coniferous forest in the southern hemisphere to match the one in the north.","a":"Levels 1–2. Level 2 interprets the map to link the missing belt to the absence of land in those southern latitudes and to the moderating effect of the surrounding ocean. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Figure 3 shows the coniferous forest as a continuous belt roughly 50 to 70 degrees north, across Canada, Scandinavia and Russia, where there is a great width of land. At the matching latitudes in the southern hemisphere the map is almost all ocean: between 50 and 70 degrees south there is only the southern tip of South America, a scatter of small islands and the tip of the Antarctic Peninsula, so there is almost nowhere for such a forest to grow. The little land there is lies close to the sea on all sides, and the ocean keeps winters milder and summers cooler than in the middle of a large continent, so the climate is not the one coniferous forest is suited to. The trees of the taiga are suited to long, very cold, dry continental winters, and no such winters occur in a band of open ocean. The belt is therefore missing because the land is missing, not because the latitude is wrong: where there is land at those southern latitudes, such as Tierra del Fuego, there is forest, but it is a narrow strip rather than a belt running round the world.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Compare what lies at 60 degrees north on the map with what lies at 60 degrees south.","An ecosystem needs a surface it can grow on as well as the right climate.","Say what covers those southern latitudes instead, then explain what that does to the climate there."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (global ecosystems)"]},{"q":"Explain why the world’s large scale ecosystems lie in bands that follow lines of latitude.","a":"Levels 1–3. Level 3 links latitude to climate through the global circulation: insolation and the angle of the sun, rising air and low pressure at the Equator, sinking air and high pressure near 30 degrees, the seasonal shift of the rain belt, with named ecosystems attached. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Ecosystems lie in latitude bands because the plants that define them are controlled by climate, and climate is controlled by latitude. At the Equator the sun is high, so its energy is concentrated on a small area and temperatures stay near 27 degrees Celsius all year. That heat makes air rise, forming low pressure, and as the rising air cools it condenses and rains: over 2000 mm a year falls in the Amazon and Congo basins, which is why tropical rainforest grows there. That air spreads out at height and sinks again near 30 degrees north and south under high pressure. Sinking air warms and dries, so clouds rarely form and under 250 mm of rain falls — this is the belt of the Sahara, Arabia, the Kalahari and central Australia. Between the two, the rain belt swings north and south with the overhead sun, giving one wet season and one long dry season, which suits grasses with occasional trees rather than dense forest, so tropical grassland forms. Towards the poles the sun is always low and its energy is spread over a wider area, so temperatures and growing seasons shorten: deciduous forest gives way to coniferous forest and then to tundra. Because these controls change with latitude and not with longitude, the ecosystems appear as bands running east to west.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what changes as you travel from the Equator towards the pole, and what stays the same as you travel east.","The link runs in two steps: latitude sets the climate, and the climate sets the vegetation.","Take the pressure belts in order from the Equator outwards and attach a named ecosystem to each one."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (global ecosystems)"]},{"q":"Using a small scale UK ecosystem you have studied, assess the impact on the whole ecosystem of a change to one component.","a":"Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–3, Clear 4–6, Detailed 7–9). Level 3 needs comprehensive knowledge of a named small scale ecosystem, thorough understanding of the interrelationships between its biotic and abiotic parts, and a substantiated judgement on how far the whole system is affected. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"The ecosystem I have studied is a lowland freshwater pond in an English farming area. Its biotic components are the producers — algae in the water, submerged pondweed and reeds at the margin — grazed by water fleas, pond snails and tadpoles, which are eaten in turn by dragonfly nymphs and three-spined sticklebacks, with a grey heron at the top and bacteria and fungi decomposing everything in the mud. They are held in balance by abiotic components: sunlight, water temperature, dissolved oxygen and the nutrient supply.\n\nThe change I will assess is nutrient enrichment, when nitrate and phosphate fertiliser is washed off the surrounding field after heavy rain. The first component affected is the producers: with plenty of nutrients the algae multiply into a bloom across the surface. That bloom shades the water, so the submerged pondweed cannot photosynthesise and dies. Bacteria decomposing the dead plants and the dying algae use dissolved oxygen, and the concentration falls sharply. Invertebrates and fish take their oxygen from the water, so mayfly nymphs and sticklebacks suffocate or move out, and the heron loses the prey it came for. A change in water chemistry has therefore removed animals three and four links away from it.\n\nNot every part is affected equally, which is what the judgement turns on. Decomposers gain, because the dead algae give them more material than usual. The reeds at the margin are rooted in the sediment with their leaves in the air, so they keep photosynthesising whatever happens to the oxygen in the water, and they benefit from the extra nitrogen. The scale of the impact also depends on conditions: a warm, still summer makes it far worse, because warm water holds less oxygen and nothing mixes it, while a large, deep pond dilutes the same runoff.\n\nOverall the impact on the whole ecosystem is severe, because the change hit an abiotic component that every organism depends on. Changes that work through light, oxygen or nutrients are carried furthest, since they act on the producers first and the effect then travels up every chain at once. A change to one consumer, such as the loss of the tadpoles, spreads far less, because the web offers alternative prey. So a change to one component does affect the whole ecosystem, but how far depends on which component is changed and on how long the change lasts: if the fertiliser stops, the pond can recover over a season or two, although phosphate stored in the sediment may keep it enriched for years.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Choose the change carefully: one that starts with an abiotic component will give you the longest chain to trace.","Set out the components of your named ecosystem first, then follow the change through them link by link.","Build the essay as cause, chain of consequences, the parts barely affected, then a judgement on how far and for how long."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)"]},{"q":"\"The global distribution of large scale ecosystems is the result of climate.\"\nTo what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"Levels 1–3. Level 3 needs accurate knowledge of where the major ecosystems are, thorough understanding of the climatic controls (insolation, pressure belts, seasonal rainfall) and a reasoned appraisal of the degree to which other factors — the distribution of land, altitude, ocean currents, continentality and human activity — also shape the pattern. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Climate is the main control on where large scale ecosystems are found, and at the global scale it explains the bands clearly. Rising air and low pressure at the Equator bring rain in every month, so tropical rainforest grows within about ten degrees of it, in the Amazon Basin, the Congo Basin and South East Asia. Air sinking near 30 degrees north and south under high pressure keeps skies clear, so hot deserts with under 250 mm of rain a year form two belts, from the Sahara across Arabia to the Thar and from the Kalahari to central Australia. Between them the rain belt migrates with the overhead sun, giving the savanna its single wet season. Towards the poles the sun is low, growing seasons shorten and coniferous forest and then tundra take over. To that extent the statement is strongly supported.\n\nIt is not the whole answer, though. The pattern is broken wherever there is no land: there is no coniferous belt in the southern hemisphere because between 50 and 70 degrees south there is almost nothing but ocean. Altitude cuts across latitude, so the high Andes carry cold, treeless environments at the same latitude as rainforest, and relief also creates rain shadows. Ocean currents matter on coasts: cold currents beside the Atacama and the Namib chill the air above them, reduce evaporation and help make those deserts among the driest places on Earth, even though they sit beside the sea. Continentality explains why the temperate grasslands of the Prairies and the Steppes lie in the interiors, far from moist maritime air, while western Europe at the same latitude has deciduous forest. Soils and drainage decide the detail within a band.\n\nHuman activity has also changed what is actually there: much of the temperate deciduous forest of Britain and of the eastern United States was cleared for farming long ago, so the map of natural ecosystems is not a map of present land cover.\n\nOn balance I largely agree. Climate sets the global framework, and almost every other factor listed works by modifying climate — altitude by cooling air, currents by drying it, continentality by removing its moisture. Those factors explain the exceptions and the blurred boundaries rather than the pattern itself. The statement holds to a great extent for natural ecosystems, but it is weaker as a description of the vegetation now covering the land, which people have altered.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["\"To what extent\" asks how far the claim holds, so you need the evidence for it before the evidence against.","Build the case for climate through the pressure belts, then ask what could break a neat latitude band.","Group the other factors so each one is a reason the bands are interrupted, and judge in the conclusion whether they replace climate or only modify it."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (global ecosystems)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, explain how the arrival of an invasive species could upset the balance of a pond ecosystem.","a":"Levels 1–3. Level 3 applies the web in Figure 1 to a named invader, tracing effects through more than one link — predation on the organisms shown, competition with them, and damage to the abiotic conditions — rather than describing the invader. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"A pond stays in balance because the numbers in Figure 1 are held in check by what eats them and what they eat. An invader arrives with neither of those controls. The American signal crayfish, brought to Britain in the 1970s to be farmed and now widespread in rivers and ponds, is a good example. It is an omnivore, so it would feed at several points of this web at once: it eats water plants such as pondweed, it takes invertebrates including pond snails and water fleas, and it eats fish eggs, tadpoles and small fish such as the three-spined stickleback. Removing prey from three trophic levels at the same time leaves the dragonfly nymph and the stickleback short of food, so their numbers fall and the grey heron finds less to hunt. It also changes the abiotic conditions, because signal crayfish burrow into the banks and stir up the sediment, making the water cloudy so that less light reaches the pondweed, which weakens the producers the whole web depends on. Worst of all, it carries crayfish plague, which is lethal to the native white-clawed crayfish, so a species is lost as well as reduced. Because nothing in the pond preys on it in numbers, the invader keeps increasing and the balance does not return on its own.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what is different about a species that arrives without its usual predators.","A strong answer attacks the web from more than one direction: what the invader eats, what it competes with, and what it does to the water itself.","Name a real invader, place its feeding on the organisms shown in Figure 1, then follow the loss upwards to the top consumer."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)"]},{"q":"\"Decomposers matter more to a small scale ecosystem than the animals at the top of the food web.\"\nDiscuss this statement.","a":"Levels 1–3. Level 3 gives a balanced discussion with developed ideas on both sides — the nutrient cycle would stop without decomposers, while top consumers regulate prey numbers — supported by a named small scale ecosystem, and reaches a substantiated conclusion. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"In the lowland pond I have studied, the decomposers are bacteria and fungi in the mud and the animal at the top of the web is the grey heron. Judging which matters more means asking what would fail without each of them.\n\nThe case for the decomposers is that they close the nutrient cycle. Every leaf that blows into the pond, every dead water flea and every strand of dead pondweed holds nitrogen and phosphorus. Only decomposition returns those nutrients to the water as nitrates and phosphates for the algae and pondweed to absorb. Without it, dead material would pile up on the bed, the nutrients would stay locked inside it, and the producers would starve within a few seasons. Since every consumer, including the heron, depends on the energy the producers capture, the whole system would run down from the bottom. Decomposers also work constantly and everywhere, and no ecosystem on Earth is known to function without them.\n\nThe case for the top consumers is about regulation rather than supply. A heron removes sticklebacks, and sticklebacks eat water fleas; if the heron stops visiting, stickleback numbers can rise, water fleas are grazed harder, and with fewer grazers the algae can spread and shade the plants beneath. A top predator also tends to take the sick and the slow, and its presence is a sign that the chain beneath it is intact. Losing it can therefore change the whole structure of the web.\n\nThe difference is in the kind of change each loss causes. Removing the top consumer shortens the chain and shifts the numbers, but energy still enters through the producers and nutrients still circulate, so the ecosystem continues in an altered form — many small ponds are never visited by a heron at all and function perfectly well. Removing the decomposers breaks the cycle itself, and nothing else in the pond can do that job.\n\nI therefore agree with the statement, but with a qualification. Decomposers matter more to whether a small scale ecosystem can keep working, because they alone return the nutrients on which the producers and so everything else depend. Top consumers matter more to what the ecosystem looks like — which species are present and in what numbers. If the test is survival of the system, the decomposers win; if it is balance between the species in it, the animals at the top are at least as important.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["\"Discuss\" wants the case on both sides developed, not a preference announced in the first line.","Test each side by asking what would happen to the pond if that group vanished tomorrow.","Separate two different questions in your conclusion: what keeps the system running, and what keeps the numbers in it balanced."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does an arrow in a food web mean?","a":"\"Is eaten by\" — the energy passes in the direction the arrow points."},{"q":"Which organisms release the nutrients in dead leaves and dead animals back into a pond?","a":"Decomposers: bacteria and fungi."},{"q":"Between which latitudes do most of the world’s hot deserts lie?","a":"About 15 and 35 degrees north and south of the Equator."},{"q":"Fertiliser washes into a pond. What happens to the dissolved oxygen, and why?","a":"It falls: the nutrients cause an algal bloom, the shaded plants beneath die, and the bacteria decomposing them use up the oxygen."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Ecosystem","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","open":false,"back":"The living (biotic) community of an area together with the non-living (abiotic) conditions it depends on — light, temperature, water, oxygen, nutrients, soil — and all the links between them."},{"front":"Abiotic components of a pond","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","open":true,"back":"Sunlight reaching the water; the water temperature; the dissolved oxygen in it. Any one of the three counts — they are non-living conditions, not living parts of the pond."},{"front":"Producer","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","open":false,"back":"An organism that makes its own food by photosynthesis, capturing energy from sunlight. In a UK pond: the algae, the pondweed and the reeds. Every food chain begins with one."},{"front":"Decomposer","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","open":false,"back":"Bacteria and fungi that break down dead plants, dead animals and waste, releasing nitrates and phosphates back into the water and soil for producers to take up again."},{"front":"The arrow in a food chain or food web","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","open":false,"back":"It means \"is eaten by\" and points the way the energy travels — from the organism eaten to the one that eats it. Drawn the other way round it says the opposite."},{"front":"A pond food chain","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","open":true,"back":"Algae → water flea → three-spined stickleback → grey heron; pondweed → pond snail → mallard; algae → tadpole → dragonfly nymph → grey heron."},{"front":"Nutrient cycling in a pond","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","open":false,"back":"Dead leaves and dead organisms sink to the mud; detritivores shred them; bacteria and fungi release nitrates and phosphates into the water; algae and pondweed absorb them; grazers pass them on."},{"front":"Energy transfer between trophic levels","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (small scale UK ecosystem)","open":false,"back":"Only about 10% passes to the next level; the rest is lost as heat, movement and waste. That is why food chains rarely hold more than four or five organisms."},{"front":"Eutrophication","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)","open":false,"back":"Fertiliser runoff adds nitrates and phosphates; algae bloom and block the light; the plants beneath die; bacteria decomposing them use up the dissolved oxygen; fish and invertebrates suffocate."},{"front":"American signal crayfish","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)","open":false,"back":"Brought to Britain in the 1970s to be farmed, now widespread in rivers and ponds. It eats plants, invertebrates and small fish, burrows into banks, and carries crayfish plague, which kills native white-clawed crayfish."},{"front":"One change that can upset a UK pond","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (balance between components)","open":true,"back":"Fertiliser runoff causing eutrophication; a hot dry summer lowering the water level and its dissolved oxygen; an invasive species such as the American signal crayfish."},{"front":"Tropical rainforest — where, and what climate","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (global ecosystems)","open":false,"back":"Within about 10 degrees of the Equator: the Amazon Basin, the Congo Basin, South East Asia. Hot all year at about 25–27 °C, with rain in every month and about 1800–2000 mm a year."},{"front":"Hot desert — where, and what climate","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (global ecosystems)","open":false,"back":"Two belts about 15–35 degrees north and south: Sahara, Arabian and Thar; Kalahari, Namib, Atacama and central Australia. Under 250 mm of rain a year, very hot days and cold nights."},{"front":"Coniferous forest (taiga) and tundra","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (global ecosystems)","open":false,"back":"Taiga forms a belt about 50–70 degrees north across Canada, Scandinavia and Russia. Tundra lies beyond it, around the Arctic Circle: permafrost, no trees, under 250 mm of precipitation."},{"front":"Why hot deserts sit near 30 degrees north and south","code":"3.1.2.1 — Ecosystems exist at a range of scales and involve the interaction between biotic and abiotic components (global ecosystems)","open":false,"back":"Air that rose and rained at the Equator sinks there under high pressure. Sinking air warms and dries, so cloud and rain rarely form, while the rising air at the Equator feeds the rainforest."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Food web of a small freshwater pond in lowland England. Written for this room; every species named lives in British ponds, but the web itself is a simplified one drawn for these questions. Each arrow means \"is eaten by\". The eleven arrows are:\nalgae → water flea\nalgae → pond snail\nalgae → tadpole\npondweed → pond snail\npondweed → mallard\npond snail → mallard\nwater flea → three-spined stickleback\nwater flea → dragonfly nymph\ntadpole → dragonfly nymph\nthree-spined stickleback → grey heron\ndragonfly nymph → grey heron\nPrinted beneath the web: \"Decomposers — bacteria and fungi in the mud — break down every organism shown when it dies.\"","Figure 2 — Survey of the same pond, March to July. Written for this room; the figures are illustrative, not measurements from a real pond, although the pattern they show is the one eutrophication produces. A note under the table reads: \"Fertiliser was spread on the field beside the pond in early April.\"\nMonth | Nitrate in the water (mg/l) | Surface covered by algae (%) | Dissolved oxygen (mg/l) | Sticklebacks caught in ten net sweeps\nMarch | 2 | 5 | 10 | 12\nApril | 6 | 20 | 9 | 11\nMay | 14 | 55 | 6 | 7\nJune | 18 | 80 | 3 | 2\nJuly | 12 | 60 | 4 | 3","Figure 3 — World map of the large scale natural ecosystems. Written for this room; the pattern described is the real one. Latitude lines are printed at the Equator, the Tropic of Cancer and the Tropic of Capricorn (23.5 degrees north and south) and the Arctic Circle (66.5 degrees north). A dark green band of tropical rainforest lies along the Equator through the Amazon Basin, the Congo Basin and the islands of South East Asia. Either side of it a pale band of tropical grassland runs through East Africa and the Sahel, the interior of Brazil and northern Australia. Beyond that, yellow desert shading covers the Sahara, Arabia and the Thar in the north, the Kalahari and Namib, the Atacama on the west coast of South America and the interior of Australia in the south, with the deserts of the south-west USA and northern Mexico just north of the Tropic of Cancer. In the mid-latitudes of the northern hemisphere, brown temperate grassland covers the continental interiors (the Prairies of North America and the Steppes of Eurasia) and mid-green deciduous forest covers western Europe, the eastern USA and eastern China. A continuous dark belt of coniferous forest crosses northern Canada, Scandinavia and Russia between roughly 50 and 70 degrees north, and a grey band of tundra lies north of it along the Arctic coasts of Canada, Alaska, Greenland and Siberia. At these latitudes in the southern hemisphere the map shows almost nothing but ocean.","Figure 4 — Climate of four large scale ecosystems. Written for this room; the values are typical figures for each ecosystem rather than readings from one weather station.\nEcosystem | Annual precipitation | Temperature | Latitude band\nTropical rainforest | about 1800–2000 mm, falling in every month | hot all year, about 25–27 °C | within about 10 degrees of the Equator\nTropical grassland (savanna) | about 500–1500 mm, in one wet season | hot all year, about 25–30 °C | about 5–15 degrees north and south\nHot desert | under 250 mm, and none at all in some years | days over 40 °C in summer, cold nights | about 15–35 degrees north and south\nTundra | under 250 mm, much of it as snow | below freezing for much of the year, brief summer a few degrees above | north of about 66 degrees north"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the small scale UK ecosystem: a lowland freshwater pond","items":["The example: a lowland freshwater pond in an English farming landscape — small, shallow, standing fresh water. Ponds are among the richest freshwater habitats in Britain, and many farmland ponds were filled in or lost during the twentieth century.","Abiotic components: sunlight reaching the water, water temperature, dissolved oxygen, the nitrate and phosphate supply, pH, water depth, and the mud and leaf litter on the bed.","Producers: microscopic algae (phytoplankton) suspended in the water, submerged pondweed, floating duckweed and water lilies, and emergent reeds and bulrushes at the margin.","Primary consumers: water fleas (Daphnia), pond snails, mayfly nymphs and tadpoles, grazing algae and soft plant material.","Secondary consumers: dragonfly nymphs, the great diving beetle and the three-spined stickleback (Gasterosteus aculeatus), all hunting invertebrates.","Top consumers: the grey heron (Ardea cinerea) and the kingfisher take fish and amphibians from the water; a grass snake will take frogs at the margin.","Decomposers and detritivores: bacteria and fungi in the mud do the decomposing; freshwater shrimps and water lice shred the leaf litter first and speed the process up.","A food chain to learn: algae → water flea → three-spined stickleback → grey heron. Only about 10% of the energy passes on at each step, which is why the chain is short.","Nutrient cycle: autumn leaves and dead organisms form litter on the bed → detritivores shred it → bacteria and fungi release nitrates and phosphates → algae and pondweed absorb them → grazers and predators pass them on → the dead return to the mud.","Season by season: common frogs spawn in late winter and early spring; algae grow fastest as light and warmth increase in spring; leaves fall into the water in autumn; decomposition almost stops in cold winter water.","Main threats: fertiliser and slurry runoff causing eutrophication; drought and low water in hot summers, when warm water also holds less oxygen; shading as trees grow up around the pond; and invasive species.","Invasive species: the American signal crayfish (Pacifastacus leniusculus) was brought to Britain in the 1970s to be farmed, escaped into rivers, lakes and ponds, eats plants, invertebrates, fish eggs and small fish, burrows into banks, and carries crayfish plague, which has killed native white-clawed crayfish (Austropotamobius pallipes)."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the processes and terms of this room","items":["Ecosystem: a community of living things interacting with each other and with the non-living conditions of the place they live in. Ecosystems exist at every scale, from a rotting log to a rainforest.","Biotic components: the living parts — plants, animals, bacteria and fungi. Abiotic components: the non-living parts — light, temperature, water, air, oxygen, nutrients, pH, soil or sediment.","Producer: an organism that makes its own food by photosynthesis, using energy from sunlight, carbon dioxide and water. Producers begin every food chain.","Consumer: an animal that eats other organisms. A primary consumer (herbivore) eats producers; a secondary consumer eats primary consumers; a top consumer, or apex predator, is eaten by nothing above it.","Trophic level: the feeding level an organism occupies in a chain. About 10% of the energy passes to the next level, the rest lost as heat, movement and waste.","Food chain: one route energy takes through an ecosystem. Food web: all the chains of a place joined together, showing that most organisms have more than one food and more than one predator.","Decomposer: bacteria and fungi that break dead material down and release its nutrients. Detritivore: an animal, such as a freshwater shrimp or an earthworm, that shreds dead material into smaller pieces first.","Nutrient cycling: the movement of nutrients such as nitrates and phosphates from the soil or water into producers, along the food chain, back to the litter when organisms die, and out again through decomposition. Energy flows through once; nutrients go round.","Balance: a state in which inputs and outputs roughly match and populations vary around a level. Because the components are interdependent, changing one changes the others, and the ecosystem settles into a new state rather than returning to the old one.","Eutrophication: nutrient enrichment of water, usually from fertiliser runoff, causing an algal bloom that blocks light; the plants beneath die and bacteria decomposing them strip the dissolved oxygen, killing fish and invertebrates.","Invasive species: a species introduced from elsewhere that spreads because the predators, competitors and diseases that limited it at home are absent.","Biome: a large scale ecosystem, such as tropical rainforest or tundra, defined by its climate and the vegetation that climate supports."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the large scale global ecosystems: where they are and what their climate is like","items":["Tropical rainforest: within roughly 10 degrees of the Equator — the Amazon Basin, the Congo Basin, the islands of South East Asia. Hot all year at about 25–27 °C with rain in every month, about 1800–2000 mm a year. Dense, layered evergreen forest.","Tropical grassland (savanna): roughly 5–15 degrees north and south — East Africa, the Sahel, the interior of Brazil, northern Australia. Hot all year with one wet and one long dry season, about 500–1500 mm of rain. Grasses with scattered drought-resistant trees.","Hot desert: two belts centred about 15–35 degrees north and south — the Sahara, Arabian and Thar deserts, the Kalahari and Namib, the Atacama, central Australia, the south-west USA and northern Mexico. Under 250 mm of rain a year, very hot summer days and cold nights. Sparse, drought-adapted plants.","Mediterranean: roughly 30–40 degrees, mostly on western sides of continents — around the Mediterranean Sea, California, central Chile, the Cape of South Africa and southern Australia. Hot dry summers and warm wetter winters; tough, small-leaved evergreen shrubs and trees.","Temperate deciduous forest: the mid-latitudes of the northern hemisphere, including western Europe and the UK, the eastern USA and eastern China. Four clear seasons, warm summers and cool winters, rain throughout the year. Broadleaved trees that lose their leaves in autumn.","Temperate grassland: the dry interiors of the mid-latitude continents — the Prairies of North America, the Pampas of South America, the Steppes of Eurasia, the Veld of South Africa. Hot summers, cold winters and moderate rainfall; grasses with few trees.","Coniferous forest (taiga or boreal forest): a belt roughly 50–70 degrees north across Canada, Scandinavia and Russia. Long, very cold winters and short cool summers, with low precipitation, much of it snow. Evergreen conifers with needles and a conical shape.","Tundra: north of the taiga, around and beyond the Arctic Circle (66.5 degrees north) — northern Canada and Alaska, the coasts of Greenland, northern Siberia and Svalbard. Below freezing for much of the year, under 250 mm of precipitation, permafrost beneath a thin active layer. No trees: mosses, lichens, sedges and dwarf shrubs.","Polar: Antarctica and the high Arctic, permanently ice-covered, with almost no precipitation and no soil for plants to root in.","Why the bands lie where they do: the sun is high at the Equator, so its energy is concentrated and temperatures are high all year; towards the poles the same energy is spread over a wider area, so it is colder and growing seasons are shorter.","Why the rainforest and the deserts pair up: air rises at the Equator under low pressure, cools and rains heavily; it sinks again near 30 degrees north and south under high pressure, warming and drying, so cloud rarely forms and hot deserts result.","Why the savanna has a dry season: the belt of rising air and heavy rain moves north and south with the overhead sun through the year, so the land between the rainforest and the desert gets rain for part of the year and none for the rest.","Interruptions to the pattern: there is no coniferous belt in the southern hemisphere because there is almost no land between 50 and 70 degrees south; altitude creates cold environments within tropical latitudes, as in the Andes; cold ocean currents beside the Atacama and the Namib make those coastal deserts drier still; and distance from the sea gives the Prairies and Steppes their dry continental climate."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) on \"assess the impact on the whole ecosystem of a change to one component\" (9 marks)","items":["The change that does most to a pond is one to an abiotic component, because every organism depends on it. When nitrate and phosphate fertiliser washes off the field beside the pond after heavy rain, the algae — never short of light or warmth in an English summer — are released from the nutrient shortage that was limiting them and multiply into a bloom across the surface. That bloom shades the water below it, so the submerged pondweed cannot photosynthesise and dies, and the bacteria decomposing both the dead pondweed and the dying algae strip the dissolved oxygen out of the water. Three-spined sticklebacks, which take their oxygen through their gills, suffocate or move out, and the grey heron loses the prey it came for; a change in water chemistry has therefore removed a bird four links away from it. That is why nutrient enrichment matters more than the loss of a single consumer: if the tadpoles die the web offers the dragonfly nymph alternative prey, but nothing in the pond can replace the oxygen the whole community breathes.","Why this is Level 3: the AO1 strand is met by comprehensive and accurate knowledge — the named species, the named nutrients and the named process; the AO2 strand by a thorough understanding of the interrelationships, since each sentence hands the effect on to the next component rather than asserting that everything is linked; the AO3 strand by the final sentence, which weighs this change against a different change and gives a reason for the judgement.","What to copy: one point, one named example carrying real detail, a chain of at least four links each explained through to its consequence, and a mini-judgement that compares rather than repeats."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–3) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["If you put fertiliser in a pond it changes the ecosystem. All the plants and animals are linked together, so if one thing changes then the other things change as well. The algae grow a lot and that is bad for the pond. Some of the fish can die and then the birds have less food. This shows that ecosystems are very delicate and people should be careful what they put in them.","Why this is only Level 1: it meets \"Demonstrates limited knowledge of locations, places and processes\" — no named ecosystem, no species, no nutrients, no mention of light or oxygen — and it misses \"Shows thorough geographical understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes\", because the link is asserted (\"all linked together\") instead of traced. There is no evaluation at all, so the AO3 strand scores nothing: \"bad for the pond\" is an opinion, not a judgement of how far the ecosystem is affected.","The two moves that would lift it: replace every general noun with a named one (algae, pondweed, stickleback, heron, nitrate, dissolved oxygen), and make each sentence explain how the previous effect causes the next one."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — \"Using a small scale UK ecosystem you have studied, assess the impact on the whole ecosystem of a change to one component.\" (9 marks)","items":["Decode the command word: \"assess\" means weigh the parts and reach an informed overall judgement. You are not asked to describe a pond, nor to list what lives in it; you are asked how far one change reaches, and the marks are split evenly between knowledge of the ecosystem, understanding of the links inside it, and the judgement itself.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly nine or ten minutes, of which one is spent choosing the change and sketching the chain in the margin. In the specimen paper SPaG is carried by the natural hazards 9-marker, question 01.9, and not by this one, but AQA indicates the SPaG-bearing question afresh on each paper, and specialist terms — producer, trophic level, decomposer, eutrophication, dissolved oxygen — are what carry the knowledge marks.","Opening (two or three sentences): name the ecosystem — a lowland freshwater pond in an English farming area — and set out its components in one sweep: producers (algae, pondweed, reeds), consumers (water fleas, snails, tadpoles, dragonfly nymphs, sticklebacks, grey heron), decomposers (bacteria and fungi in the mud), and the abiotic conditions they depend on (light, temperature, dissolved oxygen, nutrients). Name the change you will assess: fertiliser runoff from the field beside the pond.","Paragraph 1 — the chain downwards from the change: nutrients rise → algal bloom → light blocked → submerged pondweed dies → bacteria decomposing it use the dissolved oxygen → fish and invertebrates suffocate → the heron loses its prey. Use the pond example throughout, and make every arrow a sentence of explanation, not a list.","Paragraph 2 — the parts that are barely affected, because an assessment needs both sides: decomposers gain from the extra dead material; the reeds at the margin photosynthesise in the air whatever happens to the water and benefit from the nitrogen; a deep or large pond dilutes the same runoff.","Paragraph 3 — compare it with a change to a biotic component: losing the tadpoles moves numbers around the web but leaves the dragonfly nymph other prey, so the effect fades. This is the paragraph that turns description into assessment.","The evaluation thread to run through all three: which component was changed. Changes to abiotic conditions that the producers depend on — light, oxygen, nutrients, water — travel furthest, because they act at the base of every chain at once; changes to a single consumer are absorbed by the web.","Conclusion (three or four sentences): answer the question directly — a change to one component does affect the whole ecosystem, because the components are interdependent — then qualify it twice: the size of the effect depends on which component is changed, and the length of it depends on whether the change stops. Say that the pond can recover over a season or two if the fertiliser stops, but that phosphate stored in the sediment can keep it enriched for years. No new example in the conclusion."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:56:30Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Factually clean and unusually well anchored: every one of the eleven food-web arrows, all twenty cells of the survey table, the climate table and the biome latitudes recount correctly against the printed resources, and the crayfish, species names and eutrophication mechanism are right — publish it, and only tidy the one worked example whose last step is exam advice rather than an answer."}}},"3.1.2.2":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.2.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"The living world section of Paper 1 always comes back to rainforests, and it comes back in the same three ways: a resource question that asks you to read vegetation or a climate graph and explain it through the climate, a question on the causes and impacts of deforestation that only scores well if you carry a real case study with real figures, and an extended question that asks you to weigh something up — economic gain against environmental damage, or one management strategy against another. This room gives you the processes, one fully stocked case study (the Brazilian Amazon) and the judgement sentences that lift an answer out of the middle band.","idea":[{"h":"Hot, wet, layered and interdependent: what makes a rainforest distinctive","t":"Tropical rainforests grow within roughly ten degrees of the equator, where mean monthly temperatures stay at about 26-27 °C all year, the annual temperature range is only a few degrees, and rain falls in every month to a total of over 2000 mm. That climate builds the ecosystem: constant heat and moisture mean year-round growth, so the vegetation sorts itself into emergents standing above the canopy, a closed canopy at roughly 30-45 m, an under-canopy, a shrub layer and a dark, almost bare forest floor. The same climate destroys dead material fast. Litter rots in weeks, the trees take the nutrients straight back through shallow roots and fungi, and heavy rain leaches whatever is left downwards, so the nutrient store is the living biomass, not the soil: the red, iron-rich latosols are fertile only in a thin surface layer. Plants answer the climate directly — drip tips and waxy leaves shed rain, thin smooth bark needs no cold protection, buttress roots hold up trees over 40 m tall in shallow soil, lianas and epiphytes climb to the light. Animals answer it too, with camouflage, nocturnal habits and life spent in the canopy where the food is. Biodiversity is the result: rainforests cover roughly 6% of the land surface yet hold over half of all land species, which is why clearance costs so much more than the timber is worth. A top-level answer never lists these features; it links each one to a named element of the climate or the soil and says what would happen without it.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"},{"h":"How fast is it going? Reading rates of deforestation","t":"Rates matter because they change, and because the changes have reasons you can argue about. Worldwide, the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization estimated deforestation at about 10 million hectares (100,000 km²) a year over 2015-2020, down from roughly 16 million hectares a year in the 1990s. Brazil measures its own losses: the National Institute for Space Research (INPE) runs the PRODES satellite survey, which reports the area of the Brazilian Legal Amazon clear-cut in each year running from 1 August to 31 July. That series fell from about 27,800 km² in 2004 to about 4,600 km² in 2012, the lowest recorded, rose again to about 13,000 km² in 2021, then fell back to about 9,100 km² in 2023. The falls follow tighter enforcement, satellite detection that finds new clearings within weeks, protected areas and trade agreements such as the soya moratorium, in force since 2006 and now barring soya grown on Amazon land cleared after July 2008; the rises follow weaker enforcement and high commodity prices. Rates also differ within a country: clearance concentrates in the arc of deforestation along the southern and eastern edge of the Brazilian Amazon, in Pará, Mato Grosso and Rondônia, and spreads in a fishbone pattern out from new roads. A top-level answer quotes two or three of these figures, states the direction of change in words, and then uses the turning points as evidence that deforestation responds to policy and price rather than being inevitable.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (changing rates)"},{"h":"The Brazilian Amazon: why the forest is cleared and what follows","t":"The Amazon covers about 5.5 million km² across nine countries, roughly 60% of it in Brazil, and the specification wants one rainforest studied properly. The causes run together. Cattle ranching takes the largest share: most land cleared in the Brazilian Amazon ends up as pasture, because beef sells at home and abroad and cleared land is cheap. Commercial soya farming spreads on the drier southern edge in Mato Grosso. Logging removes hardwoods such as mahogany and opens tracks others follow. Road building came first: the Trans-Amazonian Highway, built in the 1970s and over 4000 km long, and BR-364 through Rondônia carried government-sponsored settlers into the forest. Mineral extraction means the Carajás iron ore mine in Pará, worked since 1985, with a railway of roughly 900 km to the coast at São Luís, and illegal gold mining that poisons rivers with mercury. Energy development means dams: Belo Monte on the Xingu, about 11,000 MW and generating since 2016, flooded roughly 500 km². Settlement and population growth do the rest, with Manaus now a city of around two million people. The impacts split the same way. Economically, clearance brings exports, jobs, tax and infrastructure, though the wealth is unevenly shared and indigenous peoples lose land. Environmentally, bare latosols erode and gully under 2000 mm of rain, rivers silt up, the ash fertility is gone within two or three harvests, habitats and species are lost, and burning releases carbon dioxide while removing the trees that stored carbon and recycled up to half of the basin's own rainfall. A top-level answer pins each cause and impact to one of these named places or figures and then says which it thinks weighs most, and why.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)"},{"h":"Worth more standing: value and sustainable management","t":"Rainforests are valuable to people directly — timber, rubber, Brazil nuts, cocoa and medicines, quinine for malaria coming from the bark of the South American cinchona tree — and valuable indirectly through the water and carbon they regulate, the soil they hold and the species they shelter, along with the homes and culture of indigenous peoples. Sustainable management tries to keep those values while still using the forest. Selective logging fells mature trees individually and leaves the canopy largely intact, and replanting with native species puts the timber back; certification by the Forest Stewardship Council, set up in 1993, lets a buyer pay for timber that can be traced to a managed forest. Conservation and education mean national parks and reserves — Brazil created the Tumucumaque Mountains National Park in Amapá in 2002, covering 38,874 km² — together with ranger patrols and work with local schools and communities. Ecotourism earns money from the forest left standing, employing local guides and boatmen in reserves such as Mamirauá in Amazonas, provided visitor numbers stay small. International agreements cover the trade: CITES restricts trade in threatened species including big-leaf mahogany, the International Tropical Timber Organization promotes sustainable tropical timber, and at COP26 in 2021 more than a hundred countries holding around 90% of the world's forests signed the Glasgow Leaders' Declaration on Forests and Land Use, promising to halt and reverse forest loss by 2030. Debt reduction is the money side: in a debt-for-nature swap a rich creditor cancels part of a poorer country's debt in return for spending on forest protection, as the United States did with Brazil in 2010 for around 20 million US dollars, an agreement that funds the Atlantic Forest, the Caatinga and the Cerrado rather than the Amazon. A top-level answer never describes these strategies in a list; it judges each one by whether it changes the economics on the ground, and says which combination it would back.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforests need to be managed to be sustainable"}],"examples":[{"title":"Explaining plant adaptations from a photograph (6 marks)","setup":"Figure 2 is a photograph of the inside of the Amazon rainforest near Manaus. One very tall tree rises above the rest, its straight, branchless trunk ending in a crown of leaves high above the others. The trunks in the middle of the picture are supported at the base by wide, flared buttress roots taller than the person standing beside them. Thick woody vines (lianas) loop from the ground up into the canopy, and small plants perch on high branches. The leaves in the foreground are dark green, glossy, and end in a narrow pointed tip with water running off it. The forest floor is dark, damp and almost bare apart from fallen leaves and a few seedlings. The climate at this station: mean monthly temperature 26-27 °C all year, over 2000 mm of rain a year with rain in every month. The question: Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, explain how the plants shown are adapted to the climate of the tropical rainforest. [6 marks] (AO1 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks)","steps":["Take the narrow pointed leaf tips first: they shed the rain quickly so the leaf is neither torn by the weight of water nor left wet enough for algae and fungi to grow on it.","Tie the buttress roots to the same climate: year-round warmth and moisture let a tree exceed 40 m, but its feeding roots must stay in the thin fertile surface layer, so the base is flared outwards for stability instead of anchored deep.","Read the tall tree and the lianas as a competition for light: under a closed canopy an emergent wins by growing straight up and branching only at the top, while a liana climbs somebody else's trunk and spends nothing on building its own.","Finish with the bare floor, which proves the point: almost no light gets through and litter rots within weeks in the heat and damp, so only shade-tolerant seedlings survive down there."],"cues":["Which leaf feature in the photograph answers the rainfall, and how does it work?","Why would a tree this tall need help at the base in this soil?","What are the emergent and the lianas competing for?","If growth is so fast here, why is the floor almost empty?"]},{"title":"Suggesting why a cleared plot fails (4 marks)","setup":"Figure 4 is a short extract written for this room. A smallholder family in Rondônia, Brazil, says: 'We came to Rondônia along the new road. The government gave us a plot of forest beside it, so we cut the trees and burned what we could not sell. The ash made the first harvest of maize very good. By the third year the crop was poor and the rain had cut gullies down the slope where the soil is bare. Our neighbour sold his plot to a cattle rancher and moved further along the road to clear a new plot.' You also know the rainforest climate here: over 2000 mm of rain a year, falling in every month, and soils whose nutrients sit in a thin surface layer. The question: Using Figure 4, suggest why the family's plot produced a poor crop by the third year. [4 marks] (AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks)","steps":["Begin with the ash the extract names: burning released in one go the nutrients that were locked in the trees, which is why the first maize crop was so good — but that is a single deposit, drawn down by each harvest.","Point out what has stopped: with no trees there is no leaf litter falling and rotting, so nothing replaces what the maize removes.","Bring in the rain: over 2000 mm a year landing on bare ground washes the soluble nutrients down beyond the roots (leaching) and carves the gullies the family describes, taking the fertile surface layer with them.","End on the consequence the extract itself shows: yields collapse, the plot is sold to a rancher, and a fresh patch of forest is cleared further along the road."],"cues":["Why was the first harvest the best one?","What input has the clearance switched off?","What does heavy rain do to bare, sloping ground?","What does the neighbour's decision tell you happens next?"]},{"title":"Reading and calculating from the deforestation table (1 + 2 + 2 marks)","setup":"Figure 1 gives the area of forest clear-cut in the Brazilian Legal Amazon in selected years, from Brazil's National Institute for Space Research (INPE) PRODES satellite survey, rounded to the nearest 100 km²: 2004, 27,800 km²; 2012, 4,600 km²; 2019, 10,100 km²; 2021, 13,000 km²; 2023, 9,100 km². The questions: (a) State the year shown in which the greatest area was cleared. [1 mark] (b) Calculate the difference between the area cleared in 2012 and the area cleared in 2021, showing your working. [2 marks] (c) Describe the trend shown between 2004 and 2023. [2 marks]","steps":["Rank the five figures before writing anything: the peak is 2004 at 27,800 km² and the trough is 2012 at 4,600 km², so (a) is 2004.","For (b) subtract the smaller from the larger and keep the unit: 13,000 - 4,600 = 8,400, so the increase is 8,400 km².","For (c) give the shape first, then the evidence: a steep fall from 27,800 km² in 2004 to 4,600 km² in 2012, a rise to 13,000 km² in 2021, then a fall again to 9,100 km² in 2023.","Close the description with the overall direction, which is what the second mark rewards: the 2023 figure is about a third of the 2004 figure, so the long-run movement is downwards, but the rise after 2012 shows the fall has not been steady."],"cues":["Which column decides (a), and which value are you hunting for?","Which two figures does (b) need, and in which order?","Is the trend one movement or several?","What single sentence sums up 2004 against 2023?"]},{"title":"Building a 9-mark 'to what extent' answer on causes","setup":"The question: Using a case study of a tropical rainforest, to what extent are the causes of deforestation economic? [9 marks] (AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks). Your case study is the Brazilian Amazon, and you have these facts: most cleared land becomes cattle pasture; commercial soya farming spreads in Mato Grosso; hardwoods such as mahogany are logged; the Trans-Amazonian Highway was built in the 1970s and BR-364 opened Rondônia; the Carajás iron ore mine in Pará has been worked since 1985, with a railway of roughly 900 km to São Luís; the Belo Monte dam on the Xingu is about 11,000 MW; settlers arrived with no land elsewhere; Manaus now holds around two million people; clearance fell to about 4,600 km² in 2012 and rose to about 13,000 km² in 2021.","steps":["Decode the command before planning: 'to what extent' asks how far the causes are economic, so the plan must include a paragraph that argues the other way, not a list of causes.","Lead with the strongest economic cause and explain the profit motive: cattle pasture takes most of the cleared land because beef sells at home and abroad while forest land is cheap to occupy.","Develop a second economic strand as a chain rather than a list: ore at Carajás needed a railway to the coast, the railway needed a cleared corridor, and the access roads then let farmers and loggers clear outwards from it.","Write the counter-paragraph honestly: the Trans-Amazonian Highway was a government strategy to occupy and secure a thinly settled frontier, and a family burning a hectare to grow maize is surviving rather than investing.","Judge in the conclusion instead of summarising: because the rate of clearance itself rises and falls with world prices and with enforcement rather than with need, the causes are largely economic, but government policy decides how far profit is allowed to run."],"cues":["What does the command phrase force into the plan?","Which single cause takes the most land, and why is it profitable?","How does one mine end up clearing more than its own pit?","Which causes are hardest to call economic?","What evidence turns the last paragraph into a judgement?"]},{"title":"Explaining a sustainable management strategy (6 marks)","setup":"The question: Explain how selective logging and replanting can make the use of a tropical rainforest more sustainable. [6 marks] (AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks). What you know: in selective logging only mature trees are felled, individually, so the canopy is left largely intact; replanting uses native species; Forest Stewardship Council certification, running since 1993, guarantees that timber came from a forest managed this way; the climate delivers over 2000 mm of rain a year onto soils whose nutrients lie in a thin surface layer; and illegal logging is hard to police far from towns.","steps":["Define the target first: sustainable use means taking timber at a rate the forest replaces, so the resource and the ecosystem are still there for the next generation.","Chain the physical mechanism: leaving the canopy standing keeps light levels at the floor roughly unchanged, keeps the soil covered against 2000 mm of rain, and keeps litter falling, so nutrient cycling carries on and the gaps close.","Add the economic mechanism, because that is what actually changes behaviour: certification lets a buyer in the UK pay more for traceable timber, so managing the forest earns more than clearing it and replanting becomes worth the cost.","Finish with a limitation and a verdict: policing single trees across an area the size of the Brazilian Amazon is expensive, so the strategy works where it is certified and monitored and barely at all where it is not."],"cues":["What has to be true of the felling rate for this to count as sustainable?","What does leaving the canopy protect, and from what?","Why would a logging company choose the slower method?","Where does the strategy break down?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why is the living biomass, rather than the soil, the main nutrient store in a tropical rainforest?","a":"Heat and constant moisture rot the litter within weeks, the trees take those nutrients straight back through shallow roots, and over 2000 mm of rain a year leaches whatever is left downwards, so only a thin surface layer of the latosol holds nutrients."},{"q":"Figure 1 shows about 4,600 km² cleared in 2012 and about 13,000 km² in 2021. What is the increase, and what does that change suggest about the causes of clearance?","a":"An increase of 8,400 km². Because the rate can more than double in under a decade, clearance is not inevitable: it responds to enforcement, satellite monitoring and the prices paid for beef, soya and timber."},{"q":"Name two features of a rainforest plant that answer the heavy rainfall, and say how each one works.","a":"Drip tips channel water off the leaf so it is not torn and stays dry enough to resist algae and fungi; waxy, glossy leaf surfaces shed water and reflect strong sunlight. Thin, smooth bark also lets water run off freely."},{"q":"What is a debt-for-nature swap?","a":"A creditor country or organisation cancels part of a poorer country's debt in return for that country spending the money saved on protecting its rainforest; the United States made such an agreement with Brazil in 2010 worth around 20 million US dollars, directed at the Atlantic Forest, the Caatinga and the Cerrado rather than the Amazon."}]},"room":{"facts":["Rainforest climate: within roughly 10 degrees of the equator, mean monthly temperatures of about 26-27 °C with an annual range of only a few degrees, and rain in every month totalling over 2000 mm a year.","Rainforests cover roughly 6% of the Earth's land surface but hold over half of all land species. The Amazon covers about 5.5 million km² across nine countries, roughly 60% of it in Brazil, and is home to around one in ten of all known species.","Clear-cut deforestation in the Brazilian Legal Amazon, measured by INPE's PRODES satellite survey: about 27,800 km² in 2004, about 4,600 km² in 2012 (the lowest recorded), about 13,000 km² in 2021, about 9,100 km² in 2023.","Causes in the Brazilian Amazon: cattle ranching takes most cleared land, plus commercial soya in Mato Grosso, hardwood logging, the Trans-Amazonian Highway of the 1970s and BR-364, the Carajás iron ore mine in Pará (worked since 1985), the Belo Monte dam on the Xingu (about 11,000 MW, generating since 2016) and settlement around cities such as Manaus, population about two million.","The nutrient store is the biomass, not the soil: litter rots within weeks, the trees recycle it immediately through shallow roots, and heavy rain leaches the rest, so red latosols are fertile only in a thin surface layer and a burnt plot loses its fertility within two or three harvests.","Sustainable management: selective logging and replanting, Forest Stewardship Council certification (since 1993), national parks such as Tumucumaque in Amapá (38,874 km², created 2002), ecotourism, debt-for-nature swaps (United States and Brazil, 2010, around 20 million US dollars) and the Glasgow Leaders' Declaration on Forests and Land Use signed at COP26 in 2021.","Question forms in the Living world section of Paper 1 (Question 2): 1-mark multiple-choice and single-answer questions, marked AO4 where they read a resource and AO1 where they test recall; 2-mark questions that ask you to state one impact, way or reason and then develop it, one mark for the point and a second for the development; one 6-mark level-of-response question set on a resource; and one 9-mark extended question.","Tariffs and AO splits to know cold: the Living world 6-marker in the specimen (02.4) is AO1 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks, marked on Levels 1 (Basic, 1-2), 2 (Clear, 3-4) and 3 (Detailed, 5-6), and it is capped at Level 1 for description alone or explanation alone; the 9-marker (02.9) is AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks on Levels 1 (Basic, 1-3), 2 (Clear, 4-6) and 3 (Detailed, 7-9).","Timing and SPaG: Paper 1 allows 1 hour 30 minutes for 88 marks, about a minute a mark, so roughly two minutes planning and ten minutes writing on the 9-mark question and six to seven minutes on a 6-marker. The Living world questions carry no SPaG marks: Paper 1 puts its 3 SPaG marks on question 01.9, in the natural hazards section."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, state the year in which the greatest area of forest was cleared.","a":"One mark for the correct year: 2004. No credit for stating more than one year. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"2004. The table gives about 27,800 km² cleared in that year, which is the largest figure in the column; the next highest is about 13,000 km² in 2021.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The years are only labels — the comparison you need is in the other column.","Set the five areas against each other rather than reading down the table in order.","The answer is a single year, written on its own, with no reason attached."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (changing rates)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, calculate the difference between the area of forest cleared in 2012 and the area cleared in 2021. Show your working.","a":"One mark for the correct answer, 8,400 km²; one mark for an indication of the calculation, eg 13,000 - 4,600. Units expected. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Area cleared in 2021 = 13,000 km². Area cleared in 2012 = 4,600 km². Difference = 13,000 - 4,600 = 8,400 km². The area cleared in 2021 was therefore 8,400 km² greater than in 2012 (close to three times as much).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only two of the five rows are involved here.","A difference means one figure taken from the other, so decide which is larger before you subtract.","Write the subtraction out in full and carry the unit into the answer — a bare number loses a mark."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (changing rates)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, describe the rainfall shown on the climate graph.","a":"One mark for a basic description of the pattern using the graph; a second mark for a developed point using data from it. No credit for describing the temperature line. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Rain falls in every month of the year, so there is no genuinely dry season (1). The wettest month is March with about 310 mm and the driest is August with about 50 mm, a difference of about 260 mm, and the annual total is about 2,240 mm, with the wetter months running from November to April (2).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The bars, not the line, carry this answer.","A description needs the overall pattern first and then a figure that proves it.","Two sentences are enough: one for the pattern across the year, one quoting months and millimetres."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Give one environmental impact of deforestation and develop your answer.","a":"Credit one impact only, and it must be environmental rather than economic or social. One mark for stating the impact, a second for developing it. AO1 = 1 mark, AO2 = 1 mark.","sol":"Soil erosion increases once the tree cover is removed (1), because heavy rainfall of over 2000 mm a year now lands directly on bare ground, washing the thin fertile surface layer into the rivers, gullying the slopes and silting up the channels downstream (2). Equally creditworthy: burning the forest releases carbon dioxide (1) and removes the trees that were storing carbon, adding to global climate change (2); or habitats are destroyed (1), so species with small ranges may be lost altogether (2).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Environmental means the soil, the air, the water or the wildlife — not jobs or money.","The second mark comes from carrying your impact one step further: what does it lead to?","Write it as one sentence with a 'because' or a 'which means' in the middle."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)"]},{"q":"Outline one international agreement or scheme that helps tropical rainforests to be used sustainably.","a":"Credit one way only; the answer must show understanding of a worldwide initiative. One mark for naming the scheme, a second for a developed explanation of how it works. AO1 = 1 mark, AO2 = 1 mark.","sol":"The Forest Stewardship Council, set up in 1993, certifies timber as sustainably produced (1); its label guarantees that the wood was cut from a forest where felling is selective and replaced, so buyers in richer countries can choose it and the sustainably managed forest earns more than the cleared one (2). Equally creditworthy: a debt-for-nature swap, in which part of a country's debt is cancelled (1) in return for the government protecting an agreed area of rainforest (2); or CITES, which restricts international trade in threatened species (1) including big-leaf mahogany, so the market for illegally cut hardwood shrinks (2).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what crosses borders: trade, debt or a treaty.","Name the scheme first — a vague 'countries work together' earns nothing.","The second sentence must say what the scheme actually changes for a logger or a buyer."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforests need to be managed to be sustainable"]},{"q":"State what is meant by the term 'biodiversity'.","a":"One mark for a definition referring to the variety or range of living species. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"Biodiversity is the variety of plant and animal species living in an ecosystem. Tropical rainforests have the highest biodiversity of any land ecosystem: they cover roughly 6% of the Earth's land surface yet contain over half of all land species.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The word splits into two halves that each tell you something.","A definition names what is being counted and where it is being counted.","One sentence, no example needed."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, explain how the plants shown are adapted to the climate of the tropical rainforest. [6 marks]","a":"Responses must both identify features in the photograph and explain them through the climate; no credit for features not visible in Figure 2, and an answer that only describes or only explains stays in Level 1. AO1 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The leaves in the foreground of Figure 2 end in a narrow point with water running off it. These drip tips answer rainfall of over 2000 mm a year: they channel water off the blade so that it is not torn by the weight of water and dries quickly enough to stop algae and fungi colonising it. Their glossy, waxy surface does the same job and also reflects the strong equatorial sun. The wide, flared buttress roots at the base of the trunks support trees that grow over 40 m tall; constant heat and moisture allow that growth all year, but the nutrients lie in a thin surface layer, so roots spread sideways rather than deep and the flared base provides the stability that depth cannot. The single very tall tree, with a straight branchless trunk and a crown high above the rest, is an emergent, and it shows how fierce the competition for light is beneath a closed canopy: height is rewarded, side branches lower down are not. The lianas looping up into the canopy and the plants perched on high branches reach that same light without paying to build a trunk. Finally, the dark and almost bare forest floor confirms the pattern, because so little light penetrates and litter rots within weeks in the heat and damp.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Every feature you pick has to be visible in the photograph itself.","Pair each feature with the element of the climate it answers — the rain, the heat or the light.","Three features explained properly beat six named; end each one with what would happen to the plant without it."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, suggest why the family's plot produced a poor crop by the third year. [4 marks]","a":"Answers must interpret the extract rather than recite soil theory; credit reasoning that links burning, the loss of litter and the effect of heavy rain on bare ground. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Burning the felled trees released in a single flush the nutrients that had been stored in the living biomass, which is why the family's first maize harvest was so good. That release is a one-off deposit, and each harvest draws it down. At the same time the clearance switched off the input that normally replaces those nutrients: with no trees there is no leaf litter falling and rotting, so nothing is returned to the soil. The rainforest climate then works against the family. Over 2000 mm of rain a year falling on bare, unprotected ground washes the soluble nutrients downwards out of reach of the maize roots, a process called leaching, and on the slope the family describes it cuts gullies that carry the thin fertile surface layer away altogether. Within three seasons the plot is therefore both stripped of nutrients and physically eroded, so yields fall. The extract shows what follows: the neighbour sells to a cattle rancher and clears a fresh plot further along the road, which is why settlement spreads outwards from new roads.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The extract names three things that matter: the ash, the bare soil and the rain.","Ask what the burning put in, and what the clearance stopped putting in.","Two developed reasons, each tied to a phrase from Figure 4, will carry this tariff."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)"]},{"q":"Explain why the soils of a tropical rainforest are infertile a short distance below the surface. [6 marks]","a":"Credit a logical chain of reasoning linking the climate to rapid decomposition, immediate uptake by vegetation and leaching; a description of soil appearance alone stays in Level 1. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"The soil is poor because the rainforest keeps its nutrients somewhere else. Temperatures of about 26-27 °C all year, with high humidity, allow bacteria, fungi and termites to break down fallen leaves and dead wood within weeks rather than the months or years a cooler climate would take. As soon as those nutrients are released they are taken straight back up by the dense mat of shallow roots, helped by fungi living on the roots, because the competition for them is intense. The nutrient store is therefore the living biomass, not the ground. What little is not captured immediately is exposed to over 2000 mm of rain a year; water moving downwards through the soil dissolves the remaining minerals and carries them beyond the reach of roots, a process called leaching, leaving behind insoluble iron and aluminium compounds that give the latosol its red colour. The result is a thin, dark, fertile layer at the very top, produced by litter that is constantly being replaced, sitting on deeply weathered and nutrient-poor material below. This is why clearance is so damaging: remove the trees and the litter supply stops, the thin layer is quickly used up or washed away, and the land supports crops for only two or three years.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Start from the climate, not the soil — the temperature and the rainfall each do something different.","Follow one nutrient: where does it come from, who takes it, and where does the rest go?","Finish by saying what this means for a farmer who clears the land, which is what the examiner is waiting for."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, suggest reasons for the changes in the rate of deforestation shown between 2004 and 2023. [6 marks]","a":"Answers must analyse the table and offer reasons for both the fall and the later rise; no credit for simply describing the figures in isolation. AO3 = 4 marks, AO2 = 2 marks.","sol":"Figure 1 shows a steep fall from about 27,800 km² in 2004 to about 4,600 km² in 2012, then a rise to about 13,000 km² in 2021 and a fall again to about 9,100 km² in 2023. The fall after 2004 is best explained by enforcement becoming possible: INPE's satellite monitoring began detecting new clearings within weeks, so fines and land seizures could follow, while large new protected areas and indigenous reserves put land beyond legal clearance. Trade rules mattered too, because under the soya moratorium agreed in 2006 the major traders will not buy soya grown on Amazon land cleared after a cut-off date, since moved to July 2008, which removes the buyer at the end of the chain. The rise towards 2021 suggests the opposite: where enforcement staff and fines are reduced, illegal clearance becomes low-risk again, and high world prices for beef and soya raise the reward for taking the risk. The renewed fall by 2023 suggests that enforcement can work quickly when it returns. The pattern as a whole implies that deforestation in the Amazon is driven by profit and restrained by policing, so it is neither inevitable nor permanently solved.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The table has two turning points, and each needs a different kind of reason.","Ask what makes clearing land more or less profitable, and what makes getting caught more or less likely.","Quote two figures from Figure 1 as evidence, then say what the shape of the series implies about the causes."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (changing rates)","3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)"]},{"q":"Discuss the value of tropical rainforests to people and to the environment. [6 marks]","a":"Credit a balanced discussion covering both direct value to people and wider environmental functions, developed rather than listed. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Rainforests are valuable to people in ways that can be counted and in ways that cannot. Directly, they supply hardwood timber, rubber, Brazil nuts and cocoa, and medicines: quinine, long used against malaria, comes from the bark of the South American cinchona tree, and the huge number of untested plant species makes the forest a likely source of more. They are home to indigenous peoples whose food, shelter and culture depend on the forest standing, and they draw tourists whose money can pay local guides and boatmen. The environmental value is larger but easier to overlook. Rainforest trees store enormous quantities of carbon in their trunks, roots and soil, and release it as carbon dioxide when they are burned, so an intact forest slows climate change. Transpiration from the canopy recycles moisture back into the atmosphere, and up to half of the rain falling over the Amazon has been evaporated or transpired by the forest itself, meaning clearance dries the region and the farmland beyond it. The canopy also intercepts rainfall and roots bind the soil, preventing the erosion, gullying and river siltation that follow clearance, while the sheer biodiversity of a forest holding over half of all land species is a store of genetic material that cannot be recreated.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The question names two audiences, so the answer needs two clear halves.","For each one, ask what the forest provides that would have to be replaced or lost if it went.","Give at least one named product and one measurable environmental function rather than general goodness."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforests need to be managed to be sustainable"]},{"q":"Using a case study of a tropical rainforest, to what extent are the causes of deforestation economic? [9 marks]","a":"Requires a weighed judgement about how far economic motives explain clearance, supported by a named rainforest; credit non-economic causes such as government strategy, settlement and population growth. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon, which holds roughly 60% of a forest covering about 5.5 million km², is driven mainly by the prospect of profit, though not entirely. The largest single cause is cattle ranching: most of the land cleared in the Brazilian Amazon ends up as pasture, because beef sells both at home and on export markets while land is cheap once the forest has been felled and burned. Commercial soya farming works the same way on the drier southern edge in Mato Grosso, feeding livestock across the world. Logging is economic twice over, since valuable hardwoods such as mahogany pay for the clearance and the logging tracks then open the interior to everyone who follows. Mineral extraction shows how one investment multiplies clearance: the Carajás iron ore mine in Pará, worked since 1985, required a railway of roughly 900 km to the coast at São Luís, the railway required a cleared corridor, and roads built for access allowed farmers to cut outwards from it in the fishbone pattern visible from space. Energy development follows the same logic, with the Belo Monte dam on the Xingu generating about 11,000 MW since 2016 after flooding roughly 500 km².\n\nHowever, not every cause is straightforwardly commercial. Road building came first and was a government strategy: the Trans-Amazonian Highway, built in the 1970s and over 4000 km long, and BR-364 through Rondônia were intended to occupy and secure a thinly populated frontier and to relieve land pressure elsewhere in Brazil. The settlers who came were often subsistence farmers burning a hectare to feed a family, which is survival rather than investment, and continued population growth in Amazon cities such as Manaus, now home to around two million people, drives clearance for housing, food and services regardless of export prices.\n\nOn balance the economic explanation is the stronger one, because even the state-led causes were justified by the wealth they were expected to release, and because the rate of clearance behaves like a market. It fell to about 4,600 km² in 2012 when enforcement, satellite detection and the soya moratorium raised the cost of clearing illegally, and rose again to about 13,000 km² in 2021 when that pressure eased and commodity prices were high. Deforestation is therefore economic to a great extent, but it is government policy that decides how far the profit motive is allowed to run.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The command phrase asks how far, which means one part of your answer has to push back.","Sort your causes into those with a buyer at the end of the chain and those without one.","Choose the evidence that lets you judge rather than list: what happens to the rate of clearance when profit or policing changes?"],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)","3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (changing rates)"]},{"q":"'The environmental impacts of deforestation matter more than the economic gains it brings.' Using a case study of a tropical rainforest, to what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks]","a":"Requires knowledge of both economic and environmental impacts in a named rainforest and an evaluation of their relative significance; credit consideration of scale and of who gains and who loses. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"In the Brazilian Amazon the economic gains from deforestation are real and immediate, while the environmental costs are larger but slower and shared by people who never received the money. The gains first: cleared land has made Brazil one of the world's leading exporters of beef and soya, and the earnings pay for roads, schools and hospitals in states that had almost none. The Carajás iron ore mine in Pará, worked since 1985, supports thousands of jobs directly and many more along its railway to São Luís, and the Belo Monte dam on the Xingu, generating since 2016, has an installed capacity of about 11,000 MW feeding the national grid, although as a run-of-river scheme its average output across the year is well below half that capacity. Development on this scale lifts incomes in one of the poorest parts of a middle-income country, and it is not reasonable to dismiss it.\n\nSet against this, the environmental impacts are severe and largely irreversible. Once the canopy goes, over 2000 mm of rain a year falls on bare latosol whose nutrients lie only in a thin surface layer, so the soil is leached, gullied and washed into rivers that silt up downstream, and the ash fertility of a burnt plot is exhausted within two or three harvests, which drives yet more clearance. Burning releases carbon dioxide from trees that had been storing carbon, adding to global climate change, and because up to half of the rain falling over the Amazon has been evaporated or transpired by the forest itself, large-scale clearance risks drying the region and the farmland beyond it. Habitat loss threatens species in a forest that holds around one in ten of all known species, and indigenous communities lose the land their way of life depends on.\n\nMy judgement is that the statement is largely correct, but for reasons of scale and permanence rather than sentiment. The economic gains are genuine yet concentrated, short-lived on any individual plot and achievable by other means; the environmental damage is global, cumulative and cannot be bought back. The strongest evidence is that the rate itself can be controlled: clearance fell to about 4,600 km² in 2012 and rose to about 13,000 km² in 2021, which shows the choice is not between development and forest but between forms of development. To that extent the environmental impacts do matter more.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The statement has two sides and you must argue both before you decide.","Compare them on something specific: how big, how long-lasting, and who actually benefits or suffers.","Your conclusion should agree or disagree in a measured way and give the reason that decided it."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)","3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (changing rates)"]},{"q":"Assess the effectiveness of strategies used to manage tropical rainforests sustainably. [9 marks]","a":"Requires an informed judgement on effectiveness, examining several named strategies rather than describing them; credit consideration of enforcement, cost and who benefits. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Sustainable management means using the rainforest at a rate it can replace, and the strategies differ sharply in how well they achieve that. Selective logging and replanting are effective where they are enforced: felling mature trees individually leaves the canopy largely intact, so light at the forest floor barely changes, the soil stays protected from over 2000 mm of rain a year, nutrient cycling continues and gaps close. Certification by the Forest Stewardship Council, running since 1993, strengthens this by changing the economics: a buyer who can trace the timber will pay more for it, so managing the forest becomes worth more than clearing it. The weakness is policing, because checking individual trees across an area the size of the Brazilian Amazon is expensive and illegal logging continues far from any inspector.\n\nConservation through national parks and reserves protects the largest areas at once. Brazil created the Tumucumaque Mountains National Park in Amapá in 2002, covering 38,874 km², and satellite monitoring by INPE allows new clearings inside protected land to be detected within weeks. Yet a park is only as strong as the rangers funded to patrol it, and the rise in clearance to about 13,000 km² in 2021 shows how quickly protection weakens when enforcement is cut. Ecotourism is effective on a small scale, paying local guides and boatmen to keep the forest standing, but it earns far less than ranching and can damage the habitat it sells if visitor numbers grow.\n\nInternational action tackles the demand side. CITES restricts trade in threatened species including big-leaf mahogany, debt-for-nature swaps such as the United States agreement with Brazil in 2010, worth around 20 million US dollars for the Atlantic Forest, the Caatinga and the Cerrado, turn debt repayments into conservation funding, and at COP26 in 2021 more than a hundred countries holding around 90% of the world's forests pledged to halt and reverse forest loss by 2030. These agreements can move large sums, but they depend on governments choosing to honour them.\n\nOverall, no single strategy is sufficient. The most effective are those that change the money and are then policed: certification and trade rules remove the buyer, monitoring and protected areas raise the risk of clearing. Education, ecotourism and international pledges matter chiefly because they make those two things politically possible.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Assessing means examining the parts, so take the strategies one at a time rather than describing all of them first.","Judge each one by the same test: does it change what clearing the forest is worth, and can it be enforced?","Finish with an overall verdict that ranks them, not a summary that repeats them."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforests need to be managed to be sustainable"]}],"exit":[{"q":"About how much rain falls in a year in a tropical rainforest climate?","a":"Over 2000 mm, with rain falling in every month."},{"q":"Which land use takes up most of the land cleared in the Brazilian Amazon?","a":"Cattle pasture, for cattle ranching."},{"q":"Why does a newly cleared and burnt plot in the Amazon lose its fertility within two or three harvests?","a":"The nutrients released as ash are used up and not replaced, because there is no leaf litter falling, and heavy rain leaches and erodes the thin fertile surface layer."},{"q":"What is the name of the strategy in which part of a country's debt is cancelled in return for protecting its rainforest?","a":"A debt-for-nature swap (debt reduction)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The tropical rainforest climate","back":"Within about 10 degrees of the equator. Mean monthly temperature 26-27 °C all year, annual range only a few degrees; rain in every month, over 2000 mm a year; high humidity and convectional downpours.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"The layers of a tropical rainforest","back":"Emergents standing alone above the rest, a closed canopy at roughly 30-45 m, an under-canopy of younger trees, a shrub layer, and a dark, almost bare forest floor.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Drip tips","back":"Narrow pointed leaf tips that channel heavy rain off the leaf, so it is not torn by the weight of water and dries too quickly for algae or fungi to grow on it.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Buttress roots","back":"Wide flared roots at the base of a trunk. They stabilise trees over 40 m tall whose feeding roots must stay shallow, because the nutrients lie in a thin surface layer.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Why rainforest latosols are infertile below the surface","back":"Litter rots within weeks in the heat and damp, trees take the nutrients straight back through shallow roots, and over 2000 mm of rain leaches the rest downwards, leaving red iron-rich subsoil.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforest ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Name one way tropical rainforests are valuable to people","back":"Hardwood timber, rubber, Brazil nuts, cocoa; medicines such as quinine for malaria from cinchona bark; a home and culture for indigenous peoples.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforests need to be managed to be sustainable","open":true},{"front":"PRODES, the Brazilian deforestation survey","back":"INPE's satellite survey of clear-cut area in the Brazilian Legal Amazon: about 27,800 km² in 2004, about 4,600 km² in 2012, about 13,000 km² in 2021, about 9,100 km² in 2023.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (changing rates)","open":false},{"front":"The arc of deforestation","back":"The belt along the southern and eastern edge of the Brazilian Amazon, through Pará, Mato Grosso and Rondônia, where clearance concentrates and spreads in a fishbone pattern out from new roads.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (changing rates)","open":false},{"front":"Cattle ranching in the Amazon","back":"The single largest cause of deforestation: most cleared land becomes pasture, because beef sells at home and abroad and forest land is cheap to occupy once felled and burned.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)","open":false},{"front":"The Trans-Amazonian Highway","back":"A government road built in the 1970s, over 4000 km long, to occupy the frontier. Settlers cleared plots beside it, and BR-364 opened Rondônia the same way.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)","open":false},{"front":"Carajás iron ore mine","back":"A very large open-cast iron ore mine in Pará, worked since 1985. Its railway of roughly 900 km to São Luís, and the access roads, cleared far more forest than the pit itself.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)","open":false},{"front":"Belo Monte dam","back":"A hydroelectric scheme on the Xingu river in Pará, about 11,000 MW and generating since 2016. It flooded roughly 500 km² of forest and displaced river communities.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Deforestation has economic and environmental impacts (case study of a tropical rainforest)","open":false},{"front":"Selective logging and replanting","back":"Felling only mature trees, individually, so the canopy and soil are left largely intact, then replanting with native species. FSC certification, running since 1993, guarantees timber managed this way.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforests need to be managed to be sustainable","open":false},{"front":"Debt-for-nature swap","back":"A creditor cancels part of a poorer country's debt in return for spending on forest protection. The United States and Brazil agreed one in 2010, around 20 million US dollars, for the Atlantic Forest, the Caatinga and the Cerrado.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Tropical rainforests need to be managed to be sustainable","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Data table written for this room from Brazil's National Institute for Space Research (INPE) PRODES satellite survey: the area of the Brazilian Legal Amazon clear-cut in selected years. Each PRODES year runs from 1 August to 31 July, and the areas below are real figures rounded to the nearest 100 km².\nYear | Area of forest cleared (km²)\n2004 | 27,800\n2012 | 4,600\n2019 | 10,100\n2021 | 13,000\n2023 | 9,100","Figure 2 — Photograph described for this room: the inside of the Amazon rainforest near Manaus, Brazil. One very tall tree rises above all the others, its straight, branchless trunk ending in a crown of leaves high above the canopy. The trunks in the middle of the picture are supported at the base by wide, flared buttress roots taller than the person standing beside them. Thick woody vines (lianas) loop from the ground up into the canopy, and small plants perch on branches high up. The leaves in the foreground are dark green and glossy and end in a narrow pointed tip, with water running off the tip. The forest floor is dark, damp and almost bare apart from fallen leaves and a few seedlings.","Figure 3 — Climate graph written for this room for a station in the Amazon rainforest; the plotted values are illustrative of a rainforest station, not a measured record. Horizontal axis: the twelve months, January to December. Left vertical axis: monthly rainfall in millimetres, scaled 0 to 350, plotted as bars. Right vertical axis: mean monthly temperature in °C, scaled 0 to 40, plotted as a line. Rainfall bars, January to December: 280, 290, 310, 280, 180, 110, 80, 50, 80, 130, 200, 250. Temperature line, January to December: 26, 26, 26, 27, 27, 27, 27, 28, 28, 28, 27, 27. Annual rainfall total 2,240 mm, rain in every month; wettest month March at 310 mm, driest month August at 50 mm; annual temperature range 2 °C.","Figure 4 — Text extract written for this room. The family is illustrative; the processes described are real. A smallholder family in Rondônia, Brazil, says: 'We came to Rondônia along the new road. The government gave us a plot of forest beside it, so we cut the trees and burned what we could not sell. The ash made the first harvest of maize very good. By the third year the crop was poor and the rain had cut gullies down the slope where the soil is bare. Our neighbour sold his plot to a cattle rancher and moved further along the road to clear a new plot.'"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon","items":["Location and scale: the Amazon rainforest covers about 5.5 million km² across nine South American countries, with roughly 60% of it in Brazil; the Brazilian Legal Amazon is the area Brazil monitors and manages.","Biodiversity at stake: the Amazon is home to around one in ten of all known species on Earth, and tropical rainforests as a whole hold over half of all land species on roughly 6% of the land surface.","Measuring the rate: Brazil's National Institute for Space Research (INPE) has run the PRODES satellite survey of clear-cut area since the 1980s; each survey year runs from 1 August to 31 July.","Rates: about 27,800 km² cleared in 2004; about 4,600 km² in 2012, the lowest recorded; about 10,100 km² in 2019; about 13,000 km² in 2021; about 9,100 km² in 2023 (INPE PRODES, rounded).","Where: clearance concentrates in the arc of deforestation along the southern and eastern edge of the Brazilian Amazon — the states of Pará, Mato Grosso and Rondônia — spreading in a fishbone pattern from new roads.","Cause, commercial farming: cattle ranching is the single largest cause, with most cleared land in the Brazilian Amazon ending up as pasture; commercial soya farming expands on the drier southern margin, especially in Mato Grosso.","Cause, subsistence farming: settlers clear small plots by slash and burn, grow maize or manioc for two or three years while the ash fertility lasts, then sell up or move on and clear again.","Cause, logging: hardwoods such as mahogany are felled for export furniture and construction, and the tracks cut to reach them open the interior to farmers and ranchers.","Cause, road building: the Trans-Amazonian Highway, begun in the 1970s and over 4000 km long, and BR-364 through Rondônia were built to occupy the frontier and carried government-sponsored settlers into the forest.","Cause, mineral extraction: the Carajás iron ore mine in Pará has been worked since 1985 and is one of the largest in the world; its railway of roughly 900 km to the port of São Luís required a cleared corridor. Illegal gold mining also pollutes rivers with mercury.","Cause, energy development: the Belo Monte hydroelectric dam on the Xingu river in Pará, about 11,000 MW and generating since 2016, flooded roughly 500 km² and forced river communities to move; the older Tucuruí dam flooded forest on the Tocantins.","Cause, settlement and population growth: Manaus, in the heart of the forest, now holds around two million people, and urban growth clears land for housing, food and services.","Impact, economic development: cleared land has helped make Brazil one of the world's leading exporters of beef and soya; mining, dams and farming bring jobs, export earnings, taxes and infrastructure, though the wealth is unevenly shared.","Impact, soil erosion: with the canopy gone, over 2000 mm of rain a year lands on bare latosol, leaching nutrients beyond the reach of roots and gullying slopes, so the thin fertile layer is lost, rivers silt up and yields fail within two or three harvests.","Impact, climate change: burning and clearance release carbon dioxide from trees that had stored carbon, and because up to half of the rainfall over the Amazon has been evaporated or transpired by the forest itself, large-scale clearance risks drying the region.","Impact, people and species: habitats are fragmented and species with small ranges may be lost; indigenous peoples lose land, and in Brazil about 1.7 million people identified as indigenous in the 2022 census.","Management in Brazil: satellite monitoring by INPE, protected areas such as the Tumucumaque Mountains National Park in Amapá (38,874 km², created 2002), the soya moratorium agreed by traders from 2006, FSC-certified timber, ecotourism lodges in reserves such as Mamirauá in Amazonas, and the Amazon Fund set up in 2008 to receive international donations, of which Norway has been much the largest donor."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — rainforest processes, terms and figures","items":["Distribution: tropical rainforests lie within roughly 10 degrees of the equator, in the Amazon and Congo basins, West Africa, South East Asia including Malaysia and Indonesia, and northern Australia.","Climate: mean monthly temperatures of about 26-27 °C with an annual range of only a few degrees; over 2000 mm of rain a year with rain in every month; humidity high and convectional rain common in the afternoon.","Structure: emergents rise above a closed canopy at roughly 30-45 m; below it an under-canopy, a shrub layer and a dark forest floor where only shade-tolerant seedlings grow.","Nutrient cycle: the largest store is the living biomass, then the litter, and least of all the soil; litter rots within weeks, nutrients are taken straight back by shallow roots helped by fungi, and rain leaches what is left.","Soils: latosols are deeply weathered and red from iron and aluminium compounds, fertile only in a thin dark surface layer fed by falling litter.","Plant adaptations: drip tips and waxy leaf surfaces shed heavy rain; thin smooth bark needs no cold protection and lets water run off; buttress roots stabilise tall trees in shallow soil; lianas climb other trunks and epiphytes perch on branches to reach the light.","Animal adaptations: many species live entirely in the canopy where the food is; camouflage, bright warning colours, nocturnal habits and strong climbing or gripping limbs are common answers to dense vegetation and intense competition.","Interdependence: climate drives growth and decay, vegetation returns moisture to the air by transpiration and protects the soil, the soil depends on litter from the vegetation, and animals and people depend on both — so changing one part changes all the others.","Biodiversity: rainforests cover roughly 6% of the Earth's land surface but contain over half of all land species; many are found nowhere else, so habitat loss can mean extinction.","Global deforestation: the UN Food and Agriculture Organization estimated deforestation at about 10 million hectares (100,000 km²) a year over 2015-2020, down from roughly 16 million hectares a year in the 1990s.","Value to people: timber, rubber, Brazil nuts and cocoa; medicines such as quinine for malaria, from the bark of the South American cinchona tree; homes and culture for indigenous peoples; income from ecotourism.","Value to the environment: a carbon store whose loss releases carbon dioxide; a moisture recycler through transpiration; protection against soil erosion and river siltation; and a reservoir of genetic material.","Sustainable management: selective logging and replanting; FSC certification since 1993; conservation through national parks, reserves and ranger patrols with education of local communities; ecotourism on a small scale; international agreements including CITES protection for big-leaf mahogany, the International Tropical Timber Organization, and the Glasgow Leaders' Declaration on Forests and Land Use signed at COP26 in 2021 by more than a hundred countries holding around 90% of the world's forests; and debt reduction through debt-for-nature swaps, such as the United States agreement with Brazil in 2010 worth around 20 million US dollars, which funds the Atlantic Forest, the Caatinga and the Cerrado rather than the Amazon."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7-9) on the 9-mark question: 'The environmental impacts of deforestation matter more than the economic gains it brings.' To what extent do you agree?","items":["The paragraph: The environmental damage in the Brazilian Amazon is harder to undo than the economic gain is to repeat. Once the canopy is removed, over 2000 mm of rain a year falls directly onto latosols whose nutrients lie in a thin surface layer, so the soluble minerals are leached beyond the reach of roots and the surface itself is gullied and carried into rivers that silt up downstream. That is why a burnt plot in Rondônia supports maize for only two or three harvests before the family sells to a rancher and clears again, which turns a single act of clearance into a moving front. Burning also releases carbon dioxide from trees that had been storing carbon, and because up to half of the rain falling over the Amazon has been evaporated or transpired by the forest itself, clearance on this scale threatens the rainfall that the region's own farmland depends on. Against that, the beef and soya exports the same land produces have made Brazil a leading exporter and have paid for roads and hospitals in states that had few. Weighing the two, the gains are real but concentrated and repeatable elsewhere, whereas the soil, the species and the recycled rainfall cannot be bought back — so on this evidence the environmental impacts do matter more.","Why this reaches Level 3 (Detailed, 7-9): it demonstrates detailed knowledge of impacts on both people and the environment in a named rainforest (AO1); it shows thorough understanding of the interrelationships between climate, soil, vegetation and people, carried as a chain rather than a list (AO2); and it evaluates the relative significance of the two sets of impacts and reaches a reasoned position using specific evidence (AO3). The prose is accurate and uses a wide range of specialist terms — latosol, leaching, transpiration, canopy — which is how a Level 3 (Detailed) answer reads.","What to copy from it: one named place per point, one figure per point, a 'so' or 'because' in every sentence that carries a process, and a final sentence that decides rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1-3) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The paragraph: Deforestation is very bad for the environment. Lots of trees are cut down in the Amazon and this destroys animals' habitats so they might die out. The soil gets ruined as well and there are floods. On the other hand it makes money for the country and people get jobs, which is good for them. So it is quite bad for the environment but also quite good for the economy, and both sides are important.","Which descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic) — it demonstrates limited knowledge of the impacts, shows slight understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes, and its evaluation of significance is limited.","Which descriptor it misses: Level 3 (Detailed) asks for detailed knowledge of impacts on people and the environment, thorough understanding of the interrelationships, and a reasoned evaluation of relative significance. This answer names no place beyond 'the Amazon', gives no figure and no process — 'the soil gets ruined' never explains leaching or erosion — and its last sentence sits on the fence instead of judging.","The three fixes that would move it up: replace each general statement with a named example and a figure; explain one mechanism all the way through, for example rainfall on bare latosol causing leaching and gullying; and end by deciding which side weighs more and saying why."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Using a case study of a tropical rainforest, to what extent are the causes of deforestation economic? [9 marks]'","items":["Decode the command: 'to what extent' does not ask whether the causes are economic but how far they are, so the answer must argue both sides and then place itself. The case study must be named and used throughout, and only causes count — impacts and management earn nothing here.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly ten minutes writing after two minutes planning; four paragraphs is the right ambition, not six.","Paragraph 1 — the strongest economic cause: cattle ranching, which takes most of the cleared land in the Brazilian Amazon because beef sells at home and abroad and forest land is cheap once cleared; add commercial soya in Mato Grosso as the same motive on the drier southern edge.","Paragraph 2 — economic causes that multiply themselves: the Carajás iron ore mine in Pará, worked since 1985, needed a railway of roughly 900 km to São Luís, the railway needed a cleared corridor, and its access roads let others clear outwards; hardwood logging pays for clearance and opens tracks the same way; the Belo Monte dam, about 11,000 MW since 2016, flooded roughly 500 km².","Paragraph 3 — the counter-argument: road building as government strategy, with the Trans-Amazonian Highway of the 1970s built to occupy a frontier; subsistence settlers burning a hectare to feed a family rather than to invest; and population growth in cities such as Manaus, around two million people, clearing land for housing and services.","The evaluation thread that runs through all three: for each cause ask whether there is a buyer at the end of the chain, and keep returning to the rate itself — about 4,600 km² cleared in 2012 against about 13,000 km² in 2021 — as evidence that clearance responds to price and policing.","What the conclusion must say: that the causes are economic to a great extent, because even state-led road building was justified by the wealth it was expected to release and because the rate rises and falls with profit and enforcement; but that government policy decides how far the profit motive is allowed to run, which is why the same forest can lose 13,000 km² in one year and a third of that in another."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:47:00Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Factually this is the strongest kit of its kind I have checked — every PRODES figure in Figure 1 matches INPE's consolidated series to the rounding it claims (27,772 / 4,571 / 10,129 / 13,038 / 9,064 km2), Figure 3's arithmetic recounts exactly, and the case-study figures (Carajas 892 km railway from 1985, Belo Monte 11,233 MW from 2016 flooding 516 km2, Tumucumaque 2002, FAO 10 Mha vs 16 Mha, COP26 141 countries and about 90% of forests) all stand up; publish it once the three 9-markers stop claiming SPaG marks that Paper 1 puts on the natural hazards question, and once the 2010 US-Brazil debt swap is said to cover the Atlantic Forest rather than left to imply the Amazon."}}},"3.1.2.3":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.2.3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Hot deserts are one of the two optional ecosystems in The living world, and the paper examines them in a fixed shape: short skills questions on a climate table or a map of desert distribution, a mid-tariff question on adaptation or interdependence, and a nine-mark question on how far a hot desert offers opportunities as well as challenges for development. The mark scheme for that nine-marker refuses credit for management of hot desert environments, so the room drills the case study as development, not conservation, and keeps desertification separate: its six named causes and the strategies that slow it. Everything here is built for a named case study, because an answer without places, figures and processes cannot reach the top level.","idea":[{"h":"What makes a hot desert distinctive, and how life survives there","t":"A hot desert receives less than 250 mm of precipitation a year, and most hot deserts lie between about 15 and 35 degrees north and south of the equator. Air that rose and rained at the equator sinks again in these belts; sinking air warms, so cloud cannot form and rain is rare and unreliable. Cloudless skies also mean the ground heats fiercely by day, with summer daytime temperatures above 40 °C, and loses that heat quickly at night, so the daily temperature range is large. Little rain means sparse plants, sparse plants mean little leaf litter, and so the soils are thin, sandy or stony and low in organic matter; strong evaporation draws dissolved salts to the surface, leaving a salty crust. Plants and animals survive by specialising. Xerophytes cut water loss (waxy skins, spines instead of leaves, stomata that open at night) or store it in fleshy stems; roots either spread wide and shallow to catch a shower, as a cactus does in American deserts, or drive down many metres to groundwater, as the khejri tree does in the Thar. Ephemerals wait as seeds and complete a whole life cycle in the weeks after rain. Animals avoid the heat rather than fight it: the fennec fox of the Sahara is nocturnal, burrows, and radiates heat from huge ears, while the camel stores fat in its hump, lets its body temperature rise so it sweats less, and produces concentrated urine and dry dung. Because so few species can live here, biodiversity is low and every species is specialised, which makes the ecosystem fragile: remove the plants by grazing, extraction or vehicles and they take years to return. A top answer never lists adaptations on their own; it names the physical condition first and shows the adaptation solving it, then says what that dependence means for the ecosystem as a whole.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"},{"h":"The Thar Desert: opportunities and challenges of development","t":"The case study this room uses is the Thar Desert of north-west India (mainly Rajasthan) and south-east Pakistan, roughly 200,000 km² and one of the most densely populated deserts in the world. Development opportunities fall into the four headings the specification names. Mineral extraction: limestone, gypsum and Jaisalmer sandstone are quarried for the building industry. Energy: cloudless skies and flat, cheap, empty land suit solar power, and Bhadla Solar Park in western Rajasthan is one of the largest in the world with a capacity above 2,000 MW, while wind farms near Jaisalmer use the steady wind over treeless ground. Farming: traditional subsistence herding of goats, sheep and camels continues, but the Indira Gandhi Canal, which carries Sutlej and Beas water about 650 km from Punjab into western Rajasthan, has made commercial irrigated wheat, cotton and mustard possible. Tourism: camel safaris over the dunes near Jaisalmer, the desert festival and Jaisalmer Fort bring outside money into a region with few employers. The challenges are the same physical conditions seen from the other side: extreme temperatures (May and June regularly above 40 °C, and about 51 °C at Phalodi in the Thar in May 2016), water supply (under 250 mm of unreliable monsoon rain, huge evaporation, falling groundwater, and waterlogging and salinisation on over-watered canal land), and inaccessibility (settlements far apart, and blown sand drifting across the few roads). A top answer holds the two sides against each other rather than in two separate lists, and notices that every opportunity here is conditional on money and technology defeating the heat and the water shortage.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Development of hot desert environments creates opportunities and challenges"},{"h":"Why the desert fringes are degrading: the six causes","t":"Desertification is the degradation of land in arid and semi-arid areas until it becomes desert-like and can no longer support crops, animals or people. It is examined on the fringes of hot deserts, and the Sahel — the semi-arid belt across Africa south of the Sahara, running from Senegal and Mauritania to Sudan and Eritrea, with roughly 150 to 600 mm of rain in one short wet season — is the standard example. The specification names six causes and they interlock. Climate change: Sahel rainfall fell sharply from the late 1960s into the 1980s, with severe droughts in 1968–73 and 1983–85, and rising temperatures increase evaporation from soil and reservoirs. Population growth: fast-growing populations (Niger's is among the fastest-growing in the world) put more people on the same land. Removal of fuel wood: most households cook with wood or charcoal, so trees near settlements are cut, and when wood runs out dung is burned instead of being spread on fields. Overgrazing: larger herds of cattle and goats strip shrubs and seedlings faster than they regrow. Over-cultivation: fallow periods are shortened or abandoned, so nutrients are never replaced and yields fall. Soil erosion is usually the result of the other five: once the plant cover and its roots have gone, the dry, dusty harmattan wind blows the topsoil away in winter and the first heavy storms wash it off in summer. A top answer builds one of these chains through to the soil rather than listing all six, and says which link it thinks matters most.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)"},{"h":"Slowing desertification: water, soil, trees and appropriate technology","t":"The specification names four kinds of response and the best-evidenced examples come from the Sahel. Water management keeps scarce rain where it falls: earth and stone bunds along the contour, sand dams and small reservoirs, hand-dug wells with rope pumps, and drip irrigation that puts water at the root instead of losing it to evaporation. Soil management rebuilds what erosion took: zaï planting pits, dug in the dry season and filled with manure so that water and nutrients are concentrated around each millet seed, terracing on slopes, mulching, crop rotation and leaving the land fallow again. Tree planting holds the soil with roots, shelters crops from the wind and supplies fuel and fodder, so people stop cutting living trees; farmer managed natural regeneration in Niger, which protects and prunes the stumps already in farmers' fields rather than planting seedlings, is estimated to have restored around five million hectares, and the African Union's Great Green Wall, launched in 2007, aims to restore a belt of land about 8,000 km long from Senegal to Djibouti. Appropriate technology is the thread through all of it: schemes that are cheap, built from local materials, maintainable by the people who use them and matched to local skills — stone lines, zaï pits, fuel-efficient stoves and solar cookers that cut firewood demand — tend to last, while large, imported, expensive schemes often fail when the funding or the spare parts stop. A top answer judges a strategy on evidence (what it costs, who maintains it, what it actually changed) rather than praising it in general terms.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (strategies)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing a rainfall pattern from Figure 1","setup":"Figure 1 is the climate table written for this room for a station in the Thar Desert. It gives, month by month, the mean daily maximum temperature in °C and the rainfall in mm: January 24 and 2, February 27 and 2, March 33 and 2, April 38 and 3, May 42 and 6, June 42 and 20, July 39 and 70, August 37 and 70, September 36 and 20, October 36 and 5, November 31 and 2, December 26 and 2, with a yearly rainfall total of 204 mm. Question: Using Figure 1, describe the pattern of rainfall through the year. [2 marks]","steps":["Rain is concentrated in the middle of the year: July and August stand out, with 70 mm falling in each, far above every other month.","Seven months — January, February, March, April, October, November and December — have 5 mm or less, so most of the year is effectively rainless.","July and August together give 140 mm out of the yearly 204 mm, which is about two thirds of all the rain the station receives.","Named as a pattern: one short, intense wet season in high summer with a long dry season either side of it, which is the monsoon rainfall the Thar depends on."],"cues":["Which two months stand out, and by how much?","How many months are almost completely dry?","What share of the year's rain falls in the wet months?","What single sentence names this pattern?"]},{"title":"Calculating the temperature range from Figure 1","setup":"Using the same Figure 1 climate table (mean daily maximum temperature in °C: January 24, February 27, March 33, April 38, May 42, June 42, July 39, August 37, September 36, October 36, November 31, December 26). Question: Using Figure 1, calculate the range in mean daily maximum temperature. Show your working. [2 marks]","steps":["Pick out the largest value in that column: 42, shared by May and by June.","Pick out the smallest: 24, in January, the coolest month of the year.","Range = highest − lowest = 42 − 24 = 18 °C.","Write the unit in, because a temperature range quoted as a bare number is an incomplete answer, and show the subtraction so the working mark is safe."],"cues":["Which months are the hottest here?","Which month is the coolest?","What does 'range' ask you to do with those two values?","What still has to be added to the number?"]},{"title":"Reading opportunities out of Figure 2, the photograph","setup":"Figure 2 is the description written for this room of a photograph taken near Jaisalmer in the Thar Desert: pale sand and stony ground with bare patches between knee-high thorny shrubs; a single khejri tree on the left with a thin crown and its lower branches cut back; a herd of about thirty goats with one herder in the middle distance; a single-track tarmac road crossing the view, with a tongue of sand drifted over one edge and tyre tracks curving around it; seven wind turbines evenly spaced along the ridge on the horizon; no buildings and no surface water anywhere in the view. Question: Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, suggest how the area shown could provide opportunities for development. [4 marks]","steps":["Take the evidence first: seven turbines already stand on the ridge, so the site earns money from wind blowing unobstructed across open, treeless ground.","Develop that into the wider opportunity: the same cloudless skies and flat, cheap, empty land suit large solar arrays, which is why the Thar holds Bhadla Solar Park, one of the largest in the world.","Second opportunity from the photograph: the goat herd proves that pastoral farming already works on this vegetation, and the tarmac road gives the milk, meat and wool a route to market.","Tie both back to the question: each opportunity rests on something visible in the view — wind, open space and access — which is what a 'Using Figure' question demands before any outside knowledge is added."],"cues":["Which object in the view is already producing something of value?","What other energy source do those same ground conditions favour?","What economic activity do the animals and the road imply?","What must every point be anchored to here?"]},{"title":"Building a chain of causes from Figure 4, the Sahel extract","setup":"Figure 4 is the short text extract written for this room, spoken by a composite Sahel farmer: 'My grandfather kept twenty cattle on this land and rested a field for six years before sowing it again. I keep sixty, because I have eight children and each family needs its own animals. There is no fallow now: every field is sown every year, and the millet is shorter than it was. When I was a boy I cut firewood at the edge of the village. Now I walk four hours to find wood, and my wife burns the dung we used to spread on the fields. The rains came late again, and when they came they fell in two storms that ran straight off the bare ground.' Question: Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, suggest why this land is at risk of desertification. [6 marks]","steps":["Begin with the herd: twenty cattle have become sixty on the same land, so each shrub is bitten more often than it can regrow — classic overgrazing, and goats and cattle also trample seedlings.","Chain it to the soil: with the plant cover thinned there are few roots to bind the ground, so when rain arrives in two storms it runs off instead of soaking in and carries the topsoil away.","Take the fallow: a field once rested for six years is now sown every year, so nutrients are never replenished, which is why the millet is shorter — over-cultivation lowering yields.","Take the fuel: a four-hour walk for wood shows the trees near the village have already gone, and burning dung instead of spreading it strips the soil of its last organic matter.","Finish with the judgement the mark scheme wants: these causes are not separate. More people means more animals, no fallow and less wood, and each of those removes the vegetation that protects the soil the others depend on."],"cues":["What has changed about the number of animals?","What happens to bare soil when rain falls hard?","What has been lost from the farming year?","Why does the walk for firewood matter to the fields?","What connects all four of these changes?"]},{"title":"Planning the nine-mark question on a hot desert case study","setup":"The question is: 'To what extent do hot desert environments provide opportunities as well as challenges for development? Use a case study of a hot desert you have studied. [9 marks]' The case study available is the Thar Desert of north-west India and south-east Pakistan, and the mark scheme gives no credit for writing about managing or conserving hot desert environments.","steps":["Decode the command first: 'to what extent' asks how far one side outweighs the other, so the answer has to weigh opportunities against challenges and commit to a position, not list both.","Opportunity paragraph one, energy: Bhadla Solar Park in western Rajasthan exceeds 2,000 MW because the sky is almost cloudless and the land is flat, empty and cheap, and wind farms near Jaisalmer use the same open ground.","Opportunity paragraph two, farming and tourism: the Indira Gandhi Canal carries Sutlej and Beas water about 650 km into western Rajasthan for irrigated wheat, cotton and mustard, while camel safaris and Jaisalmer Fort draw visitors into a region with few other employers.","Challenge paragraph one, water and heat: under 250 mm of unreliable monsoon rain, fierce evaporation, falling groundwater, and summers above 40 °C that stop outdoor work — with about 51 °C recorded at Phalodi in the Thar in May 2016.","Challenge paragraph two, the cost of the fixes: over-watering along the canal has waterlogged and salinised land, and blown sand across the few roads keeps transport costs high in a region where settlements are far apart.","Conclusion, which carries the judgement: the opportunities are real but conditional, because each one exists only where capital and technology have beaten the heat and the water shortage, so the extent depends less on the desert than on who can afford to develop it."],"cues":["What does the command phrase demand beyond description?","Which opportunity does the desert climate itself create?","Which opportunity needed water brought in from outside?","Which physical conditions limit work and settlement?","What new problems have the solutions themselves caused?","What position should the last paragraph commit to?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why do the world's hot deserts sit in two belts either side of the equator rather than on the equator itself?","a":"Air that rose and rained over the equator sinks again at about 15 to 35 degrees north and south. Sinking air warms and can hold more moisture, so cloud rarely forms and rainfall stays under 250 mm a year."},{"q":"A cactus has wide, shallow roots and a khejri tree has a deep tap root. How can both be adaptations to the same problem?","a":"Both solve water shortage, but from different stores: shallow spreading roots grab a brief shower over a wide area before it evaporates, while a tap root reaches groundwater metres down that no shower can supply."},{"q":"What is the difference between a drought and desertification?","a":"A drought is a temporary shortage of rainfall; the land recovers when the rains return. Desertification is longer-term degradation — vegetation and topsoil are lost, so productivity does not return with the next wet season."},{"q":"Name the six causes of desertification the specification lists.","a":"Climate change, population growth, removal of fuel wood, overgrazing, over-cultivation and soil erosion."}]},"room":{"facts":["A desert receives less than 250 mm of precipitation a year, and most hot deserts lie between about 15° and 35° north and south of the equator, where the sinking air of the subtropical high pressure belt warms and suppresses cloud.","Hot desert soils are thin, sandy or stony and low in organic matter because plants are sparse; high evaporation draws dissolved salts upwards, leaving a salty surface crust, and bare soil is easily blown or washed away.","Thar Desert: about 200,000 km² in north-west India (mainly Rajasthan) and south-east Pakistan; under 250 mm of rain a year, nearly all in the summer monsoon; May and June daytime temperatures above 40 °C, with about 51 °C recorded at Phalodi in May 2016.","Thar opportunities: solar power at Bhadla (over 2,000 MW) and wind farms near Jaisalmer; limestone, gypsum and sandstone quarrying; irrigated wheat, cotton and mustard along the Indira Gandhi Canal (Sutlej and Beas water carried about 650 km from Punjab); tourism at Jaisalmer Fort and the dunes.","Thar challenges: extreme temperatures, water supply (low unreliable rainfall, high evaporation, falling groundwater, waterlogging and salinisation on over-watered canal land) and inaccessibility (settlements far apart, sand drifting over roads).","The Sahel is the semi-arid belt across Africa south of the Sahara, from Senegal and Mauritania to Sudan and Eritrea, with roughly 150 to 600 mm of rain in one short wet season; severe droughts struck in 1968–73 and 1983–85.","The six causes of desertification: climate change, population growth, removal of fuel wood, overgrazing, over-cultivation and soil erosion. The four strategy headings: water management, soil management, tree planting and appropriate technology."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, describe the pattern of rainfall through the year at this station in the Thar Desert. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic description of the pattern taken from the table (eg rainfall is concentrated in the middle of the year); second mark for a developed point using figures from the table (eg July and August each receive 70 mm while seven months receive 5 mm or less). No credit for simply listing month names. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Rainfall is concentrated into one short wet season in the middle of the year. July and August are the wettest months, with 70 mm each, and June and September have 20 mm each. Either side of that the station is effectively rainless: the seven months from October to April all receive 5 mm or less. The 140 mm falling in July and August alone is about two thirds of the annual total of 204 mm.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The table has one column of rainfall figures; look at how uneven they are before writing anything.","A description of the pattern over a year needs the busy part of the year and the empty part of the year, not a month-by-month commentary.","Two sentences: one naming the pattern, one supporting it with figures lifted straight from the table."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, calculate the range in mean daily maximum temperature. Show your working. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer, 18 °C (only possible answer); one mark for an indication of the calculation, eg highest 42 minus lowest 24. Units required for full credit. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Highest mean daily maximum = 42 °C (May and June). Lowest mean daily maximum = 24 °C (January). Range = 42 − 24 = 18 °C.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only one column of the table matters here, and only two rows of it.","Range is the gap between the extremes of a set of values, so find the extremes first.","Write the subtraction out, then copy the unit from the column heading onto your answer."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"What is meant by the term 'desertification'? [1 mark]","a":"One mark for the idea of land in arid or semi-arid areas being degraded so that it becomes desert-like and less productive. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"Desertification is the degradation of land in arid and semi-arid areas until it becomes desert-like, losing its vegetation and topsoil so that it can no longer support crops, animals or people as it once did.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about land at the edge of a desert over decades, not about one dry summer.","The word describes a change in the quality of land in a particular type of climate.","One sentence: what happens to the land, where it happens, and what it can no longer do."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which a desert plant is adapted to survive the shortage of water. [2 marks]","a":"Credit one adaptation only. One mark for stating the adaptation, second mark for developing it into its effect on water loss or water supply, eg spines instead of leaves (1) reduce the surface area from which transpiration can occur (2). AO1 = 1 mark, AO2 = 1 mark.","sol":"A cactus has spines instead of broad leaves (1). This gives it a very small surface area, so far less water is lost by transpiration in the daytime heat, and the water stored in its thick fleshy stem lasts until the next rainfall (2).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A desert plant has two problems with water: losing too much of it, and finding any in the first place.","Choose one feature you can picture, then ask what that feature physically does to the water.","Two clauses joined by 'so' or 'which means': the feature, then what it does to water loss or water supply."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, suggest one challenge of developing the area shown in the photograph. [2 marks]","a":"Answers must be based on an interpretation of the photograph. One mark for suggesting a challenge visible in the view (eg sand drifting over the road), second mark for developing it into a consequence for development (eg vehicles are forced onto soft ground, so transport is slow and clearing the road costs money). AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"A tongue of sand has drifted across one edge of the tarmac road and tyre tracks curve around it onto the desert surface (1). Blown sand like this makes the only route into the area unreliable, so lorries are forced onto soft ground where they can become stuck, and the road has to be cleared repeatedly, which raises the cost of moving people, quarried stone or livestock out of the area (2).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Everything you need is in the description of the view; one object in it is being interfered with by the desert itself.","A challenge only counts once you say what it stops someone doing.","Name what you can see, then finish the sentence with the consequence for anyone trying to work or invest there."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Development of hot desert environments creates opportunities and challenges"]},{"q":"Give two causes of desertification on the fringes of hot deserts. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each correct cause stated, to a maximum of two, from: climate change, population growth, removal of fuel wood, overgrazing, over-cultivation, soil erosion. No development required. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"Overgrazing, because herds strip the vegetation faster than it can regrow (1). Removal of fuel wood, because trees near settlements are cut down for cooking fuel (1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification names six of them, and they divide into what the climate does and what people do.","Two separate causes are wanted, so do not give one cause twice in different words.","A short phrase each is enough; this question does not reward explanation."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, suggest how the area shown could provide opportunities for development. [4 marks]","a":"Levels 1–2. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear understanding of desert development opportunities, with sound interpretation of features in the photograph. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): limited understanding with basic interpretation. Credit energy from the turbines and the cloudless open ground, pastoral farming from the herd, access from the road, tourism from the empty, unbuilt sand landscape. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"The seven wind turbines on the ridge show that the area is already producing energy, and they work here because the wind blows across open, treeless ground with nothing to slow it. The same conditions — flat, empty, low-value land under a sky that is cloudless for most of the year — are exactly what large solar arrays need, which is why the Thar holds Bhadla Solar Park, one of the largest in the world. A second opportunity is farming. The herd of about thirty goats shows that pastoral farming already works on this sparse vegetation, and animals that need no irrigation are well suited to land that cannot support arable crops. The single-track road crossing the view matters for both: it gives milk, meat and wool a route to market and allows heavy equipment to reach the turbines for maintenance. Finally, the emptiness itself is an asset: pale sand running to an open horizon with no buildings anywhere in the view is the kind of landscape the desert tourism based at Jaisalmer sells, and that visitor spending brings outside money into an area with almost no other employers.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Two different kinds of opportunity can be seen in that view, one on the horizon and one in the middle distance.","For each one, explain why this particular environment suits it better than a wetter, greener place would.","Two developed points, each opening from something in the photograph and then widening into what you know about the Thar."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Development of hot desert environments creates opportunities and challenges"]},{"q":"Explain how plants and animals are adapted to the physical conditions of hot deserts. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): detailed explanation linking named adaptations to specific physical conditions (heat, drought, unreliable rainfall, sandy ground). Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): accurate knowledge of adaptations with clear understanding of the interrelationship. Emphasis is on explanation, so a list of features without the condition each solves is capped. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Hot desert species are adapted to three conditions: intense daytime heat, rainfall under 250 mm a year, and the unreliability of that rainfall. Plants tackle water loss directly. A cactus has spines rather than leaves, which cuts the surface area available for transpiration, and stores water in a thick, waxy, fleshy stem so it can survive months without rain; many desert plants also open their stomata only at night, when the air is cooler and evaporation is slower. Roots answer the second problem in two opposite ways: shallow, wide-spreading roots catch a brief shower over a large area before it evaporates, while the khejri tree of the Thar drives a tap root many metres down to groundwater that no shower can reach. Ephemeral plants avoid the problem altogether, lying dormant as seeds and completing flowering and seeding within weeks of rain. Animals mostly avoid the heat instead of enduring it. The fennec fox of the Sahara is nocturnal and shelters in a burrow by day, losing heat through very large ears. The camel stores fat in its hump rather than water, lets its body temperature rise several degrees before it begins to sweat, produces concentrated urine and dry dung to save water, and has wide feet that spread its weight on soft sand and long eyelashes and closable nostrils that keep blown sand out.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Sort the desert's difficulties before you sort the species: what exactly is hard about living there?","Each paragraph should start from one of those difficulties and show a named plant or animal solving it.","Aim for three developed chains rather than a long list, and use one named desert species in each."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Explain how climate, soils, plants, animals and people are interdependent in a hot desert ecosystem. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): thorough understanding of the interrelationships, with a logical chain of reasoning showing how a change in one component affects the others. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): accurate knowledge of the components with clear understanding of some links. Credit the fragility of the system as the consequence. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"In a hot desert every part of the system depends on the scarcity of water. The climate comes first: under 250 mm of rain a year and fierce evaporation, plant cover is sparse and patchy. Because plants are sparse, very little leaf litter reaches the ground, so the soil stays thin, pale and low in organic matter, and because evaporation pulls water upwards through it, dissolved salts are left as a crust at the surface. Thin, salty soils then support only the drought-tolerant plants that produced them, so the loop is self-reinforcing. Animals depend on those plants entirely: herbivores such as goats and camels rely on the scattered shrubs and acacia or khejri leaves, and predators such as the fennec fox rely on the small animals that feed there. What roots the plants keep in the ground is what holds the soil in place when the harmattan-type winds blow or a rare storm falls. People are part of the same web, grazing herds on the vegetation and cutting wood for fuel. That is why the ecosystem is so fragile: remove the plants by overgrazing, wood-cutting or vehicle damage and the soil is exposed, blows away and cannot support regrowth, so the loss of one component pulls the others down with it and recovery takes many years.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Interdependence is a chain, so decide which component you are going to start the chain with.","Work in one direction — climate to soils to plants to animals to people — and make each link a 'because' or a 'so'.","End by saying what happens to the whole system when one link is broken; that is what turns description into understanding."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, suggest how the grazing shown in the photograph could damage this desert ecosystem. [4 marks]","a":"Levels 1–2. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear understanding of how grazing pressure removes vegetation and exposes soil, with sound interpretation of the evidence in the photograph (the herd, the bare patches, the cut-back tree). Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): limited understanding with basic interpretation. No credit for effects on people unless linked to the ecosystem. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"The photograph already shows the pressure: bare patches of sand and stone lie between the thorny shrubs, and the lower branches of the khejri tree have been cut back, which suggests it is being lopped for fodder as well as browsed. A herd of about thirty goats on vegetation this sparse will eat shrubs and tree seedlings faster than they can regrow in a place with less than 250 mm of rain a year, and goats graze right down to ground level, leaving little to regrow. As the plants go, the bare patches join up, and the roots that held the loose sandy soil together are lost, so wind and the occasional heavy storm strip the topsoil. The khejri tree in the view shows the same pressure acting on the slowest-growing part of the system: a tree whose lower branches are lopped every year for fodder casts less shade and sets fewer seeds, so fewer seedlings replace it and the scattered trees that shelter the soil gradually disappear. Because desert plants grow slowly and biodiversity is low, the specialised species that depend on that vegetation, and the soil itself, may take many years to recover.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Look again at what the ground between the shrubs looks like, and at the state of the one tree.","Follow the animals' effect past the plants and into the soil underneath them.","Two developed points anchored in the view, finishing with why recovery here is slower than in a wetter ecosystem."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, suggest why population growth increases the risk of desertification on the fringes of hot deserts. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): thorough application of understanding to the extract, with a logical chain from more people to larger herds, shorter fallow and fuel-wood cutting, and on to exposed soil. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear links made with some use of the extract. Max Level 1 for generic causes not drawn from the resource. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"The extract shows population growth working through three separate routes onto the same soil. First, herds grow: the speaker keeps sixty cattle where his grandfather kept twenty, because eight children each need animals of their own, so every shrub on the same area of land is grazed three times as hard and cannot regrow — overgrazing. Second, the fallow disappears: a field that was once rested for six years is now sown every year, so nutrients are never replaced, which is exactly why he says the millet is shorter than it used to be. Third, wood runs out: as a boy he cut firewood at the edge of the village and now walks four hours, which shows the trees nearest the settlement have already gone, and because he has no wood his wife burns the dung that used to be spread on the fields, stripping the soil of its last organic matter. All three routes end in the same place — bare ground with few roots — which is why the rains 'ran straight off' rather than soaking in, taking topsoil with them. Population growth is therefore not a separate cause so much as the multiplier that drives overgrazing, over-cultivation and fuel-wood removal at once.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The speaker gives you three different comparisons between his grandfather's time and his own; find them all before writing.","For each comparison, trace what it does to the plant cover and then to the soil.","Quote or paraphrase a detail from the extract in each paragraph, and finish by linking the three routes together."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain how soil and water management can reduce the risk of desertification. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): full analysis of the resource, using the yield and soil-loss figures as evidence, with detailed explanation of how stone lines and zaï pits change infiltration, runoff and nutrient supply. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear explanation with some use of the data. No credit for simply restating the table. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Figure 3 shows what soil and water management does on the ground: the managed plot, Field B, yielded 780 kg of millet per hectare against 320 kg on the bare, flat-sown Field A, and lost only 2 mm of soil in the wet season against 9 mm. The two techniques explain the difference. Stone lines laid along the contour act as low barriers: when a storm falls on ground that would otherwise shed it, the water is slowed and ponds behind the stones for long enough to infiltrate, while the sediment it was carrying is trapped rather than washed off the plot. That is why soil loss falls to just over a fifth of the unmanaged figure. Zaï pits work on the other problem, fertility: pits dug in the dry season catch runoff and windblown material, and the manure placed in each one concentrates both water and nutrients around the seed, so a young millet plant establishes a root system even in a poor year. More vegetation then protects the soil surface itself, so the improvement compounds. Both are appropriate technology — hand tools, local stone and local manure — so farmers can maintain them without outside funding, which is why yields more than double rather than rising for one season and then collapsing.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the two rows of the table before you explain anything: what exactly is different, and by how much?","Each technique solves a different problem — one is about where the water goes, the other about what the seed has to grow in.","Use a figure from the table as evidence inside each explanation rather than listing the numbers at the start."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (strategies)"]},{"q":"To what extent do hot desert environments provide opportunities as well as challenges for development? Use a case study of a hot desert you have studied. [9 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3, AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): comprehensive knowledge of locations, places and processes in a named hot desert; thorough understanding of the interrelationships between environment and development; thorough evaluation of the extent. Credit mineral extraction, energy, farming and tourism against extreme temperatures, water supply and inaccessibility. No credit for management of hot desert environments.","sol":"Hot deserts do offer real opportunities, but in the Thar Desert of north-west India and south-east Pakistan every one of them has had to be bought from a hostile environment, so the extent of the opportunity depends on how much money and technology are available.\n\nThe clearest opportunity is energy. The Thar has almost cloudless skies for most of the year and flat, open, low-value land, which is exactly what large solar arrays need: Bhadla Solar Park in western Rajasthan is one of the largest in the world, with a capacity above 2,000 MW, and wind farms near Jaisalmer exploit the steady wind over treeless ground. Mineral extraction is a second opportunity, with limestone, gypsum and Jaisalmer sandstone quarried for the building industry. Farming is a third: alongside traditional herding of goats, sheep and camels, the Indira Gandhi Canal, which carries Sutlej and Beas water about 650 km from Punjab into western Rajasthan, has made commercial irrigated wheat, cotton and mustard possible on land that grew only scrub. Tourism is a fourth, as camel safaris on the dunes near Jaisalmer and the fort itself bring outside money and jobs into a region with few other employers.\n\nThe challenges are the same physical conditions seen from the other side. Rainfall is under 250 mm a year and arrives in a short, unreliable monsoon, while summer temperatures above 40 °C — about 51 °C was recorded at Phalodi in the Thar in May 2016 — drive evaporation, stop outdoor work in the middle of the day and raise the cost of keeping people and livestock alive. Water is therefore the true limit on development, and the canal that eased it has created new problems: over-watering has waterlogged and salinised some irrigated land, and heavy pumping has lowered groundwater. Inaccessibility adds cost again, because settlements are far apart and blown sand drifts across the few roads.\n\nWeighing the two, the opportunities are genuine and growing, but they are conditional. Solar power and canal farming exist only because large-scale investment overcame heat and water shortage, and the environmental cost of that investment — salinised soil, falling water tables, pressure on fragile vegetation — may limit how long the gains last. For the state or an outside investor the Thar now offers considerable opportunity; for a herding family without canal water or capital the challenges still dominate. The extent is best described as substantial but very uneven.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The command phrase is asking you to decide how far, so the answer needs a position before it needs detail.","Work through the four opportunity headings and the three challenge headings the specification names, and pick the strongest two of each for your case study.","Two developed opportunity paragraphs, two developed challenge paragraphs and a conclusion that says for whom the opportunities outweigh the challenges, all carried by named places and figures."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Development of hot desert environments creates opportunities and challenges","3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"'The causes of desertification are mainly human rather than physical.' To what extent do you agree? Use examples in your answer. [9 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3, AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): comprehensive knowledge of the physical and human causes; thorough understanding of how they interact; a substantiated judgement on their relative importance. There should be a discursive element weighing climate change and drought against population growth, overgrazing, over-cultivation and fuel-wood removal.","sol":"Desertification on the fringes of hot deserts has both physical and human causes, and in the Sahel they are so tightly linked that the honest judgement is that human activity does most of the damage while the climate decides when that damage becomes visible.\n\nThe physical case is genuine. The Sahel, the semi-arid belt running south of the Sahara from Senegal and Mauritania to Sudan and Eritrea, receives only about 150 to 600 mm of rain a year, and almost all of it falls in one short wet season, so the margin between a normal year and a failed one is thin. Rainfall declined sharply from the late 1960s into the 1980s and produced severe droughts in 1968–73 and 1983–85, when crops failed, herds died and huge areas of soil were left bare. Rising temperatures make this worse by increasing evaporation from soil and reservoirs, and when rain does come it often falls in a few intense storms that run off hard, dry ground instead of soaking into it. No farming decision causes a drought.\n\nThe human case, however, explains why the land no longer recovers between droughts. Population growth in the Sahel is among the fastest in the world, so more people graze and cultivate the same land every year. Herds are larger, and overgrazing strips shrubs and tree seedlings faster than they can regrow. Fallow periods that once rested a field for several years have been shortened or abandoned, so over-cultivation exhausts thin soils. Most households still cook with wood or charcoal, so trees near settlements are cut for fuel wood, and where the wood has gone dung is burned instead of being spread on the fields. Each of these removes the plant cover and the roots that bind the soil, so the dry harmattan wind and the first storms of the wet season carry the topsoil away: soil erosion, the sixth cause, is really the consequence of the other five.\n\nWeighing them, the physical trigger and the human pressure are not alternatives but multipliers. Drought on lightly used land is survivable, because vegetation regrows when the rains return; drought on land already stripped by animals, ploughs and axes turns into permanent degradation. The strongest evidence that the human causes dominate is that human action can reverse the damage under the same climate, as zaï pits, stone lines and farmer managed natural regeneration have shown in Burkina Faso and Niger. To a large extent, then, the causes are human — but climate change is steadily reducing the room for error.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Sort the six named causes into two groups before you start, and notice that one of them belongs to neither group cleanly.","A 'to what extent' answer must weigh the two groups against each other, so plan where the weighing will happen rather than leaving it to the last line.","One paragraph for the physical case, one for the human case, then a paragraph that decides between them using evidence rather than assertion."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)","3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Assess the effectiveness of strategies used to reduce the risk of desertification. [9 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3, AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): comprehensive knowledge of named strategies (water management, soil management, tree planting, appropriate technology); thorough understanding of how each works; an informed overall judgement of effectiveness examining the parts. Evaluative comments leading to the judgement are credited at Levels 1 and 2.","sol":"Strategies against desertification work best where they are cheap, local and maintained by the people who benefit from them; the large and imported schemes have a poorer record.\n\nWater management attacks the root problem, which is that in the Sahel rain arrives in a few storms and mostly runs off. Stone lines laid along the contour slow that runoff so it infiltrates, and they trap the sediment it carries; earth bunds, small sand dams and hand-dug wells with rope pumps do the same job at village scale. Drip irrigation delivers water to the root rather than losing it to evaporation from a hot surface. These schemes are effective because they are built from local stone, soil and labour, so they survive when funding ends, but they only redistribute the rain that falls and cannot help in a year when the rains fail completely.\n\nSoil management rebuilds fertility. Zaï pits, dug in the dry season and filled with manure, concentrate water and nutrients around each millet seed, and on degraded ground in Burkina Faso they can raise yields substantially, by a wide margin in the best cases,; terracing, mulching, crop rotation and restored fallow periods all return organic matter to thin soils. The limitation is labour: digging pits by hand across a whole farm is exhausting work, so adoption depends on farmers seeing a return quickly.\n\nTree planting is the most publicised strategy and the most variable. Farmer managed natural regeneration in Niger, which protects and prunes the stumps already in farmers' fields instead of importing seedlings, is estimated to have restored around five million hectares, because it costs almost nothing and the farmer owns the result. By contrast, the African Union's Great Green Wall, launched in 2007 to restore a belt of land some 8,000 km long from Senegal to Djibouti, has progressed slowly where it meant planting seedlings that then died without care, and works better where it has shifted towards regeneration and farmer-led restoration.\n\nOverall, the strategies are effective in reverse order of their cost. The evidence from Niger and Burkina Faso is that appropriate technology — stone lines, zaï pits, regeneration, fuel-efficient stoves — measurably raises yields and holds soil, because it matches local skills and needs no spare parts from abroad. Large capital schemes deliver less per pound spent and stall when attention moves on. None of them, however, addresses the underlying pressures of population growth and a drying, warming climate, so they slow desertification rather than stopping it.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The specification names four kinds of strategy; build the answer around them rather than around countries.","Assessing means examining the parts, so for each strategy ask what it costs, who maintains it and what changed on the ground.","Three strategy paragraphs each carrying its own mini-judgement, then a conclusion that ranks them and says what none of them fixes."],"codes":["3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (strategies)","3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How much precipitation a year defines a desert?","a":"Less than 250 mm."},{"q":"Between which latitudes do most of the world's hot deserts lie?","a":"About 15° and 35° north and south of the equator."},{"q":"Which canal brings river water from Punjab into the Thar Desert for irrigated farming?","a":"The Indira Gandhi Canal."},{"q":"Which semi-arid belt of Africa, south of the Sahara, is the standard example of desertification?","a":"The Sahel."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Hot desert climate","back":"Less than 250 mm of rain a year, unreliable and often in one short season; summer daytime highs above 40 °C; clear skies let heat escape at night, so the daily temperature range is large.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Why hot deserts form where they do","back":"Air that rose and rained over the equator sinks again at about 15° to 35° north and south. Sinking air warms, so cloud cannot form and little rain falls.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Hot desert soils","back":"Thin, sandy or stony and pale, with little organic matter because plants are sparse. Evaporation draws dissolved salts upwards, so the surface is often salty and crusted, and bare soil blows away easily.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Xerophyte","back":"A plant adapted to survive with very little water: waxy or spiny leaves cut transpiration, fleshy stems store water, and roots either spread wide near the surface or reach deep to groundwater.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Camel adaptations","back":"Fat stored in the hump, not water; tolerates a rise in body temperature so it sweats less; concentrated urine and dry dung; closable nostrils and long eyelashes; wide feet spread its weight on soft sand.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Biodiversity in hot deserts","back":"Low, because few species tolerate the heat and drought, but highly specialised. That makes the ecosystem fragile: overgrazing, water extraction and vehicle damage remove plants that take years to return.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Hot desert ecosystems have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Thar Desert: where and how big","back":"A hot desert of about 200,000 km² in north-west India, mainly Rajasthan, and south-east Pakistan. Jaisalmer, Bikaner and Jodhpur lie in it or on its edge, and it is one of the most densely populated deserts in the world.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Development of hot desert environments creates opportunities and challenges","open":false},{"front":"One development opportunity in the Thar Desert","back":"Energy: Bhadla Solar Park, over 2,000 MW, and wind farms near Jaisalmer; commercial farming: irrigated wheat, cotton and mustard along the Indira Gandhi Canal; mineral extraction: limestone, gypsum and Jaisalmer sandstone quarried for the building industry.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Development of hot desert environments creates opportunities and challenges","open":true},{"front":"Indira Gandhi Canal","back":"Carries Sutlej and Beas water about 650 km from Punjab into western Rajasthan, allowing commercial irrigated farming. Over-watering has waterlogged and salinised some land, and evaporation losses are high.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Development of hot desert environments creates opportunities and challenges","open":false},{"front":"Thar Desert: challenges of development","back":"Extreme temperatures above 40 °C in May and June; water supply limited to under 250 mm of monsoon rain, with high evaporation and falling groundwater; inaccessibility, with settlements far apart and sand drifting over roads.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Development of hot desert environments creates opportunities and challenges","open":false},{"front":"Desertification","back":"Land in arid and semi-arid areas degrading until it becomes desert-like: vegetation is lost, soil is eroded, and the land can no longer support crops, animals or people as it once did.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)","open":false},{"front":"The Sahel","back":"A semi-arid belt across Africa south of the Sahara, from Senegal and Mauritania to Sudan and Eritrea. Rainfall is roughly 150 to 600 mm in one short wet season; severe droughts struck in 1968–73 and 1983–85.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)","open":false},{"front":"One cause of desertification","back":"Overgrazing: larger herds crop shrubs and tree seedlings faster than they regrow; over-cultivation: fallow periods shortened or abandoned, so thin soils are exhausted; removal of fuel wood: trees near settlements cut for cooking, leaving no roots to bind the soil.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (causes)","open":true},{"front":"One strategy that reduces the risk of desertification","back":"Soil management: zaï pits filled with manure concentrate water and nutrients around each seed, as in Burkina Faso; water management: stone lines on the contour slow runoff so it soaks in; tree planting: farmer managed natural regeneration in Niger.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (strategies)","open":true},{"front":"The Great Green Wall","back":"An African Union initiative launched in 2007 to restore degraded land in a belt about 8,000 km long from Senegal to Djibouti, increasingly through farmer managed natural regeneration rather than planting seedlings.","code":"3.1.2.3 — Areas on the fringe of hot deserts are at risk of desertification (strategies)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Climate data for a station in the Thar Desert, north-west India. Written for this room; the figures are illustrative but typical of the desert.\nMonth | Mean daily maximum temperature (°C) | Rainfall (mm)\nJanuary | 24 | 2\nFebruary | 27 | 2\nMarch | 33 | 2\nApril | 38 | 3\nMay | 42 | 6\nJune | 42 | 20\nJuly | 39 | 70\nAugust | 37 | 70\nSeptember | 36 | 20\nOctober | 36 | 5\nNovember | 31 | 2\nDecember | 26 | 2\nYear | — | 204","Figure 2 — A photograph of the Thar Desert near Jaisalmer, north-west India, described in words. Written for this room; the scene is typical of the desert rather than of one particular photograph.\nThe foreground is pale sand and stony ground, with bare patches between knee-high thorny shrubs. A single khejri tree stands on the left, its crown thin and its lower branches cut back. In the middle distance a herd of about thirty goats is spread out across the shrubs, with one herder walking beside them. A single-track tarmac road crosses the view from left to right; a tongue of blown sand has drifted over the right-hand edge of the road, and tyre tracks curve around it onto the open ground. Along the ridge on the horizon stand seven wind turbines, evenly spaced. There are no buildings and no surface water anywhere in the view.","Figure 3 — Results from two millet fields in a village on the southern edge of the Sahel, in Burkina Faso, in the same rainfall year and on the same soil type. Written for this room; figures illustrative.\nField | Soil and water management used | Millet yield (kg per hectare) | Depth of soil lost from the plot during the wet season (mm)\nField A | none: ploughed bare and sown flat | 320 | 9\nField B | stone lines along the contour, with zaï planting pits filled with manure | 780 | 2","Figure 4 — A short text extract, written for this room. The speaker is a composite of accounts from farming and herding families in the Sahel, not a real named person; the conditions described are typical of the region.\n'My grandfather kept twenty cattle on this land and rested a field for six years before sowing it again. I keep sixty, because I have eight children and each family needs its own animals. There is no fallow now: every field is sown every year, and the millet is shorter than it was. When I was a boy I cut firewood at the edge of the village. Now I walk four hours to find wood, and my wife burns the dung we used to spread on the fields. The rains came late again, and when they came they fell in two storms that ran straight off the bare ground.'"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 1 Question 02.9, 9 marks: to what extent do hot desert environments provide opportunities as well as challenges for development?)","items":["The paragraph: 'The strongest opportunity in the Thar Desert comes from the very conditions that make it hostile. Skies over Rajasthan are almost cloudless for most of the year and the land is flat, empty and cheap, so solar developers can build at a scale that is impossible in farmed regions: Bhadla Solar Park in western Rajasthan now has a capacity of more than 2,000 MW, making it one of the largest in the world, while wind farms near Jaisalmer use the steady wind blowing across treeless ground. That development brings construction jobs, electricity for towns such as Jaisalmer and Bikaner, and roads and transmission lines that other industries can use afterwards. The aridity that makes farming precarious is therefore the same aridity that makes energy profitable, which suggests that the challenges of a hot desert are not simply obstacles to development but, for some industries, the reason the development happens at all.'","Point: the physical conditions that create the challenges are also the source of the greatest opportunity.","Named evidence: Bhadla Solar Park, western Rajasthan, more than 2,000 MW; wind farms near Jaisalmer; Jaisalmer and Bikaner as the settlements served.","Developed explanation: cloudless skies mean high insolation and few generation losses; flat, low-value, unfarmed land keeps site costs down; no trees or relief means wind flows unobstructed — and the investment then leaves roads and grid connections behind it, which is the multiplier effect.","Link to the question: the paragraph answers 'to what extent' rather than 'what', because it measures the size of the opportunity against the conditions that limit it.","Mini-judgement: for capital-intensive industries the desert environment is an advantage, not a barrier.","Why an examiner puts it in Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): it demonstrates 'comprehensive and accurate knowledge of locations, places and processes in relation to a hot desert' (named park, district, capacity, settlements), shows 'thorough geographical understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes' (aridity to insolation to investment to infrastructure) and makes a 'thorough application of knowledge and understanding in evaluating the extent'."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: 'Hot deserts have lots of opportunities. There is solar power because it is very sunny and there is no rain, so they can build solar farms. People can also do mining and tourism and farming. But it is very hot and there is not much water, which is a challenge for the people who live there. Overall there are opportunities and challenges in hot deserts.'","What it does have: the right two categories (opportunities and challenges) and one correct link between sunshine and solar power, so it is not worth zero.","The descriptor it meets — Level 1 (Basic, 1–3): 'demonstrates very limited knowledge of locations, places and processes in relation to a hot desert' (no country, region, settlement, scheme or figure anywhere) and 'shows slight geographical understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes'.","The descriptor it misses — Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): it cannot show 'thorough application of knowledge and understanding in evaluating the extent to which a hot desert provides opportunities and challenges', because the last sentence announces that both exist instead of weighing them; and it misses 'comprehensive and accurate knowledge of locations, places and processes' entirely.","The single change that would lift it most: replace 'they can build solar farms' with a named scheme and its size in a named place, then say what that scale proves about the balance of opportunity and challenge.","The second change: turn 'it is very hot and there is not much water' into a chain — how much rain, how hot, and what that actually stops a farmer or a builder doing."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Thar Desert: opportunities and challenges of development","items":["Location: the Thar Desert straddles the border of north-west India and south-east Pakistan; most of it lies in the Indian state of Rajasthan, with parts in Gujarat, Punjab and Haryana, and in Sindh and Punjab provinces of Pakistan.","Size: roughly 200,000 km², and one of the most densely populated deserts in the world.","Main settlements within or on the edge of the desert: Jaisalmer, Bikaner and Jodhpur, all in Rajasthan.","Climate: less than 250 mm of rain a year, nearly all of it in the monsoon between July and September; rainfall is unreliable from year to year.","Temperature: May and June daytime temperatures regularly above 40 °C; about 51 °C was recorded at Phalodi in the Thar in May 2016, the highest reliably recorded temperature in India. Winter nights can fall close to freezing.","Vegetation: sparse thorny scrub, acacia and the khejri tree (Prosopis cineraria), whose deep tap root reaches groundwater and which supplies shade, fodder and firewood.","Opportunity — mineral extraction: limestone, gypsum and Jaisalmer's yellow sandstone are quarried for the building industry.","Opportunity — energy from the sun: Bhadla Solar Park in western Rajasthan is one of the largest solar parks in the world, with a capacity of more than 2,000 MW, built on flat, cheap, almost cloudless land.","Opportunity — energy from wind: large wind farms near Jaisalmer use the steady wind that blows across open, treeless ground.","Opportunity — traditional farming: subsistence herding of goats, sheep, cattle and camels on sparse vegetation, with millet and pulses grown where the monsoon allows.","Opportunity — commercial farming: the Indira Gandhi Canal carries water from the Sutlej and Beas rivers in Punjab about 650 km into western Rajasthan, supporting irrigated wheat, cotton and mustard.","Opportunity — tourism: camel safaris on the dunes near Jaisalmer, an annual desert festival at Jaisalmer, and Jaisalmer Fort, part of the Hill Forts of Rajasthan World Heritage Site.","Challenge — extreme temperatures: outdoor work is restricted in the middle of the day in summer, water demand for people and livestock rises, and heat raises construction and machinery costs.","Challenge — water supply: low and unreliable rainfall, very high evaporation, and falling groundwater where tube wells pump heavily; traditional storage relies on underground rainwater tanks (tankas) and rainwater-harvesting fields.","Challenge — the cost of the water solution: over-watering along the Indira Gandhi Canal has waterlogged and salinised some irrigated land, and evaporation wastes part of the supply before it reaches the fields.","Challenge — inaccessibility: settlements are far apart, roads are few and blown sand drifts across them, although a railway does reach Jaisalmer.","Examiner's warning on this case study: the nine-mark question on hot deserts asks about opportunities and challenges for development, and the mark scheme gives no credit for writing about managing or conserving hot desert environments."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Sahel: desertification and the strategies used against it","items":["The Sahel is the semi-arid belt across Africa immediately south of the Sahara, running from Senegal and Mauritania on the Atlantic to Sudan and Eritrea on the Red Sea; Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger, northern Nigeria and Chad lie within it.","Rainfall is roughly 150 to 600 mm a year, increasing southwards, and falls in a single short wet season in the northern summer.","Severe droughts struck in 1968–73 and again in 1983–85, when crops failed, herds died and famine followed.","Rainfall declined markedly from the late 1960s into the 1980s; the rains have partly recovered since the 1990s but remain very variable from year to year, and rising temperatures increase evaporation.","The harmattan is the dry, dusty north-easterly wind that blows from the Sahara across the Sahel in the northern winter and strips bare topsoil.","Cause — population growth: Sahel populations are among the fastest-growing in the world; Niger's is growing at more than 3% a year, so more people farm and graze the same land each year.","Cause — removal of fuel wood: the great majority of households cook with wood or charcoal, so trees near settlements are cut down; where wood has run out, dung is burned instead of being spread on the fields.","Cause — overgrazing: larger herds of cattle and goats crop shrubs and tree seedlings faster than they can grow back, and goats graze right down to ground level, leaving little behind.","Cause — over-cultivation: fallow periods that once rested a field for several years have been shortened or abandoned, so nutrients are never replaced and yields fall.","Cause — soil erosion: with the plant cover and its roots gone, wind removes dry topsoil and intense storms wash it off the surface; it is usually the consequence of the other causes rather than a separate one.","Strategy — water management: stone lines and earth bunds along the contour, small sand dams, hand-dug wells with rope pumps, and drip irrigation that delivers water to the root rather than losing it to evaporation.","Strategy — soil management: zaï planting pits dug in the dry season and filled with manure, so water and nutrients are concentrated around each seed; also terracing, mulching, crop rotation and restored fallow periods. Zaï cultivation was revived in Burkina Faso by farmers including Yacouba Sawadogo, who received the Right Livelihood Award in 2018.","Strategy — tree planting: farmer managed natural regeneration in Niger protects and prunes the tree stumps already present in farmers' fields instead of planting seedlings, and is estimated to have restored around five million hectares; trees shelter crops from the wind, bind the soil and supply fuel and fodder.","Strategy — the Great Green Wall: launched by the African Union in 2007, it aims to restore degraded land in a belt about 8,000 km long from Senegal to Djibouti; progress has been slow where it relied on planting seedlings that then died, and faster where it supports farmer-led regeneration.","Strategy — appropriate technology: schemes that are cheap, built from local materials and maintainable by the community, such as stone lines, zaï pits, rope pumps, fuel-efficient stoves and solar cookers that cut the demand for firewood.","Judgement an examiner rewards: local, low-cost schemes have the strongest evidence behind them, but none of the strategies removes the underlying pressures of population growth and a warming, drying climate, so they slow desertification rather than stopping it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — hot desert processes, terms and adaptations","items":["Desert: land that receives less than 250 mm of precipitation a year.","Distribution: most hot deserts lie between about 15° and 35° north and south of the equator, where air that rose and rained at the equator sinks again; sinking air warms, so cloud cannot form.","Daily temperature range: cloudless skies let the ground heat rapidly by day and lose that heat rapidly at night, so day and night temperatures differ sharply.","Desert soils: thin, sandy or stony, pale and low in organic matter because plant litter is scarce; strong evaporation draws dissolved salts upwards, leaving a salty crust.","Xerophyte: a plant adapted to survive with very little water.","Succulent: a plant that stores water in a fleshy stem or leaves — the cactus family, native to the Americas, is the best-known example.","Ephemeral: a plant that waits out the drought as a seed and completes its entire life cycle within weeks of rain falling.","Two root strategies: wide, shallow roots catch a brief shower over a large area, while a deep tap root, as in the khejri tree of the Thar, reaches groundwater metres below the surface.","Reducing transpiration: spines instead of broad leaves cut surface area, waxy coatings seal the surface, and some desert plants open their stomata only at night when evaporation is slower.","Camel adaptations: fat stored in the hump rather than water, tolerance of a rise in body temperature that delays sweating, concentrated urine and dry dung, wide feet that spread weight on soft sand, long eyelashes and closable nostrils against blown sand.","Fennec fox (Sahara): nocturnal, shelters in a burrow through the heat of the day, and loses heat through very large ears.","Interdependence: the climate limits plant growth, sparse plants keep the soil thin, thin soil limits plants again, and animals and people depend on what grows — so damage to one part of the system damages all of it.","Biodiversity: low in hot deserts and made up of highly specialised species, which is why the ecosystem recovers slowly once vegetation or soil has been lost.","Threats to desert biodiversity: overgrazing, the removal of wood, water extraction that dries out oases and wetlands, off-road vehicles that break the fragile surface crust, and rising temperatures."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'To what extent do hot desert environments provide opportunities as well as challenges for development? Use a case study of a hot desert you have studied.' [9 marks]","items":["Decode the command: 'to what extent' asks how far one side outweighs the other. Both sides must appear, both must be developed, and the answer must commit to a position rather than concluding that there are some of each.","Decode the rest of the stem: 'a case study of a hot desert' means named places, schemes and figures throughout — here the Thar Desert of north-west India and south-east Pakistan. The mark scheme gives no credit for managing or conserving hot desert environments, so keep sustainable management and desertification strategies out of this answer.","Opening (two sentences): name the desert and its location, then state the line you will argue — that the opportunities are real but conditional on the money and technology available to overcome the conditions.","Paragraph 1 — opportunity, energy: Bhadla Solar Park in western Rajasthan, more than 2,000 MW, built because skies are almost cloudless and land is flat, empty and cheap; wind farms near Jaisalmer on open, treeless ground. Consequences: jobs, electricity, roads and transmission lines.","Paragraph 2 — opportunity, farming and tourism: the Indira Gandhi Canal carries Sutlej and Beas water about 650 km into western Rajasthan for irrigated wheat, cotton and mustard, alongside traditional herding; camel safaris near Jaisalmer and Jaisalmer Fort bring visitor spending into a region with few employers. Mineral extraction (limestone, gypsum, sandstone) can be a sentence inside this paragraph.","Paragraph 3 — challenge, heat and water: under 250 mm of unreliable monsoon rain, very high evaporation, falling groundwater from tube wells, and May and June temperatures above 40 °C, with about 51 °C recorded at Phalodi in May 2016, which stops outdoor work and raises every cost.","Paragraph 4 — challenge, the cost of the solutions and distance: over-watering along the canal has waterlogged and salinised land, so the fix has created a new problem; settlements are far apart and blown sand drifts across the few roads, keeping transport costs high.","Evaluation thread running through every paragraph: each opportunity exists only because capital and technology have been used to defeat a physical challenge, so the size of the opportunity tracks the size of the investment rather than anything the desert provides for free.","What the conclusion must say: state how far the opportunities outweigh the challenges and for whom — considerable for the state and outside investors, far smaller for a herding family without canal water or capital — and name the qualification (salinisation, falling water tables, pressure on fragile vegetation) that may limit how long the gains last.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly two minutes planning and ten minutes writing, then a minute rereading for spelling, punctuation and specialist terms such as evaporation, salinisation, irrigation and infrastructure, because accurate terminology is part of what lifts an answer into Level 3."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:03:42Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file: all six fixes land cleanly and nothing new is broken — question 6 now credits only what Figure 2 prints (turbines, herd, road, pale sand and empty horizon with no buildings), 'SPaG' appears nowhere in the kit while every m stays 9 with AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3 matching the specimen's 02.9, facts[3] now says Sutlej and Beas water carried about 650 km from Punjab, question 0 and its worked example both read 'pattern', 'just over a fifth' is right for 2 mm against 9 mm, and the three new open cards each give three correct answers within the 60-word limit; every Figure 1 and Figure 3 recount, the 42 − 24 = 18 °C range, the 140 of 204 mm, and the Thar and Sahel facts (Bhadla over 2,000 MW, about 51 °C at Phalodi in May 2016, the Indira Gandhi Canal, the 1968–73 and 1983–85 droughts, farmer managed natural regeneration at around five million hectares, the 2007 Great Green Wall) check out again, so this is now publishable as it stands."}}},"3.1.2.4":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.2.4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Cold environments are one of the two ways the living world section of Paper 1 can end: you answer either hot deserts or cold environments, and the nine-mark question that closes the section asks how far the environment you studied offers opportunities and challenges for development. That question wants three things at once — accurate knowledge of a real cold place, understanding of how climate, permafrost, soil, plants, animals and people hold one another in place, and a judgement you actually make rather than imply. Around it sit the resource questions: read a climate graph, read a photograph of a settlement, suggest why people disagree about a mine. This room gives you the processes, one fully stocked case study (Svalbard), Alaska and Antarctica behind it, and the judgement sentences that lift an answer out of the middle band.","idea":[{"h":"Cold, dry and frozen solid: the characteristics and the web that holds them","t":"Cold environments come in two kinds. Polar environments are permanently ice-covered: Antarctica is about 14 million km², roughly 98% of it under ice holding around 90% of the world's ice, with a mean annual temperature at the South Pole near −49 °C and the lowest natural temperature ever recorded on Earth, −89.2 °C at Vostok in July 1983. Tundra lies around the edges of the Arctic Ocean, on the margins of the northern land masses. It is treeless, with a growing season of only about two months, winter means below −10 °C and precipitation often under 250 mm a year, most of it snow — so a tundra is a cold desert. Beneath it lies permafrost, ground frozen for at least two consecutive years, hundreds of metres thick in places, with only a shallow active layer thawing each summer. These characteristics are not a list; they are a chain. The cold makes the permafrost, the permafrost stops meltwater draining so the surface waterlogs, waterlogging starves the soil of oxygen and slows decomposition, so soils stay thin, acidic and poor, so plants stay low, shallow-rooted and slow, so food chains stay short. A top-level answer never recites the characteristics: it picks one link and follows it through to people, showing that a change at any point moves everything downstream.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics"},{"h":"Svalbard: what a cold place offers and what it charges","t":"Svalbard is a Norwegian archipelago in the Arctic Ocean between roughly 74° and 81° north: about 61,000 km² of land, around 60% under glacier ice, with about 2,400 people in Longyearbyen. The opportunities are the four the specification names. Mineral extraction came first — Longyearbyen was founded as a coal town in 1906, and the Russian settlement of Barentsburg still mines coal. Energy: Norway's Arctic offshore gas and Alaska's North Slope oil show what cold regions hold, and Svalbard's own power came from its coal. Fishing: the Barents Sea holds the world's largest cod stock, and Alaska alone lands well over half the fish caught in United States waters by weight. Tourism sells the very things that make the place hostile — glaciers, polar bears, the midnight sun from late April to late August and the polar night from late October to mid-February. Against that stand the challenges: extreme temperature, inaccessibility, and the provision of buildings and infrastructure on ground that thaws if you warm it. A top-level answer does not list opportunities then list challenges. It weighs them against each other, asking what a resource has to be worth before anybody pays the cost of getting it out, and it shows the two changing over time — coal has been wound down on Svalbard while tourism and research have grown.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Development of cold environments creates opportunities and challenges"},{"h":"Why wilderness is worth more than what lies under it","t":"Wilderness means land essentially unaltered by human activity, and the polar regions hold the largest areas of it left on Earth. Their value is of four kinds. Scientific: Antarctic ice cores record the composition of the atmosphere over hundreds of thousands of years precisely because nothing has contaminated them, and the Svalbard Global Seed Vault, opened in 2008 and holding over a million seed samples, was built into permafrost because cold and stability are themselves a resource. Climatic: bright ice and snow reflect solar radiation back to space, and frozen ground stores carbon estimated at roughly twice the amount now in the atmosphere, which begins to escape as carbon dioxide and methane once it thaws. Ecological: food chains are short, so losing one species passes straight up the chain, and growth is so slow that a single vehicle track can still be visible decades later. Cultural: around four million people live in the Arctic, including Iñupiat, Inuit and Sámi communities whose food and identity come from the land. A top-level answer never simply calls a cold environment 'fragile'. It names the mechanism of the fragility — the slowness of growth, the shortness of the food chain, the fact that thawed ground does not refreeze into what it was — and it admits that for the people who live there wilderness is also a workplace.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (wilderness value)"},{"h":"Technology, governments, treaties and pressure groups","t":"Four kinds of strategy try to hold development and conservation together. Technology reduces the footprint: the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, about 1,300 km from Prudhoe Bay to Valdez and completed in 1977, is raised on supports for roughly half its length, with heat pipes drawing warmth out of the ground so that warm oil does not thaw its own foundations, and it is lifted high enough in places for caribou to pass beneath; extended-reach drilling works many wells from one gravel pad; winter ice roads melt in spring and leave no scar. Governments protect land outright: Norway has protected roughly two-thirds of Svalbard as national parks and nature reserves, and the Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act of 1980 placed about 420,000 km² into parks, refuges and wilderness. International agreements bind several states at once: the Antarctic Treaty, signed in 1959 and in force from 1961, reserves everything south of 60° south for peaceful scientific use and freezes territorial claims, while its Madrid Protocol, in force since 1998, makes Antarctica a natural reserve and bans mining, a ban that cannot be reviewed before 2048; the Ross Sea marine protected area of about 1.55 million km² took effect in 2017; the Arctic Council has coordinated the eight Arctic states and indigenous organisations since 1996. Conservation groups such as WWF and Greenpeace campaign and monitor, and the tour operators' body IAATO, formed in 1991, limits Antarctic landings to a hundred people ashore at a time. A top-level answer evaluates rather than lists: for each strategy it asks who is bound by it, who is not, and what happens when the money changes.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (balancing development and conservation)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading the cold-environment climate graph (Figure 1)","setup":"Figure 1 plots, for a high Arctic station, mean monthly temperature in °C as a line and monthly precipitation in mm as bars. Temperature, January to December: −13, −14, −14, −11, −4, 2, 6, 5, 1, −4, −8, −11. Precipitation, January to December: 18, 20, 19, 13, 9, 10, 16, 21, 22, 20, 16, 16. You are asked to calculate the annual temperature range, to say how many months are above freezing, and to describe the precipitation.","steps":["Pick out the two values the range needs: the lowest mean on the line is −14 °C, reached in both February and March, and the highest is 6 °C in July.","Annual temperature range = highest mean − lowest mean = 6 − (−14) = 20 °C, and subtracting a negative is what makes the range so large.","Count the plots sitting above the zero line: June, July, August and September — four months above freezing, and eight at or below it.","Add the twelve bars: the year's precipitation totals 200 mm, the tallest bar is September at 22 mm and the shortest is May at 9 mm, so the wettest month has under three times the rain of the driest.","Turn the numbers into a description: precipitation here is low but remarkably even, and because a place with 200 mm a year is as dry as a desert, the water that arrives matters far more than the small differences between months."],"cues":["Which two months does a range calculation actually use?","What goes wrong if you forget the minus sign?","How would you count months above freezing without guessing?","How do you get the year’s total from the bars, and which month is the tallest bar?","What is the one sentence a describe question wants about this pattern?"]},{"title":"Explaining settlement design from the photograph (Figure 2, 6 marks)","setup":"Figure 2 describes the edge of Longyearbyen, Svalbard, in late winter: wooden houses standing clear of the ground on short piles with an open gap beneath the floor; a silver insulated pipe running beside the road on low trestles and entering each block above floor level; an unsealed gravel road banked with ploughed snow; metal avalanche fencing bolted across the mountainside above the roofs; two old wooden cableway pylons on the skyline; no trees anywhere. The question: Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, explain how buildings and services in a cold environment are designed to cope with the ground conditions. [6 marks]","steps":["Name the ground condition first, because every feature answers it: the town stands on permafrost, of which only a shallow active layer thaws each summer.","Explain the piles and the air gap through the process: a heated building resting on frozen ground melts the ice within it, the thawed soil loses strength, and the building sinks unevenly — so the piles reach down into ground that stays frozen and the gap lets cold air carry the building's heat away.","Explain the raised pipe as the same problem in reverse: buried services would either thaw the ground around them or freeze solid, and a trench cut in permafrost heals very slowly, so pipes run above ground insulated, visible and repairable.","Explain the gravel: a thick unsealed pad insulates the permafrost beneath the road and can simply be re-graded when frost heave lifts it, which tarmac cannot.","Close on the fencing to show you have used the whole photograph: with no trees to anchor snow on the slope, and with warming loosening the ground, the town has to engineer the protection the landscape does not provide."],"cues":["Before explaining any feature, what single condition are they all answering?","Why is a warm house on frozen ground a problem for the house?","Why not simply bury the water pipe as we do at home?","What can a gravel road do that a tarmac one cannot?","Which feature in the photograph have you not used yet?"]},{"title":"Suggesting why people disagree about a mine (Figure 4, 4 marks)","setup":"Figure 4 is a council member speaking at a public meeting about a proposed mine in an Arctic town: the company promises a hundred jobs and money for a school the town has been promised for a decade; half the young people leave because there is no work; the access road crosses the valley where the caribou calve every June; gravel pads and road dust will thaw the ground either side; the speaker's father hunted along that valley; and if the ore price falls the company will go while the road and the thawed ground remain. The question: Using Figure 4, suggest why people living in a cold environment may disagree about a proposed mine. [4 marks]","steps":["Take the strongest case for the mine straight from the extract: jobs and a school would stop the young people leaving, so families who need work now would back it.","Set against it the loss that falls on a different group: the road crosses calving ground, and disturbance at calving can push a herd off land it has used for generations, which costs a hunting family food and identity, not just scenery.","Pull out the physical process the speaker names, because a suggest question rewards interpretation rather than opinion: darker gravel and dust absorb more heat, thawing permafrost beside the route so the damage spreads well past the road itself.","Finish on the disagreement about time: the jobs last only while the ore price holds, but the road and the thawed ground are permanent, so people judging the same mine over five years and over fifty reach opposite conclusions."],"cues":["Who in the town gains first, and from what exactly?","Who loses, and is the loss only about landscape?","Which sentence in the extract describes a physical process rather than an opinion?","Why can two reasonable residents disagree even when they agree on the facts?"]},{"title":"Building the 9-mark 'to what extent' answer on Svalbard","setup":"The question: Using a case study of a cold environment, to what extent do the opportunities for development outweigh the challenges? [9 marks] (AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks). Your case study is Svalbard, and you hold these facts: a Norwegian archipelago between about 74° and 81° north, roughly 61,000 km² of land, around 60% glacier-covered; Longyearbyen founded as a coal town in 1906, population about 2,400; Barentsburg still mines coal; the Barents Sea holds the world's largest cod stock; tourism sells glaciers, polar bears, the midnight sun from late April to late August and the polar night from late October to mid-February; the Global Seed Vault opened in 2008 with over a million seed samples; permafrost hundreds of metres thick forces buildings onto piles and pipes above ground; the Arctic is warming roughly three to four times as fast as the world as a whole; an avalanche onto houses in Longyearbyen killed two people in December 2015.","steps":["Decode the command before planning: 'to what extent' asks how far, not whether, so the plan needs a paragraph that pushes back and a conclusion that places itself, not a list of pros and cons.","Open the opportunities paragraph with the one that built the place and locate it precisely, because AO1 credit comes from location and detail: coal drew people to Longyearbyen from 1906 and still supports Barentsburg today.","Add the opportunities that are growing rather than shrinking, and explain why they suit a cold environment: fishing on the world's largest cod stock, and tourism and science that sell the cold itself rather than fight it.","Write the challenges as processes, not adjectives: permafrost means a heated building thaws its own foundations unless it stands on piles, inaccessibility means goods come by ship or plane, and warming brings slope failure of the kind that killed two people in Longyearbyen in December 2015.","Judge rather than summarise: the opportunities outweigh the challenges only where the resource is valuable enough to pay for beating the cold, which is why coal has been wound down while tourism and research have grown — so the answer is 'to a limited and changing extent, and it depends on the resource'."],"cues":["What does the command phrase force into the plan that a list would miss?","Which opportunity came first, and can you place it on a map?","Which opportunities are growing, and what do they have in common?","How do you turn 'it is very cold' into something an examiner can credit?","What evidence lets the conclusion take a position instead of sitting on the fence?"]},{"title":"Explaining a conservation strategy from the protection table (Figure 3, 6 marks)","setup":"Figure 3 lists areas protected in and around cold environments: Antarctica, made a natural reserve by the Madrid Protocol in force from 1998, 14,000,000 km²; the Ross Sea marine protected area, in force from 2017, 1,550,000 km²; Alaska's national parks, refuges and wilderness created by the Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act of 1980, 420,000 km²; the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge, Alaska, 78,000 km²; and Svalbard's national parks and nature reserves, Norway, about 40,000 km². The question: Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain why such large areas of cold environment have been protected. [6 marks]","steps":["Start by making the table say something an examiner can credit rather than repeating it: even the smallest entry in Figure 3 is larger than many countries, so protection here is deliberately done at continental scale.","Give the first reason from the ecology: the animals that matter move, so caribou that calve on the refuge coastal plain migrate hundreds of kilometres and Ross Sea seals and seabirds feed across a whole ocean — a small reserve would only be a gap in a journey.","Give the second reason from the rate of recovery: with a growing season of about two months, a track or a spill scars the surface for a generation, so protection has to prevent damage rather than repair it.","Give the third reason from science: Antarctic ice cores are valuable precisely because nothing has contaminated them, which means the protection and the value are the same thing.","Explain the pattern in the sizes rather than leaving it: the two largest entries are Antarctic, where nobody lives permanently and no profitable resource has been proven, so states could agree; the Alaskan areas sit beside working oilfields, which is why their boundaries are still argued over."],"cues":["What can you say about the sizes in the table that is not just reading it out?","Why would a small reserve fail to protect a caribou herd?","How long does a tundra scar last, and why does that change the strategy?","Why is undisturbed ice worth more to a scientist than ice near a base?","Why are the biggest protected areas the Antarctic ones?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What exactly is permafrost, and what is the active layer?","a":"Permafrost is ground — soil, sediment or rock — that stays at or below 0 °C for at least two consecutive years. The active layer is the shallow surface layer above it that thaws each summer and refreezes each winter."},{"q":"Why is tundra soil thin and poor even though the surface is waterlogged in summer?","a":"The frozen ground beneath is impermeable, so meltwater cannot drain and the surface waterlogs. Waterlogged soil has little oxygen, so decomposers work very slowly, litter builds up as peat instead of rotting, and few nutrients are released."},{"q":"Name the four kinds of development opportunity found in cold environments, with an example of each.","a":"Mineral extraction (coal at Longyearbyen and Barentsburg on Svalbard); energy (oil at Prudhoe Bay, Alaska); fishing (cod in the Barents Sea); tourism (cruises and snowmobile trips on Svalbard)."},{"q":"Why does the Trans-Alaska Pipeline run above ground for about half its length?","a":"The oil in it is warm. Buried in permafrost it would thaw the ground, which would lose strength and let the pipe sag or break. Raising it on supports fitted with heat pipes keeps the foundations frozen, and also lets caribou cross beneath."}]},"room":{"facts":["Where this room is examined and what it is worth: cold environments are the optional half of Paper 1 Section B, Question 2 The living world, and the questions there run at 1, 2, 4, 6 and 9 marks. The 1- and 2-mark questions are point-marked — one mark for the point, a second for developing it; the 4-mark questions carry Levels 1–2 (1 Basic 1–2, 2 Clear 3–4); the 6- and 9-mark questions carry Levels 1–3 (Basic, Clear, Detailed), and every grid has 0 for no relevant content. Work at about a minute a mark: two minutes on a 2-marker, six on a 6-marker, nine or ten on the 9-marker.","The 9-mark question on this section is split AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks and banded Level 1 (Basic) 1–3, Level 2 (Clear) 4–6, Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9. It carries no SPaG marks — on Paper 1 those three marks sit on the natural hazards question — so all nine have to be earned by geography: named places, figures and processes for AO1, the links between them for AO2, and a reasoned, substantiated judgement that answers the question asked for AO3.","The stems to expect: 'Using Figure 1, calculate…' and 'Using Figure 1, describe…' for the skills marks, which are AO4; 'Explain how…' and 'Suggest why…' for the 4- and 6-mark levels questions; and 'To what extent…', 'Evaluate…' or 'Assess…' for the 9-mark answer, which needs a named case study. Where a stem says 'Using Figure…', an answer that ignores the figure is capped or scores nothing; where it says 'Explain', description alone is capped at Level 1.","Cold environment climate: tundra winters with monthly means below −10 °C, a growing season of only about two months, and precipitation often under 250 mm a year, most of it snow — a cold desert. Antarctica is about 14 million km², roughly 98% ice-covered, holding around 90% of the world's ice; the lowest natural temperature recorded on Earth is −89.2 °C at Vostok in July 1983.","Permafrost is ground that stays at or below 0 °C for at least two consecutive years, hundreds of metres thick in places, with only a shallow active layer thawing each summer. Because it is impermeable, summer meltwater cannot drain, so the tundra surface waterlogs even though precipitation is tiny.","Svalbard: Norwegian archipelago between about 74° and 81° north, roughly 61,000 km² of land, around 60% glacier-covered. Longyearbyen founded as a coal town in 1906, population about 2,400; Barentsburg is the Russian mining settlement; the Global Seed Vault opened in 2008 with over a million seed samples; midnight sun late April to late August, polar night late October to mid-February.","Alaska: Prudhoe Bay oil field discovered in 1968, more than 13 billion barrels produced; the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, about 1,300 km from Prudhoe Bay to Valdez, opened in 1977 and is raised on supports for roughly half its length; the Alaska Permanent Fund was created in 1976; the Alaska Native Claims Settlement Act of 1971 transferred about 44 million acres and just under a billion dollars to Alaska Native corporations; the Exxon Valdez spilled around 11 million gallons of crude into Prince William Sound in 1989.","Protection: the Antarctic Treaty was signed in 1959 and came into force in 1961, covering everything south of 60° south, reserving it for peaceful scientific use and freezing territorial claims; the Madrid Protocol, in force since 1998, makes Antarctica a natural reserve and bans mining, a ban that cannot be reviewed before 2048; the Ross Sea marine protected area of about 1.55 million km² took effect in 2017; the Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act of 1980 protected about 420,000 km²; roughly two-thirds of Svalbard's land is national park or nature reserve.","Why the fragility matters: growth is so slow that a vehicle track can still show decades later; food chains are short, so losing one species passes straight up; frozen ground stores carbon estimated at roughly twice the amount now in the atmosphere; and the Arctic has warmed roughly three to four times as fast as the world as a whole."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following best describes the climate of a tundra environment? Shade one box. A  Warm summers, cold winters and rain in every month.  B  Short cool summers, very cold winters and low precipitation, most of it falling as snow.  C  Cold all year with heavy rainfall and no summer at all.  D  Hot summers, mild winters and a long dry season. [1 mark]","a":"B — short cool summers, very cold winters and low precipitation, most of it falling as snow. No credit if two or more boxes are shaded. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"B is correct. The tundra does have a summer, but a very short one: mean temperatures rise above freezing for only two to four months and the highest monthly mean is usually well under 10 °C. Winters are severe, with monthly means below −10 °C. Crucially, precipitation is low — often under 250 mm a year — which is why the tundra is called a cold desert, and most of it arrives as snow because the air is below freezing for most of the year. A is wrong because tundra summers are not warm and there is no rain in every month. C is wrong because there is a short summer and the total precipitation is small, not heavy. D describes a seasonally dry tropical climate, not a polar or tundra one.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how much water actually falls on the tundra in a year, not just how cold it is.","Two of the options can be ruled out on precipitation alone; check each against what you know of the annual total.","Decide first whether the tundra has a summer at all, then decide whether it is wet or dry."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, calculate the annual temperature range shown. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer, 20 °C; one mark for an indication of the calculation, eg highest mean 6 °C minus lowest mean −14 °C. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The annual temperature range is the difference between the highest and the lowest mean monthly temperature plotted on Figure 1. The highest plot on the temperature line is July at 6 °C. The lowest is −14 °C, reached in both February and March. Range = 6 − (−14) = 6 + 14 = 20 °C. The commonest mistake is to subtract without allowing for the negative sign and answer 8 °C; subtracting a negative adds, which is exactly why cold environments have such large annual ranges compared with the tropics.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A range always compares the two ends of a set of values, not an average of them.","Find the highest plot on the line and the lowest plot on the line before you do any arithmetic.","Write the subtraction out in full, brackets and all, so the minus sign in front of the winter figure is not lost."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, describe the precipitation shown. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic statement of the pattern taken from the graph, eg precipitation is low in every month; second mark for developing it with figures from the graph, eg the annual total is only about 200 mm and the range between the wettest and driest months is just 13 mm. No credit for explanation. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Precipitation in Figure 1 is low throughout the year. Every bar lies between 9 mm and 22 mm, so the annual total is only 200 mm — about as little as a hot desert receives, and a small fraction of a typical British annual total. The pattern is also remarkably even: the wettest month, September at 22 mm, gets only 13 mm more than the driest, May at 9 mm, so there is no wet season and no dry season in the way a tropical climate graph would show. If anything there is a slight late-summer and autumn maximum, from August to October, and a spring minimum in April and May.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Describe means say what the pattern is; no reasons are wanted here.","Look at the overall size of the bars first, then at how much they vary across the year.","A second mark needs a figure lifted from the graph, so decide which two numbers best sum it up."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"State what is meant by the term 'permafrost'. [1 mark]","a":"Ground — soil, sediment or rock — that remains at or below 0 °C for at least two consecutive years. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"Permafrost is ground that stays at or below 0 °C continuously for at least two years. The definition is about the ground, not the surface: permafrost may lie under a surface that thaws every summer, and that thawing surface layer is called the active layer, not part of the permafrost itself. On Svalbard permafrost reaches hundreds of metres down, and it underlies roughly a quarter of the land in the northern hemisphere. Saying only 'frozen ground' is not enough for the mark, because ground that freezes each winter and thaws each spring is not permafrost.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The clue is in the first half of the word: what has to be permanent?","A definition needs to say what the thing is and for how long the condition holds.","Decide whether your definition would also, wrongly, include a British field in January."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which an animal is adapted to survive in a cold environment. [2 marks]","a":"Credit one adaptation only. One mark for stating the adaptation, second mark for developing it into an advantage in the cold. AO2 = 2 marks.","sol":"The polar bear has a thick layer of blubber beneath its skin (1). Fat conducts heat poorly, so the blubber insulates the body core against air temperatures far below freezing and against icy water when the bear swims, and it doubles as an energy store for the months when seals are hard to catch (2). Other answers credited to the same pattern: the Arctic fox grows a dense white winter coat, which both insulates it and camouflages it against snow so it can hunt and avoid being hunted; caribou have broad hooves that spread their weight on soft snow and boggy summer ground; and many Arctic mammals have small ears and short tails, which reduces the surface area from which body heat is lost.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only one adaptation is credited, so choose the one you can take furthest.","Name a physical feature of a real named animal, then ask what problem of the cold it solves.","The second mark comes from the consequence: finish the sentence with what would happen to the animal without it."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, calculate the difference between the area protected in the Ross Sea and the area of the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer, 1,472,000 km²; one mark for an indication of the calculation, 1,550,000 − 78,000. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Read the two figures from Figure 3: the Ross Sea marine protected area is 1,550,000 km² and the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge is 78,000 km². Difference = 1,550,000 − 78,000 = 1,472,000 km². Put another way, the Ross Sea protected area is close to twenty times the size of the refuge, which is worth noticing: it is much easier to protect an ocean nobody lives beside than a coastal plain with oil under it.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both figures are already in the same units, so no conversion is needed.","A difference question wants a subtraction; identify the two rows before you touch the arithmetic.","Show the subtraction on the page, because one of the two marks is for the method."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (wilderness value)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, suggest how the climate shown makes it difficult to grow food in a cold environment. [4 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks. Answers must interpret Figure 1; no credit for generic statements about cold that cannot be read from the graph, and no credit for challenges other than food production.","sol":"Figure 1 shows a mean temperature above 0 °C in only four months of the year — June, July, August and September — and even then the warmest monthly mean is 6 °C. Above freezing is not the same as warm enough to grow: plants need a mean of roughly 6 °C before they put on new growth, and Figure 1 reaches that only in July, at 6 °C, and in August, at 5 °C — June at 2 °C and September at 1 °C are barely thawed. That leaves a growing season of roughly two months, and even in those two months the temperature is only just at the threshold, so cereals would not ripen. Frost can be expected in the other eight months, so anything sown early would be killed and the ground would be frozen hard, making ploughing and root growth impossible outside the short thaw. The precipitation bars make matters worse rather than better. The year's total is only 200 mm, and the driest month, May at 9 mm, falls exactly when a crop would need water most. Because May's mean is −4 °C, most of that precipitation arrives as snow and is locked up until it melts, so it is not available to plants when it lands. Together the graph explains why food in such a place is imported, raised in heated greenhouses, or taken from the sea rather than the land.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Everything you say has to be traceable to a value on this graph.","Work with the two axes separately: first what the temperature line rules out, then what the bars rule out.","For each point, follow it through to the crop — what actually happens to a plant under those conditions?"],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics","3.1.2.4 — Development of cold environments creates opportunities and challenges"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, explain how buildings and services in a cold environment are designed to cope with the ground conditions. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks. There must be clear evidence that the photograph has been used — the AO4 marks are for clear and effective use of it; no credit for features not described in Figure 2, and maximum Level 1 for description of the photograph without explanation.","sol":"Everything in Figure 2 is a response to ground that is frozen all year and thaws only at the surface each summer. The houses stand clear of the ground on short piles with an open gap beneath the floor. This matters because a heated building resting directly on permafrost warms the ground under it; the ice within that ground melts, the thawed soil loses its strength, and the building settles unevenly and cracks. Piles driven down into ground that stays frozen give a stable footing, while the air gap lets cold air blow through and carry the building's heat away before it reaches the soil. The insulated pipe on trestles beside the road answers the same problem in reverse. Buried water and heating pipes would either thaw the ground around them or freeze solid in winter, and a trench cut in permafrost is slow to dig and slow to heal, so services run above ground where they can be lagged, inspected and repaired. The road is unsealed gravel rather than tarmac because a thick gravel pad insulates the permafrost beneath it and can simply be re-graded when frost heave lifts the surface. Even the avalanche fencing above the roofs belongs to the same story: with no trees on the slope to anchor snow, and with warming ground loosening it, the town has had to engineer the protection the landscape does not provide.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Identify the single ground condition that every feature in the photograph is answering before you explain any of them.","Take the features one at a time and ask what would go wrong if it were built the way we build at home.","A description of the photograph on its own is capped, so make sure each feature is followed by a process."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Development of cold environments creates opportunities and challenges","3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Explain how climate, permafrost, soils and plants depend on one another in a cold environment. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Credit a logical chain of reasoning through the components; no credit for a list of characteristics with no links between them.","sol":"In a cold environment nothing can be explained on its own, because the cold holds each part in place. The climate comes first: mean temperatures below freezing for most of the year keep the ground permanently frozen, and on Svalbard that permafrost reaches hundreds of metres down. Each summer only a shallow active layer thaws, often less than a metre deep. Because the frozen ground below is impermeable, meltwater cannot drain away, so the surface becomes waterlogged and pools form even though the year's precipitation may be only about 200 mm. Waterlogged soil holds very little oxygen, which slows the bacteria and fungi that break down dead plant material, so litter accumulates as peat instead of rotting and the soil stays thin, acidic and short of nutrients. That soil, together with a growing season of only about two months and a frozen barrier beneath, is why tundra plants are low-growing, shallow-rooted and slow: mosses, lichens, cotton grass and arctic willow that creeps sideways rather than upwards. The plants in turn set the animals — lichen feeds caribou, caribou feed wolves and people — and the herds' migration stops any one patch of grazing being stripped. The interdependence shows most clearly when a link breaks. If the permafrost thaws, the water drains away, the summer pools that mosquitoes and wading birds depend on disappear, taller shrubs invade the drier ground, and because dark shrubs absorb more solar radiation than pale tundra they warm the surface further and thaw still more ground.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about links, so plan it as a chain rather than as four paragraphs.","Start with the one component that all the others depend on, and work outwards from it.","Test your chain by breaking one link and seeing whether you can say what happens next — that is what interdependence means."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, suggest why people living in a cold environment may disagree about a proposed mine. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Answers must be based on an interpretation of Figure 4; the command word is 'suggest', so any reasonable disagreement drawn from the extract may be credited.","sol":"Figure 4 shows that the mine would answer the town's biggest problem. The speaker says half the young people leave because there is no work, and the promised jobs, plus money for a school the town has waited a decade for, would keep families there and pay for services, so residents who need income now would support it strongly. Others would weigh what the mine costs the land they use. The access road crosses the valley where the caribou calve each June, and disturbance at calving time can push a herd away from ground it has used for generations; for a family that has hunted there, as the speaker's father did, that is a loss of food and identity as well as of scenery. The speaker also points to a physical process rather than an opinion: gravel pads and road dust darken and warm the surface, thawing permafrost either side of the route, so the damage spreads well beyond the road itself. Finally, the disagreement is partly about time. The jobs last only as long as the ore price holds, while the road and the thawed ground remain when the company has gone, so someone judging the mine over five years and someone judging it over fifty will reach opposite conclusions from the same evidence.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Everything you offer must come out of the extract; you are interpreting a speaker, not recalling a case study.","Sort the extract into what the town gains and what it gives up, and notice that different people gain and lose.","One sentence in the extract describes a process rather than an opinion; find it, because it turns a comment into geography."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Development of cold environments creates opportunities and challenges","3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (wilderness value)"]},{"q":"Explain how technology can reduce the environmental damage caused by development in cold environments. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Credit named technologies explained through the process they protect against; no credit for general statements that technology 'helps the environment'.","sol":"Technology works by separating the development from the ground it stands on. The clearest example is the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, about 1,300 km from Prudhoe Bay to Valdez. The oil leaves the field warm, so burying the whole line would thaw the permafrost around it, and the thawed ground would lose strength and let the pipe sag or break. Instead roughly half the route is raised on supports, those supports carrying heat pipes that draw warmth out of the ground in winter, keeping the foundations frozen. Raising the pipe also lets caribou pass beneath it rather than being cut off from a migration route. On the oilfields, extended-reach drilling steers many wells outwards underground from a single gravel pad, so one small surface site can drain a large area instead of scattering pads and access roads across the tundra. Winter ice roads, built by spraying water onto the frozen surface, carry heavy equipment and then melt in spring, leaving no permanent track on vegetation that would take decades to recover. Zero-discharge rules mean drilling waste is re-injected underground rather than left in surface pits. None of this makes development harmless — the Exxon Valdez still ran aground in Prince William Sound in 1989 and spilled around 11 million gallons of crude — and technology also makes development possible in places that would otherwise be left alone. What it does reliably is cut the area disturbed and keep the permafrost, on which everything else rests, frozen.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Pick technologies you can name and locate, not 'modern machinery'.","For each one, state the damage it is designed to prevent before you explain how it works.","A strong answer notices what technology cannot do as well as what it can."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (balancing development and conservation)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain why such large areas of cold environment have been protected. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Answers must use the areas in Figure 3 as evidence; maximum Level 1 for an answer that only describes the table.","sol":"Figure 3 shows protection on a scale found almost nowhere else: the whole of Antarctica, 14,000,000 km², is a natural reserve, and even its smallest entry, Svalbard’s parks and reserves at 40,000 km², is nearly twice the area of Wales. Areas this size are used because cold environments cannot be protected in pieces. The animals that matter most move: the caribou that calve on the refuge's coastal plain migrate hundreds of kilometres between summer and winter ranges, and the seals, whales and seabirds of the Ross Sea feed across a whole ocean, so a small reserve would only leave a gap in a journey. A second reason is the rate of recovery. With a growing season of only about two months, a vehicle track or an oil spill scars the surface for a generation, and because tundra and Southern Ocean food chains are short the loss of one species — krill, for example — passes straight up to everything above it, so protection has to prevent damage rather than repair it. A third reason is scientific: Antarctic ice cores record past atmospheres precisely because nothing has contaminated them, so here the protection and the value are the same thing. Finally, the sizes follow the politics. The two largest entries are Antarctic, where nobody lives permanently and no profitable resource has been proven, so states could agree; the Alaskan areas, enlarged and given wilderness status by Congress in 1980, sit beside working oilfields, which is why their boundaries are still argued over.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Say something about the figures in the table rather than repeating them — what do the sizes have in common?","Think about what a protected area has to contain if the species inside it move long distances.","Ask why the largest areas in the table are the ones furthest from anybody's home or livelihood."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (wilderness value)","3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (balancing development and conservation)"]},{"q":"Using a case study of a cold environment, to what extent do the opportunities for development outweigh the challenges? [9 marks]","a":"Requires a weighed judgement supported by a named cold environment, covering both opportunities and challenges; no credit for management or conservation strategies here. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Svalbard is a Norwegian archipelago in the Arctic Ocean between about 74° and 81° north: roughly 61,000 km² of land, around 60% of it under glacier ice. In Longyearbyen, the main settlement, about 2,400 people live where the mean monthly temperature rises above freezing in only about four months of the year and the annual precipitation total is about 200 mm, as little as a desert. The opportunities have nevertheless been real. Coal drew people here first: Longyearbyen was founded as a mining town in 1906, and the Russian settlement of Barentsburg still mines coal today. The Barents Sea around the islands holds the world's largest cod stock, and the fishing and processing it supports reach far beyond the archipelago. Tourism has grown quickly, and it is built on exactly what makes the place hostile — glaciers, polar bears, the midnight sun from late April to late August and the polar night from late October to mid-February. Science is an opportunity of the same kind: the Global Seed Vault, opened in 2008 and holding over a million seed samples inside the permafrost, exists because cold and stability are useful.\n\nThe challenges are equally real, and each one raises the cost of an opportunity. Permafrost hundreds of metres thick means buildings must stand on piles driven into frozen ground with an air gap beneath, so that a heated house does not thaw its own foundations, and water and heating pipes run above ground in insulated culverts rather than in trenches. Inaccessibility means almost everything arrives by ship or aircraft, which puts a freight cost on every item. Extreme cold and months of darkness limit outdoor work, and serious healthcare is a flight away. Climate change sharpens all of it: the Arctic has warmed roughly three to four times as fast as the world as a whole, thawing ground destabilises foundations, and in December 2015 an avalanche onto houses in Longyearbyen killed two people and forced the town to build defences and demolish homes.\n\nOn balance the opportunities outweigh the challenges only where the resource is valuable enough to pay for beating the cold. Coal no longer is, and Norway has wound its Svalbard mining down. Tourism and research still are, because they sell the environment itself rather than fight it, and because modern building and transport technology have cut the price of surviving there. The extent to which a cold environment is worth developing therefore depends less on the environment, which has not changed, than on what the world is currently willing to pay for what it holds.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The command phrase asks how far, so the answer has to end up somewhere rather than balancing neatly and stopping.","Plan two developed paragraphs — what the place offers and what it charges — and make sure every point is located in your named case study.","Look for a way of deciding between them: what has to be true of a resource before anyone pays the cost of extracting it in the Arctic?"],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Development of cold environments creates opportunities and challenges","3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics"]},{"q":"'Cold environments are more valuable as wilderness than as a source of resources.' To what extent do you agree? [9 marks]","a":"Requires both sides — the scientific, climatic, ecological and cultural value of wilderness against the economic value of resources — and a substantiated position; credit reference to the people who live in cold environments. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Wilderness means land that has been essentially unaltered by human activity, and the polar regions hold the largest areas of it left on Earth. Their value begins with science. Antarctica holds around 90% of the world's ice, and cores drilled from that ice record the composition of the atmosphere going back hundreds of thousands of years — a record that exists nowhere else and that would be worthless if the continent were industrialised. Cold environments also regulate the climate for everybody: bright ice and snow reflect solar radiation back to space, while frozen ground stores carbon estimated at roughly twice the amount now in the atmosphere. Thaw it and that carbon begins to escape as carbon dioxide and methane, warming the planet further and thawing more ground. There is an ecological argument too. Tundra and Southern Ocean food chains are short, so the loss of one species ripples upward almost immediately; plants grow for only about two months a year, so a vehicle track can still be visible decades later; and that same slowness means damage cannot be repaired within a human lifetime.\n\nAgainst this stands the case for resources, which is not trivial. Prudhoe Bay in northern Alaska, discovered in 1968, has produced more than 13 billion barrels of oil, and the tax and royalty income has paid for schools, clinics and airstrips across the North Slope as well as the Alaska Permanent Fund, created in 1976, which still pays an annual dividend to residents. Alaska's fisheries supply well over half the fish caught in United States waters by weight. For the roughly four million people who live in the Arctic, including Iñupiat, Inuit and Sámi communities, 'wilderness' is a home and a workplace, and a blanket ban on development can mean no work at all and young people leaving.\n\nThe honest judgement is that the two values are not equal everywhere, so the statement holds in some places and not in others. Antarctica has no permanent population and unique scientific value, and it is rightly treated as a natural reserve under the Madrid Protocol, in force since 1998, which bans mineral exploitation outright. The Arctic is different, because people live there and their livelihoods depend on what the land and sea provide. I therefore agree to a considerable extent, but with a condition: wilderness value should decide wherever the environment is irreplaceable and nobody's livelihood is at stake, while in inhabited Arctic regions the real argument is not whether development happens but how, and who decides.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Decide early what the word wilderness actually means, because the whole judgement turns on it.","Give the value of wilderness under separate headings — what it teaches, what it regulates, what lives in it, who belongs to it — so the argument has structure.","Look for a way to agree in one place and disagree in another rather than choosing a side for the whole Arctic and Antarctic at once."],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (wilderness value)","3.1.2.4 — Development of cold environments creates opportunities and challenges"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of the strategies used to balance the needs of economic development and conservation in cold environments. [9 marks]","a":"Requires a balanced appreciation of the advantages and limitations of named strategies — technology, governments, international agreements and conservation groups — and a substantiated overall judgement; no credit for simply describing the problems. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Four kinds of strategy are used, and none of them works alone. Technology reduces the footprint of development rather than preventing it. The Trans-Alaska Pipeline, completed in 1977 and about 1,300 km long from Prudhoe Bay to Valdez, carries oil warm enough to thaw permafrost, so roughly half its length is raised on supports fitted with heat pipes that draw warmth out of the ground in winter, and it is lifted high enough in places for caribou to pass beneath. Elsewhere, ice roads melt in spring and leave no permanent scar, and extended-reach drilling works many wells from a single gravel pad. This is genuinely effective at limiting local damage, but it cuts both ways: it also makes development possible where it would otherwise be uneconomic, and it did not prevent the Exxon Valdez running aground in Prince William Sound in 1989 and spilling around 11 million gallons of crude.\n\nGovernments can protect land outright. The Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act of 1980 placed roughly 420,000 km² into national parks, refuges and wilderness, and Norway has protected around two-thirds of Svalbard's land as national parks and nature reserves. This is the strongest protection available, but it is only as durable as the politics that made it: the coastal plain of the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge, the calving ground of a caribou herd of roughly 200,000 animals, has been opened to oil leasing and closed again more than once.\n\nInternational agreements bind several states at once. The Antarctic Treaty, signed in 1959 and in force from 1961, reserves the continent south of 60° south for peaceful scientific use and freezes territorial claims; its Madrid Protocol makes Antarctica a natural reserve, bans mining and cannot be reviewed before 2048. The Ross Sea marine protected area, in force from 2017 and covering about 1.55 million km², was the largest marine protected area in the world when it was created. These work because every party has to agree — but that same rule means a single objecting state can block the next step, and the Arctic, unlike Antarctica, has eight sovereign owners and no equivalent treaty.\n\nConservation groups fill the gaps. IAATO, formed by tour operators in 1991, limits Antarctic landings to a hundred visitors ashore at a time, while WWF and Greenpeace campaign and monitor without any power to enforce. Overall the strategies are most effective where nobody lives and no profit has been proven, as in Antarctica, and weakest where valuable resources lie under inhabited land. They manage development; they do not stop it, and that is the honest measure of their success.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Evaluate means judging how well each strategy works, with evidence on both sides — describing the strategies will not be enough.","Group the strategies the way the specification does, and give each group one clear strength and one clear limitation.","Find the pattern across your four groups: what do the strategies that work best have in common?"],"codes":["3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (balancing development and conservation)","3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (wilderness value)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How long must ground stay at or below 0 °C to count as permafrost?","a":"At least two consecutive years."},{"q":"Name four kinds of development opportunity found in cold environments.","a":"Mineral extraction, energy, fishing and tourism."},{"q":"In which year was the Antarctic Treaty signed, and what does the Madrid Protocol ban?","a":"Signed in 1959 (in force 1961); the Madrid Protocol bans mining, and the ban cannot be reviewed before 2048."},{"q":"Why are buildings in Longyearbyen raised on piles with a gap beneath the floor?","a":"So the building's heat cannot thaw the permafrost beneath it; thawed ground loses strength and the building would sink unevenly."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Permafrost","back":"Ground — soil, sediment or rock — that stays at or below 0 °C for at least two consecutive years. Hundreds of metres thick on Svalbard; impermeable, so summer meltwater cannot drain through it.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"The active layer","back":"The shallow surface layer above permafrost that thaws each summer and refreezes each winter, often under a metre deep. It is where all plant roots, soil water and summer waterlogging occur.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"The tundra climate","back":"Winter monthly means below −10 °C, a growing season of only about two months, and precipitation often under 250 mm a year, mostly snow — a cold desert. Annual temperature range 20 °C on Svalbard, 30–40 °C in continental tundra.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Antarctica in figures","back":"About 14 million km², roughly 98% ice-covered, holding around 90% of the world's ice. Lowest natural temperature recorded on Earth: −89.2 °C at Vostok, July 1983. No permanent population.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"Why tundra soils are thin and poor","back":"Impermeable permafrost stops meltwater draining, so the surface waterlogs; waterlogged soil lacks oxygen, so decomposers work slowly; litter builds up as acidic peat instead of rotting, releasing few nutrients.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":false},{"front":"A plant adaptation to the tundra","back":"Low, cushion-like growth that keeps the plant below the worst of the wind chill, as the arctic willow creeps sideways rather than upwards; shallow spreading roots because permafrost blocks depth; small, dark, waxy leaves that absorb heat and limit water loss.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":true},{"front":"An animal adaptation to cold environments","back":"Polar bear blubber and hollow guard hairs for insulation; Arctic fox white winter coat for camouflage and warmth; small ears and short tails reducing the surface area that loses heat.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments (polar and tundra) have a range of distinctive characteristics","open":true},{"front":"Svalbard — the case study in one card","back":"Norwegian Arctic archipelago, about 74°–81° N, roughly 61,000 km², around 60% glacier. Longyearbyen founded as a coal town in 1906, population about 2,400; Barentsburg is the Russian mining settlement.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Development of cold environments creates opportunities and challenges","open":false},{"front":"The four development opportunities in cold environments","back":"Mineral extraction (coal on Svalbard), energy (Prudhoe Bay oil, Alaska), fishing (Barents Sea cod; Alaska lands over half of United States catch by weight) and tourism (glaciers, wildlife, midnight sun).","code":"3.1.2.4 — Development of cold environments creates opportunities and challenges","open":false},{"front":"Building on permafrost","back":"Piles driven into permanently frozen ground, with an air gap under the floor so the building's heat cannot thaw its own foundations; services in insulated pipes above ground; gravel pads insulating roads against frost heave.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Development of cold environments creates opportunities and challenges","open":false},{"front":"The Trans-Alaska Pipeline","back":"About 1,300 km, Prudhoe Bay to Valdez, opened 1977. Warm oil would thaw permafrost, so roughly half is raised on supports with heat pipes keeping foundations frozen, and lifted for caribou to cross.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (balancing development and conservation)","open":false},{"front":"Why cold environments are fragile","back":"A growing season of about two months, so a vehicle track shows for decades; short food chains, so one loss passes straight up; thawed permafrost releases stored carbon and does not refreeze into what it was.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (wilderness value)","open":false},{"front":"The Antarctic Treaty and the Madrid Protocol","back":"Treaty signed 1959, in force 1961, covering everything south of 60° S: peaceful scientific use only, claims frozen. Madrid Protocol, in force 1998, makes Antarctica a natural reserve and bans mining; not reviewable before 2048.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (balancing development and conservation)","open":false},{"front":"Protected areas in the Arctic","back":"Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act 1980: about 420,000 km² of parks, refuges and wilderness. The Arctic National Wildlife Refuge covers about 78,000 km². Roughly two-thirds of Svalbard is national park or nature reserve.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (wilderness value)","open":false},{"front":"A strategy that balances development and conservation","back":"Technology: ice roads that melt in spring, or extended-reach drilling from a single gravel pad; government protection: national parks, refuges and nature reserves, as in Alaska and on Svalbard; international agreements: the Antarctic Treaty, the Madrid Protocol's mining ban and the Ross Sea marine protected area.","code":"3.1.2.4 — Cold environments are at risk from economic development (balancing development and conservation)","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Climate graph written for this room for a high Arctic station of the kind found on Svalbard (about 78° north). The plotted values are illustrative: they are chosen to match the published pattern for the high Arctic — winter monthly means well below −10 °C, a July mean of about 6 °C and an annual precipitation total of about 200 mm — but they are not a measured record. Horizontal axis: the twelve months, January to December. Left vertical axis: monthly precipitation in millimetres, scaled 0 to 40, plotted as bars. Right vertical axis: mean monthly temperature in °C, scaled −20 to +10, plotted as a line.\nMonth | Precipitation (mm) | Mean temperature (°C)\nJanuary | 18 | −13\nFebruary | 20 | −14\nMarch | 19 | −14\nApril | 13 | −11\nMay | 9 | −4\nJune | 10 | 2\nJuly | 16 | 6\nAugust | 21 | 5\nSeptember | 22 | 1\nOctober | 20 | −4\nNovember | 16 | −8\nDecember | 16 | −11","Figure 2 — Photograph described for this room: the edge of Longyearbyen, Svalbard, in late winter. In the foreground a row of brightly painted wooden houses stands clear of the ground on short steel and timber piles, with an open gap of roughly half a metre between the floor and the frozen gravel beneath. A silver insulated pipe about a metre across runs beside the road on low trestles, carrying water and heating, and turns to enter each block above floor level rather than from below. The road is unsealed gravel, banked on both sides with ploughed snow, and snowmobiles are parked outside the houses. Behind the town a steep mountainside rises, with a line of metal snow and avalanche fencing bolted across it above the roofs. On the skyline to the right stand two of the old wooden pylons that once carried buckets of coal on a cableway down from the mine to the harbour. There are no trees anywhere in the picture.","Figure 3 — Data table written for this room. The areas are real figures, rounded, for land and sea protected in and around cold environments.\nProtected area | How it was protected | Area (km²)\nAntarctica (whole continent) | Madrid Protocol, in force 1998 — a natural reserve devoted to peace and science | 14,000,000\nRoss Sea region, Antarctica | Marine protected area, in force 2017 | 1,550,000\nAlaska parks, refuges and wilderness | Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act, 1980 | 420,000\nArctic National Wildlife Refuge, Alaska | United States national wildlife refuge, established 1960 and enlarged in 1980 | 78,000\nSvalbard, Norway | Norwegian national parks and nature reserves | 40,000","Figure 4 — Text extract written for this room. The speaker is illustrative; the situation and the processes described are real. A council member speaks at a public meeting in an Arctic town about a proposed mine: 'The company says the mine will bring a hundred jobs and pay for the school we have been promised for a decade. Half the young people in this town leave because there is no work for them here. But the access road crosses the valley where the caribou calve every June, and the gravel pads and the dust from the trucks will thaw the ground either side of it. My father hunted along that valley. And if the ore price falls, the company will go — while the road, and the thawed ground, will still be here.'"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — Svalbard, Norway: a cold environment developed","items":["Location: a Norwegian archipelago in the Arctic Ocean between roughly 74° and 81° north, about midway between mainland Norway and the North Pole.","Size: roughly 61,000 km² of land, around 60% of it covered by glacier ice; the rest is tundra and bare rock.","Sovereignty: Norwegian, under the Svalbard Treaty signed in Paris in 1920, which gives Norway sovereignty while citizens of the signatory states have equal rights to carry on commercial activity there.","Main settlement: Longyearbyen, founded as a coal-mining town in 1906 and named after the American businessman John Munro Longyear; population about 2,400.","Other settlements: Barentsburg, a Russian coal-mining settlement of a few hundred people, and Ny-Ålesund, an international research station.","Climate: mean monthly temperature above freezing in only about four months of the year, a July mean around 6 °C, winter means well below −10 °C, and annual precipitation of roughly 200 mm — as dry as a desert.","Light: midnight sun from late April to late August; polar night, when the sun does not rise at all, from late October to mid-February.","Opportunity — mineral extraction: coal has been mined since the early twentieth century; Norwegian mining has been progressively wound down, while Russian mining continues at Barentsburg.","Opportunity — energy: coal supplied Longyearbyen's own power for decades; Norway's wider Arctic offshore gas shows what the region holds.","Opportunity — fishing: the Barents Sea around Svalbard holds the world's largest stock of cod, alongside haddock, shrimp and capelin.","Opportunity — tourism: cruise ships, snowmobile and dog-sled trips and glacier walks, sold on exactly what makes the archipelago hostile — ice, polar bears, the midnight sun and the polar night.","Opportunity — science: the University Centre in Svalbard teaches Arctic science at Longyearbyen, and the Global Seed Vault, opened in 2008, stores over a million seed samples inside the permafrost.","Challenge — extreme temperature: outdoor work is limited, heating costs are high, exposure can kill, and serious medical care is a flight away on the Norwegian mainland.","Challenge — inaccessibility: there are no roads between settlements; almost all freight arrives by ship or by air into Svalbard Airport at Longyearbyen, adding cost to everything.","Challenge — buildings and infrastructure: permafrost hundreds of metres thick means houses stand on piles with an air gap beneath, water and heating pipes run above ground in insulated culverts, and roads are gravel rather than tarmac.","Challenge — climate change: the Arctic has warmed roughly three to four times as fast as the world as a whole, thawing ground and destabilising foundations.","Challenge — slope hazard: in December 2015 an avalanche off the mountainside swept into houses in Longyearbyen and killed two people; the town has since built avalanche defences and removed the most exposed housing.","Conservation: roughly two-thirds of Svalbard's land area is protected as national parks and nature reserves, and polar bears have been protected from hunting there since 1973.","Wildlife: the Svalbard reindeer is a small, short-legged subspecies found nowhere else; there are also Arctic foxes, polar bears and vast summer seabird colonies, but no trees and no indigenous population."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Alaska's North Slope: the same opportunities and challenges at a larger scale","items":["Alaska is about 1.7 million km² with a population of roughly 733,000, so there is well under one person per square kilometre — most communities are not on the road network at all and are reached by plane or boat.","Prudhoe Bay, on the Arctic coast of the North Slope, was discovered in 1968 and is the largest oil field in North America, having produced more than 13 billion barrels.","The Trans-Alaska Pipeline System runs about 1,300 km from Prudhoe Bay south to the ice-free port of Valdez; it was completed in 1977, and throughput peaked at around 2 million barrels a day in 1988.","Roughly half the pipeline is raised above ground on supports, because the oil inside it is warm and would otherwise thaw the permafrost; the supports carry heat pipes that draw warmth out of the ground in winter.","The pipeline is lifted clear of the tundra in places so that migrating caribou can pass underneath it, and buried in refrigerated sections at some crossings.","The Dalton Highway, 666 km of mostly gravel road, was built alongside the pipeline in 1974 and remains the only road link to the Arctic coast.","Fishing: Alaska lands well over half of all the fish caught in United States waters by weight, dominated by Bering Sea pollock, together with salmon, cod and crab.","Tourism: more than a million visitors a year, many on cruise ships, come for glaciers, Denali and wildlife; the season is short and concentrated into the summer.","Extreme temperature: the lowest temperature recorded in Alaska is −62 °C, at Prospect Creek in January 1971; winter temperatures on the North Slope routinely fall below −30 °C.","Indigenous people: Iñupiat communities live along the Arctic coast, with a subsistence economy that includes bowhead whaling under an international quota.","The Alaska Native Claims Settlement Act of 1971 transferred about 44 million acres of land and just under a billion dollars to Alaska Native corporations, in exchange for extinguishing older land claims — clearing the way for the pipeline.","The Alaska Permanent Fund, established in 1976, invests a share of oil royalties and pays an annual dividend to every qualifying resident, so oil money reaches households directly.","The Exxon Valdez ran aground in Prince William Sound in March 1989 and spilled around 11 million gallons of crude oil, killing enormous numbers of seabirds, sea otters and seals.","The Arctic National Wildlife Refuge covers about 78,000 km² of north-eastern Alaska; its coastal plain is the calving ground of the Porcupine caribou herd of roughly 200,000 animals, and it has been opened to oil leasing and closed again repeatedly.","The Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act of 1980 protected roughly 420,000 km² as national parks, wildlife refuges and wilderness — the largest single act of land protection in United States history."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — protecting cold environments: the Antarctic Treaty system and Arctic conservation","items":["Antarctic Treaty: signed in Washington on 1 December 1959 by twelve states and in force from 1961; more than fifty countries are now party to it.","The Treaty applies to everything south of 60° south latitude.","Article I reserves Antarctica for peaceful purposes only and prohibits military bases, manoeuvres and weapons testing there.","Article II guarantees freedom of scientific investigation and Article III requires the results to be exchanged freely.","Article IV freezes all territorial claims: existing claims are neither recognised nor denied, and no new claim may be made while the Treaty is in force.","Article V prohibits nuclear explosions and the disposal of radioactive waste in Antarctica.","The Protocol on Environmental Protection (the Madrid Protocol), agreed in 1991 and in force since 1998, designates Antarctica a natural reserve devoted to peace and science and prohibits any mineral resource activity other than scientific research; the ban cannot be reviewed before 2048.","CCAMLR, the Convention on the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources, manages fishing in the Southern Ocean, including the krill fishery on which whales, seals and penguins depend.","The Ross Sea region marine protected area, agreed in 2016 and in force from December 2017, covers about 1.55 million km² and was the largest marine protected area in the world when it was created.","IAATO, the International Association of Antarctica Tour Operators, was formed in 1991 and sets rules its members follow: no more than a hundred passengers ashore at any one site at a time, minimum distances from wildlife, and boot-washing to stop non-native species arriving.","Antarctic tourism has grown fast — more than 100,000 visitors in a recent season — which is why self-regulation by operators matters alongside the Treaty.","The Arctic has no equivalent treaty because it is an ocean surrounded by eight sovereign states; instead the Arctic Council, established in 1996, brings those eight states together with organisations representing indigenous peoples.","The 1973 Agreement on the Conservation of Polar Bears, signed in Oslo by the five polar bear states, ended unregulated hunting; Norway banned polar bear hunting on Svalbard in the same year.","United States protection in Alaska rests on Congress: the Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act of 1980 created or enlarged parks, refuges and wilderness covering roughly 420,000 km².","Conservation groups such as WWF and Greenpeace, and the Antarctic and Southern Ocean Coalition, campaign, monitor and lobby, but have no power to enforce — their influence works through publicity and through governments."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — cold environment processes, terms and figures","items":["Polar environment: permanently covered by ice and snow, with no growing season; Antarctica and the interior of Greenland are the examples.","Tundra: the treeless zone around the edges of the Arctic Ocean, with a growing season of about two months, permafrost beneath and low, slow-growing vegetation.","Permafrost: ground that remains at or below 0 °C for at least two consecutive years; it underlies roughly a quarter of the land in the northern hemisphere.","Active layer: the shallow surface layer above permafrost that thaws each summer and refreezes each winter, usually less than a metre thick.","Why the tundra waterlogs: permafrost is impermeable, so meltwater from the active layer cannot drain downwards and ponds on the surface, despite an annual precipitation total that may be under 250 mm.","Why the soils are poor: waterlogging starves the soil of oxygen and the cold slows bacteria and fungi, so dead plant material accumulates as acidic peat rather than decomposing and releasing nutrients.","Interdependence in one sentence: climate makes the permafrost, permafrost makes the waterlogging, waterlogging makes the soil, the soil and the short summer make the plants, and the plants make the animals and the people.","Plant adaptations: low cushion growth below the wind, shallow spreading roots because permafrost blocks depth, small dark waxy leaves that absorb heat and limit water loss, and rapid flowering in the brief summer.","Animal adaptations: blubber and dense fur for insulation, white winter coats for camouflage, small ears and short tails to reduce heat loss, broad feet for snow, hibernation and long-distance migration.","Biodiversity: species numbers are low and food chains are short, so the loss of one species — Antarctic krill, for example — passes almost immediately to everything that feeds on it.","Slow recovery: with so short a growing season, tyre tracks across tundra can still be visible decades later, which is why prevention matters more than repair.","Carbon: frozen ground holds an enormous store of organic carbon, estimated at roughly twice the amount currently in the atmosphere; thawing releases it as carbon dioxide and methane, which warms the climate further.","Albedo feedback: ice and snow reflect most of the solar radiation that falls on them, so when they melt the darker land or sea beneath absorbs more energy and warming accelerates.","Rate of change: the Arctic has warmed roughly three to four times as fast as the global average, and the September extent of Arctic sea ice has been falling by around 13% per decade.","Wilderness: an area essentially unaltered by human activity, valued for science, for climate regulation, for its ecology and for its own sake — and, in the Arctic, lived in and used by the people whose home it is."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) on 'Using a case study of a cold environment, to what extent do the opportunities for development outweigh the challenges? [9 marks]'","items":["Point: on Svalbard the opportunities have only ever outweighed the challenges while the resource was valuable enough to pay for beating the cold.","Named evidence: Longyearbyen was founded as a coal town in 1906 and about 2,400 people now live there, at roughly 78° north, where the mean monthly temperature is above freezing in only four months of the year and the annual precipitation is about 200 mm; the Global Seed Vault opened there in 2008 and tourism sells glaciers, polar bears and the polar night.","Developed explanation: every opportunity carries the same bill. Because permafrost runs hundreds of metres down, a heated building resting on it would thaw the ice within the ground, the soil would lose strength and the building would settle unevenly — so houses stand on piles with an air gap beneath and pipes run above ground in insulated culverts, all of which costs money before a single tonne of coal is shipped. Inaccessibility adds freight to every item because there is no road to the mainland, and the Arctic is warming roughly three to four times as fast as the world as a whole, which destabilises the ground the town is built on; the avalanche that killed two people in Longyearbyen in December 2015 forced the town to build defences and demolish homes.","Link to the question: the question asks how far, and Svalbard answers it by changing its mind. Norway has wound its coal mining down, while tourism and research have grown, because those industries sell the cold rather than fight it and so pay the bill more easily.","Mini-judgement: the opportunities therefore outweigh the challenges only to a limited and shifting extent — the environment has not changed, but what the world will pay for what it holds has, and that is what decides."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–3) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The weaker paragraph: 'There are lots of opportunities in cold environments like mining, energy, fishing and tourism. Mining gives people jobs and tourism brings money into the area. But there are also challenges because it is very cold and hard to get to, and it is difficult to build things. Overall I think the opportunities and the challenges are both important.'","Which descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic). It 'demonstrates very limited knowledge of locations, places and processes in relation to a cold environment' — the four opportunities are simply the specification's own list, no place is named, and there is not a single figure or date.","Which descriptor it misses: Level 3 wants 'comprehensive and accurate knowledge of locations, places and processes' and 'thorough application of knowledge and understanding in evaluating the extent to which a cold environment provides opportunities and challenges'. Here the challenges are adjectives — 'very cold', 'hard to get to' — with no process behind them; nothing explains why cold makes building difficult, so the interrelationships AO2 rewards are absent.","Why the ending costs the most: 'both important' is the fence, not a judgement. 'To what extent' requires the answer to place itself and say on what the decision turns; the specialist vocabulary an examiner is listening for — permafrost, active layer, multiplier effect, inaccessibility — never appears either, so the AO1 knowledge marks go begging as well."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Evaluate the effectiveness of the strategies used to balance the needs of economic development and conservation in cold environments. [9 marks]'","items":["Decode the command: 'evaluate' means judging how well the strategies work with the evidence on both sides, so every strategy named must be followed by a strength and a limitation. Describing the strategies, or describing the problems they address, earns nothing on its own.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly two minutes planning and eight writing; four short evaluated groups beat eight strategies listed.","Paragraph 1 — technology: the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, about 1,300 km, completed in 1977, raised on supports for roughly half its length with heat pipes keeping the ground frozen and gaps for caribou; ice roads that melt in spring; extended-reach drilling from one pad. Strength: the disturbed area is genuinely small. Limitation: it makes development viable where it otherwise would not be, and it did not stop the Exxon Valdez spilling around 11 million gallons in 1989.","Paragraph 2 — governments: the Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act of 1980, roughly 420,000 km²; roughly two-thirds of Svalbard protected as parks and reserves. Strength: this is the only strategy that can simply forbid development. Limitation: it lasts only as long as the politics, as the repeated reopening and closing of the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge coastal plain, calving ground for about 200,000 caribou, shows.","Paragraph 3 — international agreements: the Antarctic Treaty of 1959, in force 1961, south of 60° south; the Madrid Protocol's mining ban, in force 1998 and not reviewable before 2048; the Ross Sea marine protected area of about 1.55 million km², in force 2017; the Arctic Council since 1996. Strength: they bind many states at once and have held for over sixty years. Limitation: consensus means one objector can block the next step, and the Arctic has eight sovereign owners and no equivalent treaty.","Paragraph 4 — conservation groups and industry bodies: IAATO's limit of a hundred visitors ashore at a time since 1991; WWF and Greenpeace campaigning and monitoring. Strength: they act where governments will not and they shape public opinion. Limitation: they cannot enforce anything.","The evaluation thread that runs through all four: for every strategy ask who is bound by it, who is not, and what happens when the price of the resource rises — that single test separates Antarctica, where nobody lives and nothing profitable has been proven, from the inhabited Arctic.","What the conclusion must say: that the strategies are most effective where there is no resident population and no immediate profit, and weakest where valuable resources sit under inhabited land; that together they manage development rather than prevent it; and that the most durable protection so far is the Antarctic mining ban, precisely because it was agreed before anyone had a reason to break it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:59:25Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Factually this room stands up to everything I could test — Svalbard (61,000 km², about 60% glacier, Longyearbyen 1906 and about 2,400 people, the 1920 Paris treaty, the Seed Vault 2008, the December 2015 avalanche), Alaska (Prudhoe Bay 1968 and over 13 billion barrels, TAPS about 1,300 km completed 1977 and half elevated, the Dalton Highway 666 km from 1974, −62 °C at Prospect Creek in January 1971, ANCSA 1971 with 44 million acres, the Permanent Fund 1976, Exxon Valdez 1989 and about 11 million gallons, ANWR 78,000 km² and roughly 200,000 Porcupine caribou, ANILCA 1980 and 420,000 km²) and the protection system (Antarctic Treaty 1959/1961 and 60° S, Madrid Protocol 1991/1998 and 2048, Ross Sea 1.55 million km² from 2017, Arctic Council 1996, IAATO 1991 and 100 ashore, the 1973 polar bear agreement) are all correct, every countable claim recounts against the printed resources (Figure 1's twelve bars total exactly 200 mm, 6 − (−14) = 20 °C, 22 − 9 = 13 mm, four months above freezing; Figure 3's 1,550,000 − 78,000 = 1,472,000 km² and the near-twentyfold ratio; 40,000 km² is indeed nearly twice Wales), and all fifteen answer keys re-solve correctly — publish it once the three 9-markers stop claiming SPaG marks that Paper 1 puts on the natural hazards question, question 7's AO split gives the photograph mark to AO4, and the small wording slips in questions 10, 11 and 14 are fixed."}}},"3.1.3.1":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.3.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section C of Paper 1 sets its coastal, river and glacial questions somewhere real, and the examiner expects you to know where that somewhere is. This room is the map every later room is drawn on: which parts of the UK are upland and which are lowland, what rock and what ice put them there, and where the major rivers rise, which way they run and how they reach the sea. Locational knowledge is AO1, and it is the cheapest set of marks on the paper to lose. A two-mark 'describe the distribution' question is won by naming the pattern and then proving it from the figure, never by listing places; a nine-mark answer that says 'in the north' scores less than one that says 'in the Grampian Mountains of Scotland, where Ben Nevis reaches 1,345 m'. Learn the map properly now and every landform explanation you write later has somewhere to stand.","idea":[{"h":"Upland Britain: where the high ground is, and what put it there","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","t":"Geographers usually take upland to mean land above about 200 m, and in the UK almost all of it lies north and west of a line drawn from the mouth of the River Tees to the mouth of the River Exe. Scotland holds the highest ground: the Northwest Highlands, the Grampian Mountains with Ben Nevis at 1,345 m and the Cairngorms behind it, and the Southern Uplands, with the Central Lowlands between them. England's uplands run mainly down its western and northern side: the Cheviot Hills on the border, the Pennines from the Cheviots south through the Yorkshire Dales to the Peak District with Cross Fell at 893 m, the Lake District with Scafell Pike at 978 m, and Exmoor and Dartmoor in the south-west where High Willhays reaches 621 m; the clear outlier is the North York Moors, an upland block in the north-east, east of the Pennines and east of the Tees–Exe line. Wales has Snowdonia in the north, where Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon) reaches 1,085 m, the Cambrian Mountains in the middle and the Brecon Beacons in the south with Pen y Fan at 886 m; Northern Ireland has the Sperrin Mountains and the Mourne Mountains, where Slieve Donard reaches 850 m. The pattern is not an accident. Upland Britain is built of the oldest and hardest rock in the country — Lewisian gneiss in the Northwest Highlands, formed around 3,000 million years ago, granite in the Cairngorms and on Dartmoor, volcanic rock in Snowdonia and the Lake District, and Carboniferous limestone, gritstone and coal measures in the Pennines. That rock was raised and folded by ancient mountain building, then sharpened by ice: at the height of the last ice age, roughly 20,000 years ago, an ice sheet covered Scotland, Northern Ireland, Wales and northern England, cutting the corries, arêtes and deep valleys that give the uplands their outline today. Steep slopes, thin acid soils and heavy relief rainfall of over 2,000 mm a year in parts of the Lake District and Snowdonia follow from that, which is why upland land use is moorland, rough grazing, forestry, reservoirs and national parks rather than arable farming. A top-level answer never says 'the north is hilly': it names the upland area, gives its highest point, and says which rock and which process made it high."},{"h":"Lowland Britain: the plains, vales and basins of the south and east","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","t":"Lowland Britain is the larger share of the country: land generally below about 200 m, lying mainly south and east of the Tees–Exe line. The main areas to be able to place are the Central Lowlands of Scotland, the lowland belt holding Glasgow and Edinburgh between the Highland Boundary Fault and the Southern Upland Fault; the Vale of York; the Lancashire and Cheshire plains; the Midlands; East Anglia and the Fens around the Wash; the Somerset Levels; and the London and Hampshire Basins. Some of it is very low indeed — much of the Fens lies at or near sea level, and Holme Fen in Cambridgeshire, about 3 m below sea level, is the lowest land in Great Britain. Lowland does not mean flat. Bands of more resistant chalk and limestone stand out as escarpments within it: the Cotswolds, which rise to a little over 300 m, the Chilterns, the North and South Downs, and the Lincolnshire and Yorkshire Wolds. The rock underneath is younger and weaker than the rock of the uplands — chalk, clay, sandstone and younger limestone, with the chalk of the Downs and the Chilterns laid down under a warm sea roughly 70 to 100 million years ago — so it has been worn down faster and more evenly. Being lower and further east, the lowlands lie in the rain shadow of the uplands, with under 700 mm of rain a year in the driest parts of eastern England, and they carry deeper, richer soils. That combination of gentle relief, workable soil and a drier climate is why the lowlands hold most of the UK's arable farming, its largest cities and its densest transport networks, and why lowland rivers are the ones with wide floodplains and flood risk to manage. A top-level answer uses the lowlands as the other half of a comparison: it pairs a named lowland with a named upland and explains the difference through rock, relief and rainfall rather than simply asserting it."},{"h":"The UK's major river systems: sources in the hills, estuaries on the coast","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)","t":"The rivers you must be able to place are few and they repeat. The Severn is the UK's longest at about 354 km: it rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains of mid Wales, swings east and north past Shrewsbury, then south through Worcester and Gloucester into the Severn Estuary and the Bristol Channel, which has one of the largest tidal ranges in the world. The Thames, about 346 km, is the longest river entirely in England; it rises in the Cotswolds and runs east through Oxford, Reading and London to the Thames Estuary and the North Sea. The Trent, about 298 km, rises in the Staffordshire moorlands at the southern end of the Pennines and joins the Yorkshire Ouse to form the Humber Estuary. The Tees rises on Cross Fell and drops over High Force, a waterfall of about 21 m in Upper Teesdale, before reaching the North Sea at Middlesbrough; the Tyne is formed where the North Tyne and South Tyne meet near Hexham and reaches the sea past Newcastle. West of the Pennine watershed the rivers are shorter: the Eden runs north-west through Carlisle to the Solway Firth and the Mersey west past Manchester to Liverpool and the Irish Sea. In Scotland the Tay is the longest river, about 188 km, and carries the largest discharge of any river in the UK, reaching the sea through the Firth of Tay; the Clyde rises in the Southern Uplands and flows through Glasgow into the Firth of Clyde, and the Spey runs north-east to the Moray Firth. The Great Ouse and the Nene cross East Anglia to the Wash, the Exe rises on Exmoor and flows through Exeter to the English Channel at Exmouth, and in Northern Ireland the Upper Bann flows into Lough Neagh, about 380 km² and the largest lake in the British Isles, while the Lower Bann carries the water north to the sea near Coleraine. Learn the vocabulary with the places: source, tributary, confluence, drainage basin, watershed, mouth and estuary. A top-level answer names a river and then uses it, quoting where it rises and where it ends to support the point it is making."},{"h":"Reading the pattern: how relief and drainage fit together","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)","t":"The two halves of this room are one idea. Rivers rise where rain is heaviest and where the ground cannot absorb it, which in the UK means the uplands: heavy relief rainfall falls on hard, impermeable rock under thin soils, so water runs off quickly and streams gather at height. That is why the Severn and the Wye both begin on Plynlimon, the Tees on Cross Fell and the Clyde in the Southern Uplands. Where a river then goes is decided by the watershed. The main watershed of England lies along the Pennines, much closer to the west coast than the east, so east-flowing rivers such as the Tyne, the Tees, the Yorkshire Ouse and the Trent have far more land to cross than west-flowing rivers such as the Eden, the Ribble and the Mersey, and are correspondingly longer. The two halves also explain the shape of a river's course: steep upper courses in the uplands cut V-shaped valleys, gorges and waterfalls, while lower courses crossing the lowlands lose gradient, gain discharge from tributaries, and meander across wide floodplains before opening into estuaries — the Severn, the Thames, the Humber, the Mersey, the Clyde and the Firth of Forth. Rock type shows through here too: chalk is permeable, so the Downs and the Chilterns carry few surface streams and their rivers are fed by springs, while gritstone moorland sheds almost everything that falls on it. The Tees–Exe line is a useful shorthand for all of this, but it is a teaching generalisation, not a boundary: the Central Lowlands of Scotland and the Lancashire and Cheshire plains are lowlands on the upland side, the Vale of York is a broad lowland enclosed by uplands, and the North York Moors sit on the lowland side. A top-level answer uses the pattern as an argument — it explains a river's length, direction or landforms by where its source sits on the relief map, and then says where the rule breaks down."}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing a river's course from the map (2 marks)","setup":"Figure 2 is the river map described for this room. It labels the Trent as rising in the Staffordshire moorlands at the southern end of the Pennines, running south through Stoke-on-Trent to Burton upon Trent and then north-east through Nottingham and Newark, and joining the Yorkshire Ouse to form the Humber Estuary, which opens into the North Sea. The question: Using Figure 2, describe the course of the River Trent from its source to the sea. [2 marks] (AO4 = 2 marks). The mark scheme gives one mark for a basic description taken from the map and a second for a developed point using named detail from it, and gives no credit for explaining why the river runs as it does.","steps":["Open where the map opens the river and commit to a compass direction, because a course without one is only a list of towns: the source label sits in the Staffordshire moorlands at the southern end of the Pennines, and the channel leaves it heading south.","Earn the development mark with the change of direction the map shows, since a river that turns is worth more than one that does not: below Burton upon Trent the Trent swings north-east and holds that heading through Nottingham and Newark.","Finish the way the map finishes, naming the confluence and then the estuary instead of writing 'and then it reaches the sea' — both names are printed on Figure 2, so neither has to be remembered.","Test what you have written against the command word before moving on: every clause should say where the river is or which way it is going, and any clause that has crept in to say why belongs to a different question."],"cues":["Where does Figure 2 start the river, and which way does it set off?","What does the Trent do next that a single direction would miss?","How does the map show the river reaching the North Sea?","Which kind of clause earns nothing on a 'describe' question?"]},{"title":"Calculating a mean from the table of river lengths (2 marks)","setup":"Figure 3 is the table written for this room. It gives the length of five rivers: the Severn 354 km, the Thames 346 km, the Trent 298 km, the Wye 250 km and the Tay 188 km. The question: Using Figure 3, calculate the mean length of the five rivers shown. Show your working. [2 marks] (AO4 = 2 marks). One mark is for the correct answer and one for an indication of the calculation; the unit is expected in the answer.","steps":["Settle what a mean uses before touching any arithmetic: every row of the length column goes in, including the shortest, because nothing is discarded for being untypical.","Add the five lengths on one line so that the working mark is visible on the page: 354 + 346 + 298 + 250 + 188 = 1,436.","Divide the total by how many rivers the table holds, which is five: 1,436 ÷ 5 = 287.2.","Attach the unit and then make the number say something, by setting the mean against the two rivers that sit either side of it in the table."],"cues":["Which of the five rows does a mean use?","What has to appear on the page for the second mark?","What is the total divided by?","What still has to be attached to 287.2, and what should the last sentence do with it?"]},{"title":"Suggesting why land use differs between the two halves of the map (4 marks)","setup":"Figure 1 is the relief map described for this room. Its shading picks out the main upland areas of the north and west — the Highlands and the Southern Uplands, the Pennines, the Lake District, Snowdonia, the Cambrian Mountains and the Brecon Beacons, Exmoor and Dartmoor, the Sperrins and the Mournes — and leaves the lowlands of the south and east unshaded, among them the Midlands, East Anglia and the Fens, and the London Basin. Two rainfall labels are printed on it: parts of the Lake District and Snowdonia receive over 2,000 mm of rain a year, and the driest parts of eastern England receive under 700 mm a year. The question: Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, suggest why the shaded areas are used mainly for rough grazing, forestry and reservoirs while the unshaded areas carry most of the UK's arable farming. [4 marks] (AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks). Level 2 (Clear, 3–4) needs clear understanding applied to the resource; an answer that never uses the map stays in Level 1.","steps":["Turn each thing the map prints into a condition a farmer has to work with: shading stands for height and the steep slopes that come with it, and the 2,000 mm label stands for water arriving faster than the ground can hold it.","Follow that chain as far as the soil, which is where this answer is won: on steep, wet, cold ground the soil stays thin and acid and its nutrients are washed downslope, so there is little for a plough to turn, while grass, conifers and stored water are what such ground will carry.","Now run the same chain across the unshaded half of the map yourself, taking the gentler relief, the deeper soil and the under 700 mm label one at a time and saying what each one makes possible.","Make the closing sentence tie the two halves into one pattern rather than two lists, and check that both rainfall labels have been quoted, since that is where the resource marks sit."],"cues":["What does each feature printed on Figure 1 mean for someone trying to use the land?","What do height, steep slopes and heavy rain together do to a soil?","What is the matching chain on the unshaded half of the map?","What has to be in the final sentence for the resource marks?"]},{"title":"Explaining an exception to the pattern (6 marks)","setup":"Figure 1 shades the UK's main upland areas and leaves the lower land unshaded. In Scotland it shades the Northwest Highlands and the Grampian Mountains north of the Highland Boundary Fault, and the Southern Uplands in the south, but leaves the Central Lowlands between them unshaded — the belt that runs from Ayrshire to the Firth of Tay and holds both Glasgow and Edinburgh. The question: Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, explain why the Central Lowlands of Scotland are lowland even though they lie well inside the northern, upland half of the UK. [6 marks] (AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks).","steps":["Read the exception off the map before explaining it: the unshaded belt is hemmed in by shaded upland on both sides, so whatever kept it low acted on that strip and not on its neighbours.","Name the structures that hem it in, because the locational marks are here: the Highland Boundary Fault along its northern edge and the Southern Upland Fault along its southern edge, between which the crust was dropped to form a rift valley, the Midland Valley of Scotland.","Say what floors the rift, and how old it is beside its neighbours: younger Devonian and Carboniferous sedimentary rock — sandstones, limestones and the coal measures the Scottish coalfields were worked from — against the ancient metamorphic rock of the Highlands on one side and the hard, folded rock of the Southern Uplands on the other.","Take the step from rock to relief that the question is really asking for, remembering that weathering, rivers and ice all work faster on weaker rock, and that where hard volcanic rock does occur inside the belt — the Ochil Hills, the Campsie Fells — it still stands above the plain.","Close by saying what this one exception shows about reading relief from a map, which is the move that lifts the answer clear of description."],"cues":["What does Figure 1 show on either side of the unshaded belt?","Which two structures mark the edges of that belt?","What sort of rock lies inside it, and how old is it beside its neighbours?","How does weaker rock end up as low ground rather than merely soft ground?","What does the exception tell you about the Tees–Exe line?"]},{"title":"Planning a nine-mark 'to what extent' answer (9 marks)","setup":"The question, printed as it would appear at the end of the section: 'The UK is a country of two landscapes: an upland north and west, and a lowland south and east.' Using examples, to what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks] (AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks). Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) wants detailed locational knowledge, thorough understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes, and a reasoned evaluation. You have about nine minutes to write it, plus two to plan, and no figure is provided, so every place must come from memory.","steps":["Decode the command phrase first: 'to what extent' does not ask whether the division exists but how far it holds, so the plan must have a case for, a case against, and a decision.","Paragraph one, the case for, carrying named evidence: the Grampian Mountains with Ben Nevis at 1,345 m, the Pennines with Cross Fell at 893 m and Snowdonia with Yr Wyddfa at 1,085 m, all built of old, hard rock and sharpened by ice.","Paragraph two, the same claim from the lowland side: East Anglia and the Fens, the Midlands and the London Basin on younger chalk, clay and sandstone, with wide floodplains along the Thames and the Great Ouse and under 700 mm of rain a year in the driest east.","Paragraph three, the test that lifts the answer into Level 3: the Central Lowlands of Scotland and the Lancashire and Cheshire plains are lowland inside the 'upland' half, the North York Moors are upland on the other side of the Tees–Exe line, and the Cotswolds, Chilterns and Downs give the lowlands hills of their own.","Conclusion, which must decide rather than summarise: agree to a large extent, because relief, rock and rainfall really do change across the country in that direction, but add that the line is a generalisation which hides the lowlands inside the upland half and the hills inside the lowland half."],"cues":["What is the command phrase asking you to measure?","Which three upland examples would carry the strongest locational marks?","What is the matching evidence for the other half of the claim?","What could an examiner say to break your argument, and where should you say it first?","What must the last sentence do that a summary would not?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Where is the Tees–Exe line drawn, and what does it separate?","a":"From the mouth of the River Tees on the North Sea coast to the mouth of the River Exe on the English Channel coast. It separates upland Britain to the north and west from lowland Britain to the south and east. It is a teaching generalisation, not an official boundary."},{"q":"Why do almost all of the UK's major rivers rise in upland areas?","a":"Uplands force moist Atlantic air to rise, so they receive the heaviest relief rainfall; their rock is hard and impermeable and their soils thin, so water runs off rather than soaking in, and steep slopes gather that runoff into streams."},{"q":"Name the highest point in each of the four countries of the UK, with its height and the upland area it stands in.","a":"Scotland: Ben Nevis, 1,345 m, Grampian Mountains. Wales: Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon), 1,085 m, Snowdonia. England: Scafell Pike, 978 m, Lake District. Northern Ireland: Slieve Donard, 850 m, Mourne Mountains."},{"q":"Why are England's east-flowing rivers generally longer than its west-flowing ones?","a":"The main watershed runs along the Pennines, which lie closer to the west coast than the east, so rivers flowing east — the Tyne, the Tees, the Yorkshire Ouse and the Trent — have much more land to cross than the Eden, the Ribble or the Mersey."}]},"room":{"facts":["Upland is usually taken as land above about 200 m and lowland as land below it. Almost all UK upland lies north and west of the Tees–Exe line, drawn from the mouth of the River Tees to the mouth of the River Exe; the line is a generalisation, not an official boundary.","The highest points: Ben Nevis, 1,345 m, in the Grampian Mountains of Scotland; Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon), 1,085 m, in Snowdonia; Scafell Pike, 978 m, in the Lake District, the highest in England; Slieve Donard, 850 m, in the Mourne Mountains, the highest in Northern Ireland.","The major upland areas: the Northwest Highlands, the Grampian Mountains including the Cairngorms and the Southern Uplands in Scotland; the Cheviot Hills, the Pennines (Cross Fell 893 m), the Lake District, the North York Moors, Exmoor and Dartmoor in England; Snowdonia, the Cambrian Mountains and the Brecon Beacons in Wales; the Sperrin and Mourne Mountains in Northern Ireland.","The major lowland areas: the Central Lowlands of Scotland, the Vale of York, the Lancashire and Cheshire plains, the Midlands, East Anglia and the Fens around the Wash, the Somerset Levels, and the London and Hampshire Basins. Holme Fen in Cambridgeshire, about 3 m below sea level, is the lowest land in Great Britain.","The major rivers: the Severn, about 354 km, is the longest in the UK and rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains; the Thames, about 346 km, is the longest entirely in England and rises in the Cotswolds; the Trent, about 298 km, joins the Yorkshire Ouse to form the Humber Estuary; the Tay, about 188 km, is the longest in Scotland and carries the largest discharge of any UK river.","Rock and rain divide the two halves. Uplands are built of old, resistant rock — Lewisian gneiss of around 3,000 million years in the Northwest Highlands, granite in the Cairngorms and on Dartmoor, volcanic rock in Snowdonia and the Lake District, Carboniferous limestone and gritstone in the Pennines — and receive over 2,000 mm of rain a year in parts of the Lake District and Snowdonia; lowlands sit on younger chalk, clay and sandstone and receive under 700 mm a year in the driest parts of eastern England."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 4, state which country's highest point is the lowest of the four shown. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for Northern Ireland (Slieve Donard, 850 m). No credit if more than one country is given. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"Northern Ireland. Slieve Donard, in the Mourne Mountains, is 850 m, which is lower than Scafell Pike at 978 m, Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon) at 1,085 m and Ben Nevis at 1,345 m.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Only one column of Figure 4 can answer this question.","Set all four heights against one another before you write anything, rather than reading down the table in order.","The answer is the name of a country, on its own, with no reason attached."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, calculate the difference in height between Ben Nevis and Scafell Pike. Show your working. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer, 367 m; one mark for an indication of the calculation, eg 1,345 − 978. Units expected. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Ben Nevis is 1,345 m and Scafell Pike is 978 m. Difference = 1,345 − 978 = 367 m. Ben Nevis is therefore 367 m higher than Scafell Pike, more than a third higher again.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the four rows are involved here and the table gives you both figures.","A difference means one height taken from the other, so decide which is the larger before you subtract.","Write the subtraction out in full and carry the unit into the answer — a bare number loses a mark."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, describe the distribution of the UK's major upland areas. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic description of the distribution shown on the map; a second mark for a developed point using detail from the map. No credit for simply listing the names of upland areas. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The shaded upland is concentrated in the north and west of the UK, while the south and east are almost entirely unshaded lowland (1). The shading is continuous from the Northwest Highlands through the Grampian Mountains and the Southern Uplands into the Cheviot Hills and down the Pennines, and nearly all of it lies north and west of the dashed line running from the mouth of the Tees to the mouth of the Exe; the shading reaches furthest south at Exmoor and Dartmoor, which still lie on the upland side of that line (2).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Describe means the pattern only — no reasons are wanted here.","Give the overall pattern first, then prove it with something you can point to on the map.","Two sentences are enough: one for the pattern, one using named detail from Figure 1, including the dashed line."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, calculate the difference in length between the longest and the shortest river shown. Show your working. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer, 166 km; one mark for an indication of the calculation, eg 354 − 188. Units expected. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The longest river in the table is the Severn at 354 km and the shortest is the Tay at 188 km. Difference = 354 − 188 = 166 km. The Severn is therefore 166 km longer than the Tay.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two rivers you need sit at opposite ends of the length column.","Find both extremes first, then treat it as a single subtraction.","Show the subtraction and attach the unit to the answer."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, give the upland area in which the River Severn rises and the estuary through which it reaches the sea. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the Cambrian Mountains (accept Plynlimon, mid Wales); one mark for the Severn Estuary (accept the Bristol Channel). AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The Severn rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains of mid Wales, and it reaches the sea through the Severn Estuary, which opens into the Bristol Channel.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Figure 2 labels a source and a mouth for every river drawn on it.","Trace the Severn from its label in Wales through Shrewsbury, Worcester and Gloucester to the coast.","Two place names are all that is needed, one for each mark."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, suggest why the UK's upland areas receive more rainfall than its lowland areas. [4 marks]","a":"Credit reasoning that links the shaded high ground on the map to the forced ascent, cooling and condensation of moist Atlantic air, and the unshaded east to a rain shadow; an answer that never uses Figure 1 stays in Level 1. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"The UK's prevailing winds come from the south-west, off the Atlantic, so the air arriving over the country is warm and moist. The shaded areas on Figure 1 — Snowdonia, the Lake District, the Cambrian Mountains and the Highlands — are the first high ground that air meets. Forced to rise over them, it cools, its water vapour condenses into cloud and falls as relief rainfall, which is why the map's wettest label, over 2,000 mm a year, sits on the Lake District and Snowdonia rather than anywhere further east. By the time the same air has crossed that barrier it has lost much of its moisture; it then descends over the unshaded lowland of the Midlands, East Anglia and the London Basin, warming as it sinks so that cloud evaporates instead of forming. Those areas therefore lie in a rain shadow, and Figure 1 labels the driest parts of eastern England at under 700 mm a year. The rainfall pattern on the map follows the relief pattern almost exactly: the wet label lies inside the shading and the dry label lies furthest from it.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which direction the UK's weather usually arrives from before you think about the hills.","Follow one parcel of air across the map and ask what the high ground does to it.","Quote both rainfall labels printed on Figure 1 so the examiner can see the resource was used."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and Figure 3, suggest why the River Severn is the longest river in the UK. [4 marks]","a":"Credit interpretation of the resources: a source high in the uplands of mid Wales, far from the coast it eventually reaches, and a long, indirect course across lowland before the estuary. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Figure 3 shows that the Severn rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains, which is high ground far from the coast it eventually reaches, so the river has a long distance to travel before it can get there at all. Figure 2 shows that its route is also indirect: from mid Wales it runs east and north into the English lowlands past Shrewsbury before turning south through Worcester and Gloucester, so it loops round the uplands instead of taking the shortest line to salt water. Because so much of that course crosses low, gently sloping land, the river meanders rather than running straight, which adds further length. The Wye, in the table, rises on the same mountain but takes a more direct south-easterly route to the same estuary and is about 104 km shorter, which shows that it is the length of the journey and not the height of the source that makes the Severn the longest.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The resources tell you where each river starts, where it finishes and the way it goes round to get there.","Ask why a river might travel further than the straight-line distance from its source to the sea.","Use the Wye as your comparison, since Figure 3 gives it the same source and the same estuary."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and Figure 2 and your own understanding, explain the relationship between the UK's upland areas and its major river systems. [6 marks]","a":"Credit a logical chain linking upland relief and rainfall to river sources, and lowland gradient and discharge to the lower course and estuary, using both resources; description of either map alone is capped at Level 1. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Laying Figure 2 over Figure 1 shows that almost every source label falls inside the shaded upland: the Severn and the Wye on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains, the Tees on Cross Fell in the North Pennines, the Clyde in the Southern Uplands. That is not a coincidence. Uplands force moist Atlantic air to rise, so they receive the heaviest relief rainfall, and their hard, impermeable rock and thin soils shed that water instead of storing it, so runoff on steep slopes quickly gathers into streams. Once a river crosses onto the unshaded lowland its gradient falls while tributaries add to its discharge, so it begins to meander across a wide floodplain and finally opens into an estuary — the Humber, the Severn, the Thames and the Firth of Clyde are all drawn that way on Figure 2. The position of the watershed then explains the lengths. The Pennine watershed lies much nearer the west coast than the east, so rivers flowing east, such as the Trent, the Tees and the Tyne, have far more lowland to cross than the Mersey or the Eden flowing west, and are correspondingly longer. In short, the uplands decide where the UK's rivers start and which way they run, and the lowlands decide how long they become and how they meet the sea.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Begin with what the two maps show when you imagine one laid over the other.","Take the river in order — source, middle course, mouth — and ask what the relief is doing at each stage.","One of your points should use the position of the watershed to explain why some rivers are longer than others."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)","3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)"]},{"q":"Explain how rock type and past glaciation have produced the contrast between the UK's upland and lowland landscapes. [6 marks]","a":"Credit a chain of reasoning: older, harder rock resisting erosion in the north and west, younger, weaker rock worn down in the south and east, and ice sheets sharpening upland scenery while leaving the south beyond the ice. Named rocks and places required for Level 3. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"The UK's uplands survive because of what they are made of. The Northwest Highlands are built on Lewisian gneiss, formed around 3,000 million years ago and among the oldest and hardest rock in Britain; the Cairngorms and Dartmoor are granite, Snowdonia and the Lake District are volcanic rock, and the Pennines are Carboniferous limestone, gritstone and coal measures. All of these resist weathering and erosion, so millions of years of rain, frost and rivers have worn them down more slowly than the land around them, leaving them standing high. The lowlands of the south and east sit on much younger and weaker rock — chalk, clay and sandstone, the chalk of the Downs and the Chilterns laid down under a warm sea roughly 70 to 100 million years ago — which is removed faster and more evenly, leaving broad vales and basins with only the more resistant chalk and limestone left as escarpments such as the Cotswolds. Ice then sharpened the difference. At the height of the last ice age, roughly 20,000 years ago, an ice sheet covered Scotland, Northern Ireland, Wales and northern England, and glaciers cut corries, arêtes and deep U-shaped valleys into Snowdonia and the Lake District, while southern England lay beyond the ice and kept its gentler outlines. Rock type therefore decides where the high ground is, and glaciation decides how dramatic that high ground looks.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two controls are named in the question, so the answer needs both, in that order.","For rock type, ask which rocks wear away slowly and which wear away quickly, and name examples of each.","For glaciation, be clear about how far south the ice reached and what it did to the valleys it filled."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)"]},{"q":"'Differences in rock type are the main reason why the UK has upland and lowland landscapes.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks]","a":"Requires a weighed judgement on rock type against other controls — mountain building, glaciation, weathering and the work of rivers and the sea — supported by named places. Credit answers that agree, disagree or qualify, provided the judgement is substantiated. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Rock type explains the large-scale pattern of UK relief better than any other single factor, but it does not explain the scenery on its own. The case for it is strong. Upland Britain is built of the country's oldest and most resistant rock: Lewisian gneiss of around 3,000 million years in the Northwest Highlands, granite in the Cairngorms and on Dartmoor, volcanic rock in Snowdonia and the Lake District, and Carboniferous limestone, gritstone and coal measures in the Pennines. These resist weathering and erosion, so they have been lowered far more slowly than the weaker chalk, clay and sandstone of the south and east, which have been worn into the vales and basins of the Midlands, East Anglia and the London Basin. The fit is close enough to be predictive: where the hard rock runs out, roughly along the Tees–Exe line, the upland runs out too.\n\nHowever, rock type is only the raw material. The rock of the north and west is high partly because it was raised and folded by ancient mountain building, so tectonic history matters as much as hardness. The detail of upland scenery is not geological at all but glacial: at the height of the last ice age, roughly 20,000 years ago, an ice sheet covered Scotland, Northern Ireland, Wales and northern England, and its glaciers cut the corries, arêtes and deep U-shaped valleys of Snowdonia and the Lake District and left the ribbon lakes that fill them. Beyond the ice limit, southern England kept gentler outlines on rock that is not especially soft. Rivers and the sea have done the rest, cutting the Severn and Thames valleys and their floodplains and shaping the coasts, while within the lowlands rock type reappears on a smaller scale, since resistant chalk stands up as the Chilterns and the North and South Downs above the clay vales beside them.\n\nOn balance I agree to a large extent, but with an important qualification. Rock type is the main reason the UK has uplands and lowlands where it does, because resistance to erosion decides which land survives and which is worn away. It is not the main reason those landscapes look as they do: ice, rivers and the sea sculpted the detail, and without glaciation the Lake District would be high ground of a very different shape.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The command phrase asks how far, so part of the answer has to argue against the statement.","Separate the question of where the high ground is from the question of what it looks like.","Make the strongest counter-argument a process with a date and a place attached, not a general remark."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)"]},{"q":"Assess the importance of the UK's upland areas in shaping its major river systems. [9 marks]","a":"Requires an informed judgement, weighing the upland control on sources, watersheds and long profiles against lowland controls such as gradient, rock permeability, discharge and estuaries. Named rivers and upland areas required for Level 3. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"The uplands matter more to the UK's rivers than any other single influence, because they decide where rivers begin and which way they go. Almost every major river rises on high ground: the Severn and the Wye on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains, the Tees on Cross Fell in the North Pennines, the Clyde in the Southern Uplands, the Tay in the Highlands. The reason is a chain of processes. Uplands force moist Atlantic air to rise, so they receive the heaviest relief rainfall, over 2,000 mm a year in parts of the Lake District and Snowdonia; their rock is hard and impermeable and their soils thin, so water runs off rather than soaking in; and steep slopes concentrate that runoff into streams. The uplands also act as watersheds, and this controls length as well as direction. The main English watershed lies along the Pennines, closer to the west coast than the east, which is why the Tyne, the Tees, the Yorkshire Ouse and the Trent are long east-flowing rivers while the Eden and the Mersey are short west-flowing ones.\n\nThe lowlands, however, decide what a river becomes. Once the Trent or the Thames leaves the high ground its gradient falls and tributaries add to its discharge, so it meanders across a wide floodplain and finally opens into an estuary — the Humber, the Thames and the Severn estuaries are all lowland features. Lowland rock type matters too: chalk is permeable, so the Chilterns and the Downs carry few surface streams and their rivers are spring-fed, which is a lowland control on drainage that no upland explains. Sea level and human management complete the picture, since the Fens and the Somerset Levels drain only because their channels are embanked and pumped.\n\nOverall the uplands are the more important control, because a river's source, its direction and its length are all set before it reaches lower ground, and the Severn is the longest river in the UK at about 354 km because a source high in mid Wales sends it looping east and north round the uplands before it can turn south for the sea. But importance depends on what is being shaped: the uplands create the drainage pattern, while the lowlands create the landforms and the flood risk that most people actually live with.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Assess means weighing one influence against the others and then committing to a view.","Take the river in sections and ask which part of the country is in charge of each one.","Your strongest counter-argument is a lowland control that the uplands cannot account for at all."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)","3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)"]},{"q":"Evaluate the usefulness of the Tees–Exe line as a way of describing the UK's physical landscapes. [9 marks]","a":"Requires a balanced judgement on a generalisation: credit the relief, geology, rainfall and land-use contrasts it captures against the exceptions on both sides and everything it omits. A substantiated overall verdict is needed for Level 3. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"The Tees–Exe line, drawn from the mouth of the River Tees on the North Sea to the mouth of the River Exe on the English Channel, is a useful first description of the UK and a poor final one. Its strength is that several different patterns line up along it. North and west of the line lies almost all the high ground — the Northwest Highlands and the Grampian Mountains with Ben Nevis at 1,345 m, the Southern Uplands, the Pennines with Cross Fell at 893 m, the Lake District with Scafell Pike at 978 m, Snowdonia with Yr Wyddfa at 1,085 m, and Dartmoor and Exmoor in the south-west. That is also where the oldest and hardest rock is found, where the last ice sheet reached, and where relief rainfall is heaviest, at over 2,000 mm a year in parts of the Lake District and Snowdonia. South and east lie the younger chalks, clays and sandstones of the Midlands, East Anglia and the London Basin, the driest parts of the country at under 700 mm a year, and most of its arable farming and largest cities. One line therefore summarises relief, geology, climate and land use at once, which is why it is taught.\n\nIts weakness is that it is a generalisation with obvious exceptions on both sides. The Central Lowlands of Scotland, holding Glasgow and Edinburgh, and the Lancashire and Cheshire plains are substantial lowlands inside the supposedly upland half, and the Vale of York is a broad lowland enclosed by uplands on either side. The North York Moors sit on the lowland side of the line and are unmistakably upland. Within the lowlands the Cotswolds, the Chilterns and the North and South Downs form ranges of hills, so 'lowland' does not mean flat. The line is also silent about everything that is not relief: it says nothing about where rivers rise or which way they flow, nothing about the difference between a resistant headland and a slumping clay cliff on the coast, and nothing about the huge variation within each half.\n\nOverall the line is useful in proportion to the scale of the question. For locating the broad contrast between upland and lowland Britain it is accurate enough to build an answer on, and it is quick to draw and remember. For explaining any particular landscape it is close to useless, because the explanation always comes from rock type, ice, rivers and the sea rather than from which side of a diagonal a place happens to fall.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Evaluate needs the evidence on both sides, so plan the exceptions before you plan the strengths.","Test the line against more than one pattern: relief, geology, rainfall and land use each either fit or do not.","A strong verdict here says what the line is useful for and what it is not, rather than scoring it out of ten."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which is the highest mountain in the UK, how high is it, and which upland area does it stand in?","a":"Ben Nevis, 1,345 m, in the Grampian Mountains of Scotland."},{"q":"Between which two river mouths is the Tees–Exe line drawn, and what does it separate?","a":"From the mouth of the River Tees to the mouth of the River Exe. It separates upland Britain to the north and west from lowland Britain to the south and east."},{"q":"Which is the longest river in the UK, where does it rise, and where does it reach the sea?","a":"The Severn, about 354 km. It rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains of mid Wales and reaches the sea through the Severn Estuary into the Bristol Channel."},{"q":"What is a watershed, and where does the main watershed of England run?","a":"The ridge of high land separating one drainage basin from the next. England's main watershed runs along the Pennines, closer to the west coast than the east."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Upland area","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":false,"back":"Land generally above about 200 m, on older, harder, more resistant rock, with steep slopes, thin soils and heavy relief rainfall. Almost all UK upland lies north and west of the Tees–Exe line."},{"front":"Lowland area","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":false,"back":"Land generally below about 200 m, on younger, weaker rock such as chalk, clay and sandstone, with gentler slopes, deeper soils and less rain. Mostly south and east of the Tees–Exe line."},{"front":"The Tees–Exe line","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":false,"back":"A line drawn from the mouth of the River Tees to the mouth of the River Exe, separating upland Britain in the north and west from lowland Britain in the south and east. A generalisation, not an official boundary."},{"front":"Ben Nevis","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":false,"back":"The highest mountain in the UK at 1,345 m, in the Grampian Mountains of Scotland, near Fort William."},{"front":"Scafell Pike","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":false,"back":"The highest mountain in England at 978 m, in the Lake District of Cumbria."},{"front":"Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon)","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":false,"back":"The highest mountain in Wales at 1,085 m, in Snowdonia in the north of the country."},{"front":"Slieve Donard","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":false,"back":"The highest mountain in Northern Ireland at 850 m, in the Mourne Mountains in the south-east."},{"front":"The Pennines","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":false,"back":"The backbone of England: an upland ridge of Carboniferous limestone, gritstone and coal measures running from the Cheviot Hills south to the Peak District. Cross Fell, 893 m, is its highest point."},{"front":"Name two lowland areas of the UK","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":true,"back":"East Anglia and the Fens; the Cheshire and Lancashire plains; the Central Lowlands of Scotland."},{"front":"The Central Lowlands of Scotland","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (upland and lowland areas)","open":false,"back":"The lowland belt between the Highland Boundary Fault and the Southern Upland Fault, crossed by the Clyde and the Forth and holding Glasgow and Edinburgh — lowland well north of the Tees–Exe line."},{"front":"The River Severn","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)","open":false,"back":"The UK's longest river, about 354 km. Rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains of mid Wales and flows past Shrewsbury, Worcester and Gloucester into the Severn Estuary and the Bristol Channel."},{"front":"The River Thames","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)","open":false,"back":"About 346 km, the longest river entirely in England. Rises in the Cotswolds and flows east through Oxford, Reading and London into the Thames Estuary and the North Sea."},{"front":"The River Trent and the Humber Estuary","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)","open":false,"back":"The Trent, about 298 km, rises in the Staffordshire moorlands, flows through Nottingham and joins the Yorkshire Ouse to form the Humber Estuary, which drains much of the Pennines and the Midlands."},{"front":"Drainage basin and watershed","code":"3.1.3.1 — The UK has a range of diverse landscapes (river systems)","open":false,"back":"A drainage basin is the area of land drained by a river and its tributaries. The watershed is the ridge of high land separating one basin from the next; England's main watershed runs along the Pennines."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Map described for this room: the relief of the United Kingdom. Shading marks the UK's main upland areas, the blocks of high ground in the north and west; the lower land between and beyond them is left unshaded. The shading covers, in Scotland, the Northwest Highlands and the Grampian Mountains (including the Cairngorms) north of the Highland Boundary Fault, and the Southern Uplands in the south; in England, the Cheviot Hills on the Scottish border, the Pennines running south from the Cheviots through the North Pennines and the Yorkshire Dales to the Peak District, the Lake District in the north-west, the North York Moors in the north-east, and Exmoor and Dartmoor in the south-west; in Wales, Snowdonia in the north, the Cambrian Mountains in mid Wales and the Brecon Beacons in the south; and in Northern Ireland, the Sperrin Mountains in the north-west and the Mourne Mountains in the south-east. Every area shaded rises well above 200 m. The unshaded lowland includes the Central Lowlands of Scotland, the belt running from Ayrshire to the Firth of Tay that holds both Glasgow and Edinburgh, the Vale of York, the Lancashire and Cheshire plains, the Midlands, East Anglia and the Fens around the Wash, the Somerset Levels, and the London and Hampshire Basins. Lines of low hills are drawn inside the lowland of the south and east and are not shaded, because they are escarpments standing within the lowland rather than upland areas in their own right, although parts of them do rise above 200 m: the Cotswolds, the Chilterns, and the North and South Downs. A dashed line is drawn from the mouth of the River Tees on the North Sea coast to the mouth of the River Exe on the English Channel coast, labelled 'Tees–Exe line'. Two rainfall labels are printed on the map, both real values: parts of the Lake District and Snowdonia receive over 2,000 mm of rain a year; the driest parts of eastern England receive under 700 mm a year.","Figure 2 — Map described for this room: the major river systems of the United Kingdom, drawn on the same outline as Figure 1, with the source and the mouth of each river labelled. Severn: rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains of mid Wales, runs east and north past Shrewsbury, then south through Worcester and Gloucester into the Severn Estuary and the Bristol Channel. Wye: rises on Plynlimon as well and flows south-east along part of the Wales–England border to join the Severn Estuary at Chepstow. Thames: rises in the Cotswolds in Gloucestershire and flows east through Oxford, Reading and London into the Thames Estuary and the North Sea. Trent: rises in the Staffordshire moorlands at the southern end of the Pennines, flows south through Stoke-on-Trent to Burton upon Trent, then north-east through Nottingham and Newark, and joins the Yorkshire Ouse to form the Humber Estuary. Ouse (Yorkshire): drains the eastern Pennines and flows through York to the Humber. Tees: rises on Cross Fell in the North Pennines and flows east past High Force waterfall in Upper Teesdale to the North Sea at Middlesbrough. Tyne: formed where the North Tyne and the South Tyne meet near Hexham, then flows east through Newcastle upon Tyne to the North Sea. Eden: flows north-west from the Pennines through Carlisle to the Solway Firth. Mersey: flows west past Manchester to Liverpool and the Irish Sea. Clyde: rises in the Southern Uplands and flows north, then west through Glasgow, into the Firth of Clyde. Tay: rises in the Highlands and flows east through Perth into the Firth of Tay at Dundee. Spey: flows north-east from the Highlands to the Moray Firth. Great Ouse and Nene: flow north-east across East Anglia into the Wash. Exe: rises on Exmoor and flows south through Exeter to the English Channel. Bann: the Upper Bann flows into Lough Neagh and the Lower Bann flows north out of the lough to the sea near Coleraine. Almost every source label on this map sits inside the shading of Figure 1, and the widest parts of the rivers, drawn as estuaries, sit on unshaded lowland.","Figure 3 — Data table written for this room: five of the UK's major rivers. The lengths are the commonly published figures, rounded to the nearest kilometre.\nRiver | Length (km) | Where it rises | Where it reaches the sea\nSevern | 354 | Plynlimon, Cambrian Mountains, mid Wales | Severn Estuary, into the Bristol Channel\nThames | 346 | The Cotswolds, Gloucestershire | Thames Estuary, into the North Sea\nTrent | 298 | Staffordshire moorlands, southern Pennines | Humber Estuary, into the North Sea\nWye | 250 | Plynlimon, Cambrian Mountains, mid Wales | Severn Estuary at Chepstow\nTay | 188 | The Highlands of Scotland | Firth of Tay, into the North Sea","Figure 4 — Data table written for this room: the highest point in each of the four countries of the UK. The heights are real and are given to the nearest metre.\nCountry | Highest point | Upland area it stands in | Height (m)\nScotland | Ben Nevis | Grampian Mountains | 1,345\nWales | Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon) | Snowdonia | 1,085\nEngland | Scafell Pike | Lake District | 978\nNorthern Ireland | Slieve Donard | Mourne Mountains | 850"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the UK's upland and lowland areas","items":["Upland is usually taken as land above about 200 m, lowland as land below it. The Tees–Exe line, drawn from the mouth of the River Tees to the mouth of the River Exe, separates upland Britain in the north and west from lowland Britain in the south and east; it is a teaching generalisation, not an official boundary.","Scotland's uplands: the Northwest Highlands, the Grampian Mountains including the Cairngorms, and the Southern Uplands. The Central Lowlands lie between them, bounded by the Highland Boundary Fault and the Southern Upland Fault.","Ben Nevis, in the Grampian Mountains near Fort William, is the highest mountain in the UK at 1,345 m. Ben Macdui, 1,309 m, in the Cairngorms, is the second highest.","England's uplands: the Cheviot Hills on the Scottish border; the Pennines, whose highest point is Cross Fell at 893 m; the Lake District, whose Scafell Pike at 978 m is the highest point in England; the North York Moors; and Exmoor and Dartmoor in the south-west, where High Willhays reaches 621 m.","Wales: Snowdonia in the north, with Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon) at 1,085 m, the highest mountain in Wales; the Cambrian Mountains in mid Wales; and the Brecon Beacons in the south, with Pen y Fan at 886 m.","Northern Ireland: the Sperrin Mountains in the north-west and the Mourne Mountains in the south-east, where Slieve Donard reaches 850 m, the highest point in Northern Ireland.","The Pennines are often called the backbone of England: a ridge of Carboniferous limestone, millstone grit and coal measures running south from the Cheviot Hills through the North Pennines and the Yorkshire Dales to the Peak District.","Upland rock is old, hard and resistant: Lewisian gneiss in the Northwest Highlands, formed around 3,000 million years ago and among the oldest rock in Britain; granite in the Cairngorms and on Dartmoor; volcanic rock in Snowdonia and the Lake District.","Lowland rock is younger and weaker: chalk, clay, sandstone and younger limestone. The chalk of the North and South Downs, the Chilterns and Salisbury Plain was laid down under a warm sea roughly 70 to 100 million years ago.","The major lowlands: the Central Lowlands of Scotland, the Vale of York, the Lancashire and Cheshire plains, the Midlands, East Anglia and the Fens around the Wash, the Somerset Levels, and the London and Hampshire Basins.","Much of the Fens lies at or close to sea level. Holme Fen in Cambridgeshire, about 3 m below sea level, is the lowest land in Great Britain.","Lowland Britain still has hills: the Cotswolds, which rise to a little over 300 m, the Chilterns, the North and South Downs, and the Lincolnshire and Yorkshire Wolds are escarpments of chalk or limestone standing above clay vales.","At the height of the last ice age, roughly 20,000 years ago, an ice sheet covered Scotland, Northern Ireland, Wales and northern England. Southern England lay beyond the ice, which is one reason upland scenery is so much sharper.","Relief rainfall makes the uplands wet: parts of the Lake District and Snowdonia receive over 2,000 mm of rain a year, while the driest parts of eastern England receive under 700 mm a year.","Land use follows relief: uplands carry moorland, rough grazing, forestry, reservoirs and most of the national parks, while lowlands carry most of the UK's arable farming, its largest cities and its densest transport networks."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the UK's major rivers, and the terms this room uses","items":["The River Severn is the longest river in the UK at about 354 km. It rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains of mid Wales and flows past Shrewsbury, Worcester and Gloucester into the Severn Estuary and the Bristol Channel.","The River Thames, about 346 km, is the longest river entirely in England. It rises in the Cotswolds in Gloucestershire and flows east through Oxford, Reading and London into the Thames Estuary and the North Sea.","The River Trent, about 298 km, rises in the Staffordshire moorlands at the southern end of the Pennines and flows through Stoke-on-Trent, Burton upon Trent, Nottingham and Newark before joining the Yorkshire Ouse.","The Humber Estuary is formed where the Trent and the Yorkshire Ouse meet. It drains a large part of the Pennines, the Vale of York and the Midlands into the North Sea.","The River Tees rises on Cross Fell, the highest point of the Pennines at 893 m, drops over High Force — a waterfall of about 21 m in Upper Teesdale — and reaches the North Sea at Middlesbrough.","The River Tyne is formed where the North Tyne and the South Tyne meet near Hexham, and flows east through Newcastle upon Tyne to the North Sea at Tynemouth.","The River Tay, about 188 km, is the longest river in Scotland and carries the largest discharge of any river in the UK. It rises in the Highlands and flows through Perth into the Firth of Tay at Dundee.","Other Scottish rivers: the Clyde rises in the Southern Uplands and flows through Glasgow into the Firth of Clyde; the Forth flows east past Stirling into the Firth of Forth; the Spey flows north-east from the Highlands to the Moray Firth.","Rivers of western England: the Mersey flows west past Manchester to Liverpool and the Irish Sea; the Eden flows north-west from the Pennines through Carlisle to the Solway Firth; the Ribble and the Lune also drain west.","Rivers of eastern England: the Great Ouse, the Nene and the Witham cross East Anglia and Lincolnshire to reach the Wash on the North Sea coast.","In the south-west, the Exe rises on Exmoor and flows south through Exeter to the English Channel, and the Bristol Avon flows through Bath and Bristol into the Severn Estuary.","Northern Ireland: the Upper Bann flows into Lough Neagh and the Lower Bann flows north out of the lough to the sea near Coleraine. Lough Neagh, about 380 km², is the largest lake in the British Isles.","Terms to use precisely: the source is where a river begins; a tributary is a smaller river joining a larger one; a confluence is where two rivers meet; the mouth is where a river enters the sea.","A drainage basin is the area of land drained by a river and its tributaries; the watershed is the ridge of high land separating one drainage basin from the next.","England's main watershed runs along the Pennines, closer to the west coast than the east, so east-flowing rivers such as the Tyne, the Tees, the Yorkshire Ouse and the Trent are longer than west-flowing rivers such as the Eden, the Ribble and the Mersey.","Most UK rivers rise in upland areas, where relief rainfall is heaviest and impermeable rock and thin soils shed water quickly, and widen into estuaries where they cross lowland: the Severn, the Thames, the Humber, the Mersey, the Clyde and the Firth of Forth all end in estuaries.","The Severn Estuary has one of the largest tidal ranges in the world, and the Severn bore is a tidal wave that travels up the river against the current."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) on the 9-mark question: 'Differences in rock type are the main reason why the UK has upland and lowland landscapes.' To what extent do you agree?","items":["The paragraph: Rock type explains where the UK's high ground is better than any other single factor. Upland Britain is built of the oldest and most resistant rock in the country — Lewisian gneiss of around 3,000 million years in the Northwest Highlands, granite in the Cairngorms and on Dartmoor, volcanic rock in Snowdonia and the Lake District, and Carboniferous limestone and gritstone in the Pennines — and because these rocks resist weathering and erosion they have been lowered far more slowly than the chalk, clay and sandstone of the Midlands, East Anglia and the London Basin, which have been worn into broad vales and basins. The fit is close enough to be predictive: where the hard rock runs out, roughly along the Tees–Exe line, the upland runs out with it, and Ben Nevis at 1,345 m and Scafell Pike at 978 m both stand on rock of that resistant kind. Rock type is not the whole answer, however, because it cannot explain the shape of what survives: the corries, arêtes and U-shaped valleys of Snowdonia and the Lake District were cut by glaciers at the height of the last ice age, roughly 20,000 years ago, when the ice sheet stopped short of southern England. Rock type therefore decides where the uplands are, while ice decides what they look like, and on that basis I agree with the statement to a large extent but not completely.","Why this reaches Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9): it demonstrates detailed knowledge of locations, places and processes, naming four upland rocks and two named peaks with their heights (AO1); it shows thorough understanding of the interrelationship between rock resistance, erosion and relief, carried as a chain rather than a list (AO2); and it evaluates the relative significance of rock type against glaciation and reaches a reasoned position (AO3). The prose is accurate and uses a wide range of specialist terms — resistance, weathering, erosion, corrie, arête — which is what a Level 3 answer on this question should read like.","What to copy from it: one named place and one figure per point; a 'because' or 'so' in every sentence that carries a process; a counter-argument that is itself evidenced rather than a token 'on the other hand'; and a final sentence that decides how far the statement holds instead of restating both sides."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–3) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The paragraph: The UK has uplands and lowlands because of the rocks. In the north and west the rocks are harder so the land is higher, and in the south and east they are softer so it is lower. There are mountains in Scotland and Wales and it is flatter in places like East Anglia. Ice and rivers have also changed the land a bit. So rock type is quite important but other things matter too, and both sides have a point.","Which descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic, 1–3) — it demonstrates limited knowledge of locations, places and processes, shows slight understanding of the interrelationships between them, and its evaluation is limited.","Which descriptor it misses: Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) asks for comprehensive and accurate locational knowledge, thorough understanding of the interrelationships between places, environments and processes, and a reasoned evaluation. This answer names no rock, no upland area, no mountain and no height; 'harder so the land is higher' asserts the link without ever explaining erosion; 'ice and rivers have also changed the land a bit' is the counter-argument reduced to nothing; and the last sentence refuses to decide.","The three fixes that would move it up: replace each general statement with a named rock in a named place — granite in the Cairngorms, chalk in the Chilterns; explain one mechanism all the way through, for example resistant rock being eroded more slowly than the land around it and so standing higher; and end by saying how far the statement holds and what it cannot explain."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Assess the importance of the UK's upland areas in shaping its major river systems. [9 marks]'","items":["Decode the command: 'Assess' means weigh the parts and give an informed overall judgement, so the answer must compare the influence of the uplands with the influence of everything else and then commit. Three of the nine marks (AO1) are for knowledge of places, so every claim needs a named river or upland area attached.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly two minutes planning and nine writing. Three developed paragraphs and a real conclusion is the right ambition, not five thin ones.","Paragraph 1 — the uplands decide where rivers begin: the Severn and the Wye rise on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains, the Tees on Cross Fell in the North Pennines, the Clyde in the Southern Uplands, the Tay in the Highlands. Explain the chain: relief rainfall of over 2,000 mm a year in parts of the Lake District and Snowdonia, falling on hard impermeable rock under thin soils, running off steep slopes into streams.","Paragraph 2 — the uplands decide direction and length: the main English watershed runs along the Pennines, closer to the west coast than the east, so the Tyne, the Tees, the Yorkshire Ouse and the Trent are long east-flowing rivers while the Eden and the Mersey are short west-flowing ones, and the Severn is the UK's longest at about 354 km because it starts high in mid Wales and loops round the uplands to reach the sea.","Paragraph 3 — the counter-argument, which is where Level 3 is won: the lowlands decide what a river becomes. Gradient falls and tributaries raise discharge, so rivers meander across floodplains and open into estuaries such as the Humber, the Thames and the Severn; permeable chalk in the Chilterns and the Downs produces spring-fed rivers and few surface streams; and the Fens and the Somerset Levels drain only because their channels are embanked and pumped.","The evaluation thread that runs through all three paragraphs: for each feature of a river ask whether it was settled at the source or later on the journey — source, direction and length upstream, landforms, flooding and mouth downstream.","What the conclusion must say: that the uplands are the more important control, because source, direction and length are all fixed before a river reaches lower ground; but that importance depends on what is being shaped, since the lowlands produce the meanders, floodplains, estuaries and flood risk that most people actually live with."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:13:21.865768Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Both capping faults are gone: lesson.examples[0].steps[1] now reads 'below Burton upon Trent the Trent swings north-east and holds that heading through Nottingham and Newark', which is the real course, and the setup, Figure 2 and extras[2] item[2] all agree with it and with each other, while extras[5] item[0] now says 'Three of the nine marks (AO1)', matching room.questions[10].a and levels.md's Paper 1 nine-mark grids. Re-checked on this pass with nothing new against them: all 12 answer keys re-solved, the five summit heights and five river lengths and every source and mouth in Figures 3 and 4, the 367 m, 166 km, 104 km and 287.2 km arithmetic, the Midland Valley geology, Figure 1's new shading rule, the Vale of York and Exmoor/Dartmoor claims, the agreement between questions 6 and 10 on the Severn, the absence of SPaG from all three nine-markers, and the tariffs 1/2/2/2/2/4/4/6/6/9/9/9 with their AO splits, all of which levels.md or the specimen schemes attest. The four remaining notes are about proportion and scope, not accuracy; a teacher could publish this as it stands."}}},"3.1.3.2":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.3.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Coastal landscapes is one of the UK physical landscapes most students answer on, and it is examined almost entirely from a resource: a photograph of a cliff, a map extract of a spit, a picture of a sea wall. Credit does not come from naming the landform. It comes from the chain that made it — which rock, which process, in which order — and from a named UK coastline and a named UK coastal management scheme you can quote by place, date and figure. Learn the chains, learn the two examples, and almost everything this room can ask becomes answerable.","idea":[{"h":"Waves: where the energy comes from, and the two kinds","t":"Wind blowing over water drags the surface by friction and makes waves. How much energy a wave carries depends on the wind speed, how long the wind blows for, and the fetch — the open distance of sea the wind has crossed. When a wave breaks, water rushes up the beach as swash and drains back as backwash, and which of those two is stronger decides what the wave does. A constructive wave is low and long with a wavelength far greater than its height, breaks roughly 6 to 8 times a minute, and has a swash stronger than its backwash, so it pushes sand up the beach faster than it drags it away and builds a wide, gently sloping beach. A destructive wave is tall and steep with a short wavelength, breaks far more often, one wave close behind the next, and has a backwash stronger than its swash, so it combs material down the beach and leaves it steep, often with a shingle ridge. A top answer never simply labels a wave: it says which type, gives the evidence for saying so, and says what the wave then does to the beach.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (waves)"},{"h":"The processes: weathering, mass movement, erosion, transport, deposition","t":"Four families of process shape a coast and you need the mechanism of each, not the label. Weathering breaks rock in place: mechanical weathering includes freeze-thaw, where water in a crack freezes, expands by about a tenth of its volume and prises the rock apart, and salt weathering, where salt crystals grow in pores; chemical weathering includes carbonation, where rainwater with dissolved carbon dioxide forms a weak acid that dissolves limestone and chalk. Mass movement is material moving downslope under gravity: rockfall from a steep hard cliff, sliding, and slumping, where heavy rain saturates a clay or glacial till cliff so that a whole block rotates downwards along a curved surface. Erosion is the sea removing rock: hydraulic power, abrasion and attrition. Transport moves the sediment by traction, saltation, suspension and solution, and along the shore by longshore drift, where swash runs obliquely up the beach at the angle of the prevailing wind and backwash returns straight down under gravity, giving a zigzag. Deposition happens wherever the water loses energy — in a sheltered bay, behind a spit or an island, where the coastline changes direction, or when constructive waves swash more material up than they carry away. A top answer names the process and then explains its mechanism in the next clause.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (coastal processes)"},{"h":"Rock, structure and the landforms of erosion","t":"Geology decides where erosion bites. On a discordant coast the bands of hard and soft rock meet the sea end-on, at right angles to the shore, so the soft bands are cut back into bays while the resistant bands are left standing as headlands. On a concordant coast the bands run parallel to the shore, so a single resistant band shields everything behind it and there are no bays until the sea breaks through it. Once headlands exist, wave refraction takes over: waves entering shallow water off a headland slow and bend round it, concentrating their energy on it, while the same bending spreads energy out inside the bay, so headlands are attacked and bays are filled. On a headland the sea works along joints and faults, widening a crack by hydraulic power and abrasion into a cave, cutting the cave through the narrow end of the headland to leave an arch, collapsing the unsupported arch roof to leave a stack, and finally cutting the stack down to a stump. At the foot of any cliff, erosion between high and low tide cuts a wave-cut notch; the overhang above it collapses, the cliff retreats, and a gently sloping wave-cut platform is left exposed at low tide. A top answer puts rock type, structure, named process and landform into one sequence, in order.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (erosion)"},{"h":"The landforms of deposition","t":"Beaches are the store of sediment between high and low water. Sandy beaches are gently sloping because the fine grains pack tightly, water cannot drain through them and the backwash runs back down the surface; shingle beaches are steep because water sinks into the gaps, weakening the backwash so that coarse material is left piled high. Constructive waves build berms, ridges left at the high tide line; storms throw the coarsest material to the back of the beach as a storm beach. Sand dunes form where a wide sandy beach dries at low tide and onshore wind blows sand inland: it piles against an obstacle to make an embryo dune, marram grass colonises and binds the sand with deep roots so a foredune or yellow dune grows, older dunes inland gain humus and a full plant cover and become grey dunes, and hollows where the water table reaches the surface are dune slacks. A spit is a ridge of sand or shingle built by longshore drift where the coast changes direction or a river mouth interrupts it, often with a recurved end where winds come from another direction and with salt marsh in the sheltered water behind. A bar is a spit that has grown right across a bay, trapping a lagoon. A top answer explains the sequence with the transport process named at every stage.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (deposition)"},{"h":"The named section of UK coastline: the Isle of Purbeck, Dorset","t":"The Isle of Purbeck, on the Dorset coast, is a section of coastline where one set of rocks produces two completely different coasts, which is why it answers so many questions. The beds — hard Portland limestone, then Purbeck beds, then soft Wealden clays and sands, then chalk — were folded almost upright, and they run parallel to the south-facing coast at Lulworth but end-on against the east-facing coast at Swanage. On the concordant south coast the sea has cut the arch of Durdle Door through a near-vertical band of Portland limestone, and at Lulworth Cove it broke through a narrow gap in that limestone and then widened rapidly in the soft beds behind, stopping at the chalk to leave a near-circular cove. On the discordant east coast the same rocks give headlands and bays: Peveril Point of Purbeck limestone, Swanage Bay cut into the Wealden beds, the chalk headland of Ballard Point and Handfast Point, at whose tip the arch, stack and stump of Old Harry Rocks stand in line, and then, north of the chalk, Studland Bay, cut into the younger, softer Eocene sands and clays. Deposition shows there too: the sheltered sandy beach at Swanage and the dune system behind Studland Bay. When a question gives you a map of a coast, this is the coast to describe the physical features from — bays, headlands, cliffs, beaches — and to name. A top answer names the landform, the rock and the place together, never one without the others.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (example of a section of coastline)"},{"h":"Defending a coast: what each strategy costs and what it buys","t":"Hard engineering builds a structure to stop the sea. A sea wall, usually concrete and often curved, reflects wave energy back out to sea and protects the land and buildings behind it, and doubles as a promenade — but it is the most expensive option per metre, it needs maintenance, and reflected waves can scour the beach in front of it away. Rock armour is a heap of large boulders at the cliff foot whose gaps absorb wave energy; it is far cheaper and quick to place, but the rock is usually shipped in from elsewhere, looks out of place and can be shifted by the biggest storms. Gabions are wire cages of rock: cheap and quick, but the cages rust and burst within about a decade. Groynes trap sediment moving in longshore drift and build a wider beach that makes waves break early and lose energy, which also helps tourism — but the sediment they hold back is sediment the coast further along no longer receives, so erosion speeds up downdrift. Soft engineering works with the processes instead: beach nourishment adds sand or shingle, usually dredged offshore, so the beach looks natural and absorbs energy, but it is expensive and must be repeated; reprofiling moves material back up the beach after storms, cheap but short-lived; dune regeneration replants marram and fences paths so dunes keep absorbing wave energy, cheap but easily trampled or storm-damaged. Managed retreat, or coastal realignment, deliberately lets low-value land flood so that salt marsh develops, absorbing wave energy and creating habitat at low long-term cost — but land is lost and owners must be compensated. A top answer never lists: it weighs a benefit against its cost and says who gains and who pays.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (costs and benefits)"},{"h":"The named UK coastal management scheme: Mappleton, Holderness","t":"Mappleton is a village on the Holderness coast of East Yorkshire, between Hornsea and Withernsea. Holderness runs about 61 km from Flamborough Head to Spurn Head and its cliffs are cut in glacial till, the soft clay and stones dumped by ice at the end of the last glacial period; saturated by rain the till slumps, and the coast retreats on average by roughly 2 metres a year, among the fastest rates in Europe. That was the reason for management: the cliff edge had come close to the village and to the B1242 coast road, the direct link along this stretch of coast. The strategy, built in 1991 at a cost of about 2 million pounds, was hard engineering: two rock groynes built out from the beach and a rock revetment of large boulders at the cliff foot, using granite shipped in from Norway. The effects were exactly what the design predicted. A beach built up between the groynes, waves now break further offshore and reach the cliff with less energy, and the cliff behind the village has been held. But the groynes trap sediment that longshore drift used to carry south, so the beaches south of the village are thinner and their cliffs less protected; at Great Cowden, a short way down the coast, erosion sped up and farmland and farm buildings were lost, and landowners there argued that a village had been saved at their expense. A top answer uses the same case for both halves of a judgement: the scheme worked, and it moved the problem.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (example of a coastal management scheme)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading wave and beach data, and explaining a difference in gradient","setup":"Figure 2 in this room's resources is a table of measurements taken on one January morning at three points along a bay-and-headland coastline (figures illustrative). Tip of the headland: 13 waves per minute, mean wave height 2.4 m, no beach, bare rock platform. Edge of the bay next to the headland: 10 waves per minute, mean wave height 1.6 m, beach gradient 9 degrees, shingle. Centre of the bay: 7 waves per minute, mean wave height 0.8 m, beach gradient 4 degrees, sand. The question is: using Figure 2, explain the difference in beach gradient between the centre of the bay and the edge of the bay.","steps":["Pin down what has to be explained: the gradient at the edge is more than twice the gradient in the centre, so the answer must say why bigger waves leave a steeper beach.","Classify each wave from the figures: 7 breaks a minute with a mean height of 0.8 m is a constructive wave, low and infrequent; 10 a minute at 1.6 m is taller and more frequent, so closer to destructive.","Give the mechanism in the centre: the constructive wave has a swash stronger than its backwash, so it carries sand up the beach faster than it drags it away, building it out into a wide, gently sloping face.","Give the mechanism at the edge: the stronger backwash of the taller waves drags the fine sand seaward and leaves only shingle, and water sinks into the gaps between the pebbles so little runs back down the surface, letting the material rest at a steeper angle.","Tie it back to the resource in one sentence: the table shows sand where the waves are smallest and shingle where they are largest, which is exactly the pattern needed for the gradients recorded."],"cues":["Which of the two beaches is steeper, and roughly by how much?","Do the counts and heights say constructive or destructive at each site?","What does a swash stronger than the backwash do to sand?","Why can shingle rest at a steeper angle than sand can?","What has the final sentence still got to do with the table?"]},{"title":"Explaining a stack from a photograph","setup":"Figure 1 in this room's resources describes a photograph of the chalk headland at Handfast Point, Studland, on the Dorset coast. Visible in it: a vertical white chalk cliff running out to sea; a deep notch cut along the foot of the cliff at the waterline, with loose blocks of chalk on the ledge below; an arch through the far end of the headland with daylight beneath it; beyond the arch, separated by open water, a tall narrow pillar of white chalk; and beyond that a low flat-topped rock just breaking the surface. The question is: using Figure 1, explain the formation of the tall narrow pillar of chalk.","steps":["Begin with the rock and its weakness: chalk is strong enough to stand as a vertical cliff but is cut through by vertical joints, and it is those joints the sea attacks first.","Name the erosion processes at the joint and how they work: hydraulic power drives air and water into the crack under the weight of each breaking wave and prises it wider, while abrasion hurls sand and shingle at the same spot and grinds it out.","Turn the crack into a cave and use the evidence in the photograph: the notch cut along the waterline is that undercutting at work, and where it is concentrated on a joint it deepens into a cave.","Cut the headland through: because the seaward end of the headland is narrow, erosion working on both sides eventually meets and the cave becomes the arch shown with daylight beneath it.","Collapse the roof: the arch is undercut from below and loosened from above by weathering, so it loses support and falls, leaving the pillar standing offshore as a stack.","Finish the sequence with the last feature in the frame: the low flat-topped rock beyond it is a stump, what the stack becomes once the sea has cut it down, so one photograph records three stages of a single sequence."],"cues":["What is it about chalk that gives the sea a starting point?","Which two erosion processes widen a crack, and how does each work?","What does the notch at the waterline tell you is happening?","Why does the cave not simply get deeper and stop?","What holds an arch up, and what happens when that goes?","What is the low rock beyond the pillar, and why does it matter?"]},{"title":"Using a map to explain why one coast has bays and the next does not","setup":"Figure 3 in this room's resources is a sketch map of the Isle of Purbeck, Dorset. On the south-facing coast at Lulworth the rock bands run parallel to the shore: hard Portland limestone at the sea, then softer Purbeck beds and Wealden clays, then chalk inland; Durdle Door is drawn as a limestone arch about a kilometre west of Lulworth Cove, and Lulworth Cove as a near-circular bay with a narrow entrance cut through the limestone and a chalk wall at the back. On the east-facing coast the same bands meet the sea end-on: Peveril Point of Purbeck limestone, Swanage Bay cut into the Wealden clays, the chalk headland of Ballard Point and Handfast Point with Old Harry Rocks at its tip, and beyond the chalk to the north, Studland Bay, cut into the younger, softer Eocene sands and clays. The question is: using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain why headlands and bays have formed on the east-facing coast but not along the coast at Lulworth.","steps":["Read the structure off the map first, because that is the whole answer: on the east coast the bands meet the sea end-on, at Lulworth they run parallel to it.","Name the two coast types from that evidence: the east-facing coast is discordant, the Lulworth coast concordant.","Explain the east coast: the weakly cemented Wealden clays are cut back quickly by hydraulic power and abrasion into Swanage Bay, and the softer Eocene sands and clays north of the chalk into Studland Bay, while the Purbeck limestone and the chalk resist and are left projecting as Peveril Point and Handfast Point.","Explain the Lulworth coast: the hard Portland limestone band lies across the front of everything, shielding the soft beds behind, so waves cannot reach them along the whole shore and no bays can form.","Explain the one place where they did get through, using the map: at Lulworth Cove the sea breached a narrow gap in the limestone and then widened rapidly in the soft beds behind, which is why the cove is round with a narrow mouth.","Close with the comparison the question wants: the same rocks and the same waves produce two different coasts, so it is the structure, not the geology alone, that decides where bays appear."],"cues":["What is different about the way the bands meet the sea in the two places?","What are the names for those two arrangements?","Which band on the east coast retreats fastest, and which resists?","At Lulworth, what is standing between the waves and the soft rock?","Why is the cove round rather than a long straight bay?","What does comparing the two coasts prove?"]},{"title":"Suggesting why a scheme caused conflict","setup":"Figure 4 in this room's resources is a written extract about the Holderness coast of East Yorkshire. It states that the coast runs about 61 km from Flamborough Head to Spurn Head, that its cliffs of glacial till retreat on average by roughly 2 metres a year, and that at Mappleton the cliff edge had come close to the houses and to the B1242 coast road linking Hornsea and Withernsea. In 1991 two rock groynes and a rock revetment of Norwegian granite were built there at a cost of about 2 million pounds. A beach has since built up between the groynes and the cliff behind the village has been held; the groynes also trap sediment that longshore drift used to carry south, and landowners at Great Cowden, a short way down the coast, found the sea taking their fields and farm buildings faster than before. The question is: using Figure 4, suggest why the scheme at Mappleton led to conflict.","steps":["Identify the winners from the extract: the villagers and road users got a defended cliff and a beach that had not been there before.","Work out the mechanism that creates the loser: the groynes hold sediment that drift used to carry south, so the beaches south of the village receive less and become thinner.","Follow that through to the effect at Great Cowden: a thinner beach absorbs less wave energy, so more energy reaches the till cliff and it retreats faster, taking fields and farm buildings.","Name the second grievance in the extract: public money defended one village on a coast where most landowners get nothing, so those losing land are paying for a benefit they do not receive.","State the conflict itself rather than the two stories: two groups on the same coast now have opposite interests, because the defence that protects one is the cause of the loss suffered by the other."],"cues":["Who is better off after 1991, according to the extract?","What do the groynes do to the sediment moving along the coast?","Why does a thinner beach mean a faster-retreating cliff?","What is the second thing the landowners could object to?","What turns two effects into a conflict?"]},{"title":"Building a judgement about groynes","setup":"You are asked: assess the costs and benefits of building groynes on a stretch of coast where longshore drift is strong. You may use any named UK scheme you have studied. The command word is assess, so the answer must weigh the parts and end in an informed overall judgement rather than listing advantages and disadvantages.","steps":["Open with the mechanism, because every later point depends on it: groynes are barriers across the beach that interrupt the zigzag of longshore drift so sediment piles up on their updrift side.","Turn the mechanism into the benefit and anchor it in a case: at Mappleton, on the Holderness coast, a beach built up between two rock groynes after 1991, and a wider beach makes waves break further out and arrive at the cliff with less energy.","Add the second benefit and its limit: a wider beach also keeps the seafront usable for tourism, though that matters far more in a resort town than at a village of a few dozen houses.","Turn the same mechanism into the cost, with the same case: the sediment held at Mappleton never reaches the coast to the south, so beaches at Great Cowden thinned and the till cliffs there retreated faster, taking farmland.","Weigh, do not list: the benefit is local and the cost is passed along the coast, so whether groynes are worth building depends entirely on what lies downdrift of them.","Judge, with a condition: groynes are an effective defence where the land behind is valuable and the coast downdrift is already defended or worth little, and a poor one on a drifting coast where someone else must absorb the loss."],"cues":["What exactly does a groyne do to moving sediment?","How does that turn into protection for the cliff behind?","What is the second benefit, and where does it count most?","If sediment stops here, what happens further along?","What single question decides whether they are worth it?","What condition has to be attached to the verdict?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A beach is wide, sandy and slopes gently, and the waves breaking on it are low and come about seven times a minute. Which wave type is this, and what is the evidence?","a":"Constructive. Low, long waves breaking infrequently have a swash stronger than the backwash, so more sand is pushed up the beach than is dragged away and a wide gentle beach is built."},{"q":"Put these in the order in which they form: arch, cave, joint, stack, stump.","a":"Joint, cave, arch, stack, stump."},{"q":"Why does building groynes at one place often speed up erosion further along the coast?","a":"They trap sediment moving in longshore drift, so beaches downdrift receive less material, become thinner, absorb less wave energy and leave the cliff behind them more exposed."},{"q":"Name the rock that forms Old Harry Rocks and the rock that forms Peveril Point at the southern end of Swanage Bay.","a":"Old Harry Rocks are chalk; Peveril Point is Purbeck limestone."},{"q":"Why is there no bay along the coast at Lulworth even though soft Wealden clay lies just inland?","a":"The coast is concordant: a hard band of Portland limestone runs parallel to the shore in front of the soft beds, shielding them, so the sea can only reach them where it has broken through, as at Lulworth Cove."}]},"room":{"facts":["Constructive waves are low and long, break about 6 to 8 times a minute and have a swash stronger than the backwash, so they build a wide, gently sloping beach. Destructive waves are tall and steep, break far more often, one close behind the next, and have a backwash stronger than the swash, so they comb the beach down and leave it steep.","The processes of coastal erosion to learn are hydraulic power, abrasion and attrition. Attrition wears the sediment down, not the cliff.","The erosion sequence on a headland: joint, cave, arch, stack, stump. At the cliff foot: wave-cut notch, collapse of the overhang, cliff retreat, wave-cut platform.","The sand dune sequence inland from the beach: embryo dune, foredune (yellow dune), grey dune, with dune slacks in the damp hollows; marram grass binds the sand from the foredune onwards.","The Isle of Purbeck, Dorset: Durdle Door and Lulworth Cove on the concordant south coast; Peveril Point, Swanage Bay, the chalk headland at Handfast Point with Old Harry Rocks, and Studland Bay with its dunes on the discordant east coast.","Mappleton, Holderness, 1991: two rock groynes and a rock revetment of Norwegian granite, about 2 million pounds, protecting the village and the B1242 coast road; erosion then sped up at Great Cowden to the south."],"questions":[{"q":"State one process by which the sea erodes a cliff. [1 mark]","a":"AO1 = 1 mark. One mark for naming hydraulic power (wave pounding), abrasion (corrasion) or solution (corrosion). The process only has to be named. No credit for attrition, which wears sediment down rather than the cliff.","sol":"Hydraulic power. (Abrasion or solution would also be credited.) Hydraulic power is the force of a breaking wave driving air and water into cracks in the rock, but the question says state, so naming it is enough and no explanation is needed.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The question is about the sea removing rock from the cliff, not about rock breaking down where it stands.","Run through the short list of erosion processes and cross off the one that acts on loose sediment rather than on the cliff face.","The answer is two or three words long and needs no explanation at all."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (coastal processes)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, calculate the difference in the number of waves per minute between the tip of the headland and the centre of the bay. [1 mark]","a":"AO4 = 1 mark. One mark for 6 (waves per minute). Allow the working 13 minus 7.","sol":"The tip of the headland records 13 waves per minute and the centre of the bay records 7. 13 minus 7 = 6 waves per minute.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Both figures you need are in the same column of the table.","Find the row for each of the two named sites and read across to the waves per minute column.","It is a single subtraction, and the answer is a whole number of waves per minute."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (waves)"]},{"q":"Give two characteristics of a destructive wave. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks. One mark each for any two of: tall or steep; short wavelength; high frequency, the waves arriving one close behind the next; backwash stronger than swash; removes material from the beach. No credit for a third answer.","sol":"A destructive wave is tall and steep with a short wavelength, and its backwash is stronger than its swash. Either of those, plus a high breaking frequency, with the waves arriving one close behind the next, would score the two marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the shape of the wave and about which of swash and backwash wins.","Two separate characteristics are wanted, so do not develop one point twice.","Each answer is a short phrase; only the first two will be marked."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (waves)"]},{"q":"Outline how longshore drift moves sediment along a coast. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks. One mark for swash carrying material up the beach at an angle, set by the prevailing wind; second mark for backwash returning it straight down the beach under gravity, giving a zigzag movement along the shore.","sol":"Waves driven by the prevailing wind break at an angle to the shore, so the swash carries sand and shingle obliquely up the beach. The backwash then drains straight back down the steepest line under gravity, at right angles to the water's edge. Each wave therefore shifts the material a little way along the coast in a zigzag, and repeated thousands of times this transports sediment steadily in the direction the waves approach from.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The whole process depends on the waves not arriving straight on.","Deal with the two parts of a breaking wave separately: the water going up the beach and the water coming back.","Two sentences, one for each, and a word for the shape of the path."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (coastal processes)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, describe the landforms of coastal erosion shown in the photograph. [2 marks]","a":"AO4 = 2 marks. One mark for a described observation taken from the photograph, for example a vertical chalk cliff with a notch cut along its foot; an arch through the end of the headland with daylight beneath it; a detached pillar of chalk (stack) standing offshore; a low flat-topped stump beyond it. Second mark for a further observation, or for developing one with detail from the photograph. Description only: no credit for formation.","sol":"The photograph shows a vertical white chalk cliff with a deep notch cut along its foot at the waterline and loose blocks of chalk on the ledge below. At the seaward end of the headland there is an arch, with daylight visible beneath it, and beyond the arch a tall narrow pillar of chalk stands on its own in open water.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The command is describe, so nothing about how the features formed will be credited.","Work along the photograph from the cliff on the left out to the sea on the right, taking each feature in turn.","Two separate observations, each said in a clause, with a detail from the photograph attached to each."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (erosion)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, give one piece of evidence that the coast at Lulworth is concordant. [1 mark]","a":"AO4 = 1 mark. One mark for a point taken from the map, for example the bands of rock run parallel to the shore, or a single band of Portland limestone lies along the whole coast in front of the softer beds.","sol":"On the map the bands of rock at Lulworth run parallel to the shoreline, with the Portland limestone lying along the coast in front of the Purbeck beds, Wealden clays and chalk behind it.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Concordant and discordant are about direction, not about hardness.","Look at how the rock bands on the map are lying compared with the line of the shore.","One short statement taken straight off the map is all that is wanted."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (erosion)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, explain the formation of the arch and the detached pillar of chalk shown in the photograph. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2. Credit a logical chain, using the features in the photograph: joint in the chalk widened by hydraulic power and abrasion into a cave; cave cut through the narrow headland to form the arch; roof undercut and weathered until it collapses, leaving a stack. Max Level 1 for a description of the features alone.","sol":"The headland is chalk, strong enough to stand as a vertical cliff but cut through by vertical joints, and it is those lines of weakness that the sea exploits first. Each breaking wave drives air and water into a joint under pressure and the air expands again as the wave falls back, so hydraulic power prises the crack steadily wider, while abrasion hurls sand and shingle at the same spot and grinds the chalk away. The notch the photograph shows cut along the waterline is that undercutting at work. Concentrated on a joint, it deepens into a cave. Because the seaward end of the headland is narrow, erosion working from both sides eventually meets and cuts the cave right through, leaving the arch with daylight beneath it. The arch is then attacked from below by the same processes and loosened from above by weathering and by gravity, so its roof loses support and collapses. What is left standing offshore, cut off from the headland, is the pillar in the photograph: a stack. The low flat-topped rock beyond it is a stump, what a stack becomes once the sea has worn it down, so the photograph records three stages of one sequence at once.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["A chalk cliff does not fail at random: there is something in the rock itself that decides where the sea gets in first.","Build the answer as an ordered sequence, naming the process that does the work at each stage.","Six or seven sentences in time order, each one moving the sequence on and using a feature visible in the photograph."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (erosion)","3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (coastal processes)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain why headlands and bays have formed along the east-facing coast of the Isle of Purbeck. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2. Credit use of the map: bands meet the sea end-on (discordant); soft Wealden clays cut back into Swanage Bay, and the younger, softer Eocene sands and clays north of the chalk cut back into Studland Bay; resistant Purbeck limestone and chalk left as Peveril Point and Handfast Point; wave refraction concentrating energy on headlands and spreading it in bays. No credit for generic answers that ignore the map.","sol":"Figure 3 shows that on the east-facing coast the rock bands meet the sea end-on, at right angles to the shore: Purbeck limestone at Peveril Point, Wealden clays behind Swanage Bay, then chalk at Ballard Point and Handfast Point. That structure makes this a discordant coast, and it is the reason headlands and bays have formed. The waves arriving are the same all along this shore, but the rocks are not. The Wealden beds are weakly cemented clays and sands, so hydraulic power and abrasion cut them back quickly and the sea has worn them into the curve of Swanage Bay; north of the chalk the map shows Studland Bay, worn in the same way out of the younger, softer Eocene sands and clays on that side of the ridge. The Purbeck limestone and the chalk are far more resistant and have been eroded far more slowly, so they now project seaward as headlands. Once the bays exist the difference is maintained by wave refraction: waves entering the shallow water off a headland slow down and bend round it, so their energy is concentrated on the headland, which is why the caves, arch and stack of Old Harry Rocks are cut there. The same refraction spreads energy out inside the bay, so the waves reaching Swanage are lower and constructive, and the sand they carry is deposited to build the beach shown there.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The answer starts with how the rock bands on the map are lying, not with how hard they are.","Deal with the bays first, then the headlands, then what happens to the waves once the shape exists.","Name the rocks and the places from the map at every stage; a general answer about hard and soft rock is capped low."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (erosion)","3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (example of a section of coastline)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, suggest why there is no beach at the tip of the headland. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2. Credit interpretation of the table: highest wave count and greatest wave height there, so high energy and destructive waves; strong backwash removes material faster than it is supplied; wave refraction concentrates energy on the headland; material carried into the bay, where the table shows a beach. Command word is suggest, so any reasonable interpretation of the resource is credited.","sol":"The table shows that the tip of the headland has both the most frequent waves, at 13 a minute, and the largest, at a mean height of 2.4 m, so it is by far the highest-energy site of the three. Waves breaking that often and that high are destructive: their backwash is stronger than their swash, so any sand or shingle washed onto the rock is dragged straight back out to sea rather than left behind. The headland also projects into deeper water, and waves entering the shallows around it are refracted so that their energy is concentrated on it, which keeps the attack constant. Material eroded from the headland is therefore carried away and deposited where the energy falls, in the shelter of the bay, which is exactly where the table records sand and a gentle gradient. With more material removed than supplied, no beach can accumulate and the bare wave-cut platform is left exposed.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the headland row with the two bay rows before writing anything.","A beach only exists where material is supplied faster than it is taken away, so work out which side of that balance the headland is on.","Two developed points, each using a figure from the table, and say where the missing material has gone."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (waves)","3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (coastal processes)"]},{"q":"Explain how the sand dunes behind Studland Bay have formed. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2, AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2. Credit the sequence: wide sandy beach dries at low tide, onshore wind moves sand by saltation, sand piles against an obstacle to form an embryo dune, marram grass colonises and its roots bind the sand so the foredune grows, older dunes inland gain humus and become grey dunes, hollows reaching the water table form dune slacks.","sol":"Studland Bay has a wide, gently sloping sandy beach in the shelter of the chalk headland, built by constructive waves, and at low tide a large area of it dries out. Onshore winds then move the dry sand inland, rolling and bouncing the grains by saltation. Where the sand meets an obstacle such as a line of driftwood or a clump of seaweed above the high tide line, the wind slows, loses energy and drops its load, and a small embryo dune builds up. Embryo dunes are salty and unstable, but pioneer plants and then marram grass can colonise them; marram has long roots that bind the sand and stiff leaves that trap more of it, so the dune grows upwards and becomes a foredune, sometimes called a yellow dune because bare sand still shows. As the foredune grows it shelters the ground behind it from salt spray, so dead plant material accumulates, humus forms and a fuller plant cover develops: these older dunes inland are grey dunes. Where a hollow between the ridges is cut down to the water table, damp dune slacks form with their own marshy vegetation. The dunes at Studland show this sequence, youngest at the beach and oldest inland.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Before anything can be blown inland, something has to happen to the beach at low tide.","Work inland from the water's edge, and make one plant do most of the work in the middle of the sequence.","Name each dune type in order and say what has changed to make the next one possible."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (deposition)","3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (example of a section of coastline)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, suggest why the coastal management scheme at Mappleton led to conflict. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2, AO3 = 2. Credit interpretation of the extract: groynes trap sediment that drift carried south, so beaches south are thinner and cliffs less protected; erosion at Great Cowden sped up and land was lost; those who gain (village, road users) are not those who pay the cost (landowners to the south). No credit for simply describing the defences.","sol":"The extract says that the groynes trap sediment that longshore drift used to carry south. That is exactly what protects Mappleton: material builds up between the groynes, the beach widens, and waves break further offshore and reach the cliff with less energy. But the same sediment no longer arrives at the coast to the south, so the beaches there are thinner, absorb less wave energy and leave the glacial till cliffs behind them more exposed. On a coast already retreating at roughly 2 metres a year, that is enough to speed the loss up, which is why landowners at Great Cowden found the sea taking their fields and farm buildings faster than before. The conflict follows from who gains and who pays: the villagers and road users of Mappleton got a defended cliff paid for from public funds, while farmers a short way down the coast lost land because of it. Two groups on the same coastline now have opposite interests in the same structures.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Find the sentence in the extract that says what the groynes do to sediment; everything follows from it.","Track the sediment: if it stops at Mappleton, work out what is missing further down the coast and what that means for the cliffs there.","Finish by naming the two groups with opposite interests, because a conflict needs both sides stated."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (example of a coastal management scheme)","3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (costs and benefits)"]},{"q":"'The landforms of a coastline are shaped more by its geology than by its waves.' Using a named section of UK coastline, to what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks]","a":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3. Credit detailed knowledge of a named UK coastline, understanding of the interrelationship between rock type, structure, process and landform, and a reasoned evaluation of the relative importance of geology and wave energy, ending in a substantiated judgement. Answers without a named coastline are capped low.","sol":"On the Isle of Purbeck in Dorset both claims can be tested against each other, because one set of rocks meets the sea in two different ways. The beds there are hard Portland limestone, then Purbeck beds, then soft Wealden clays and sands, then chalk, folded almost upright. On the east-facing coast they meet the sea end-on, and the result is a discordant coast of headlands and bays: the Wealden clays are cut back into Swanage Bay while the Purbeck limestone stands out at Peveril Point and the chalk at Ballard Point and Handfast Point, and north of the chalk the sea has cut Studland Bay into younger, softer sands and clays. Round the corner at Lulworth the identical beds run parallel to the shore, and there are no bays at all, because the band of Portland limestone shields everything behind it. That comparison is strong evidence for the statement. The waves reaching the two coasts come from the same seas, yet the landforms are completely different, so it must be the rock and the way it is arranged that decides where erosion happens. Lulworth Cove makes the point twice over: the sea only produced a bay there once it had broken through a narrow gap in the limestone, and then widened rapidly in the soft beds behind until it reached the resistant chalk, which is why the cove is round with a narrow mouth. Against that, geology explains where but not how, and it cannot make a landform on its own. Old Harry Rocks exist because hydraulic power and abrasion worked along vertical joints in the chalk to cut a cave, then an arch, then a stack; the joints were supplied by the geology but the energy was supplied by waves with a long fetch down the Channel, and wave refraction concentrated that energy on the headland. The beach in Swanage Bay is there because refraction lowers wave energy inside the bay so that constructive waves can deposit sand. On the exposed south coast the same rocks are attacked far harder than in the shelter of the bay, so energy clearly controls the rate. Overall I largely agree, but with a condition. Geology and structure decide the pattern of a coastline, which is why one set of rocks gives Purbeck two utterly different coasts, while the waves decide the speed at which that pattern is cut and the detail of the landforms within it. Neither alone would produce Old Harry Rocks; the geology chose the site, the waves did the work.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The phrase 'to what extent' means you have to decide how far the claim holds, not simply whether it does.","Pick one coastline where the same rocks meet the sea in two different ways, so the comparison does the arguing for you.","Build two developed sides and a conclusion that separates what geology controls from what the waves control."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (example of a section of coastline)","3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (erosion)","3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (waves)"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of a coastal management scheme in a named UK location. [9 marks]","a":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3. Credit detailed knowledge of a named scheme (reasons, strategy, effects and conflicts), understanding of how the strategies work, and a balanced evaluation of how well they met their aim and at what cost, ending in a substantiated judgement. No credit for simply describing the defences.","sol":"Mappleton is a village on the Holderness coast of East Yorkshire, between Hornsea and Withernsea. Holderness runs about 61 km from Flamborough Head to Spurn Head and its cliffs are cut in glacial till, a soft clay and stone deposit left by ice at the end of the last glacial period. Saturated by rain the till slumps, and the coast retreats on average by roughly 2 metres a year, among the fastest rates in Europe. By 1991 the cliff edge had come close to the houses and to the B1242, the direct coast road between the two towns, so hard engineering was chosen: two rock groynes built out from the beach and a rock revetment of large boulders at the cliff foot, using granite shipped in from Norway, at a cost of about 2 million pounds. Judged against its own aim the scheme has worked. The groynes interrupt longshore drift, so sediment moving south has piled up between them and a beach now exists where there was almost none. A wider beach forces waves to break further offshore and lose energy in the shallows, and the revetment absorbs what is left in the gaps between the boulders rather than reflecting it down onto the cliff foot. The cliff behind the village has been held ever since, and the road is still in use, which for a spend of about 2 million pounds is good value against the cost of rebuilding a road and a village inland. The evaluation turns on what happened elsewhere. The scheme works precisely by trapping sediment, and that sediment used to travel south to feed the beaches below. Those beaches are now thinner, absorb less wave energy and leave the till cliffs behind them more exposed, so at Great Cowden, a short way down the coast, erosion sped up and farmland and farm buildings were lost. The landowners there gained nothing from public money spent on the village and lost land because of it, which is the conflict the scheme created. There is a wider cost too: the material eroded from Holderness is carried south to build Spurn Head, so holding it back has effects far beyond the village. My judgement is that the scheme is effective locally and only partly effective as coastal management. It has defended the village and the B1242 for more than thirty years, which is exactly what it was built to do, but it did not reduce erosion on this coast; it relocated it onto people who were not consulted and were not compensated.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Evaluate means judging how well something works, with the evidence on both sides, so decide first what the scheme was actually built to achieve.","Use one named scheme for both halves of the argument: the same mechanism that protects one place is usually what damages another.","Give the place, the date, the strategy and the figures early, then spend most of the answer weighing benefits against costs before a conditional verdict."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (example of a coastal management scheme)","3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (costs and benefits)"]},{"q":"Assess the costs and benefits of using hard engineering to protect a stretch of coast. [9 marks]","a":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3. Credit knowledge of named hard engineering strategies and how each works, understanding of their effects on the coastal system, and an informed overall judgement weighing protection and cost against sediment starvation, appearance, maintenance and effects elsewhere. Reference to a named scheme is expected at the top level.","sol":"Hard engineering means building a structure to stop the sea reaching the land, and its great benefit is certainty. A sea wall, usually concrete and curved at the top, reflects wave energy back out to sea and protects the buildings and land directly behind it; it also serves as a promenade, which matters in a resort. Rock armour, a heap of large boulders at the cliff foot, absorbs wave energy in the gaps between the rocks instead of reflecting it, and is much cheaper and faster to place than a wall. Groynes trap sediment moving in longshore drift, building a wider beach that makes waves break further offshore and reach the cliff with less energy. Gabions, wire cages of rock, do a similar job very cheaply on a small scale. Where the land behind is valuable, these structures are the only realistic option: at Mappleton on the Holderness coast, two rock groynes and a rock revetment of Norwegian granite built in 1991 for about 2 million pounds have held a cliff of glacial till that had been retreating at roughly 2 metres a year, saving the village and the B1242 coast road. The costs are of three kinds. First, money: a sea wall is the most expensive option per metre and every structure needs maintenance, while gabion cages rust and burst within about a decade. Second, appearance and access: imported rock looks out of place on a natural coast, and boulders are hazardous to cross. Third, and most seriously, hard engineering interferes with the coastal system rather than accommodating it. Groynes protect one place by taking sediment from the next, and Mappleton shows the result: beaches south of the village are starved of material, and at Great Cowden erosion sped up and farmland and buildings were lost. A reflecting sea wall can scour away the very beach that was absorbing energy in front of it, undermining its own foundations. My judgement is that hard engineering is worth its costs only where the value of what is protected is high and the consequences downdrift are either small or already accounted for. Defending a town or a main road is justified; defending an open stretch of farmland with groynes is not, because the protection is bought from someone further along the coast. Hard engineering buys time and certainty at a high price, and the price is not always paid by the people who benefit.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Assess means weighing the parts and reaching an informed overall judgement, so sort the costs into types rather than listing them.","Take each structure in turn and explain the mechanism first; the cost usually follows from the same mechanism as the benefit.","Carry one named scheme through both sides of the argument and end with a verdict that states the conditions under which it is worth it."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (costs and benefits)","3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (example of a coastal management scheme)"]},{"q":"'Managed retreat is always a better answer to coastal erosion than hard engineering.' Discuss. [9 marks]","a":"AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3. Credit a balanced discussion: managed retreat is cheap in the long term, creates salt marsh that absorbs wave energy and provides habitat, and works with processes, but loses land and requires compensation; hard engineering gives certain protection for high-value land but is costly and shifts erosion downdrift. Expect a substantiated conclusion that is conditional, not absolute.","sol":"The case for managed retreat, also called coastal realignment, is that it works with the coastal system rather than against it. Low-value land behind the shoreline is deliberately allowed to flood, and salt marsh develops on it. Salt marsh is an efficient absorber of wave energy, so the flood risk to the land further inland falls, and the marsh becomes a habitat for wading birds and a nursery for fish. Once created it needs little maintenance, so the long-term cost is far lower than repeatedly repairing a wall, and it is sustainable as sea levels rise, because the marsh can build upwards and migrate inland instead of being overtopped. It also avoids the worst fault of hard engineering: because nothing traps sediment, the coast further along is not starved. The case against is that land is lost on purpose. Farmers and homeowners have to be compensated or relocated, which is expensive at the outset and deeply unpopular, and no compensation replaces a farm that a family has worked for generations. It is also simply unavailable where the land behind the shore is a town, a power station or a main road, because the value of what would be flooded is far too high. That is why Mappleton, on the Holderness coast, was defended instead: in 1991 two rock groynes and a rock revetment of Norwegian granite were built for about 2 million pounds to hold a till cliff retreating at roughly 2 metres a year, and they have kept the village and the B1242 coast road. Hard engineering delivered a certain, immediate result there that no amount of realignment could have offered. But Mappleton also shows why the statement has force: by trapping the sediment that longshore drift carried south, the scheme starved the beaches below and erosion sped up at Great Cowden, where farmland and farm buildings were lost. I therefore disagree with the word always. Managed retreat is the better answer over most of a coastline like Holderness, where the land is agricultural, the cliffs are soft and defending every kilometre is unaffordable and would only move the problem along. Hard engineering remains the better answer for the short, high-value stretches within it. The real judgement is not which strategy is superior but where each belongs, which is why shoreline management plans defend settlements and let the coast between them retreat.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Discuss means setting out and developing the case on both sides before judging, and the word 'always' in the statement is the part to test.","Argue managed retreat from what salt marsh does to wave energy, and argue hard engineering from a named scheme and the land value behind it.","End by deciding where each belongs on the same coastline rather than declaring one strategy the winner everywhere."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (costs and benefits)","3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (example of a coastal management scheme)","3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (coastal processes)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which type of wave has a swash stronger than its backwash?","a":"A constructive wave."},{"q":"Which process of erosion forces air and water into cracks in a cliff under the weight of a breaking wave?","a":"Hydraulic power."},{"q":"What landform is left when the roof of an arch collapses?","a":"A stack."},{"q":"Which plant is planted in dune regeneration because its roots bind the sand?","a":"Marram grass."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Constructive wave","back":"Low, long wavelength, breaking about 6 to 8 times a minute. Swash stronger than backwash, so it pushes sand up the beach and builds a wide, gently sloping beach. Common in calm weather and in sheltered bays.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (waves)","open":false},{"front":"Destructive wave","back":"Tall and steep, short wavelength, breaking far more often than a constructive wave, one close behind the next. Backwash stronger than swash, so it drags material seaward, combing the beach down and leaving it steep. Common in storms.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (waves)","open":false},{"front":"The processes of coastal erosion","back":"Hydraulic power: air and water forced into cracks. Abrasion: waves hurl sand and shingle at the rock. Attrition: fragments knock together and wear down, so it wears the sediment rather than the cliff.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (coastal processes)","open":false},{"front":"Slumping","back":"Mass movement in a soft cliff of clay or glacial till. Heavy rain saturates the rock, making it heavy and slippery, and a whole block rotates downwards along a curved surface, leaving a concave scar.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (coastal processes)","open":false},{"front":"Longshore drift","back":"Waves driven by the prevailing wind break at an angle, so swash carries sediment obliquely up the beach and backwash returns it straight down under gravity. The zigzag moves material along the coast.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The coast is shaped by a number of physical processes (coastal processes)","open":false},{"front":"Discordant coastline","back":"Bands of hard and soft rock meet the sea end-on, at right angles to the shore. Soft bands erode into bays, resistant bands stand out as headlands, as on the east coast of the Isle of Purbeck.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (erosion)","open":false},{"front":"Wave-cut platform","back":"Erosion cuts a notch between high and low tide, the overhang above it collapses and the cliff retreats. Left behind is a gently sloping rock ledge at the cliff foot, exposed at low tide.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (erosion)","open":false},{"front":"Cave, arch, stack, stump","back":"A joint in a headland is widened by hydraulic power and abrasion into a cave; the cave cuts through to form an arch; the unsupported roof falls, leaving a stack; the stack is worn down to a stump.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (erosion)","open":false},{"front":"Spit","back":"A ridge of sand or shingle built by longshore drift where the coast changes direction or a river mouth interrupts it. The end is often recurved by winds from another direction, with salt marsh behind.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (deposition)","open":false},{"front":"Sand dune succession","back":"Embryo dunes form round obstacles above the high tide line; marram grass binds the sand into a foredune; older grey dunes inland gain humus and full plant cover; damp hollows between them are dune slacks.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (deposition)","open":false},{"front":"Lulworth Cove, Dorset","back":"A near-circular cove on a concordant coast. The sea broke through a narrow gap in the hard Portland limestone, then widened quickly in the soft Purbeck and Wealden beds behind, stopping at the chalk.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (example of a section of coastline)","open":false},{"front":"Old Harry Rocks, Handfast Point, Studland","back":"An arch, a chalk stack and a stump at the seaward tip of the chalk headland on the Dorset coast: the landforms of coastal erosion standing in sequence in a single view.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Distinctive coastal landforms are the result of rock type, structure and physical processes (example of a section of coastline)","open":false},{"front":"Groynes","back":"Timber or rock barriers built out across the beach. They trap sediment moving in longshore drift and build a wider beach that absorbs wave energy, but starve the coast downdrift, where erosion speeds up.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (costs and benefits)","open":false},{"front":"Managed retreat (coastal realignment)","back":"Low-value land is deliberately allowed to flood. Salt marsh develops, absorbing wave energy and creating habitat, and long-term costs are low, but land is lost and owners have to be compensated.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (costs and benefits)","open":false},{"front":"Mappleton, Holderness","back":"1991: two rock groynes and a rock revetment of Norwegian granite, about 2 million pounds, protecting the village and the B1242 coast road. Erosion then sped up at Great Cowden to the south.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Different management strategies can be used to protect coastlines from the effects of physical processes (example of a coastal management scheme)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Photograph A (a description written for this room of a real place). A photograph taken from the clifftop path looking north-east along the chalk headland at Handfast Point, Studland, on the Dorset coast. On the left of the frame a vertical white chalk cliff runs out to sea, with grassy downland behind it. At the waterline a deep notch is cut along the foot of the cliff, and loose blocks of chalk lie on the rock ledge below it. At the far end of the headland the sea has cut right through the chalk, leaving an arch with daylight showing beneath it. Beyond the arch, separated from the headland by open water, stands a tall, narrow pillar of white chalk. Beyond the pillar, a low flat-topped rock just breaks the surface. The tide is low and the sea in the foreground is calm.","Figure 2 — Table B (written for this room; figures illustrative). Wave and beach measurements taken on one January morning at three points along a bay-and-headland coastline.\nSite | Waves per minute | Mean wave height (m) | Beach gradient (degrees) | Beach material\nTip of the headland | 13 | 2.4 | no beach | bare rock platform\nEdge of the bay, next to the headland | 10 | 1.6 | 9 | shingle\nCentre of the bay | 7 | 0.8 | 4 | sand","Figure 3 — Sketch map C (drawn for this room; the place names, rock types and directions are real, the map is not drawn to scale). The map shows the coast of the Isle of Purbeck in Dorset. Along the south-facing coast at Lulworth the bands of rock run parallel to the shore: hard Portland limestone at the sea, then softer Purbeck beds and Wealden clays and sands, then chalk inland. Durdle Door is drawn about a kilometre west of Lulworth Cove as an arch of limestone standing out in the sea; Lulworth Cove is drawn as a near-circular bay with a narrow entrance cut through the limestone and a chalk wall at the back. Round the corner, on the east-facing coast, the same bands meet the sea end-on, at right angles to the shore. From south to north the map shows Peveril Point, a low headland of Purbeck limestone; Swanage Bay, cut into the Wealden clays and sands, with a sandy beach and a line of groynes along the town front; Ballard Point and Handfast Point, a single headland of chalk, with Old Harry Rocks drawn at its tip as an arch, a stack and a stump; and, north of the chalk ridge, Studland Bay, cut into the younger, softer Eocene sands and clays that lie beyond it, with a wide sandy beach backed by sand dunes. An arrow in the corner shows the prevailing wind blowing from the south-west.","Figure 4 — Extract D (written for this room; the places, dates and figures are real). The Holderness coast in East Yorkshire runs for about 61 km from the chalk of Flamborough Head in the north to the spit at Spurn Head in the south. Its cliffs are cut in glacial till — soft clay and stones dumped by ice at the end of the last glacial period — and they retreat on average by roughly 2 metres a year, among the fastest rates in Europe. At the village of Mappleton the cliff edge had come close to the houses and to the B1242 coast road linking Hornsea and Withernsea. In 1991 two rock groynes and a rock revetment at the foot of the cliff were built there, using granite shipped in from Norway, at a cost of about 2 million pounds. A beach has since built up between the groynes and the cliff behind the village has been held. The groynes also trap sediment that longshore drift used to carry south, and landowners at Great Cowden, a short way down the coast, found the sea taking their fields and farm buildings faster than before."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the named section of UK coastline: the Isle of Purbeck, Dorset","items":["Where: the Isle of Purbeck on the Dorset coast of south-west England, from Durdle Door in the west round to Studland Bay in the north-east.","Status: this stretch lies on the Jurassic Coast, inscribed by UNESCO as a World Heritage Site in 2001 and running about 153 km (95 miles) from Orcombe Point at Exmouth in East Devon to Old Harry Rocks at Studland.","The rocks, from the sea inland on the south coast: Portland limestone (hard), Purbeck beds, Wealden clays and sands (soft), chalk (moderately resistant).","The structure: the beds were folded so that many of them now stand almost upright. They run parallel to the south-facing coast, making it concordant, and end-on against the east-facing coast, making it discordant.","Landform of erosion — Durdle Door: an arch cut through a near-vertical band of Portland limestone, about a kilometre west of Lulworth Cove.","Landform of erosion — Lulworth Cove: a near-circular cove with a narrow entrance, formed where the sea broke through the Portland limestone and then widened rapidly in the soft beds behind, halted at the back by the chalk.","Landforms of erosion — Old Harry Rocks at Handfast Point, Studland: an arch, a chalk stack and a stump at the seaward tip of the chalk headland, the eastern end of the chalk ridge that runs across southern England.","Headlands and bays on the discordant east coast: Peveril Point (Purbeck limestone) and Ballard Point with Handfast Point (chalk) project seaward; Swanage Bay, between the two headlands, is cut into the softer Wealden beds, and Studland Bay, north of the chalk rather than between them, into the younger, softer Eocene sands and clays.","Landform of deposition — Swanage Bay: a sandy beach built by constructive waves in the shelter of the bay, held in place by a line of groynes along the town front.","Landform of deposition — the dunes behind Studland Bay: an active dune system running inland from the beach, from embryo dunes near the shore to older vegetated dunes behind, part of a National Nature Reserve at Studland.","Deposition further west on the same Dorset coast — Chesil Beach: a shingle barrier about 29 km long joining the Isle of Portland to the mainland and enclosing the Fleet lagoon, its pebbles graded from pea-sized at the western end to cobbles at the Portland end.","Wind and waves: the prevailing wind is from the south-west, so the fetch is long and the south-facing coast takes the highest-energy waves, while the east-facing bays are far more sheltered.","Why examiners like it: one set of rocks produces a concordant coast and a discordant coast within a few kilometres, so it can be used to argue either side of a question about geology and waves."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the named UK coastal management scheme: Mappleton, Holderness","items":["Where: Mappleton, a village on the Holderness coast of East Yorkshire, between the towns of Hornsea and Withernsea.","The coast: Holderness runs about 61 km from Flamborough Head in the north to Spurn Head in the south, and its cliffs are cut in glacial till — clay and stones left by ice at the end of the last glacial period.","Reason for management (the process): the till is soft and slumps once heavy rain saturates it, so the coast retreats on average by roughly 2 metres a year, among the fastest rates of coastal erosion in Europe.","Reason for management (what was at risk): by 1991 the cliff edge had come close to the houses of the village and to the B1242, the direct coast road between Hornsea and Withernsea.","The strategy, 1991: hard engineering — two rock groynes built out from the beach and a rock revetment of large boulders placed at the foot of the cliff.","The material: granite, shipped in from Norway because no suitable hard rock is available locally on a coast made of clay.","The cost: about 2 million pounds.","Effect on the village: sediment moving south in longshore drift is trapped against the groynes, a beach has built up between them, waves now break further offshore and lose energy, and the cliff behind the village has been held.","Effect down the coast: less sediment passes south, so the beaches there are thinner, absorb less wave energy and leave the till cliffs behind them more exposed.","The conflict: at Great Cowden, a short distance south of the village, erosion sped up after 1991 and farmland and farm buildings were lost; the landowners had gained nothing from the scheme and argued that a village had been saved at their expense.","The wider conflict: most of the Holderness coastline is left undefended, so people whose land is being lost ask why public money was spent defending one village and one B road and not their own property.","The system-wide point: the till eroded from Holderness is carried south by longshore drift and is the material that builds Spurn Head, so trapping sediment at Mappleton has consequences far beyond the village.","The judgement an examiner rewards: the scheme met its own aim and has held the cliff for more than thirty years, but because it works by trapping sediment it moved the erosion south rather than stopping it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the processes and terms this room is marked on","items":["Fetch: the open distance of sea a wind crosses. The longer the fetch, the more energy the waves carry when they break.","Swash and backwash: the water running up the beach after a wave breaks, and the water draining back down it under gravity.","Constructive wave: low, long wavelength, about 6 to 8 breaks a minute, swash stronger than backwash, builds a wide, gently sloping beach.","Destructive wave: tall, steep, short wavelength, breaking far more often than a constructive wave, backwash stronger than swash, combs the beach down and leaves it steep.","Freeze-thaw weathering: water in a crack freezes and expands, prising the rock apart; repeated cycles shatter the cliff face.","Salt weathering: seawater evaporates in pores and cracks and the growing salt crystals push the rock apart.","Carbonation: rainwater with dissolved carbon dioxide forms a weak acid that slowly dissolves limestone and chalk.","Rockfall: blocks break free from a steep, hard cliff face and fall to the foot of the cliff.","Slumping: saturated clay or glacial till rotates downwards along a curved surface, leaving a concave scar and a bulge at the cliff foot.","Hydraulic power: the force of a breaking wave driving air and water into cracks, which widens them as the pressure is released.","Abrasion: waves hurl sand and shingle at the cliff, wearing it away like sandpaper.","Attrition: transported fragments knock against each other and become smaller, smoother and rounder — it wears the sediment, not the cliff.","Solution (corrosion): weak acids in seawater slowly dissolve soluble rock such as limestone and chalk — the chemical side of the sea’s attack, alongside the erosion done by hydraulic power, abrasion and attrition.","Traction, saltation, suspension, solution: boulders rolled along the bed, pebbles bounced, fine particles carried within the water, minerals carried dissolved.","Longshore drift: swash carries material obliquely up the beach at the angle of the prevailing wind, backwash returns it straight down, and the zigzag moves it along the coast.","Why deposition happens: waves lose energy in sheltered water, behind an obstacle such as a spit or island, where the coastline changes direction, or when constructive waves swash more material up than the backwash removes.","Discordant coast: rock bands meet the sea end-on, giving headlands and bays. Concordant coast: rock bands run parallel to the shore, giving a straight coast until the sea breaks through.","Wave refraction: waves bend as they enter shallow water off a headland, concentrating energy on the headland and spreading it out inside the bay.","Wave-cut notch and wave-cut platform: erosion between the tides undercuts the cliff, the overhang collapses, the cliff retreats and a gently sloping rock ledge is left, exposed at low tide.","Berm and storm beach: ridges of material left at the high tide line by constructive waves, and the coarsest material thrown to the back of the beach by storm waves.","Spit and bar: a drift-built ridge of sand or shingle growing out from the shore where the coast changes direction; a spit that has grown right across a bay to trap a lagoon behind it.","Sea wall: reflects wave energy and protects the land behind, doubles as a promenade; the most expensive option per metre, needs maintenance, and can scour the beach in front of it away.","Rock armour: boulders at the cliff foot absorb wave energy in the gaps between them; cheap and quick, but usually imported, hazardous to cross and can be shifted by the largest storms.","Gabions: wire cages of rock absorbing wave energy; very cheap, but the cages rust and burst within about a decade.","Groynes: trap drifting sediment and build a wider beach that makes waves break early; starve the coast downdrift, where erosion speeds up.","Beach nourishment: sand or shingle added, usually dredged offshore; looks natural and absorbs energy, but is expensive, must be repeated and can damage the seabed where the material is dredged.","Beach reprofiling: material moved from the lower beach back to the upper beach, usually after winter storms; cheap, but short-lived and only suited to lower-energy coasts.","Dune regeneration: marram planting and fenced boardwalks so that dunes keep absorbing wave energy; cheap and good for habitat, but easily trampled or storm-damaged and it restricts access.","Managed retreat (coastal realignment): low-value land is allowed to flood and salt marsh develops, absorbing wave energy and creating habitat at low long-term cost; land is lost and owners must be compensated."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) on the 9-mark question: 'The landforms of a coastline are shaped more by its geology than by its waves.' Using a named section of UK coastline, to what extent do you agree?","items":["The paragraph: Geology sets the shape of the Isle of Purbeck coast before the waves do anything at all. On the east-facing coast the Purbeck limestone, the Wealden clays and the chalk meet the sea end-on, so the same Channel waves have cut the weak Wealden beds back into Swanage Bay while the limestone of Peveril Point and the chalk of Ballard Point are left standing out as headlands. Round the corner at Lulworth the identical beds run parallel to the shore, and there the waves have produced no bays at all: they could only reach the soft rock where they broke through the narrow gap in the Portland limestone, and having broken through they widened it rapidly into the round shape of Lulworth Cove before the chalk stopped them. Same waves, same rocks, different structure, different coast — which is strong evidence that on this coastline it is the arrangement of the beds, not the energy of the sea, that decides where erosion happens.","The point it makes: structure, not wave energy, determines where the sea cuts a bay.","The named evidence: the Isle of Purbeck, Dorset; Purbeck limestone at Peveril Point, Wealden clays behind Swanage Bay, chalk at Ballard Point; Portland limestone and Lulworth Cove on the concordant south coast.","The developed chain: bands end-on to the sea means the weak band can be attacked directly and cut back into a bay, while bands parallel to the sea means the resistant band shields everything behind it, so a bay can only form where the sea breaches it.","The link and the mini-judgement: the same waves acting on the same rocks give two different coasts a few kilometres apart, so structure is doing the deciding — stated as a conclusion the evidence supports, not as an opinion.","The descriptor it meets: Level 3 (Detailed) — detailed knowledge of places and processes, thorough understanding of the interrelationship between rock, structure and process, and application of that understanding in a reasoned way to evaluate the relative importance of geology and waves."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–3) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The paragraph: The Dorset coast has lots of landforms. There are bays and headlands because some of the rock is harder than the other rock. The soft rock gets worn away by the waves and the hard rock is left sticking out into the sea. Old Harry Rocks is a stack there and Lulworth Cove is a cove. So geology is more important than waves.","What it does have: the right general idea, that differences in rock resistance produce headlands and bays, and two correct place names from the right coastline.","The descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic) — limited knowledge of places and processes, slight understanding of the interrelationships between them, and limited application in evaluating.","The descriptor it misses — knowledge: no rock is named. 'Hard rock' and 'soft rock' are not Purbeck limestone, Wealden clay and chalk, and no process of erosion is named anywhere.","The descriptor it misses — understanding: nothing is said about structure. Because the arrangement of the beds is never mentioned, the answer cannot explain why Lulworth has a cove and Swanage has a bay.","The descriptor it misses — evaluation: the last sentence asserts a verdict without weighing anything against it. The command phrase 'to what extent' needs the case for the waves to be put and then judged, and here it is never put at all.","The one change that would lift it most: take the two named landforms it already has and say which rock each is cut in and which process cut it, then set Lulworth against Swanage as a comparison."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Evaluate the effectiveness of a coastal management scheme in a named UK location. [9 marks]","items":["Decoding the command word: evaluate means judging how well something works, with the evidence on both sides. The first move is to decide what the scheme was built to achieve, because effectiveness can only be measured against its own aim. Describing the defences earns nothing on its own.","Timing: roughly a minute a mark, so about nine or ten minutes of writing, plus a minute to plan and a minute at the end to read it back — where the question carries marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms, that final minute is the cheapest way to earn them.","Opening, one sentence: name the scheme, the place and the reason it was needed. Mappleton, on the Holderness coast of East Yorkshire, where cliffs of glacial till retreat by roughly 2 metres a year and by 1991 the sea had come close to the village and the B1242 coast road.","Paragraph 1 — what the scheme is: two rock groynes and a rock revetment at the cliff foot, built in 1991 using granite shipped from Norway, at a cost of about 2 million pounds. Keep it to two sentences; the detail is there to be used, not admired.","Paragraph 2 — how well it did its job: the groynes interrupt longshore drift, a beach has built up between them, waves now break further offshore and arrive with less energy, and the revetment absorbs what is left instead of reflecting it down onto the cliff. Mini-judgement: judged on the village and the road alone, it works, and it has worked for over thirty years.","Paragraph 3 — the price paid elsewhere: the sediment trapped at Mappleton no longer feeds the coast to the south, so the beaches there thinned and at Great Cowden the till cliffs lost their protection and retreated faster, taking fields and farm buildings. Mini-judgement: the erosion was not reduced, it was relocated.","Paragraph 4 — cost against value: about 2 million pounds of imported rock to hold a village and a B road on a coast where most of the shoreline is left undefended, and where the eroded till is the material that builds Spurn Head. Mini-judgement: whether that is good value depends entirely on whose land is being counted.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: effective for whom, and for how long? Raise it in the first judgement and return to it in each one, so the conclusion is the end of an argument rather than a new idea.","What the conclusion must say: a judgement, not a summary. The scheme met its own aim and has held the cliff at Mappleton since 1991, so it is effective locally; but because it works by trapping sediment it passed the problem south onto people who were not consulted and were not compensated, so as a way of managing this coastline it is only partly effective.","What loses marks here: describing the groynes and revetment for half the answer; writing about Holderness erosion in general without ever naming the scheme; giving advantages and disadvantages as two lists with no weighing; ending with 'so it was quite effective' and no reason attached."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:58:47.127044Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Every fault in the previous verdict is properly fixed rather than papered over: Studland Bay is now the younger, softer Eocene sands and clays north of the Ballard Down chalk ridge in all six places, Figure 3 states that rock type so the 'Using Figure 3' key and model answer can be read off the resource, Swanage Bay correctly stays Wealden, the tombolo and the fourth erosion process are gone, the unverified destructive-wave frequency is replaced by a qualitative comparison that no longer collides with Figure 2's 10 waves a minute, and question 4 is now the attested 2-mark AO4 block with the key, model answer and third hint all re-cut to two observations; the tariffs across the room are 1, 2, 4, 6 and 9 only, every countable claim recounts correctly against Figures 1-4, and the Purbeck and Mappleton case-study detail is sound throughout."}}},"3.1.3.3":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.3.3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"River landscapes is one of the three landscape options in Section C of Paper 1, and it is the one most schools teach. Fifteen marks hang on it: short map, graph and calculation questions, a four-mark question suggesting effects from a resource, and a six-mark question asking you to explain the formation of the landforms shown in a photograph or on a map. Almost every mark is earned by chaining a named process to a named landform, or by reading a hydrograph or an Ordnance Survey extract properly. Two named examples are compulsory — a UK river valley and a UK flood management scheme — and an answer without them cannot reach the top level.","idea":[{"h":"The long profile and the changing cross profile","t":"The long profile is the river seen from the side, source to mouth: steep and stepped in the upper course, flattening into a smooth concave curve towards the sea. The cross profile is a slice across channel and valley at one point: in the upper course vertical erosion cuts a narrow V-shaped valley with steep sides and a shallow, rocky channel; by the lower course lateral erosion and deposition have opened it into a wide, flat-floored valley with gentle sides and a deep, smooth channel. The surprise is that velocity rises downstream even though the gradient falls, because a deeper, smoother channel wastes far less energy on friction. A top-level answer never simply describes the two profiles: it uses them as a frame, saying which process dominates where and therefore which landform belongs where.","code":"3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (long and cross profiles)"},{"h":"Erosion, transportation and deposition","t":"Four processes erode: hydraulic action, the force of water and trapped air widening cracks; abrasion, the load scraping the bed and banks; attrition, load particles wearing each other smaller and rounder; and solution, soluble rock such as limestone dissolving. Erosion is vertical in the upper course, cutting down, and lateral in the middle and lower courses, cutting sideways. Four processes transport: traction, saltation, suspension and solution. Deposition happens whenever velocity falls — inside a bend, on the flood plain the moment the river leaves its banks, in low flow, and at the mouth where the current meets the tide. The mark scheme is blunt about this: there is no credit for an answer that does not explain the mechanism, so a top-level answer names the process and then shows what it physically does to the rock or the sediment.","code":"3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (fluvial processes)"},{"h":"Landforms of erosion: interlocking spurs, waterfalls and gorges","t":"In the upper course the river has little discharge and a large, angular bedload, so it cuts downwards and winds around ridges of harder ground it cannot erode through: these are interlocking spurs. Where the river crosses a band of hard rock lying over softer rock, the soft rock is cut back faster by hydraulic action and abrasion — differential erosion — leaving the hard cap overhanging. The overhang collapses into the plunge pool, whose swirling blocks deepen it by abrasion, and each collapse moves the fall upstream, leaving a steep-sided gorge of recession behind it. A top-level answer turns that into one continuous chain, naming the rocks and the processes at each link, and takes its evidence from the photograph or map in front of it rather than from memory alone.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)"},{"h":"Meanders, ox-bow lakes and the depositional landforms of the lower course","t":"On a bend the deepest, fastest water swings to the outside, so hydraulic action and abrasion undercut it into a river cliff, while the shallow, slow water on the inside deposits its bedload as a slip-off slope. Erosion and deposition on opposite banks make the loop migrate until the neck between two bends is narrow enough for a flood to cut straight across; deposition seals the old loop off as a crescent-shaped ox-bow lake. When a river floods it slows the instant it leaves its channel, dropping the coarsest sediment first as raised levées and the finer silt further out, building a flat, fertile flood plain; at the mouth the meeting of river and tide drops the rest as the mudflats and saltmarsh of an estuary. A top-level answer explains each landform as a change in energy, not as a list of stages.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion and deposition)"},{"h":"A named UK river valley: the Tees from Cross Fell to Teesmouth","t":"The River Tees rises on Cross Fell, 893 m, the highest point of the Pennines, and runs roughly east for about 137 km to the North Sea at Teesmouth. It gives you every landform on the specification in one valley: interlocking spurs in upper Teesdale; Cauldron Snout, a long cascade over the stepped dolerite of the Whin Sill below Cow Green Reservoir; High Force, a single drop of about 21 m where the same hard dolerite caps softer limestone, sandstone and shale, with a plunge pool and a gorge below it; large meanders at Yarm, where the town sits inside a loop with the river on three sides; a broad flood plain and levées through Stockton-on-Tees; and mudflats and saltmarsh at Seal Sands in the estuary. A top-level answer places each landform in its course and says which process put it there, and can point to the same evidence on a large-scale map — contours crowded in the upper course, far apart across the flood plain.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (example of a river valley)"},{"h":"Flood risk and the storm hydrograph","t":"Four things decide how quickly rain becomes river water. Precipitation: long or very intense rain saturates the soil so the rest runs off. Geology: impermeable clay or granite stops infiltration, while chalk and sandstone soak water up and release it slowly. Relief: steep slopes move water downhill faster than gentle ones. Land use: tarmac, roofs and drains are impermeable and deliver water to the channel in minutes, whereas woodland intercepts rain and takes it up through roots. A storm hydrograph puts rainfall and discharge on the same time axis; the lag time between peak rainfall and peak discharge, together with the height of the peak and the steepness of the rising limb, is the measure of flood risk. A top-level answer quotes figures from the hydrograph and then explains them with those four factors, rather than describing the shape of the line.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (flood risk)"},{"h":"Hard and soft engineering: the costs and the benefits","t":"Hard engineering builds against the river. Dams and reservoirs hold back the peak and generate power and water supply, but are hugely expensive, drown land and trap the sediment the flood plain needs. Straightening moves water away quickly, but sends the problem downstream. Embankments are cheap and let land be used right up to the channel, but are ugly and catastrophic if they fail. Flood relief channels lower levels where people live, but cost a great deal and can raise risk below the point where they rejoin the river. Soft engineering works with it: flood warnings and preparation save lives and possessions cheaply but save no buildings; flood plain zoning prevents future damage but cannot undo what is already built; tree planting and river restoration slow run-off and improve habitat cheaply but act slowly and take land out of farming. A top-level answer weighs one against the other for a particular place rather than listing both.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (costs and benefits)"},{"h":"A named UK flood management scheme: the Jubilee River","t":"Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton stand on the flood plain of the Thames on land that flooded badly in 1947, and their centres cannot be moved. The Environment Agency's answer was the Jubilee River, a man-made flood relief channel about 11.6 km long, opened in 2002 at a cost of about £110 million, which leaves the Thames near Taplow, curves north of the three towns and returns the water near Datchet, its share of the flow set by gates and weirs. The three towns stayed dry in January 2003 and were largely spared in February 2014, but Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor, below the point where the channel rejoins the river, flooded on both occasions; scour damaged some weirs; and the landscaped channel, good for wetland birds and footpaths, has suffered algal blooms in warm weather. A top-level answer uses exactly this split — the scheme succeeded where it was aimed and moved risk elsewhere — to build a judgement rather than a description.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (example of a flood management scheme)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading lag time and peak discharge from Figure 1","setup":"Figure 1 (the hydrograph data table written for this room) gives discharge in two small drainage basins after one storm. The same storm dropped 30 mm of rain on both basins over three hours, with the heaviest rain in hour 2. Basin A is mostly woodland on permeable sandstone; basin B is mostly built-up, on impermeable clay. Hours after the storm began | Discharge in basin A (cumecs) | Discharge in basin B (cumecs) — 0 | 5 | 5 · 2 | 6 | 8 · 4 | 9 | 22 · 6 | 14 | 31 · 8 | 19 | 24 · 10 | 22 | 16 · 12 | 20 | 11 · 14 | 16 | 8 · 16 | 12 | 6 · 18 | 9 | 5. Work out the lag time of each basin and compare the two responses.","steps":["Scan each column for its highest figure: basin A peaks at 22 cumecs in hour 10, basin B at 31 cumecs in hour 6.","Lag time runs from peak rainfall to peak discharge, and the rain peaked in hour 2, so basin A's lag time is 10 − 2 = 8 hours and basin B's is 6 − 2 = 4 hours.","Set the two peaks against each other: basin B's is 31 − 22 = 9 cumecs higher and it arrives in half the time, although the same 30 mm of rain fell on both basins.","Read the falling limbs to finish the comparison: by the end of the table basin B is already back to its base flow of 5 cumecs while basin A is still falling through 9 cumecs, so the urban clay basin fills and empties about twice as fast as the wooded one."],"cues":["Which row holds the biggest figure in each column?","Which two moments does lag time run between?","How much higher is basin B's peak, and how much sooner does it come?","What have the two basins done by the end of the table?"]},{"title":"Explaining the waterfall and gorge in Figure 2","setup":"Figure 2 (the photograph description written for this room) shows High Force on the River Tees: a single white fall about 21 m high dropping into a dark pool; a thick band of dark grey rock at the lip, overhanging; paler, thinly layered rock set back beneath it at the rear of the pool; angular blocks of the dark rock lying in the pool; near-vertical rock walls close on either side of the river downstream; and a broad, open, gently sloping valley above the falls. Using Figure 2, explain the formation of the landforms shown. [6 marks]","steps":["Start by naming what has to be explained: a waterfall, a plunge pool at its foot and a narrow gorge running downstream from it.","Read the geology out of the picture: the dark band at the lip is hard dolerite of the Whin Sill and the pale layered rock set back beneath it is softer limestone, sandstone and shale.","Bring in the processes: hydraulic action drives water and air into the bedding planes of the soft rock while abrasion hurls sand and pebbles against it, so the soft rock is cut back faster than the cap — differential erosion, which is why there is an overhang.","Develop the consequence: the unsupported cap collapses into the pool, and those fallen blocks are then swirled round by the falling water and drill the pool deeper by abrasion.","Close the chain: each collapse shifts the lip a little upstream, so over thousands of years the retreating fall leaves behind exactly the steep-walled gorge the picture shows below the pool."],"cues":["Which three landforms are in the frame?","What do the two rock bands tell you?","Which processes attack the lower band?","What happens to rock with nothing beneath it?","Where does the lip of the fall go next?"]},{"title":"Suggesting economic effects of a flood from Figure 3","setup":"Figure 3 (the sketch map of the River Tees at Yarm drawn for this room) shows the river entering from the west and swinging north, then east, then south, so the town lies inside a loop with water on three sides; the High Street, lined with shops, pubs and offices, runs north to south down the middle of the loop; a road bridge crosses the river at the north end of the High Street and a railway viaduct crosses the loop; the neck of land at the south is the only land route in; the land on both sides of the river is flat and open for several hundred metres; illustrative spot heights are 12 m by the road bridge, 13 m halfway down the High Street, 14 m at the neck and 23 m on the hill beyond the river to the north. Using Figure 3, suggest the economic effects that a flood of the Tees would have on Yarm. [4 marks]","steps":["Pick the map evidence that costs money: everything inside the loop lies within two metres of the level at the bridge, and the businesses are concentrated along one street in the middle of it.","Turn the first piece of evidence into a developed effect: flood water in the shops and pubs on the High Street ruins stock and fittings, and the owners lose weeks of trade while the buildings are dried out and refitted.","Develop a second, different effect from the map: the bridge and the neck are the only ways in and out, so if they are cut the town's workers cannot reach their jobs and deliveries stop, which costs businesses money even where no water reaches them.","Push the interpretation from limited to full, which is what lifts the answer out of Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) and into Level 2 (Clear, 3–4) on this grid: the lost trade and the repair bills are immediate, but the higher insurance premiums and harder property sales afterwards keep the cost running for years."],"cues":["Which map details imply money at risk?","What happens to a flooded shop's income?","What if the bridge and neck are cut?","Which of these costs lasts longest?"]},{"title":"Reading a meander and its future from Figure 3","setup":"Figure 3 (the sketch map of the River Tees at Yarm drawn for this room) marks, on the northern bend, a steep bank and a line of trees on the outer, northern side of the channel, and a shingle bank with a flat green strip on the inner, town side of the same bend; the loop takes the river round three sides of the town and the neck of land at the south is the narrowest part; the land either side is flat and open for several hundred metres. Using Figure 3, explain how the bend shown formed and how it could one day become an ox-bow lake. [6 marks]","steps":["Match each mark on the map to a process: a steep bank with trees on the outside is a river cliff, and a shingle bank on the inside is a slip-off slope.","Explain why they are opposite each other: the deepest, fastest water swings to the outside of the bend, where hydraulic action and abrasion undercut the bank, while the shallow, slow water on the inside has too little energy to carry its bedload and drops it.","Develop what that does over time: because erosion and deposition work permanently on opposite banks, the loop migrates outwards and downstream and the neck at the south is squeezed narrower.","Finish the sequence: in a large flood the river cuts straight across the neck, takes the shorter, steeper route, then deposits silt across the ends of the old loop until the abandoned bend is sealed off as a crescent-shaped ox-bow lake."],"cues":["What landform does each bank mark show?","Why is one bank steep and the other flat?","What happens to the loop over time?","What finally cuts the bend off?"]},{"title":"Building a nine-mark judgement from Figure 4","setup":"Figure 4 (the text extract on the Jubilee River written for this room) records that Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton sit on the Thames flood plain on land that flooded in 1947; that the Environment Agency built a flood relief channel about 11.6 km long from Taplow to Datchet, opened in 2002, costing about £110 million, with gates and weirs controlling the share of the flow it takes; that in January 2003 the three towns stayed dry, and that in February 2014 they were largely spared, while Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor downstream flooded on both occasions; that scour around some weirs needed repair; and that the landscaped channel carries footpaths and wetland birds but suffers algal blooms in warm weather. Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, assess the effectiveness of the scheme. [9 marks]","steps":["Sort the evidence into two columns before writing a word: it worked — the three towns stayed dry in 2003 and were largely spared in 2014; it did not — the villages below the outfall flooded in both floods, the weirs scoured and the price ran far over budget.","Attach a process to each column so the answer explains rather than lists: taking a share of the discharge out of the main channel lowers the level through the towns, but that water goes back into the same river at the far end of the 11.6 km channel, near Datchet, where the Thames is no larger than before.","Weigh the money and the environment against each other: about £110 million plus repair costs on one side; reed beds, footpaths and habitat on the other, offset by the algal blooms that slow-moving water brings.","Write the judgement the top level is awarded for: highly effective for the places it was built to defend, only partly effective across the catchment, because the risk was redistributed rather than removed."],"cues":["Which facts count for, and which against?","Why does protection upstream cost someone downstream?","What do the money and the habitat add?","So what is the overall verdict?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A river's velocity is higher at its mouth than at its source, even though the gradient there is gentler. Why?","a":"The channel in the lower course is deeper, wider and smoother, so far less of the river's energy is lost to friction with the bed and banks."},{"q":"Which processes work on which bank of a meander, and why?","a":"Erosion by hydraulic action and abrasion on the outer bank, where the water is deepest and fastest, cutting a river cliff; deposition on the inner bank, where it is shallow and slow, building a slip-off slope."},{"q":"Two basins get the same storm: one is woodland on chalk, one is housing on clay. Which has the shorter lag time, and why?","a":"The housing on clay. Clay is impermeable and roofs, tarmac and drains prevent infiltration, so water reaches the channel in minutes as surface run-off instead of slowly as throughflow."},{"q":"Why can a flood relief channel protect one town and increase the risk to another?","a":"It takes water out of the river above the town it defends and returns it to the same river below, so communities downstream can be left arguing that the peak now reaches them sooner and higher than it otherwise would — the case residents below the Jubilee River make, and one the Environment Agency contests."}]},"room":{"facts":["Discharge = cross-sectional area × velocity, measured in cumecs (m³/s). Lag time is the gap between peak rainfall and peak discharge on a storm hydrograph.","The four processes of erosion are hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition and solution; the four processes of transportation are traction, saltation, suspension and solution.","The River Tees rises at Tees Head on the flanks of Cross Fell, whose summit at 893 m is the highest point of the Pennines, and flows about 137 km east to the North Sea at Teesmouth; High Force is a single drop of about 21 m where hard dolerite of the Whin Sill caps softer limestone, sandstone and shale.","The Jubilee River flood relief channel is about 11.6 km long, opened in 2002, cost about £110 million and protects Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton; Datchet and Wraysbury downstream flooded in January 2003 and February 2014.","A four-mark answer is marked in two levels (1 Basic 1–2, 2 Clear 3–4); a six-mark answer in three (1 Basic 1–2, 2 Clear 3–4, 3 Detailed 5–6); a nine-mark answer in three (1 Basic 1–3, 2 Clear 4–6, 3 Detailed 7–9).","Allow about a minute a mark: roughly five minutes on a four-mark question, seven on a six-mark explanation and ten on a nine-mark judgement.","Where a question says 'Using Figure…', evidence from that figure is compulsory: an answer that ignores the resource is capped at the lowest level, and there is no credit for features that cannot be seen in it."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, state the peak discharge recorded in basin B. [1 mark]","a":"AO4 = 1 mark. One mark for the correct answer: 31 cumecs (m³/s), reached in hour 6.","sol":"Work down the basin B column and compare each value with the one above it: 5, 8, 22, 31, then 24, 16, 11, 8, 6, 5. The highest figure the column reaches is 31 cumecs, six hours after the storm began, so the peak discharge is 31 cumecs. Give the units — the same figure without cumecs or m³/s is not a discharge.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The peak of a hydrograph is the single highest discharge reached, not the point where the rise is steepest.","Take one column of Figure 1 at a time and compare each value with the one above it until the values start to fall.","Write the value with its units, and you may add the hour at which it occurs."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (flood risk)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, calculate the lag time for basin A. Show how you worked it out. [2 marks]","a":"AO4 = 2 marks. One mark for indication of the calculation (heaviest rain in hour 2; peak discharge of 22 cumecs in hour 10); one mark for the correct answer: 8 hours.","sol":"Lag time is the time between peak rainfall and peak discharge. The note above the table in Figure 1 says the heaviest rain fell in hour 2. Reading down the basin A column, the discharge rises 5, 6, 9, 14, 19 to its highest value of 22 cumecs in hour 10, then falls. So the lag time is 10 − 2 = 8 hours. Set the subtraction out to earn the working mark and give the unit with the answer.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Lag time is measured between two peaks; the start of the rain is not one of them.","The hour of heaviest rain is written above the table; the hour of highest discharge has to be read out of the basin A column.","Set the two hours out as a subtraction and give the answer in hours."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (flood risk)"]},{"q":"Give the name of the process by which a river bounces pebbles along its bed. [1 mark]","a":"AO1 = 1 mark. One mark for the correct answer: saltation. The process only has to be named.","sol":"Saltation. Pebbles and coarse sand that are too heavy to be carried within the water, but light enough to be lifted briefly by turbulence, hop or bounce along the bed in a series of short jumps. It is one of the four processes of transportation, alongside traction (rolling), suspension (fine material carried in the flow) and solution (dissolved minerals).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is one of the four ways a river moves its load, not one of the ways it erodes.","Sort the four transport processes by the size of material each one moves and by whether it touches the bed.","One word is enough; no explanation is needed."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (fluvial processes)"]},{"q":"Outline how attrition reduces the size of the material a river carries. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks. One mark for the general idea of collision between load particles, one for the development that repeated impacts chip them so they become smaller and rounder downstream. No credit for an answer that does not explain the mechanism, such as 'the rocks break up'.","sol":"As a river transports its load, the boulders, pebbles and sand grains are thrown against one another and against the bed by the turbulent flow. Each collision knocks off small fragments and blunts the sharp edges, so the same particle becomes steadily smaller and rounder the further it travels. That is why the bedload in the upper course is large and angular while the material on a lower-course bed is fine and smooth. Attrition wears down the load itself, not the channel — the process that wears the channel is abrasion.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Attrition acts on the load, not on the bed and banks; the two are easy to confuse.","Ask what two objects are hitting each other, and what each impact does to them.","Say what collides, then say what the collisions do to the shape and size of the particles."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (fluvial processes)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, describe two features of the waterfall and its channel. [2 marks]","a":"AO4 = 2 marks. One mark for each feature clearly observed in Figure 2, for example a single vertical drop of about 21 m; a deep, dark plunge pool at its foot; an overhanging band of dark rock at the lip; angular blocks lying in the pool; near-vertical rock walls close to the channel downstream. No credit for features not visible in the resource.","sol":"Two features taken straight from the description, for example: first, the river falls in one unbroken drop of about 21 m rather than in a series of steps, and the hard dark band at the lip juts out over the pale rock beneath it. Second, at the foot of the fall there is a deep, dark, churning pool with angular blocks of the dark rock lying in it, and immediately downstream the river runs between near-vertical walls only a little wider than the channel. Describe what is in the frame; do not explain it, because this question asks only for description.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The command is Describe, so reasons and processes earn nothing here.","Work through the resource from the top of the fall, to its foot, to the reach below it.","Write two separate sentences, each about something you could point to in the picture."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)"]},{"q":"Describe how the cross profile of a river valley differs between the upper course and the lower course. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks. One mark for the upper course as a narrow, steep-sided, V-shaped valley with a shallow, rocky channel; one mark for the lower course as a wide, flat-floored valley with gentle sides and a wide, deep, smooth channel. No credit for statements about the long profile or gradient.","sol":"In the upper course the cross profile is a narrow V: the valley sides are steep, the floor is little wider than the channel itself, and the channel is shallow, rocky and rough because vertical erosion is dominant and weathered material slides in from the slopes. In the lower course the cross profile is almost flat: lateral erosion and repeated flooding have widened the floor into a flood plain hundreds of metres or more across, the valley sides are low and gentle, and the channel is wide, deep and smooth. Keep to the shape of the slice across the valley — the steepness of the gradient belongs to the long profile.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A cross profile is a slice across the valley, so everything you say is about width and steepness of sides.","Decide which type of erosion dominates in each course, and shape the valley to match.","Give one sentence for the upper course and one for the lower, each covering valley sides and channel."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (long and cross profiles)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, explain the formation of the landforms shown. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Credit a logical chain of reasoning tied to the resource: a hard dolerite cap over softer sedimentary rock; differential erosion by hydraulic action and abrasion; undercutting and overhang; collapse into the plunge pool; abrasion by fallen blocks deepening the pool; upstream retreat leaving a gorge. Processes must be outlined as well as the sequence of formation.","sol":"Figure 2 shows three linked landforms: a single fall of about 21 m, a deep plunge pool and a narrow, steep-walled gorge running downstream. They are the product of one river working on two rocks of very different hardness. The dark band at the lip is dolerite of the Whin Sill, which is hard; the paler, thinly layered rock set back beneath it at the rear of the pool is softer limestone, sandstone and shale. As the water pours over the lip, hydraulic action forces water and trapped air into the bedding planes of the softer rock while abrasion hurls sand and pebbles against it, so the soft rock is cut back faster than the cap above. That differential erosion is exactly why Figure 2 shows the dark band overhanging. Undercut and unsupported, the cap eventually collapses, and the angular blocks lying in the pool are pieces of it. Those blocks are then swirled round by the falling water and, by abrasion, drill the plunge pool deeper. Each collapse moves the lip a little further upstream, so over thousands of years the retreating fall has left behind the near-vertical walls downstream — a gorge of recession. The broad, open valley above the falls shows how little the river has yet cut there.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Everything in the picture follows from one fact about the rock; find it before you write.","Name the erosional processes and say what each physically does to the softer band.","Build one chain that starts at the geology and ends at the gorge, quoting something you can see at every link."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, suggest why basin B has a shorter lag time and a higher peak discharge than basin A. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Credit answers that use the data (lag times of 4 hours against 8 hours; peaks of 31 against 22 cumecs) and explain them through impermeable clay, urban surfaces and drains giving rapid surface run-off, against permeable sandstone and woodland giving interception, infiltration and slow throughflow. Max Level 1 for an answer that ignores the resource.","sol":"Figure 1 shows basin B peaking at 31 cumecs in hour 6, four hours after the heaviest rain, while basin A peaks at only 22 cumecs in hour 10, a lag time of eight hours. Basin B therefore responds twice as fast and 9 cumecs higher from the same 30 mm of rain, and the two causes are geology and land use. Basin B lies on clay, which is impermeable, so once the thin soil is wet almost nothing infiltrates and the rest must travel to the channel as surface run-off. Its built-up surfaces make that worse: roofs, roads and car parks shed water instantly, and gutters and storm drains carry it to the river in minutes rather than hours, which both shortens the lag time and concentrates the water into a single sharp peak. Basin A works in the opposite direction. Sandstone is permeable, so water soaks in and reaches the channel slowly as throughflow and groundwater flow, and the woodland intercepts rain on leaves and branches, from which much evaporates, while roots take up more. The result is a gentle rising limb, a lower peak and a long falling limb — Figure 1 shows basin A still at 9 cumecs in hour 18 when basin B is already back to its base flow of 5 cumecs. Basin B is therefore far more likely to flood.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The two basins differ in exactly two ways, and both change how quickly rain reaches the channel.","Follow one raindrop in each basin from the moment it lands to the moment it enters the river.","Quote the figures for both lag times and both peaks, then explain each difference with a named factor."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (flood risk)"]},{"q":"Explain the formation of a meander and an ox-bow lake. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Credit a sequence: fastest, deepest flow on the outside of a bend; erosion by hydraulic action and abrasion forming a river cliff; slow, shallow flow inside depositing a slip-off slope; migration of the loop and narrowing of the neck; a flood cutting through the neck; deposition sealing the loop off as an ox-bow lake. Emphasis is on explanation, so processes must be outlined as well as the sequence.","sol":"A meander begins where the flow in a channel is uneven. The deepest and fastest water swings to the outside of a bend, so the river has most energy there: hydraulic action drives water and air into cracks in the bank and abrasion scours it with the load, undercutting the bank into a steep river cliff. On the inside of the same bend the water is shallow and slow, so the river no longer has the energy to carry its bedload and deposits it as a gently sloping slip-off slope of shingle. Because erosion and deposition act permanently on opposite banks, the bend grows outwards and migrates slowly downstream, and where two bends lie back to back the neck of land between them is squeezed narrower and narrower. Eventually the river is in flood, carrying far more water and energy than usual, and it breaks straight across the neck. That new route is shorter and steeper, so it carries the main flow, and the old loop is left with little current. Deposition then takes over at the two ends of the loop, where the slow water drops its sediment and seals the bend off from the channel entirely. What is left is a crescent-shaped ox-bow lake on the flood plain, which gradually silts up and dries out into a marshy scar.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Start from where the water is fastest in a bend and where it is slowest; the landforms follow from that.","Pair a named process with each bank, then say what repeating that for years does to the shape of the loop.","Set out the stages in order and finish with the event that separates the loop from the river."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion and deposition)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, suggest the economic effects that a flood of the River Tees would have on Yarm. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. The focus must be economic effects, interpreted directly from Figure 3: damage to shops, pubs and offices on the High Street and the cost of repair; lost trade while businesses are closed; the bridge and the neck cut, so workers, customers and deliveries cannot reach the town; higher insurance premiums afterwards. No credit for social or environmental effects unless a related economic impact is given.","sol":"Figure 3 shows the whole town inside a loop of the river on flat land, with illustrative spot heights of only 12 m by the bridge and 13 m in the middle of the High Street, so flood water would spread across the built-up area rather than staying in the channel. The most immediate economic cost is to the businesses: the High Street is lined with shops, pubs and offices, and water in them would ruin stock, fittings and equipment, with owners losing weeks of trade as well as paying for repairs. The second is access. The map shows only one road bridge, at the north end of the High Street, and one neck of dry land at the south, so if those are cut the town is isolated: employees cannot reach work, customers cannot reach the shops and deliveries stop, which costs money even to businesses whose own premises stay dry. The cost does not end when the water does, because insurance premiums for property on a flood plain rise sharply and stay high.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Only effects that cost somebody money count here; a flooded home is a social effect unless you take it further.","Pick two different map details — what is inside the loop, and how people get in and out of it.","Develop each one into a consequence for trade, jobs or repair bills rather than listing damage."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (example of a river valley)","3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (flood risk)"]},{"q":"Explain how levées and flood plains are formed. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Credit the loss of velocity as the river leaves its channel; the coarsest material dropped first against the bank building levées; finer silt carried further and spread as alluvium; repetition over many floods; the contribution of lateral erosion by migrating meanders to the width of the flood plain.","sol":"Both landforms are made by the same event: a river spilling out of its channel. When discharge rises above bankfull the water spreads across the valley floor, where it is suddenly shallow and meets far more friction from grass, soil and crops, so its velocity collapses. A river can only carry sediment while it has the energy to do so, and the heaviest particles need the most energy, so sand and gravel are dropped almost at once, within a metre or two of the bank. Each flood adds another layer in the same place, and over many floods these build into levées — natural embankments of coarse material standing above the level of the flood plain, which is why a river in its lower course can sit higher than the fields beside it. The finer silt and clay stay in suspension longer and are carried out across the whole valley floor before settling as a thin sheet of alluvium. Repeated season after season, those sheets build the flat, fertile flood plain. Its width comes from erosion as well: as meanders migrate across the valley they use lateral erosion to undercut the valley sides, widening the floor that the silt then covers. That is why flood plains are flat, wide and among the most fertile farmland in the country.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Both landforms are built at the moment a river stops being a channel and becomes a sheet of shallow water.","Think about which sizes of sediment are dropped first and which travel furthest, and why.","Explain one landform, then the other, then say what repeating the process for centuries produces."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion and deposition)"]},{"q":"Explain how relief, geology and land use affect the flood risk in a drainage basin. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Credit steep slopes speeding overland flow and shortening lag time; impermeable rock such as clay or granite preventing infiltration against permeable chalk or sandstone storing and slowly releasing water; urbanisation, drains and deforestation increasing run-off against woodland interception and infiltration. Expect development of the link to lag time and peak discharge.","sol":"Flood risk depends on how quickly rain becomes river water, and each of these three factors changes that. Relief acts on speed: on steep valley sides gravity moves water downhill as overland flow within minutes, while on gentle slopes it lingers long enough to infiltrate, so an upland basin has a much shorter lag time and a sharper peak than a lowland one. Geology acts on storage. Impermeable rock such as clay or granite has few pores or joints, so once the soil is saturated almost all further rain must run over the surface; permeable rock such as chalk or sandstone absorbs it, holds it as groundwater and releases it into the channel over days, flattening the hydrograph. Land use acts on both. Woodland intercepts rain on leaves and branches, from which much evaporates before it reaches the ground, and roots open the soil and take water up, so a forested basin delivers far less water far more slowly. Replacing that with tarmac, roofs and concrete removes infiltration altogether, and gutters and storm drains are designed to move water to the river as fast as possible, so an urban basin has a very short lag time and a high peak. The three factors combine: the worst risk is intense rain on a steep, urbanised clay basin, and the least is gentle rain on a wooded chalk lowland.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Every one of these three factors changes the same thing: how fast rain gets into the channel.","For each factor, contrast the extreme that raises risk with the extreme that lowers it.","Tie each explanation back to lag time and peak discharge rather than stopping at 'more run-off'."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (flood risk)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, assess the effectiveness of the Jubilee River flood relief channel in managing flood risk on the River Thames. [9 marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Credit detailed knowledge of the scheme (11.6 km, opened 2002, about £110 million, Taplow to Datchet, gates and weirs); understanding of how a relief channel lowers levels upstream and redistributes water downstream; and a substantiated judgement weighing the protection of Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton against the flooding of Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor, the cost overrun, the scour repairs and the mixed environmental record.","sol":"The Jubilee River was built because Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton stand on the flood plain of the Thames, on land that flooded badly in 1947, and because town centres of that age and value cannot simply be abandoned. The Environment Agency's answer was hard engineering on a large scale: a flood relief channel about 11.6 km long, opened in 2002 at a cost of about £110 million, which leaves the Thames near Taplow, curves north of the three towns and returns the water to the river near Datchet, with gates at the intake and a series of weirs controlling the share of the discharge it takes.\n\nJudged against the job it was given, the scheme has worked. Figure 4 records that in January 2003, when it was first tested in earnest, the three towns stayed dry, and that they were largely spared again in the Thames floods of February 2014. The mechanism is sound: removing part of the peak discharge from the main channel lowers the water level through the towns, so the river stays within its banks where thousands of homes and businesses lie close to the water. Measured in property saved and lives undisturbed, that is a very large benefit for a one-off cost.\n\nThe difficulty is that the water is moved, not removed. It re-enters the same river at the far end of the 11.6 km channel, near Datchet below Windsor and Eton, where the Thames is no bigger than it was before. Figure 4 shows that Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor flooded in 2003 and again in 2014, and residents there argue that the channel now delivers the peak to them sooner and higher, so that they carry a risk created for someone else's benefit. Whether the scheme really made those floods worse is contested between them and the Environment Agency: the benefit upstream is clear, the effect downstream disputed. There are further costs. The price ran far above the original budget, and fast water scoured the bed around some of the weirs so that they had to be repaired. Environmentally the record is mixed: reed beds, shallow margins and riverside footpaths have created habitat and public access, but slow-moving water has produced algal blooms in warm weather.\n\nOverall the scheme is highly effective in the narrow sense and only partly effective in the wider one. For Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton it does exactly what it was designed to do. Across the catchment as a whole it has redistributed flood risk rather than reduced it, which is why newer schemes pair relief channels with soft engineering such as flood plain zoning and upstream storage.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Effectiveness depends on who you ask, so decide early whose flood risk you are measuring.","Set what the scheme was designed to do against what actually happened in 2003 and 2014, and add the money and the habitat.","Carry the judgement through every paragraph and finish with a verdict that says 'effective for whom' rather than yes or no."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (example of a flood management scheme)","3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (costs and benefits)"]},{"q":"'Hard engineering is the most effective way of protecting river landscapes from flooding.' To what extent do you agree? [9 marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Credit knowledge of named hard strategies (dams and reservoirs, straightening, embankments, flood relief channels) and soft ones (flood warnings and preparation, flood plain zoning, tree planting, river restoration), understanding of their costs and benefits, and a discursive judgement of the degree to which hard engineering is superior, supported by an example such as the Jubilee River.","sol":"Hard engineering has one decisive advantage: it changes what the river can physically do, and it does so immediately. A dam and reservoir hold back the flood peak and release it slowly, while also supplying water, generating electricity and creating recreation. Embankments raise the bank so the river can carry a greater discharge before it spills, and are cheap enough for a small town to afford. A flood relief channel takes a share of the flow away from a settlement altogether. The Jubilee River, a channel about 11.6 km long opened in 2002 at a cost of about £110 million, kept Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton dry in the floods of January 2003 and largely spared them in February 2014. Where a town centre is already built on a flood plain and cannot be moved, no soft strategy could have achieved that.\n\nBut the same example shows the limits. The water the Jubilee River took out of the Thames was returned to it near Datchet, and Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor flooded on both occasions, so risk was transferred rather than removed. Hard engineering is expensive, the Jubilee River costing far more than planned and needing repair after scour damaged some weirs. It can also damage the landscape it defends: dams drown valleys and trap the sediment that flood plains need, and straightening simply delivers the problem faster to the next town down.\n\nSoft engineering is weaker in a crisis but stronger over time. Flood warnings and preparation cost little and save lives and possessions, though they save no buildings. Flood plain zoning prevents damage permanently by keeping housing off the most dangerous land, but it cannot undo what has already been built. Planting trees in a catchment increases interception and infiltration, lengthening lag time and lowering the peak for the whole basin, and river restoration returns meanders and wetlands that store water naturally, at modest cost and with real gains for habitat.\n\nSo the claim holds only in part. Hard engineering is the most effective response where high-value property already sits on a flood plain and protection must be certain and immediate. It is not the most effective way of managing flood risk overall, because it is costly, it treats the symptom at one point on the river and it can push the danger downstream. The strongest schemes now combine the two: hard defences at the settlements that need them, inside a catchment managed by zoning, warnings and tree planting.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["'To what extent' asks how far the claim holds, so a straight yes or no cannot reach the top level.","Put a named hard strategy and a named soft strategy side by side and compare cost, speed and how long the protection lasts.","Decide the circumstances in which the statement is true, and make your conclusion state those circumstances."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (costs and benefits)","3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (example of a flood management scheme)"]},{"q":"Using a named example of a river valley in the UK, discuss how erosion and deposition have shaped its landforms. [9 marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. Credit accurate located detail for a named UK valley (for the Tees: Cross Fell 893 m, interlocking spurs, Cauldron Snout, High Force about 21 m over the Whin Sill, meanders at Yarm, flood plain and levées at Stockton, the estuary at Seal Sands), understanding of the processes behind each landform, and a balanced discussion of the relative contribution of erosion and deposition down the course.","sol":"The River Tees rises on Cross Fell, at 893 m the highest point of the Pennines, and runs roughly east for about 137 km to the North Sea at Teesmouth. Its landforms divide almost exactly between erosion and deposition, and which dominates depends on where you stand on the long profile.\n\nIn the upper course the Tees has a steep gradient, a small discharge and a large, angular bedload, so its energy goes into cutting downwards. Vertical erosion has left interlocking spurs in upper Teesdale, ridges the young river winds around because it lacks the power to cut through them. The most striking erosional landform is High Force, a single drop of about 21 m where the river crosses the Whin Sill, a hard dolerite intruded between softer limestone, sandstone and shale. Hydraulic action and abrasion cut the soft rock back faster than the hard cap, leaving an overhang that collapses into the plunge pool; the fallen blocks then deepen the pool by abrasion, and the fall retreats upstream, leaving the gorge below it. Cauldron Snout, immediately below Cow Green Reservoir, is cut in the same rock but not in the same way: there the Tees runs down the stepped, jointed face of the sill itself in a long cascade, rather than over a hard cap undercut into an overhang that collapses.\n\nIn the middle and lower courses the balance shifts. Lateral erosion widens the valley into large meanders, most obviously at Yarm, where the town sits inside a loop with the river on three sides; on the outer banks hydraulic action and abrasion cut river cliffs, while on the inner banks deposition builds slip-off slopes. Below Yarm, overbank flooding drops its coarsest load immediately as levées and spreads fine silt across a wide, flat flood plain through Stockton-on-Tees. At Teesmouth the current meets the tide, loses all its remaining energy and deposits the rest of its load as the mudflats and saltmarsh of Seal Sands.\n\nDiscussing the two together, erosion is the senior partner in the sense that it supplies everything deposition later lays down, and it produces the most dramatic landforms — High Force and the gorge. But deposition shapes far more of the valley by area: the flood plain and estuary of the lower Tees cover many times the ground occupied by the upper valley's features. The honest answer is that neither works alone; the Tees is a single system in which material eroded from Cross Fell's slopes ends up as mud at Seal Sands.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["You cannot reach the top level without a real valley and located detail, so choose the one you know best and commit to it.","Work down the long profile and give each course the landforms and the processes that belong to it.","Discuss means both sides weighed, so decide which of erosion and deposition has done more, and on what measure."],"codes":["3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (example of a river valley)","3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion and deposition)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which process of erosion forces water and trapped air into cracks in the bank until pieces break away?","a":"Hydraulic action."},{"q":"What is meant by the lag time of a storm hydrograph?","a":"The time between peak rainfall and peak discharge."},{"q":"Which hard rock caps High Force on the River Tees?","a":"Dolerite of the Whin Sill."},{"q":"In which year did the Jubilee River flood relief channel open?","a":"2002."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Hydraulic action","back":"The sheer force of moving water. Water and trapped air are driven into cracks in the bank and bed; the pressure widens them until pieces break away. Strongest in floods and on the outer bank of a bend.","code":"3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (fluvial processes)","open":false},{"front":"Abrasion","back":"The river's load is thrown against the bed and banks by the current, scraping and grinding them away like sandpaper. It does most of the work of deepening a channel and of drilling potholes upstream.","code":"3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (fluvial processes)","open":false},{"front":"Attrition","back":"Load particles collide with one another and with the bed as they travel; each impact chips them, so they become smaller and rounder downstream. Attrition wears the load, not the channel.","code":"3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (fluvial processes)","open":false},{"front":"The four ways a river transports its load","back":"Traction — boulders rolled along the bed. Saltation — pebbles bounced along. Suspension — silt and clay carried within the water. Solution — minerals dissolved and carried invisibly.","code":"3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (fluvial processes)","open":false},{"front":"Long profile of a river","back":"A side view from source to mouth. Steep and irregular near the source where vertical erosion dominates, flattening into a smooth concave curve towards the mouth as lateral erosion and deposition take over.","code":"3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (long and cross profiles)","open":false},{"front":"Cross profile: upper course against lower course","back":"Upper: a narrow V-shaped valley, steep sides, shallow rocky channel. Lower: a wide, flat-floored valley, gentle sides, deep smooth channel. Velocity rises downstream because friction falls.","code":"3.1.3.3 — The shape of river valleys changes as rivers flow downstream (long and cross profiles)","open":false},{"front":"Interlocking spurs","back":"Ridges of higher ground that a young river winds around in its upper course because it has too little energy to erode through them. Looking up the valley they appear to interlock like teeth.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","open":false},{"front":"Gorge of recession","back":"The narrow, steep-sided valley left behind as a waterfall retreats upstream. Each collapse of the undercut cap rock moves the fall back, so the gorge records where the waterfall used to be.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","open":false},{"front":"Ox-bow lake","back":"A crescent-shaped lake cut off from a river. Erosion narrows the neck between two bends until a flood cuts straight across, and deposition then seals the abandoned loop off from the channel.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion and deposition)","open":false},{"front":"Levées","back":"Raised banks of coarse sediment along a channel. In a flood the river slows the instant it leaves its banks, so it drops its heaviest load first, building the banks a little higher with every flood.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion and deposition)","open":false},{"front":"Lag time","back":"The time between peak rainfall and peak discharge on a storm hydrograph. A short lag time with a high peak means water is reaching the channel quickly, so the flood risk is high.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (flood risk)","open":false},{"front":"Name one physical factor that increases flood risk in a drainage basin","back":"Steep relief, so water runs off the slopes quickly; impermeable geology such as clay or granite, which prevents infiltration; prolonged or very intense rainfall that saturates the soil.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (flood risk)","open":true},{"front":"Flood relief channel","back":"Hard engineering: a new channel carrying part of a river's discharge around a settlement and returning it downstream. Benefit — lowers levels where people live. Cost — expensive, and risk can rise downstream.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (costs and benefits)","open":false},{"front":"High Force, River Tees","back":"A single fall of about 21 m in upper Teesdale, County Durham, where hard dolerite of the Whin Sill caps softer limestone, sandstone and shale. A plunge pool and a gorge lie below it.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Distinctive fluvial landforms result from different physical processes (example of a river valley)","open":false},{"front":"The Jubilee River","back":"A Thames flood relief channel about 11.6 km long, opened in 2002 at a cost of about £110 million. It protects Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton, but Datchet and Wraysbury downstream flooded in 2003 and 2014.","code":"3.1.3.3 — Different management strategies can be used to protect river landscapes from the effects of flooding (example of a flood management scheme)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Storm hydrograph data for two small drainage basins. A table written for this room; the figures are illustrative, not measured. The same storm dropped 30 mm of rain on both basins over three hours, with the heaviest rain in hour 2. Basin A is mostly woodland on permeable sandstone. Basin B is mostly built-up, on impermeable clay.\nHours after the storm began | Discharge in basin A (cumecs) | Discharge in basin B (cumecs)\n0 | 5 | 5\n2 | 6 | 8\n4 | 9 | 22\n6 | 14 | 31\n8 | 19 | 24\n10 | 22 | 16\n12 | 20 | 11\n14 | 16 | 8\n16 | 12 | 6\n18 | 9 | 5","Figure 2 — Photograph: High Force on the River Tees, upper Teesdale, County Durham. A description written for this room of what such a photograph shows; the waterfall, its height and its geology are real. The river arrives across open moorland from the left and drops in one unbroken white fall, about 21 m high, into a dark, churning pool at the foot of a cliff. The rock at the lip, and for several metres below it, is a single thick band of dark grey rock — dolerite of the Whin Sill. Beneath that band, at the back of the pool, the cliff is made of paler rock in thin horizontal layers — limestone, sandstone and shale — and it is set back, so that the dark band juts out above it. Angular blocks of the dark rock lie in the pool and on the ledges below. Downstream of the pool the river runs away between near-vertical rock walls only a little wider than the channel itself. Above the falls the valley is broad, open and gently sloping.","Figure 3 — Sketch map: the River Tees at Yarm, North Yorkshire. Drawn for this room; the meander loop, the High Street and the road and rail crossings are real, the spot heights are illustrative. The Tees enters the map from the west and swings north, then east, then south, so that the town lies inside a loop with the river on three sides. The High Street runs north to south down the middle of the loop, lined on both sides with shops, pubs and offices. A road bridge crosses the river at the north end of the High Street, and a railway viaduct crosses the loop from south-west to north-east. The neck of land at the south of the loop is the only land route into the town. On the northern bend the map marks a steep bank and a line of trees on the outer, northern side of the channel, and a shingle bank with a flat green strip on the inner, town side of the same bend. The land on both sides of the river is flat and open for several hundred metres. Spot heights, illustrative: 12 m beside the road bridge, 13 m halfway down the High Street, 14 m at the neck, 23 m on the hill beyond the river to the north.","Figure 4 — Text extract: the Jubilee River flood relief channel. Written for this room; the places, dates and figures are real. Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton sit on the flood plain of the River Thames west of London, on land that flooded badly in 1947. To protect them, the Environment Agency built the Jubilee River, a man-made flood relief channel about 11.6 km long, which leaves the Thames near Taplow, curves north of the three towns and returns the water to the Thames downstream near Datchet. Gates at the intake and a series of weirs along the channel control how much of the Thames's flow it takes. The channel opened in 2002 and cost about £110 million, far more than first planned. It was used in earnest for the first time in January 2003: Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton stayed dry, but Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor, downstream of the point where the channel rejoins the Thames, flooded, and residents there argued that their own risk had been increased for the benefit of the towns upstream. Fast water scoured out the bed around some of the weirs, which had to be repaired at further cost. The channel was landscaped with shallow margins, reed beds and riverside footpaths and now carries wetland birds, but in warm weather its slow-moving water has suffered algal blooms. In the Thames floods of February 2014 the same pattern repeated: the three towns were largely spared, while Datchet and Wraysbury flooded again."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the River Tees: the named UK river valley and its landforms of erosion and deposition","items":["Source: Cross Fell, 893 m, the highest point of the Pennines, on the Cumbrian side of the North Pennines.","Course and mouth: flows roughly east for about 137 km through Teesdale, Barnard Castle, Yarm and Stockton-on-Tees, reaching the North Sea at Teesmouth between Redcar and Hartlepool.","Upper course, erosion — interlocking spurs: in upper Teesdale the river cuts vertically into the moorland and winds around ridges of higher ground it lacks the energy to erode through.","Upper course, erosion — Cauldron Snout: a long cascade where the Tees falls down the stepped dolerite of the Whin Sill, immediately below Cow Green Reservoir, which was built at the head of the dale and completed in the early 1970s.","Upper course, erosion — High Force: a single drop of about 21 m in Teesdale, County Durham, upstream of Middleton-in-Teesdale, where hard dolerite of the Whin Sill lies over softer limestone, sandstone and shale.","How High Force works: differential erosion by hydraulic action and abrasion cuts the soft rock back faster than the dolerite cap, leaving an overhang that collapses into the plunge pool; fallen blocks deepen the pool by abrasion and the fall retreats upstream, leaving a steep-sided gorge below it.","Middle course, erosion and deposition — meanders: large meanders below Barnard Castle and at Yarm, where the town is built inside a loop with the river on three sides; river cliffs on the outer banks, slip-off slopes of shingle on the inner banks.","Lower course, deposition — flood plain and levées: a wide, flat flood plain through Yarm, Stockton-on-Tees and Middlesbrough, built from silt dropped by overbank floods, with natural levées of coarser sediment along the banks.","Mouth, deposition — estuary: the Tees widens into a heavily industrialised estuary at Teesmouth, with extensive mudflats and saltmarsh at Seal Sands, where the current meets the tide and the remaining load settles.","The single geological fact that explains the upper valley: the Whin Sill, a hard igneous dolerite intruded between softer sedimentary rocks, is what makes both Cauldron Snout and High Force.","On a large-scale map of the valley: contours crowded together and the river a single thin blue line in the upper course; contours far apart, the river a broad double blue line looping across an open flood plain in the lower course."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the Jubilee River: the named UK flood management scheme","items":["Where: on the River Thames west of London, running from Taplow, above Maidenhead, north of Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton, and rejoining the Thames near Datchet.","Why it was required: the three towns stand on the Thames flood plain, on land that flooded badly in 1947, and their built-up centres cannot be relocated; thousands of homes, businesses and major historic buildings sit close to the water.","The strategy: hard engineering — a man-made flood relief channel about 11.6 km long, with gates at the intake and a series of weirs along it, which takes a share of the Thames's discharge away from the towns and returns it to the river downstream.","Built by: the Environment Agency. Opened: 2002.","Cost: about £110 million, far more than first planned.","Economic issue — the benefit: Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton stayed dry in the floods of January 2003 and were largely spared in February 2014, so property damage, business closure and insurance losses in those towns were avoided.","Economic issue — the cost: the overspend, plus repairs after fast water scoured out the bed around some of the weirs.","Social issue: Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor, downstream of the point where the channel rejoins the Thames, flooded in January 2003 and again in February 2014; residents there argue their risk was raised for the benefit of the towns upstream.","Environmental issue — the gain: the channel was landscaped with shallow margins, reed beds and riverside footpaths, and now supports wetland birds and public access.","Environmental issue — the loss: the water in the channel moves slowly, and in warm weather it has suffered algal blooms.","The judgement an examiner rewards: effective for the places it was built to defend, only partly effective across the catchment, because flood risk was redistributed downstream rather than removed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — river processes, landforms and terms an examiner rewards","items":["Hydraulic action: the force of moving water drives water and trapped air into cracks in the bed and banks until pieces break away.","Abrasion: the load is thrown against the bed and banks by the current, scraping and grinding them away.","Attrition: load particles collide with each other and the bed, chipping until they are smaller and rounder downstream.","Solution (erosion): slightly acidic river water dissolves soluble rock such as limestone and chalk.","Vertical erosion cuts downwards and dominates the upper course; lateral erosion cuts sideways and dominates the middle and lower courses.","Transportation: traction (rolling), saltation (bouncing), suspension (fine material carried within the flow) and solution (dissolved minerals).","Deposition happens whenever velocity falls: on the inside of a bend, the moment the river leaves its banks in a flood, in low flow, and at the mouth where the current meets the tide. The heaviest material is dropped first.","Hydrograph terms: peak rainfall, peak discharge, lag time, rising limb, falling limb, base flow, bankfull discharge. Discharge = cross-sectional area × velocity, in cumecs (m³/s).","Factors raising flood risk: prolonged or intense precipitation; impermeable geology such as clay or granite; steep relief; urban land use with tarmac, roofs and storm drains; deforestation.","Hard engineering — dams and reservoirs: hold back the peak and supply water and power; very expensive, drown land and trap sediment the flood plain needs.","Hard engineering — straightening: moves water past a settlement quickly; increases discharge and flood risk downstream and destroys habitat.","Hard engineering — embankments: cheap and let land be used up to the channel; ugly, need maintenance and cause severe flooding if they fail.","Hard engineering — flood relief channels: lower levels where people live; costly, and they can raise risk below the point where they rejoin the river.","Soft engineering — flood warnings and preparation: cheap and save lives and possessions; save no buildings and depend on people acting on them.","Soft engineering — flood plain zoning: prevents future damage by keeping vulnerable land uses off the flood plain; cannot undo what is already built.","Soft engineering — tree planting: increases interception and infiltration, lengthening lag time and lowering the peak; slow to work and takes land out of farming.","Soft engineering — river restoration: returning meanders, wetlands and natural banks slows flow and improves habitat cheaply; needs land and offers less certain protection to a town."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) on 'Using Figure 2, explain the formation of the landforms shown. [6 marks]'","items":["The question: Using Figure 2, explain the formation of the landforms shown. [6 marks] — AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","The paragraph: The landforms in Figure 2 — a single fall of about 21 m, a deep plunge pool and a narrow gorge downstream — are all produced by one river working across two rocks of different hardness. The dark band at the lip is dolerite of the Whin Sill, which is hard, while the paler, thinly layered rock set back beneath it is softer limestone, sandstone and shale. Hydraulic action forces water and trapped air into the bedding planes of that softer rock and abrasion hurls sand and pebbles against it, so it is cut back faster than the cap above: differential erosion, which is precisely why the photograph shows the dark band overhanging. Once the overhang can no longer support its own weight it collapses, and the angular blocks lying in the pool in Figure 2 are pieces of it; swirled round by the falling water, they deepen the pool further by abrasion. Each collapse shifts the lip a little upstream, so the near-vertical walls below the pool are the gorge of recession left by that retreat. The broad, open valley above the falls confirms how little the river has yet cut there.","Point: the landforms are the product of one river crossing two rocks of different hardness.","Named evidence: the dolerite of the Whin Sill, the limestone, sandstone and shale beneath it, the fall of about 21 m, the overhang, the angular blocks in the pool and the near-vertical walls downstream — every item taken from Figure 2.","Developed explanation: hydraulic action and abrasion act faster on the softer rock, so differential erosion undercuts the cap; the overhang collapses; the fallen blocks deepen the pool by abrasion; the lip retreats.","Link to the question: each link is tied to something visible in the resource, so the formation of the features shown is what is being explained.","Mini-judgement: the open valley above the falls shows that the gorge marks where the fall has already been, so the waterfall is still retreating.","Why it is Level 3: it meets 'AO3 Demonstrates thorough application of knowledge and understanding to analyse geographical information, giving detailed explanation of formation of the features shown' and 'AO3 Makes full analysis of the resource, using evidence to support response', because the processes are outlined as well as the sequence of formation and the evidence is drawn from the photograph at every step."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The question: Using Figure 2, explain the formation of the landforms shown. [6 marks]","The paragraph: Figure 2 shows a big waterfall with a pool at the bottom and steep sides. Waterfalls are formed when there is hard rock and soft rock. The soft rock gets eroded away by the river and this makes the rock above stick out. In the end the rock falls down into the water. The river keeps eroding and the waterfall moves back, and a gorge is formed. The valley is narrow and steep here and there are rocks in the water.","What it does meet: 'AO1 Demonstrates some knowledge of river processes and environments' — the hard-rock-over-soft-rock idea is correct and the sequence of collapse and retreat is roughly in the right order.","What it misses, descriptor by descriptor: it does not reach 'AO2 Shows thorough geographical understanding of the interrelationships between river environments and processes', because no erosional process is named — there is no hydraulic action, no abrasion, no mechanism, and the mark scheme gives no credit for an answer that does not explain the mechanism.","What it misses at the top level: it comes nowhere near 'Makes full analysis of the resource, using evidence to support response'. 'A big waterfall' and 'rocks in the water' are vague gestures at the picture rather than evidence; the rock types are never identified, the height is never quoted and the plunge pool is never explained.","The three fixes that would lift it: name dolerite and the softer limestone, sandstone and shale; name hydraulic action and abrasion and say what each physically does; quote the overhang, the blocks in the pool and the walls downstream as the evidence for each stage."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) on the nine-mark question 'Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, assess the effectiveness of the Jubilee River flood relief channel in managing flood risk on the River Thames.'","items":["The question: Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, assess the effectiveness of the Jubilee River flood relief channel in managing flood risk on the River Thames. [9 marks] — AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","The paragraph: The difficulty with the Jubilee River is that it moves flood water rather than removing it. A channel about 11.6 km long, opened in 2002 at a cost of about £110 million, can take a large share of the Thames's discharge away from Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton, and lowering the level through those towns is exactly why they stayed dry in January 2003 and were largely spared in February 2014. But that water is returned to the same river near Datchet, where the channel is no larger than it was before, so residents downstream argue that the peak now reaches them sooner and higher. Figure 4 records Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor flooding on both of those occasions; the Environment Agency contests the link, but the pattern is at least evidence that the risk may have been redistributed rather than reduced. Effectiveness therefore depends entirely on where the question is asked: for the three towns the scheme has done precisely what it was designed to do, while for the catchment as a whole it has bought protection for one group of communities at the expense of another.","Point: the scheme relocates flood water rather than removing it, so effectiveness depends on whose risk is measured.","Named evidence: 11.6 km, opened 2002, about £110 million, the outfall near Datchet, the three protected towns, and the flooding of Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor in January 2003 and February 2014, all anchored in Figure 4.","Developed explanation: removing part of the peak discharge lowers the level upstream; returning it to a channel of unchanged capacity raises the peak downstream, so the benefit and the harm share one mechanism.","Mini-judgement: highly effective at the scale it was designed for, only partly effective at the scale of the whole river.","Why it is Level 3 on a nine-mark question, where Level 3 (Detailed) is 7–9 marks: it carries detailed and accurate knowledge of the scheme, shows understanding of the interrelationship between the channel and the reach below it, and applies both in a coherent, reasoned evaluation rather than describing the scheme. A Level 2 (Clear, 4–6) version would know the scheme but state that it 'works well' without explaining why the downstream villages flood."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, assess the effectiveness of the Jubilee River flood relief channel in managing flood risk on the River Thames. [9 marks]'","items":["The command word decoded: 'Assess' means weigh the parts and give an informed overall judgement. Describing the channel earns Level 1 at best; the marks are in deciding how well it works, for whom, and on what measure.","The resource rule: the stem says 'Using Figure 4', so evidence from that extract is compulsory. Quote the dates, the places and the figures it gives; an answer built only on memory is capped.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly ten minutes — one minute planning the two columns, seven writing, two checking spelling, punctuation and specialist terms.","Introduction (two sentences): say what the scheme is and where — a flood relief channel about 11.6 km long from Taplow to Datchet, opened in 2002 — and signal the line the answer will take, that effectiveness depends on whose flood risk is being measured.","Paragraph 1 — why it was needed (example: Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton on the Thames flood plain, land that flooded in 1947): established town centres cannot be moved off a flood plain, so protection had to be engineered.","Paragraph 2 — how it works and where it succeeds (example: the three towns in January 2003 and February 2014): gates and weirs divert a share of the discharge, lowering the level through the towns; both floods left them dry.","Paragraph 3 — where it fails (example: Wraysbury, Datchet and Old Windsor, flooded in January 2003 and February 2014): the water re-enters a channel of unchanged capacity below the outfall, so residents argue the peak arrives sooner and higher there (contested by the Environment Agency).","Paragraph 4 — the economic and environmental balance (example: the Jubilee River's own record): about £110 million and well over budget, plus repairs after scour damaged some weirs; against that, reed beds, footpaths and wetland birds, offset by algal blooms in warm weather.","The evaluation thread that runs through every paragraph: measured at the three towns the scheme is a success; measured along the whole river it is a transfer of risk. Return to that comparison in each paragraph rather than saving all judgement for the end.","What the conclusion must say: a clear verdict with its conditions — highly effective for the places it was built to defend and only partly effective for the catchment, because risk was redistributed rather than removed; and therefore that a relief channel works best combined with soft engineering such as flood plain zoning and upstream storage.","Two traps: do not drift into how waterfalls or meanders form, and do not list the advantages and disadvantages of hard engineering in general — every point must be about this scheme, on this river.","Write it in accurate, well-punctuated prose using the specialist terms — discharge, peak flow, relief channel, flood plain, catchment — because precise terminology is what separates a Detailed answer from a Clear one."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:52:04Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Factually clean and unusually well anchored — every Tees and Jubilee River figure checks out (Cross Fell 893 m, High Force 21 m over the Whin Sill, Yarm's loop, 11.6 km, 2002, ~£110m, the 2003 and 2014 downstream flooding) and all four figures recount exactly against the questions built on them; fix the AO splits on the two 6-mark explanation questions (should be AO1 2 / AO2 4) before publishing."}}},"3.1.3.4":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.1.3.4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Ice shaped the ground most people in Britain walk on, and this is the physical landscape option that rewards sequence above everything else. Examiners ask you to explain how a corrie, a glacial trough or a moraine came to be where it is, and their mark schemes are blunt about it: describing a landform earns nothing, because ‘processes should be outlined as well as the sequence of formation’. They also ask you to weigh what people now do in these uplands — walk, farm, plant, quarry — against the damage that does, and to judge how well it is managed. So this room drills two things at once: a chain of glacial processes you can write down in order, and two real UK upland areas, Eryri (Snowdonia) and the Lake District, whose landforms, land uses, conflicts and management strategies you can name with figures.","idea":[{"h":"How far the ice actually reached","t":"At the height of the last cold stage, roughly 20,000 years ago, an ice sheet buried most of Britain. Ice grew and flowed outwards from centres over the Scottish Highlands, the Lake District and the mountains of north Wales, and its southern limit ran approximately from the Bristol Channel, across the English Midlands, to the north Norfolk coast. South of that line there was no ice, but the ground was frozen tundra. At its deepest the ice over the British Isles was of the order of 1,500 m thick, against over 2,500 m over Scandinavia; treat both as illustrative of the scale rather than figures to quote as exact. The ice had gone by about 11,700 years ago, apart from a final cold snap that put small glaciers back into hollows in Eryri and the Lake District. A top answer uses the limit as an explanation rather than a fact: glacial landforms cluster in the north and west because that is where the ice was thickest and moved fastest, and southern England has none because the ice never got there.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (extent of ice cover)"},{"h":"The four things ice does to rock","t":"Weathering comes first: water seeps into joints, freezes below 0 °C, expands by about nine per cent and levers the rock apart, and after many freeze-thaw cycles angular fragments fall onto the ice. Erosion is two named processes. Abrasion is the debris frozen into the base and sides of the glacier scraping the bedrock like sandpaper, leaving scratches called striations and fine rock flour. Plucking is meltwater refreezing onto loosened, jointed rock so that blocks are torn away as the ice moves forward. Movement carries the debris: rotational slip is ice rotating in a hollow, deepening it, and bulldozing is the snout shoving loose material ahead of it. Deposition happens when melting outpaces snowfall so the ice thins and can no longer carry its load: material dropped straight out of the ice is till, unsorted and angular, while material carried on beyond the snout by meltwater is outwash, sorted and rounded. A top answer never writes ‘the glacier eroded it’; it names which process did what, and in what order.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (glacial processes)"},{"h":"The landforms ice cuts out","t":"Snow collects in a shaded, north- or east-facing hollow, compacts through firn into ice, then plucking steepens the back wall while rotational slip and abrasion scoop out the floor; less erosion at the open end leaves a rock lip, and the hollow becomes a corrie, often holding a tarn. Two corries eroding back to back leave a knife-edged ridge between them, an arête; three or more around one mountain leave a pyramidal peak. In the valley below, a glacier cannot bend, so it slices off the interlocking spurs of the old river valley, leaving truncated spurs, and widens, deepens and straightens the valley into a glacial trough with a flat floor and steep sides. Where the trough floor was scoured deeper — in softer rock, or where tributary ice added mass — a long, narrow ribbon lake forms. Smaller tributary glaciers eroded less deeply, so their valleys are left perched above the main floor as hanging valleys, usually with a waterfall. A top answer explains one or two of these in a sequence, each step caused by the one before it.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)"},{"h":"The landforms ice builds","t":"Everything a glacier deposits started as debris it was carrying. Freeze-thaw shatters the crags above the ice and the fragments fall onto its edges; plucking and abrasion add rock from beneath. Debris carried along the margins is dropped as a lateral moraine, a ridge banked against the valley side. Where two glaciers merge, the two inner lateral moraines join and are carried down the middle as a medial moraine, which is why a medial moraine proves a glacier had more than one source. At the snout, where melting balances forward movement, everything is dumped in a ridge across the valley: a terminal moraine, marking the furthest extent of the ice, with recessional moraines behind it marking pauses in the retreat. Ground moraine spread beneath the ice forms a till plain, and meltwater beyond the snout builds a sorted outwash plain. Drumlins are smooth, egg-shaped mounds of till moulded by ice moving over it, steep and blunt at the up-ice end and tapering down-ice. An erratic is a single boulder carried far from its source rock and stranded when the ice melted. A top answer explains two landforms fully rather than listing five.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (transportation and deposition)"},{"h":"Reading a real glaciated upland: Eryri (Snowdonia)","t":"Eryri (Snowdonia) has been a National Park since 1951 and covers about 2,130 km² of north Wales. Its summit, Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon), 1,085 m, is the classic UK pyramidal peak: corries bite into it on several sides, two of them holding Llyn Glaslyn, directly below the summit crags, and Llyn Llydaw, lower and further east. The ridges left between them — Crib Goch, Crib y Ddysgl and Y Lliwedd — are arêtes. North-west of the massif, the Llanberis Pass and the valley running down from it form a glacial trough with a flat floor and steep sides, with the ribbon lakes Llyn Peris and Llyn Padarn end to end on the valley floor. In the Ogwen valley, Cwm Idwal is a corrie hanging above the Nant Ffrancon trough, with ridges of unsorted moraine across its floor from the last glaciers to occupy it; it became the first National Nature Reserve in Wales in 1954. On a large-scale map you recognise these by their symbols: crag marks and contours almost touching on a corrie back wall, contours dropping steeply on both sides of an arête, widely spaced contours on a trough floor between packed ones on the sides. A top answer names the landform, names the place and then explains it.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (example of an upland area)"},{"h":"What people do with a glaciated upland","t":"Four activities, and each one is the landscape’s own fault. Tourism comes because the scenery is dramatic and the lakes and crags suit walking, climbing and water sports: the Lake District took 18.11 million visitors in 2023, and about 590,000 walkers reached the summit of Yr Wyddfa in 2019, with roughly 140,000 more arriving on the rack railway that has climbed from Llanberis since 1896. Farming is hill sheep farming, because thin, stony soils on steep slopes above the frost line will grow little else: Herdwick sheep are hefted to the Lakeland fells. Forestry uses the same poor slopes for conifer plantations that tolerate acid soil and exposure, such as Whinlatter and Grizedale in Cumbria and the Gwydir forest in Eryri. Quarrying follows the hard volcanic and metamorphic rock the ice stripped bare: Honister in Borrowdale is England’s last working slate mine, and the slate landscape of north-west Wales, including Penrhyn at Bethesda and Dinorwig at Llanberis, became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2021. A top answer links each activity back to a glacial characteristic — the relief, the rock, the soil or the water — rather than listing jobs.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (economic activities)"},{"h":"Why those land uses collide","t":"Conflict happens because the same narrow valley floor and the same open fell are wanted for incompatible things. Walkers cross grazing land and leave gates open or let dogs run among ewes, while farmers are asked to cut stocking rates so the fell can recover. Visitor traffic and quarry lorries share single-track lanes built for carts, and cars parked on verges narrow them further. Housing is the sharpest conflict of all: cottages bought as second homes and holiday lets push prices beyond local wages, so young families leave and the school roll and the bus service shrink. Water is contested too — Windermere carried powerboats and water-skiers until a 10 mph speed limit, confirmed in 2000, came into force in March 2005, ending the sport but protecting anglers, wildlife and quiet users. Behind all of it sits development against conservation: Dinorwig pumped-storage power station, opened in 1984 and able to generate 1,728 MW, was built inside Elidir Fawr in a National Park, and reservoirs such as Haweswater drowned the village of Mardale Green in 1935 to supply Manchester. A top answer explains who loses, not just what changes.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (conflicts)"},{"h":"Managing tourism in the Lake District","t":"The Lake District has been a National Park since 1951 and a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2017. It covers 2,362 km², has 38,993 residents at the 2021 Census and took 18.11 million visitors in 2023 — roughly 460 visitors a year for every resident — so the attractions and the problems are the same thing. Management works on three fronts. The ground itself: Fix the Fells, a partnership hosted by the National Trust with the National Park Authority, Natural England, the Lake District Foundation and Friends of the Lake District, repairs eroded upland paths by stone pitching and soil inversion, with stone flown in by helicopter because there is no vehicle access. Movement: park-and-ride and seasonal bus services, lake launches and ferries, cycle routes and the ‘Miles without Stiles’ accessible paths spread people off the worst-hit routes. Behaviour and rules: the 10 mph speed limit on Windermere in force since March 2005, parking restrictions and verge protection, Countryside Code messaging and visitor giving through the Lake District Foundation. A top answer judges these, saying which pressure each strategy actually removes and which it only moves somewhere else.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (tourism example)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading a glaciated upland off a large-scale map (Figure 1)","setup":"Figure 1, the map extract printed for this room, shows the eastern side of the Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon) massif. The summit, Yr Wyddfa, 1,085 m, is towards the western edge. North-east of the summit the contour lines are drawn almost on top of one another and the map carries the black tick symbols used for crags. Immediately below those crags, in a rounded hollow open to the east, lies a small lake, Llyn Glaslyn. Lower down and further east lies a second, longer lake, Llyn Llydaw, which Glaslyn drains into down a steep stream. Two narrow strips of high ground run out from the summit with contours falling steeply on both sides of each: Crib Goch to the north-east and Y Lliwedd to the south-east. In the north-west corner, beyond the summit, the ground drops into a wide valley with a flat floor and very steep sides, holding two long narrow lakes end to end, Llyn Peris and Llyn Padarn, with the village of Llanberis between them. The question: using Figure 1, describe the physical features of the glaciated landscape shown.","steps":["Classify before you describe: a rounded, steep-backed hollow holding a lake is a corrie with a tarn, so Glaslyn and Llydaw give you two, one stepped above the other on the eastern flank.","Use the map’s own evidence for steepness — contours packed almost together plus crag ticks north-east of the summit means the corrie back wall is near-vertical rock, which is what plucking leaves.","Read the strips of high ground: narrow crests with steep drops on both sides are arêtes, and Crib Goch and Y Lliwedd both run out from the summit — but claim only what the extract carries, because a pyramidal peak needs three or more corries cutting back around one mountain and this map shows the eastern side alone, the further corries lying off its western and southern edges.","Finish with the feature outside the massif: a wide, flat-floored, steep-sided valley holding long narrow lakes is a glacial trough with ribbon lakes, so hollows, ridges and valley are all covered."],"cues":["What landform is a steep-backed hollow holding a lake?","What on the map proves the back wall is rock, not grass?","What are those narrow crests, and how much of the pyramidal-peak test can this extract show?","Which named feature has the answer not used yet?"]},{"title":"Explaining a corrie in the order the mark scheme wants","setup":"The question: explain the formation of a corrie, using an example you have studied. The example is Red Tarn on Helvellyn, 950 m, in the Lake District: a rounded hollow on the north-east side of the mountain, with a steep back wall of bare crag, a lake held in the basin behind a raised rock lip, and the knife-edged ridges Striding Edge and Swirral Edge running out on either side of it.","steps":["Begin before there is any ice: snow settles in a shaded, north-east-facing hollow, out of the afternoon sun and in the lee of the prevailing south-westerly wind, so it survives the summer and is topped up by drifting.","Change the snow into ice: each year’s fall buries the last, air is squeezed out and the snow compacts through firn into dense glacier ice.","Set it moving and attack the back: the ice slides downhill in a circular, rotational motion, and meltwater under pressure refreezes onto the jointed rock at the rear, so blocks are plucked away and the back wall is steepened into crag.","Deepen the floor with the debris: those plucked blocks, frozen into the base of the ice, grind the rock beneath by abrasion, scooping the hollow into a deep armchair-shaped basin.","Explain the lip and the lake: at the open, downhill end the ice is thinner and presses less hard, so it erodes less and leaves a raised rock lip, which now dams meltwater into a tarn — Red Tarn, between Striding Edge and Swirral Edge."],"cues":["Why does snow last the summer on that side?","What has to happen before the snow can move?","Which process makes the back wall vertical?","What are the plucked blocks then used for?","Why is there a lake rather than an open slope?"]},{"title":"Two landforms of deposition, explained rather than described","setup":"The question: explain the formation of two landforms produced by glacial transportation and deposition. Two examples you have studied are the ridges lying across the floor of Cwm Idwal in Eryri, below Llyn Idwal, left by the last glaciers to occupy the hollow; and the Norber erratics in the Yorkshire Dales, where blocks of dark Silurian sandstone rest on top of pale Carboniferous limestone that they did not come from.","steps":["Load the glacier first: freeze-thaw shatters the crags standing above the ice and angular fragments tumble onto its surface and edges, while plucking and abrasion add rock torn from beneath.","Move the load: debris on the margins travels as a lateral moraine, and where two glaciers merge the two inner lateral moraines join and ride down the middle as a medial moraine.","Say why it is ever put down: when melting outpaces snowfall the snout stops advancing, the ice thins and can no longer carry its load, so everything is dropped together — unsorted and angular, which is till.","Name the first landform that leaves: a terminal moraine, a ridge of till thrown across the valley at the furthest point the ice reached, with recessional ridges behind it where the retreat paused — the ridges below Llyn Idwal.","Do the second landform: an erratic is a single block carried far from its parent rock and stranded where the ice melted, which is why Silurian sandstone now sits on limestone at Norber."],"cues":["Where does a glacier get its debris from?","What happens to debris carried along the edges?","What makes a glacier let go of its load?","What shape does that dumping leave at the snout?","How can a boulder end up on the wrong rock?"]},{"title":"Suggesting environmental pressures from a photograph (Figure 3)","setup":"Figure 3, the photograph printed for this room, shows a fell above a lake in the Lake District on a Saturday afternoon in August. A footpath climbs the grassy fellside but has spread into a band of bare, stony ground several paces wide, with loose gravel washed into a shallow channel down its centre and the turf broken along both edges; about thirty walkers are strung along it, several on the grass beside the worn strip. A stone wall separates the open fell from a field of grazing sheep, with litter caught against it and a gate standing open. Below, a small car park on a single-track road is full and about twenty more cars are parked with two wheels on a verge churned to mud. On the lake, two motorboats leave white wakes running to the shore. The question: using Figure 3, suggest the pressures that tourism is placing on the physical environment shown. The focus is the physical environment only — effects on the economy or the local community score nothing.","steps":["Take the widest scar first and name the mechanism: walkers avoid the wet, trampled middle and tread the edges instead, so the route creeps sideways, the turf is killed and bare soil is exposed.","Push that one stage further: with no plant roots left to bind it, rain washes the loose soil downslope, cutting the shallow channel into a gully and carrying silt into the water below.","Move to the verge: tyres compact and kill the grass, mud replaces turf so rain runs off rather than soaking in, and oil and fuel wash off the tarmac into the soil.","Finish on the lake and the fell margin: the boats’ wakes beat against the shoreline and erode it while their noise and fuel disturb fish and birds, and litter and an open gate put grazing stock at risk."],"cues":["Why would a path get wider rather than deeper?","What does rain do once the turf has gone?","What does parking on grass do to the ground?","Which pressures in the photograph are not on land?"]},{"title":"Planning an ‘Assess’ answer on tourism using the figures (Figure 2)","setup":"The question: assess the economic and environmental impacts of tourism in a glaciated upland area of the UK, using Figure 2 and your own understanding. Figure 2 is the table printed for this room: the Lake District National Park, designated 1951, a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2017, area 2,362 km², resident population 38,993 at the 2021 Census, 18.11 million visitors in 2023, highest mountain Scafell Pike 978 m, deepest lake Wastwater 74 m, longest lake Windermere 17 km. The named example is the Lake District. The command word is ‘assess’: weigh the two strands against each other and commit to which of them matters more, rather than listing impacts.","steps":["Tie the tourism to the ice before anything else: Scafell Pike at 978 m is a glacially sculpted summit and Wastwater, 74 m deep, and Windermere, 17 km long, are ribbon lakes in over-deepened troughs, so the visitors, the jobs and the damage you go on to weigh all rest on a landscape the ice cut.","Anchor the economic gain in the table: 18.11 million visitors arriving in a park of 38,993 residents supports hotels, cafés, outdoor shops and mountain guides, and lets farmers diversify into bed and breakfast and campsites.","Give the economic cost honestly: that work is seasonal and low paid, and the same demand turns cottages into second homes and holiday lets, pushing prices past local wages so young families leave.","Turn to the environment with a figure again: 18.11 million people funnelled onto a handful of honeypots strip vegetation from paths, leaving erosion scars, while verge parking compacts soil and boat wash erodes the shoreline.","Build the judgement into the ending: the environmental damage is severe but concentrated and repairable, since pitched paths and speed limits can undo it, whereas the housing change reshapes who is able to live in the valleys at all."],"cues":["What in Figure 2 shows that this tourism rests on a glaciated landscape?","Which figure shows the scale of the economic gain?","What is the economic case against tourism here?","How do you make the environmental point measurable?","Which of the two impacts is harder to reverse?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why do corries in the UK most often face north or east?","a":"They are shaded from the sun, so snow survives the summer, and they lie in the lee of the prevailing south-westerly winds, so snow is blown in and the drift deepens."},{"q":"What is the difference between till and outwash?","a":"Till is dropped directly by the ice, so it is unsorted and angular. Outwash is carried beyond the snout by meltwater, so it is sorted, with the finest material furthest away, and its fragments are rounded."},{"q":"A valley has a flat floor and steep sides, and a tributary valley enters high above the floor with a waterfall. Name both landforms.","a":"A glacial trough (U-shaped valley) and a hanging valley."},{"q":"Which four economic activities does the specification expect in glaciated upland areas?","a":"Tourism, farming, forestry and quarrying."},{"q":"Which two processes steepen a corrie back wall and deepen its floor?","a":"Plucking steepens the back wall; abrasion (helped by rotational slip) deepens the floor."}]},"room":{"facts":["Extent: at the maximum, about 20,000 years ago, ice reached roughly a line from the Bristol Channel across the Midlands to north Norfolk; the maximum ice depth over the British Isles was of the order of 1,500 m, against over 2,500 m over Scandinavia — both illustrative of the scale.","Processes in three groups: weathering — freeze-thaw; erosion — abrasion and plucking; movement — rotational slip and bulldozing; deposition — till (unsorted, dropped by ice) and outwash (sorted, dropped by meltwater).","Eryri (Snowdonia): National Park since 1951, about 2,130 km². Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon), 1,085 m, is a pyramidal peak; Crib Goch is an arête; Llyn Glaslyn and Llyn Llydaw are corrie lakes; the Llanberis Pass is a glacial trough holding the ribbon lakes Llyn Peris and Llyn Padarn; Cwm Idwal, Wales’s first National Nature Reserve (1954), is a hanging corrie with moraine ridges on its floor.","The Lake District: National Park 1951, UNESCO World Heritage Site 2017, area 2,362 km², 38,993 residents at the 2021 Census, 18.11 million visitors in 2023, Scafell Pike 978 m, Wastwater 74 m deep, Windermere 17 km long.","Management in the Lake District: Fix the Fells repairs eroded upland paths by stone pitching and soil inversion with stone flown in by helicopter; a 10 mph speed limit on Windermere, confirmed in 2000, has been in force since March 2005; park-and-ride, seasonal buses and ‘Miles without Stiles’ routes spread visitors out.","Explaining a landform only scores if the processes are named and put in order — the mark scheme gives no credit for describing what a landform looks like or where it is."],"questions":[{"q":"State what is meant by the term ‘till’.","a":"One mark for the unsorted material — clay, sand and angular rock of all sizes — deposited directly by ice as it melts. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"Till is the sediment a glacier drops straight out of the ice as it melts. Because ice carries everything it picks up, till is unsorted: clay, sand, pebbles and boulders lie jumbled together, and the fragments are angular because they have never been rounded by running water. It is also called boulder clay.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a glacier is still carrying at the moment it finally melts.","Compare it with material moved by a river: what does water do to sediment that ice cannot do?","One sentence is enough: say what puts it there, and add a word describing how mixed it is."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (transportation and deposition)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, state the year in which the Lake District became a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","a":"One mark for 2017. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"2017. Figure 2 lists the Lake District’s UNESCO World Heritage Site inscription against the year 2017; it was listed as a cultural landscape, so the farmed fells and the field walls count alongside the scenery.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Everything you need sits in a single row of the table.","Find the row whose label mentions UNESCO rather than the National Park.","Take the figure from the right-hand column of that row and write it on its own."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (tourism example)"]},{"q":"Explain how freeze-thaw weathering breaks up rock on a mountainside.","a":"One mark for stating that water in a crack freezes and expands; a second for developing the repetition of the cycle and the angular fragments it produces. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"Rain or meltwater seeps into joints and cracks in bare rock. When the temperature falls below 0 °C the water freezes and expands by about nine per cent, pushing the sides of the crack apart. When it thaws the meltwater drains deeper and more water enters, so the crack is widened a little further each time. After many freeze-thaw cycles the rock is levered apart along its joints and angular fragments break away, collecting as scree below the crag. Because the rock is broken in place rather than carried off, this is weathering rather than erosion — and on a glaciated mountainside those fragments then fall onto the ice and become the debris the glacier uses to abrade its bed.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what happens to the volume of water when it turns into ice.","You need a cycle, not a single night — say what repeats and where it repeats.","Two sentences: one about the water inside the crack, one about what repetition does to the rock."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (glacial processes)"]},{"q":"Give two characteristics of a corrie.","a":"One mark each for two characteristics, for example a deep armchair-shaped hollow; a steep back wall of bare crag; a raised rock lip at the open end; often holds a tarn. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"A corrie is a deep, rounded, armchair-shaped hollow cut into a mountainside, with a very steep back wall of bare crag rising above it. At its open, downhill end there is a raised rock lip, and because that lip dams the basin a small round lake, a tarn, often sits in it — Red Tarn below Helvellyn in the Lake District, or Llyn Glaslyn beneath the summit crags of Yr Wyddfa.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Picture the shape of an armchair and ask which part is the back and which the seat.","Two separate characteristics score; one characteristic described twice does not.","One characteristic about the shape of the hollow, one about what is found in or around it."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)"]},{"q":"Describe the maximum extent of ice cover across the UK during the last ice age.","a":"One mark for a basic statement of how far the ice reached; a second for development using named places, a limit line or the depth of ice. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"At its maximum, roughly 20,000 years ago, an ice sheet covered almost all of Scotland, Northern Ireland, Wales, northern England and the Midlands. Its southern limit ran approximately from the Bristol Channel, across the English Midlands, to the north Norfolk coast, so southern England — London, the South Downs, most of Devon and Cornwall — lay beyond the ice, although the ground there was frozen tundra. The ice was thickest over the uplands where it grew: AQA’s own resource puts the maximum depth over the British Isles at just over 1,500 m.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which parts of the country have glacial landforms today, and which have none.","You are describing a limit, so describe the line that separated ice from ice-free ground.","Name the two ends of that line, one on the west coast and one on the east."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (extent of ice cover)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, describe the distribution of the lakes shown on the map extract.","a":"One mark for a basic description of where the lakes lie on the extract; a second for a developed point using detail from the map, such as relative height, size or direction from the summit. No credit for naming them alone. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The lakes fall into two groups. Llyn Glaslyn and Llyn Llydaw lie on the eastern side of the massif, below the summit of Yr Wyddfa, and form a step-like sequence down that flank: Glaslyn is the higher and smaller of the two, sitting immediately beneath the summit crags in a rounded hollow with steep ground on three sides, while Llydaw is lower, longer and further east, with Glaslyn draining into it. Llyn Peris and Llyn Padarn lie in the opposite corner of the extract, to the north-west, and are quite different: they are long and narrow, lie end to end on the flat floor of a wide valley rather than in hollows, and the village of Llanberis sits between them.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Start by asking whether the lakes are spread evenly across the extract or clustered.","Naming the lakes scores nothing on its own — the marks are for where they are and how they compare.","One point placing them relative to the summit, one comparing their height, shape or setting."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (example of an upland area)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, suggest the pressures that tourism is placing on the physical environment shown. [4 marks]","a":"Levels 1–2 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4). Credit any reasonable environmental pressure read from the photograph: footpath widening and loss of vegetation, soil erosion and gullying, verge damage from parking, litter, shoreline erosion and pollution from boats, disturbance of stock and wildlife. No credit for effects on the economy or the local community. AO1 = 1 mark, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 1 mark.","sol":"The clearest pressure in Figure 3 is footpath erosion. The path has spread into a band of bare stony ground several paces wide because walkers step onto the grass at the edges to avoid the trampled, wet centre, so the turf beside it is broken and killed. Once the vegetation has gone there are no roots to bind the soil, so rain washes it downslope — the loose gravel channelled down the middle of the path shows this is already happening — and the scar will deepen into a gully and wash silt into the lake below. A second pressure is on the verge: about twenty cars are parked with two wheels on the grass, which has been churned to mud, compacting the soil so that rain runs off it instead of soaking in, and oil and fuel from the vehicles wash into it. On the water, the wakes from the two motorboats break against the shore and erode it, while their noise and fuel disturb the heron and other wildlife at the water’s edge.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Work only from what is visible in the photograph, and only on damage to the physical environment.","Take one feature at a time and ask what it does to soil, vegetation or water.","Two pressures, each carried one stage further than the obvious, beat four one-line observations."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (tourism example)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, suggest why land uses in a glaciated upland area come into conflict. [4 marks]","a":"Levels 1–2 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4). Credit reasons interpreted from the extract: the same fell is wanted for grazing and for recreation; visitors fill lanes and car parks that residents depend on; housing bought as holiday lets is lost to people who work locally; conservation work is undone by the numbers it is meant to serve. Answers must interpret the extract, not list generic problems. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Conflict arises in Figure 4 because the same limited space is wanted for incompatible purposes. The farmer needs the open fell for grazing Herdwicks, but the fell is also the only route walkers can take, so gates are left open and dogs run among ewes in lamb; the farmer is then asked to cut the flock to let the fell recover, which removes the income the farm depends on. The café owner wants the visitors who fill the lane, while the same lane is the residents’ only road, so the traffic that supports the shops is the traffic people complain about. The sharpest conflict is over housing: because cottages are worth more as holiday lets than as homes, someone who works in the valley cannot afford to live in it, and the school roll falls. Even conservation and recreation conflict — the ranger repairs the path with pitched stone, but the visitors it is built for wear it out again, and improving the car park would only draw more cars.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Conflict needs two named users who want the same thing — find the pairs in the extract.","For each pair, ask what one of them gains and what that costs the other.","Two developed conflicts, each traced to a consequence, is what the top level rewards."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (conflicts)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, explain the formation of the landforms of glacial erosion shown on the map extract. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). Credit a sequenced explanation of two or more landforms visible on the extract — corries holding tarns, arêtes, the pyramidal peak, the glacial trough with its ribbon lakes. Processes must be outlined as well as the sequence of formation; explanation of two landforms is enough to reach the top of Level 2. No credit for description alone. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Figure 1 shows Llyn Glaslyn in a rounded hollow immediately below the crags north-east of the summit of Yr Wyddfa; that hollow is a corrie. Snow collected there because the site faces north-east, shaded from the afternoon sun and in the lee of the prevailing south-westerly wind, so it survived successive summers and compacted through firn into ice. As the ice thickened it moved by rotational slip, and meltwater under pressure refroze onto the jointed rock at the back and tore blocks away as the ice pulled forward. That plucking is why the map shows crag symbols and contours almost touching on the back wall. The plucked blocks, frozen into the base of the ice, then ground the floor down by abrasion, scooping out a deep basin, while at the open end thinner ice eroded less and left a rock lip, which now dams the tarn. The same processes working back to back in neighbouring corries left the narrow ridge of Crib Goch between them — an arête — and Y Lliwedd is a second arête running out from the same summit; where three or more corries eat back around one mountain the peak left between them is a pyramidal peak, as Yr Wyddfa is, although this extract shows only its eastern side. The wide, flat-floored valley in the north-west, holding Llyn Peris and Llyn Padarn, is a glacial trough: a valley glacier could not bend around the old river spurs, so it truncated them and widened and deepened the valley, leaving ribbon lakes in the over-deepened floor.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Pick the landforms you can actually point to on the extract before you write anything.","Explanation means sequence: what happened first, what that made possible, what it left behind.","Two landforms explained fully, each tied to map evidence, beat four landforms named."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (example of an upland area)"]},{"q":"Explain the formation of two landforms produced by glacial transportation and deposition. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). Credit any two of the moraines, till plain, outwash plain, drumlin or erratic. Processes of weathering, erosion, transport and deposition should be outlined as well as the sequence of formation; explanation of two landforms is enough to reach the top of Level 2. No credit for describing the position or appearance of the landforms only. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Both landforms begin with debris the glacier is carrying. Freeze-thaw weathering shatters the crags standing above the ice and angular fragments fall onto its surface and edges, while plucking and abrasion tear more rock from the bed. Material carried along the margins travels as a lateral moraine, and where two glaciers merge the two inner lateral moraines join and ride down the centre as a medial moraine. A terminal moraine forms when melting at the snout balances the forward movement of the ice, so the glacier stops advancing and dumps its entire load in one place. Because ice deposits everything together, the ridge is unsorted and angular till, and it lies across the valley marking the furthest point the ice reached; where the retreat paused, recessional ridges are left behind it, as on the floor of Cwm Idwal in Eryri. An erratic forms differently. A single boulder is plucked from its bedrock, carried within or on the ice — sometimes for tens of kilometres and even uphill, since ice does not need a downhill gradient the way water does — and simply stranded when the ice melts. That is why blocks of dark Silurian sandstone now sit on pale Carboniferous limestone at Norber in the Yorkshire Dales: the rock beneath is not the rock the boulder came from.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Start further back than the landform itself: where did the glacier get the material?","The key move is explaining why a glacier lets go of its load at all.","Choose two landforms that form differently, so the second is not a repeat of the first."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (transportation and deposition)","3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (glacial processes)"]},{"q":"Explain how glacial processes changed a V-shaped river valley into a glacial trough. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). Credit a reasoned account of how and why a trough forms: ice occupying a pre-glacial river valley, fed by tributary glaciers from corries; abrasion and plucking widening, deepening and straightening it; truncated spurs, hanging valleys and ribbon lakes as consequences. Processes must be outlined as well as the sequence. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Before the ice there was a river valley, narrow and V-shaped in cross section, with interlocking spurs of higher ground projecting alternately from each side. When the climate cooled, ice built up in corries at the valley head and tributary glaciers joined the main valley, so a thick, heavy mass of ice came to occupy the whole valley. Ice is solid and cannot weave between obstacles the way water does, so the glacier drove straight through the spurs, cutting them off and leaving steep, cliff-like truncated spurs on both sides. As it moved, meltwater under pressure refroze onto the jointed rock of the sides and floor and plucked blocks away, while debris frozen into the base and flanks abraded the rock like sandpaper. Because the ice eroded sideways as well as downwards, the valley was widened and deepened into a steep-sided trough with a broad, flat floor — the U-shaped cross profile. Smaller tributary glaciers had less ice and less erosive power, so their valleys were left perched as hanging valleys above the main floor, often with a waterfall. Where the ice was thickest, or the rock softer, the floor was over-deepened, and after the ice melted those hollows filled with water as ribbon lakes, as in the Llanberis Pass.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Describe what the valley looked like before the ice arrived — that is where the explanation starts.","Ask what a solid mass of ice does when it meets a spur that a river would have flowed around.","Name the erosion processes and then say what each one changed about the cross profile."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (glacial processes)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, explain how a glaciated upland area provides opportunities for different economic activities. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). Credit tourism, farming, forestry and quarrying, each linked to a characteristic of the glaciated landscape — relief and lakes for recreation, thin stony soils and steep slopes for hill sheep and conifers, hard exposed rock for quarrying. Max Level 1 if the activities are not tied to a glaciated upland. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks — the AO3 marks for analysing Figure 2 and using its figures as evidence.","sol":"Each activity in a glaciated upland exists because of something the ice left behind. Tourism is the largest: the corries, arêtes and ribbon lakes are the scenery people come for, and Figure 2 shows the scale — 18.11 million visitors in 2023 to a National Park of 2,362 km² with only 38,993 residents. Scafell Pike at 978 m gives walkers a summit, and Windermere, 17 km long, gives sailing and cruising space, so hotels, cafés, outdoor shops and guides all find work. Farming takes the land tourism cannot use. Glacial erosion stripped the fells to thin, stony soils on steep slopes, and at that altitude arable farming is impossible, so hill sheep farming is the only viable use — hardy Herdwicks graze the open fell and lamb on the lower, flatter trough floors where the better soil lies. Forestry uses the same poor, acidic, exposed slopes, which conifers such as Sitka spruce will tolerate, as at Whinlatter and Grizedale. Quarrying follows the hard volcanic and metamorphic rock the ice scoured bare and made accessible: Honister in Borrowdale is England’s last working slate mine. Between them the four activities share one landscape, which is exactly why they collide over the valley floors and the lanes.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Take the four activities the specification names and deal with them one at a time.","For each one, ask which feature of a glaciated landscape makes it possible — relief, rock, soil or water.","Use at least two figures from the table so the answer is anchored in the resource."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (economic activities)"]},{"q":"‘The landscape of a glaciated upland area in the UK owes more to erosion than to deposition.’ To what extent do you agree? Use a named example. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). Expect a balanced discussion weighing erosional landforms (corries, arêtes, pyramidal peaks, troughs, hanging valleys, ribbon lakes) against depositional ones (moraines, till, drumlins, erratics) in a named UK upland, reaching a judgement about relative importance rather than listing both. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"In Eryri the claim largely holds, but not completely. What gives the massif its shape is erosion. Yr Wyddfa, 1,085 m, is a pyramidal peak only because corries cut back into it on several sides, plucking steepening their walls and abrasion deepening their floors, and the arêtes Crib Goch and Y Lliwedd are simply what is left between them. The Llanberis Pass and the valley running down from it form a glacial trough, its spurs truncated and its floor over-deepened into the ribbon lakes Llyn Peris and Llyn Padarn. These are the features visible from kilometres away, and they involve the removal of enormous volumes of rock, so at the scale of the whole landscape erosion clearly dominates. Deposition, though, decides the detail of where people live and farm. The moraine ridges on the floor of Cwm Idwal, the till spread over the valley floors and the erratics stranded on the slopes are what produced the flatter, better-drained ground that carries the fields, the farms and the roads, and a terminal moraine can dam a lake and so create a landform usually credited to erosion. My judgement is that erosion made the skyline and deposition made the valley floor: erosion is the more important at the landscape scale, but the ground people actually use is depositional.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["‘To what extent’ means deciding how far the claim holds, not simply whether it does.","Sort the landforms of your named area into the two groups before you argue either way.","Find a measure that lets you compare them — scale, visibility, or which one people depend on."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (transportation and deposition)","3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (example of an upland area)"]},{"q":"Assess the effectiveness of the strategies used to manage the impact of tourism in a glaciated upland area of the UK. Use Figure 2 and your own understanding. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). Expect named strategies — footpath repair, traffic and parking management, public transport, speed limits and byelaws, visitor giving — each judged for what it actually removes and what it leaves untouched, leading to a substantiated overall assessment. Max Level 2 if there is no assessment. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"The Lake District carried 18.11 million visitors in 2023 into a park of 2,362 km² with 38,993 residents, so management is judged on whether it can absorb that number rather than reduce it. Footpath repair is the most effective. Fix the Fells re-pitches the worst routes with stone flown in by helicopter and inverts the soil so vegetation recovers, which halts gullying and heals visible scars; but it treats the symptom, costs money every year and has to be repeated as soon as numbers rise again. The 10 mph speed limit on Windermere, in force since March 2005, is more decisive: it ended water-skiing and high-speed powerboating outright, cutting noise, wash and fuel pollution at a stroke, although it also removed a form of recreation and pushed some of that activity elsewhere. Transport measures — park-and-ride, seasonal buses, launches and cycle routes — reduce cars on single-track lanes only where the service is frequent enough to be worth using; on a dry Saturday people still drive and park on verges. Overall, the strategies are effective at protecting the physical environment, because damage to paths and water can be repaired or forbidden, but far weaker against the social pressures of traffic and second homes, which no byelaw addresses.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["‘Assess’ means weighing the parts and committing to an overall verdict, not describing schemes.","For each strategy ask two questions: what pressure does it remove, and what does it leave alone?","Use a figure from the table to show the scale the strategies are working against."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (tourism example)","3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (conflicts)"]},{"q":"‘In glaciated upland areas, conservation should come before economic development.’ Discuss this statement. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). Expect a balanced discussion using real land uses — quarrying, forestry, farming, energy schemes, reservoirs and tourism development — against protective designations, with a reasoned position rather than a list of advantages and disadvantages. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"The case for conservation first is strong because the landscape is the economy. The Lake District was designated a National Park in 1951 and a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2017, and the 18.11 million visitors it received in 2023 came for scenery that quarrying, plantations or new building would spoil; once a corrie wall is quarried away it cannot be put back, and rare arctic-alpine plants on the crags cannot be relocated. Development, though, is what keeps people in these valleys. Only 38,993 people live in the Lake District, and hill farming, forestry and quarrying provide year-round work where tourism offers seasonal, low-paid jobs; Honister, England’s last working slate mine, is both a quarry and a visitor attraction. Some development is also of national importance: Dinorwig pumped-storage power station in Eryri, opened in 1984 and able to generate 1,728 MW, was built inside Elidir Fawr precisely so that a National Park could host it without the mountain being visibly changed. That example decides it for me. Conservation should set the limits, because the damage is irreversible and the landscape is the asset, but it should do so by shaping development rather than forbidding it — otherwise the communities the National Park protects cease to exist.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["‘Discuss’ needs both sides developed, not one side with a token sentence against it.","Test the statement against a real scheme in a named upland area, not against the idea in general.","Ask which kind of damage can be undone and which cannot — that gives you a basis for the position."],"codes":["3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (conflicts)","3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (economic activities)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which erosion process steepens a corrie back wall, and which deepens its floor?","a":"Plucking steepens the back wall; abrasion deepens the floor."},{"q":"What is the difference between a lateral moraine and a terminal moraine?","a":"A lateral moraine is a ridge of debris along the side of a glacier; a terminal moraine is a ridge across the valley at the snout, marking the furthest extent of the ice."},{"q":"Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon), 1,085 m, has arêtes running out from its summit on several sides. Which glacial landform is the summit itself?","a":"A pyramidal peak."},{"q":"From Figure 2, how many visitors did the Lake District receive in 2023?","a":"18.11 million."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Corrie","back":"Armchair-shaped hollow with a steep plucked back wall, a floor deepened by rotational slip and abrasion, and a rock lip at the open end that often dams a tarn. Example: Red Tarn, Helvellyn.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","open":false},{"front":"Arête and pyramidal peak","back":"An arête is a knife-edged ridge left where two corries erode back to back, such as Crib Goch. Where three or more corries erode back around one mountain, a sharp pyramidal peak is left: Yr Wyddfa, 1,085 m.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","open":false},{"front":"Truncated spur and hanging valley","back":"A truncated spur is an interlocking spur of the old river valley sliced off by ice that could not bend round it. A hanging valley is a tributary valley left perched above the main trough floor, usually with a waterfall.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","open":false},{"front":"Glacial trough and ribbon lake","back":"A glacial trough is a former river valley widened, deepened and straightened by ice into a steep-sided valley with a flat floor. A ribbon lake is a long, narrow lake in an over-deepened part of that floor.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (erosion)","open":false},{"front":"Freeze-thaw weathering","back":"Water enters a joint, freezes below 0 °C and expands by about nine per cent, widening the crack. Repeated cycles lever off angular fragments, which form scree or fall onto the glacier.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (glacial processes)","open":false},{"front":"Abrasion and plucking","back":"Abrasion: debris frozen into the ice scrapes the bedrock like sandpaper, leaving striations and rock flour. Plucking: meltwater refreezes onto jointed rock and tears blocks away as the ice moves on.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (glacial processes)","open":false},{"front":"Rotational slip and bulldozing","back":"Rotational slip: ice moving in a circular path within a hollow, deepening it into a corrie basin. Bulldozing: the snout pushing loose material ahead of it as the glacier advances.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (glacial processes)","open":false},{"front":"Till and outwash","back":"Till is dropped straight out of the melting ice, so it is unsorted and angular. Outwash is carried beyond the snout by meltwater, so it is sorted, finest furthest away, and rounded.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (transportation and deposition)","open":false},{"front":"Lateral, medial and terminal moraine","back":"Lateral: a ridge of till along the glacier’s side. Medial: two lateral moraines joined mid-ice where glaciers merge. Terminal: a ridge across the valley at the snout, marking the furthest extent of the ice.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (transportation and deposition)","open":false},{"front":"Drumlin and erratic","back":"A drumlin is a smooth, egg-shaped mound of till moulded by ice, blunt at the up-ice end and tapered down-ice, so it shows the flow direction. An erratic is a boulder carried far from its parent rock and stranded.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (transportation and deposition)","open":false},{"front":"Maximum extent of ice cover in the UK","back":"About 20,000 years ago ice reached roughly a line from the Bristol Channel across the Midlands to north Norfolk. Southern England stayed ice-free tundra. Maximum depth over the British Isles: of the order of 1,500 m (illustrative).","code":"3.1.3.4 — Ice was a powerful force in shaping the physical landscape of the UK (extent of ice cover)","open":false},{"front":"Eryri (Snowdonia) in figures","back":"National Park since 1951, about 2,130 km². Yr Wyddfa 1,085 m is a pyramidal peak; Crib Goch is an arête; Llyn Glaslyn and Llyn Llydaw are corrie lakes; Cwm Idwal, Wales’s first National Nature Reserve (1954), holds moraine ridges.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Distinctive glacial landforms result from different physical processes (example of an upland area)","open":false},{"front":"The Lake District in figures","back":"National Park 1951; UNESCO World Heritage Site 2017; 2,362 km²; 38,993 residents (2021 Census); 18.11 million visitors in 2023; Scafell Pike 978 m; Wastwater 74 m deep; Windermere 17 km long.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (tourism example)","open":false},{"front":"An economic activity in a glaciated upland area — name one and the landscape feature it depends on","back":"Tourism — the corries, arêtes and ribbon lakes people come to walk and sail on; hill sheep farming — thin stony soils on steep slopes where nothing else will grow, grazed by Herdwicks; quarrying — the hard rock the ice stripped bare, as at Honister in Borrowdale.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (economic activities)","open":true},{"front":"A land use conflict in a glaciated upland area — name one","back":"Walkers crossing grazing land, leaving gates open and disturbing sheep; second homes and holiday lets pricing local workers out of housing; powerboats against anglers, wildlife and quiet users, which the 10 mph limit on Windermere settled in 2005.","code":"3.1.3.4 — Glaciated upland areas provide opportunities for different economic activities, and management strategies can be used to reduce land use conflicts (conflicts)","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Map extract: the eastern side of the Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon) massif, Eryri. Written for this room as a description of a large-scale (1:25 000) map; the places and the summit height are real, but this is a written sketch, not a copy of any published map. The summit, Yr Wyddfa, 1,085 m, is towards the western edge of the extract. North-east of the summit the contour lines are drawn almost on top of one another and the map carries the black tick symbols used for crags. Immediately below those crags, in a rounded hollow open to the east, lies a small lake, Llyn Glaslyn. Lower down and further east lies a second, longer lake, Llyn Llydaw; a stream falls steeply from Glaslyn into it. A footpath, the Miners’ Track, runs from the road at Pen-y-Pass in the north-east, along the northern shore of Llyn Llydaw, up past Llyn Glaslyn and on to the summit. Two narrow strips of high ground run out from the summit with contours falling steeply on both sides of each: Crib Goch to the north-east and Y Lliwedd to the south-east. In the north-west corner of the extract, beyond the summit, the ground drops into a wide valley with a flat floor and very steep sides, holding two long narrow lakes end to end, Llyn Peris and Llyn Padarn, with the village of Llanberis between them; a railway line climbs from Llanberis towards the summit.","Figure 2 — Data table: the Lake District National Park in figures. Real data, as published by the Lake District National Park Authority.\nMeasure | Figure\nDesignated a National Park | 1951\nInscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site | 2017\nArea | 2,362 km² (912 square miles)\nResident population (2021 Census) | 38,993\nVisitors (2023) | 18.11 million\nHighest mountain: Scafell Pike | 978 m\nDeepest lake: Wastwater | 74 m\nLongest lake: Windermere | 17 km (10.5 miles)","Figure 3 — Photograph: a fell above a lake in the Lake District, Saturday afternoon in August. Written for this room; the scene is illustrative and nothing in it is measured data. In the foreground a footpath climbs a grassy fellside, but it is no longer a single line: it has spread into a band of bare, stony ground several paces wide, with loose gravel washed into a shallow channel down its centre and the turf broken and undercut along both edges. About thirty walkers are strung out along it, several of them walking on the grass beside the worn strip. Halfway down the slope a stone wall separates the open fell from a field where sheep are grazing; a gate in the wall stands open, and litter — cans and a plastic bag — is caught against the wall. Below the wall a single-track tarmac road runs along the valley side. A small car park beside it is full, and about twenty more cars are parked with two wheels on the grass verge, which has been churned to mud. On the lake beyond, two motorboats are moving fast enough to leave clear white wakes running to the shore; a heron stands at the water’s edge and a canoe is close in to the bank.","Figure 4 — Text extract: four views heard at a parish meeting in a Lake District valley. Written for this room; the speakers are not real people and the details they give are illustrative rather than measured data, although the views are typical of those expressed in the National Park.\nA hill farmer: ‘My family have grazed Herdwicks on this fell for four generations. Walkers leave gates open and let dogs off the lead among ewes in lamb. Now I am told to cut the flock so the fell can recover — but the sheep are what pays for the farm.’\nThe owner of a café and outdoor shop: ‘Nearly everything I take, I take between Easter and October. The visitors fill the car park and the lane, and the same people complain about the traffic. Without them half the shops in the village would close and the bus would stop running.’\nA young resident who works at the hotel: ‘I was born here and I cannot afford to live here. Every cottage that comes up is bought as a holiday let, and the school roll has halved since I was in it.’\nA National Park ranger: ‘The main path off this fell is now wider than the lane. We are repairing it with pitched stone flown in by helicopter, but every dry weekend undoes some of the work. And if we surface the car park, we will simply attract more cars.’"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Eryri (Snowdonia) — an upland area in the UK affected by glaciation","items":["Location: north-west Wales, in the county of Gwynedd; designated a National Park in 1951, covering about 2,130 km².","Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon), 1,085 m, is the highest mountain in Wales and the classic UK pyramidal peak, with corries cut back into it on several sides.","Corries and tarns: Llyn Glaslyn sits in a hollow directly beneath the summit crags; Llyn Llydaw lies lower and further east, and Glaslyn drains into it.","Arêtes: Crib Goch, Crib y Ddysgl and Y Lliwedd are knife-edged ridges left between corries eroding back to back around the summit.","Glacial troughs: the Llanberis Pass and Nant Ffrancon have flat floors, steep sides and truncated spurs, cut by valley glaciers fed from corries at their heads.","Ribbon lakes: Llyn Peris and Llyn Padarn lie end to end on the flat floor of the Llanberis valley, with the village of Llanberis between them.","Hanging valley and deposition: Cwm Idwal, in the Ogwen valley above Nant Ffrancon, holds Llyn Idwal and ridges of unsorted, angular moraine left by the last glaciers to occupy it; it became the first National Nature Reserve in Wales in 1954.","Erratics and drumlins elsewhere in the UK: the Norber erratics near Austwick in the Yorkshire Dales are blocks of dark Silurian sandstone stranded on pale Carboniferous limestone; drumlin swarms occur in the Eden valley in Cumbria and around Ribblehead.","Economic use: slate quarrying at Penrhyn (Bethesda) and Dinorwig (Llanberis) — the Slate Landscape of Northwest Wales became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in July 2021; forestry in the Gwydir forest; hill sheep farming on the open mountain.","Energy and conflict: Dinorwig pumped-storage power station, opened in 1984 and able to generate 1,728 MW, was built inside Elidir Fawr on the site of the old Dinorwig slate quarries, keeping a large development largely out of sight in a National Park.","Tourism pressure: the Snowdon Mountain Railway has climbed from Llanberis to the summit since 1896; in 2019 about 590,000 walkers reached the summit of Yr Wyddfa, with roughly 140,000 more arriving by train.","Timing: ice covered the area at the last glacial maximum about 20,000 years ago and had gone by about 11,700 years ago, apart from a final cold phase when small glaciers re-formed in hollows such as Cwm Idwal."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Lake District — a glaciated upland area in the UK used for tourism","items":["Location and status: Cumbria, north-west England; designated a National Park in 1951 and inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site, as a cultural landscape, in 2017.","Size and people: 2,362 km² (912 square miles), England’s largest National Park, with 38,993 residents at the 2021 Census.","Visitors: 18.11 million in 2023 — roughly 460 visitors a year for every resident.","Attractions from the ice: Scafell Pike, 978 m, England’s highest mountain; Wastwater, 74 m, England’s deepest lake; Windermere, 17 km, England’s longest; Red Tarn, a corrie lake below Helvellyn (950 m) between the arêtes Striding Edge and Swirral Edge; glacial troughs such as Great Langdale.","Social impacts: seasonal jobs in hotels, cafés and the outdoor industry; services such as village shops and bus routes kept alive by visitor spending; but housing bought as second homes and holiday lets pushes prices beyond local wages, so young people leave and school rolls fall.","Economic impacts: tourism spending supports the local economy and lets farmers diversify into bed and breakfast and campsites; against that, the work is seasonal and often low paid, and congestion on single-track lanes costs residents time.","Environmental impacts: footpath erosion widens paths into bare scars and washes soil into becks; honeypot sites such as Bowness, Ambleside, Keswick and Grasmere are overcrowded; verge parking compacts and kills grass; litter increases in season; boat wash erodes shorelines and fuel pollutes the water.","Management — the ground: Fix the Fells, a partnership hosted by the National Trust with the Lake District National Park Authority, Natural England, the Lake District Foundation and Friends of the Lake District, repairs eroded upland paths by stone pitching and soil inversion, with stone flown in by helicopter because there is no vehicle access.","Management — the water: a 10 mph speed limit on Windermere, confirmed in 2000 and in force since March 2005, ended water-skiing and high-speed powerboating on the lake.","Management — movement: park-and-ride and seasonal bus services, lake launches and ferries, cycle routes, and ‘Miles without Stiles’ accessible paths that spread visitors away from the worst-hit routes.","Other land uses that compete with tourism: hill sheep farming with hefted Herdwick flocks; conifer forestry at Whinlatter and Grizedale; slate quarrying at Honister in Borrowdale, England’s last working slate mine; and water supply — Haweswater reservoir, completed in 1935, drowned the village of Mardale Green to supply Manchester.","Land ownership matters: the National Trust owns about a fifth to a quarter of the National Park, so much of the fell land is managed for conservation as well as farming."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: glacial processes and terms for this room","items":["Freeze-thaw weathering (frost shattering): water in a joint freezes below 0 °C and expands by about nine per cent; repeated cycles lever off angular fragments that form scree or fall onto the glacier.","Abrasion: rock debris frozen into the base and sides of the glacier scrapes the bedrock like sandpaper, leaving scratches called striations and fine rock flour.","Plucking: meltwater under pressure refreezes onto loosened, jointed rock, so blocks are torn away as the ice moves forward, leaving a jagged surface.","Rotational slip: ice rotating in a circular path within a hollow, which is what deepens a corrie basin.","Bulldozing: the snout of an advancing glacier pushing loose material ahead of it.","Why glaciers deposit: when ablation (melting) outpaces accumulation (snowfall), the ice thins and cannot carry its load, so it drops it — all at once, and therefore unsorted.","Till: sediment deposited directly by ice — unsorted, angular, sometimes called boulder clay. Outwash: sediment carried beyond the snout by meltwater — sorted, with the finest material furthest from the ice, and rounded.","Firn (névé): compacted, granular old snow, the stage between fresh snow and glacier ice.","Corrie (cwm in Wales, cirque): armchair-shaped hollow with a steep plucked back wall, a basin deepened by rotational slip and abrasion, and a rock lip that often dams a tarn.","Arête: knife-edged ridge between two corries eroding back to back. Pyramidal peak: sharp summit left where three or more corries erode back around one mountain.","Truncated spur: an interlocking spur of the pre-glacial river valley cut off by ice that could not bend round it. Hanging valley: a tributary valley left above the main trough floor, usually with a waterfall.","Glacial trough: former river valley widened, deepened and straightened into a steep-sided valley with a flat floor. Ribbon lake: a long, narrow lake in an over-deepened stretch of that floor.","Moraine: lateral (along the glacier’s side), medial (two lateral moraines joined mid-ice where glaciers merge), terminal (across the valley at the snout, marking the furthest extent of the ice) and recessional (marking pauses during retreat).","Drumlin: smooth, egg-shaped mound of till moulded by moving ice, blunt at the up-ice end and tapering down-ice, so a drumlin field records the direction of flow. Erratic: a single boulder carried far from its parent rock and stranded when the ice melted."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) on the six-mark question ‘Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, explain the formation of the landforms of glacial erosion shown on the map extract’","items":["The paragraph: Figure 1 shows Llyn Glaslyn lying in a rounded hollow immediately below the crags north-east of the summit of Yr Wyddfa, and that hollow is a corrie. It formed because snow collected in a shaded, north-east-facing hollow, out of the afternoon sun and in the lee of the prevailing south-westerly wind, so it survived successive summers and compacted through firn into glacier ice. As the ice thickened it began to move downhill by rotational slip, and meltwater under pressure at the back of the hollow refroze onto the jointed rock and tore blocks away as the ice pulled forward; that plucking is why the map shows crag symbols and contours almost touching on the back wall. The plucked blocks were then dragged across the floor and ground it down by abrasion, scooping the basin deeper. At the open, downhill end the ice was thinner and pressed less hard, so it eroded less and left a rock lip, which now dams Glaslyn. The steep ground on both sides of Crib Goch on the map is the result of the same processes working back to back in neighbouring corries, leaving an arête between them; Crib Goch and Y Lliwedd both converge on the summit, and where three or more corries eat back around one mountain the peak left between them is a pyramidal peak, as Yr Wyddfa is — though this extract shows only its eastern side.","The point: it names the landform before explaining it — ‘that hollow is a corrie’ — so the examiner knows from the first line which feature is being explained.","The named evidence: Llyn Glaslyn, Yr Wyddfa and Crib Goch are named from the extract, and the map evidence itself is quoted — crag symbols and contours almost touching — so the answer uses the resource rather than ignoring it.","The developed explanation: four processes appear in order, each caused by the one before — snow accumulation, compaction through firn, rotational slip and plucking, then abrasion — which is what the Level 3 descriptor means by ‘detailed explanation of formation of the features shown’.","The link to the question: every process is tied back to something visible on Figure 1, so the answer analyses the resource rather than reciting a textbook sequence, meeting ‘makes full analysis of the resource, using evidence to support response’.","The mini-judgement: the last sentence connects the corrie to the arête and the pyramidal peak, showing the interrelationship between the three landforms instead of treating them as a list — the AO2 strand the Clear level asks for and the Detailed level assumes."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) on the same six-mark question, visibly weaker","items":["The paragraph: There is a lake on the map called Llyn Glaslyn. It is in a big hollow in the mountain with steep sides all around it, and there is a thin ridge next to it. The glacier eroded the hollow out of the rock and made it much deeper, and afterwards the lake filled up with water. The ridges are very steep and narrow. Yr Wyddfa is a very tall mountain at 1,085 m and there are lots of glacial landforms in this area.","The descriptor it meets: ‘Demonstrates some knowledge of processes operating and the resultant landforms in glacial environments’ — the word ‘eroded’ and the recognition that ice made the hollow are just enough to be worth a mark or two.","The descriptor it misses: ‘Demonstrates thorough application of knowledge and understanding to analyse geographical information, giving detailed explanation of formation of the features shown.’ No process is named — there is no plucking, no abrasion, no rotational slip — so ‘the glacier eroded it’ carries the whole explanation.","It also misses ‘makes full analysis of the resource, using evidence to support response’: the map is used only as a place to read names off, and no map evidence such as the crag symbols or the spacing of the contours is quoted.","It describes instead of explaining: ‘steep sides all around it’, ‘very steep and narrow’ and the summit height are appearance and location, which the mark scheme explicitly refuses to credit on formation questions.","There is no sequence and no interrelationship: nothing says why snow gathered there in the first place, why the lake is held in, or that the ridge exists because two corries met — so the landforms are left as separate objects rather than one connected landscape.","The fix in one move: take each sentence and ask ‘by which process, and what did that leave?’ — adding named processes in order is what lifts the same content from Basic to Detailed."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan: ‘Assess the economic and environmental impacts of tourism in a glaciated upland area of the UK. Use Figure 2 and your own understanding. [6 marks]’","items":["The command word decoded: ‘assess’ means weigh the parts and give an informed overall judgement. Listing impacts without weighing them is capped below the top level, and impacts that are not specific to a glaciated upland area are capped at the bottom, so every point must be recognisably about fells, lakes and narrow valleys.","What the stem demands: two strands are named — economic and environmental — and both must appear; ‘a glaciated upland area of the UK’ means a named example, here the Lake District; ‘use Figure 2’ means the table’s figures must do work in the argument, not sit in a first sentence and be abandoned.","Paragraph 1 — the economic gain, using the Lake District: 18.11 million visitors in 2023 arriving in a park of 2,362 km² with 38,993 residents supports hotels, cafés, outdoor shops and mountain guides, and lets hill farmers diversify into bed and breakfast and campsites, keeping village shops and bus routes alive.","Paragraph 2 — the economic cost, same example: the work is seasonal, concentrated between Easter and October, and often low paid; meanwhile demand turns cottages into second homes and holiday lets, so people who work in the valley cannot afford to live in it and school rolls fall.","Paragraph 3 — the environmental cost: 18.11 million visitors funnel onto a few honeypots such as Bowness, Ambleside and Grasmere, so paths spread into bare scars, soil is washed into becks, verges are churned to mud by parked cars, litter increases in season and boat wash erodes shorelines.","Paragraph 4 — the environmental case the other way: tourism is why the landscape is protected and funded at all — National Park designation in 1951, World Heritage inscription in 2017, Fix the Fells repairing paths with pitched stone, and the 10 mph limit on Windermere in force since March 2005, which removed the noise, wash and fuel pollution of powerboating outright.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: which impacts can be reversed and which cannot? Path erosion can be re-pitched and boat damage can be forbidden by byelaw; a valley whose houses have all become holiday lets does not come back.","The conclusion must do three things: name which impact you judge the greater and say so in a sentence; justify it with the thread you have been running, not with a new point; and tie it back to the named example, for example that in the Lake District the environmental damage is severe but repairable while the economic reshaping of who can live there is lasting.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly seven minutes — two minutes to plan the four points and the verdict, five to write. Two developed points on each side beat six undeveloped ones.","A note on tariff: on the physical landscapes section of Paper 1 the extended question that closes the section is the six-mark one, and this plan is built to that shape; where a nine-mark question carries three extra marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology, add a minute at the end to check them."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:54:44Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"No wrong answer keys and no wrong facts: every figure in Figure 2 matches the Lake District National Park Authority's own facts-and-figures page exactly (2,362 km², 38,993 residents at the 2021 Census, 18.11 million visitors in 2023, Scafell Pike 978 m, Wastwater 74 m, Windermere 17 km, 1951, 2017), the Eryri figures check out (2,130 km²/823 sq miles, 1951, Yr Wyddfa 1,085 m, Cwm Idwal Wales's first NNR in 1954, Dinorwig 1,728 MW opened 1984 inside Elidir Fawr, slate World Heritage inscription 28 July 2021, 590,984 walkers on Yr Wyddfa in 2019 plus about 140,000 by rack railway since 1896, Windermere's 10 mph byelaw confirmed in 2000 and in force from 29 March 2005), and every countable claim in the four printed figures recounts correctly in the questions, solutions, worked examples, fact files, plan and exit ticket; the absence of a nine-mark question is right, because specimen Paper 1 Question 5 runs 05.1–05.6 at 1, 1, 1, 2, 4 and 6 marks for 15 in total and Section C carries no SPaG, so the six-mark plan and the Level 1/Level 3 model pair are correctly built to the only extended tariff the glacial option has — publish once the two AO splits on questions 10 and 11 are fixed and the pyramidal-peak step is squared with Figure 1."}}},"3.2.1":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.2.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Urban issues and challenges is Section A of Paper 2 and carries 33 marks, more than any other single section you will meet on that paper, and it closes with a nine-mark extended question that also carries three marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology. Almost every mark in it depends on named places: an examiner can tell in one sentence whether you have a real LIC or NEE city and a real UK city in your head, or only the words slum, jobs and pollution. This room fixes two case studies and two smaller examples in place — Rio de Janeiro and the Favela Bairro Project, Bristol and the Harbourside regeneration — and teaches you to carry their dates, figures and mechanisms into an answer that weighs both sides and reaches a judgement, because that is what separates a Detailed answer from a Clear one.","idea":[{"h":"Urbanisation: why the world is becoming urban, and how the pattern differs","t":"Urbanisation is the increase in the proportion of a country's population living in urban areas. It is not the same as urban growth, which is the increase in the number of urban dwellers. The world passed the halfway mark in 2007, when for the first time more people lived in urban than in rural areas; about 55% of the world was urban in 2018 and the UN projects roughly 68% by 2050. The pattern is uneven. High income countries such as the UK urbanised early, during the Industrial Revolution, and are now about 84% urban with slow urban growth, and in places with counter-urbanisation as people move out to the countryside and commuter settlements. Africa and Asia are the least urbanised continents but are urbanising fastest, so most of the world's urban growth from now to 2050 will happen there. Two processes drive the rate: rural-urban migration, explained by push factors that make rural life hard and pull factors that make the city attractive, and natural increase, because migrants are mostly young adults so births outnumber deaths in the city. Megacities, urban areas of more than 10 million people, are the extreme result: the UN counted 33 in 2018 and projects 43 by 2030, and a growing majority of them are in LICs and NEEs. A top-level answer never treats urbanisation as one global process — it names the contrast between HIC and LIC or NEE trends and explains why the rates differ.","code":"3.2.1 — A growing percentage of the world’s population lives in urban areas"},{"h":"Rio de Janeiro: where it is, why it matters, and why it grew","t":"The specification wants location and importance at three scales, and growth from two causes. Rio de Janeiro sits on the Atlantic coast of south-east Brazil, on the western shore of Guanabara Bay, with steep granite hills rising straight out of the built-up area. Regionally it is the commercial and administrative centre of its state, with a large port and Brazil's oil and gas industry; Petrobras, the state oil company, is headquartered there. Nationally it was the capital of Brazil until 1960, when government moved to the purpose-built Brasília, and it remains a centre of media, culture and tourism; it is Brazil's second city after São Paulo, with about 6.2 million people in the municipality at the 2022 census and roughly 12 million in the metropolitan area. Internationally, Christ the Redeemer, Sugarloaf Mountain and the Copacabana and Ipanema beaches draw visitors from across the world, and the city hosted the final of the 2014 FIFA World Cup at the Maracanã and the 2016 Olympic and Paralympic Games. Growth came from rural-urban migration, especially from the drought-prone Nordeste of north-east Brazil where farm incomes are low and services thin, and from natural increase, because those migrants were young adults whose children were born in the city while health care cut the death rate. A top-level answer gives importance at all three scales and separates the two causes of growth rather than merging them into people moved there.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (location, importance and growth)"},{"h":"The opportunities urban growth creates in Rio","t":"The specification splits opportunities into social and economic, and the mark scheme warns that environmental points score nothing unless they touch living standards. Socially, a city concentrates services: Rio has schools and secondary education within reach of children who would have none in a remote rural district, and Brazil's Family Health Strategy, begun in the 1990s, puts clinics and community health workers into poor urban neighbourhoods, so vaccination, antenatal care and treatment are available where they were not. Access to resources works the same way: treated water from the Guandu works west of the city, one of the largest water treatment plants in the world, and mains electricity reach most of the built-up area, and connections have been extended into favelas, which cuts water-borne disease and allows refrigeration, lighting and study after dark. Economically, urban industrial areas act as a stimulus: construction, the port, oil and gas, manufacturing such as food, clothing and footwear, and tourism all pay regular wages far above subsistence farming, and those wages are spent locally, creating further jobs in shops, transport and services — the multiplier effect. A top-level answer explains the mechanism by which an opportunity improves a life, and then concedes that the opportunity is unevenly shared, because the command words on this content are usually to what extent or assess.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (opportunities)"},{"h":"The challenges urban growth creates in Rio, and planning for the urban poor","t":"Growth outruns planning, and the result is the favela: an informal, largely self-built settlement, usually on steep hillsides or marshy ground that nobody else wanted. Over a fifth of Rio's residents live in one: the 2010 Brazilian census (IBGE) counted about 22% of the municipality's population in favelas and the 2022 census records more, so the defensible share to quote is about a fifth to a quarter, dated to the census. Rocinha, above São Conrado in the South Zone, is the largest, with around 70,000 counted at the 2010 census and local estimates higher. The challenges follow from the informality. Housing is unplanned and crowded on unstable slopes, so heavy summer rain brings landslides. Water and sanitation lag behind, and untreated sewage still reaches Guanabara Bay, which remains badly polluted despite a pledge to clean it before the 2016 Olympics. Electricity is often taken by illegal connections, which is dangerous. Schools and clinics are overcrowded, and narrow stepped alleys keep ambulances and refuse lorries out. Unemployment is masked by insecure informal work, and drug gangs controlled many favelas, which is why Pacifying Police Units were placed in selected favelas from 2008, with contested results. Waste, air pollution and congestion complete the list. The planning answer is upgrading rather than clearance: the Favela Bairro Project, begun in 1994 with Inter-American Development Bank funding, paved access roads, laid mains water, sewerage and drainage, stabilised slopes, put in street lighting, legal electricity, health posts, day care and training, and worked towards legal title for residents. A top-level answer links each challenge to the physical site or the informality that causes it, and judges the planning scheme rather than merely listing its features.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (challenges)"},{"h":"Where people live in the UK, and how migration made Bristol","t":"UK population is densest in London and the South East, the West Midlands around Birmingham, the North West around Manchester and Liverpool, West Yorkshire around Leeds, the North East around Newcastle, and the Central Lowlands of Scotland around Glasgow and Edinburgh; it is sparsest in the Scottish Highlands, mid-Wales, the Pennines and the Lake District, where relief, climate and distance from work keep numbers low. London alone had about 8.8 million people at the 2021 census. Bristol lies in south-west England on the River Avon, about 190 km west of London and close to where the Avon meets the Severn Estuary, with the M4 and M5 meeting just north of the city. It had about 470,000 people at the 2021 census, the largest city in the south-west. It grew rich from Atlantic trade, including the transatlantic slave trade, and today it matters for aerospace at Filton, where Airbus and Rolls-Royce have sites, for financial and legal services, for two universities, and for a creative sector including Aardman Animations and the BBC's Natural History Unit; Bristol was named European Green Capital in 2015, the first UK city to hold the title. Migration shaped its character: Caribbean arrivals from the late 1940s settled in inner areas such as St Paul's, where the St Paul's Carnival was first held in 1968, and later Somali, Polish and other communities followed, while national migration brings students and graduates in and moves families out to South Gloucestershire and North Somerset. A top-level answer ties migration to a named district and a named consequence, not to the word multicultural.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (distribution, location and migration)"},{"h":"The opportunities urban change creates in Bristol","t":"The specification names five: cultural mix, recreation and entertainment, employment, integrated transport systems, and urban greening. In Bristol the cultural mix shows in food, music, places of worship and events such as the St Paul's Carnival, and it gives employers a workforce with a wide range of languages and skills. Recreation and entertainment cluster on the regenerated Harbourside, with the Arnolfini arts centre in a former tea warehouse on Narrow Quay, the Watershed media centre in former dockside transit sheds, the M Shed museum, the science centre that opened in 2000 and is now called We The Curious, and Brunel's SS Great Britain, returned to the city in 1970 and restored in the dry dock where she was built. Employment has shifted from docks and heavy industry to aerospace, creative industries, finance and higher education, which raises average wages but demands qualifications. Integrated transport means linking modes so a journey works without a car: MetroBus, opened in 2018, runs partly on segregated busway and partly on ordinary roads and shared bus lanes, and connects park-and-ride sites and suburban rail, and Bristol was designated England's first Cycling City in 2008. Urban greening is the deliberate increase of vegetation and green space: parks, street trees, green roofs and the restoration of Queen Square in the 1990s, when the through road driven across it was removed. A top-level answer states who gains from each opportunity and, in an evaluation, notices who does not.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (opportunities)"},{"h":"The challenges urban change creates in Bristol, sprawl, and the Harbourside regeneration","t":"Urban deprivation is the concentration of several disadvantages in one place: low incomes, poor housing, weaker exam results, worse health and higher unemployment reinforcing one another. Bristol contains neighbourhoods among the most deprived tenth in England and others among the least deprived, sometimes only a few kilometres apart, and house prices and rents have risen faster than local wages, so affordable housing is short. Environmentally, old docks, factories and railway land were left derelict; brownfield land is expensive to build on because it must be cleared and sometimes decontaminated, while greenfield land on the edge is cheaper but consumes countryside. Urban sprawl is the outward spread of the built-up area into that countryside: growth towards Cribbs Causeway in South Gloucestershire, where The Mall opened in 1998, and into North Somerset, has taken farmland, created commuter settlements and lengthened journeys, which is one reason a Clean Air Zone began charging in Bristol in November 2022. The regeneration example answers the dereliction. Bristol's city docks closed to commercial traffic in 1975 once ships outgrew the tidal Avon and trade moved to Avonmouth and later Royal Portbury, leaving empty quaysides, transit sheds and railway land in the middle of the city. Harbourside reused them: warehouses became arts venues, Millennium Square and the science centre opened in 2000, M Shed opened in 2011 in a former transit shed, and new flats, offices, hotels and restaurants brought people back to live in the centre. A top-level answer gives both the reason the area needed regeneration and the features of the scheme, and then judges who benefited.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (challenges and regeneration)"},{"h":"Sustainable urban living: water, energy, waste and green space","t":"Sustainable urban living means running a city so that it meets the needs of people now without damaging the ability of future generations to meet theirs. The specification names four features. Water conservation cuts demand and loss: rainwater harvesting for gardens and toilets, low-flush toilets and water-efficient appliances, metering so households see what they use, and repairing leaking mains, which in many cities loses a large share of treated water before it reaches a tap. Energy conservation cuts demand and emissions: insulation and better glazing, solar panels, district heating, and designing streets so that walking, cycling and public transport are the easy choices. Waste recycling keeps material out of landfill and out of the atmosphere: kerbside collection of separated waste, composting of food and garden waste, and energy recovery from what remains. Creating green space delivers several gains at once, which is why it is often the strongest paragraph in an answer: parks, street trees, green roofs and allotments cool a city in a heatwave, absorb rainfall so drains are less likely to flood, support wildlife, and improve physical and mental health. Bristol, European Green Capital in 2015, and the Vauban district of Freiburg in Germany, built from the late 1990s with parking kept to the edge of the neighbourhood and homes built to high insulation standards, show the ideas working together. A top-level answer shows the features reinforcing one another and asks who pays for them, because that is where the judgement lies.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban sustainability requires management of resources and transport (sustainable urban living)"},{"h":"Urban transport strategies and the congestion they are meant to cure","t":"Congestion happens because many people need to reach a small central area on a road network that cannot easily be widened, so cities manage demand instead of adding road space. The strategies are: charging, as with London's congestion charge from 17 February 2003, which began at £5 a day for driving in the central zone on weekdays; improving public transport, such as Manchester's trams, London's Elizabeth line, which opened in May 2022, or Bristol's MetroBus; park and ride, as at Oxford, so cars stop at the edge; pedestrianisation of central streets; bus lanes and cycle lanes, with public cycle hire, which London introduced in 2010; ring roads to keep through traffic out; car sharing and staggered working hours; and low-emission zones, such as London's Ultra Low Emission Zone, which began in central London in April 2019 charging older vehicles £12.50 a day and now covers every London borough. Evaluating them means naming the mechanism and the evidence: Transport for London reported that in the congestion charge's first year traffic entering the zone fell by about a fifth and congestion within it by roughly 30%, but the flat charge falls hardest on lower-income drivers, some traffic re-routed around the boundary, and congestion crept back as private-hire and delivery traffic grew. A top-level answer weighs advantages against disadvantages for one named scheme and concludes, because the mark scheme gives no credit for simply describing the problems.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban sustainability requires management of resources and transport (transport)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading a difference off a table for two marks","setup":"Figure 1, the table written for this room, gives the percentage of people living in urban areas in 2018: Africa 43, Asia 50, Oceania 68, Europe 74, Latin America and the Caribbean 81, Northern America 82, World 55. The question is: Using Figure 1, describe the difference between Africa and Northern America in the percentage of people living in urban areas. [2 marks]","steps":["Pick out only the two rows named: Africa reads 43 and Northern America reads 82, so Northern America is the more urbanised of the two.","Write it as one comparative sentence rather than two separate statements, because a description of difference must hold both places together: Northern America has a much higher urban percentage than Africa.","Quantify the gap to earn the developed second mark: 82 against 43 is a difference of 39 percentage points, so Northern America's figure is almost double Africa's."],"cues":["Which two rows of the table does the question actually allow you to use?","What is wrong with writing one sentence about each place in turn?","What can you do with the two values to turn a basic point into a developed one?"]},{"title":"A one-mark calculation from a line graph","setup":"Figure 3, the line graph written for this room, plots the percentage of the world's population living in urban areas: 1950 = 30, 1970 = 37, 1990 = 43, 2007 = 50, 2018 = 55, 2030 = 60 projected, 2050 = 68 projected. The question is: Using Figure 3, calculate the increase in the percentage of the world's population living in urban areas between 1950 and 2018. [1 mark]","steps":["Take the two values the question names off the line and ignore every other plotted point: 1950 reads 30% and 2018 reads 55%.","Decide what increase means here. Because both readings are already percentages, an increase is a subtraction in percentage points, not a percentage change, so the working is 55 minus 30.","55 minus 30 = 25, so the increase is 25 percentage points. On a one-mark calculation the single mark is for the answer itself, so what matters is that the figure and its unit are written clearly; working is worth jotting down as a check on yourself, but it is the two-mark version of a calculation that can credit it."],"cues":["Which two points on the line does the question restrict you to?","Should you subtract the readings or divide one by the other, and why?","The single mark pays for one thing only - what is it, and what unit must it carry?"]},{"title":"Planning a to what extent answer on opportunities in an NEE city","setup":"The question is: To what extent do urban areas in LICs and NEEs provide social and economic opportunities for people? [6 marks] The city you have studied is Rio de Janeiro in Brazil, where about a fifth to a quarter of residents live in a favela (about 22% at the 2010 census), where city schools and the clinics and community health teams of Brazil's Family Health Strategy reach poor districts, where treated water from the Guandu works and mains electricity serve most of the built-up area, and where construction, the port, oil and gas, manufacturing and tourism provide wage work. Plan the answer.","steps":["Decode the command before writing anything: to what extent asks how far the opportunities are real, so the answer must weigh them against their limits and end in a judgement, not list benefits.","Develop one social opportunity through to a changed life: a favela child can reach a school and a clinic that a remote rural district cannot provide, so literacy rises and treatable illness is treated.","Develop one economic opportunity through to a changed life: construction, the port and tourism pay regular wages far above subsistence farming, so a family can afford brick walls, piped water and electricity, and can keep children in school.","Supply the qualification the command word demands: much of the work is informal and insecure, city rents and food cost more, and the poorest favelas still have crowded schools and thin services, so the opportunity people migrate towards is not always the one they find.","Close with the judgement itself: the city does provide substantial social and economic opportunity compared with rural life, but unequally, so the extent depends on where in the city a household ends up."],"cues":["What does the command phrase tell you the last sentence of the answer must do?","A social opportunity has been named — what has to be added before it earns credit?","Which economic activities in this city would you choose, and what do the wages let a family buy?","The answer so far is one-sided; what must come next for the command word to be satisfied?","What exactly is left to write once both sides are on the page?"]},{"title":"Suggesting reasons for regeneration from a text extract","setup":"Figure 4, the text extract written for this room, says that as cargo ships grew larger they could no longer be brought up the tidal River Avon and through the Avon Gorge, that trade moved downstream to the deep-water docks at Avonmouth and later Royal Portbury, that Bristol's city docks closed to commercial traffic in 1975, and that what was left in the middle of the city was empty quaysides, disused transit sheds and warehouses, redundant cranes and railway sidings, with the dock jobs gone, some ground contaminated by its industrial past and the harbour water dirty. The question is: Using Figure 4, suggest why the Harbourside area of Bristol needed regenerating. [4 marks]","steps":["Lift the trigger from the extract first, because an answer that ignores the resource is capped: the docks shut to commercial traffic in 1975 once ships had outgrown the Avon, so the area lost the single activity it existed for.","Turn that loss into an economic reason: with the ships and the dock jobs gone, quayside employment collapsed and nothing replaced it, so the area emptied of workers, customers and visitors alike.","Turn the physical evidence into a land-use and environmental reason: empty sheds, warehouses, redundant cranes and railway sidings left a large derelict brownfield site in the city centre, some of it contaminated and beside dirty water.","Say why that matters enough to justify acting, which is what suggest is asking for: derelict land in the middle of a city is wasted space that drags down the appearance, safety and value of everything around it, so there was a strong case for bringing it back into use."],"cues":["What single event in the extract set everything else off?","Once the ships had gone, what happened to the people who had worked there?","The extract lists physical leftovers — what kind of site do they add up to?","Why would a council not simply leave the land as it was?"]},{"title":"Opening and balancing a nine-mark evaluation of a transport scheme","setup":"The question is: Evaluate the effectiveness of an urban transport scheme in reducing traffic congestion. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks] The scheme you have studied is London's congestion charge, which began on 17 February 2003 at £5 a day for driving in the central charging zone on weekdays. Transport for London reported that in the first year traffic entering the zone fell by about a fifth and congestion inside it by roughly 30%. Revenue is reinvested in public transport, mainly buses; a public cycle-hire scheme opened in 2010; the Ultra Low Emission Zone began in central London in April 2019 and now covers every borough. Critics say the flat charge falls hardest on lower-income drivers, that some traffic re-routed around the zone, and that central London congestion later crept back up as private-hire and delivery vehicles increased. Plan and open the answer.","steps":["Decode the command: evaluate wants a verdict on how well the scheme works, so every paragraph ends in a judgement and none of them simply describes what congestion is like.","Open with the scheme named, located and dated, because that is what the knowledge strand rewards: the congestion charge, central London, weekdays, from February 2003 at £5 a day.","Build the effective side around the mechanism rather than the figure alone: charging each car journey directly makes the least essential trips not worth making, and the money raised improves the buses those drivers switch to, which makes the shift stick.","Build the less effective side from the same scheme, because a balanced appreciation of advantages and disadvantages is what the top level names: a flat charge is regressive, some drivers re-routed around the boundary, and later growth in private-hire and delivery traffic ate into the gain.","Finish with a qualified overall verdict, written in accurate prose with the specialist terms spelt correctly because marks ride on that too: effective and immediate inside the charged area, weaker as a permanent cure, and dependent on having a public transport network good enough to switch to."],"cues":["What does evaluate require every paragraph to end with?","Which details about the scheme must appear in the first two sentences?","Why is quoting the fall in traffic not enough on its own?","What must the next paragraph do that the previous one did not?","What two things does the final paragraph have to deliver at once?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between urbanisation and urban growth?","a":"Urbanisation is a rise in the proportion of a population living in urban areas; urban growth is a rise in the number of urban dwellers. A city can grow in numbers while the country's urbanisation rate barely moves."},{"q":"Why does a city keep growing even if rural-urban migration stops?","a":"Because of natural increase. Migrants are mostly young adults of childbearing age, so births in the city outnumber deaths, especially where better health care has lowered the death rate."},{"q":"Why is building on brownfield land often more expensive than building on greenfield land?","a":"Brownfield land has been built on before, so old structures must be demolished and cleared and the ground may need decontaminating before anything new can go up; greenfield land needs none of that."},{"q":"In an answer about opportunities in an LIC or NEE city, why is it not enough to write that the city has hospitals?","a":"Because credit comes from the mechanism and its consequence. The point has to be developed: a clinic within reach means treatable illness is treated and vaccination rates rise, which a remote rural district cannot deliver."}]},"room":{"facts":["Urbanisation is the increase in the proportion of a population living in urban areas. More than half the world's people have lived in urban areas since 2007; the figure was about 55% in 2018 and the UN projects about 68% by 2050.","A megacity is an urban area of more than 10 million people. The UN counted 33 in 2018 and projects 43 by 2030; Tokyo, at around 37 million, is the largest urban agglomeration.","Urban growth has two causes and both must be named: rural-urban migration, explained by push and pull factors, and natural increase, the birth rate exceeding the death rate because migrants are mostly young adults.","The LIC or NEE city: Rio de Janeiro, south-east Brazil, on Guanabara Bay. About 6.2 million people in the city at the 2022 census, roughly 12 million in the metropolitan area; Brazil's capital until 1960; host of the 2016 Olympics; about a fifth to a quarter of residents live in a favela (about 22% at the 2010 census).","The UK city: Bristol, south-west England on the River Avon, about 190 km west of London, about 470,000 people at the 2021 census, European Green Capital in 2015. Its city docks closed to commercial traffic in 1975, and the derelict quaysides became the regenerated Harbourside.","The nine-mark urban question is marked on Levels 1 to 3 (Basic 1-3, Clear 4-6, Detailed 7-9) with three further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology, so allow about ten to eleven minutes and write balanced, judged prose."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, describe the difference between Africa and Northern America in the percentage of people living in urban areas in 2018. [2 marks]","a":"AO4 = 2 marks. One mark for a basic comparison taken from the table (Northern America is more urbanised than Africa); a second mark for developing it with figures from the table (82% against 43%, a difference of 39 percentage points, almost double). No credit for describing other regions.","sol":"Northern America is far more urbanised than Africa. In 2018, 82% of Northern America's population lived in urban areas compared with only 43% of Africa's, a difference of 39 percentage points, so the urban share in Northern America was almost double that in Africa.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only two rows of the table are allowed here; the rest of the table is a distraction.","A difference has to be written as one comparison, not as two separate sentences about two places.","Think about what you can do with the two numbers themselves to develop a basic point into a marked one."],"codes":["3.2.1 — A growing percentage of the world’s population lives in urban areas"]},{"q":"State what is meant by the term megacity. [1 mark]","a":"AO1 = 1 mark. An urban area with a population of more than 10 million people. The figure only has to be stated; no explanation is required.","sol":"A megacity is an urban area with a total population of more than 10 million people.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The term is defined by size alone, not by wealth or by which country the city is in.","The UN uses one population threshold, counted in millions, to separate these from other cities.","Decide whether you mean the area inside the council boundary or the whole built-up area, because the definition uses the urban area."],"codes":["3.2.1 — A growing percentage of the world’s population lives in urban areas"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, calculate the increase in the percentage of the world's population living in urban areas between 1950 and 2018. [1 mark]","a":"AO4 = 1 mark. 25 percentage points (only possible answer), from 55 minus 30. Credit an indication of the calculation.","sol":"Figure 3 gives 30% for 1950 and 55% for 2018. 55 minus 30 = 25, so the increase is 25 percentage points.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Find the two years the question names on the horizontal axis and read straight up to the line.","Both readings are already percentages, so an increase here is found by taking one away from the other, not by dividing.","Take the earlier reading away from the later one, and write the answer as a number of percentage points; on a one-mark calculation the mark is for that figure."],"codes":["3.2.1 — A growing percentage of the world’s population lives in urban areas"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, describe two problems of living in the area shown in the photograph. [2 marks]","a":"AO3 = 1 mark, AO4 = 1 mark. One mark for each problem clearly observed in the photograph, eg crowded self-built housing on a steep cut slope (landslide risk); tangled unofficial electricity cables; loose refuse and rubble on the slope; stepped alleys too narrow for vehicles. No credit for problems that cannot be seen in the photograph.","sol":"The houses are self-built of unrendered brick and block and are packed together with no gaps on a steep slope that has been cut away below them, so the settlement is overcrowded and at risk of landslides in heavy rain (1). Bundles of electricity cables are strung untidily between the buildings and loose refuse and rubble lie on the bare earth below the lowest houses, so residents face the danger of unofficial power connections and uncollected waste (1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Everything written must be something the photograph actually shows.","Look at the ground the houses stand on and at the routes between them, not only at the buildings.","Two separate observations are wanted, and each needs a few words saying why it is a problem for the people living there."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (challenges)"]},{"q":"Explain one economic problem caused by traffic congestion in a city. [2 marks]","a":"AO1 = 1 mark, AO2 = 1 mark. One mark for stating an economic problem, a second for developing the explanation. Credit one problem only. No credit for environmental problems unless they are linked through to a cost.","sol":"Lorries delivering goods are held up in slow-moving traffic, which wastes fuel and drivers' paid time (1). That raises firms' delivery costs and makes deliveries unreliable, and those higher costs are passed on to customers in higher prices (2).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The problem you choose has to cost somebody money, directly or indirectly.","Pick a single problem and take it one step further rather than listing several undeveloped ones.","Follow your delay through the chain until you reach the person or business that ends up paying."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban sustainability requires management of resources and transport (transport)"]},{"q":"For a major city in an LIC or NEE you have studied, explain why the city is important and why it has grown rapidly. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Requires a named city, located by country, region and site. Credit importance developed at regional, national and international scale (administration, port and industry; capital status, media and culture; global landmarks, tourism and major events), and both causes of growth - rural-urban migration explained through named push and pull factors, and natural increase explained through the young age structure of migrants and a falling death rate. Importance without growth, or growth without importance, cannot reach Level 3.","sol":"Rio de Janeiro lies on the Atlantic coast of south-east Brazil, on the western shore of Guanabara Bay, with steep granite hills rising straight out of the built-up area. Its importance works at three scales. Regionally it is the commercial and administrative centre of its state, with a large port and Brazil's oil and gas industry; Petrobras, the state oil company, has its headquarters there. Nationally it was the capital of Brazil until 1960, when government moved to the purpose-built city of Brasília, and it remains a centre of media, culture and tourism and Brazil's second largest city after São Paulo, with about 6.2 million people in the municipality at the 2022 census. Internationally, Christ the Redeemer, Sugarloaf Mountain and the Copacabana and Ipanema beaches draw visitors from across the world, and the city hosted the final of the 2014 FIFA World Cup at the Maracanã and the 2016 Olympic and Paralympic Games. Its growth has two causes. Rural-urban migration, especially from the drought-prone Nordeste of north-east Brazil, was pushed by low and unreliable farm incomes and by a lack of schools and clinics, and pulled by wage work in construction, the port and tourism. Natural increase then carried the growth on from within, because the migrants were mostly young adults of childbearing age, so births in the city outnumbered deaths while improving health care lowered the death rate.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The question has two halves - why the city matters and why it became so big - and an answer that does only one of them is capped.","Importance is easiest to organise by working outwards through three scales, and growth by separating the people who arrived from the people who were born there.","Open by locating the city precisely - country, region and site - then take the first of those scales of importance."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (location, importance and growth)"]},{"q":"To what extent do urban areas in LICs and NEEs provide social and economic opportunities for people? [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Credit social opportunities (health care, education, water supply, energy) and economic opportunities (wage work in construction, industry, the port, tourism), each developed, then weighed against the mismatch between perceived and actual opportunity. The command phrase requires an appraisal of the degree, so a reasoned judgement is needed. No direct credit for environmental aspects unless they affect living standards.","sol":"Urban areas in LICs and NEEs do offer real opportunities. In Rio de Janeiro, city schools and the clinics and community health teams of Brazil's Family Health Strategy place education and basic health care within reach of favela families, which a remote rural district cannot do, so literacy rises and treatable illness is treated. Treated water from the Guandu works and mains electricity reach most of the built-up area, cutting water-borne disease and allowing refrigeration and study after dark. Economically, construction, the port, oil and gas, manufacturing and tourism pay regular wages far above what subsistence farming yields, so families can afford brick housing with piped water and can keep children in school, which raises earnings again in the next generation. To a large extent, therefore, the city does provide social and economic opportunity. The extent is limited by who receives it: much of the work is informal and insecure, rents and food cost more than in the countryside, and the poorest favelas still have overcrowded schools and thin services, so the opportunity migrants expect is not always the one they find. Overall the opportunities are substantial compared with rural life but unequally shared, so how far they exist depends on where in the city a household ends up.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The command phrase means the answer cannot simply be a list of good things about cities.","The specification splits these opportunities into two named types, and both are wanted here.","Plan two developed opportunities, then a paragraph that qualifies them, then one sentence of verdict."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (opportunities)"]},{"q":"Explain how urban planning is improving the quality of life for the urban poor in a city you have studied in an LIC or NEE. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Requires a named planning example and a chain of reasoning from what was done to how life improved. Credit upgrading rather than clearance, infrastructure (paved roads, water, sewerage, electricity, lighting), slope stabilisation, services and training, and legal title. Generic answers with no named example are capped low.","sol":"The Favela Bairro Project in Rio de Janeiro, begun in 1994 with funding that included a loan from the Inter-American Development Bank, improved favelas where they stood instead of clearing them, which mattered because residents kept the homes they had built and the jobs they could reach. Access roads were paved and widened, so refuse lorries, ambulances and the police could reach the middle of a settlement that had only stepped alleys before; that single change cuts uncollected waste, shortens the time to reach a hospital and makes the area easier to police. Mains water, sewerage and drainage were laid, which reduces the diarrhoeal disease that spreads where sewage runs in open channels, and legal electricity connections replaced dangerous unofficial ones. Steep slopes were stabilised and drained, lowering the risk of landslides during heavy summer rain. Health posts, day-care centres, sports areas and adult education and training were added, so parents could work while children were cared for and adults could gain skills for better-paid jobs. The project also worked towards giving residents legal title to their homes, and once a family is secure it invests in its own building. Together these changes raise health, safety, income and security of tenure, which is what quality of life means.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The question wants a named scheme in a named city, not planning in general.","Think about what the scheme decided not to do, as well as what it built.","For each thing installed, carry the answer through to the change it makes to a household's health, safety or income."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (challenges)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, suggest why the Harbourside area of Bristol needed regenerating. [4 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks. Level 2 (3-4) for clear interpretation of the extract: ships outgrew the tidal Avon, trade moved to Avonmouth and later Royal Portbury, the city docks closed to commercial traffic in 1975, dock jobs were lost and a large contaminated brownfield area of empty quaysides, sheds and sidings was left in the city centre. Level 1 (1-2) for basic interpretation. No credit for reasons that cannot be drawn from the extract.","sol":"The extract shows that the area lost its reason to exist. As cargo ships grew larger they could no longer be brought up the tidal Avon through the narrow gorge, so trade moved downstream to the deep-water docks at Avonmouth, and later to Royal Portbury Dock, and the city docks closed to commercial traffic in 1975. With the ships went the dock jobs, so employment in the area collapsed and few people had any reason to go there. What remained was a large brownfield site in the middle of the city: empty quaysides, disused transit sheds and warehouses, redundant cranes and railway sidings, some of the ground contaminated by its industrial past and the harbour water dirty. Derelict land of that size in a city centre is wasted space that lowers the appearance, safety and value of everything around it, so there was a strong case for bringing it back into use.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Everything credited has to come out of the extract, so read it before planning.","One physical change in the river and the ships sets off everything else in the extract.","Sort what is left behind into the jobs that went and the land that was abandoned, and treat each as a separate reason."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (challenges and regeneration)"]},{"q":"Explain how national and international migration have affected the growth and character of a major UK city you have studied. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Requires a named UK city and both kinds of migration, each carried through to an effect on growth (numbers, age structure) or character (culture, food, events, districts, workforce). Credit named districts and named communities.","sol":"In Bristol both kinds of migration have shaped the city. Internationally, Caribbean migration from the late 1940s onwards settled largely in inner areas such as St Paul's, where the St Paul's Carnival was first held in 1968; later Somali, Polish and other communities arrived, so the city now has a wide range of languages, places of worship, food shops and music. This changed character rather than size above all: it gave employers a workforce with varied languages and skills, and it made cultural events a reason for visitors to come. Nationally, Bristol's two universities and its aerospace, creative and financial employers pull in students and young graduates from across the UK, which keeps the age structure young and, because young adults have children, adds to growth through natural increase as well as through the migration itself. At the same time national migration works outwards: families priced out of the city, and people wanting more space, move to South Gloucestershire and North Somerset, so the built-up area spreads and commuter settlements grow while the inner city stays comparatively young and mobile. The combined effect is a city that grows steadily, has a young and culturally mixed population in its centre, and pushes suburban and commuter growth outwards into the surrounding counties.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two kinds of migration are named in the question and both must appear.","Growth and character are two different effects; make sure each gets attention.","Anchor each point in a named district, community or employer rather than writing about migrants in general."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (distribution, location and migration)"]},{"q":"Discuss the effects of urban sprawl on people and the environment. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Urban sprawl is the expansion of an urban area into the countryside. Expect a balanced discussion: negative effects (loss of farmland, habitat and open space, congestion and pollution on the rural-urban fringe, loss of trade in old centres, longer commuting) and positive effects (cheaper land, pleasant semi-rural living, edge-of-town sites with good road access). No credit for methods of controlling outward spread such as green belts or using brownfield sites.","sol":"Urban sprawl is the spread of a built-up area outwards into the countryside at its edge, and its effects run both ways. For people there are gains: land on the rural-urban fringe is cheaper, so housing is more affordable and gardens are larger, and families can live in quiet semi-rural surroundings while still reaching city jobs. Businesses gain too, because edge-of-town sites offer space and good road access; the growth of the Cribbs Causeway area north of Bristol, where The Mall opened in 1998, shows retailers taking exactly that advantage. The costs, however, fall on both people and the environment. Farmland, hedgerows and habitat are built over permanently, and open space that absorbed rainfall is replaced by tarmac and roofs, so surface run-off and flood risk increase downstream. Commuter settlements grow, which lengthens journeys and puts more traffic on fringe roads, raising congestion and air pollution in places that were previously quiet, and traditional city-centre shops lose trade to the edge-of-town sites. On balance, sprawl transfers benefits to those who can afford to move outwards while transferring the environmental costs to the countryside and the traffic costs to everyone, so the disadvantages are more widely shared than the advantages.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Discuss means both sides, so do not plan an answer made only of problems.","The question names people and the environment separately; treat them as separate strands.","Ways of stopping sprawl are outside this question, so keep green belts and brownfield policy out of the plan."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (challenges and regeneration)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, suggest why the percentage of people living in urban areas is lower in Africa and Asia than in Europe and Northern America. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks. Must use the table and apply understanding to it. Credit the timing of industrialisation in HICs, the large share of the workforce still in subsistence and small-scale farming in many African and Asian countries, and the point that a low percentage does not mean slow change, since these are the fastest urbanising continents. Answers that only restate the table are capped low.","sol":"Figure 1 shows Africa at 43% and Asia at 50% urban, both below the world figure of 55%, while Europe is at 74% and Northern America at 82%. The main reason is timing. Europe and Northern America industrialised first, from the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, when factories drew people off the land in huge numbers; that transfer finished long ago, so their urban percentages are high and now change only slowly. Much of Africa and parts of Asia industrialised far later, and a large share of the workforce is still in subsistence or small-scale farming, which keeps people distributed across the countryside; where farming is still done by hand, land supports many workers rather than few. Colonial economies also concentrated on exporting raw materials rather than on building manufacturing cities, which delayed the growth of large urban workforces. The low percentages are not a sign of stagnation, though. Because these are the continents where rural populations are still large, they are the fastest urbanising, and the UN expects most of the world's urban growth to 2050 to happen in Asia and Africa. So the table records how far each region has travelled along the same process rather than showing that some regions are not urbanising at all.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Quote the table first, because an answer that ignores the figure is capped.","Think about when each region's factories were built, not only about how rich it is now.","A low percentage and a fast rate of change are different things; make sure the answer separates them."],"codes":["3.2.1 — A growing percentage of the world’s population lives in urban areas"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of an urban transport scheme in reducing traffic congestion. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, plus 3 SPaG marks. Requires a specific named scheme and an evaluation of how far it reduced cars on the road, eased congestion or improved efficiency, with a balanced appreciation of advantages and disadvantages and a substantiated overall verdict. No credit for simply describing the problems of congestion.","sol":"Traffic congestion in London arises because very large numbers of people need to reach a small historic central area on a road network that cannot be widened, so the city manages demand instead of adding road space. Its best known scheme is the congestion charge, introduced on 17 February 2003, under which drivers pay a daily charge to drive within a central zone on weekdays; the charge began at £5 and has been raised several times since. Judged against its main aim, reducing the number of cars on the road, it worked quickly. Transport for London reported that in the first year traffic entering the zone fell by about a fifth and congestion within the zone by roughly 30%. The mechanism explains why. Putting a direct price on each car journey makes the least essential trips not worth making, so drivers switch to the Underground, buses, walking and cycling, and because the revenue is reinvested in public transport, mainly buses, the alternative improves and the switch is reinforced. The same principle has since been extended to emissions through the Ultra Low Emission Zone, which began in central London in April 2019 charging non-compliant vehicles £12.50 a day and now covers every London borough, while the public cycle-hire scheme that opened in 2010 and the Elizabeth line, opened in May 2022, have added capacity that makes leaving the car at home realistic. The scheme is less effective in other respects. A flat daily charge is regressive: it is trivial for a wealthy driver and heavy for a low-paid worker who must drive, so congestion is managed by ability to pay rather than by need. Some drivers re-routed around the boundary, which displaced congestion rather than removing it. Most importantly, the gain has eroded, because as private-hire vehicles and delivery vans multiplied, central London traffic speeds fell back, which shows that a charge fixed at one level is overtaken by changes in the kinds of traffic on the road. Overall the congestion charge has been effective, but partially and conditionally. It cut traffic sharply and immediately inside the zone and it funds the public transport that makes the reduction stick, yet it has not permanently solved congestion and it works only because London already had an alternative network dense enough for drivers to switch to. In a city without that alternative the same charge would achieve far less, so the scheme should be judged a successful component of a transport strategy rather than a cure on its own.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["One specific named scheme is worth more here than a survey of every strategy a city uses.","Evaluate means each paragraph should end in a verdict, and describing what congestion is like earns nothing.","Plan the balance first: what the scheme achieved, how it achieved it, where it falls short, and the condition on which its success depends."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban sustainability requires management of resources and transport (transport)"]},{"q":"To what extent has urban change in a major UK city you have studied created more opportunities than challenges? [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, plus 3 SPaG marks. Requires a named UK city and both sides: opportunities (cultural mix, recreation and entertainment, employment, integrated transport, urban greening) set against challenges (urban deprivation, inequalities in housing, education, health and employment, dereliction, brownfield and greenfield building, waste, sprawl and commuter growth). The command phrase requires an appraisal of the degree, so the conclusion must qualify rather than simply agree.","sol":"Bristol, a city of about 470,000 people on the River Avon in south-west England, has changed from a working port into a service, aerospace and creative economy, and that change has produced both opportunities and challenges. The opportunities are substantial. Employment shifted from the docks and heavy industry to aerospace at Filton, where Airbus and Rolls-Royce have sites, to finance, to two universities and to creative firms such as Aardman Animations and the BBC's Natural History Unit, and those are better-paid jobs than the ones they replaced. International migration, particularly Caribbean arrivals from the late 1940s who settled in inner areas such as St Paul's, and later Somali and Polish communities, has given the city a cultural mix visible in its food, music and events, including the St Paul's Carnival, first held in 1968. Recreation and entertainment have been concentrated on the regenerated Harbourside, where the Arnolfini occupies a former tea warehouse on Narrow Quay and the Watershed occupies former transit sheds on the harbourside, Millennium Square and the science centre now called We The Curious opened in 2000 and M Shed opened in 2011 in a former transit shed. Transport and greening have improved too: MetroBus opened in 2018, running partly on segregated busway and partly on ordinary roads, Bristol was designated England's first Cycling City in 2008, the through road across Queen Square was removed in the 1990s, and the city was named European Green Capital in 2015. The challenges, however, are not small, and they fall on different people. Bristol contains neighbourhoods among the most deprived tenth in England within a few kilometres of some of the least deprived, so inequalities in housing, education, health and employment persist even as the city's average prosperity rises. House prices and rents have risen faster than local wages, so the regenerated centre is not affordable for the people who lost the old dock and factory jobs. Growth outwards towards Cribbs Causeway, where The Mall opened in 1998, and into North Somerset has taken farmland and lengthened commuting, and traffic and air quality were poor enough for a Clean Air Zone to begin charging in November 2022. To a considerable extent, then, urban change has created more opportunities than challenges in total: the city has more jobs, more visitors and more life in its centre than when the docks closed. The qualification is that the opportunities and the challenges have been distributed to different groups, so for a graduate arriving to work in aerospace the opportunities clearly dominate, while for a family in a deprived neighbourhood facing high rents, the balance is much closer. The extent therefore depends less on the city as a whole than on who within it is being asked.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The case study has to be named and located in the first two sentences.","The specification lists the opportunities and the challenges under separate headings; use both lists to plan.","The command phrase means the conclusion must settle how far the claim holds, and for whom, rather than simply agreeing with it."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (opportunities)","3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (challenges and regeneration)"]},{"q":"Assess the effectiveness of urban planning in improving the quality of life for the urban poor in cities in LICs and NEEs. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, plus 3 SPaG marks. Requires a named planning example and an informed judgement on effectiveness, examining what the scheme delivered against what it could not reach. Credit upgrading in place, infrastructure, services, slope stabilisation and legal title; credit limits such as cost, maintenance, scale against continuing growth, and the persistence of insecure work and gang control.","sol":"Urban planning for the poor in LIC and NEE cities is usually judged against one test: does it change the daily conditions of life in the settlements where poor people already are? The Favela Bairro Project in Rio de Janeiro, begun in 1994 and part-funded by a loan from the Inter-American Development Bank, is a strong example because it chose upgrading rather than clearance, so residents kept the homes they had built and the jobs they could reach on foot. Its effectiveness is clearest in infrastructure. Access roads were paved and widened, which let refuse lorries, ambulances and police reach the interior of settlements previously served only by stepped alleys; mains water, sewerage and drainage replaced open channels, cutting the diarrhoeal disease that spreads where sewage runs in the street; legal electricity connections replaced dangerous unofficial ones; and steep slopes were stabilised and drained, which lowers the landslide risk that heavy summer rain brings to hillside favelas. Those are permanent, physical changes to health and safety, and across the programme's two phases, from 1994 to 2008, published counts put the number of favelas upgraded at about 140 to 160 and the Municipal Housing Secretariat puts the residents reached at some 500,000, so the scale was real rather than symbolic. Beyond infrastructure it added health posts, day-care centres, sports areas and adult training, so parents could work while children were cared for, and it worked towards legal title, which matters because a family with secure tenure invests in its own building. Its limits are equally clear. Upgrading is expensive, and funding a programme of this size depends on loans that a city cannot repeat indefinitely, so coverage was never complete while the city kept growing. Maintenance after construction was patchy, and infrastructure that is not maintained decays back towards its original state. Most importantly, the project changed the setting of poverty rather than its causes: it did not create secure, formal employment, it did not raise wages, and in some areas it did not displace the drug gangs whose control shaped daily life, which is why Pacifying Police Units were introduced separately from 2008 with contested results. The overall assessment, therefore, is that urban planning of this kind is effective but bounded. Judged on health, safety, access and dignity within the settlement it works well and its gains are measurable and lasting. Judged on whether it lifts households out of poverty, it cannot succeed alone, because income depends on the wider economy rather than on drainage. The most defensible verdict is that upgrading is a necessary first step that delivers real improvement in quality of life, but one that must be paired with employment, education and policing policy if the improvement is to be more than physical.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Assess means weighing the parts of the scheme and reaching an overall verdict on it, not describing it.","Separate what the scheme physically installed from what it was never designed to change.","The strongest conclusions say what the scheme is effective at and what it cannot be effective at, rather than giving it a single score."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (challenges)"]},{"q":"Assess the contribution that water conservation, energy conservation, waste recycling and green space make to sustainable urban living. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, plus 3 SPaG marks. Requires understanding of sustainable urban living and a judgement on the relative contribution of the four named features, supported by examples. Credit the interactions between them and the constraint that they cost money and require behaviour change, so their contribution varies with wealth and with the rate of urban growth.","sol":"Sustainable urban living means running a city so that it meets people's needs now without damaging the ability of future generations to meet theirs, and the four features named all contribute, though not equally. Water conservation contributes by cutting the volume that must be abstracted, treated and pumped, which saves both water and the energy used to move it. Metering, low-flush toilets and water-efficient appliances reduce household demand, rainwater harvesting substitutes untreated water for treated water in gardens and toilets, and repairing leaking mains recovers water already paid for, which in many older cities is the single largest saving available. Energy conservation contributes most directly to emissions. Insulation and better glazing cut heating demand at source, solar panels and district heating change where the remaining energy comes from, and street design that makes walking, cycling and public transport the easy choices cuts transport energy as well. The Vauban district of Freiburg in Germany, built from the late 1990s with parking kept to the edge of the neighbourhood and homes built to high insulation standards, shows how much further the saving goes when layout and building design are planned together rather than added afterwards. Waste recycling contributes by keeping material out of landfill, which reduces methane emissions and the land consumed by tipping, and by returning glass, metal and paper to industry, which uses far less energy than making them from raw materials. Its contribution depends on participation, however: kerbside collection of separated waste works only if households sort reliably, so recycling is the feature most dependent on behaviour rather than on engineering. Green space arguably contributes the most per pound spent, because it delivers several benefits at once. Parks, street trees, green roofs and allotments cool a city during a heatwave, absorb rainfall so drains are less likely to be overwhelmed, support wildlife and improve physical and mental health, and unlike the other three they improve the experience of living in the city rather than merely reducing a cost. Bristol, European Green Capital in 2015, has used greening in exactly this way, including the removal of the through road across Queen Square in the 1990s. Assessing them together, the four features are complementary rather than alternatives, and the largest contribution comes from the ones built into the fabric of the city, because insulation, permeable green space and a repaired water main keep working whether or not residents think about them, while recycling and household water saving depend on continuing effort. The decisive limit is cost and growth rate: these measures are affordable in wealthy, slow-growing cities and much harder in a city adding hundreds of thousands of residents a year, where basic sanitation must come first. Their contribution to sustainable urban living is therefore real and substantial, but it is greatest where a city can afford to design sustainability in from the start rather than retrofit it later.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Assess asks which of the four matters most and why, not for four descriptions in a row.","Look for the features that reinforce one another, because that is where the strongest analysis lies.","A judgement needs a condition attached to it: think about what kind of city could actually afford each measure."],"codes":["3.2.1 — Urban sustainability requires management of resources and transport (sustainable urban living)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the minimum population of a megacity?","a":"More than 10 million people."},{"q":"In which year did the world's urban population first exceed its rural population?","a":"2007."},{"q":"What is the largest favela in Rio de Janeiro called?","a":"Rocinha."},{"q":"In which year did Bristol's city docks close to commercial traffic?","a":"1975."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Urbanisation","back":"The increase in the proportion of a country's population living in urban areas. It happens when rural-urban migration and natural increase add people to towns and cities faster than to the countryside.","code":"3.2.1 — A growing percentage of the world’s population lives in urban areas","open":false},{"front":"Megacity","back":"An urban area with more than 10 million people. The UN counted 33 in 2018 and projects 43 by 2030. Tokyo is the largest urban agglomeration, with around 37 million.","code":"3.2.1 — A growing percentage of the world’s population lives in urban areas","open":false},{"front":"Give one push factor that drives rural-urban migration","back":"Drought or crop failure; low and unreliable farm incomes; few schools or clinics in rural areas; mechanisation of farming cutting the work available; conflict or natural disaster forcing people out.","code":"3.2.1 — A growing percentage of the world’s population lives in urban areas","open":true},{"front":"Natural increase","back":"Birth rate minus death rate. Migrants to cities are mostly young adults, so births outnumber deaths and a city keeps growing from within even if migration stops.","code":"3.2.1 — A growing percentage of the world’s population lives in urban areas","open":false},{"front":"Rio de Janeiro: location and importance","back":"On Brazil's Atlantic coast beside Guanabara Bay, south-east Brazil. About 6.2 million people (2022 census). Brazil's second city, a major port and oil centre, capital until 1960, host of the 2016 Olympics.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (location, importance and growth)","open":false},{"front":"Rocinha","back":"The largest favela in Rio de Janeiro, built on a steep slope above São Conrado in the South Zone. The 2010 census counted roughly 70,000 residents; local estimates are higher.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (challenges)","open":false},{"front":"The Favela Bairro Project","back":"Rio's favela upgrading scheme, begun in 1994 with Inter-American Development Bank funding: paved roads, mains water, sewerage, legal electricity, slope stabilisation, health posts, day care and training, improving favelas in place rather than clearing them.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (challenges)","open":false},{"front":"Social opportunities of urban growth in Rio de Janeiro","back":"City schools, and clinics and community health teams from Brazil's Family Health Strategy, reach poor districts. Treated water from the Guandu works and mains electricity serve most of the built-up area.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban growth creates opportunities and challenges for cities in LICs and NEEs (opportunities)","open":false},{"front":"Bristol: location and importance","back":"South-west England on the River Avon, about 190 km west of London near the Severn Estuary. About 470,000 people (2021 census). Aerospace at Filton, two universities, creative industries; European Green Capital 2015.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (distribution, location and migration)","open":false},{"front":"The Bristol Harbourside regeneration","back":"The city docks closed to commercial traffic in 1975, leaving derelict brownfield quaysides. Dockside buildings became the Arnolfini and the Watershed; Millennium Square and the science centre opened in 2000; M Shed opened in 2011.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (challenges and regeneration)","open":false},{"front":"Urban sprawl","back":"The spread of a built-up area outwards into the countryside at its edge. It takes farmland and habitat on the rural-urban fringe and creates commuter settlements, longer journeys and more congestion.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (challenges and regeneration)","open":false},{"front":"Brownfield and greenfield sites","back":"Brownfield land has been built on before, so it is usually central but must be cleared and sometimes decontaminated. Greenfield land has not been built on, so it is cheaper to develop but consumes countryside.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (challenges and regeneration)","open":false},{"front":"Urban greening","back":"Deliberately increasing vegetation and green space in a city through parks, street trees, green roofs and allotments. It cools the city, absorbs rainfall, supports wildlife and improves residents' health.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban change in cities in the UK leads to a variety of social, economic and environmental opportunities and challenges (opportunities)","open":false},{"front":"Name one way a city can conserve water","back":"Rainwater harvesting for gardens and toilets; low-flush toilets and water-efficient appliances; repairing leaking mains; metering so households see and reduce what they use.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban sustainability requires management of resources and transport (sustainable urban living)","open":true},{"front":"The London congestion charge","back":"Began on 17 February 2003 at £5 a day for driving in the central zone on weekdays. Transport for London reported traffic entering the zone fell by about a fifth in the first year.","code":"3.2.1 — Urban sustainability requires management of resources and transport (transport)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 (written for this room) — Table: the percentage of the population living in urban areas by region, 2018. Real data, rounded to the nearest whole per cent, from the United Nations World Urbanization Prospects, 2018 revision.\nRegion | Urban population (%)\nAfrica | 43\nAsia | 50\nOceania | 68\nEurope | 74\nLatin America and the Caribbean | 81\nNorthern America | 82\nWorld | 55","Figure 2 (written for this room) — Description of a photograph: a hillside favela in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The photograph looks up a steep hillside. Hundreds of small houses of unrendered brick and concrete block, two and three storeys high, are packed against one another with no gardens and almost no gaps between them; many have flat concrete roofs with steel reinforcing bars sticking up, ready for another storey to be added. Bundles of electricity and television cables are strung untidily between the buildings and run down the slope. The only routes up are narrow stepped alleys barely wide enough for one person, and no road reaches the middle of the settlement. Plastic water pipes run along the outside of the walls, and black water tanks and satellite dishes stand on many roofs, with washing hanging from the windows. Loose refuse and building rubble lie on the bare earth of the slope below the lowest houses, where the ground has been cut away. In the background, at the foot of the hill, stand tall modern apartment blocks and a dual carriageway.","Figure 3 (written for this room) — Line graph: the percentage of the world's population living in urban areas, 1950 to 2050. Real data and UN projections, rounded to the nearest whole per cent, from the United Nations World Urbanization Prospects, 2018 revision. Horizontal axis: year, from 1950 to 2050. Vertical axis: percentage of the world's population living in urban areas, scaled 0 to 80. Plotted values: 1950 = 30; 1970 = 37; 1990 = 43; 2007 = 50; 2018 = 55; 2030 = 60 (projected); 2050 = 68 (projected). The line rises throughout and crosses the 50 line in 2007, the first time in history that more of the world's people lived in urban than in rural areas. The section of the line after 2018 is dashed to show that it is a projection.","Figure 4 (written for this room) — Text extract: why Bristol's Harbourside needed regenerating. Written for this room in plain words from the documented history of the city docks; every fact in it is real. 'For centuries ships unloaded in the heart of Bristol, in the Floating Harbour beside the city centre. As cargo ships grew larger through the twentieth century they could no longer be brought up the tidal River Avon and through the narrow Avon Gorge, and trade moved downstream to the deep-water docks at Avonmouth, and later to Royal Portbury Dock, on the Severn Estuary. The city docks closed to commercial traffic in 1975. What was left in the middle of Bristol was a large area of brownfield land: empty quaysides, disused transit sheds and warehouses, redundant cranes and railway sidings, the dock jobs gone and little reason for anyone to go there. Some of the ground was contaminated by its industrial past, and the water of the harbour was dirty.'"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 2 Question 01.8, 9 marks: Evaluate the effectiveness of an urban transport scheme in reducing traffic congestion)","items":["Question: Evaluate the effectiveness of an urban transport scheme in reducing traffic congestion. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","Point: London's congestion charge has been highly effective at cutting the number of cars inside the charged area, but much less effective at holding congestion down over the long term.","Named evidence: the charge began on 17 February 2003 at £5 a day for driving within the central zone on weekdays, and Transport for London reported that in the first year traffic entering the zone fell by about a fifth and congestion within it by roughly 30%.","Developed explanation: the scheme works by pricing rather than by building. Attaching a direct cost to every car journey into the centre makes the least essential trips not worth making, so drivers transfer to the Underground, buses, walking and cycling; because the revenue is reinvested in public transport, mainly buses, the alternative itself improves, which reinforces the transfer instead of letting it decay.","Link to the question: judged against the scheme's own aim of reducing the number of cars on the road, the charge succeeded quickly and measurably, which is the core of any verdict on its effectiveness.","Mini-judgement: its effectiveness is nevertheless partial. A flat daily charge is regressive, falling hardest on lower-income drivers who must drive; some traffic simply re-routed around the zone boundary, displacing congestion rather than removing it; and central London traffic speeds later fell back as private-hire and delivery vehicles multiplied. The charge therefore slowed the problem rather than curing it, and it did so only because London already had a public transport network dense enough to absorb the drivers it displaced.","Why this reaches Level 3 (Detailed, 7-9): it satisfies the AO1 descriptor 'thorough and detailed knowledge of an urban transport scheme(s)' by naming the scheme, its location, its start date and its price; the AO2 descriptor 'a detailed and balanced appreciation of its advantages and disadvantages' by setting a measured fall in traffic against regressiveness, displacement and the later rebound; and the AO3 descriptor 'thorough application of knowledge and understanding in evaluating the effectiveness' by reaching a qualified verdict with a stated condition rather than describing congestion. Written in accurate prose with terms such as congestion charging, displacement and public transport used correctly, it would also reach High performance for SPaG."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: Evaluate the effectiveness of an urban transport scheme in reducing traffic congestion. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","Point: The congestion charge in London is good because it stops traffic.","Evidence offered: drivers have to pay money to drive into London, so lots of people do not drive there any more.","Explanation offered: this means there are fewer cars and the roads are less busy and the air is cleaner as well. Traffic in London is really bad and people get stuck in jams for ages, which is annoying and makes them late.","Judgement offered: so the congestion charge works well.","The descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic, 1-3), 'AO1 Demonstrates limited knowledge of an urban transport scheme(s)' — the scheme is named but there is no start date, no charge, no zone and no figure — and 'AO2 Shows limited understanding of the effectiveness of an urban transport scheme(s) by demonstrating limited appreciation of its advantages and disadvantages', since not a single disadvantage appears.","The descriptors it misses: it does not reach Level 2's 'reasonable knowledge' or 'some appreciation of its advantages and disadvantages', and it is nowhere near Level 3's 'detailed and balanced appreciation'. The mark scheme also states 'No credit for simply describing the problems', which is exactly what the sentence about being stuck in jams does, and the vague wording ('really bad', 'for ages') would hold SPaG at Threshold rather than High performance."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Rio de Janeiro: location, importance and causes of growth (LIC/NEE city case study)","items":["Rio de Janeiro stands on the Atlantic coast of south-east Brazil, on the western shore of Guanabara Bay, in the state of Rio de Janeiro; steep granite hills such as Corcovado and Sugarloaf rise directly out of the built-up area, squeezing the city onto narrow coastal strips and valley floors.","Brazil is a newly emerging economy (NEE) and about 87% of Brazilians live in urban areas.","Population: the 2022 Brazilian census recorded about 6.2 million people in the municipality of Rio de Janeiro; the wider metropolitan area holds roughly 12 million. Rio is Brazil's second largest city after São Paulo.","Regional importance: it is the commercial and administrative centre of its state, with a large port and Brazil's oil and gas industry; Petrobras, the state oil company, has its headquarters in the city.","National importance: Rio was the capital of Brazil until 1960, when government moved to the purpose-built city of Brasília. It remains a national centre for media, television, culture and tourism.","International importance: Christ the Redeemer on Corcovado, Sugarloaf Mountain and the Copacabana and Ipanema beaches draw visitors from across the world; Rio hosted the final of the 2014 FIFA World Cup at the Maracanã stadium and the 2016 Olympic and Paralympic Games.","Cause of growth 1, migration: rural-urban migration, especially from the drought-prone Nordeste of north-east Brazil, pushed by low and unreliable farm incomes, drought and a lack of schools and clinics, and pulled by wage work, services and the perception of a better life. International migration, historically from Portugal and from neighbouring South American countries, has added to it.","Cause of growth 2, natural increase: migrants are mostly young adults of childbearing age, so births in the city outnumber deaths, while improving health care has lowered the death rate. The city therefore keeps growing from within as well as from arrivals."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Rio de Janeiro: the opportunities and the challenges of urban growth","items":["Social opportunity, health: Brazil's Family Health Strategy, begun in the 1990s, places clinics and community health teams in poor urban districts, so vaccination, antenatal care and treatment reach families who would have none in a remote rural area.","Social opportunity, education: primary and secondary schools are within reach across the city, so children can complete more years of schooling than in remote rural districts, and adult training is available.","Social opportunity, resources: treated water from the Guandu works west of the city, one of the largest water treatment plants in the world, and mains electricity serve most of the built-up area, and connections have been extended into favelas.","Economic opportunity: construction, the port, oil and gas, manufacturing such as food, clothing and footwear, and tourism provide regular wages far above subsistence farming; those wages are spent locally, creating further jobs in shops, transport and services through the multiplier effect.","Challenge, slums and squatter settlements: a favela is an informal, largely self-built settlement, usually on steep hillsides or marshy ground. The 2010 Brazilian census (IBGE) counted about 22% of the municipality's population living in favelas and the 2022 census records more, so the share to quote is about a fifth to a quarter of Rio's residents, dated to the census. Rocinha, above São Conrado in the South Zone, is the largest; the 2010 census counted around 70,000 residents and local estimates are higher.","Challenge, water and sanitation: untreated sewage still reaches Guanabara Bay, which remains badly polluted; a pledge to clean up most of the bay's sewage before the 2016 Olympics was not met.","Challenge, energy: many favela households take electricity through unofficial connections, which is dangerous for residents and a loss to the supplier.","Challenge, services: schools and clinics are overcrowded, and narrow stepped alleys keep ambulances, fire engines and refuse lorries out of the interior of hillside favelas.","Challenge, unemployment and crime: much favela work is informal and insecure rather than formally unemployed; drug gangs controlled many favelas, which is why Pacifying Police Units were placed in selected favelas from 2008, with mixed and contested results and the programme later scaled back.","Challenge, environment: the Jardim Gramacho landfill in the metropolitan area, one of the largest open dumps in Latin America, closed in 2012; waste collection on steep slopes is difficult, and traffic congestion is severe because the terrain funnels movement onto a few coastal routes and tunnels.","Challenge, physical site: houses built on steep, unstable slopes are vulnerable to landslides during the heavy summer rains."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — The Favela Bairro Project (an example of urban planning improving quality of life for the urban poor)","items":["What it is: a favela upgrading programme in Rio de Janeiro, launched by the city authorities in 1994, part-funded by a loan from the Inter-American Development Bank (IDB).","The key decision: upgrading in place rather than clearance and rehousing, so residents keep the homes they built and the jobs they can reach on foot.","Feature, access: roads paved and widened so that refuse lorries, ambulances, fire engines and police can reach the interior of settlements that previously had only stepped alleys.","Feature, water and sanitation: mains water, sewerage and surface drainage installed, replacing open channels and cutting the spread of diarrhoeal disease.","Feature, energy and safety: legal electricity connections and street lighting installed, replacing dangerous unofficial connections and making streets safer at night.","Feature, site safety: steep slopes stabilised and drained to reduce the landslide risk in heavy summer rain.","Feature, services: health posts, day-care centres, sports and public spaces, and adult education and training added, so parents can work while children are cared for and adults can gain skills.","Feature, tenure: the project worked towards giving residents legal title to their homes, which encourages families to invest in improving their own buildings.","Scale: across the two phases (1994-2000 and 2000-2008), funded by Inter-American Development Bank loans from 1994, published counts put the number of favelas upgraded at about 140 to 160, and the city's Municipal Housing Secretariat puts the number of residents reached at some 500,000.","Limits an evaluation must use: the cost per favela was high and depended on borrowing; maintenance after construction was patchy; coverage never caught up with continuing growth; and the scheme changed the setting of poverty rather than its causes, since it created no secure formal jobs and did not by itself remove gang control."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Bristol: UK population distribution, the city's location, importance and migration","items":["UK distribution: population is densest in London and the South East, the West Midlands around Birmingham, the North West around Manchester and Liverpool, West Yorkshire around Leeds, the North East around Newcastle, and the Central Lowlands of Scotland around Glasgow and Edinburgh. It is sparsest in the Scottish Highlands, mid-Wales, the Pennines and the Lake District, where relief, climate and distance from work keep numbers low.","London had about 8.8 million people at the 2021 census, by far the largest UK city.","Bristol's location: south-west England, on the River Avon, about 190 km (120 miles) west of London and close to where the Avon reaches the Severn Estuary. The M4 and M5 motorways meet just north of the city and Bristol Temple Meads is a main-line station.","Population: about 470,000 at the 2021 census, the largest city in the south-west of England; the surrounding built-up area and commuter belt are larger still.","Historic importance: a port from the Middle Ages, which grew rich in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries on Atlantic trade, including the transatlantic slave trade, and on tobacco, sugar and chocolate.","Modern importance: aerospace at Filton to the north of the city, where Airbus and Rolls-Royce have sites; financial and legal services; two universities, the University of Bristol and the University of the West of England; and a creative sector that includes Aardman Animations and the BBC's Natural History Unit.","The port today: commercial shipping moved downstream to Avonmouth and Royal Portbury Dock on the Severn Estuary, where large vessels can dock; the city docks closed to commercial traffic in 1975.","International standing: Bristol Airport lies south-west of the city at Lulsgate, and Bristol was named European Green Capital in 2015, the first UK city to hold the title.","International migration: Caribbean migration from the late 1940s onwards settled largely in inner areas such as St Paul's, where the St Paul's Carnival was first held in 1968; later arrivals include Somali, Polish and other communities, so many languages are spoken in the city's schools.","National migration: the universities and the aerospace, creative and financial employers draw students and young graduates in from across the UK, keeping the age structure young; at the same time families and commuters move out to South Gloucestershire, North Somerset and Bath, spreading the built-up area."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Bristol: the opportunities and the challenges of urban change","items":["Opportunity, cultural mix: Caribbean, Somali, Polish and other communities have shaped the city's food, music, places of worship and events, including the St Paul's Carnival, and give employers a workforce with a wide range of languages and skills.","Opportunity, recreation and entertainment: the regenerated Harbourside carries the Arnolfini and Watershed arts centres, the M Shed museum, the science centre that opened in 2000 and is now called We The Curious, and Brunel's SS Great Britain, returned to Bristol in 1970 and restored in the dry dock where she was built. The Bristol International Balloon Fiesta is held each summer at Ashton Court.","Opportunity, employment: the economy has shifted from docks and heavy industry to aerospace, creative industries, finance and higher education, which pay better but require qualifications.","Opportunity, integrated transport: MetroBus, opened in 2018, links park-and-ride sites with the centre and with suburban rail, but only part of its route is segregated busway and substantial lengths run on ordinary roads and shared bus lanes, which is the standard criticism of the scheme; Bristol was designated England's first Cycling City in 2008.","Opportunity, urban greening: parks, street trees and waterside green space; the through road that had been driven across Queen Square was removed in the 1990s and the square restored; the city was European Green Capital in 2015.","Challenge, urban deprivation and inequality: Bristol contains neighbourhoods among the most deprived tenth in England and others among the least deprived, sometimes only a few kilometres apart, with matching differences in health, exam results and employment.","Challenge, housing: house prices and rents have risen faster than local wages, so affordable housing is short and some of the people who lost dock and factory work cannot afford the regenerated centre.","Challenge, dereliction and land: old docks, factories and railway land were left derelict. Brownfield sites must be cleared and sometimes decontaminated, which is expensive; greenfield sites on the edge are cheaper but take countryside.","Challenge, urban sprawl and commuter settlements: growth towards Cribbs Causeway in South Gloucestershire, where The Mall opened in 1998, and into North Somerset has taken farmland, lengthened journeys and grown commuter settlements around the city.","Challenge, traffic and air quality: congestion on the M32 and the radial routes into the centre, and air quality poor enough that a Clean Air Zone began charging older, more polluting vehicles in Bristol in November 2022."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — The Bristol Harbourside regeneration (an example of an urban regeneration project)","items":["Reason for regeneration 1: as cargo ships grew larger through the twentieth century they could no longer be brought up the tidal River Avon and through the narrow Avon Gorge, so trade moved downstream to the deep-water docks at Avonmouth, and later to Royal Portbury Dock.","Reason for regeneration 2: the city docks closed to commercial traffic in 1975, and the dock and dock-related jobs went with them, so employment in the area collapsed.","Reason for regeneration 3: that left a large brownfield area in the middle of the city — empty quaysides, disused transit sheds and warehouses, redundant cranes and railway sidings — with some ground contaminated by its industrial past and dirty water in the Floating Harbour.","Feature 1: the Floating Harbour was retained as water and reused for leisure, with waterside walkways, moorings, ferries and events instead of cargo.","Feature 2: dockside buildings were converted for arts and media rather than demolished — the Arnolfini arts centre in a former tea warehouse on Narrow Quay, and the Watershed media centre in converted dockside sheds from the early 1980s.","Feature 3: Millennium Square and the science centre that opened in 2000 and is now called We The Curious were built with Millennium Commission lottery funding, alongside an aquarium, cafes and restaurants.","Feature 4: M Shed, a museum of Bristol, opened in 2011 in a former dockside transit shed, keeping the quayside cranes, the dockside railway and historic boats working as exhibits.","Feature 5: new flats, offices, hotels, bars and restaurants were built on vacant quayside land, bringing residents back to live in the city centre.","Feature 6: the SS Great Britain, Brunel's iron steamship built in Bristol, was returned to the city in 1970 and restored in the dry dock in which she was built; she is now one of the city's main visitor attractions.","The judgement an examiner rewards: the project turned derelict, contaminated brownfield land into jobs, visitors and city-centre living, and it reused buildings rather than wasting them — but the new flats are expensive, so the benefits have not been shared evenly with the deprived neighbourhoods elsewhere in the city."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — The processes and terms of this room","items":["Urbanisation: the increase in the proportion of a population living in urban areas. Urban growth is different: it is the increase in the number of urban dwellers.","The global pattern: more than half the world's people have lived in urban areas since 2007; about 55% were urban in 2018 and the UN projects roughly 68% by 2050.","HIC trend: countries such as the UK urbanised early, during the Industrial Revolution. About 84% of the UK population is urban, urban growth is slow, and counter-urbanisation and suburbanisation move people out of the largest cities to commuter settlements.","LIC and NEE trend: Africa and Asia are the least urbanised continents but are urbanising fastest, so most of the world's urban growth between now and 2050 will occur there.","Megacity: an urban area of more than 10 million people. The UN counted 33 in 2018 and projects 43 by 2030; Tokyo, at around 37 million, is the largest urban agglomeration, and most megacities are now in LICs and NEEs.","Push factors (rural): drought and crop failure, low and unreliable farm incomes, mechanisation of farming, few schools or clinics, poor services, natural disaster and conflict.","Pull factors (urban): more and better-paid work, schools and universities, hospitals and clinics, electricity and piped water, entertainment, and the perception of a better quality of life.","Natural increase: birth rate minus death rate. Migrants are mostly young adults, so births exceed deaths and the city grows from within even without further migration.","Terms for the UK city: urban deprivation (several disadvantages concentrated in one place), dereliction (land or buildings left unused and decaying), brownfield (previously built on), greenfield (not previously built on), urban sprawl, rural-urban fringe, commuter settlement, urban greening.","Sustainable urban living: meeting the needs of people now without damaging the ability of future generations to meet theirs, through water conservation (rainwater harvesting, low-flush fittings, metering, repairing leaking mains), energy conservation (insulation, solar panels, district heating, walkable street design), waste recycling (kerbside collection of separated waste, composting, energy recovery) and creating green space (parks, street trees, green roofs, allotments).","Urban transport strategies: congestion charging, improving public transport, park and ride, bus lanes, cycle lanes and public cycle hire, pedestrianisation, ring roads, car sharing, staggered working hours, and low-emission zones."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — London's transport management (the named scheme for the transport question)","items":["The congestion charge began on 17 February 2003: drivers pay a daily charge to drive within a central London zone on weekdays. The charge started at £5 a day and has been raised several times since.","Effect: Transport for London reported that in the first year traffic entering the zone fell by about a fifth and congestion within the zone by roughly 30%.","How the money is used: revenue is reinvested in London's public transport, mainly bus services, which improves the alternative that displaced drivers switch to.","Public cycle hire launched in July 2010, with docking stations across central London, giving short journeys an alternative to both car and Underground.","The Ultra Low Emission Zone (ULEZ) began in central London in April 2019, charging older, more polluting vehicles £12.50 a day; it expanded to the area within the North and South Circular roads in October 2021 and to all London boroughs in August 2023.","The Elizabeth line, a new high-capacity railway running across London from east to west, opened in May 2022 and greatly increased central rail capacity.","Trams returned to south London with Tramlink, which opened in 2000 and is centred on Croydon.","Limitations an evaluation must use: a flat daily charge is regressive, bearing hardest on low-income drivers who have no alternative; some traffic re-routed around the zone boundary, displacing rather than removing congestion; central London traffic speeds later fell back as private-hire vehicles and delivery vans increased; and the ULEZ expansion was politically contested.","Other named schemes worth knowing for comparison: the trams of Manchester, park and ride at Oxford, pedestrianisation at Exeter and Oxford, ring roads such as Watford's, and Bristol's MetroBus busways, opened in 2018."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — To what extent has urban change in a major UK city you have studied created more opportunities than challenges? [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","items":["The question as the board would set it: To what extent has urban change in a major UK city you have studied created more opportunities than challenges? [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks] — AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, marked on Levels 1 to 3 (Basic 1-3, Clear 4-6, Detailed 7-9) with 3 further marks for SPaG.","Decoding the command phrase: 'to what extent' does not ask whether opportunities exist; it asks how far the claim that they outweigh the challenges holds. The answer must therefore argue both sides, state a position and qualify it. A list of good things followed by a list of bad things, with no verdict, cannot reach Level 3.","Timing: about a minute a mark plus time for the SPaG marks, so roughly ten to eleven minutes — two of those minutes on a plan, because the balance has to be designed before the first sentence.","Opening (two sentences): name and locate the case study, and signal the argument. Bristol, about 470,000 people (2021 census), on the River Avon in south-west England, changed from a working port into a service, aerospace and creative economy.","Paragraph 1 — economic and social opportunities. Example: employment shifting to aerospace at Filton (Airbus, Rolls-Royce), finance, two universities and creative firms such as Aardman Animations and the BBC's Natural History Unit; cultural mix from Caribbean migration to St Paul's from the late 1940s, with the St Paul's Carnival first held in 1968. Mini-judgement: better-paid work and a richer culture, but the new jobs demand qualifications.","Paragraph 2 — environmental opportunities and transport. Example: the Harbourside regeneration (the Arnolfini in a former tea warehouse and the Watershed in former transit sheds, Millennium Square and the science centre in 2000, M Shed in 2011), MetroBus busways from 2018, Cycling City designation in 2008, the removal of the road across Queen Square in the 1990s, European Green Capital in 2015. Mini-judgement: the city centre is a far better place to be than when the docks closed.","Paragraph 3 — social and economic challenges. Example: neighbourhoods among the most deprived tenth in England within a few kilometres of some of the least deprived; house prices and rents rising faster than local wages. Mini-judgement: the gains have gone disproportionately to the qualified and the already housed.","Paragraph 4 — environmental challenges and sprawl. Example: dereliction left by the docks closing to commercial traffic in 1975; the cost of clearing and decontaminating brownfield land against the countryside lost to greenfield building; sprawl towards Cribbs Causeway, where The Mall opened in 1998, and into North Somerset; the Clean Air Zone charging from November 2022. Mini-judgement: the costs of change are exported to the fringe and to the air everyone breathes.","The evaluation thread that must run through every paragraph: not how much changed, but who received the opportunity and who received the challenge. Carry that single question from paragraph to paragraph so the conclusion is already argued for before it is written.","What the conclusion must say: take a position and qualify it. In total the opportunities outweigh the challenges — the city has more jobs, more visitors and more life in its centre than when the docks closed — but they are distributed unequally, so for a graduate arriving to work in aerospace the opportunities clearly dominate, while for a family in a deprived neighbourhood facing high rents the balance is much closer. The extent therefore depends on who within the city is being asked. A conclusion that merely repeats the paragraphs cannot lift the answer into Level 3.","SPaG: three marks ride on accurate spelling and punctuation, controlled grammar and a wide range of specialist terms, so use and spell terms such as regeneration, brownfield, deprivation, counter-urbanisation, urban greening and rural-urban fringe correctly, and write in full paragraphs rather than bullet points."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T09:52:01Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.87,"notes":"Factually this is a clean kit — the UN 2018 urbanisation table and line graph reproduce the UN's own published roundings exactly, and the Rio 2022 census, Rocinha 2010 census, Bristol 2021 census, congestion charge 18%/30% first-year figures, ULEZ, Harbourside and Favela Bairro details all check out — so the only work needed before publishing is the MetroBus 'segregated' overstatement, the Watershed/warehouse slip, and a question on Rio's location, importance and causes of growth, which the room currently teaches but never tests."}}},"3.2.2":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.2.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the biggest human topic on Paper 2 and the one that carries the most named material: a whole case study of an LIC or NEE, a worked tourism example, a sustainable industry example, two contrasting rural areas and the UK's own economic story. The paper tests it three ways at once — reading development data off a map, graph or table, explaining why the gap between countries exists and how strategies try to close it, and then a nine-mark question that wants a judgement backed by a named country. Almost every mark here is lost for the same two reasons: an answer that stays generic when the question asked for a case study, and an answer that lists effects instead of weighing them. Learn Nigeria, Jamaica and the UK in figures and you can answer anything this topic asks.","idea":[{"h":"Classifying and measuring development","code":"3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (classifying and measuring development)","t":"Development means the standard of living and quality of life of a country's people, and it is measured in two families of indicator. Economic measures use money: gross national income (GNI) per head is the total value of what a country produces plus income earned abroad, divided by its population, usually in US dollars so countries can be compared. Social measures use people: birth rate, death rate, infant mortality (deaths under one year per 1,000 live births), life expectancy, people per doctor, literacy rate and access to safe water. The Human Development Index combines a health strand (life expectancy at birth), an education strand (mean and expected years of schooling) and an income strand (GNI per head) into one score between 0 and 1. Countries are then grouped as LICs, NEEs and HICs. A top-level answer never just names an indicator: it says what the indicator measures, quotes a figure from the resource and explains what that figure implies about daily life."},{"h":"Why one measure misleads, and how the DTM fits","code":"3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (limitations and the DTM)","t":"Every single measure has a blind spot. An average hides inequality: a state with a high GNI per head can still have most of its income held by a tiny minority, so the average tells you nothing about the majority. Economic measures ignore health, schooling and rights; social measures ignore wealth; data may be estimated, out of date or miss the informal economy altogether. That is the argument for a composite such as HDI. The Demographic Transition Model gives the same story as a sequence: stage 1 high birth and death rates; stage 2 death rate falls steeply as water, food and medicine improve while birth rate stays high, so population grows fast; stage 3 birth rate falls as women are educated and child mortality drops; stage 4 both rates low; stage 5 birth rate below death rate and population declines. Most LICs sit in stage 2 or 3 and the UK sits in stage 4 or 5, so the model is a rough map of development. A top-level answer uses the model to explain why an indicator changes, rather than describing the graph."},{"h":"Causes and consequences of uneven development","code":"3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (causes and consequences of uneven development)","t":"Uneven development has physical, economic and historical causes. Physical: a landlocked position raises the cost of trade, unreliable rainfall and drought wreck harvests, tropical disease such as malaria cuts working days, and a country may simply have few raw materials or, worse, one very valuable one. Economic: depending on a single low-value primary export means income swings with a world price the country does not set, while manufactured imports rise steadily in price. Historical: colonial rule built railways to ports rather than between regions and left economies shaped around exporting raw materials, and conflict since independence has destroyed what development there was. The consequences are disparities in wealth and health — income, life expectancy and infant mortality differ sharply between neighbouring countries — and international migration, as people move towards work, safety and services. A top-level answer links a named cause to a named consequence rather than listing both sides."},{"h":"The strategies used to close the development gap","code":"3.2.2 — Various strategies exist for reducing the global development gap (overview of strategies)","t":"The specification names seven: investment (foreign direct investment by firms and governments, which brings capital a poor country cannot raise itself); industrial development and tourism (using a resource or a landscape to earn foreign currency and create a multiplier); aid (money, goods or expertise given by governments, international organisations or charities); intermediate technology (small, cheap equipment local people can build, run and repair, such as a rope pump or a treadle irrigation pump); fairtrade (a guaranteed minimum price plus a premium paid to a farmers' co-operative); debt relief (cancelling repayments so money can go to schools and clinics instead); and microfinance loans (very small loans to people banks will not serve, often women starting a business). Each has a standard criticism: aid can be tied or wasted, microfinance rarely lifts a whole region, tourism leaks profits abroad. A top-level answer pairs every strategy with both its mechanism and its weakness before judging it."},{"h":"Tourism as a strategy: Jamaica","code":"3.2.2 — Various strategies exist for reducing the global development gap (tourism example)","t":"Jamaica shows how one strategy works in practice. Tourism is the island's largest earner of foreign exchange: more than 4 million visitors came in 2019 and spent over US$3 billion, and on the World Travel and Tourism Council's total measure, which counts indirect and induced jobs in transport, construction, farming and craft, the industry supported at least a quarter of all jobs before the pandemic. Visitor spending is foreign currency, which pays for imports and for government borrowing; a levy on every arriving passenger funds the Tourism Enhancement Fund, which pays for training, beach and heritage projects and infrastructure residents also use. The multiplier spreads wages into shops, farms and building sites. The limits are equally examinable: work is seasonal, peaking between December and April; many resorts are all-inclusive, so guests spend little outside the gates; foreign-owned airlines, cruise lines and hotel chains take payment abroad, so money leaks out; and reefs, beaches and water supplies come under pressure. A top-level answer quotes the figures and then weighs the leakage against the earnings."},{"h":"Nigeria: where it is and why it matters","code":"3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (location and context)","t":"Nigeria lies in West Africa on the Gulf of Guinea, bordered by Benin to the west, Niger to the north, Chad to the north-east and Cameroon to the east. It covers about 924,000 km², nearly four times the area of the UK, and has over 200 million people — the largest population in Africa. The capital is Abuja, built inland and made the capital in 1991; Lagos, on the coast, is the largest city and one of the fastest-growing in the world. Nigeria became independent from Britain on 1 October 1960, fought a civil war from 1967 to 1970, and is a member of the Commonwealth, OPEC, ECOWAS and the African Union; in 2014 it overtook South Africa to become Africa's largest economy. Its context is plural: over 500 languages, a mainly Muslim north and mainly Christian south, and a Boko Haram insurgency in the north-east since 2009. A top-level answer uses this context to explain why development is uneven inside the country, not just between countries."},{"h":"Nigeria's changing industrial structure and its TNCs","code":"3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (industrial structure and TNCs)","t":"Nigeria's economy is shifting out of agriculture. Farming still occupies roughly a third of workers, but manufacturing and services now produce most output: food processing, cement, brewing, telecommunications, banking and Nollywood, one of the world's largest film industries by number of films made. Oil dominates trade rather than jobs — oil and gas earn about 90% of export income but well under a tenth of GDP. Manufacturing stimulates development because it adds value inside the country, pays higher wages than subsistence farming, needs roads and power that others can then use, and produces a multiplier as wages are spent. Transnational corporations drive much of this: Shell has extracted oil since the first commercial discovery at Oloibiri in 1956; Unilever has manufactured in Nigeria for about a century; Guinness opened a brewery in Lagos in 1963, its first outside the British Isles. A top-level answer names the TNC and the place, then weighs jobs, skills and infrastructure against exported profit, low wages and pollution."},{"h":"Nigeria's trade, aid, environment and quality of life","code":"3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (trade, aid, environment and quality of life)","t":"Nigeria's trading relationships have turned outwards and eastwards: exports are still dominated by crude oil sold to Europe, India and other Asian buyers, while China has become the biggest source of imports and the main financier of new infrastructure — the Abuja to Kaduna railway opened in 2016, the Lagos to Ibadan line in 2021 and the Lekki deep-sea port in 2023, all Chinese-built. Aid comes as emergency relief, long-term development aid and the work of non-governmental organisations; the campaign against polio, backed by the World Health Organization and the Gates Foundation, ended wild poliovirus in Nigeria, and the WHO African Region was certified free of it in August 2020. The environmental cost is heavy: pipeline spills and gas flaring in the Niger Delta, and a United Nations Environment Programme report on Ogoniland in 2011 that asked for a US$1 billion clean-up fund and warned restoration could take 25 to 30 years. Quality of life is rising but unevenly: HDI is about 0.55 and life expectancy about 55 years. A top-level answer sets a gain against a cost in the same paragraph."},{"h":"Why the UK economy changed, and what replaced industry","code":"3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (causes of change and the post-industrial economy)","t":"Three forces reshaped the UK economy. De-industrialisation: heavy industry built on coal, iron, steel, shipbuilding and textiles lost its markets as machinery replaced workers and reserves ran out; manufacturing employment fell from about 6.7 million in the late 1970s to under 3 million today, and the last deep coal mine, Kellingley in North Yorkshire, closed in December 2015. Globalisation: goods could be made more cheaply abroad, and firms moved production to countries with lower wages while importing the result. Government policy: from the 1980s the state stopped subsidising loss-making industries, privatised others and encouraged services and foreign investment instead. What grew in their place is a post-industrial economy — services, finance, research, information technology and the quaternary sector now provide roughly four fifths of UK jobs. Science and business parks are its landscape: Cambridge Science Park, opened by Trinity College, Cambridge in 1970, was the UK's first. A top-level answer explains the mechanism of each cause rather than naming it."},{"h":"Industry, the environment and the changing countryside","code":"3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (environment and the rural landscape)","t":"Industry damages the physical environment: quarrying scars landscapes, factories and power stations emit greenhouse gases and acidic pollutants, waste and warm water are discharged into rivers, and land, roads and airports are taken from farmland and habitat. Modern development can be less damaging. Nissan's Sunderland car plant, opened in 1986 and employing around 6,000 people, generates part of its own electricity from on-site wind turbines and a large solar farm, built the all-electric Leaf, and has a battery factory alongside it. Rural areas are changing in opposite directions. In South Cambridgeshire, an area of population growth, commuter villages are expanding and a new town, Northstowe, is being built on the former RAF Oakington airfield: more houses, more traffic, higher prices, but services survive. In the Outer Hebrides, an area of decline, young people leave for work and study, so the population has fallen from around 46,000 a century ago to roughly 26,000, leaving an ageing population, closing schools and shrinking bus and ferry services. A top-level answer contrasts the two areas directly rather than describing each in turn."},{"h":"Infrastructure, the north-south divide and the UK's links","code":"3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (infrastructure, the north–south divide and the UK in the wider world)","t":"The UK is rebuilding its links. Rail: the Elizabeth line opened across London in May 2022, HS2 is under construction between London and Birmingham, and the Transpennine Route Upgrade is electrifying the line between Manchester, Leeds and York — though the HS2 leg to Manchester was cancelled in October 2023. Ports and airports: Felixstowe is the UK's largest container port and Heathrow its busiest airport, handling around 80 million passengers a year. The north-south divide is the gap in incomes, output per head, health and house prices between London and the south east and the north of England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland; London's output per head is far above every other region and healthy life expectancy is lower in the north east than in the south east. Strategies to close it include devolution to elected metro mayors, the Northern Powerhouse announced in 2014, enterprise zones, moving public bodies north, and the Levelling Up White Paper of February 2022. The UK's wider links run through trade, culture, transport and electronic communication: it left the European Union on 31 January 2020 and remains in the Commonwealth's 56 member states. A top-level answer judges whether a strategy has actually narrowed the gap, using evidence."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading the development table: a two-mark comparison","setup":"Figure 1, the development indicators table written for this room, gives four countries. Norway: GNI per head about US$95,000, life expectancy about 83 years, HDI about 0.97. United Kingdom: about US$50,000, about 81 years, HDI about 0.94. Bangladesh: about US$2,800, about 74 years, HDI about 0.67. Nigeria: about US$2,000, about 55 years, HDI about 0.55. The question is: Using Figure 1, compare the level of development of Bangladesh and Nigeria. [2 marks]","steps":["Hold both countries in one sentence instead of describing them one after the other: Bangladesh is ahead of Nigeria on every indicator printed.","Earn the second mark by quoting figures and the gap between them: an HDI of about 0.67 against about 0.55, a difference of roughly 0.12.","Back the comparison with a second indicator rather than trying to rank gaps measured in different units: life expectancy is about 74 years in Bangladesh against about 55 in Nigeria, roughly nineteen years higher.","Add no reasons at all: the command word is compare, so an explanation of why Nigeria lags earns nothing on this question."],"cues":["Which country is ahead here, and how should the sentence be worded?","What turns a bare statement into a developed comparison?","Which further indicator could you quote to widen the comparison?","The command word is compare, so what must you leave out?"]},{"title":"Using one indicator to show differences in quality of life","setup":"Figure 1 gives life expectancy at birth of about 83 years in Norway, about 81 in the United Kingdom, about 74 in Bangladesh and about 55 in Nigeria, alongside GNI per head and HDI for the same four countries. The question is: Using Figure 1, explain how one indicator of development shows differences in quality of life between the countries shown. [4 marks] The mark scheme gives no additional credit for a second indicator.","steps":["Pick a single indicator and define what it actually counts: life expectancy is the average number of years a baby born today can expect to live.","Quote both ends of the range from the figure so the answer is anchored in the resource: about eighty-three years in Norway, about fifty-five in Nigeria.","Explain the mechanism behind a high figure: clean piped water, vaccination, enough doctors per person and a reliable diet keep people alive into old age.","Turn that back into quality of life: where the figure is low, families lose children and earners young, illness costs working days, and schooling and income suffer with it.","Refuse the temptation to bring in HDI as well, because a second indicator adds nothing and steals the words the first one needed."],"cues":["Which single indicator can you explain in most depth?","Which two values from the figure frame the whole range?","What has to be true of a country for that figure to be high?","How does that connect back to the words quality of life?","What does the mark scheme say about offering a second indicator?"]},{"title":"Calculating a fall and describing a trend from the graph","setup":"Figure 3, written for this room, plots employment in UK manufacturing in millions of jobs against the year. The plotted values are 1978, 6.7 million; 1990, 4.9 million; 2000, 4.0 million; 2010, 2.6 million; 2023, 2.6 million. Two questions follow. (a) Using Figure 3, calculate the fall in manufacturing employment between 1978 and 2023. [2 marks] (b) Using Figure 3, describe the trend shown. [2 marks]","steps":["Take the two years the question names and set up a subtraction: 6.7 million minus 2.6 million.","Write the working down as well as the result, because one mark is for the calculation and one for the evidence of method: 6.7 - 2.6 = 4.1, so a fall of 4.1 million jobs.","For part (b), give the direction of the trend first in one clause: manufacturing employment falls steeply over most of the period and then levels off.","Develop it with the rate of change, which is what separates a second mark from a first: the steepest fall comes between 1978 and 1990, and after 2010 the line is flat, with no further loss.","Stop there and give no reasons, because describe asks only for what the plot shows."],"cues":["Which two of the five plotted points does the question actually need?","Why is writing the sum down worth a mark on its own?","What is the overall direction of the line, and does it hold right to the end?","Where on the line is the change fastest, and where does it level off?","Why would a sentence about cheap imports score nothing here?"]},{"title":"Building a six-mark answer on tourism in Jamaica","setup":"Figure 4, the extract written for this room, states that tourism is Jamaica's largest earner of foreign exchange; that the island received more than 4 million visitors in 2019 and earned over US$3 billion from them; that most guests stay in all-inclusive resorts at Montego Bay, Ocho Rios and Negril; that a levy on every arriving passenger funds a Tourism Enhancement Fund spent on training, beach and heritage projects and infrastructure; that the World Travel and Tourism Council's total measure puts at least a quarter of all jobs in the tourism chain; and that the peak season runs from December to April while foreign-owned hotels, airlines and cruise lines are paid abroad. The question is: Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, explain how the growth of tourism helps to reduce the development gap in an LIC or NEE. [6 marks]","steps":["Open with the foreign currency, because closing a gap is about income a country can spend outside its own borders: visitor spending buys imports and services debt.","Chain that to employment: hotels, airports, taxis, farms and building sites hire local workers, and their wages are spent again in shops and markets, spreading the gain through a multiplier.","Bring in the government's share and say what it buys: the passenger levy feeds the Tourism Enhancement Fund, which pays for training and for infrastructure residents use as well as visitors.","Show the development indicators moving, since that is the point of the question: better water, roads and schooling raise HDI, not only GNI per head.","Close with the honest limit that lifts an answer into the top level: seasonal work and profits taken abroad by foreign-owned chains mean the gap narrows more slowly than the arrivals figure suggests."],"cues":["Why does foreign currency matter more than ordinary spending here?","Who else gains when a hotel worker is paid?","How does the government get a share of the visitors' money?","Which measures of development would you expect to move, and why?","What stops all of the money reaching Jamaican households?"]},{"title":"Planning the nine-mark judgement on TNCs","setup":"The question is: 'The advantages of transnational corporations to the countries in which they operate outweigh the disadvantages.' Do you agree with this statement? Use a named example in your answer. Justify your decision. [9 marks] You have about ten minutes. The case study you have learned is Shell in Nigeria, where oil was first found in commercial quantities at Oloibiri in 1956 and where oil and gas now earn about 90% of national export income.","steps":["Decode the command word before writing a word of geography: justify means take a side and defend it, so sentence one states a position rather than a definition.","Fix the named example and keep it there: Shell in the Niger Delta, because an answer about transnational corporations in general is capped at the bottom level.","Draft paragraph one, the advantages, developed rather than listed: export revenue funds the federal budget, the industry trains engineers, and the roads, power and ports built for oil serve other businesses too.","Draft paragraph two, the disadvantages, with the same named detail: repeated pipeline spills and gas flaring have poisoned farmland and fishing grounds in Ogoniland, and most profit returns to shareholders abroad.","Weigh the two rather than balancing them: say which side matters more and for whom, noting that the gains reach the national budget while the damage falls on Delta villagers.","Write a conclusion that answers the statement in its own words and qualifies it, because repeating both sides without deciding keeps the answer out of the top level."],"cues":["What does the command word oblige your first sentence to do?","Why is a general answer about transnational corporations self-limiting?","Which advantages can you tie to this company and this country?","Which disadvantages can you tie to a named place in the Delta?","Who gains and who loses, and does that change the verdict?","What must the conclusion add that the two paragraphs have not?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which three strands does the Human Development Index combine?","a":"Life expectancy at birth, education (mean and expected years of schooling) and gross national income per head."},{"q":"Why can a high GNI per head give a misleading picture of a country?","a":"It is an average, so it hides inequality: a small, very wealthy minority can lift the figure while most people remain poor. It also says nothing about health or education."},{"q":"At which stage of the Demographic Transition Model does the death rate fall steeply while the birth rate stays high?","a":"Stage 2, which is where many LICs sit; population therefore grows fast."},{"q":"Name one advantage and one disadvantage of Shell's presence in Nigeria.","a":"Advantage: oil and gas earn about 90% of Nigeria's export income, funding government spending and infrastructure. Disadvantage: spills and gas flaring have polluted farmland and fishing grounds in the Niger Delta."},{"q":"What is a post-industrial economy, and roughly what share of UK jobs is now in services?","a":"An economy in which services, finance, research and information technology, rather than manufacturing, produce most output and jobs; services provide roughly four fifths of UK employment."}]},"room":{"facts":["GNI per head is a country's total output plus income earned abroad, divided by its population; HDI combines life expectancy at birth, education and GNI per head into a score between 0 and 1.","The Demographic Transition Model: stage 1 high birth and death rates; stage 2 death rate falls steeply; stage 3 birth rate falls; stage 4 both low; stage 5 birth rate below death rate. Most LICs are in stage 2 or 3, the UK in stage 4 or 5.","Nigeria: over 200 million people, the largest population in Africa; capital Abuja, largest city Lagos; independent from Britain on 1 October 1960; oil and gas earn about 90% of export income; HDI about 0.55 and life expectancy about 55 years.","Jamaica: more than 4 million visitors in 2019 earning over US$3 billion; tourism is the island's largest earner of foreign exchange and, on the World Travel and Tourism Council's total measure, supported at least a quarter of all jobs before the pandemic.","The UK: manufacturing employment fell from about 6.7 million in the late 1970s to under 3 million today; services now provide roughly four fifths of UK jobs; the last deep coal mine, Kellingley, closed in December 2015.","Cambridge Science Park, opened by Trinity College, Cambridge in 1970, was the UK's first science park; the UK left the European Union on 31 January 2020 and remains one of the Commonwealth's 56 member states."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, compare GNI per head in the United Kingdom with GNI per head in Nigeria. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a comparative statement using data from the figure; a second mark for developing it with the difference or the ratio. AO4 = 2 marks. No credit for describing the two countries in separate sentences.","sol":"Figure 1 gives GNI per head of about US$50,000 in the United Kingdom against about US$2,000 in Nigeria, so the UK figure is roughly twenty-five times the Nigerian one, a difference of about US$48,000 for every person. Put the other way round, the average Nigerian's share of national income is worth about four pence in every pound of the average Briton's. The comparison has to be made inside one sentence: a sentence about the UK followed by a separate sentence about Nigeria is a description and loses the second mark.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two countries you need sit at opposite ends of the same column of Figure 1.","A comparison holds both values in one sentence and states how they relate.","Quote each value with its unit, then add the relationship between them, as a difference or as a number of times."],"codes":["3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (classifying and measuring development)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, calculate the fall in employment in UK manufacturing between 1978 and 2023. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer, 4.1 million (allow 4.0 to 4.2 million); one mark for indication of the calculation, eg 6.7 - 2.6. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Read the two plotted values Figure 3 gives for the years the question names: about 6.7 million jobs in 1978 and about 2.6 million in 2023. Subtract the later value from the earlier one: 6.7 - 2.6 = 4.1. The answer is a fall of about 4.1 million jobs, and the unit matters, so write 'million jobs' and not simply '4.1'. Writing the subtraction down earns the method mark even if the arithmetic slips, so never hand in a bare number.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only two of the five plotted points are named in the question.","A fall is found by subtracting one reading from another, not by reading a single point.","Put the earlier value first, take the later value away from it, and attach the unit to your answer."],"codes":["3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (causes of change and the post-industrial economy)"]},{"q":"Give two social measures of development. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for any two of: birth rate; death rate; infant mortality rate; life expectancy; people per doctor; literacy rate; access to safe water. AO1 = 2 marks. No credit for GNI per head, which is an economic measure.","sol":"Two acceptable answers are the infant mortality rate, the number of babies who die before their first birthday for every 1,000 live births, and the literacy rate, the percentage of adults who can read and write. The others the specification names are birth rate, death rate, life expectancy, people per doctor and access to safe water. Social measures describe how people live rather than how much a country earns, which is why gross national income per head does not count here; the Human Development Index is a composite of both strands, so it is not a safe answer either.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Split the specification's list of indicators into money measures and people measures.","Think about what a clinic, a classroom or a tap would tell you about a country.","Two names are enough at this tariff; no explanation is required."],"codes":["3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (classifying and measuring development)"]},{"q":"Explain one limitation of using a single measure of development. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for stating a limitation, a second for developing it with a consequence or an example. AO2 = 2 marks. Credit one reason only; no credit for a second.","sol":"A single measure is an average for a whole country, so it hides the inequality inside it (1). An oil-rich state can record a high gross national income per head while most of that income is held by a very small and very wealthy minority, so the figure says almost nothing about how the majority actually live (2). The same objection applies to economic measures generally: they ignore health, schooling and rights. That is why the Human Development Index combines life expectancy, education and income, and why this question rewards one limitation taken two steps rather than a list of three mentioned once each.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Ask yourself what an average for a whole country cannot possibly show.","One limitation developed fully beats three named; choose the one you can take two steps.","State the limitation, then show its consequence using a country where the figure misleads."],"codes":["3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (limitations and the DTM)"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why science parks have grown in the UK. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for two reasons, eg they support new and growing businesses through research and new ideas; demand for new high-technology products drives research; universities with strong research departments attract them. AO1 = 2 marks. No credit for vague statements such as 'skilled labour supply' or 'close to universities' on their own.","sol":"First, science parks support new and growing firms by putting laboratories, research and investors on one site, so a start-up can develop a product without building facilities it could never afford alone (1). Second, demand for new products such as smartphones, vaccines and software means companies must keep researching, and that research builds directly on work done in university departments, which is why parks cluster beside strong research universities such as Cambridge (1). A bare phrase like 'close to universities' is not credited: the mark scheme wants the reason the link matters, which is the flow of research, graduates and spin-out companies from the university into the park.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a young technology firm cannot afford to build for itself.","Two reasons, each a complete idea rather than a phrase.","Tie each reason either to research or to the products the market now demands."],"codes":["3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (causes of change and the post-industrial economy)"]},{"q":"Explain one way in which fairtrade helps to reduce the development gap. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for stating a way, eg fairtrade guarantees farmers a minimum price; a second mark for developing it, eg so families can meet basic needs or the co-operative can invest the premium. AO2 = 2 marks. Credit one way only; no credit for a second.","sol":"Fairtrade guarantees the farmers in a certified co-operative a minimum price for their crop, which does not fall when the world market price falls (1). Because their income is predictable, families can pay for food, school fees and medicines instead of borrowing in a bad year, and the separate Fairtrade premium paid on top of the price is spent by the co-operative on shared projects such as a well, a classroom or a lorry (2). That is how the strategy narrows the gap: it raises and steadies income at the bottom of the supply chain instead of adding to the profits of the exporter. Only one way is credited, so a second is wasted writing.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what changes for a coffee or cocoa farmer on the day the co-operative is certified.","One way, taken two steps: what fairtrade does, then what that lets the family or the village do.","Name the guaranteed minimum price or the premium, then follow the money to what it buys."],"codes":["3.2.2 — Various strategies exist for reducing the global development gap (overview of strategies)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, explain the advantages of the location of Cambridge Science Park. [4 marks]","a":"Level 2 (Clear) 3-4: clear application of locational factors to analyse the map, with thorough use of map evidence. Level 1 (Basic) 1-2: limited application and basic map use. AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks. No credit for generic factors that cannot be read from Figure 2.","sol":"Figure 2 shows the park pressed against the A14 dual carriageway on the northern edge of Cambridge, with the Milton interchange a little over a kilometre to the north-east, where the A10 leaves it and runs north, and the M11 ending at the same road about five kilometres west. That puts the site on the national motorway and trunk road network, so components and visitors can reach it quickly and London, roughly a hundred kilometres to the south, is within an easy drive. Second, Cambridge North station is drawn about a kilometre south of the park, with trains to London King's Cross in about an hour, and the guided busway runs past it, so staff can commute without a car. Third, the residential estates of Arbury, King's Hedges and Chesterton lie immediately west and south, within the walking and cycling distance the cycle paths on the map are drawn for, giving the firms a labour force on the doorstep. Finally, the university's colleges and science departments are marked about four kilometres south in the city centre, close enough for shared research, while the landscaped grounds and lakes on the site itself make it an attractive place to work.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Everything you credit has to be visible on Figure 2 itself.","Work outwards from the site: roads, then rail and bus, then the people and the university.","Take each feature you name one step further by saying what it lets the firms on the park do."],"codes":["3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (causes of change and the post-industrial economy)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, explain how one indicator of development shows differences in quality of life between the countries shown. [4 marks]","a":"Level 2 (Clear) 3-4: sound understanding of one relevant measure and sound application in interpreting how it shows differences in quality of life. Level 1 (Basic) 1-2: limited understanding and limited application. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. No additional credit for considering more than one indicator.","sol":"Life expectancy at birth is the average number of years a baby born now can expect to live, and Figure 1 shows it ranging from about 83 years in Norway down to about 55 years in Nigeria. A figure at the Norwegian end means clean piped water, vaccination, enough doctors for the population and a reliable diet, because those are the conditions under which people survive into old age; quality of life is therefore high, and adults live long enough to work, save and educate their children. A figure at the Nigerian end means the opposite: infectious disease, unsafe water and too few doctors kill people young, families lose earners and children, and time and money that could go into schooling go into illness instead. Bangladesh is the instructive middle case, at about 74 years: its GNI per head of about US$2,800 is about 40% above Nigeria's US$2,000, yet its people live around nineteen years longer, a far wider gap than that income difference by itself would predict, which shows that spending on health, sanitation and girls' education lifts quality of life faster than income alone. One indicator, developed like this, scores everything available.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Choose the single indicator you can explain most deeply, and stay with it.","Define what the indicator counts before you interpret any number.","Quote the top and bottom of the range, then say what daily life must be like to produce each figure."],"codes":["3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (classifying and measuring development)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, explain how the growth of tourism in an LIC or NEE helps to reduce the development gap. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed) 5-6: detailed knowledge of the example, thorough understanding of the link between tourism and development, and full use of the resource. Level 2 (Clear) 3-4: clear knowledge and clear understanding with some use of the resource. Level 1 (Basic) 1-2: generic or undeveloped. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Tourism closes a development gap mainly by earning foreign currency. Figure 4 records more than 4 million visitors to Jamaica in 2019 and over US$3 billion spent, and because that money arrives in dollars it can pay for imported machinery, fuel and medicines and service the national debt. It also creates work: hotels, Sangster International Airport, taxis, farms supplying the kitchens and builders putting up new rooms all hire local people, and Figure 4 puts at least a quarter of the island's jobs in that chain on the World Travel and Tourism Council's total measure. Those wages are spent again in shops, schools and markets, so a multiplier spreads the income well beyond the resorts. The government takes its own share through the levy charged on every arriving passenger, which feeds the Tourism Enhancement Fund; that money goes into training, beach and heritage projects and infrastructure that residents use as well as visitors. The effect on the indicators is what matters: better water supply, roads and schooling raise the Human Development Index rather than just GNI per head. The narrowing is real but slower than the arrivals figure suggests, because all-inclusive resorts keep spending inside the gates and foreign-owned airlines, cruise lines and hotel chains are paid abroad.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Start with the kind of money tourism earns and why that kind is especially useful.","Follow the money in three stages: households, businesses around them, then the government.","End by saying which measures of development move, and name one reason they move slowly."],"codes":["3.2.2 — Various strategies exist for reducing the global development gap (tourism example)","3.2.2 — Various strategies exist for reducing the global development gap (overview of strategies)"]},{"q":"Explain how the Demographic Transition Model is linked to a country's level of development. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed) 5-6: detailed knowledge of the stages and thorough understanding of why birth and death rates change with development. Level 2 (Clear) 3-4: clear knowledge with some explanation. Level 1 (Basic) 1-2: describes the model without linking it to development. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"The model tracks birth rate, death rate and total population through five stages, and each move between stages is driven by something that also counts as development. In stage 1 both rates are high and population is stable because disease, famine and poor water kill as fast as births replace them. In stage 2 the death rate falls steeply while the birth rate stays high, so population grows fast; the cause is development in its most basic form, clean water, better food supply, vaccination and sanitation. In stage 3 the birth rate falls too, because child mortality has dropped so families no longer need many children as security, women are educated and enter paid work, and contraception becomes available. Stage 4 has low birth and death rates and a large, ageing population, and stage 5 has a birth rate below the death rate, so population declines. Level of development therefore maps onto position in the model: many LICs sit in stage 2 or 3, NEEs move through stage 3, and HICs such as the UK sit in stage 4 or 5. The link is not exact, which is why it is a model: war, migration and government policy can move a country off the curve.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The model is a sequence of causes, not a picture of two lines.","For each stage, ask what has to improve in a country for the next change to happen.","Finish by placing LICs, NEEs and HICs on the model, then note one reason the fit is imperfect."],"codes":["3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (limitations and the DTM)"]},{"q":"Explain how physical and historical factors have caused uneven development. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed) 5-6: detailed knowledge of named factors and thorough understanding of the chain from cause to unequal development. Level 2 (Clear) 3-4: clear knowledge with some development. Level 1 (Basic) 1-2: a list of factors with little explanation. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Physical factors set the starting conditions. A landlocked country must pay a neighbour to move its exports to a port, so the same goods cost more and earn less, and investment goes elsewhere. Unreliable rainfall means that in a drought year subsistence farmers lose the harvest, sell livestock and take children out of school, so one bad season sets development back years. Tropical disease such as malaria removes working days and kills children, cutting output and pushing the birth rate up. A shortage of raw materials leaves nothing to trade; ironically an abundance of one, like Nigeria's oil, can also distort an economy so that agriculture and manufacturing are neglected. Historical factors then lock those conditions in. Colonial rule built railways from mines and plantations to ports rather than between regions, so transport still serves exports rather than internal trade, and it left economies organised around selling raw materials whose prices are set abroad and fluctuate wildly. Independence was often followed by conflict, which destroys infrastructure and deters investment; the Nigerian civil war of 1967 to 1970 is one example. The two sets of causes compound: a physically difficult country with a colonial export economy finds it hardest of all to catch up.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Take physical and historical causes as two separate paragraphs, not one list.","Every factor needs a chain: the condition, what it does to trade or health, then what that does to development.","Name a real country for at least one factor in each paragraph."],"codes":["3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (causes and consequences of uneven development)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, suggest why some regions of the UK were affected more than others by the change the graph shows. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed) 5-6: thorough application of knowledge to analyse the resource, with a logical chain of reasoning about regional difference. Level 2 (Clear) 3-4: clear application with some development. Level 1 (Basic) 1-2: generic or describes the graph only. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Figure 3 shows manufacturing employment falling from about 6.7 million in 1978 to about 2.6 million in 2023, with the steepest loss in the 1980s and no further fall after 2010. Those jobs were not spread evenly across the UK. Heavy industry had located where the coal, iron ore and deep-water estuaries were: the north east, south Wales, central Scotland, Yorkshire and Lancashire. When steelworks, shipyards, collieries and textile mills closed, whole towns lost their single largest employer at once, and because those towns had grown around one industry there was nothing to absorb the workers. Skills were specific and hard to transfer, terraced housing near the works was cheap and hard to sell, and unemployed men could not easily move south to where work was appearing. The jobs that replaced manufacturing, in finance, research, media and business services, chose different places: London and the south east, with international airports, an educated workforce and existing financial institutions, plus science-park clusters like Cambridge. Foreign investors making the same calculation reinforced it. The result is that the fall in Figure 3 produced long-term unemployment, derelict land and out-migration in the old industrial regions while the south east grew, which is the origin of today's north-south divide.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The graph gives you the scale of the loss; your own knowledge has to supply the geography.","Ask where the jobs on that line actually were, and why they were there rather than anywhere else.","Then ask where the replacement jobs went, and why those places and not the old industrial towns."],"codes":["3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (causes of change and the post-industrial economy)","3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (infrastructure, the north–south divide and the UK in the wider world)"]},{"q":"'The advantages of transnational corporations to the countries in which they operate outweigh the disadvantages.' Do you agree with this statement? Use a named example in your answer. Justify your decision. [9 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed) 7-9: comprehensive and specific knowledge of one or more named TNCs; thorough understanding of advantages and disadvantages for host countries; a substantiated conclusion with detailed and balanced justification. Level 2 (Clear) 4-6: reasonable knowledge, clear understanding, a supported conclusion. Level 1 (Basic) 1-3: largely generic, justification limited to simple points. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. No credit for impacts on the source country.","sol":"Transnational corporations bring real gains to their host countries, but in Nigeria those gains have been captured unevenly, so I agree with the statement only in part.\n\nShell has extracted oil in Nigeria since the first commercial discovery at Oloibiri in 1956, and the industry it leads now earns roughly 90% of the country's export income. That money matters. It funds the federal budget and helped pay for the building of the new capital at Abuja, and the roads, power supplies and port capacity built for the oil industry are used by other businesses as well. TNCs also employ and train Nigerians as engineers, drivers and contractors on wages far above subsistence farming, and those wages circulate locally in a multiplier that supports traders, transport and housing. Manufacturing TNCs make the case more cleanly still: Unilever has produced in Nigeria for about a century and Guinness has brewed in Lagos since 1963, and both add value inside the country rather than shipping a raw material out of it.\n\nAgainst that, the costs of the oil industry fall on people who see little of the revenue. Pipeline leaks, ageing equipment, sabotage and gas flaring have repeatedly contaminated farmland, creeks and fishing grounds in the Niger Delta. A United Nations Environment Programme study of Ogoniland published in 2011 recommended an initial US$1 billion clean-up fund and warned that restoration could take 25 to 30 years, and in 2015 Shell agreed a settlement of about £55 million with the Bodo community over two spills in 2008. The number of jobs is also small set against a population of over 200 million, most profit returns to shareholders abroad, and an enclave industry tied to one volatile world price has drawn investment away from agriculture.\n\nWeighing the two, the advantages are national and financial while the disadvantages are local and environmental, which is why the statement is only half true. Where a TNC manufactures inside the host country, as Unilever does, the balance clearly tips towards advantage. Where it extracts a raw material for export, as Shell does, the host keeps the pollution and exports the resource. My judgement is that TNCs bring more advantages than disadvantages only where the host government is strong enough to enforce environmental law and tax profits properly, and in the Niger Delta it has not been.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The command word decides the shape: you are being asked to come down on one side.","Choose one company and one country and refuse to leave them, because generic answers are capped low.","Plan two developed paragraphs and a conclusion that says which side matters more, and for whom."],"codes":["3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (industrial structure and TNCs)","3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (location and context)"]},{"q":"'Rapid economic development in an LIC or NEE improves quality of life for most of the population.' To what extent do you agree? Use a case study of one LIC or NEE in your answer. [9 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed) 7-9: comprehensive knowledge of the case study; thorough understanding of the links between economic development, environment and quality of life; a substantiated judgement about the extent of improvement. Level 2 (Clear) 4-6: reasonable knowledge and a supported judgement. Level 1 (Basic) 1-3: generic, with a weak judgement. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Nigeria has developed fast, overtaking South Africa in 2014 to become Africa's largest economy, and quality of life has risen with it, but the improvement is far from evenly shared, so I agree only to a limited extent.\n\nThe gains are real. The industrial structure has shifted away from subsistence agriculture towards manufacturing and services: food processing, cement, brewing, telecommunications, banking and Nollywood, one of the world's largest film industries by number of films made. Manufacturing jobs pay more than farming and produce a multiplier, since wages are spent in local shops and on housing. Growth has funded infrastructure, much of it Chinese-financed: the Abuja to Kaduna railway opened in 2016, the Lagos to Ibadan line in 2021 and the Lekki deep-sea port in 2023. Aid has worked alongside it, most visibly in health, where the campaign backed by the World Health Organization and the Gates Foundation ended wild poliovirus transmission, and the WHO African Region was certified free of it in August 2020.\n\nThe qualifications are equally strong. Nigeria's HDI is still only about 0.55 and life expectancy about 55 years, roughly twenty-five years below the UK's, so the average has moved slowly. Oil and gas earn about 90% of export income but employ very few people, so growth has been concentrated rather than shared, and it collapses whenever the world oil price does. The environmental cost falls on the poorest: repeated pipeline spills and gas flaring have poisoned farmland and fishing grounds in the Niger Delta, and the 2011 United Nations Environment Programme report on Ogoniland asked for a US$1 billion clean-up fund and warned that restoration could take 25 to 30 years, so the people losing their livelihoods are not the ones gaining the revenue. Development is also regionally uneven: Lagos and the south have grown while the north east has suffered the Boko Haram insurgency since 2009.\n\nTo what extent, then? Economic development has clearly improved quality of life for a growing urban middle class and has bought health and transport improvements that reach much further. But because the growth is built on an export that employs few people and damages the land others live from, the phrase 'most of the population' overstates it. My judgement is that quality of life has improved for many Nigerians and will improve for more as manufacturing and services grow, but that it will only reach most of them if the benefits of oil are spread and the Delta is cleaned up.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["'To what extent' asks how far, not whether: plan a verdict with a degree in it.","Balance a paragraph of gains against a paragraph of costs, both anchored in the same country.","Use the indicators themselves, not just examples, to measure how far quality of life has actually moved."],"codes":["3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (trade, aid, environment and quality of life)","3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (location and context)"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of strategies used to reduce regional differences in the UK. [9 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed) 7-9: detailed and balanced appreciation of the advantages and disadvantages of named strategies, with a substantiated overall evaluation. Level 2 (Clear) 4-6: clear knowledge of strategies with a reasonable judgement. Level 1 (Basic) 1-3: describes the north-south divide rather than evaluating strategies. AO1 = 3 marks, AO2 = 3 marks, AO3 = 3 marks. No credit for simply describing the problems.","sol":"The north-south divide is the gap in incomes, output per head, health and house prices between London and the south east and the rest of the UK, and governments have attacked it for decades with mixed results.\n\nSome strategies have plainly worked in places. Attracting foreign investment has rebuilt parts of the old industrial map: Nissan's car plant at Sunderland, opened in 1986, employs around 6,000 people and supports thousands more in its supply chain, and it has become a centre for electric vehicles and battery manufacture. Moving public institutions north has had a similar local effect, from the BBC's departments at MediaCityUK in Salford to the Treasury campus opened in Darlington. Devolution has given northern city regions their own budgets and elected metro mayors, with Greater Manchester and the Liverpool City Region electing their first in 2017, so transport and skills decisions are taken locally. The Northern Powerhouse, announced in 2014, and the Levelling Up White Paper of February 2022 put the problem at the centre of national policy, and enterprise zones have offered tax relief and simpler planning on derelict industrial land.\n\nThe weaknesses are equally clear. The effects are local rather than regional: a car plant transforms Sunderland but not County Durham. Transport investment, which matters most for connecting northern cities to one another, has been the least reliable, and the cancellation of the HS2 leg to Manchester in October 2023 removed the single largest promised improvement, while the Transpennine Route Upgrade continues slowly. Funding has often been short-term and competitive, so councils bid against each other for small pots rather than planning for decades, and the money involved is trivial beside London's continuing advantage: its output per head remains far above every other region and its house prices roughly double the UK average. Some strategies also attract branch plants that can close again, leaving the same dependence in a new form.\n\nOverall, the strategies have slowed the divide rather than closed it. They have been effective where they combined investment with real devolved power over a long period, as in Greater Manchester, and ineffective where they were announced as a slogan and funded in short bursts. My judgement is that regional difference in the UK will only narrow when transport between northern cities and skills funding are treated as a permanent programme rather than a political cycle.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Evaluate means judge how well they work, so the answer cannot be a description of the divide.","Group the strategies, then judge each group on whether it moved a whole region or only one town.","Finish with a verdict that says which kind of strategy works and why the others do not."],"codes":["3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (infrastructure, the north–south divide and the UK in the wider world)","3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (environment and the rural landscape)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which three strands are combined in the Human Development Index?","a":"Life expectancy at birth, education (mean and expected years of schooling) and gross national income per head."},{"q":"At which stage of the Demographic Transition Model does the death rate fall steeply while the birth rate stays high?","a":"Stage 2."},{"q":"Which transnational corporation has extracted oil in the Niger Delta since the first commercial discovery at Oloibiri in 1956?","a":"Shell."},{"q":"Roughly what share of UK employment is now in services?","a":"About four fifths, that is roughly 80%."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Gross national income (GNI) per head","back":"The total value of the goods and services a country produces in a year plus income earned abroad, divided by its population. Usually quoted in US dollars so countries can be compared.","code":"3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (classifying and measuring development)","open":false},{"front":"Human Development Index (HDI)","back":"A composite measure scored between 0 and 1 combining life expectancy at birth, education (mean and expected years of schooling) and gross national income per head, so it reflects social as well as economic development.","code":"3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (classifying and measuring development)","open":false},{"front":"Two limitations of using a single measure of development","back":"An average hides inequality inside a country, as where a wealthy minority lifts GNI per head; economic measures ignore health and education; data may be estimated, out of date or miss the informal economy.","code":"3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (limitations and the DTM)","open":true},{"front":"The five stages of the Demographic Transition Model","back":"1 high birth and death rates; 2 death rate falls steeply; 3 birth rate falls; 4 both low; 5 birth rate below death rate. Most LICs sit in stage 2 or 3, the UK in stage 4 or 5.","code":"3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (limitations and the DTM)","open":false},{"front":"Three causes of uneven development","back":"Physical: landlocked position, drought, tropical disease, few raw materials; economic: dependence on one low-value primary export whose price is set abroad; historical: colonial economies built around exporting raw materials, and later conflict.","code":"3.2.2 — There are global variations in economic development and quality of life (causes and consequences of uneven development)","open":true},{"front":"Intermediate technology","back":"Small-scale, low-cost equipment that local people can build, run and repair with local materials and skills. A rope pump on a hand-dug well or a treadle irrigation pump are typical.","code":"3.2.2 — Various strategies exist for reducing the global development gap (overview of strategies)","open":false},{"front":"Fairtrade","back":"A scheme guaranteeing farmers in a certified co-operative a minimum price for their crop, plus a premium paid on top which the co-operative spends on shared projects such as a well, a classroom or a lorry.","code":"3.2.2 — Various strategies exist for reducing the global development gap (overview of strategies)","open":false},{"front":"Tourism in Jamaica: the key figures","back":"More than 4 million visitors in 2019 and over US$3 billion earned. Tourism is the island's largest earner of foreign exchange and, on World Travel and Tourism Council estimates, supported at least a quarter of all jobs before the pandemic.","code":"3.2.2 — Various strategies exist for reducing the global development gap (tourism example)","open":false},{"front":"Nigeria: location and importance","back":"West Africa, on the Gulf of Guinea, bordering Benin, Niger, Chad and Cameroon. Over 200 million people, Africa's largest population; capital Abuja, largest city Lagos; in 2014 it became Africa's largest economy.","code":"3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (location and context)","open":false},{"front":"Two advantages of a TNC for its host country","back":"Jobs and training that raise skills and wages; tax revenue and a local multiplier as wages are spent; roads, power and port capacity that other businesses can also use.","code":"3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (industrial structure and TNCs)","open":true},{"front":"The environmental cost of Nigeria's oil industry","back":"Pipeline spills and gas flaring pollute farmland and fishing grounds in the Niger Delta. A 2011 United Nations report on Ogoniland asked for a US$1 billion clean-up fund and warned restoration could take 25 to 30 years.","code":"3.2.2 — Some LICs and NEEs are experiencing rapid economic development which leads to significant social, environmental and cultural change (trade, aid, environment and quality of life)","open":false},{"front":"De-industrialisation","back":"The decline of a country's manufacturing industry, seen in falling output and employment. UK manufacturing jobs fell from about 6.7 million in the late 1970s to under 3 million today.","code":"3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (causes of change and the post-industrial economy)","open":false},{"front":"Cambridge Science Park","back":"The UK's first science park, opened by Trinity College, Cambridge in 1970 on the city's northern edge beside the A14, about 5 km east of where the M11 ends, now home to over 100 high-technology firms.","code":"3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (causes of change and the post-industrial economy)","open":false},{"front":"Rural population decline: the Outer Hebrides","back":"Young people leave the islands for work and study, so the population has fallen from around 46,000 a century ago to roughly 26,000, leaving an ageing population, closing schools and fewer bus and ferry services.","code":"3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (environment and the rural landscape)","open":false},{"front":"The north-south divide","back":"The gap in incomes, output per head, health and house prices between London and the south east of England and the north of England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland.","code":"3.2.2 — Major changes in the economy of the UK have affected, and will continue to affect, employment patterns and regional growth (infrastructure, the north–south divide and the UK in the wider world)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Development indicators for four contrasting countries (written for this room; the figures are real, rounded from World Bank and United Nations Development Programme data for the early 2020s, and should be read as orders of magnitude; every value in the HDI column comes from one source and one year, the United Nations Development Programme's Human Development Report 2023/24, which reports data for 2022).\nCountry | GNI per head (US$) | Life expectancy at birth (years) | HDI\nNorway | 95,000 | 83 | 0.97\nUnited Kingdom | 50,000 | 81 | 0.94\nBangladesh | 2,800 | 74 | 0.67\nNigeria | 2,000 | 55 | 0.55","Figure 2 — Sketch map: Cambridge Science Park and its surroundings (written for this room; the places and roads are real, the distances approximate).\nThe map covers the northern edge of Cambridge. The Science Park is drawn as a block of low buildings among landscaped grounds and two ornamental lakes, on the east side of Milton Road. Immediately north of the park runs the A14 dual carriageway, east to west, with the Milton interchange about 1.3 km to the north-east, where the A10 leaves it and runs north towards Ely. The M11 is shown ending at the A14 about 5 km west of the park, so the site joins the motorway network; London is roughly 100 km to the south by road. Cambridge North railway station is marked about 1 km south of the park, labelled with trains to London King's Cross in about an hour, and the Cambridgeshire guided busway is drawn passing the station. The residential estates of Arbury, King's Hedges and Chesterton lie west and south of the park, with cycle paths drawn linking them and the station to the site. The University of Cambridge colleges and science departments are marked about 4 km south in the city centre. North of the A14 the map shows open farmland and the village of Milton.","Figure 3 — Graph: employment in UK manufacturing, 1978 to 2023 (written for this room; the values are real but approximate, rounded to the nearest 0.1 million, from Office for National Statistics workforce jobs estimates).\nVertical axis: jobs in manufacturing, in millions, scaled 0 to 8. Horizontal axis: year, 1978 to 2023.\nPlotted values: 1978, 6.7 | 1990, 4.9 | 2000, 4.0 | 2010, 2.6 | 2023, 2.6.\nThe line falls between every pair of readings up to 2010 and then levels off. The steepest fall is between 1978 and 1990; between 2010 and 2023 the line is flat at about 2.6 million.","Figure 4 — Extract: tourism in Jamaica (written for this room; the figures quoted in it are real, rounded).\nJamaica is a Caribbean island of about 2.8 million people lying south of Cuba. Tourism is its largest earner of foreign exchange: in 2019 the island received more than 4 million visitors and earned over US$3 billion from them. Most guests stay in all-inclusive resort hotels strung along the north and west coasts at Montego Bay, Ocho Rios and Negril, and most arrive through Sangster International Airport at Montego Bay or on a cruise ship. On the World Travel and Tourism Council's total measure, which adds the indirect and induced jobs in transport, construction, farming and craft to those in hotels and attractions, travel and tourism supported at least a quarter of all jobs on the island before the pandemic; counting only the jobs directly in tourism, Jamaica's own official statistics put the share nearer one in ten. A levy charged on every arriving passenger is paid into a Tourism Enhancement Fund, which the government spends on training, on beach and heritage projects and on local infrastructure. The industry is seasonal: the peak runs from December to April, and work thins out in the wet late summer, which is also the hurricane season — Hurricane Beryl struck the island on 3 July 2024. Some of the money never reaches Jamaica at all, because foreign-owned hotel chains, airlines and cruise lines are paid abroad; the largest all-inclusive chain, Sandals, was founded at Montego Bay in 1981 and is Jamaican-owned."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Nigeria — the case study of one LIC or NEE","items":["Location: West Africa, on the Gulf of Guinea, bordered by Benin to the west, Niger to the north, Chad to the north-east and Cameroon to the east.","Size and population: about 924,000 km², nearly four times the area of the UK; over 200 million people, the largest population in Africa.","Cities: Abuja became the capital in 1991, built inland and centrally; Lagos, on the coast, is the largest city and one of the fastest-growing in the world.","Political context: independent from Britain on 1 October 1960; civil war 1967 to 1970; a member of the Commonwealth, of OPEC since 1971, of ECOWAS, whose headquarters are in Abuja, and of the African Union.","Social and cultural context: over 500 languages; a mainly Muslim north and a mainly Christian south; the main ethnic groups are the Hausa-Fulani, the Yoruba and the Igbo; a Boko Haram insurgency in the north-east since 2009.","Economic importance: in 2014 Nigeria overtook South Africa to become Africa's largest economy, and it is one of Africa's largest oil producers.","Industrial structure: agriculture still occupies roughly a third of workers, while manufacturing and services produce most output — food processing, cement, brewing, telecommunications, banking and Nollywood, one of the world's largest film industries by number of films made.","Oil in the economy: oil and gas earn about 90% of export income but well under a tenth of GDP, so the industry dominates trade without employing many people.","TNCs: Shell has produced oil since the first commercial discovery at Oloibiri in 1956; Unilever has manufactured in Nigeria for about a century; Guinness opened a brewery in Lagos in 1963, its first outside the British Isles.","Advantages of TNCs here: export revenue for the federal budget, wages well above subsistence farming, training for engineers and technicians, and roads, power and ports that other businesses use.","Disadvantages of TNCs here: most profit returns to shareholders abroad, few jobs relative to the population, low wages in some operations, and heavy pollution.","Environmental cost: repeated pipeline spills, sabotage and gas flaring in the Niger Delta; a United Nations Environment Programme report on Ogoniland in 2011 recommended an initial US$1 billion clean-up fund and warned restoration could take 25 to 30 years; in 2015 Shell agreed a settlement of about £55 million with the Bodo community over two spills in 2008.","Changing trade: crude oil is sold mainly to Europe, India and other Asian buyers, while China has become the biggest source of imports and the main financier of infrastructure.","Chinese-built infrastructure: the Abuja to Kaduna railway opened in 2016, the Lagos to Ibadan line in 2021 and the Lekki deep-sea port in 2023.","Aid: emergency relief, long-term development aid and NGO work; the polio campaign backed by the World Health Organization and the Gates Foundation ended wild poliovirus in Nigeria, and the WHO African Region was certified free of it in August 2020.","Quality of life: HDI about 0.55 and life expectancy about 55 years, roughly twenty-five years below the UK's; roughly six adults in ten can read and write."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: tourism in Jamaica — the example of tourism reducing the development gap","items":["Location: a Caribbean island in the Greater Antilles, south of Cuba; population about 2.8 million; capital Kingston on the south coast.","Development position: GNI per head of roughly US$5,500 and an HDI of about 0.7, far below the UK's, so Jamaica sits inside the development gap the strategy is meant to close.","Why tourists come: warm dry winters, beaches and coral reefs, mountains and waterfalls, and a music and food culture known worldwide.","Where they go: the resort strip on the north and west coasts at Montego Bay, Ocho Rios and Negril, with Sangster International Airport at Montego Bay as the main gateway.","Scale: more than 4 million visitors in 2019, a large share of them cruise-ship passengers, earning over US$3 billion in foreign exchange.","Economic importance: tourism is the island's largest earner of foreign exchange; on the World Travel and Tourism Council's total measure, which adds indirect and induced jobs in transport, construction, farming and craft to those in hotels and attractions, it supported at least a quarter of all jobs before the pandemic, while jobs directly in tourism are nearer one in ten.","Multiplier: wages paid in hotels, taxis, building sites and farms are spent again in shops, schools and markets, spreading income beyond the resorts.","Government income: a levy charged on every arriving passenger is paid into the Tourism Enhancement Fund, which pays for training, beach and heritage projects and local infrastructure.","Local ownership matters: Sandals, the largest all-inclusive chain, was founded at Montego Bay in 1981 and is Jamaican-owned, so more of its profit stays in the country.","Leakage: foreign-owned hotel chains, airlines and cruise lines are paid abroad, and all-inclusive packages keep guest spending inside the resort gates.","Seasonality: the peak season runs from December to April, so much hotel and transport work is temporary and low-paid.","Environmental pressure: coral reefs, beaches and water supplies are stressed by construction, sewage and visitor numbers.","Hazard risk: the island lies in the hurricane belt and Hurricane Beryl struck it on 3 July 2024, damaging homes, farms and tourism infrastructure.","The judgement to make: tourism earns the foreign currency and creates the jobs that narrow the gap, but seasonality and leakage mean it narrows more slowly than the arrivals figure suggests."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Nissan at Sunderland — the example of more sustainable modern industry","items":["What and where: Nissan's car plant at Sunderland, in the north east of England, opened in 1986 on a former airfield.","Scale: around 6,000 people work at the plant, with thousands more in supplier firms nearby.","Why it is more sustainable: on-site wind turbines and a large solar farm generate part of the electricity the plant uses, so less comes from fossil fuels.","Product: the plant built the all-electric Nissan Leaf as well as the Qashqai and Juke, so the vehicles leaving it produce no exhaust emissions in use.","Supply chain: a battery factory next to the plant supplies electric-vehicle batteries, cutting the transport emissions of shipping batteries in.","Regional effect: the plant is the clearest case of foreign direct investment replacing lost heavy industry in the north east, where shipbuilding and coal mining closed.","The wider point for the exam: modern industry reduces its impact by generating its own renewable power, recycling waste and water, building on brownfield land and locating suppliers alongside it.","The limitation worth naming: a single plant transforms one town, not a whole region, and a branch plant of a foreign company can be closed or relocated."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the changing UK economy — deindustrialisation, science parks, rural change and the divide","items":["Causes of change: de-industrialisation, as coal, steel, shipbuilding and textiles lost markets and reserves; globalisation, as production moved to lower-wage countries; and government policy, which ended subsidies, privatised industries and encouraged services and foreign investment.","Scale of deindustrialisation: manufacturing employment fell from about 6.7 million in the late 1970s to under 3 million today; the last deep coal mine, Kellingley in North Yorkshire, closed in December 2015; the last coal-fired power station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar, closed on 30 September 2024.","The post-industrial economy: services, finance, research and information technology now provide roughly four fifths of UK jobs, with manufacturing under a tenth and farming around 1%.","Science parks: Cambridge Science Park, opened by Trinity College, Cambridge in 1970, was the UK's first; it sits on the northern edge of Cambridge beside the A14, about 5 km east of where the M11 ends at that road, near Cambridge North station, and houses over 100 high-technology firms.","Why science and business parks locate where they do: beside a research university, on the edge of a city with room to expand, on a motorway or trunk road, near an airport or fast rail link, and in an attractive landscaped setting that helps recruit staff.","Impacts of industry on the physical environment: quarrying scars landscapes, factories and power stations emit greenhouse gases and acidic pollutants, waste and warm water are discharged into rivers, and land is taken from farmland and habitat for works, estates, roads and airports.","Rural area of population growth — South Cambridgeshire: commuter villages are expanding and a new town, Northstowe, is being built on the former RAF Oakington airfield; more housing and jobs and services that survive, but heavier traffic, higher house prices and pressure on countryside and water supply.","Rural area of population decline — the Outer Hebrides: young people leave for work and study, so the population has fallen from around 46,000 a century ago to roughly 26,000; the result is an ageing population, school closures, fewer shops and reduced bus and ferry services.","Road and rail: the Elizabeth line opened across London in May 2022; HS2 is under construction between London and Birmingham, but the leg to Manchester was cancelled in October 2023; the Transpennine Route Upgrade is electrifying the line between Manchester, Leeds and York.","Ports and airports: Felixstowe is the UK's largest container port and Heathrow its busiest airport, handling around 80 million passengers a year.","The north-south divide: London's output per head is far above every other region, house prices in London are roughly double the UK average, and healthy life expectancy is lower in the north east than in the south east.","Strategies to reduce regional difference: devolution to combined authorities with elected metro mayors, with Greater Manchester and the Liverpool City Region electing their first in 2017; the Northern Powerhouse, announced in 2014; enterprise zones; moving public bodies north, such as the BBC to MediaCityUK in Salford and a Treasury campus to Darlington; and the Levelling Up White Paper of February 2022.","The UK in the wider world: links through trade, culture, transport and electronic communication; the UK left the European Union on 31 January 2020 and remains one of the Commonwealth's 56 member states."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the room's processes and terms","items":["Development: the standard of living and quality of life of a country's people, and the progress a country makes in improving them.","Development gap: the difference in wealth and wellbeing between the richest and poorest countries.","LIC, NEE, HIC: low-income countries, newly emerging economies whose industry is growing fast, and high-income countries.","GNI per head: total national output plus income earned abroad, divided by population, usually in US dollars.","Infant mortality rate: deaths of children under one year old per 1,000 live births per year; it falls quickly as water, sanitation and health care improve.","Life expectancy: the average number of years a baby born now can expect to live.","Literacy rate: the percentage of adults who can read and write.","Human Development Index: a composite scored 0 to 1 from life expectancy at birth, education and GNI per head.","Demographic Transition Model: a five-stage model of how birth rate, death rate and total population change as a country develops.","Uneven development: physical, economic and historical causes producing disparities in wealth and health and driving international migration.","Strategies to close the gap: investment, industrial development and tourism, aid, intermediate technology, fairtrade, debt relief and microfinance loans.","Aid: emergency (short-term relief after a disaster), development (long-term projects) and tied aid (given on condition it is spent with the donor's own firms).","Transnational corporation: a company that produces or sells in more than one country, with its headquarters usually in an HIC.","Multiplier effect: the way money spent by new workers and businesses circulates, creating further jobs and income in the local economy.","Economic sectors: primary (extracting raw materials), secondary (manufacturing), tertiary (services) and quaternary (research and information).","De-industrialisation and the post-industrial economy: the decline of manufacturing output and employment, and its replacement by services, finance, research and information technology."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7–9) on the nine-mark TNC question","items":["The question: 'The advantages of transnational corporations to the countries in which they operate outweigh the disadvantages.' Do you agree with this statement? Use a named example in your answer. Justify your decision. [9 marks]","The paragraph: The heaviest cost of Shell's presence in Nigeria is environmental, and it falls on people who see almost none of the revenue. Oil has been extracted from the Niger Delta since the first commercial discovery at Oloibiri in 1956, and decades of pipeline leaks, sabotage and gas flaring have contaminated creeks, farmland and fishing grounds across the Delta. The chain matters: crude oil coats the mangrove roots where fish breed, so the catch collapses; hydrocarbons soak into soil and shallow groundwater, so cassava yields fall and drinking water is unsafe; families who had two livelihoods are left with neither and must buy food with money they no longer earn. The scale is documented. A United Nations Environment Programme study of Ogoniland published in 2011 recommended an initial US$1 billion clean-up fund and warned that restoring the area could take 25 to 30 years, and in 2015 Shell agreed a settlement of about £55 million with the Bodo community over two spills in 2008. Set against oil and gas earning roughly 90% of Nigeria's export income, this is the real trade-off in the question: the revenue is national and reaches the federal budget, while the damage is local and permanent for a generation. On this evidence the advantages do not simply outweigh the disadvantages; they are enjoyed by different people from the ones who bear the costs.","Why this reaches Level 3: it carries comprehensive and specific knowledge of a named TNC (AO1), it explains the mechanism linking extraction to lost livelihoods rather than asserting that pollution is bad (AO2), and it ends with a substantiated judgement that answers the statement and is balanced against the revenue (AO3). The specialist terms are used accurately throughout.","What it deliberately avoids: any material about impacts on Shell's home country, which the mark scheme does not credit; and any list of advantages and disadvantages left unweighed."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–3) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The question: the same nine-mark question on whether the advantages of transnational corporations outweigh the disadvantages.","The paragraph: TNCs are big companies that operate in lots of different countries around the world. They have many advantages because they give people jobs and the workers get paid, which is good for the country. They also build things like roads and schools sometimes. However there are disadvantages as well, such as pollution, and the profits go back to the richer country instead of staying where the factory is. Also the wages can be low and the working conditions can be bad. So overall I think TNCs have both advantages and disadvantages and it depends on the country.","The descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic), 'Demonstrates limited knowledge of the characteristics of one or more TNCs. Answers may be largely generic', and 'Justification is limited to one or more simple points'. Everything in it is true and relevant, which is why it is not zero.","The descriptors it misses: it names no TNC, no country, no place and no figure, so it cannot reach 'comprehensive and specific knowledge'; nothing is explained through to a consequence, so there is no 'thorough and accurate geographical understanding'; and the last sentence lists both sides without deciding, so it cannot be a 'substantiated conclusion'.","The single change that would lift it most: replace 'lots of different countries' with Shell in the Niger Delta and add one figure, then take one advantage and one disadvantage two steps further each and commit to a verdict."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'To what extent has economic development improved quality of life in an LIC or NEE?' [9 marks]","items":["The question in the board's own words: 'Rapid economic development in an LIC or NEE improves quality of life for most of the population.' To what extent do you agree? Use a case study of one LIC or NEE in your answer. [9 marks]","Decoding the command: 'to what extent' asks how far the statement holds, not whether it holds at all. The answer must reach a degree — largely, partly, only for some — and defend it. Note the phrase 'most of the population': the statement can be true of many people and still be false as written.","Timing: about ten minutes, which is roughly one minute a mark. Spend one minute planning the three paragraphs and the verdict, then write without stopping to reread.","Opening: one sentence naming Nigeria as the case study and stating the verdict you are going to defend, for example that development has improved life for many but not yet for most.","Paragraph 1 — the gains, using the changing industrial structure: the shift from subsistence agriculture towards manufacturing and services (food processing, cement, brewing, telecommunications, banking, Nollywood), higher wages than farming and the multiplier that follows; infrastructure funded by growth, including the Abuja to Kaduna railway of 2016, the Lagos to Ibadan line of 2021 and the Lekki deep-sea port of 2023.","Paragraph 2 — the gains that come from outside the market, using aid: the polio campaign backed by the World Health Organization and the Gates Foundation, and the certification of the WHO African Region as free of wild poliovirus in August 2020; a health gain that reaches rural people the oil economy never touches.","Paragraph 3 — the limits, using the indicators and the environment: HDI still about 0.55 and life expectancy about 55 years, roughly twenty-five years below the UK's; oil and gas earning about 90% of export income while employing very few people; pipeline spills and gas flaring in the Niger Delta, with the 2011 United Nations Environment Programme report on Ogoniland asking for a US$1 billion clean-up fund and warning restoration could take 25 to 30 years; the regional unevenness between Lagos and the insurgency-hit north east.","The evaluation thread that runs through all three: who gains and who pays. Keep returning to the phrase 'most of the population', because that is what the statement claims and what the evidence has to be tested against.","What the conclusion must say: a degree, not a summary. It must state how far the statement holds (for a growing urban middle class, and increasingly for rural people through health gains, but not yet for most), give the reason it is limited (growth built on an export that employs few and damages the land others live from), and name the condition under which it would become true (spreading oil revenue and cleaning up the Delta). A conclusion that repeats the two sides without a degree stays in Level 2."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:01:42Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged against the fixed kit file: all seven faults from the first pass are properly closed - Figure 2 and q6 now put the Milton interchange about 1.3 km north-east where the A10 leaves the A14 for Ely (correct), Figure 3 is replotted on the real ONS workforce jobs series (6.7, 4.9, 4.0, 2.6, 2.6, verified against series JWR7) with the untrue 'falls at every reading' claim gone and q1's key, q11 and worked example 2 all recalculated to 4.1 million, the unrankable 'widest contrast' step is replaced by a sound second-indicator step, the M11 wording matches the map, q7 says 'about 40% above', the Jamaica jobs claim is attributed to the WTTC total measure with the direct share given as nearer one in ten, and the HDI column is one vintage at Nigeria 0.55. A diff against the previous version shows the fixer touched nothing else, so the rest of the kit - the Nigeria, Nissan and UK fact files, all three nine-mark solutions, the tariffs (2/4/6/9 only), the level labels and the AO splits against Paper 2 specimen questions 02.3, 02.5 and 02.10 with no SPaG - stands as verified. Publishable."}}},"3.2.3.1":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.2.3.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section C of Paper 2 opens with the resource management overview, and every student meets it whichever option follows. It asks what food, water and energy actually do for people's well-being, and how the UK's own supply of all three is changing. Nearly every question here is set on a figure — a rainfall map, a graph of the energy mix, an extract of opposing views — so marks are thrown away by students who know the topic but never quote the resource, and by students who write that pollution is bad instead of naming a place, a figure and a process. This room gives you the UK detail (food miles and agribusiness, deficit, surplus and transfer, the collapse of coal and the rise of wind) and the habit of carrying every point through to its effect and then weighing it.","idea":[{"h":"Why food, water and energy decide how well people live","t":"Food supplies the energy to work, grow and learn: where diets fall below what an adult needs, illness rises and children miss school, so incomes stay low. Clean water prevents disease and gives back the hours otherwise spent walking to fetch it, hours that fall mostly on women and girls. Energy lights homes, refrigerates vaccines, cooks food without smoke, pumps irrigation water and powers the factories that create jobs. The three are linked: growing food takes water, treating and pumping water takes energy, and producing energy takes water. Supply is deeply unequal — around 2 billion people do not have safely managed drinking water, roughly 700 to 800 million face hunger, and of the order of 700 million live without electricity, most of them in sub-Saharan Africa and parts of South Asia — and consumption is more unequal still, because a person in a high income country uses many times the energy and water of a person in a low income one. A top-level answer never stops at the shortage: it names the resource, gives a scale, and then follows the shortage through to health, education or income, which is what 'human development' means in the question.","code":"3.2.3.1 — Food, water and energy are fundamental to human development"},{"h":"The UK's resource position in one view","t":"The UK is a rich country that is short of none of the three outright, and comfortable in none of them either. It grows about 60 to 65 per cent of the food it consumes, about three-quarters of the kinds of food its climate allows, and imports the rest, which ties its shelves and its prices to other countries' harvests. It receives plenty of rain, but in the wrong corner: the wet uplands of the north and west are thinly populated, while the dry south and east hold London and the fastest housebuilding. It sat on its own oil, gas and coal for a generation, and now imports energy, having been a net importer since about 2004. So the UK's story in all three strands is the same shape — demand and supply are mismatched in place, in season or in time, and the response is either to move the resource, to change what people use, or to change where it comes from. A top-level answer uses this overview as a hinge: it states the mismatch first, then spends its detail on the strand the question names rather than describing all three.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (overview)"},{"h":"Food in the UK: miles, seasons and agribusiness","t":"Three changes sit behind the UK's food. First, supermarkets buy high-value produce from low income countries — Kenyan green beans and cut flowers are flown in — which earns those countries foreign currency and employs many people, but uses land and water that could grow local staples, and leaves growers exposed to a buyer who can switch supplier. Second, shoppers expect seasonal food all year and organic produce alongside it, so strawberries and salad arrive in January from Spain, Morocco or further, and the 'food miles' travelled climb; the mode matters more than the distance, because air freight emits far more carbon dioxide per tonne-kilometre than a ship, so a flown bean can outweigh an apple that crossed the world by sea. Third, farming has moved towards agribusiness: large-scale commercial units with big fields, heavy machinery, agrochemicals and supermarket contracts, typically the arable estates of Lincolnshire, Cambridgeshire and Norfolk. That delivers cheap, reliable food, and it also removes hedgerows, exposes light and peaty soils to erosion, sends nitrates into rivers and employs far fewer people. Moves towards local sourcing — farm shops, markets, box schemes — cut transport and keep money local, but the range is narrow and food grown out of season in heated UK glasshouses can carry a bigger footprint than the imported version. A top-level answer weighs one of these against another instead of listing all three.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)"},{"h":"Water in the UK: demand, quality, deficit and transfer","t":"Demand keeps rising: the population grows, households get smaller, and dishwashers, washing machines, power showers and garden watering push the average person's use to about 140 litres a day. Supply is fixed in the wrong places. The western uplands take over 2,000 mm of rain a year, with the wettest Lake District valleys over 3,000 mm; East Anglia and the London area receive under 700 mm. Set that against population and you get areas of surplus in the north and west and areas of deficit in the south and east, where demand exceeds supply. Three responses compete. Transfer moves water to the people: the Elan Valley aqueduct has fed Birmingham from mid-Wales by gravity since 1904, and Kielder Water in Northumberland, completed in 1982 and holding about 200 million cubic metres, regulates the River North Tyne so that more can be abstracted downstream. Conservation attacks demand and waste instead, since roughly a fifth of the water put into the mains is lost to leaks. Quality management protects what already exists, because water polluted by nitrate and phosphate run-off, storm-overflow sewage, road run-off or old mine drainage is supply lost: nutrients trigger eutrophication, algae block the light, decomposition strips the oxygen and fish die. A top-level answer treats transfer as one option among three and says what it is being judged against — volume gained, cost, or damage done.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)"},{"h":"Energy in the UK: a mix that has been rebuilt","t":"The energy mix is the range of sources a country uses, and the UK's has been rebuilt in little more than a decade. In 2012 coal generated about 40 per cent of UK electricity; by 2023 renewables generated about 45 per cent, gas about a third, nuclear about an eighth and coal about 1 per cent, and Britain's last coal-fired power station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar in Nottinghamshire, closed at the end of September 2024. Domestic fossil fuel supplies have shrunk behind that shift: North Sea oil and gas production peaked around 1999 and has fallen since, the last deep coal mine closed in 2015, and the UK has been a net energy importer since about 2004, so bills follow world markets. Every replacement carries its own argument. Offshore wind is now the largest renewable source and Hornsea 2, off the Yorkshire coast, was the world's largest operating offshore wind farm when completed in 2022 at about 1.3 GW, with blades made in Hull — but the capital cost is huge and the wind is intermittent. Shale gas would cut imports, yet tremors at Preston New Road near Blackpool, the largest magnitude 2.9 in August 2019, brought a moratorium on fracking in England in November 2019. Nuclear is low-carbon but Hinkley Point C is over budget and late, and the waste lasts. A top-level answer pairs an economic point with an environmental one for each source and then ranks them, because the command word is almost always evaluate or assess.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Energy)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing a distribution without simply listing places (Figure 1)","setup":"Figure 1 is the two-panel UK map written for this room. Panel A shades average annual rainfall: over 2,000 mm on the western Highlands of Scotland, the Lake District and Snowdonia, with the wettest valleys over 3,000 mm; 1,000 to 2,000 mm across much of Wales, the Pennines, northern England and the south-west; 700 to 1,000 mm across the Midlands; under 700 mm across East Anglia, Essex, Kent and the London area. Panel B shades population density, densest in London and the South East. The question set is: Using Figure 1, describe the distribution of the areas of the UK that receive the heaviest rainfall. [2 marks]","steps":["Take the broad pattern off Panel A and say it in a direction: the heaviest rainfall lies in the north and west, and it clings to high ground.","Attach the map's own evidence to that statement, which is what turns one mark into two: the western Highlands, the Lake District and Snowdonia carry the shading over 2,000 mm, and the wettest valleys pass 3,000 mm.","Give the scattered uplands a shape, because a pattern scores and a list does not: together those blocks form a broken band down the western edge of the country.","Leave out what the question did not ask for: the dry corner of England belongs to a different answer and earns nothing here."],"cues":["Which panel answers this question, and what is its broad pattern?","What would turn that bare statement into a developed point?","Three uplands named separately is a list — what shape do they make together?","Is there anything on Figure 1 you should deliberately not write about?"]},{"title":"Making two maps say one thing: why water has to be moved (Figure 1)","setup":"Figure 1 again: Panel A shows average annual rainfall (over 2,000 mm on the western uplands, under 700 mm over East Anglia, Essex, Kent and London), Panel B shows population density (densest in London and the South East, sparsest in the Scottish Highlands, mid-Wales and the Pennines). The question set is: Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, explain why water has to be transferred from one part of the UK to another. [3 marks] The mark scheme gives no credit for describing one map, or both maps, in isolation.","steps":["Read Panel A as supply: ground taking over 2,000 mm a year receives far more water than the few people living on it can use.","Read Panel B as demand: the densest population sits in London and the South East, which is exactly the ground Panel A shades under 700 mm.","Fuse the two panels in one sentence, which is the move the mark scheme is paying for: rainfall is highest where people are fewest, so the north and west hold a surplus while the south and east run a deficit.","Finish on the consequence the question actually asks for: a mismatch in place cannot be solved in place, so water is moved from the surplus catchments to the deficit ones."],"cues":["What does the rainfall panel tell you about supply?","What does the density panel tell you about demand?","How do you write one sentence that uses both panels at once?","The question said 'why transfer' — what still has to be said?"]},{"title":"Reading change off a graph and explaining it (Figure 3)","setup":"Figure 3 is the bar graph written for this room: share of UK electricity generation, plotted for 2012 and 2023, rounded to the nearest 5 per cent. Coal 2012 = 40, 2023 = 0 (its real share was about 1 per cent). Gas 2012 = 30, 2023 = 35. Nuclear 2012 = 20, 2023 = 15. Renewables 2012 = 10, 2023 = 45. The question set is: Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain why the UK's energy mix has changed since 2012. [6 marks]","steps":["Quantify before explaining, because the figure is half the marks: coal falls from 40 to the foot of the scale, about 1 per cent in reality, while renewables climb from 10 to 45, a rise of 35 percentage points.","Notice what moved least: gas edges up only from 30 to 35 and nuclear slips from 20 to 15, so almost all of the share coal lost has gone to renewables rather than to gas.","Explain the fall with a mechanism, not a label: carbon pricing and air quality rules made ageing coal stations expensive to run, so they closed one by one, the last of them at Ratcliffe-on-Soar at the end of September 2024.","Explain the rise the same way: the cost of offshore wind fell steeply and huge North Sea farms such as Hornsea 2, about 1.3 GW, came on line from 2022.","Land every point back on the mix itself rather than on the technology, which is what the question asked about."],"cues":["Before any explaining, what do the bars actually say?","Which sources moved least, and why does that matter?","Why would a power station that worked in 2012 be uneconomic later?","What changed on the other side of the graph to replace it?","What does each paragraph have to end on?"]},{"title":"Turning four viewpoints into conflict (Figure 4)","setup":"Figure 4 is the extract written for this room: four views on a proposed scheme to dam an upland valley in the north-west and pipe the water to a growing city region in the drier south-east. A water company engineer says demand will outgrow supply within twenty years and the scheme guarantees water for hundreds of thousands of households. A farmer in the valley says the reservoir will drown his best land and cut the rest in two, and that compensation buys fields but not a family farm. A householder in the city region wants a reliable supply after a hosepipe ban but objects to paying for a reservoir while the company leaks water from its mains. A wildlife trust officer says the river below the dam will run lower and warmer, harming salmon and trout, that moving water between catchments can spread invasive species, and that leaks and meters should come first. The question set is: Using Figure 4, suggest why a water transfer scheme may lead to conflict between different interest groups. [6 marks]","steps":["Pair two views that cannot both be satisfied: the engineer needs the valley flooded to secure the city's supply, and the farmer loses his livelihood if it is.","Give each side its own reason in its own terms, rather than labelling one right: the engineer is protecting hundreds of thousands of households, the farmer is losing land that cannot be replaced.","Develop why the disagreement is structural: the benefits land in one region and the costs land in another, and the people paying the cost receive none of the gain.","Add the second axis the extract offers: the householder and the wildlife officer both accept the city needs water, yet split over whether the answer is a new reservoir or mending leaks and fitting meters.","Judge the balance so the answer is an evaluation and not a summary: economic security set against environmental and social cost is why evidence alone cannot settle the argument."],"cues":["Which two speakers want opposite outcomes?","What is each of them protecting?","Why is this disagreement built into the scheme rather than a misunderstanding?","Two speakers agree on the problem — where do they part company?","What has to be added so this is more than a list of opinions?"]},{"title":"Planning a six-mark 'Using Figure 4' evaluation before writing a word","setup":"The question set is: 'Transferring water from areas of surplus to areas of deficit is the best way of matching water supply and demand in the UK.' Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, evaluate this view. [6 marks] (AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks) Figure 4 gives four speakers on a proposal to dam an upland valley in the north-west and pipe the water to a growing city region in the drier south-east: a water company engineer, a farmer in the valley, a householder in the city region and a wildlife trust officer. You also have the room's material: Kielder Water in Northumberland, completed in 1982, about 200 million cubic metres, regulating the River North Tyne; the Elan Valley aqueduct carrying Welsh water about 120 km to Birmingham by gravity since 1904; the flooding of Capel Celyn in the 1960s to supply Liverpool; leakage of roughly a fifth of mains water; metering, efficient appliances and rainwater harvesting; average use of about 140 litres per person per day.","steps":["Decode the command before choosing content: 'evaluate' asks how far transfer beats the alternatives, so the answer has to rank options rather than praise one, and 'Using Figure 4' means the speakers are worked, not decorated.","Count the marks before counting the paragraphs: a six-mark levelled answer with four of its marks on the resource pays for two developed paragraphs and a verdict, not the four paragraphs an extended essay would take.","Sort the four speakers into those two paragraphs: the engineer carries the case for transfer, and the farmer, the wildlife trust officer and the householder carry the case against.","Spend one piece of your own knowledge in each paragraph rather than a list: Kielder for storage and scale in the first, leakage and metering in the second.","Decide the conclusion now, because it controls both paragraphs: saving water cannot make it rain in the driest corner, so transfer is necessary, but it is the most expensive and most damaging option and belongs after leakage and demand management, not before them."],"cues":["What does this command word demand that 'explain' would not?","How many paragraphs will six marks actually pay for?","Which speaker belongs in which paragraph, and why?","What one named piece of your own knowledge will you spend in each paragraph?","What is your verdict, and why must you settle it before writing?"]}],"check":[{"q":"The western Highlands receive over 2,000 mm of rain a year and the London area under 700 mm. Why is it London, not the Highlands, that has a water deficit?","a":"Because deficit means demand exceeding supply, not low rainfall on its own. The Highlands take huge rainfall and hold very few people, so they have a surplus; the London area takes the least rain and holds the densest population and the fastest housebuilding, so demand overtakes what falls there."},{"q":"A student writes: 'Food miles are bad because lorries cause pollution.' What two things have to be added before it can score?","a":"Evidence and a mechanism. Name the source and the mode with a figure — green beans air-freighted about 6,800 km from Kenya — and carry it through to the effect: air freight emits far more carbon dioxide per tonne-kilometre than shipping, so the carbon footprint of the meal rises."},{"q":"Coal generated about 40 per cent of UK electricity in 2012 and about 1 per cent in 2023. Give the two halves of the explanation.","a":"Push and pull. Carbon pricing, air quality rules and old, inefficient plant made coal stations expensive to run, so they closed, the last at Ratcliffe-on-Soar at the end of September 2024; meanwhile the cost of offshore wind fell and large farms such as Hornsea 2 came on line, so renewables took the share."},{"q":"Why does an answer about local sourcing of food need a caveat rather than a cheer?","a":"Because the transport saved is not the whole footprint. Local sourcing cuts food miles and keeps money in the local economy, but the range is limited by season and food grown out of season in heated UK glasshouses can emit more than the same food shipped or driven in from a warmer country."}]},"room":{"facts":["The UK produces about 60 to 65 per cent of the food it consumes, and about three-quarters of the kinds of food its climate allows it to grow; the rest is imported.","Rainfall: over 2,000 mm a year on the western uplands, with the wettest Lake District valleys over 3,000 mm; under 700 mm over East Anglia and the London area. Surplus in the north and west, deficit in the south and east.","Kielder Water, Northumberland: completed 1982, about 200 million cubic metres, the UK's largest reservoir by capacity, regulating the River North Tyne. The Elan Valley aqueduct has fed Birmingham from mid-Wales by gravity since 1904.","The average person in the UK uses about 140 litres of water a day, and roughly a fifth of the water put into the mains is lost through leaks.","Electricity mix: coal about 40 per cent in 2012 and about 1 per cent in 2023; renewables about 45 per cent in 2023, gas about a third, nuclear about an eighth. Britain's last coal-fired power station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar, closed at the end of September 2024.","Section C of Paper 2 sets no extended nine-mark question. Question 3, the challenge of resource management, is worth 14 marks and its longest part is the 6-mark levelled question — 03.3 in the specimen — marked in Levels 1 to 3 (Basic 1-2, Clear 3-4, Detailed 5-6) with AO2 = 2 marks and AO3 = 4 marks and no marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar; the specimen's three Paper 2 SPaG marks sit on the urban issues question. The rest of the section is short answers, so the paper’s Section C parts run 1, 2, 3 and 6 marks, and this room also sets 4-mark practice questions. Allow about a minute a mark — six minutes on the 6-mark question — and a moment at the end to read it back.","A question that begins 'Using Figure...' is capped if the figure is ignored: quote a value, a place or a phrase from the resource in every paragraph."],"questions":[{"q":"Give one reason why the demand for water in the UK is increasing. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for one valid reason; credit one reason only, eg population growth (1); more water-using appliances such as dishwashers and power showers (1); more garden watering (1). AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"The UK's population is growing and households are getting smaller, so more homes each draw water for drinking, washing and sanitation. That is enough for the mark: on a one-mark question a second reason earns nothing, so one clear change, written as a complete sentence, is all the examiner needs.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Demand is about how much water people take out of the supply, not about how much rain falls on them.","Think of one thing that has changed in UK homes or in the size of the population, and ask what it does to the water drawn each day.","One sentence, one reason, no list — extra reasons cannot earn extra marks here."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, describe the distribution of the areas of the UK that receive the heaviest rainfall. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic description of the distribution shown on the map; second mark for a developed point using detail from the map. No credit for simply listing place names. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The heaviest rainfall is found in the north and west of the UK, and it is concentrated on high ground (1 mark). Figure 1 shades the western Highlands of Scotland, the Lake District and Snowdonia at over 2,000 mm a year, with the wettest valleys above 3,000 mm, so the wettest ground forms a broken band running down the western edge of the country from northern Scotland through Cumbria to Wales and the south-west (2 marks). Notice what makes the second mark: every place name is tied to a direction, to high ground or to a rainfall figure read off the map. A list of three upland names on its own is not a distribution and scores nothing extra, and describing the dry south-east answers a question that was not asked.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Distribution means where something is found across the whole country, not which places are named on the key.","Give the pattern in compass directions first, then reach back into the map for something that develops it.","Two sentences: the pattern, then the same pattern with map evidence attached."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)"]},{"q":"State two reasons why the food eaten in the UK now travels more food miles than it did fifty years ago. [2 marks]","a":"Two reasons stated, one mark each, eg all-year demand for seasonal produce (1); demand for food that cannot be grown in the UK's climate (1); supermarkets sourcing high-value produce from low income countries (1); cheaper air and sea freight (1). AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"First, shoppers now expect seasonal food all year round, so salad and soft fruit are brought in from warmer countries during the British winter instead of being eaten only in their own short season (1). Second, supermarkets buy high-value produce such as green beans and cut flowers from low income countries thousands of kilometres away, because it can be grown cheaply there and flown in quickly (1). The command word is 'state', so neither reason needs developing; two separate, clearly different reasons are worth more here than one reason explained twice.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Food miles rise when food comes from further away — so ask what has changed about where the UK buys its food.","One reason should come from what shoppers expect, and one from where supermarkets choose to buy.","Two short separate sentences, no explanation needed; make sure the two reasons are genuinely different."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, calculate the change, in percentage points, in the share of UK electricity generated from renewables between 2012 and 2023. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for the correct answer: 35 percentage points (an increase). AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"Figure 3 plots renewables at 10 in 2012 and at 45 in 2023. The change is 45 minus 10, which is 35, so renewables gained 35 percentage points of UK electricity generation over the eleven years, and that increase is almost exactly the share coal lost over the same period.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The word 'change' between two years points you to one pair of bars on Figure 3 and nothing else.","This is a subtraction of two shares, not a percentage increase, so take the earlier value away from the later one.","Give the answer as a number of percentage points and say whether it is a rise or a fall."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Energy)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, compare the distance travelled by the UK-grown food with the distance travelled by the air-freighted food. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic comparison drawn from the table; second mark for development using figures from the table. The comparison must be explicit, not two separate descriptions. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The UK-grown food travels a far shorter distance than the air-freighted food (1 mark). Figure 2 gives the Norfolk carrots 190 km, while the green beans flown from Kenya travel about 6,800 km and the asparagus flown from Peru about 10,200 km, which is more than thirty times and more than fifty times the distance the carrots cover (2 marks). A comparison has to hold the two side by side in one sentence: writing a sentence about carrots and then a separate sentence about beans describes the table rather than comparing it, and the second mark depends on the figures being used, not just quoted.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A comparison needs the two things in the same sentence, joined by a word like 'while' or 'whereas'.","Pull the smallest distance and the two flown distances out of the table before you write anything.","State the difference first, then make it bigger by turning the figures into 'times further'."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, explain why water has to be transferred from one part of the UK to another. [3 marks]","a":"One mark for each developed point that uses both panels: surplus where rainfall is high and population sparse; deficit where rainfall is low and population dense; hence the need to move water. No credit for describing one or both maps in isolation. AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"Panel A of Figure 1 shows that the heaviest rainfall, over 2,000 mm a year, falls on the western Highlands, the Lake District and Snowdonia, and Panel B shows that these are among the most thinly populated parts of the UK, so far more water arrives there than the people living there can use: these are areas of surplus (1). The reverse is true in the south and east, where Figure 1 shades East Anglia, Essex, Kent and the London area under 700 mm while Panel B makes London and the South East the most densely populated ground in the country, with the most new housing, so demand exceeds supply and these are areas of deficit (2). Because the rain cannot be made to fall where the people are, the only way to close that gap is to move the water itself, by aqueduct, pipeline or regulated river, from the surplus catchments of the north and west to the deficit catchments of the south and east (3).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each panel on its own answers nothing here; the marks live in what the two panels say when they are laid on top of each other.","Work through supply first, then demand, then the gap between them.","Three points, each one sentence: surplus and why, deficit and why, and what that forces water companies to do."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)","3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (overview)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, suggest the environmental effects of sourcing food in the way the table shows. [4 marks]","a":"Levels 1-2. Level 2 (Clear, 3-4): clear interpretation of the table with effects developed through to the environment. Level 1 (Basic, 1-2): basic interpretation with generic effects. AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The most damaging effect shown is the air freight. Figure 2 has green beans flown about 6,800 km from Kenya and asparagus flown about 10,200 km from Peru, and because aircraft emit far more carbon dioxide per tonne of cargo than any other mode, those two items carry a much larger carbon footprint than their weight suggests, adding to the greenhouse gases driving climate change. Distance alone is not the measure, though: the New Zealand apples travel 18,800 km, the furthest in the table, but they come by ship, which is efficient per tonne, so their transport emissions may be lower than the flown beans'. There are effects at the growing end too. Spanish tomatoes travelling 1,800 km are grown under plastic in a region where irrigation draws heavily on groundwater, so the cost is water depletion rather than fuel. The Norfolk carrots, at 190 km, have the smallest transport footprint, although keeping them until February needs refrigerated storage. The overall effect is a food supply whose emissions depend on mode and method as much as on miles.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The command word is 'suggest', so reasonable effects that follow from the table are credited — but they must follow from the table.","Sort the rows by how they travel before you write, not by how far they travel.","Two developed effects beat four one-line ones; make each effect end on the environment, not on the food."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, suggest why a water transfer scheme may lead to conflict between different interest groups. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1-3. The focus is conflict, so opposing viewpoints must be set against each other with the reasons for the difference developed from the extract. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"The sharpest conflict in Figure 4 is between the water company engineer and the farmer, and it is structural rather than a misunderstanding. The engineer is responsible for a region whose demand will outgrow supply within twenty years, so flooding one upland valley secures water for hundreds of thousands of households; the farmer loses his best land to that reservoir and has the rest of the farm cut in two. The benefit lands in one region and the cost lands in another, and the farmer receives none of the gain his loss creates, which is why compensation does not settle it: as he says, money buys fields but not four generations of a working farm. A second conflict runs between people who agree on the problem. The householder in the city region wants a reliable supply after a hosepipe ban, but objects to paying for a reservoir while the company loses water from leaking mains, and the wildlife trust officer makes the same argument from the other end: below the dam the river will run lower and warmer, harming salmon and trout, and moving water between catchments can spread invasive species, so leaks and meters should come first. Both are really disputing which solution is fair, not whether water is needed. Conflict is therefore built into transfer, because it asks a rural donor area to carry an environmental and social cost so that a distant urban area can keep its taps running.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Conflict needs two named parties who want different outcomes, so pair the speakers before you plan paragraphs.","For each pair, give both sides their own reason in their own terms rather than deciding who is right.","Two developed conflicts, each ending on why the two sides cannot both be satisfied, will outscore four opinions listed."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain why the UK's energy mix has changed since 2012. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1-3. Expect the change to be quantified from the graph and then explained by mechanism, not simply described. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Figure 3 shows the change clearly: coal falls from 40 per cent of UK electricity generation in 2012 to the foot of the scale in 2023, when its real share was about 1 per cent, while renewables rise from 10 to 45, a gain of 35 percentage points; gas edges up from 30 to 35 and nuclear slips from 20 to 15. Almost all of the share coal lost has gone to renewables. Coal fell because it became uneconomic and unacceptable at the same time: a price on carbon and tighter air quality rules added cost to every tonne burned, the stations were old, and the Government set a date for ending unabated coal generation, so operators closed plant rather than reinvest, the last at Ratcliffe-on-Soar in Nottinghamshire at the end of September 2024. Renewables rose because the cost of offshore wind fell steeply as turbines grew larger and long-term contracts guaranteed developers a price, so very large North Sea farms such as Hornsea 2, about 1.3 GW, came on line from 2022. Nuclear’s small decline is ageing: old reactors are closing faster than new ones are built. Gas has held and slightly increased its share because it is flexible and can be turned up when the wind drops, which a mix with 45 per cent renewables needs; its output has changed little while total generation has fallen, so the same output is a larger slice of the mix.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Half the credit here is for the graph, so the first job is to say what actually changed and by how much.","Then ask, for each source, what made it more expensive or cheaper to run than it was in 2012.","Explain the fall and the rise separately, and finish by accounting for the source that barely moved."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Energy)","3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (overview)"]},{"q":"Explain how the quality of water in UK rivers can be protected from pollution. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1-3. An understanding of what pollutes rivers should be shown, with at least one management strategy developed through to its effect on water quality. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"UK rivers are polluted mainly by nitrate and phosphate fertiliser washed off farmland, by sewage released through storm overflows during heavy rain, by run-off from roads, and by drainage from abandoned mines. Nutrients do the most widespread damage through eutrophication: they feed algae, the bloom blocks light so water plants die, bacteria decompose the dead material and use up the dissolved oxygen, and fish and invertebrates suffocate. Protection therefore works at the source first. Limiting when and how much fertiliser may be spread, and leaving uncultivated buffer strips between fields and watercourses, means run-off is intercepted before it reaches the river, so the nutrient load never arrives; planting and restored wetlands do the same job on a larger scale by slowing water and letting sediment settle. Second, the sewerage system is attacked directly: investment in treatment works and in storm tanks that hold the first flush of rainwater means fewer overflow discharges, and tighter permits with monitoring and prosecution by the Environment Agency give companies a financial reason to comply. Third, demand management helps quietly, because metering and water-efficient appliances reduce the volume passing through treatment works in the first place. No single measure is enough: farm run-off is diffuse and cannot be piped to a treatment works, so protecting quality depends on regulation, investment and land management acting together.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["You cannot explain the cure until you have named the disease — what actually gets into UK rivers?","Group the strategies by where they act: on the land, in the sewerage system, or on demand.","Develop one strategy fully through to cleaner water rather than listing five; the marks are for the chain, not the number."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)"]},{"q":"Explain the effects of the trend towards agribusiness on the UK countryside. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1-3. Expect an understanding of what agribusiness is, with effects on landscape, soil, water or employment developed through to their consequence. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Agribusiness means farming run as a large-scale commercial business: big fields, heavy machinery, high inputs of fertiliser and pesticide, contracts with supermarkets and very few workers. The arable estates of Lincolnshire, Cambridgeshire and Norfolk are the clearest UK example. The first effect is on the landscape. Large machines work most efficiently in large fields, so hedgerows and small woods have been removed; that takes away nesting sites and wildlife corridors, so farmland bird and insect numbers fall, and it removes the windbreaks that once sheltered the soil. The second effect follows from the first: bare, light or peaty soils left exposed after harvest are blown and washed away, and repeated passes by heavy machinery compact them, so water runs off rather than soaking in and flood risk downstream rises. The third effect reaches the rivers, because fertiliser applied at high rates does not all stay on the field: nitrate and phosphate run-off causes eutrophication, stripping oxygen from watercourses. The fourth is social: mechanisation has replaced farm workers, so rural employment falls, young people leave, and village shops, schools and bus services lose the customers that keep them open. Set against all of this is the reason it happened at all — yields per hectare are high and food is cheap and available all year, which is exactly what UK shoppers and supermarkets demand.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Every effect in this answer follows from one thing: what agribusiness actually changes on the ground.","Follow one physical chain (field size to soil) and one human chain (machinery to jobs) rather than mixing them.","An explanation that only attacks agribusiness is incomplete; the last move is to say what it delivers."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)"]},{"q":"'Transferring water from areas of surplus to areas of deficit is the best way of matching water supply and demand in the UK.' Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, evaluate this view. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1-3. The focus is the judgement, so the opposing views in Figure 4 must be analysed and set against named schemes and the alternatives of leakage reduction and demand management, ending with a statement of how far transfer is best. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Figure 4 shows why transfer is wanted at all. The water company engineer's region will see demand outgrow supply within twenty years because new homes are going up there faster than anywhere in the country and the summers are drier, and saving water in a dry region will not make it rain there. The schemes that exist bear this out: Kielder Water in Northumberland, completed in 1982 and holding about 200 million cubic metres, stores winter rain and releases it into the River North Tyne so that far more can be abstracted downstream in a dry summer, and the Elan Valley aqueduct has carried Welsh water to Birmingham by gravity since 1904. Judged on volume and reliability, transfer is the most effective tool the UK has. Judged on cost and damage it is the worst, and the other three speakers say so. The farmer loses his best land and the rest cut in two, which compensation cannot buy back; the wildlife trust officer warns that the river below the dam will run lower and warmer, harming salmon and trout, and that moving water between catchments can spread invasive species; the householder objects to paying while roughly a fifth of mains water leaks away. To a large extent transfer is necessary, because the mismatch between a wet north-west and a crowded, dry south-east is permanent. But 'best' is the wrong word: fixing leaks and metering are cheaper and harm nobody, so transfer should carry only what they leave.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'Evaluate' is asking how far transfer beats the other ways of closing the gap, so the alternatives have to appear.","Six marks pay for two developed paragraphs and a verdict, not four: one for what transfer achieves, one for what it costs.","Four of the six marks are for working the resource, so answer a named speaker from Figure 4 in each paragraph and let the last sentence say what you judged them against."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)","3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (overview)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, evaluate the economic and environmental issues associated with the exploitation of energy sources in the UK. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1-3. Expect the shift shown in Figure 3 to be used as evidence, named UK sources weighed economically and environmentally, and a substantiated judgement. No credit for simply describing the problems. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Figure 3 dates the change: coal falls from 40 per cent of UK electricity generation in 2012 to the foot of the scale in 2023, when its real share was about 1 per cent, while renewables rise from 10 to 45. Coal is where the economic and the environmental arguments converged. Burning it emitted the carbon dioxide the UK is legally committed to cutting, and a price on carbon and tighter air quality rules added cost to every tonne, so operators closed old plant rather than reinvest; the last station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar, closed at the end of September 2024. Shale gas was offered as the economic answer, since domestic gas would cut imports and support jobs, but fracking uses large volumes of water, risks contaminating groundwater and caused tremors at Preston New Road near Blackpool, the largest magnitude 2.9 in August 2019, after which the Government imposed a moratorium in England in November 2019: here the environmental objection decided the economics. The renewables bar Figure 3 shows rising reverses the pattern. Offshore wind such as Hornsea 2, about 1.3 GW and completed in 2022, costs a great deal to build and produces nothing when the wind drops, which is why the gas bar edges up from 30 to 35 as back-up, but its running costs and emissions are near zero and it makes work in Hull. Weighed on both counts together, fossil fuels now lose twice over; the issue left is paying the capital cost while keeping supply reliable.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Evaluate means judging with evidence on both sides, so no source should appear without both an economic and an environmental point.","Six marks will not hold five sources: take the two bars Figure 3 moves furthest and add a third in a sentence, each with a real UK place attached.","Quantify the change from the graph once, early, then spend the rest of the answer explaining and judging it."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Energy)"]},{"q":"'The UK's growing demand for food creates more problems for other countries than it does for the UK.' Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, evaluate this view. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1-3. Expect both sides developed from the table — the exporting countries and the UK itself — with the reasons for the difference analysed and a judgement on which problems are the greater. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Figure 2 shows a February basket in which only the carrots are British, travelling 190 km from Norfolk, while the green beans come 6,800 km from Kenya and the asparagus 10,200 km from Peru, both by air. That pattern is the demand the statement is about, and it makes problems at both ends. Abroad, the trade earns foreign currency and employs large numbers of people, many of them women, but land and irrigation water in Kenya are committed to a crop eaten four thousand miles away rather than to staples for local markets, and growers depend on a handful of powerful buyers who set the price and can switch supplier. In the UK the same demand lengthens the supply chain: air freight emits far more carbon dioxide per tonne-kilometre than the ship carrying the New Zealand apples, so the carbon footprint of the basket rises for a country legally committed to cutting emissions, and importing about 35 to 40 per cent of its food leaves it exposed to harvest failures and trade disruption abroad. On balance the statement is only half right. The overseas problems are the more severe, because they touch food security, water and livelihoods for people with far fewer alternatives, whereas the UK's are environmental and economic and fall on a rich society that can absorb them. But both come from the same demand for cheap, varied food all year round.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'Evaluate' wants both sides developed and then judged, so decide early what the two sides actually are here.","Figure 2 gives you the overseas side in one row and the UK side in another; carry a named country and a distance from the table into each paragraph.","The conclusion must compare the two kinds of problem rather than counting them; ask who can most easily cope with what they get."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)","3.2.3.1 — Food, water and energy are fundamental to human development"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, assess the significance of food, water and energy supply for economic and social well-being in low income countries. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1-3. Expect the table to be used as the way in to the three supplies, each linked to health, education or income, and an informed judgement about which matters most or how they interact. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Figure 2 shows green beans flown 6,800 km from Kenya and asparagus 10,200 km from Peru, and both rows rest on all three supplies at once: land and a reliable harvest, irrigation water in countries with a marked dry season, and the energy that chills the crop and flies it. That is why the three decide well-being. Food matters first, because everything else depends on the body: a diet below what an adult needs leaves people too weak to farm or work and, in children, damages growth and concentration so that schooling is wasted, and because most people in low income countries work in agriculture a poor harvest cuts income and food together. Water decides health and time: unsafe supplies spread diarrhoeal disease, which kills young children, while the hours spent walking to a distant source fall on women and girls, taking girls out of school. Energy multiplies the other two and creates income, refrigerating vaccines, lighting classrooms after dark and running the pumps that irrigate and the plants that pack the beans in Figure 2, so that value is added locally instead of leaving as raw produce. In the immediate term food and water are decisive, because malnutrition and unsafe water kill. Over the longer term energy changes the trajectory. Their real significance is their interdependence: energy pumps water, water grows food, and food gives people the strength to work.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Assess means weighing the parts and giving an overall judgement, so you need a basis for saying one matters more than another.","Use Figure 2 as the way in: the two air-freighted rows are grown in low income countries and need land, water and power long before they reach the plane.","Push each resource through to health, education or income rather than stopping at the shortage, then let the conclusion turn on how the three depend on one another."],"codes":["3.2.3.1 — Food, water and energy are fundamental to human development"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which parts of the UK have a water deficit, and why?","a":"The south and east of England: rainfall is lowest there (under 700 mm a year around London and East Anglia) while population and housebuilding are greatest, so demand exceeds supply."},{"q":"What are food miles?","a":"The distance food travels from where it is produced to where it is eaten."},{"q":"When did Britain's last coal-fired power station close, and what was it called?","a":"The end of September 2024; Ratcliffe-on-Soar in Nottinghamshire."},{"q":"Name the largest renewable source of electricity in the UK.","a":"Wind, mostly offshore."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Food miles","back":"The distance food travels from where it is produced to where it is eaten. The further it comes, and the more of that distance is flown, the larger the carbon footprint of the meal.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)","open":false},{"front":"Agribusiness","back":"Farming run as a large-scale commercial business: big fields, heavy machinery, agrochemicals, supermarket contracts and few workers. The arable estates of Lincolnshire, Cambridgeshire and Norfolk are the classic UK case.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)","open":false},{"front":"Local sourcing of food","back":"Buying food produced nearby, through farm shops, markets or box schemes. It cuts transport emissions and keeps money local, but the range is narrow and out-of-season UK produce may need heated glasshouses.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)","open":false},{"front":"High-value food exports from low income countries","back":"Crops grown for rich-country supermarkets, such as Kenyan green beans and cut flowers flown to the UK. They earn foreign currency and employment, but use land and water that could feed local people.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Food)","open":false},{"front":"Water surplus and water deficit","back":"Surplus: supply exceeds demand, as in the wet, thinly populated north and west of the UK. Deficit: demand exceeds supply, as in the drier, crowded south and east of England.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)","open":false},{"front":"Water transfer","back":"Moving water by pipeline, aqueduct or regulated river from a catchment with a surplus to one with a deficit. The Elan Valley aqueduct has carried Welsh water to Birmingham by gravity since 1904.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)","open":false},{"front":"Kielder Water","back":"Reservoir in Northumberland, completed 1982, about 200 million cubic metres, the UK's largest. It regulates the River North Tyne so more can be abstracted downstream, and a tunnel from Riding Mill carries water to the Wear and Tees.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)","open":false},{"front":"Eutrophication","back":"Nitrates and phosphates washed off farmland feed algae; the bloom blocks light, plants die, bacteria decompose them and use up the dissolved oxygen, so fish and invertebrates suffocate.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)","open":false},{"front":"Three ways UK water quality is protected","back":"Buffer strips and fertiliser limits that intercept farm run-off; investment in sewage treatment works and storm tanks; monitoring, permits and prosecution by the Environment Agency.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Water)","open":true},{"front":"Energy mix","back":"The range of sources a country uses to generate energy. The UK's has shifted from coal to gas and renewables, and wind is now the largest renewable source of UK electricity.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Energy)","open":false},{"front":"The UK's electricity mix in 2012 and 2023","back":"2012: coal about 40 per cent, gas about 30, nuclear about 20, renewables about 10. 2023: renewables about 45 per cent, gas about a third, nuclear about an eighth, coal about 1.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Energy)","open":false},{"front":"Ratcliffe-on-Soar","back":"Britain's last coal-fired power station, in Nottinghamshire. It closed at the end of September 2024, ending coal-fired electricity generation in the UK.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Energy)","open":false},{"front":"Fracking in England","back":"Hydraulic fracturing for shale gas, mainly in the Bowland Shale of Lancashire. Tremors at Preston New Road near Blackpool, the largest magnitude 2.9 in August 2019, led to a moratorium in November 2019.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (Energy)","open":false},{"front":"The UK's resource position in three lines","back":"Food: about 60 to 65 per cent produced at home, the rest imported. Water: plentiful in the north and west, in deficit in the south and east. Energy: a net importer since about 2004.","code":"3.2.3.1 — The changing demand and provision of resources in the UK create opportunities and challenges (overview)","open":false},{"front":"Global inequality in resources","back":"Around 2 billion people lack safely managed drinking water, roughly 700 to 800 million face hunger, and of the order of 700 million have no electricity, most in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia.","code":"3.2.3.1 — Food, water and energy are fundamental to human development","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — written for this room. Rainfall and people in the UK: a two-panel sketch map.\nPanel A, average annual rainfall: over 2,000 mm shaded on the western Highlands of Scotland, the Lake District and Snowdonia, with the wettest valleys over 3,000 mm; 1,000 to 2,000 mm over much of Wales, the Pennines, northern England and the south-west; 700 to 1,000 mm over the Midlands and central southern England; under 700 mm over East Anglia, Essex, Kent and the London area.\nPanel B, population density: the densest shading covers London and the South East, with further dense patches at Birmingham, Manchester, Leeds and Glasgow; the sparsest shading covers the Scottish Highlands, mid-Wales, the Pennines and much of Northumberland.\nThe rainfall pattern and the population pattern are real; the shading classes are simplified and rounded for this room.","Figure 2 — written for this room; figures illustrative. A UK supermarket basket in February: where five items came from.\nFood | Country of origin | Usual transport | Approximate distance to the UK (km)\nGreen beans | Kenya | Air freight | 6800\nAsparagus | Peru | Air freight | 10200\nTomatoes | Spain | Lorry and ferry | 1800\nApples | New Zealand | Ship | 18800\nCarrots | United Kingdom (Norfolk) | Lorry | 190\nThe countries and the transport modes are typical of a UK supermarket in winter; the distances are rounded straight-line figures and are illustrative, not measured freight routes.","Figure 3 — written for this room; shares rounded to the nearest 5 per cent. The UK's changing electricity mix: a bar graph. Horizontal axis: four sources — coal, gas, nuclear, renewables. Vertical axis: share of UK electricity generation, 0 to 50 per cent, marked every 10 per cent. Two bars are plotted for each source, 2012 and 2023.\nCoal | 2012: 40 | 2023: 0 (its real share was about 1 per cent)\nGas | 2012: 30 | 2023: 35\nNuclear | 2012: 20 | 2023: 15\nRenewables | 2012: 10 | 2023: 45\nNote printed under the graph: rounding to the nearest 5 per cent means the bars need not total exactly 100; the small remaining share is oil and other fuels; wind is the largest of the renewables; Britain's last coal-fired power station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar in Nottinghamshire, closed at the end of September 2024.","Figure 4 — written for this room; the speakers are invented but the arguments are typical of real schemes. Four views on a proposal to dam an upland valley in the north-west and pipe the water to a growing city region in the drier south-east.\nWater company engineer: 'Our region's demand will outgrow supply within twenty years. New homes are going up faster here than anywhere in the country and the summers are drier. This scheme guarantees water for hundreds of thousands of households.'\nFarmer in the valley: 'The reservoir will drown the best of my land and cut the rest in two. Compensation buys the fields; it does not buy back a farm my family has worked for four generations.'\nHouseholder in the city region: 'We had a hosepipe ban two summers ago and I want a supply I can rely on. But why should my bill pay for a new reservoir while the company loses so much water through leaking pipes?'\nWildlife trust officer: 'Below the dam the river will run lower and warmer, which is bad for salmon and trout, and moving water between catchments can spread invasive species. Fix the leaks and fit meters before flooding a valley.'"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 5-6) on the six-mark question about water transfer","items":["The question it belongs to: 'Transferring water from areas of surplus to areas of deficit is the best way of matching water supply and demand in the UK.' Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, evaluate this view. [6 marks] (AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks)","The paragraph: Figure 4's engineer makes the case for transfer, and the schemes that already exist show the claim is sound. The region's demand will outgrow supply within twenty years because new homes are going up there faster than anywhere in the country and the summers are drier, and no amount of saving water in a dry region will make it rain there. Kielder Water in Northumberland, completed in 1982, holds about 200 million cubic metres, more than any other UK reservoir, and it is used to regulate the River North Tyne: water is released from the dam so that far more can be abstracted downstream, and a tunnel from Riding Mill on the Tyne carries supplies across to the Wear and on to the Tees. Because the reservoir stores winter rain falling on ground that receives well over 1,000 mm a year and lets it out through a dry summer, it converts an uneven supply into a reliable one for the whole North East, and the same principle stretched over a longer distance has taken Welsh water to Birmingham through the Elan Valley aqueduct, by gravity, since 1904. Judged on security of supply alone, transfer is the most effective tool the UK has — although this paragraph has judged it only on volume and reliability, and the farmer's drowned land and the wildlife officer's warmer river below the dam are still to be weighed.","Why it reaches Level 3 (Detailed, 5-6): the descriptor asks for thorough analysis of the resource, and the paragraph deconstructs the engineer's case rather than quoting it — it takes the twenty years and the drier summers and tests them against schemes that already do the job; the knowledge brought to the resource is specific and accurate, with a named reservoir, a date, a capacity, a named aqueduct and its date; the understanding is a mechanism rather than a label, since it says how winter storage released in summer allows abstraction to rise downstream; and the evaluation is well-focused and balanced, stating the criterion it has tested and naming the two speakers still to be weighed. The specialist terms (abstraction, regulate, catchment, surplus, deficit) are the ones the Detailed descriptor expects an answer at this level to use."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1-2) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The paragraph: Water transfer is good because some places have too much water and some places do not have enough. In the north it rains a lot but in the south there are more people so they need more water for their houses. So the water gets moved to where the people are. There are big reservoirs like Kielder that store the water up. Some people do not like reservoirs because they flood things and they cost a lot of money to build. So transfer is quite good but it does have some problems.","The descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic, 1-2) — limited analysis of the resource and limited development of the issues. Figure 4 is never touched: not one of the four speakers is answered, so the four AO3 marks for deconstructing the resource cannot be reached. One place is mentioned but nothing is attached to it: no date, no capacity, no aqueduct, no figure for rainfall or population. The understanding stops at 'it rains a lot', with no mechanism for how storage in winter secures supply in summer. The judgement is a single simple point, 'quite good but it does have some problems'.","The descriptor it misses: Level 3 (Detailed, 5-6) asks for thorough analysis of the resource and a well-focused, balanced evaluation of the issues. The repair is mechanical rather than stylistic: answer a named speaker in each paragraph, attach one figure and one place name to every claim, add the process that makes the claim work, and name what the judgement is being made against. 'Reservoirs flood things' becomes 'the village of Capel Celyn was flooded in the 1960s to supply Liverpool', and the answer moves two levels.","One more difference worth copying: the weaker paragraph never uses the words surplus, deficit, catchment or abstraction, so it also loses ground on the specialist terminology a Detailed answer is expected to use."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the terms of the room, and the global picture","items":["Resource: anything people need and use — here food, water and energy. Its significance is measured by what its absence does to health, education and income, which is why the specification calls these three fundamental to human development.","How the three lock together: growing food needs water, treating and pumping water needs energy, and producing energy needs water. Progress in one is limited by the weakest of the three.","Global inequality in supply: around 2 billion people do not have safely managed drinking water, roughly 700 to 800 million face hunger, and of the order of 700 million have no access to electricity — most of them in sub-Saharan Africa and parts of South Asia.","Global inequality in consumption: high income countries use many times more energy and water per person than low income countries, so the gap in consumption is wider than the gap in supply. Around 2,000 calories a day is the figure usually used for an adult's needs.","Surplus and deficit: a surplus is where supply exceeds demand, a deficit where demand exceeds supply. Both are relative — a wet country can hold a deficit if its demand is concentrated in its driest corner, which is exactly the UK's position.","Security: food, water or energy security means reliable and affordable access to enough of it. Insecurity usually shows first in price, then in rationing, then in conflict.","Carbon footprint: the total greenhouse gases caused by an activity, counted as carbon dioxide equivalent. For food it includes growing, processing, transport, storage and retail — which is why transport alone never settles the argument."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — food in the UK","items":["Self-sufficiency: the UK produces about 60 to 65 per cent of the food it consumes, and about three-quarters of the kinds of food its climate allows it to grow; the remainder is imported.","Food miles: the distance food travels from producer to consumer. Mode matters more than distance, because air freight emits far more carbon dioxide per tonne-kilometre than shipping.","High-value food exports: Kenya air-freights green beans and cut flowers to the UK. The trade earns foreign currency and employs large numbers of people, many of them women, but commits land and irrigation water to crops eaten abroad, and concerns have been raised about water use around Lake Naivasha.","All-year demand: UK strawberries ripen outdoors roughly from June to September, so winter supplies come from Spain, Morocco or further afield; Spanish salad crops are grown under a vast expanse of plastic greenhouses irrigated from groundwater.","Organic produce: grown without manufactured pesticides and fertilisers, and certified by bodies such as the Soil Association. Organic land is only a few per cent of UK farmland and yields are usually lower, so the produce costs more.","Agribusiness: farming run as a large-scale commercial business — large fields, heavy machinery, high chemical inputs, supermarket contracts and few workers. The arable counties of Lincolnshire, Cambridgeshire and Norfolk are the clearest UK example.","Effects of agribusiness: cheaper, more reliable food and higher yields per hectare, set against hedgerow removal and habitat loss, soil erosion and compaction, nitrate and phosphate run-off into rivers, and falling rural employment.","Local sourcing: farm shops, farmers' markets and vegetable box schemes cut transport and keep money in the local economy, but the range is narrower and food grown out of season in heated UK glasshouses can carry a larger footprint than the imported equivalent."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — water in the UK","items":["Rainfall: the western uplands receive over 2,000 mm a year, with the wettest Lake District valleys over 3,000 mm; East Anglia and the London area receive under 700 mm.","Deficit and surplus: the south and east of England run a deficit because rainfall is lowest where population, housebuilding and irrigated farming are greatest; the north and west hold a surplus because heavy rain falls on thinly populated uplands.","Demand: the average person in the UK uses about 140 litres of water a day. Demand has risen with population growth, smaller households, dishwashers, washing machines, power showers and garden watering.","Leakage: roughly a fifth of the water put into the mains is lost to leaks before it reaches a tap, which is why mending pipes is always the first comparison made against building new supply.","Kielder Water, Northumberland: completed in 1982, about 200 million cubic metres, the UK's largest reservoir by capacity. It regulates the River North Tyne, releasing water so that more can be abstracted downstream, and a tunnel from Riding Mill on the Tyne carries water across to the Wear and on to the Tees.","Elan Valley aqueduct: has carried water about 120 km from reservoirs in mid-Wales to Birmingham by gravity since 1904. Lake Vyrnwy, also in Wales, supplies Liverpool.","The cost of transfer: the village of Capel Celyn in north Wales was flooded in the 1960s to create a reservoir supplying Liverpool, and the resentment it caused is remembered still.","Water quality: the main pollutants are nitrates and phosphates washed off farmland, sewage released through storm overflows, run-off from roads and drainage from abandoned mines. Nutrients cause eutrophication — algae bloom, block the light, then decay, and decomposition strips the oxygen, killing fish.","Pollution management: monitoring, permits and prosecution by the Environment Agency in England; investment in treatment works and storm tanks; limits on fertiliser use with uncultivated buffer strips beside watercourses; wetlands and planting to intercept run-off; metering to cut the volume needing treatment."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — energy in the UK","items":["Energy mix: the range of sources a country uses. In 2012 coal generated about 40 per cent of UK electricity; by 2023 renewables generated about 45 per cent, gas about a third, nuclear about an eighth and coal about 1 per cent.","The end of coal: Britain's last coal-fired power station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar in Nottinghamshire, closed at the end of September 2024. The last deep coal mine, Kellingley in North Yorkshire, closed in December 2015.","North Sea oil and gas: UK production peaked around 1999 and has fallen since. The UK has been a net energy importer since about 2004, so prices follow world markets rather than domestic supply.","Wind: wind is the largest renewable source of UK electricity. Hornsea 2, off the Yorkshire coast, was the world's largest operating offshore wind farm when completed in 2022, at about 1.3 GW; blades for offshore turbines are made in Hull.","Biomass: Drax power station in North Yorkshire converted units from coal to wood pellets, most of them imported, which raises questions about transport emissions and about how quickly the wood is replaced.","Nuclear: most UK reactors are old and closing. Hinkley Point C in Somerset, about 3 GW when finished, is under construction, over budget and behind schedule; Sizewell C in Suffolk is planned.","Shale gas: fracking in the Bowland Shale of Lancashire was halted after tremors at Cuadrilla's Preston New Road site near Blackpool, the largest measuring magnitude 2.9 in August 2019; a moratorium on fracking in England followed in November 2019, was lifted briefly in September 2022 and was reinstated the following month.","The trade-off to carry into every answer: each source pairs an economic argument — jobs, tax revenue, security of supply, price — with an environmental one — carbon emissions, landscape and habitat, waste, decommissioning."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, evaluate the economic and environmental issues associated with the exploitation of energy sources in the UK.' [6 marks]","items":["Decode the command word first: 'evaluate' means judging how well something works with the evidence on both sides, so every paragraph carries an economic point and an environmental one and ends with a verdict. The mark schemes are blunt about the trap — there is no credit for simply describing the problems.","Know where the marks sit: this is the Section C levelled question, so AO2 = 2 marks for understanding how exploiting each source works and AO3 = 4 marks for analysing Figure 3 and weighing the issues. Unsupported opinion and unused knowledge both score nothing, and a graph left undescribed forfeits the larger half of the marks.","Budget the space before choosing examples: six marks buy two developed paragraphs and a conclusion, so two sources carry the answer and the rest get a sentence. Quantify Figure 3 once at the start — coal from 40 per cent in 2012 to the foot of the scale in 2023, renewables from 10 to 45 — and let that change set up the argument.","Paragraph 1, fossil fuels in decline, which is the coal bar reaching the floor. Example: North Sea oil and gas (production peaked around 1999, the UK a net energy importer since about 2004) and the closure of Ratcliffe-on-Soar at the end of September 2024. Economic: tax revenue and skilled jobs, but ageing fields cost more and imports expose bills to world prices. Environmental: carbon dioxide and air pollution. Verdict: economics and environment now point the same way.","Paragraph 2, what replaced it, which is the renewables bar climbing from 10 to 45. Example: Hornsea 2 off the Yorkshire coast, about 1.3 GW, completed in 2022, with blades made in Hull. Economic: huge capital cost and intermittency needing gas back-up, which is why Figure 3's gas bar edges up from 30 to 35, set against falling costs and new industrial jobs. Environmental: no emissions in operation, but seabed disturbance and objections to turbines on the skyline. Verdict: the strongest option, with a real price attached.","One sentence, not a paragraph, for the rest: shale gas, refused in England after the magnitude 2.9 tremor at Preston New Road near Blackpool in August 2019 and the moratorium of November 2019; Hinkley Point C, about 3 GW, over budget and late; Drax burning imported wood pellets. Each buys its benefit dearly, and at six marks naming that is enough.","The evaluation thread that holds it together: ask the same question of every source — what does it cost, who pays, and what does it do to the environment? One criterion, used twice in full and once in passing, is what makes a conclusion follow rather than arrive.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly six minutes of writing plus a moment reading it back for spelling and specialist terms, because accurate writing and the right vocabulary are part of what separates a Detailed answer from a Clear one. No marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar are attached to this question — on Paper 2 the specimen puts those three marks on the urban issues question.","What the conclusion must say: not that there are advantages and disadvantages. It must rank the sources against the criterion chosen — for instance that the environmental cost of fossil fuels is now decisive, that renewables carry the highest capital cost but the lowest running cost and emissions, and that the UK's real issue is keeping supply reliable and affordable while the mix shifts, which is why gas remains and nuclear is still being built."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:16:32Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"The structural fix is right and checks out against the specimen: mark scheme 80352 shows Paper 2 question 3 (The challenge of resource management) running 03.1 (1, AO1), 03.2 (2, AO4), 03.3 (6, Levels 1-3, AO2 = 2 / AO3 = 4), 03.4 (1, AO4), 03.5 (1, AO4) and 03.6 (3, AO3) for 14 marks with no 9-mark part, and levels.md puts Paper 2's three SPaG marks on 01.8 in urban issues. The four rebuilt 6-markers sit on the 03.3 grid with the right level labels and AO split, their solutions are 231-247 words (all inside the 150-250 band, and questions[8] is now 239, down from the 273 I flagged last time), the model paragraphs are retitled Level 3 (Detailed, 5-6) and Level 1 (Basic, 1-2) with the weaker one now failing on exactly the four AO3 resource marks, the plan and worked example 4 are budgeted for six marks, and every claim the new solutions make about Figures 2, 3 and 4 was recounted and holds (190 km Norfolk carrots against 6,800 km Kenyan beans and 10,200 km Peruvian asparagus by air, the New Zealand apples by ship, coal 40 to the foot of the scale with its real 1 per cent noted, renewables 10 to 45, nuclear 20 to 15, and Figure 4's four speakers quoted as printed). Publishable; the remaining items are wording, not fact."}}},"3.2.3.2":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.2.3.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Food is one of the three optional topics that close Paper 2, and it is examined in a very predictable shape: Question 4 is worth eleven marks in four parts — one mark, then two marks, then two marks on the figures and on recall, and finally a six-mark question, marked in three levels, on how food security can be improved. That six-mark part is the longest answer the option ever asks for: there is no nine-mark question on Food and no SPaG marks anywhere in Section C, so the whole of the weighing up has to be done in a paragraph or two. None of those parts can be answered from general worry about hunger. Each one wants the board's own vocabulary (surplus, deficit, security, insecurity, undernutrition), a real mechanism carried through to its consequence, and two named examples you can spend figures on: a large scale agricultural development and a local scheme in an LIC or NEE. This room gives you the global pattern and why it exists, the impacts that make insecurity a source of conflict, the full list of strategies for increasing supply, the sustainable alternatives, and both named examples stocked with dates, places and figures — the Indus Basin Irrigation System in Pakistan and the sand dam scheme in Makueni County in Kenya.","idea":[{"h":"Surplus and deficit: where the food is, and why consumption keeps rising","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","t":"Food security means having reliable physical and economic access to enough safe, sufficient and nutritious food for an active, healthy life; food insecurity is the absence of that access. A country with a food surplus supplies more than its population needs, a country with a deficit supplies less and must import, receive aid or go without. The global pattern of calorie supply follows that split closely: North America, Western Europe and Australasia are supplied with over 3,400 kcal per person per day, the world average is around 2,900 kcal, and the poorest countries are below 2,000 kcal. Sub-Saharan Africa holds the largest regional share of the world's undernourished people and much the highest prevalence, with parts of South and South-East Asia the other main concentration. Demand is climbing from two directions at once. Population is the first: the world passed 8 billion people in 2022, and the fastest growth is in the countries that are already in deficit. Economic development is the second and it is often the stronger: as incomes rise people do not simply eat more calories, they switch to meat and dairy, and a kilogram of beef takes many kilograms of grain, a great deal of land and a great deal of water to produce. Supply, meanwhile, is held back by six factors the specification names: climate, technology, pests and disease, water stress, conflict and poverty. A top-level answer never lists those six. It picks two, shows how each works on a named place — unreliable rainfall in a semi-arid interior, conflict driving farmers off their land — and then shows how they combine, because poverty is what stops a country buying its way out of a bad harvest."},{"h":"What insecurity does to people and to the land","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","t":"The specification names five impacts and they are not a list of separate misfortunes: they are a chain, and an examiner rewards the chain. Undernutrition comes first — too few calories or too few nutrients over time, causing stunting and wasting in children, weakened immunity, deficiency diseases such as anaemia, and adults too weak to farm, which deepens the shortage that caused it. At its extreme this becomes famine, a widespread extreme shortage causing mass death, declared at Phase 5 of the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification scale. Rising prices spread the damage far beyond the area that lost its harvest, because when supply falls prices rise and the poorest, who spend the largest share of their income on food, are priced out first. That is how food insecurity becomes social unrest: food riots in Haiti in April 2008 helped force the prime minister from office, and competition for farmland and water can tip into open conflict. Meanwhile the land pays. Hungry households push cultivation onto steep or dry marginal ground, crop it with no fallow year and graze too many animals on it, so vegetation is stripped, bare soil is attacked by wind and heavy rain, topsoil is gullied away and yields fall further. A top-level answer makes that circularity explicit: soil erosion is both an impact of food insecurity and a cause of the next round of it, which is why insecurity is so hard to escape."},{"h":"Increasing supply: irrigation, soil-free growing, biotechnology and appropriate technology","code":"3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)","t":"Irrigation supplies water when the rain does not, so a field that carried one rain-fed harvest can carry two or three; drip systems deliver water straight to the root and lose far less to evaporation and seepage than flood or furrow irrigation. Hydroponics grows plants with their roots in nutrient-rich water instead of soil, and aeroponics suspends the roots in air inside a closed chamber and sprays them with a nutrient mist: both remove soil-borne disease, control the nutrients exactly, use a fraction of the water and can be stacked indoors, which is why they suit urban and desert settings. The green revolution of the 1960s and 1970s raised yields by combining high-yielding varieties such as IR8 rice, released by the International Rice Research Institute in the Philippines in 1966, with fertiliser, pesticides and irrigation. The new green revolution keeps the yields but tries to avoid the costs, adding water harvesting, soil care, local knowledge and crop varieties bred or engineered to resist drought and pests — insect-resistant Bt crops, or Golden Rice, engineered to carry beta-carotene against vitamin A deficiency. Against all of that sits appropriate technology: cheap, locally made and locally repaired equipment matched to the money and skills of the people using it, such as stone lines laid along the contour, treadle pumps and sand dams. A top-level answer never treats these as a menu. It argues that the right strategy depends on who is farming — the Indus Basin Irrigation System commands about 14 million hectares of canal-served land but needs a state to build it, while a sand dam needs only a village — and it carries one strategy through to its consequences, good and bad."},{"h":"A sustainable food future: growing differently, and losing less","code":"3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (sustainable resource future)","t":"Sustainability asks a different question from supply: not how much can be grown, but how much can be grown without spending the soil, water and species the next harvest needs. Organic farming refuses manufactured fertilisers and pesticides and relies on rotation, manure, compost and biological pest control, which protects soil and water but usually gives lower yields and higher prices. Permaculture goes further and designs the whole farm to copy a natural ecosystem, with perennial crops, mixed planting, and water and nutrients recycled on site so almost nothing is bought in. Urban farming — allotments, community gardens, rooftop plots and vertical farms — uses waste ground inside cities, cuts the distance food travels and puts fresh produce and income where the people are. Fish and meat from sustainable sources means quotas, mesh-size limits, no-take zones and responsible aquaculture, and it also means eating less meat, since grain fed to animals feeds far fewer people than grain eaten directly. Eating seasonal food avoids the energy burned on heated greenhouses, cold storage and air freight. The largest single gain, though, is waste: roughly a third of all the food produced for people is lost or wasted, mostly after harvest in low-income countries and mostly in shops and homes in rich ones, so better storage and smaller portions release food with no extra land at all. A top-level answer uses that comparison as its judgement: it asks what each route costs, and it says plainly that the cheapest food in the world is the food already grown and not thrown away."}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing a distribution from a world map (2 marks)","setup":"Figure 2 — World map described for this room: the percentage of the population that is undernourished, shaded in four classes. The class boundaries are the ones the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization uses on its hunger maps, and the countries placed in each class show the broad, well-documented pattern rather than a reading for one named year.\nClass 4, the darkest shading, 25% and over: the Central African Republic, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Chad, South Sudan, Somalia, Zimbabwe, Mozambique and Madagascar in Africa, together with Yemen and Afghanistan in Asia, Haiti in the Caribbean and North Korea in east Asia.\nClass 3, 15% to 24.9%: Ethiopia, Tanzania, Angola, Nigeria, Pakistan and Guatemala.\nClass 2, 5% to 14.9%: India, Bangladesh, Indonesia, the Philippines, Iraq, Peru, Colombia, Egypt and South Africa.\nClass 1, the palest shading, under 5%: the whole of North America and Europe, Russia, China, Japan, Australia, New Zealand, Brazil, Argentina, Chile, Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Turkey and Saudi Arabia.\n\nThe question: Using Figure 2, describe the distribution of countries in which 25% or more of the population is undernourished. [2 marks] (AO4 = 2 marks). The mark scheme adds: no credit for listing names of countries, and no credit for describing the areas with low levels of undernourishment.","steps":["Look for the shape the darkest shading makes rather than reading the list: it forms one broad band right across the centre of Africa, so the opening sentence is that most of the worst-affected countries lie in sub-Saharan Africa.","Give that band a direction, which is what turns a statement into a distribution: it runs from Chad and South Sudan in the north, crosses the equator in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and reaches Zimbabwe and Mozambique in the south-east.","Take the second mark by adding detail from the map to the pattern you have already described, noting the two African countries that fall outside the band — Somalia out on the Horn, and the island of Madagascar — and that the only countries in the same class beyond Africa are scattered and isolated: Yemen, Afghanistan, Haiti and North Korea.","Check the proportion your two sentences imply, because a distribution has to be weighted as well as located: eight of the twelve countries in the darkest class are African, so sub-Saharan Africa has to carry the main statement and everything outside it is an exception, not half the answer."],"cues":["Where on the map is the darkest shading concentrated?","What word would turn that statement into a description of a distribution?","What would lift a basic description into a developed one here?","How many of the darkest-shaded countries are actually in Africa, and what should that do to the balance of your answer?"]},{"title":"Reading and then explaining a calorie supply table (2 marks and 4 marks)","setup":"Figure 1 — Data table written for this room: average daily calorie supply per person, selected countries. The values are rounded to the nearest 100 kilocalories and are illustrative of the well-documented global pattern rather than a reading for one named year.\nCountry | Average daily calorie supply (kcal per person)\nUnited States of America | 3,700\nUnited Kingdom | 3,300\nBrazil | 3,200\nWorld average | 2,900\nIndia | 2,500\nKenya | 2,200\nChad | 2,000\nCentral African Republic | 1,800\n\nThe questions: (i) Using Figure 1, calculate the difference in average daily calorie supply between the United States of America and the Central African Republic. [2 marks] (AO4 = 2 marks) (ii) Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, suggest why average daily calorie supply varies between the countries shown. [4 marks] (AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks)","steps":["Pull out the two rows the calculation names before touching anything else, and notice they are also the extremes of the table: the United States of America at 3,700 kcal and the Central African Republic at 1,800 kcal.","Subtract in that order and write the working down, because one of the two marks is for showing it: 3,700 − 1,800 = 1,900 kcal per person per day, a gap larger than the whole daily supply of a person in the Central African Republic.","Turn to part (ii) and sort the table by wealth first: the only countries above the world average of 2,900 kcal are the United States, the United Kingdom and Brazil, so income is the first factor to suggest, because it buys fertiliser, machinery, irrigation and imports.","Add a factor of a different kind so the answer is not one idea twice: Chad and the Central African Republic sit in a semi-arid, landlocked interior with low and unreliable rainfall, and conflict in both has driven farmers off their land.","Finish by joining the two rather than listing them, since that is where the second application mark sits: a poor country cannot buy its way out of a failed harvest, which is why the same drought is an inconvenience in one place and a deficit in another."],"cues":["Which two rows does the calculation name?","Besides the answer, what does the examiner need to see on the page?","Which countries sit above the world average, and what do they have in common?","What second factor does the lower half of the table point to?","How do you turn two separate factors into one reasoned suggestion?"]},{"title":"Building a six-mark answer on improving food security","setup":"The question: Explain how food security can be improved. [6 marks] (AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks). The mark scheme expects an understanding of food security to be shown in the answer and at least one strategy developed in detail. What you have studied: the Indus Basin Irrigation System in Pakistan, a network of dams, barrages, link canals and distributaries on the Indus, the Jhelum and the Chenab whose canal-commanded area is about 14 million hectares of farmland in Punjab and Sindh; Mangla Dam on the Jhelum was completed in 1967 and Tarbela Dam on the Indus in 1976; the main crops are wheat, rice, cotton and sugar cane. You also know that IR8 rice was released in 1966 and that roughly a third of the food produced for people worldwide is lost or wasted.","steps":["Bank the definition in the opening sentence, because the mark scheme asks for it before it asks for anything else: food security is reliable physical and economic access to enough safe and nutritious food for an active, healthy life.","Choose one strategy and attach it to a named place immediately, since a generic answer is held down: irrigation, as carried out by the Indus Basin Irrigation System.","Explain the mechanism rather than describing the scheme: water held behind the two dams during the snowmelt and monsoon flood is released through the canals in the dry season, so land that could carry one rain-fed harvest carries wheat in winter and rice or cotton in summer.","Carry the chain through to security and not merely to supply, which is where the understanding marks are: two harvests a year make the supply steadier as well as larger, and a steadier supply holds prices down, which is the economic half of the definition.","Add a short second strategy so the answer has range at different scales: cutting the losses between field and market with sealed silos, drying and better roads, which turns food already grown into food actually eaten without needing another hectare."],"cues":["What does this mark scheme want stated before any strategy?","Why must the strategy be tied to a named place?","What does the reservoir actually change about when water is available?","The question says security, not supply — what extra link is needed?","What does a second, shorter strategy add to a six-mark answer?"]},{"title":"Reading a bar graph of food loss and waste (4 marks)","setup":"Figure 4 — Bar graph described for this room; the shares are illustrative of the well-documented contrast between country groups, not a measured data set. Title: where food is lost or wasted along the food chain, as a percentage of all the food lost or wasted in that group of countries. Horizontal axis: four stages of the chain — on the farm at harvest; in storage and transport; in processing and retail; in the home. Vertical axis: percentage of that group's total loss and waste, scaled 0 to 50. Two bars are plotted at each stage, one for a group of low-income countries and one for a group of high-income countries.\nStage | Low-income countries (%) | High-income countries (%)\nOn the farm at harvest | 25 | 10\nIn storage and transport | 40 | 10\nIn processing and retail | 25 | 30\nIn the home | 10 | 50\n\nThe question: Using Figure 4, suggest why reducing food loss and waste would need different action in low-income countries from high-income countries. [4 marks] (AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks)","steps":["Find the tallest bar in each set, because the contrast between them is the whole answer: the low-income group loses most in storage and transport, at 40%, while the high-income group wastes most in the home, at 50%.","Read the low-income shape as a cause: with another 25% lost on the farm at harvest, roughly two-thirds of the loss happens before the food reaches a shop, which points to grain rotting or eaten by pests, no cooling and slow journeys on poor rural roads.","Do the same for the other set: home waste at 50% is five times the 10% lost at harvest there, so nothing is failing technically — the food arrives in good condition and is thrown away, because it is cheap, over-bought and confusingly date-labelled.","Now answer the question asked, which is about action: investment in sealed metal silos, drying floors, cooling and rural roads for one group; smaller packs, clearer labelling and redistribution of unsold stock for the other.","Guard the claim before you stop, because each column of the graph adds up to the same total: these are shares of each group's own losses, not quantities, so the graph shows where each group loses its food and cannot show which group loses more of it — write that the stages differ, never that rich countries waste more."],"cues":["Which bar is tallest in each of the two sets?","What physical causes would produce losses at that stage?","Is waste in a rich country's kitchen a technical failure or a choice?","The question asks about action — what does each cause actually need?","Both columns of the graph add up to the same total — what does that stop you claiming?"]},{"title":"Turning a local scheme into a six-mark evaluation","setup":"The question: Evaluate the effectiveness of a local scheme in an LIC or NEE in increasing sustainable supplies of food. [6 marks] (AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks). There are no SPaG marks on this question, and six marks buys three short paragraphs, not an essay. What you have studied: sand dams in Makueni County, south-eastern Kenya. Makueni is semi-arid, with roughly 300 to 600 mm of rain a year in the lower areas falling in two short, unreliable seasons, and its rivers are seasonal sand rivers that run for a day or two after a storm. A community self-help group builds a reinforced concrete wall across a river bed and keys it into the bedrock; floods drop coarse sand behind it; water is then stored between the sand grains, which is roughly a quarter to a third of the volume behind the wall. Water is drawn from a hand pump or a shallow well. Households grow drought-tolerant sorghum, cowpeas and pigeon peas, and irrigate kale, tomatoes, onions and mango trees for sale at markets such as Wote. The county has about a million people.","steps":["Open by placing the scheme and naming the problem it answers, because the knowledge marks are for locations and processes: a semi-arid county whose rivers vanish within days, so water, not land, is the limit on farming.","Explain the mechanism in enough detail to earn the understanding marks: the wall traps coarse sand, and water held in the pore spaces between grains is shaded from the sun, so very little evaporates, it is filtered as it moves and livestock cannot foul it.","Convert the mechanism into food, which is what the question is actually about: dry-season water lets families irrigate vegetables and establish fruit trees, adding vitamins to a maize and bean diet and cash income, while contour trenches keep rain and topsoil on the slope.","Test the word sustainable deliberately rather than assuming it: local sand, stone and labour, no fuel, no imported parts and community maintenance mean the gains continue after any outside help stops.","Give the limits before the judgement, because an evaluation without them cannot reach the top level: one dam serves only nearby households, a badly sited wall silts up with fine mud instead of sand, and a multi-year drought still empties the store.","Close with a judgement that answers the word effectiveness and accepts the scale: highly effective and genuinely sustainable for its cost, but a way of making a dry district liveable rather than a cure for national food insecurity."],"cues":["What is the actual limiting factor on farming here?","Why does so little of the stored water evaporate?","The question says food — how does stored water become food?","Which features of the scheme make it sustainable, not just useful?","What would stop this answer reaching the top level?","What must the final sentence decide?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the definition of food security in the form the mark scheme expects.","a":"Having physical and economic access to enough safe, sufficient and nutritious food to meet dietary needs and food preferences for an active and healthy lifestyle."},{"q":"Name the six factors the specification lists as affecting food supply.","a":"Climate, technology, pests and disease, water stress, conflict and poverty."},{"q":"Why is soil erosion both an impact of food insecurity and a cause of more of it?","a":"Hungry households cultivate marginal land without fallow and overgraze it, so vegetation is lost and topsoil is stripped by wind and rain; the thinner soil then gives lower yields, which deepens the insecurity that caused the erosion."},{"q":"What is the difference between hydroponics and aeroponics?","a":"Hydroponics grows the plant with its roots in nutrient-rich water; aeroponics suspends the roots in air in a closed chamber and sprays them with a nutrient mist. Neither uses soil."},{"q":"Why does eating less meat release more food than eating less of anything else?","a":"Animals are fed grain, and a kilogram of meat takes many kilograms of grain plus large amounts of land and water, so grain eaten directly feeds far more people than the same grain fed to livestock."}]},"room":{"facts":["Food security is having physical and economic access to enough safe, sufficient and nutritious food to meet dietary needs and food preferences for an active and healthy lifestyle. A surplus means supply exceeds need; a deficit means it falls short.","The global pattern: over 3,400 kcal per person per day across North America, Western Europe and Australasia; a world average of roughly 2,900 kcal; under 2,000 kcal in the poorest countries. Sub-Saharan Africa holds the largest regional share of the world's undernourished people, with parts of South and South-East Asia the other main concentration. Between 600 and 700 million people are undernourished worldwide: about 700 million at the 2022 peak, and lower since.","Consumption is rising for two reasons: population, which passed 8 billion in 2022, and economic development, which shifts diets towards meat and dairy that need far more grain, land and water per kilogram.","Six factors affect food supply: climate, technology, pests and disease, water stress, conflict and poverty. Agriculture takes about 70% of the world's freshwater withdrawals.","Five impacts of food insecurity: famine, undernutrition, soil erosion, rising prices and social unrest. Famine is declared at Phase 5 of the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification scale; food riots in Haiti in April 2008 helped force the prime minister from office.","Indus Basin Irrigation System, Pakistan: the world's largest continuous irrigation system, with a canal-commanded area of about 14 million hectares in Punjab and Sindh; extended after the Indus Waters Treaty of 1960; Mangla Dam on the Jhelum completed 1967, Tarbela Dam on the Indus completed 1976; wheat, rice, cotton and sugar cane; it underpins the food supply of a country of about 240 million people, where agriculture employs roughly 40% of the workforce.","Makueni County, Kenya: semi-arid, roughly 300 to 600 mm of rain a year in the lower areas, about a million people. Sand dams store water between the grains of sand trapped behind a concrete wall — about a quarter to a third of the volume behind the wall — so little evaporates, and communities build and maintain them from local materials.","The green revolution began in the 1960s with high-yielding varieties such as IR8 rice, released in 1966 by the International Rice Research Institute in the Philippines, plus fertiliser, pesticides and irrigation. Roughly a third of all food produced for people is lost or wasted."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, state the country with the lowest average daily calorie supply per person. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for the correct country: the Central African Republic. No credit if more than one country is named. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"The Central African Republic, at 1,800 kcal per person per day. That is the smallest value in the table, 200 kcal below Chad, the next lowest, and just under half the United States of America's 3,700 kcal.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The country names are only labels; the comparison you need is in the second column.","Set all seven country values against each other rather than reading down the table in order.","The answer is one country name written on its own, with no reason attached."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, calculate the difference in average daily calorie supply between the United States of America and the Central African Republic. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer, 1,900 kcal per person per day; one mark for an indication of the calculation, eg 3,700 − 1,800. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"3,700 − 1,800 = 1,900 kcal per person per day. The gap on its own is larger than the entire daily supply of a person in the Central African Republic, and it means the United States figure is more than twice theirs.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two rows of the table are named in the question; find them before doing anything else.","A difference is one value taken from the other, and the examiner wants to see which way round you did it.","Set the subtraction out in full with its units, not just the number at the end."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, describe the distribution of countries in which 25% or more of the population is undernourished. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic description of the distribution shown on the map, eg most lie in sub-Saharan Africa; a second mark for a developed point using detail from the map, eg the band runs from Chad and South Sudan in the north, crosses the equator in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and reaches Zimbabwe and Mozambique in the south-east. No credit for listing names of countries or for describing the palest class. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Most of the countries in the highest class lie in sub-Saharan Africa, where they form one broad band across the centre of the continent. That band runs from Chad and South Sudan in the north, crosses the equator in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and reaches south-eastwards to Zimbabwe and Mozambique. Two more African countries sit outside it: Somalia, out on the Horn in the east, and Madagascar, the large island off the south-east coast. Outside Africa the pattern is scattered rather than continuous, with only four isolated countries in the same class.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The examiner wants where they are, not which they are.","Find the shape the darkest shading makes first, then give that shape a direction.","Two sentences: one for the main concentration, one that adds detail from the map to it."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"State two causes of food insecurity. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for two stated causes, physical or human, eg drought or flood (1); crop or animal disease, including locust swarms (1); conflict reducing production (1); over-cultivation with no fallow, or overgrazing (1); poverty preventing investment in irrigation or fertiliser (1). AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"Drought, because a failed rainy season means the crop never grows and there is nothing to harvest. Conflict, because fighting drives farmers off their land, destroys stores and roads, and stops what is grown reaching a market.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The question says state, so no explanation is being paid for here.","Pick one physical cause and one human cause so the two answers cannot be treated as the same point.","Two short, separate sentences are all this tariff needs."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, state the stage of the food chain at which the greatest percentage of food is lost or wasted in low-income countries. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for the correct stage: in storage and transport. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"In storage and transport, at 40% of all the food lost or wasted in the low-income group. It is the tallest of that group's four bars and four times the 10% lost in the home.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Only one of the two sets of bars is being asked about.","Compare the four bars in that set against one another before reading off a label.","The answer is the name of a stage, not a percentage."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"Outline one way in which irrigation can increase food supply. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for stating the way, eg water is delivered to crops when rainfall is insufficient (1); a second mark for developing it, eg so land that carried one rain-fed harvest can be cropped twice a year (1). AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"Irrigation delivers water to the field when the rain does not, so crops can be grown right through a dry season instead of only during the rains. Land that supported a single rain-fed harvest can then carry two or even three harvests a year, and the yield is steadier as well, because a late or short rainy season no longer destroys the crop.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One way, developed once, is the whole of this answer.","The second mark is for what the water then allows the farmer to do.","Say what irrigation changes about when water is available, then what that changes about the harvest."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, suggest why average daily calorie supply varies between the countries shown. [4 marks]","a":"Level 2 (Clear, 3–4) applies knowledge to interpret the table, linking the values to wealth, climate, technology or conflict; Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) offers a limited interpretation. No credit for simply repeating figures from the table. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"The table sorts almost exactly by wealth. The only three countries above the world average of 2,900 kcal are the United States at 3,700, the United Kingdom at 3,300 and Brazil at 3,200, and wealth is what buys fertiliser, machinery, irrigation and imported food, so a poor harvest at home never becomes a shortage. The three lowest, Kenya at 2,200, Chad at 2,000 and the Central African Republic at 1,800, are far poorer, and poverty leaves farmers unable to invest in the very things that would raise their yields. Wealth is not the whole answer, though. Chad and the Central African Republic also lie in a semi-arid, landlocked interior where rainfall is low and unreliable and transport is slow and expensive, and conflict in both has driven farmers off their land and disrupted markets. The two causes work together rather than separately: a poor country cannot buy its way out of a failed harvest, which is why the same drought is an inconvenience in one country and a deficit in another.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Sort the table before you explain it — what do the top three have in common?","Two different kinds of reason will score better than the same reason twice.","Use a figure from the table as evidence for each reason you suggest."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, suggest why reducing food loss and waste would need different action in low-income countries from high-income countries. [4 marks]","a":"Level 2 (Clear, 3–4) interprets both sets of bars and links the different stages to different causes and therefore different action; Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) makes limited use of the graph. AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"In the low-income group the tallest bar is storage and transport at 40%, and with another 25% lost on the farm at harvest roughly two-thirds of the loss happens before the food ever reaches a shop. That points to physical causes the farmer does not choose: grain rotting or eaten by pests in heat and damp, no cooling, and slow journeys on poor rural roads. The action needed is therefore investment in equipment and infrastructure — sealed metal silos, drying floors, cold storage and better roads. In the high-income group the tallest bar is the home at 50%, five times the 10% lost at harvest there. Nothing in that is a technical failure; the food arrives in good condition and is then thrown away because it is cheap, over-bought, confusingly date-labelled or left on the plate. The action needed is behavioural and commercial rather than technical: smaller pack sizes, clearer labelling, and redistribution of unsold stock. The same total loss therefore has to be attacked at opposite ends of the chain.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Start from the tallest bar in each set; the contrast between them is the answer.","Ask what physical or human cause would produce a loss at that particular stage.","Finish each half with the action that cause would actually need."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"Explain how food security can be improved. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) shows thorough understanding of the interrelationships between environments and processes in the context of food security and demonstrates in detail how improvements provide a secure source of food; an understanding of food security should be indicated and at least one strategy developed. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Food security means reliable physical and economic access to enough safe and nutritious food for an active, healthy life, so improving it means making supply both larger and steadier. Irrigation does both. In the Indus Basin Irrigation System in Pakistan, water stored behind Tarbela Dam on the Indus and Mangla Dam on the Jhelum during the snowmelt and monsoon flood is released through barrages and canals in the dry season, so the roughly 14 million hectares of canal-commanded land in Punjab and Sindh can be cropped when the rain has stopped. Land that once carried a single rain-fed harvest now carries wheat in winter and rice or cotton in summer, which raises output and, just as importantly, means a weak monsoon no longer empties the national stores. Technology multiplies the effect: high-yielding varieties such as IR8 rice, released in 1966, together with fertiliser and pesticides, raise the yield of every irrigated hectare instead of needing new land. Improvement is not only about growing more, though. Roughly a third of the food produced for people worldwide is lost or wasted, most of it in low-income countries between field and market, so sealed metal silos, drying and better rural roads turn food already grown into food actually eaten — often more cheaply than watering new land.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The mark scheme wants one thing stated before any strategy appears.","One strategy developed through its mechanism beats four strategies named.","Security is not the same as supply — make sure something in your answer explains the difference."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)","3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Explain the impacts of food insecurity on people and on the environment. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) develops impacts on both people and the environment and shows thorough understanding of how they interrelate; expect undernutrition, famine, rising prices, social unrest and soil erosion. Max Level 2 for impacts on one of the two only. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"The first impacts fall on people. Undernutrition means too few calories or too few nutrients over a long period, so children are stunted and wasted, immunity falls and adults become too weak to farm, which deepens the shortage that caused it. At its extreme this becomes famine, a widespread extreme shortage causing mass death, declared at Phase 5 of the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification scale. Prices are the mechanism that spreads the damage far beyond the area that lost its harvest: when supply falls prices rise, and because the poorest spend the largest share of their income on food they are priced out first. That is how insecurity turns into social unrest — food riots in Haiti in April 2008 helped force the prime minister from office — and how competition for farmland and water can tip into open conflict. The environment then pays as well. Households short of food push cultivation onto steep or dry marginal land, crop it with no fallow year and graze too many animals on it, so vegetation is stripped, bare soil is attacked by wind and heavy rain and topsoil is gullied away. Yields then fall further, which is why the human and environmental impacts feed each other rather than sitting side by side.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The question names two targets; an answer that hits only one is capped.","Chain the impacts rather than listing them — each one should cause the next.","Price is the link that carries food insecurity beyond the place where the harvest failed."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain how a local scheme can increase sustainable supplies of food in an LIC or NEE. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) makes full use of the extract and explains in detail how the scheme raises food supply and why it is sustainable; Level 2 explains one of the two clearly. No credit for a generic account that ignores Figure 3. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Figure 3 describes a sand dam scheme in Makueni County in south-eastern Kenya, a semi-arid area where the seasonal rivers run for only a day or two after a storm. The self-help group's concrete wall across the river bed traps the coarse sand the flood carries, and water is then held in the spaces between the grains — roughly a quarter to a third of the volume behind the wall. Because the water sits inside the sand rather than in an open pond, very little evaporates in the heat and, as the farmer says, animals cannot foul it, which is why she can still pump water months into the dry season. That water is what turns subsistence into security: sorghum and cowpeas are kept because they stand the drought, but irrigated tomatoes and young mango trees add vitamins to the diet and an income at Wote market. The trenches dug along the contour on the slope above are what make the scheme sustainable rather than merely useful, because they slow the runoff so rain soaks in and soil stays on the field instead of silting the river. Everything is built from local sand, stone and labour and maintained by the group itself, so it keeps working without outside money or fuel.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two words in the question are doing different jobs: increase, and sustainable.","Every point should be traceable to something the farmer actually says in the extract.","Explain why the water does not evaporate before you explain what it grows."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"'The advantages of a large scale agricultural development outweigh its disadvantages.' Using a named example, to what extent do you agree? [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) weighs advantages against disadvantages using one named large scale agricultural development, carries at least one cost or benefit through its mechanism, and reaches a supported judgement; Level 2 (Clear, 3–4) gives specific and accurate knowledge of the scheme and sound understanding of both sides; Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) is limited or generic. A generic answer with no named scheme is held at Level 1. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"The Indus Basin Irrigation System in Pakistan is the world's largest continuous irrigation system: dams, barrages and canals on the Indus, the Jhelum and the Chenab, with a canal-commanded area of about 14 million hectares in Punjab and Sindh. It was extended after the Indus Waters Treaty of 1960; Mangla Dam was completed in 1967 and Tarbela Dam in 1976.\n\nThe advantages are large. Storing the snowmelt and monsoon flood and releasing it through the dry season allows double cropping — wheat in winter, rice or cotton in summer — on land that could otherwise carry one rain-fed harvest, so a weak monsoon no longer empties the national stores of a country of about 240 million people. The dams generate electricity as well, so the same water does two jobs.\n\nThe disadvantages, though, accumulate. Unlined canals lose water to seepage, which raises the water table until land is waterlogged; evaporation from bare fields then leaves salt at the surface, so waterlogging and salinisation have damaged millions of hectares. Silt steadily fills the reservoirs, and so little water now reaches the delta that the sea has pushed inland and killed mangroves.\n\nOn balance the advantages do outweigh the disadvantages, because nothing else could water a dry plain on that scale. But the agreement is conditional: the costs grow each year, so the system stays worth having only if canals are lined and drainage installed.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The command phrase asks how far, so part of the answer has to push back against the statement.","Sort the costs by whether they get worse with time — that sorting is your evaluation.","Locate the scheme and give it dates before you argue anything; the knowledge marks are paid for that."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of a local scheme in an LIC or NEE in increasing sustainable supplies of food. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) names a local scheme, explains in detail how it raises the food supply and why the gain lasts, and reaches a substantiated judgement with its limits acknowledged; Level 2 (Clear, 3–4) gives accurate knowledge of the scheme and sound understanding of how it works; Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) is limited or generic. No credit for large scale national schemes. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Makueni County in south-eastern Kenya is semi-arid: rainfall in the lower areas is generally only about 300 to 600 mm a year and falls in two short, unreliable seasons, and the rivers are seasonal sand rivers that run for a day or two after a storm and then vanish. Water, not land, is the limit on farming.\n\nA community self-help group builds a reinforced concrete wall across a sand river and keys it into the bedrock. The next floods drop their coarse sand behind it, and water is then stored in the pore spaces between the grains, roughly a quarter to a third of the volume held back. Because the water sits inside the sand it is shaded from the sun, so very little evaporates and livestock cannot foul it, and it is drawn by hand pump months into the dry season. Families irrigate kale, tomatoes and mango trees for sale at Wote alongside drought-tolerant sorghum and cowpeas, while contour trenches keep rain and topsoil on the slope.\n\nIt is sustainable because local sand, stone and labour and community maintenance mean no fuel, no imported parts and no continuing outside money, and because it improves the soil and groundwater it depends on. The limits are of scale: one dam serves only nearby households, and a multi-year drought still empties the store. It is highly effective for what it costs, but it makes a dry district liveable rather than curing national food insecurity.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Effectiveness has to be judged against something — decide what the scheme was trying to do first.","Sustainable is a separate claim from successful, so test it separately.","An evaluation that names no limits cannot reach the top level, so plan where the limits will go."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"Discuss the view that technology is the most important way of increasing the world's food supply. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) develops technological strategies and non-technological ones with named support and reaches a supported judgement between them; Level 2 (Clear, 3–4) shows sound understanding of both sides with some specific detail; Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) names strategies without development. Credit irrigation, HYVs, biotechnology, hydroponics and aeroponics on one side, and appropriate technology, waste reduction, diet and access on the other. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Technology has done more than anything else to raise the amount of food the world produces. The green revolution combined high-yielding varieties such as IR8 rice, released by the International Rice Research Institute in the Philippines in 1966, with fertiliser, pesticides and irrigation, and irrigation on the scale of the Indus Basin Irrigation System, whose canal-commanded area is about 14 million hectares, lets a dry plain be cropped twice a year instead of once. Hydroponics, aeroponics and biotechnology such as insect-resistant Bt crops push yields further still.\n\nHowever, the world already grows enough calories for everyone and people are still hungry, so production is not the only constraint. Roughly a third of all food produced for people is lost or wasted, so better storage and smaller portions release food without a single extra hectare. Poverty is the other constraint: a farmer who cannot afford seed, fertiliser or a pump cannot use high technology at all, which is why appropriate technology such as the sand dams built by community groups in Makueni County in Kenya often does more per pound spent. Technology can also spend the soil and water the next harvest needs, as the salinisation and depleted aquifers left by the first green revolution show.\n\nTechnology is therefore the most important single route to a larger supply, because nothing else has changed yield per hectare on that scale. But it is not sufficient: it matters most when it is matched to the people using it.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Discuss means both sides get developed, not one side and a sentence of doubt.","Decide early what would count as the alternative to technology, then argue against it fairly.","Two named examples pulling in different directions will do more work here than a list of methods."],"codes":["3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)","3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (sustainable resource future)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What two kinds of access does the definition of food security require people to have to food?","a":"Physical access and economic access — the food must be there, and they must be able to afford it."},{"q":"Name the two dams that store water for the Indus Basin Irrigation System, and the rivers they are on.","a":"Mangla Dam on the Jhelum, completed in 1967, and Tarbela Dam on the Indus, completed in 1976."},{"q":"In a sand dam, where exactly is the water stored, and why does so little of it evaporate?","a":"In the pore spaces between the grains of coarse sand trapped behind the wall; the sand covers and shades the water, so the sun cannot reach it."},{"q":"Roughly what share of the food produced for people in the world is lost or wasted, and where does most of the loss happen in a low-income country?","a":"About a third; most of it between the field and the market, in storage and transport."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Food security","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Having reliable physical and economic access to enough safe, sufficient and nutritious food to meet dietary needs and food preferences for an active, healthy life. Food insecurity is the absence of that access."},{"front":"Food surplus and food deficit","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"A surplus is where more food is produced or supplied than the population needs, giving security. A deficit is where supply falls short of need, giving insecurity and dependence on imports or aid."},{"front":"The global pattern of calorie supply","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Highest in North America, Western Europe and Australasia, above about 3,400 kcal per person per day; lowest in sub-Saharan Africa and parts of South Asia, with the poorest countries below 2,000 kcal. World average about 2,900 kcal."},{"front":"Why global food consumption is rising","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Rising population — the world passed 8 billion people in 2022 — and economic development: as incomes rise people eat more, and switch to meat and dairy, which need far more grain, land and water per kilogram."},{"front":"The six factors affecting food supply","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Climate; technology; pests and disease; water stress; conflict; poverty. Agriculture already takes about 70% of the world's freshwater withdrawals, so water stress bites first."},{"front":"Give one impact of food insecurity on people and one on the environment","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","open":true,"back":"On people: famine and undernutrition, with stunting and wasting in children; rising food prices that hit the poorest hardest; social unrest, as in the 2008 food riots. On the environment: soil erosion from over-cultivation and overgrazing."},{"front":"Undernutrition","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","open":false,"back":"Taking in too few calories or too few nutrients over time. It causes stunting, wasting, weakened immunity and deficiency diseases such as anaemia, and leaves adults too weak to farm, which deepens the shortage."},{"front":"Famine","code":"3.2.3.2 — Demand for food resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","open":false,"back":"A widespread, extreme shortage of food causing mass undernutrition and death, declared at Phase 5 of the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification scale. Usually follows drought, conflict or a collapse in access to food."},{"front":"Hydroponics and aeroponics","code":"3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"Hydroponics grows plants without soil, with the roots in nutrient-rich water. Aeroponics suspends the roots in air inside a closed chamber and sprays them with a nutrient mist. Both save water, exclude soil pests and allow indoor, stacked growing."},{"front":"The green revolution and the new green revolution","code":"3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"The 1960s green revolution raised yields with high-yielding varieties such as IR8 rice, fertiliser, pesticides and irrigation. The new green revolution keeps the yields but adds water harvesting, soil care, local knowledge and drought- or pest-resistant biotechnology."},{"front":"Appropriate technology","code":"3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"Technology matched to the money, skills and needs of local people: cheap, made and repaired locally, needing no fuel or imported parts. Sand dams, treadle pumps, contour stone lines and drip kits all qualify."},{"front":"The Indus Basin Irrigation System, Pakistan","code":"3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"The world's largest continuous irrigation system: dams, barrages and canals on the Indus and its tributaries, with a canal-commanded area of about 14 million hectares in Punjab and Sindh. Mangla Dam completed 1967, Tarbela Dam 1976."},{"front":"Costs of the Indus Basin Irrigation System","code":"3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"Waterlogging and salinisation of millions of hectares; water lost to seepage and evaporation from unlined canals; tail-end farmers and Sindh receive less than head-reach Punjab; silting reservoirs; seawater intruding into the starved delta."},{"front":"Sand dams in Makueni County, Kenya","code":"3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (sustainable resource future)","open":false,"back":"A concrete wall keyed into the bed of a seasonal sand river. Sand builds up behind it and water is stored between the grains, so little evaporates. Communities build and maintain them, then irrigate vegetables and fruit trees."},{"front":"The six routes to a sustainable food supply","code":"3.2.3.2 — Different strategies can be used to increase food supply (sustainable resource future)","open":false,"back":"Organic farming (no manufactured fertiliser or pesticide); permaculture (a farm designed to copy a natural ecosystem, recycling water and nutrients); urban farming; sustainably sourced fish and meat; seasonal eating; cutting waste and losses."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Data table written for this room: average daily calorie supply per person, selected countries. The values are rounded to the nearest 100 kilocalories and are illustrative of the well-documented global pattern rather than a reading for one named year.\nCountry | Average daily calorie supply (kcal per person)\nUnited States of America | 3,700\nUnited Kingdom | 3,300\nBrazil | 3,200\nWorld average | 2,900\nIndia | 2,500\nKenya | 2,200\nChad | 2,000\nCentral African Republic | 1,800","Figure 2 — World map described for this room: the percentage of the population that is undernourished, shaded in four classes. The class boundaries are the ones the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization uses on its hunger maps, and the countries placed in each class show the broad, well-documented pattern rather than a reading for one named year.\nClass 4, the darkest shading, 25% and over: the Central African Republic, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Chad, South Sudan, Somalia, Zimbabwe, Mozambique and Madagascar in Africa, together with Yemen and Afghanistan in Asia, Haiti in the Caribbean and North Korea in east Asia.\nClass 3, 15% to 24.9%: Ethiopia, Tanzania, Angola, Nigeria, Pakistan and Guatemala.\nClass 2, 5% to 14.9%: India, Bangladesh, Indonesia, the Philippines, Iraq, Peru, Colombia, Egypt and South Africa.\nClass 1, the palest shading, under 5%: the whole of North America and Europe, Russia, China, Japan, Australia, New Zealand, Brazil, Argentina, Chile, Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Turkey and Saudi Arabia.","Figure 3 — Text extract written for this room. The farmer is illustrative; the place, the scheme and the processes described are real. A woman farming in Makueni County, south-eastern Kenya, says: 'The river beside our village only runs for a day or two after a storm and then it is dry sand again. Our self-help group built a concrete wall across it, and after two wet seasons the sand behind the wall was deep. The water is in the sand now, so the sun cannot take it and the animals cannot foul it. I used to walk three hours for water in the dry months; now I pump it from beside the river bed. We dug trenches along the contour on the slope above, so the rain soaks in instead of running off with our soil. We still plant sorghum and cowpeas because they stand the drought, but I water tomatoes and young mango trees through the dry season and sell them in Wote market.'","Figure 4 — Bar graph described for this room; the shares are illustrative of the well-documented contrast between country groups, not a measured data set. Title: where food is lost or wasted along the food chain, as a percentage of all the food lost or wasted in that group of countries. Horizontal axis: four stages of the chain — on the farm at harvest; in storage and transport; in processing and retail; in the home. Vertical axis: percentage of that group's total loss and waste, scaled 0 to 50. Two bars are plotted at each stage, one for a group of low-income countries and one for a group of high-income countries.\nStage | Low-income countries (%) | High-income countries (%)\nOn the farm at harvest | 25 | 10\nIn storage and transport | 40 | 10\nIn processing and retail | 25 | 30\nIn the home | 10 | 50"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Indus Basin Irrigation System, Pakistan (a large scale agricultural development)","items":["What it is: the world's largest continuous irrigation system — a linked network of storage dams, barrages, link canals and distributary canals that spreads river water across the plains of Punjab and Sindh in Pakistan.","The rivers: the Indus and its tributaries. Under the Indus Waters Treaty Pakistan uses the three western rivers — the Indus, the Jhelum and the Chenab.","Scale: the canal-commanded area — the farmland the canal network is laid out to serve — is roughly 14 million hectares, which is about 140,000 km².","The Indus Waters Treaty, signed in 1960 and brokered by the World Bank, gave India the three eastern rivers (the Ravi, the Beas and the Sutlej) and Pakistan the three western ones, so Pakistan had to build storage and link canals to move water westwards.","Mangla Dam, on the Jhelum, completed in 1967.","Tarbela Dam, on the Indus, completed in 1976; one of the largest earth-filled dams in the world.","How it works: snowmelt and monsoon floodwater is stored in the reservoirs, released through barrages into link canals, and distributed by smaller canals and watercourses to the fields; tube wells lift groundwater to supplement it.","Main crops: wheat in the cool season, and rice, cotton, sugar cane and maize in the hot season.","Who depends on it: a Pakistani population of about 240 million, in a country where agriculture employs roughly 40% of the workforce.","Advantage — more food: reliable dry-season water allows double cropping, so land that could carry one rain-fed harvest carries two, which turns a deficit into a surplus of wheat and rice.","Advantage — steadier food: because supply no longer depends on the monsoon alone, a weak monsoon does not empty the national stores, which is the security half of food security.","Advantage — income: rice and cotton grown on irrigated land are among Pakistan's leading exports, and the canals also supply drinking water to the towns along them.","Advantage — energy: Tarbela and Mangla generate a substantial share of Pakistan's electricity, so the same water does two jobs.","Disadvantage — waterlogging and salinisation: seepage from unlined canals raises the water table, and water evaporating from bare fields leaves salt behind, damaging millions of hectares of the irrigated area.","Disadvantage — wasted water: much of the water diverted never reaches a crop, lost to seepage and evaporation from unlined canals in a hot, dry climate.","Disadvantage — unequal shares: head-reach farmers in Punjab take water first, so tail-end farmers and the province of Sindh receive less, and water allocation is a long-running political dispute between provinces.","Disadvantage — silting: sediment trapped behind Tarbela and Mangla steadily reduces the storage the whole system depends on.","Disadvantage — the delta: so little water now reaches the Indus delta that seawater has pushed inland, killing mangroves and damaging the fishery and the farmland behind them.","The judgement line to carry into an essay: the advantages are large and immediate, the disadvantages are cumulative, so the scheme is worth its costs only if canals are lined, drainage installed and water shared more evenly."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — sand dams in Makueni County, Kenya (a local scheme in an LIC or NEE)","items":["Where: Makueni County, south-eastern Kenya. The county has about a million people and its administrative centre is Wote.","Status: Kenya is classed by the World Bank as a lower-middle-income country and is treated in GCSE Geography as an LIC or NEE, so the scheme satisfies the specification's requirement for a local scheme in an LIC or NEE.","The environment: semi-arid, with rainfall in the lower areas generally only about 300 to 600 mm a year, falling in two short seasons — the long rains around March to May and the short rains around October to December — and unreliable in both.","The problem: the rivers are seasonal sand rivers that flow for only a day or two after a storm, so water has to be fetched from far away for most of the year and rain-fed farming is a gamble.","What a sand dam is: a reinforced concrete wall built across the bed of a seasonal sand river and keyed into the bedrock on both banks and underneath.","How it fills: the next floods drop their coarse sand behind the wall; after two or three wet seasons the sand is deep enough to store water in the pore spaces between the grains, roughly a quarter to a third of the volume held back.","Why the water keeps: because it is inside the sand it is shaded from the sun, so very little evaporates; it is filtered as it moves through the sand; livestock cannot foul it and mosquitoes cannot breed in it.","How it is used: drawn by hand pump, from a shallow well beside the river bed or from a scoop hole; the stored water also recharges the ground upstream, so the water table rises along the valley.","Who builds it: a community self-help group, using local sand, stone and labour with cement and reinforcement bought in; groups such as the Africa Sand Dam Foundation and Excellent Development have supported hundreds of sand dams in south-eastern Kenya.","The farming that goes with it: contour terraces and trenches on the slopes above, which slow runoff so rain infiltrates and topsoil stays on the field.","Crops: drought-tolerant sorghum, pearl millet, cowpeas, pigeon peas and green grams as the staples, with irrigated kale, tomatoes and onions and mango and pawpaw trees grown for sale.","Food impact: dry-season water means vegetables and fruit through the months when there was nothing, which adds vitamins to a maize and bean diet and gives a cash income at markets such as Wote.","Other impacts: hours saved fetching water each day, mostly by women and girls, so more time for school and for the farm; higher and more reliable staple yields from the terraced, moister soil.","Why it counts as sustainable: local materials, local labour, no fuel and no imported parts; the community maintains it; and it improves the soil and groundwater it depends on rather than spending them.","Why it counts as appropriate technology: it is cheap enough for a village to fund and simple enough for a village to repair, which is what large-scale engineering is not.","The limits to carry into an evaluation: one dam serves only nearby households, so hundreds are needed for a county; a badly sited wall fills with fine silt instead of coarse sand and stores far less; a multi-year drought still empties the store."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the processes, terms and figures this room is marked on","items":["Food security: physical and economic access to enough safe, sufficient and nutritious food to meet dietary needs and food preferences for an active and healthy lifestyle. Food insecurity is its absence.","Surplus and deficit: a surplus is supply greater than need, a deficit is supply less than need. Surplus countries export; deficit countries import, take aid, or go short.","Calorie supply: over 3,400 kcal per person per day in North America, Western Europe and Australasia; a world average of roughly 2,900 kcal; under 2,000 kcal in the poorest countries. Between 600 and 700 million people are undernourished worldwide: about 700 million at the 2022 peak, and lower since.","Rising consumption, cause one — population: the world passed 8 billion people in 2022, and growth is fastest in countries already in deficit.","Rising consumption, cause two — economic development: rising incomes shift diets to meat and dairy, and grain fed to animals feeds far fewer people than grain eaten directly.","Factors affecting supply: climate (growing season, rainfall amount and reliability, drought and flood); technology (machinery, seed, fertiliser, irrigation, storage); pests and disease (locust swarms, crop rusts, livestock disease); water stress (agriculture takes about 70% of world freshwater withdrawals); conflict; poverty.","Impacts of insecurity: undernutrition (stunting, wasting, weakened immunity, deficiency diseases such as anaemia); famine, declared at Phase 5 of the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification scale; soil erosion from over-cultivation and overgrazing; rising prices, which hit the poorest hardest; social unrest, as in the food riots that helped force Haiti's prime minister from office in April 2008.","Irrigation: applying water artificially, from canals, wells or tube wells. Drip irrigation delivers water to the root and loses far less to evaporation and seepage than flood or furrow irrigation.","Hydroponics: growing plants without soil, with the roots in nutrient-rich water. Aeroponics: roots suspended in air in a closed chamber and sprayed with a nutrient mist.","The green revolution: from the 1960s, high-yielding varieties such as IR8 rice, released in 1966 by the International Rice Research Institute in the Philippines, plus fertiliser, pesticides and irrigation. It raised yields sharply but also raised water use, salinisation and the cost of inputs.","The new green revolution and biotechnology: keeps improved varieties but adds water harvesting, soil conservation, local knowledge and biodiversity; genetically modified examples include insect-resistant Bt crops and Golden Rice, engineered to carry beta-carotene against vitamin A deficiency.","Appropriate technology: cheap, locally made and locally repaired equipment matched to the money and skills of the people using it — sand dams, treadle pumps, stone lines laid along the contour, drip kits.","Organic farming: no manufactured fertilisers or pesticides; rotation, manure, compost and biological pest control instead. Better for soil and water, but usually lower yields and higher prices.","Permaculture: designing the whole farm to copy a natural ecosystem — perennial crops, mixed planting, and water and nutrients recycled on site so almost nothing is bought in.","Urban farming: allotments, community gardens, rooftop plots and vertical farms inside cities; uses waste ground, cuts the distance food travels and puts fresh food and income where people live.","Sustainable fish and meat: quotas, net mesh-size limits, no-take zones, certification and responsible aquaculture; and eating less meat, because animals convert grain to food inefficiently.","Seasonal food: eating what is in season locally avoids the energy burned on heated greenhouses, cold storage and air freight.","Waste and losses: roughly a third of all food produced for people is lost or wasted — mostly after harvest in low-income countries, through poor storage, pests and slow transport, and mostly in shops and homes in high-income countries."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) on the six-mark question: 'The advantages of a large scale agricultural development outweigh its disadvantages.' Using a named example, to what extent do you agree?","items":["The paragraph: The disadvantages of the Indus Basin Irrigation System matter because they accumulate, while its advantages are repeated every season. Unlined canals carrying water across a canal-commanded area of about 14 million hectares in Punjab and Sindh lose a great deal of it to seepage, and the water that seeps raises the water table until land is waterlogged; as that water evaporates from bare fields it leaves its dissolved salts at the surface, so waterlogging and salinisation have damaged millions of hectares of the very land the canals were built to water. Silt trapped behind Tarbela, completed in 1976, and Mangla, completed in 1967, steadily reduces the storage the dry-season release depends on, and so little water now reaches the Indus delta that the sea has pushed inland and killed mangroves. Set against that, double cropping wheat and rice on land that once carried one rain-fed harvest steadies the food supply of a country of about 240 million people when the monsoon is weak, which no alternative could do on that scale. The advantages therefore still outweigh the disadvantages, but only conditionally: a system whose costs grow each year stays worth having only while canals are lined and drainage installed.","Why this reaches Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): it demonstrates specific and accurate knowledge of a named large scale agricultural development, with dates, places and figures (AO1 = 2 marks); and it shows thorough understanding of the interrelationships between the canals, the water table, the soil and the delta, carried as a chain of cause and effect rather than a list, then uses that understanding to weigh the two sides and reach a reasoned, qualified judgement (AO2 = 4 marks). Nothing here is earned for length: this is a six-mark question with no SPaG marks, so every mark comes from the geography.","What to copy from it: one named place and one figure or date per point; a 'so' or 'because' in every sentence that carries a process; a sorting principle that does the evaluating for you (here, whether a cost accumulates or a benefit repeats); and a final sentence that decides rather than summarises."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) on the same six-mark question, visibly weaker","items":["The paragraph: Big farming schemes have good points and bad points. The Indus one in Pakistan gives lots of water to farmers so they can grow more food, which is good because there are a lot of people there and they need feeding. But it also causes problems for the environment and some people do not get enough water, which is unfair. Dams can be bad for rivers too. So there are advantages and disadvantages and both of them are important.","Which descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) — it demonstrates limited knowledge of the named development and shows only a simple understanding of the interrelationships between the scheme, the land and the people.","Which descriptor it misses: Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) asks for thorough understanding of the interrelationships between environments and processes in the context of food security and demonstrates in detail how the improvement helps to provide a secure source of food, used to weigh the two sides. This answer gives no date, no figure and no place beyond \"Pakistan\"; \"problems for the environment\" names no process, so salinisation and the starved delta are never explained; and the final sentence sits on the fence instead of judging.","The three fixes that would move it up: replace each general statement with a named feature and a figure (Tarbela 1976, Mangla 1967, a canal-commanded area of about 14 million hectares); explain one mechanism the whole way through, for example seepage raising the water table, evaporation leaving salt and the land going out of production; and end by deciding which side weighs more, and on what condition."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Evaluate the effectiveness of a local scheme in an LIC or NEE in increasing sustainable supplies of food. [6 marks]'","items":["Decode the command: 'evaluate' asks how well it works, with evidence on both sides, not a description of it. Two words fix what has to be judged — effectiveness (did food supply actually rise?) and sustainable (will the gain last, and does it cost the soil and water it depends on?). The scheme must be local and in an LIC or NEE, so a national irrigation system earns nothing here.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly one minute planning and six minutes writing. There are no SPaG marks in Section C, so nothing is set aside for them. Three tight paragraphs is the right ambition at this tariff; four is already too many, and the six-mark grid rewards one mechanism explained properly far more than five named in passing.","Paragraph 1 — locate the scheme and name the problem it answers, then give the mechanism, because Level 2 is where accurate knowledge of the place is paid for: Makueni County in south-eastern Kenya, semi-arid, roughly 300 to 600 mm of rain a year in the lower areas in two short unreliable seasons, and rivers that run for a day or two after a storm, so water rather than land is the limiting factor. A self-help group builds a reinforced concrete wall across the sand river and keys it into bedrock; floods drop coarse sand behind it; water is stored in the pore spaces between the grains, roughly a quarter to a third of the volume held back, shaded from the sun so very little evaporates.","Paragraph 2 — turn the water into food and apply the sustainability test in the same breath, because that is where the four AO2 marks sit: dry-season irrigation of kale, tomatoes, onions and mango trees sold at Wote, alongside drought-tolerant sorghum, cowpeas and pigeon peas; contour trenches that keep rain and topsoil on the slope so staple yields rise; and then local sand, stone and labour, no fuel, no imported parts, community maintenance, with the soil and groundwater improved rather than spent.","Paragraph 3 — the limits and the judgement together, because at six marks they cannot each have a paragraph: one dam serves only nearby households, so hundreds are needed across a county of about a million people; a badly sited wall silts with fine mud instead of coarse sand; a multi-year drought still empties the store. Then decide.","The evaluation thread that runs through all three paragraphs: keep asking what happens after the outside help leaves. Every point is judged against whether the village could keep it going alone — which is the difference between a scheme that works and a scheme that lasts.","What the conclusion must say: that the scheme is highly effective for its cost and genuinely sustainable, because it improves the soil and water it relies on instead of depleting them; but that it operates at the scale of a valley, so it makes a dry district liveable rather than curing national food insecurity — which is exactly what should be expected of a local scheme, and is the right basis on which to judge it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:17:55Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file rather than the stale copy in the judge prompt: every fault in the previous verdict is fixed and nothing was broken in the fixing. Figure 2 now reads correctly (Zimbabwe in Class 4, about 38%; Pakistan in Class 3, 20.7%) and all four of its readers recount — twelve countries in the darkest class, eight of them African, four outside Africa, the band running from Chad and South Sudan through the Democratic Republic of the Congo to Zimbabwe and Mozambique with Somalia and Madagascar named as lying outside it. The undernourishment headcount now matches FAO's current series (SOFI 2026: 645 million in 2025, about 688 million at the 2022 peak) and sub-Saharan Africa is now 'the largest regional share', which SOFI 2026 supports. The Indus figure is qualified as canal-commanded area at all ten places it appears, 240 million is Pakistan's population, questions[10] says 'LIC or NEE', and the two restating worked-example steps are now real moves (the eight-of-twelve weighting, and the warning that each column of Figure 4 totals 100 so the graph cannot show which group loses more). Figures 1, 3 and 4 recount exactly as before (3,700 − 1,800 = 1,900; 25 + 40 = 65; 50 is five times 10; both columns total 100), the three rewritten solutions run 233, 242 and 245 words inside the 150–250 band, all fifteen card backs are inside the word limits, and the validator passes. Publish once the AO split on the three judgement questions is decided."}}},"3.2.3.3":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.2.3.3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Water is one of the three topics you may be entered for at the end of Paper 2, and the marks always come back in the same shapes: a skills question read straight off a map or graph of water availability, a levels-marked question on how water security can be improved, and a six-mark question that asks you to weigh a scheme up rather than describe it, because Section C sets no extended nine-mark question. The examiner will not reward an answer that says some places are dry and some are wet. It wants the thresholds that define scarcity, the reasons consumption keeps climbing, the chain from insecurity to disease, hunger and conflict, one named large-scale transfer scheme carried with its costs as well as its benefits, and one named local scheme in an LIC or NEE. This room stocks you with both examples, the processes behind them and the judgement sentences that lift an answer out of the middle band.","idea":[{"h":"Surplus and deficit: where the water is, and why demand keeps climbing","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","t":"About 97 per cent of the water on Earth is salt water, and of the small remainder roughly two thirds is frozen in ice caps and glaciers, so the fresh water people can actually reach is a thin slice of the total. A place has a water surplus when supply is greater than demand and a water deficit, or water insecurity, when demand is greater than supply. The usual thresholds are useful because the examiner can see you know them: below about 1700 m³ per person per year a country is said to be under water stress, and below 1000 m³ it is in water scarcity. Surpluses sit where it is wet and people are few — the Congo basin, Amazonia, Canada, Scandinavia, New Zealand, the western side of Britain. Deficits sit where it is dry, where people are many, or both: North Africa and the Sahara, the Arabian peninsula and the Middle East, central Asia, the south-west United States, central Australia, and increasingly northern China, north-west India and south-east England. Consumption is rising for two linked reasons. Population growth adds mouths: world population passed 8 billion in November 2022. Economic development multiplies what each person uses, because industry, power-station cooling and irrigated farming all drink heavily, and rising affluence brings showers, washing machines, swimming pools and meat-rich diets — a kilogram of beef takes thousands of litres of water to produce, many times what a kilogram of wheat needs. Availability itself depends on climate (total rainfall, its reliability, and evaporation rates), geology (permeable rocks such as chalk and sandstone store groundwater in aquifers, impermeable rocks do not), pollution of the supply, over-abstraction that takes water faster than it is replaced, limited infrastructure such as pipes, reservoirs and treatment works, and poverty, which is what stops a country building any of them. A top-level answer never says a place is dry and stops there: it names the threshold the place has crossed, separates the physical reason from the human one, and says which of the two is doing more of the work."},{"h":"What water insecurity does to people: disease, hunger, lost output and conflict","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","t":"Water insecurity is not one problem but a chain of them. Where there is not enough safe water, people drink what they can get, and water contaminated by untreated sewage carries cholera, typhoid and dysentery; the World Health Organization attributes hundreds of thousands of diarrhoeal deaths a year to unsafe drinking water, poor sanitation and poor hygiene, most of them children under five. Around two billion people still do not have safely managed drinking water at home. Pollution makes the shortage worse rather than being a separate issue: industrial effluent, fertiliser run-off and sewage take usable water out of a supply that was already short. Food production is hit next, because irrigation is by far the largest user of fresh water worldwide — about 70 per cent of withdrawals — so a shortage means failed crops, thinner livestock, malnutrition and rising food prices. Industrial output falls for the same reason: power stations need cooling water, and factories that cannot get water cut production or shut, which costs jobs and export earnings. Finally, where demand exceeds supply the water itself becomes something to argue over. Inside a country the argument is usually between farmers, cities and industry, or between people upstream and people downstream. Between countries it involves rivers that cross borders: Ethiopia's Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Blue Nile, begun in 2011, worries Egypt, which depends on the Nile for the great majority of its fresh water; Turkish dams on the Tigris and Euphrates reduce the flow reaching Syria and Iraq; the Colorado is so heavily abstracted by seven US states and Mexico that it often no longer reaches the sea; and India and Pakistan have shared the Indus under a treaty brokered by the World Bank since 1960. A top answer chains these impacts rather than listing them — no safe water, so cholera, so adults too ill to work, so no income, so still no pump — and then judges which impact hits hardest, and whom."},{"h":"Getting more water in: storage, transfers and desalination","code":"3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (increasing supply)","t":"There are three big ways to raise supply, and each buys water at a price. The first is to divert supplies and increase storage. A dam across a valley holds back the wet season's flow in a reservoir so it can be released through the dry season, which turns an unreliable river into a dependable supply and often generates electricity as well: the Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze, fully generating by 2012, has an installed capacity of 22 500 MW and its reservoir required the resettlement of roughly 1.3 million people. Dams also drown land, trap the sediment that farmland downstream depends on, and lose water to evaporation. The second is a water transfer, which moves water by canal, pipeline or tunnel from a basin with a surplus to a basin with a deficit — China's South–North Water Transfer Project is the largest ever built, and South Africa's Gauteng region is supplied through tunnels from the Lesotho Highlands. Transfers work, but they cost a great deal, they usually displace people, and they take water from a donor region that gets nothing back; the Aral Sea, drained by irrigation diversions from the Amu Darya and Syr Darya from the 1960s, is the warning. The third is desalination, taking the salt out of sea water either by distillation (boiling it and condensing the steam) or, far more commonly now, by reverse osmosis, which forces water through a membrane under pressure. It is the only method that makes new fresh water from an unlimited source, which is why Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates and Israel rely on it; Israel's Sorek plant on the Mediterranean coast, working since 2013, is one of the world's largest reverse-osmosis plants and supplies well over a hundred million cubic metres a year. But it is expensive and energy-hungry, and it leaves concentrated brine to dispose of, which is why London's Thames Gateway plant at Beckton, opened in 2010, is run only when supplies are short. At the top level the answer is never a list of strategies: it is a weighing of who gains, who pays, what happens to the donor area and whether the supply will last."},{"h":"A sustainable water future: use less, waste less, put some back","code":"3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (sustainable resource future)","t":"Sustainable means meeting the needs of today without wrecking the supply for tomorrow, so the whole of this idea is about making the water already available go further. Water conservation attacks demand: metering so that people pay for what they use, low-flush and dual-flush toilets, efficient washing machines and dishwashers, drip irrigation instead of spraying fields in the heat of the day, and above all fixing leaks — roughly a fifth of the water put into public supply in England and Wales leaks away before it reaches a tap. Groundwater management protects the store: abstraction licences and monitored water tables stop an aquifer being emptied, because over-abstraction dries wetlands and springs, lets sea water seep into coastal aquifers and can make the ground itself subside, and artificial recharge deliberately puts treated water or monsoon run-off back underground. Recycling treats used water so it can be used again: Singapore's NEWater plants, running since 2003, treat wastewater to a standard that can meet up to about 40 per cent of the country's water demand, mostly in industry. Grey water is the gentlest version of the same idea — the lightly used water from baths, showers, washbasins and washing machines, kept separate from the sewage and reused on gardens or to flush toilets, so drinking-quality water is not spent on jobs that do not need it. In an LIC or NEE, sustainability usually means appropriate technology instead: rainwater harvesting, hand-dug wells, rope pumps and small earth dams that local people can build from local materials and repair themselves, as in the Wakel river basin in Rajasthan. A Level 3 answer defines sustainable before using the word, then tests the scheme against that definition: is it affordable, can the people who use it repair it, and does it stop the water table falling rather than simply pumping harder?"}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing a distribution from the map (2 marks)","setup":"Figure 1 is a choropleth map of Africa showing total renewable water resources per person per year in four classes: less than 1000 m³, 1000 to 2500 m³, 2500 to 10 000 m³, and more than 10 000 m³. In the lowest class the map names Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Libya, Egypt, Kenya, Burkina Faso and South Africa. In the 1000 to 2500 m³ class it names Nigeria and Tanzania. In the 2500 to 10 000 m³ class it names Angola and Zambia. In the highest class it names the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Gabon and Liberia. The question: Using Figure 1, describe the distribution of countries with less than 1000 cubic metres of water per person per year. [2 marks]","steps":["Say where on the continent the shading sits before naming anybody: the scarce countries lie at the two ends of Africa, the far north and the far south, and not across the middle.","Turn that into a developed point with detail lifted from the map: an unbroken belt of five countries runs right across North Africa, from Morocco on the Atlantic coast to Egypt on the Red Sea.","Sweep up the rest of the pattern so nothing shaded is left out: two isolated countries in tropical Africa, Kenya in the east and Burkina Faso in the west, plus South Africa alone at the southern tip.","Check what would score nothing: a bare list of the shaded names, or a description of the wet countries in the Congo basin, because the question asked only about the distribution of the scarcest class."],"cues":["Before you name a single country, what is the overall shape of the pattern?","Which detail from the map turns that basic statement into a developed one?","Which shaded countries has your statement not covered yet?","What could you write here that the examiner would give no credit for at all?"]},{"title":"Reading a photograph for a 4-mark 'suggest' (4 marks)","setup":"Figure 4 is a photograph of a village in southern Rajasthan, north-west India, at the end of the dry season. A woman is lowering a bucket on a rope through a small square opening in a low circular rim set almost flush with the ground; a swept, gently sloping apron of plastered ground runs down to that opening, and a shallow channel leads to it from the roof of the nearest house. Behind the houses a curved bank of packed earth about waist high runs across a shallow valley, holding back a crescent of muddy water perhaps thirty metres across, with cattle drinking at the edge and a wide ring of cracked dried mud showing how far the water has already shrunk back. Beyond the bank the land is brown, stony and bare, and a narrow earth channel runs along the hillside contour towards a small bright green field of crops. The sky is cloudless. The question: Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, suggest how the structures shown help this village to cope with the dry season. [4 marks]","steps":["Take the covered opening first and say what feeds it: the swept sloping apron and the channel from the roof collect monsoon rain and send it into an underground tank, a taanka, so rain that fell months ago is still in the village now.","Explain why it is buried and covered rather than open: sealing the store keeps out the heat, so almost nothing is lost to evaporation in a Rajasthani summer, and keeps out animals and dust, so the water stays fit to drink.","Read the curved earth bank as a johad: it ponds the run-off long enough for the water to soak downwards and recharge the groundwater, which is why there is still water for the cattle and why nearby wells last further into the dry season, even though the pond itself has visibly shrunk.","Finish on the contour channel and the green field: it is a pat, carrying water along the slope to the crops, so the harvest no longer depends on rain arriving at exactly the right moment — and that is the answer to the question, which asked about coping with the dry season."],"cues":["What are the sloping apron and the roof channel for?","Why put that store underground and put a lid on it?","The pond has shrunk, so why is the earth bank still doing useful work?","One structure in the photograph is still unexplained — what is it doing?"]},{"title":"Building a six-mark answer on improving water security (6 marks)","setup":"The question is: Explain how water security can be improved. [6 marks] No figure is given, so the answer comes from your own knowledge. The examiner expects the answer to show what water security means and to develop at least one strategy in detail rather than list several. You may use any of the strategies in this room: dams and reservoirs, water transfers, desalination, water conservation and metering, leak repair, groundwater management, recycling and grey water, and rainwater harvesting with appropriate technology.","steps":["Define the target in the first sentence so every later point can be measured against it: water security means having enough safe water to meet people's needs without the way it is taken damaging the supply for the future.","Develop one strategy that adds water, all the way through the chain: a dam holds back the wet season's flow in a reservoir, that stored water is released through the dry months, so an unreliable river becomes a dependable supply for homes, irrigation and power-station cooling.","Develop one strategy that saves water, with the same chain: metering makes households pay for what they use so they use less, and repairing leaking mains puts water already treated back into supply — worth doing because about a fifth of the water put into public supply in England and Wales never reaches a tap.","Show you can work at more than one scale, which is what the mark scheme rewards: a village taanka or a hand pump built and repaired by local people improves security for a few hundred people at a tiny cost, where a reservoir would be unaffordable and unrepairable.","Close by linking back to your own definition: say which of your strategies protects the source as well as adding supply, because that is what makes the improvement last."],"cues":["What single word in the question must be defined before anything else can be judged?","Take one supply strategy — what is the chain from the wet season to the tap?","The question says improve security, not just add water. What else could you do?","Every example so far has been a big one. What is missing?","How do you turn a list of strategies into an answer that has a point of view?"]},{"title":"Finding the conflict inside a text extract (6 marks)","setup":"Figure 3 is a text extract about China's South–North Water Transfer Project. A water engineer in Beijing says that before the Central Route opened in December 2014 the city was pumping its aquifer faster than the rain refilled it and the water table fell year after year; now a canal of about 1400 km brings water from the Danjiangkou reservoir on the Han river and the city reports its groundwater rising again, with cleaner tap water. A farmer in Hubei, below the Danjiangkou dam, says the raised dam's reservoir took his village, that he was one of more than 300 000 people moved for the Central Route, that the compensation did not buy land as good, and that the Han river below the dam now runs lower so the fishing and the boats have suffered. An official on the Eastern Route says that route follows the old Grand Canal, has to be pumped uphill the whole way so it costs electricity every day, needed a long line of sewage treatment works built before any water could be sent north, and that the whole project is costed at more than 60 billion US dollars. The question: Using Figure 3, suggest why a large-scale water transfer scheme may lead to conflict between different interest groups. [6 marks]","steps":["Name the two sides the extract sets against each other before explaining anything: the receiving region, Beijing, and the donor region, Hubei below the dam.","Take the receiving side's case from the engineer: a city whose aquifer was being emptied gains a supply it cannot get any other way, and the rising groundwater shows the transfer is repairing the damage, so Beijing will defend the scheme.","Take the donor side's case from the farmer and show why compensation does not settle it: more than 300 000 people were moved and the land they were given is poorer, while the lower Han river below the dam damages fishing and navigation for people who receive none of the water — the costs and the benefits land on different populations.","Add the third interest group from the official: taxpayers and electricity users, because a bill above 60 billion US dollars and a route that must be pumped uphill every day mean money and energy that could have gone elsewhere, which is exactly the argument environmental groups and southern provinces make.","Judge, because the command is suggest and the marks are for reasoning rather than retelling: conflict arises here not because the scheme fails but because it succeeds for one region at another region's expense, and nothing in the extract gives the donor region a say."],"cues":["Who is quoted, and whose interests do they represent?","Why would the city fight to keep the scheme?","The farmer was paid. Why is he still aggrieved?","Which group has not been mentioned yet, and what is their complaint?","What is the single sentence that explains why all three disagree?"]},{"title":"Weighing a large-scale transfer scheme in a six-mark answer (6 marks)","setup":"The question is: Using an example of a large-scale water transfer scheme, discuss the advantages and disadvantages of its development. [6 marks] It is marked on the Paper 2 question 05.4 grid, Levels 1–3, AO1 = 2 marks and AO2 = 4 marks, with no SPaG marks. The example studied is China's South–North Water Transfer Project. The facts available: roughly four fifths of China's water is in the humid south while much of its farmland and cities such as Beijing and Tianjin are in the drier north; three routes were planned, Eastern, Central and Western, and the Western route has never been built; the Eastern Route has supplied water since 2013 along the line of the old Grand Canal and must be pumped uphill; the Central Route opened in December 2014 and runs about 1400 km by gravity from the Danjiangkou reservoir on the Han river, a tributary of the Yangtze, to Beijing and Tianjin; more than 300 000 people were resettled for the Central Route when the Danjiangkou dam was raised; the cost is put at more than 60 billion US dollars; Beijing reports its groundwater level rising since the transfers began; the Han river below the dam runs lower.","steps":["Open with the reason the scheme exists, because at six marks there is no room for a paragraph that judges nothing and this is the standard both sides will be judged against: China's water and China's people are in the wrong places relative to each other, and the north had been closing the gap by emptying its aquifers.","Give the strongest advantage with its evidence attached: the Central Route, about 1400 km by gravity from Danjiangkou since December 2014, now supplies a large share of Beijing's tap water, so the city pumps less and its groundwater level has begun to recover.","Turn to the disadvantage in the same detail and name who carries it, because the AO2 marks are for the interrelationships and not for the list: more than 300 000 people were resettled and the Han river below the dam runs lower, so the donor region pays in land, fishing and navigation for water it does not receive, while the Eastern Route burns electricity being pumped uphill every day.","Land the judgement in one conditional sentence rather than summarising: the scheme is justified for the receiving region because nothing else could refill an exhausted aquifer that quickly, but it moves the problem rather than shrinking it, so unless the north also meters, recycles and mends its leaks, demand will grow to fill the canal and the south will be asked for more."],"cues":["Why was this scheme built at all — what problem is it the answer to?","What is the single best piece of evidence that it has worked?","Who is worse off because of it, and how do you know?","The question says discuss, and six marks leaves one sentence for it. What must that sentence do?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A country has 900 m³ of renewable water per person per year. Which threshold has it crossed, and what does that tell you about its likely problems?","a":"It is below 1000 m³ per person per year, so it is in water scarcity, not merely water stress (which begins below about 1700 m³). At that level there is not enough to meet domestic, agricultural and industrial demand together, so choices have to be made between farmers, cities and industry — which is where conflict starts."},{"q":"Figure 2 shows agriculture taking 70 per cent of global freshwater withdrawals, industry 20 and domestic 10. Why does that make drought a food problem before it is a tap problem?","a":"Agriculture takes seven times as much water as homes do, so irrigation is where a shortage bites first and hardest. Crops fail, livestock thin, yields and food supplies fall and prices rise, long before domestic supply — a much smaller and usually better protected share — has to be rationed."},{"q":"Why can a village taanka be described as sustainable when a large reservoir often cannot?","a":"A taanka is built from local materials by local labour, is repaired by the people who use it, stores rain that would otherwise run off, displaces nobody and floods nothing. A large reservoir drowns land, moves people, traps sediment, loses water to evaporation and needs money and expertise from outside to keep it working."},{"q":"Name two things that a water transfer scheme does to the donor basin, and say why that causes conflict.","a":"It reduces river flow downstream of the extraction point, damaging fishing, navigation and wetlands, and it usually floods land and resettles people where a reservoir is enlarged. The conflict arises because these costs fall on one region while the benefits are delivered to another, which gets the water."}]},"room":{"facts":["Water stress begins below about 1700 m³ of renewable water per person per year; water scarcity below 1000 m³ per person per year.","About 97 per cent of the Earth's water is salt water, and roughly two thirds of the fresh water that remains is frozen in ice caps and glaciers.","Agriculture takes about 70 per cent of global freshwater withdrawals, industry about 20 per cent and domestic use about 10 per cent.","Water security is sufficient access to meet people's water needs while limiting the negative consequences of that withdrawal; a water surplus is supply greater than demand, a deficit is demand greater than supply.","The large-scale transfer example: China's South–North Water Transfer Project — Eastern Route supplying since 2013, Central Route opened December 2014, about 1400 km from the Danjiangkou reservoir on the Han river to Beijing and Tianjin, cost put at more than 60 billion US dollars, more than 300 000 people resettled.","The local sustainable example: the Wakel river basin, southern Rajasthan, India — taankas (covered underground rainwater tanks), johads (earth banks that pond run-off and recharge groundwater) and pats (contour irrigation channels), built and maintained by the villagers themselves.","Grey water is the lightly used water from baths, showers, washbasins and washing machines, reused for flushing toilets or watering gardens; it is not sewage.","Paper 2 Section C, Question 5 Water is worth 11 marks in all: one 1-mark question, two 2-mark questions and one 6-mark levels question (05.4, Levels 1–3, AO1 = 2 marks and AO2 = 4 marks). Section C sets no extended 9-mark question and no SPaG marks, so work at about a minute a mark — roughly six minutes on the 6-marker."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, state the class of total renewable water resources per person per year that Egypt falls in. [1 mark]","a":"Less than 1000 m³ per person per year (the water scarcity class). AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"Find Egypt on the map and read its shading against the key. Egypt is in the lowest of the four classes, less than 1000 cubic metres per person per year. That is the class the key labels water scarcity, and it is the same class as the other four North African countries named on the map.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The key has four classes, and only one of them can be right for any one country.","Locate Egypt in the north-east corner of the continent, then match its shading to the key.","Your answer is one of the four class labels printed in the key, copied exactly, units and all."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, describe the distribution of countries with less than 1000 cubic metres of water per person per year. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic statement of distribution from the map, one for a developed point using detail from it. No credit for simply listing names of countries. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The countries with less than 1000 cubic metres of water per person per year are found at the extreme north and the extreme south of the continent rather than in the middle. In the north an unbroken belt of five countries stretches right across North Africa, from Morocco on the Atlantic coast to Egypt on the Red Sea, while South Africa sits alone at the southern tip. There are also two isolated countries in tropical Africa, Kenya in the east and Burkina Faso in the west.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The question asks where the class is, not which countries are in it.","Work across the map in bands — top, middle, bottom — and say what the class does in each.","Give one statement about the overall pattern, then add a piece of detail from the map that develops it."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, calculate how many times greater agriculture's share of global freshwater withdrawals is than the domestic share. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for 7 (times). One mark for an indication of the calculation, eg 70 ÷ 10. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Read the two bars against the vertical axis: agriculture stands at 70 per cent and domestic at 10 per cent. Divide the larger by the smaller: 70 ÷ 10 = 7. Agriculture's share is 7 times the domestic share. Show the division as well as the answer, because a mark is available for the working.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["'How many times greater' is a division, not a subtraction.","Read both bars off the vertical axis first and write the two percentages down.","Set it out as larger share ÷ smaller share, and write the division on the page."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"State two causes of water insecurity. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for two stated causes, physical or human, eg population growth and rising demand; climate change increasing drought risk; pollution of rivers, aquifers and lakes; over-abstraction; limited infrastructure; poverty. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"Two causes, each stated clearly and separately. First: population growth, because a rising population in the same river basin divides the same supply between more people. Second: pollution of rivers, lakes and aquifers by sewage, industrial effluent and fertiliser run-off, because polluted water is not usable water, so a country can have plenty of rain and still be insecure. No explanation is needed for the marks here — the command word is state — but each cause must be a separate point, not the same idea twice.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Insecurity can come from the sky or from people. You need two causes, and they may be one of each.","Run through what makes water either scarce or unusable.","Write two short separate sentences; no development is required for these marks."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"State what is meant by the term 'water security'. [1 mark]","a":"Sufficient access to meet people's water needs while limiting the negative consequences of that water withdrawal. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"Water security means having reliable access to enough safe water to meet people's needs, in a way that does not damage the supply or the environment it is taken from. The second half matters: a city that meets its needs by emptying an aquifer faster than the rain refills it is not water secure, because the supply will not last.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Two halves: enough safe water, and taken in a way that can continue.","Think about what would make you say a place is not secure even though its taps run today.","Write the first half of the sentence first — what people must be able to get hold of — then add the second half, the condition the supply itself has to meet."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Give one way in which grey water can be used to reduce demand for treated water, and develop your answer. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for stating a use of grey water, eg flushing toilets or watering gardens; second mark for developing it, eg because drinking-quality water is then not spent on a job that does not need it. AO1 = 1 mark, AO2 = 1 mark.","sol":"Grey water is the lightly used water from baths, showers, washbasins and washing machines, kept separate from sewage. It can be piped to flush toilets. That reduces demand because a household flushing toilets with grey water is no longer using water that has been abstracted, treated to drinking standard and pumped through the mains for a job where its purity is irrelevant, so the treated supply goes further and less has to be taken from the river or aquifer in the first place.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Grey water is not clean enough to drink but is far too clean to throw away.","Find a household job where the quality of the water genuinely does not matter.","State the use in the first sentence, then explain in the second why that saves treated water."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, suggest how the structures shown help this village to cope with the dry season. [4 marks]","a":"Levels 1–2. Credit interpretation of the photograph: the roof channel and sloping apron feeding a covered underground tank (taanka) storing monsoon rain with little evaporation; the earth bank (johad) ponding run-off to recharge groundwater and water livestock; the contour channel (pat) irrigating the field. AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The swept, sloping apron and the channel running from the roof of the nearest house both lead to the small covered opening in the ground, so rain falling in the monsoon months is collected and stored in an underground tank, a taanka, instead of running away across the stony ground. Because the tank is buried and covered, very little of it is lost to evaporation in the Rajasthani heat and animals and dust are kept out, so the water is still there and still drinkable months later — which is why the woman can draw a bucket at the end of the dry season. The curved bank of packed earth behind the houses is a johad: it ponds monsoon run-off long enough for the water to soak downwards and recharge the groundwater, so wells nearby last further into the year, and while it lasts it waters the cattle. The ring of cracked mud shows the pond is shrinking, but the water it has already put underground is not lost. Finally, the narrow channel along the contour is a pat, carrying water from the pond to the small green field, which is why there is a crop growing at all in a landscape that is otherwise brown and bare.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["There are three separate water structures in the photograph. Find all three before writing.","For each one, ask what it does with the monsoon rain that would otherwise run off or evaporate.","Take one structure at a time, say what it collects or holds, and then say what the village gets from it in the dry months."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, suggest why a large-scale water transfer scheme may lead to conflict between different interest groups. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. The focus is conflict, so opposing points of view must be set against each other and developed: the receiving region's gain in supply and recovering groundwater against the donor region's resettlement and reduced river flow, plus the cost and energy arguments. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"Conflict arises because the extract shows the benefits and the costs of the scheme landing on completely different people. The engineer in Beijing speaks for the receiving region: the city was pumping its aquifer faster than the rain refilled it, and since the Central Route opened in December 2014 the groundwater level has started to rise again and the tap water is cleaner, so Beijing will defend the scheme. The farmer in Hubei speaks for the donor region, and his complaint is that compensation does not settle the account: he was one of more than 300 000 people moved, the replacement land is poorer, and the Han river below the dam now runs lower, which damages fishing and navigation for people who receive none of the transferred water. That is the heart of the conflict — one region pays in land, livelihood and river flow so that another region can drink. A third group appears in the official's account: the taxpayers and electricity users who meet a bill of more than 60 billion US dollars and pay to pump the Eastern Route uphill every day it runs, money and energy that other regions could have used. Conflict here is not a sign that the scheme has failed; it follows from the fact that it succeeds for one region at another's expense, and nothing in the extract gives the donor region a say.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Three people speak in the extract. Ask whose interests each of them represents.","Set the quotations against each other rather than summarising them one after the other.","Work out who gains, who pays, and whether the two groups are the same people — then build the answer around that gap."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (increasing supply)","3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)"]},{"q":"Explain how water insecurity can affect food production and industrial output. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. Expect developed chains rather than lists: irrigation as the dominant water user, so shortage means crop failure, malnutrition and rising prices; industry and power stations needing process and cooling water, so shortage means reduced output, closures and lost jobs. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Food production is hit first because irrigation is by far the largest user of fresh water in the world, taking about 70 per cent of all withdrawals. When supply falls short, farmers cannot irrigate at the stage when crops most need water, so yields drop or the crop fails altogether; livestock lose both drinking water and grazing. The effects then spread beyond the field. A poor harvest means less food available locally and less to sell, so farming families lose income at the same moment that food becomes scarcer, and prices rise for everybody. Where that happens in an LIC with little irrigation infrastructure, the result is malnutrition and dependence on imports or aid, and farmers may abandon the land and migrate. Industrial output is affected by the same shortage through a different route. Manufacturing needs water for processing, washing and cooling, and thermal power stations need very large volumes of cooling water; if river levels fall or abstraction is restricted, factories have to cut production or shut down and power stations may have to reduce output, which causes power cuts that stop other industry as well. Jobs, wages, tax revenue and export earnings all fall with production. The two effects then reinforce each other, because a country with a failed harvest needs export earnings to buy food at exactly the point when its industry is least able to produce them — which is why water insecurity is an economic problem, not only a health one.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Start from who actually uses the water, in order of size.","For each of the two, carry the chain past the farm gate or the factory gate to the people affected.","Deal with food production first, then industry, and finish by linking the two."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)"]},{"q":"Explain how water security can be improved. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. An understanding of water security should be shown. Credit strategies at different scales — dams and reservoirs, transfers, desalination, wells and tanks, rainwater harvesting, metering, leak repair, recycling and grey water — with at least one developed in detail. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Water security means having enough safe water to meet people's needs without the way it is taken damaging the supply for the future, so it can be improved by adding water or by making what is available go further. Adding water usually means storage: a dam holds back the wet season's flow in a reservoir and releases it through the dry months, so an unreliable river becomes a dependable supply for homes, irrigation and power stations. Where there is no river worth damming, water can be transferred by canal from a basin in surplus, or made from sea water by reverse osmosis, though desalination is costly and energy-hungry. Saving water is often cheaper and faster. Metering makes households pay for what they use and so cuts consumption, efficient appliances cut it further, drip irrigation puts water to the roots instead of losing it to the air, and repairing leaking mains puts treated water back into supply — worth doing when roughly a fifth of the water put into public supply in England and Wales never reaches a tap. Recycling wastewater and reusing grey water for flushing and gardens stop drinking-quality water being spent where purity is irrelevant. Scale matters too: in an LIC a covered rainwater tank or a hand pump villagers can repair themselves improves security for a few hundred people at a cost they can meet. The strategies that last protect the source, through licensed abstraction and recharged groundwater, rather than simply pumping harder.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Improving security is not only about finding more water.","Sort your strategies into ones that add supply and ones that reduce demand, and take one of each all the way through.","Define the term in your opening sentence, then work down the scales from national schemes to village ones."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (increasing supply)","3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"Explain why the demand for water is rising in many parts of the world. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. Credit developed explanation of rising population and of economic development and rising affluence — industry, irrigation, sanitation, changing diets — rather than a list of causes. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Two things are happening at once: there are more people, and each of them uses more. World population passed 8 billion in November 2022, and every extra person needs drinking water, water for cooking and washing, and a share of the water used to grow their food, so demand in a river basin rises even if nobody changes how they live. Rapid urbanisation concentrates that demand, because a city draws from one supply for millions of people and for the sanitation systems that serve them, and improved sanitation itself raises water use per person. Economic development then multiplies consumption again. Industry needs water for processing and washing, thermal power stations need very large volumes for cooling, and irrigated agriculture — already about 70 per cent of global freshwater withdrawals — expands as countries try to feed themselves and grow crops for export. Rising affluence changes households in the same direction, bringing showers, washing machines, dishwashers, car washing and garden watering. Diet matters more than most students realise, because producing a kilogram of beef takes thousands of litres of water, many times what a kilogram of wheat needs, so as diets become more meat-rich the water used to feed one person climbs sharply. Climate change adds to the pressure, since higher temperatures increase evaporation and irrigation need. Demand therefore rises fastest exactly where populations are growing and economies are developing quickly, which is often where supply was already least reliable.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Demand can rise because there are more users or because each user takes more. Both are happening.","Think about what changes in a country's farms, factories and homes as it develops.","Take population first and development second, and put a figure or an example in each half."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, suggest reasons for the pattern of water availability shown in Africa. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. Credit application of climate, geology and human factors to the mapped pattern: the wet equatorial belt of the Congo basin against the subtropical deserts north and south, with population size, over-abstraction and infrastructure explaining exceptions. No credit for describing the map alone. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks.","sol":"The pattern follows the climate belts first. The countries in the highest class — the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Gabon and Liberia — lie in or beside the equatorial zone, where convectional rainfall is heavy and falls all year and the Congo river collects run-off from an enormous basin, so there is a great deal of water and relatively few people to divide it between. The lowest class runs across North Africa because Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Libya and Egypt lie in the subtropical belt of sinking, drying air that produces the Sahara, where rainfall is minimal and evaporation high; South Africa is scarce for the equivalent reason at the southern edge of the continent. Geology reinforces this, since water falling on desert rock may sink into aquifers that are slow or impossible to recharge. But the map cannot be explained by climate alone, and the exceptions prove it. The measure is water per person, so a large population divides the total: Egypt receives almost no rain yet has the Nile, and it appears in the scarcest class chiefly because that one river is shared between so many people. Kenya and Burkina Faso sit in tropical Africa, where rain does fall, but it is seasonal and unreliable, populations are growing quickly, and storage and treatment infrastructure is limited, so much of the rain is never captured. Pollution and over-abstraction reduce usable supply further. The pattern is physical in outline and human in detail.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two of the four classes line up with well-known climate belts. Identify them first.","The measure is water per person, so ask what the denominator does to a country like Egypt.","Explain the broad north–south pattern with climate, then use the odd ones out to bring in human factors."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Using an example of a large-scale water transfer scheme, discuss the advantages and disadvantages of its development. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. A named scheme is required, used throughout. Credit specific and accurate knowledge of the scheme and thorough understanding of the interrelationships between the donor and the receiving region, developed into a balanced judgement rather than two lists. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"China's South–North Water Transfer Project exists because China's water and China's people are in the wrong places: roughly four fifths of the country's water lies in the humid south, while the north holds much of the farmland and cities such as Beijing and Tianjin. For decades the north closed that gap by pumping groundwater until the water table beneath the North China Plain fell year after year.\nThe advantages are measurable. The Central Route, opened in December 2014, carries water about 1400 km by gravity from the Danjiangkou reservoir on the Han river to Beijing and Tianjin, and now supplies a large share of the capital's tap water. Because the city pumps less, Beijing has reported its groundwater level rising again, which also slows the subsidence that over-abstraction had been causing.\nThe disadvantages fall on different people. The bill is put at more than 60 billion US dollars; more than 300 000 people were resettled for the Central Route when the Danjiangkou dam was raised; and below the dam the Han river runs lower, so the donor region loses fishing and navigation for water it never receives. The Eastern Route must also be pumped uphill.\nOn balance the scheme is justified for the receiving region, because nothing else could have let an exhausted aquifer recover so quickly. But it moves the problem rather than removing it: unless the north also meters supply, recycles wastewater and repairs its leaks, demand will grow to fill the canal.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Discuss means both sides developed, not a list of good points followed by a list of bad ones.","Ask of every point you make: who gains from this, and who pays for it?","Open with the problem the scheme was built to solve, so you have a standard to judge both sides against."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (increasing supply)"]},{"q":"'Water insecurity is caused more by human factors than by physical ones.' To what extent do you agree? [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. 'To what extent' requires an evaluation of the relative importance of the two sets of factors, not a list. Credit climate, geology and drought set against population growth, over-abstraction, pollution, poverty and limited infrastructure, with examples, and a judgement that follows from the evidence. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"Physical factors set the ceiling on how much water a place can have, so they cannot be dismissed. Climate decides the total: the belt of sinking air that creates the Sahara gives Libya and Egypt almost no rain, while the equatorial Congo basin receives heavy convectional rain all year. Geology decides how much of that rain is stored, since porous rocks such as sandstone hold groundwater in aquifers while impermeable rocks shed it, and drought alters both rainfall and evaporation.\nBut the map of water insecurity does not match the map of rainfall, and that is the strongest evidence for the human side. Insecurity is measured per person, so population growth alone can push a country below the scarcity threshold of 1000 m³ per person per year without a millimetre of rain being lost: Egypt has the Nile and is still in the scarcest class because that one river is divided between so many people. Beijing's crisis was made by over-abstraction, which is a decision rather than a climate. Pollution removes usable water from supplies that were physically adequate, and poverty and missing infrastructure turn a shortage into a catastrophe: around two billion people lack safely managed drinking water at home, often where the rain falls but nothing captures and cleans it.\nI agree to a large extent. Physical geography explains the broad global pattern, but human decisions explain almost all the variation within it, and they are the factors that can be changed.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'To what extent' asks how far, not whether, so the answer has to end somewhere between the two extremes.","Test the claim by looking for places where the physical factors are similar but the outcomes are not.","Give the physical case properly first, then show what it fails to explain, and let that gap carry your judgement."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)"]},{"q":"Assess the effectiveness of a local scheme in an LIC or NEE in increasing sustainable supplies of water. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. A named local scheme is required. Credit specific and accurate knowledge of the techniques used, thorough understanding of why they provide a lasting supply, and an overall judgement of effectiveness that recognises the limits as well as the benefits. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"The Wakel river basin in southern Rajasthan, north-west India, is one of the driest parts of the country: almost all the rain arrives in the June to September monsoon, and tube-well pumping had been lowering the water table, so wells failed in the hot season. The project harvests the monsoon rain locally instead.\nThree techniques do most of the work. A taanka is a covered underground tank fed by a roof or a swept catchment apron; a large one holds in the order of twenty thousand litres and, being buried and sealed, loses little to evaporation. A johad is a crescent-shaped earth bank that ponds run-off long enough for it to soak downwards and recharge the groundwater, so nearby wells last further into the year. A pat is a contour channel carrying water to the fields.\nJudged against sustainability the scheme is effective. It is built from local materials by local labour, so villagers can repair it; it floods nothing and displaces nobody; and it puts water back into the aquifer instead of taking more out. Shorter walks for water free girls to attend school, and more reliable irrigation makes a second crop possible.\nIts limits are equally clear. Everything depends on the monsoon, the volumes suit households and fields rather than a city, and it does nothing about polluted supplies. It is highly effective at the scale it was designed for, but a complement to larger schemes rather than a replacement.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Assess means reaching an informed overall judgement, so decide what counts as effective before you weigh anything.","Sustainable has a definition. Test the scheme against each part of it in turn.","Name the scheme, describe its techniques in detail, then judge it on cost, repairability and what happens to the water table."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"'Only large-scale schemes such as dams, water transfers and desalination can solve water insecurity.' Evaluate this view. [6 marks]","a":"Levels 1–3. Credit a balanced evaluation with evidence on both sides: what large schemes achieve and what they cost, set against conservation, recycling, groundwater management and small-scale appropriate technology, leading to a supported conclusion. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks.","sol":"There is a serious case for the view, because only large schemes deliver water at the volume a city or an irrigated plain needs. A dam turns an unreliable river into a dependable supply and generates power. A transfer moves water between basins on a scale nothing else matches: the Central Route of China's South–North Water Transfer Project, opened in December 2014, carries water about 1400 km to Beijing and has allowed the city's groundwater level to begin recovering. Desalination makes fresh water from an effectively unlimited source, as Israel's Sorek plant has done since 2013.\nThe case against is that these schemes are expensive, slow and unevenly fair. The South–North project is costed at more than 60 billion US dollars and resettled more than 300 000 people, while the Han river below the Danjiangkou dam now runs lower, so the donor region pays for water it does not receive. Adding supply without controlling demand also invites demand to grow until the new supply is short too.\nSmaller measures answer that cheaply. Metering, efficient appliances and leak repair release water already treated, since roughly a fifth of the water put into public supply in England and Wales leaks away; the covered rainwater tanks of the Wakel river basin in Rajasthan recharge the water table instead of draining it.\nThe view is therefore half right: large engineering is necessary where the deficit is large and urgent, but it is not sufficient, and for the rural poor it is irrelevant.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Evaluate means the evidence on both sides and a judgement, not a description of the schemes.","Ask what large schemes can do that nothing else can, and then what they cannot do at all.","Give the strongest version of the statement first, then attack it, and decide in the final paragraph."],"codes":["3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (increasing supply)","3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (sustainable resource future)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Below how many cubic metres of renewable water per person per year is a country said to be in water scarcity?","a":"1000 m³ per person per year (water stress begins below about 1700 m³)."},{"q":"Which sector takes about 70 per cent of global freshwater withdrawals?","a":"Agriculture — mainly irrigation."},{"q":"In which month and year did the Central Route of China's South–North Water Transfer Project open?","a":"December 2014."},{"q":"What is a taanka?","a":"A covered underground tank that stores rainwater collected from a roof or a prepared sloping catchment."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Water security","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Sufficient access to enough safe water to meet people's needs, taken in a way that does not damage the supply or environment for the future."},{"front":"Water surplus and water deficit","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Surplus: supply of water is greater than demand. Deficit (water insecurity): demand is greater than supply, so needs cannot all be met."},{"front":"The water stress and water scarcity thresholds","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Water stress below about 1700 m³ of renewable water per person per year; water scarcity below 1000 m³ per person per year."},{"front":"Two reasons why water consumption is rising globally","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":true,"back":"Population growth, so the same supply is divided between more people; economic development, as industry, power stations and irrigation expand; rising affluence, bringing showers, washing machines and meat-rich diets."},{"front":"Factors that affect how much water is available in a place","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":true,"back":"Climate and its reliability; geology, since permeable rock stores groundwater; pollution of the supply; over-abstraction; limited infrastructure such as pipes and reservoirs; poverty."},{"front":"Over-abstraction","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Taking water from a river or aquifer faster than it is replaced, so water tables fall, wells and wetlands dry out, sea water can seep into coastal aquifers and the ground may subside."},{"front":"The impacts of water insecurity","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","open":true,"back":"Waterborne disease and polluted supplies; reduced food production, so malnutrition and higher prices; lower industrial output and lost jobs; conflict wherever demand exceeds supply."},{"front":"Waterborne diseases carried by unsafe water","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","open":false,"back":"Cholera, typhoid and dysentery, spread where sewage contaminates drinking water; unsafe water, sanitation and hygiene cause hundreds of thousands of diarrhoeal deaths a year, mostly children under five."},{"front":"Rivers where water is disputed between countries","code":"3.2.3.3 — Demand for water resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","open":true,"back":"The Nile, where Ethiopia's Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam concerns Egypt and Sudan; the Tigris and Euphrates, where Turkish dams cut flow to Syria and Iraq; the Indus, shared by India and Pakistan under a 1960 treaty."},{"front":"Strategies used to increase water supply","code":"3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (increasing supply)","open":true,"back":"Diverting supplies and increasing storage; dams and reservoirs; water transfers between basins; desalination of sea water."},{"front":"Desalination","code":"3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"Removing salt from sea water, by distillation or, more usually, reverse osmosis through a membrane. It makes new fresh water but is costly, energy-hungry and leaves brine to dispose of."},{"front":"China's South–North Water Transfer Project: the key facts","code":"3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"Eastern Route supplying since 2013 along the old Grand Canal; Central Route opened December 2014, about 1400 km by gravity from the Danjiangkou reservoir on the Han river to Beijing and Tianjin."},{"front":"Two disadvantages of the South–North Water Transfer Project","code":"3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (increasing supply)","open":true,"back":"Cost put at more than 60 billion US dollars; more than 300 000 people resettled for the Central Route; the Han river below the Danjiangkou dam runs lower, harming the donor region; the Eastern Route must be pumped uphill."},{"front":"Grey water","code":"3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (sustainable resource future)","open":false,"back":"Lightly used water from baths, showers, washbasins and washing machines, kept apart from sewage and reused for flushing toilets or watering gardens, so treated water is not wasted."},{"front":"The Wakel river basin project: the three techniques","code":"3.2.3.3 — Different strategies can be used to increase water supply (sustainable resource future)","open":false,"back":"Taanka, a covered underground rainwater tank; johad, an earth bank that ponds run-off and recharges groundwater; pat, a contour channel carrying water to fields."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Map described for this room: a choropleth map of Africa showing total renewable water resources per person per year, drawn in four classes with a key, a scale bar and a north arrow. The classes, from the lightest shading to the darkest, are: less than 1000 m³ per person per year (the water scarcity class), 1000 to 2500 m³, 2500 to 10 000 m³, and more than 10 000 m³. The countries named on the map, with the class each falls in:\nLess than 1000 m³ | Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Libya, Egypt, Kenya, Burkina Faso, South Africa\n1000 to 2500 m³ | Nigeria, Tanzania\n2500 to 10 000 m³ | Angola, Zambia\nMore than 10 000 m³ | Democratic Republic of the Congo, Gabon, Liberia\nOnly the lowest boundary, 1000 m³, is a recognised threshold: it is the water scarcity line. The 2500 and 10 000 m³ boundaries are map classes chosen for this room, not thresholds, and the water stress threshold of about 1700 m³ is not a boundary here at all — it falls inside the 1000 to 2500 m³ class. Each country named is placed in the class that published estimates of its total renewable water resources per person put it in.","Figure 2 — Graph described for this room: a bar chart of how the world's freshwater withdrawals are shared between the three main users. Horizontal axis: three bars, labelled agriculture, industry and domestic. Vertical axis: percentage of global freshwater withdrawals, scaled 0 to 80 and marked every 10. Plotted values: agriculture 70, industry 20, domestic 10. The shares are the real global figures, rounded to the nearest ten per cent; agriculture here means irrigation and livestock, industry includes cooling water for power stations, and domestic means the water used in homes and by municipal services.","Figure 3 — Text extract written for this room. The scheme, the places, the dates and the engineering are real; the three speakers are illustrative.\nA water engineer in Beijing: 'Before the Central Route opened in December 2014 we were pumping the aquifer under this city faster than the rain could refill it, and the water table dropped year after year. Now a canal of about 1400 km brings water north from the Danjiangkou reservoir on the Han river, and the city has reported its groundwater level rising again for the first time in decades. The tap water is cleaner as well.'\nA farmer in Hubei, below the Danjiangkou dam: 'They raised the dam and the enlarged reservoir took our village. More than 300 000 of us were moved for the Central Route. The compensation was paid, but the new land is poorer, and the Han river below the dam runs lower now, so the fishing and the boats are not what they were. The water goes to Beijing; the loss stays here.'\nAn official on the Eastern Route: 'Our route follows the old Grand Canal and has to be pumped uphill the whole way, so it costs electricity every day it runs. We also had to build a long line of sewage treatment works before we could send that water north at all, because the canal was badly polluted. The bill for the whole project is put at more than 60 billion US dollars.'","Figure 4 — Photograph described for this room: a village in southern Rajasthan, north-west India, photographed at the end of the dry season. In the foreground a woman is lowering a bucket on a rope through a small square opening in a low circular rim of plastered brick set almost flush with the ground; a swept, gently sloping apron of plastered ground runs down towards the opening, and a shallow channel leads to the same opening from the roof of the nearest house. Behind the houses a curved bank of packed earth, about waist high, runs across a shallow valley and holds back a crescent of muddy brown water perhaps thirty metres across, with cattle drinking at its edge and a wide ring of cracked, dried mud showing how far the water has already shrunk back. Beyond the bank the land is brown, stony and bare. A narrow earth channel runs along the hillside contour from the far end of the pond towards a small bright green field of crops. The sky is cloudless."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — a large-scale water transfer: the South–North Water Transfer Project, China","items":["What it is: the largest water transfer scheme ever built, moving water from the Yangtze river basin in the humid south of China to the dry north.","Why it was needed: roughly four fifths of China's water is in the south, while the north holds much of the country's farmland and its biggest northern cities, Beijing and Tianjin.","The problem it answers: for decades the north made up the shortfall by pumping groundwater, so the water table beneath the North China Plain fell year after year and parts of the plain subsided.","The plan: three routes — Eastern, Central and Western. The Eastern and Central routes have been built; the Western route, which would have crossed the Tibetan plateau into the Yellow river, has not.","Eastern Route: supplying water since 2013, it follows and enlarges the line of the ancient Grand Canal from the lower Yangtze northwards, and needs large pumping stations because the water must be lifted uphill all the way.","Central Route: opened in December 2014, about 1400 km long, running by gravity from the Danjiangkou reservoir on the Han river (a tributary of the Yangtze) in Hubei to Beijing and Tianjin.","Engineering: the Danjiangkou dam was raised to enlarge the reservoir feeding the Central Route, and the canal crosses the Yellow river in tunnels beneath its bed.","Cost: put at more than 60 billion US dollars, more than the Three Gorges Dam, making it one of the most expensive engineering projects ever undertaken.","Resettlement: more than 300 000 people were moved for the Central Route as the enlarged reservoir and the canal corridor took their land.","Advantage — supply: the Central Route now provides a large share of Beijing's tap water, a supply the city could not have obtained any other way.","Advantage — recovering groundwater: Beijing has reported its groundwater level rising again since the transfers began, because the city no longer has to pump its aquifer so hard.","Advantage — water quality: the Eastern Route required a long line of sewage treatment works to be built before polluted canal water could be sent north, which improved water quality along the corridor; Beijing's tap water is also cleaner.","Advantage — economy: secure water protects irrigated farming and industrial output in the north, including power-station cooling, which is a national as well as a local benefit.","Disadvantage — the donor region: below the Danjiangkou dam the Han river runs lower, damaging fishing, navigation and wetlands for people who receive none of the transferred water.","Disadvantage — energy and losses: the Eastern Route burns electricity every day it is pumped uphill, and open canals lose water to evaporation and seepage along their length.","Disadvantage — the deeper objection: a bigger supply can invite bigger demand, so unless the north also meters, recycles and repairs leaks the scheme moves the shortage rather than removing it, and in a drought both regions suffer at once.","A second transfer example, if you prefer it: the Lesotho Highlands Water Project sends water by dam and tunnel from the mountains of Lesotho to South Africa's industrial Gauteng region; Lesotho receives royalty payments in return, but reservoirs such as Katse, completed in the 1990s, flooded highland valleys and displaced farming families."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — a local sustainable scheme in an LIC or NEE: the Wakel river basin, Rajasthan, India","items":["Where: the Wakel river basin in southern Rajasthan, north-west India — Rajasthan is India's largest state by area and one of its driest, on the edge of the Thar Desert.","Climate: almost all the rain falls in the June to September monsoon, the annual total is low and unreliable from year to year, and very high summer temperatures mean heavy evaporation losses.","The physical problem: the rivers of the basin flow only after rain, so there is no dependable surface supply through the long dry season.","The human problem: tube-well pumping had been taking groundwater faster than the monsoon could recharge it, so water tables fell and wells and hand pumps failed in the hot season.","Who it affects: women and girls do most of the water carrying, so a falling water table means longer walks, less time at school and more illness from poor-quality water.","The approach: a community-based project, run in partnership with the Global Water Partnership, that harvests and stores the monsoon rain locally and trains villagers to build, run and maintain the structures themselves.","Taanka: a covered underground tank, lined and sealed, fed by rainwater running off a roof or a swept, plastered sloping catchment; a large one holds in the order of twenty thousand litres, enough to carry a family through the dry months.","Why the taanka works: being buried and covered, it loses very little water to evaporation and keeps out animals, dust and sunlight, so the water stays both plentiful and fit to drink.","Johad: a crescent-shaped bank of packed earth built across a slope or gully that ponds monsoon run-off so the water has time to soak downwards and recharge the groundwater, making nearby wells last further into the year and watering livestock while it lasts.","Pat: a channel dug along the hillside contour that diverts water from a stream or pond to irrigate fields, with each user responsible for maintaining the stretch that serves them.","Education: villagers are taught about water conservation, crop choice and maintenance, which is what keeps the structures working once the project staff have gone.","Why it is sustainable: it is built from local materials by local labour, so it is affordable and repairable by the people who use it; it recharges the aquifer instead of draining it; it floods nothing and displaces nobody.","Benefits: more reliable drinking water, shorter walks so girls can attend school, better health, a second crop made possible by irrigation, and less need to migrate for work.","Limits: everything depends on the monsoon, so a failed monsoon leaves little run-off to harvest; the volumes suit households and fields but could not supply a city or heavy industry; it does not address polluted supplies; and it needs continuing community organisation.","The wider point it illustrates: appropriate (intermediate) technology — simple, cheap equipment that local people can build, maintain and repair — is how non-governmental organisations such as WaterAid and Practical Action improve water security in small communities."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — water processes, terms and figures","items":["The global store: about 97 per cent of the Earth's water is salt water; of the small fresh remainder, roughly two thirds is frozen in ice caps and glaciers, and most of the rest is groundwater.","Thresholds: water stress below about 1700 m³ per person per year; water scarcity below 1000 m³ per person per year.","Water security: sufficient access to meet people's water needs while limiting the negative consequences of that water withdrawal.","Areas of surplus: the wet equatorial and temperate regions with few people relative to their rainfall — the Congo basin, Amazonia, Canada, Scandinavia, New Zealand and western Britain.","Areas of deficit: North Africa and the Sahara, the Middle East and Arabian peninsula, central Asia, the south-west United States, central Australia, and the densely populated dry regions of northern China, north-west India and south-east England.","Why consumption rises: population growth (world population passed 8 billion in November 2022); economic development in industry, power generation and irrigation; rising affluence, including meat-rich diets, since a kilogram of beef takes thousands of litres of water to produce.","Who uses the water: agriculture about 70 per cent of global freshwater withdrawals, industry about 20 per cent, domestic use about 10 per cent.","Availability depends on: climate; geology (permeable rocks such as chalk and sandstone hold groundwater in aquifers); pollution; over-abstraction; limited infrastructure; and poverty.","Health impact: around two billion people do not have safely managed drinking water at home, and unsafe water, sanitation and hygiene cause hundreds of thousands of diarrhoeal deaths each year, mostly among children under five; cholera, typhoid and dysentery are the classic waterborne diseases.","Conflict: the Nile and Ethiopia's Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam, begun in 2011, on which Egypt depends heavily for fresh water; the Tigris and Euphrates below Turkey's dams; the Colorado, so heavily abstracted by seven US states and Mexico that it often fails to reach the sea; the Indus, shared by India and Pakistan under a World Bank-brokered treaty since 1960.","Dams and reservoirs: the Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze, fully generating by 2012, has an installed capacity of 22 500 MW and its reservoir required the resettlement of roughly 1.3 million people; dams also drown land, trap sediment and lose water to evaporation.","A diversion that failed: irrigation canals taking the Amu Darya and Syr Darya for cotton from the 1960s shrank the Aral Sea to a fraction of its former size, leaving salt flats, fishing ports stranded inland and a damaged local climate.","Desalination: distillation boils sea water and condenses the steam; reverse osmosis forces it through a membrane under pressure. Israel's Sorek plant, working since 2013, is one of the world's largest reverse-osmosis plants and supplies well over a hundred million cubic metres a year; London's Thames Gateway plant at Beckton, opened in 2010, is run only when supplies are short because it is so expensive.","Water conservation: metering, dual-flush toilets, efficient appliances, drip irrigation and leak repair — roughly a fifth of the water put into public supply in England and Wales leaks away before it reaches a tap.","Groundwater management: abstraction licences, monitored water tables and artificial recharge, because over-abstraction dries wetlands and springs, lets sea water into coastal aquifers and causes subsidence.","Recycling: treated wastewater reused for industry and agriculture — Singapore's NEWater plants, running since 2003, can meet up to about 40 per cent of that country's water demand.","Grey water: the lightly used water from baths, showers, washbasins and washing machines, kept separate from sewage and reused for flushing toilets or watering gardens."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) on the 6-mark question: 'Using an example of a large-scale water transfer scheme, discuss the advantages and disadvantages of its development.'","items":["The paragraph: The clearest advantage of China's South–North Water Transfer Project is that it has done something no amount of local saving could have done in the time available. The Central Route, opened in December 2014, carries water about 1400 km by gravity from the Danjiangkou reservoir on the Han river to Beijing and Tianjin, and it now supplies a large share of the capital's tap water. Because the city no longer has to pump its own aquifer so hard, Beijing has reported its groundwater level rising again after decades of falling, and the subsidence that over-abstraction had been causing beneath the North China Plain is slowed with it. Set against that, the costs fall on people who receive none of the water: more than 300 000 people were resettled for the Central Route when the Danjiangkou dam was raised, and below that dam the Han river now runs lower, damaging fishing and navigation in Hubei. The scheme is therefore defensible for Beijing, whose aquifer was genuinely being exhausted, but only if the north now meters, recycles and repairs its leaks, because otherwise demand will grow to fill the canal and the south will be asked for more.","Why this reaches Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) on the question 05.4 grid: AO1 — it demonstrates specific and accurate knowledge of a way of improving water security, with the route, the date, the distance, the source reservoir and the number resettled; AO2 — it shows thorough understanding of the interrelationships between environments and processes in the context of water security issues, carried as a chain from the exhausted aquifer to the canal to the donor basin rather than as a list; and AO2 — it demonstrates in detail how the improvement provides a secure supply of water while still weighing what that supply costs, which is what 'discuss' asks for at this tariff.","What to copy from it: one named place and one figure attached to every point; a 'because' or 'so' in every sentence that carries a process; the donor region and the receiving region always named separately, so the examiner can see you know who pays; and a final sentence that decides something and attaches a condition to it."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The paragraph: Water transfer schemes move water from a place that has lots of water to a place that does not have much. China has built a very big one to take water to the north because the north is dry. This is good because people there now get water and can drink it and use it for farming. However it is bad as well because it cost a lot of money and some people had to move house, which they would not have liked. Also it might damage the environment. So there are advantages and disadvantages and both sides are important.","Which descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) — AO1 demonstrates limited knowledge of ways of improving water security, and AO2 shows only simple understanding of the interrelationships between environments and processes in the context of water security issues.","Which descriptor it misses: Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) asks for thorough understanding of the interrelationships between environments and processes in the context of water security issues and for detail on how the improvement provides a secure supply of water. This answer names no route, no river, no reservoir, no date and no figure; 'it might damage the environment' explains no mechanism; and the last sentence refuses to decide anything at all.","The three fixes that would move it up: name the route, the source reservoir, the date it opened and the distance, and attach a figure to the cost and to the number resettled; explain one mechanism the whole way through, for example over-abstraction lowering the water table and the transfer letting it recover; and end by saying which side weighs more and under what condition."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Using an example of a large-scale water transfer scheme, discuss the advantages and disadvantages of its development. [6 marks]'","items":["Decode the command: 'discuss' wants both sides developed and then weighed, not a list of good points followed by a list of bad ones. The scheme must be named and used throughout; a general answer about transfers earns Level 1 however well written. Only the development of the scheme counts — its advantages and disadvantages — so do not drift into other ways of increasing supply. At six marks it is marked on the Paper 2 question 05.4 grid, Levels 1–3, with AO1 = 2 marks and AO2 = 4 marks, so most of the credit is for understanding how the scheme changes the supply and who it changes it for, not for the facts on their own.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly six minutes of writing after a minute of planning. Section C sets no extended nine-mark question and awards no SPaG marks here, so there is nothing to be gained by writing longer. Three short paragraphs is the right ambition, not five, and leave the last thirty seconds to read the answer back, because an examiner can only credit an argument they can follow.","Paragraph 1 — the problem and the strongest advantage together, because six marks will not carry a separate scene-setting paragraph: roughly four fifths of China's water is in the humid south while much of the farmland and cities such as Beijing and Tianjin are in the north, which had been closing the gap by pumping groundwater until the water table beneath the North China Plain fell and the ground subsided; then the Central Route, opened December 2014, about 1400 km by gravity from the Danjiangkou reservoir on the Han river, now supplies a large share of Beijing's tap water, with the city reporting its groundwater level rising again.","Paragraph 2 — the disadvantages, and who carries them: a cost put at more than 60 billion US dollars; more than 300 000 people resettled for the Central Route when the Danjiangkou dam was raised; the Han river below the dam running lower, so the donor region loses fishing and navigation for water it never receives; and the Eastern Route burning electricity to pump uphill every day. Two costs developed beat five costs listed.","The evaluation thread that runs through every paragraph: ask of each point, who gains from this and who pays for it? Keep returning to the fact that the benefit is concentrated in the receiving region and the cost is concentrated in the donor region, because that single asymmetry explains both the scheme's success and the objections to it.","What the conclusion must say: that the scheme is justified for the receiving region, because nothing else could have refilled an exhausted aquifer so quickly, but that it moves the problem rather than removing it — so it is defensible only as part of a wider strategy in which the north also meters supply, recycles wastewater and repairs its leaks, since otherwise demand will grow to fill the canal and the south will be asked for more."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:17:44Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"I checked the structural claim myself before judging the rebuild, and it holds: the specification (page 8) gives Paper 2 Section C 25 marks for 'question 3 and one from questions 4, 5 or 6', and in 80352-SMS question 3 runs 1 + 2 + 6 + 1 + 1 + 3 = 14 marks while question 5 Water runs 05.1 = 1 (AO4), 05.2 = 2 (AO4), 05.3 = 2 (AO1) and 05.4 = 6 (AO1 = 2, AO2 = 4) = 11 marks, so 14 + 11 = 25 and the Water option sets no 9-mark question and carries no SPaG, which is on question 01.8 in Section A. That is a real fidelity fault I missed at both of my earlier passes and it is now properly fixed, not papered over: the four extended questions are 6-markers with m = 6, the stems read '[6 marks]', the solutions are 232-246 words and all nine 6-mark solutions sit inside the 150-250 band, the model paragraph pair is retitled to the right bands ('Level 3 (Detailed, 5-6)' and 'Level 1 (Basic, 1-2)') and quotes the genuine 05.4 descriptors, the plan and worked example 4 are rebuilt to a six-mark shape with the three-paragraph plan merged to two and the example's steps and cues cut to four, and lesson.why and the new room.facts item state the Section C tariffs correctly. The previous verdict's one cosmetic problem, the editorial SPaG note inside room.questions[12].a, is gone. Nothing else regressed: all 16 stems are at tariffs the papers set (1, 2, 4, 6), six questions at d=1, six at d=2 and four at d=3 with the d=3 four genuinely the harder evaluative commands, which is the only honest reading of the tiers once no 9-marker is available; Figure 1's four classes and fifteen country placements, Figure 2's 70/20/10 and Figure 3's quoted figures are unchanged and I re-recounted Q0, Q1, Q2, Q7 and Q11 against them; and every date, place, figure and process in the rewritten solutions is one I had already verified (December 2014 and about 1400 km from Danjiangkou, more than 300 000 resettled, more than US$60 billion, Eastern Route 2013, Sorek since 2013, the 1000 m3 scarcity threshold, about 70 per cent of withdrawals to agriculture, roughly a fifth of English and Welsh public supply lost to leaks, the taanka, johad and pat of the Wakel basin). Publishable."}}},"3.2.3.4":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.2.3.4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Energy is one of the three resource options that close Paper 2, and it is the one where students most often write about wind turbines in general and never about supply, security, or who pays. The examiner is looking for two named examples you can carry into an answer under pressure: one that shows the extraction of a fossil fuel bringing real benefits as well as real damage, and one that shows a small renewable scheme giving a community in an LIC or NEE a sustainable supply. This room fixes both in place — the Camisea natural gas project in the Peruvian Amazon and the Chambamontera micro-hydro scheme in the mountains of northern Peru — with the places, dates, processes and figures that go with them. It also fixes the vocabulary the mark scheme uses: energy security is the extent to which an affordable, reliable and stable energy supply can be achieved, and every strategy you name has to be judged against those three words. The marks at the top of every levels grid go to answers that weigh two sides and reach a judgement, not to answers that list.","idea":[{"h":"Surplus and deficit: who has energy, who needs it, and how you describe the pattern","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","t":"A country with an energy surplus produces more than it consumes and can export the difference: Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Russia, Norway, Canada and Australia are the familiar examples. A country in energy deficit consumes more than it produces and must import, which is the position of Japan, of most of the European Union and, since North Sea output passed its peak around 1999, of the United Kingdom, which became a net importer of natural gas in 2004. Energy security is the extent to which an affordable, reliable and stable energy supply can be achieved; energy insecurity is what you have when any one of those three words fails. The global pattern of consumption is very uneven. Use per person, not in total, is highest in North America, the Gulf states, Europe, Australia and increasingly China, and lowest across sub-Saharan Africa, where roughly 700 to 800 million people worldwide still have no electricity supply at all and more than two billion cook on wood, charcoal or dung. Supply is uneven in a different way again, because the fossil fuels that still provide about 80% of the world's primary energy are concentrated in a small number of places — around half of proven oil reserves lie around the Persian Gulf — so the countries that use the most are rarely the countries that hold the most. A top-level answer never stops at the pattern: it states the distribution from the resource, then explains the mismatch between where energy is used and where it is found, because that mismatch is what makes supply insecure."},{"h":"Why demand keeps rising: development, population and technology","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","t":"The specification gives three reasons and expects you to link them. Economic development is the largest: as a country industrialises, factories, offices, construction, freight and air travel all draw power, and as incomes rise people buy fridges, televisions, air conditioning and cars, so energy use per person climbs. This is why the fastest growth in demand is in NEEs such as China and India rather than in the HICs, where demand has been roughly flat or falling. Rising population multiplies that effect: world population passed 8 billion in November 2022, and almost all further growth is in the countries whose energy use per person is rising fastest. Technology cuts both ways, and saying so is worth a mark. New technologies create demand — vehicles, data centres, cooling — while other technologies cut it, because an LED bulb, a condensing boiler or a modern gas turbine does the same job for far less fuel. A top-level answer multiplies rather than lists: more people, each using more, in countries where more of that energy now comes from the grid rather than from firewood, is the sentence the examiner is waiting for."},{"h":"What decides whether the supply is there: physical factors, cost, technology and politics","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","t":"Physical factors come first, because you can only extract what geology and climate provide. Coal, oil and gas sit where ancient swamps and seas were buried; geothermal power needs hot rock close to the surface, which is why Iceland has it; hydro-electricity needs steep relief and reliable rainfall; solar output is highest in low latitudes with clear skies; wind needs exposed coasts, uplands and shallow seas. Cost of exploitation and production decides which of those resources is actually worth taking: deepwater oil, Arctic drilling and tar sands are expensive and only pay when prices are high, while the cost of electricity from large solar farms has fallen by roughly 90% since 2010, which is why solar has spread so fast. Technology changes the sum: hydraulic fracturing combined with horizontal drilling unlocked shale gas and oil and turned the United States into the world's largest natural gas producer, and ever larger offshore turbines made deep-water wind viable. Political factors can override all three. Decisions by oil-exporting states, sanctions and war can cut supply overnight, as Europe found when Russia invaded Ukraine in February 2022; Germany closed its last nuclear reactors in April 2023 by choice; and the United Kingdom halted fracking in England in November 2019 after tremors, the largest of magnitude 2.9, at the Preston New Road site in Lancashire. A top-level answer names the factor that dominates in the case it is discussing and says why the others matter less there."},{"h":"What energy insecurity does to places and people","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","t":"Insecurity pushes exploration into difficult and environmentally sensitive areas: deepwater, where the Deepwater Horizon rig exploded in the Gulf of Mexico in April 2010, killing 11 workers and releasing millions of barrels of oil over nearly three months; the Arctic, where ice, darkness and distance raise costs and any spill is almost impossible to clean; and rainforest, as at Camisea. The economic costs are felt by everyone: when supply tightens, prices rise, and because energy is an input to everything else, inflation follows and poorer households fall into fuel poverty. Food production is hit twice over, because nitrogen fertiliser is manufactured from natural gas and because irrigation pumps, tractors, processing and refrigerated transport all run on fuel; when European gas prices spiked in 2021 and 2022, fertiliser plants shut down and food prices rose with them. Industrial output falls where supply is unreliable: South Africa's scheduled power cuts, at their worst in 2023, have repeatedly shut factories and mines for hours at a time. Finally, where demand exceeds supply there is potential for conflict — over pipelines and the pricing of gas, over shipping routes such as the Strait of Hormuz, through which roughly a fifth of the world's oil passes, and over reserves in disputed waters. A top-level answer chains insecurity to one named consequence and does not leave the chain hanging at 'prices go up'."},{"h":"Strategies to increase supply: what each source is actually good at","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)","t":"The specification names the renewables you must be able to talk about — biomass, wind, hydro, tidal, geothermal, wave and solar — and the non-renewables, fossil fuels and nuclear power. Judge each on four things: reliability, cost, carbon, and local impact. Fossil fuels are reliable, transportable and cheap to burn in plant that already exists, but they are finite and are the main cause of climate change, although burning gas releases roughly half the carbon dioxide of coal for the same electricity. Nuclear power gives large, steady, low-carbon output — about two-thirds of France's electricity — but costs a great deal to build and leaves radioactive waste, and public support falls after accidents such as Fukushima in March 2011. Hydro is reliable and long-lived and can be built very large: the Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze, fully operational in 2012, has an installed capacity of 22,500 MW, but around 1.3 million people had to be moved for it. Wind and solar are now cheap and quick to build but intermittent, so they need storage, interconnectors or gas plant behind them. Tidal is entirely predictable but needs a large range and a suitable estuary; wave power is still small-scale; geothermal is limited to volcanic areas; biomass is renewable only if what is burnt is regrown. A top-level answer compares on these criteria instead of announcing that renewables are good and fossil fuels are bad."},{"h":"The fossil fuel example: Camisea, and why extraction is always a trade-off","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)","t":"Camisea is a natural gas field in the Lower Urubamba valley of the Cusco region, in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru. Gas was found there by Shell in the 1980s, and after Shell withdrew a consortium led by the Argentine company Pluspetrol brought the field into production in 2004. Two pipelines run roughly 700 km from the rainforest and over the Andes at more than 4,000 m, one carrying natural gas to Lima, on the Pacific coast, and one carrying natural gas liquids to the coast at Pisco. For Peru the gains are national: a cheap domestic fuel in place of imported oil, gas-fired power stations that are now, with hydropower, one of the two main sources of the country's electricity, thousands of Lima taxis converted to run on gas, export earnings since the LNG plant at Pampa Melchorita opened in 2010, and royalties shared with the Cusco region through a government fund. The costs are local and environmental: the field lies beside a reserve set aside for indigenous groups living in voluntary isolation, for whom contact carries disease; well pads and the pipeline route cut clearings through primary rainforest; and there were several spills from the liquids pipeline in the first years after 2004. A top-level answer never leaves the trade-off as a list of pluses and minuses: it asks who gains, who loses, at what scale and for how long."},{"h":"Towards a sustainable future: carbon footprints, conservation and efficiency","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)","t":"A carbon footprint is the total greenhouse gas emissions caused by a person, product or organisation, usually expressed as carbon dioxide equivalent, and individual energy use is where most of it comes from: heating, electricity, the car and flights. Demand reduction is the cheapest energy of all, because energy you never use needs no power station. Homes are designed for it with thick insulation, triple glazing, draught-proofing and south-facing windows for passive solar gain, as at BedZED in Sutton in south London, about a hundred homes completed in 2002 with super-insulation and wind cowls that ventilate the rooms while recovering heat. Workplaces use sensors, LED lighting and combined heat and power, which burns fuel for electricity and uses the waste heat instead of throwing it away. Transport is designed for it with public transport, cycle networks, congestion charging and a switch to electric vehicles. Technology also makes the fossil fuels we do burn go further: a modern combined cycle gas turbine converts around 60% of the energy in its fuel into electricity, where an older coal station managed under 40%. A top-level answer is clear about the limit: conservation shrinks the problem, and it is the fastest and cheapest thing to do, but it cannot by itself supply a growing population, so it belongs alongside new supply rather than instead of it."},{"h":"The local renewable example: Chambamontera micro-hydro, Peru","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)","t":"Chambamontera is a small farming community in the mountains of the Jaen province, in the Cajamarca region of northern Peru. It sits in steep, broken terrain far from the national grid, so extending grid lines to it would cost far more than the community could ever repay. Its answer is micro-hydro: schemes of this kind generate up to about 100 kW, enough for lighting, radio, television, refrigeration and small tools, but not for heavy industry. The design is run-of-river, so there is no large dam and no reservoir to flood land. A small intake weir on a fast mountain stream diverts part of the flow into a channel, which leads to a settling tank; from there a steel penstock pipe drops steeply down the hillside, and the fall turns a turbine coupled to a generator in a small powerhouse, before the water returns to the stream. The scheme was built with help from the international NGO Practical Action, with the community providing labour and a share of the cost and local people trained to run and repair it. A top-level answer measures a scheme like this against its alternatives — grid extension, a diesel generator, kerosene lamps — and is honest about its limits: a high initial cost that needed outside help, and an output that falls when the stream drops in the dry season."}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning Figure 1 into a one-mark calculation","setup":"Figure 1 — Table: where the world's primary energy came from in 2022 (written for this room; the shares are real and are given as whole per cents and adjusted so that they total 100)\n\nSource | Share of the world's primary energy, 2022\nOil | 32%\nCoal | 27%\nNatural gas | 23%\nHydro-electricity | 7%\nOther renewables (wind, solar, biomass, geothermal) | 7%\nNuclear | 4%\n\nThe question: Using Figure 1, calculate the percentage of the world's primary energy that came from fossil fuels in 2022. [1 mark]","steps":["Sort the rows before adding anything: oil, coal and natural gas are the fuels formed from buried organic matter, so those are the rows this question wants, and the hydro, other renewables and nuclear rows are left out.","Add the three rows you have chosen: 32 + 27 + 23.","That gives 82%, and the per cent sign matters, because the question asks for a percentage and a bare number invites a lost mark.","Check it a different way: the rows you left out add to 18%, and 82 + 18 = 100%, which is what the table should do."],"cues":["Which rows in Figure 1 are fossil fuels, and which are not?","What do you now do with the rows you have chosen?","What is the total, and what has to be written after the number?","How could you check that total without adding the same rows again?"]},{"title":"Describing a trend from Figure 2 for two marks","setup":"Figure 2 — Table: coal's share of the electricity generated in the United Kingdom (written for this room; the shares are real and are rounded)\n\nYear | Coal's share of UK electricity generation\n2012 | about 40%\n2015 | about 22%\n2019 | about 2%\n2025 | none\n\nNote printed beneath the table: the United Kingdom's last coal-fired power station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar in Nottinghamshire, closed on 30 September 2024, ending more than 140 years of coal-fired electricity in the country.\n\nThe question: Using Figure 2, describe the change in coal's share of the electricity generated in the United Kingdom between 2012 and 2025. [2 marks]","steps":["Decide the direction first: every figure in the table is smaller than the one above it, so this is a fall, and a steep one.","Take the first mark with a simple statement of that change using the two ends of the table: coal's share fell from about 40% of the electricity generated to none at all.","Take the second mark by developing it with detail from the table: most of the fall had already happened by 2019, when only about 2% was left, so the decline was fastest in the earlier years.","Resist explaining why: the command word is describe, so reasons such as cheaper gas or the closure programme earn nothing on this question."],"cues":["Reading down the table, is the share rising, falling or steady?","Which two rows would you quote to state that change in one sentence?","What extra detail in the table shows when most of the change happened?","What kind of sentence would waste your time on a question with this command word?"]},{"title":"Using Figure 3 for a four-mark 'suggest' answer","setup":"Figure 3 — Text extract: the Camisea natural gas project, Peru (written for this room in our own words; the project and its effects are real)\n\nCamisea is a natural gas field in the Lower Urubamba valley of the Cusco region, in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru. Gas was found there by Shell in the 1980s; Shell later withdrew, and a consortium led by the Argentine company Pluspetrol brought the field into production in 2004. No road was built to the gas field: workers, pipe and equipment arrive by air and by river, and the wells sit on cleared pads in primary rainforest. Two pipelines run roughly 700 km from the forest and over the Andes at more than 4,000 m: one carries natural gas to Lima, on the Pacific coast, and the other carries natural gas liquids to the coast at Pisco. Peru now burns its own gas instead of imported oil: gas-fired stations are, with hydropower, one of the two main sources of the country's electricity, thousands of taxis in Lima have been converted to run on natural gas, and the country has exported gas since the liquefied natural gas plant at Pampa Melchorita opened in 2010. Royalties from the field are shared with the Cusco region through a government fund that pays for roads, schools and water supplies. The field also lies beside a reserve set aside for indigenous groups living in voluntary isolation, for whom contact with outsiders brings diseases they have no resistance to, and there were several spills from the pipeline carrying natural gas liquids in the first years after production began.\n\nThe question: Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, suggest two economic benefits of the Camisea gas project for Peru. [4 marks]","steps":["Hunt the extract for money before writing a word: it gives royalties shared with the Cusco region through a government fund, a switch from imported oil to Peru's own gas, taxis in Lima converted to natural gas, and exports through the plant at Pampa Melchorita.","Take the royalties first and develop them one step: money paid into the region funds roads, schools and water supplies that it could not otherwise afford, so incomes and services improve well away from the wells themselves.","Take a second, different benefit and develop it the same way: burning Peruvian gas instead of imported oil keeps money inside the country, and exporting gas earns foreign currency, so the balance of trade improves.","Check the focus before moving on: damage to the rainforest and the risk to isolated communities are real and important, but this question says economic, so they would earn nothing here."],"cues":["Which sentences of Figure 3 are about money rather than about the forest?","How would you turn the royalties sentence into a benefit someone in Cusco would notice?","Which second benefit is genuinely different from the first, rather than the same point again?","What kind of true material would score nothing on this particular question?"]},{"title":"Building a six-mark answer on improving energy security","setup":"The question: Explain how energy security can be improved. [6 marks] There is no figure for this one, so everything has to come from what you know. The mark scheme defines energy security as the extent to which an affordable, reliable and stable energy supply can be achieved, and it expects at least one strategy to be developed rather than a list.","steps":["Open by fixing the meaning, because the examiner rewards it: say that a secure supply is affordable, reliable and stable, so every strategy you name must be judged against those three words.","Choose two strategies that work in different ways and at different scales — increasing renewable supply nationally, and cutting the demand that has to be met at all — rather than naming four and developing none.","Develop the first through to security itself: wind and solar farms add supply that never runs out and needs no imported fuel, so a country burning less imported gas is less exposed to price spikes and to a supplier that changes its mind.","Develop the second the same way: insulation, LED lighting and combined heat and power cut demand, and a modern combined cycle gas turbine converts around 60% of its fuel into electricity where an older coal station managed under 40%, so the same gas goes further.","Finish with a sentence that weighs them, because the top level wants understanding and not a list: added renewable capacity improves affordability and sustainability most, while demand reduction is the fastest and cheapest of the two and helps with all three at once."],"cues":["What has to be in the first sentence if the examiner is to know you understand the term?","Is it better here to name four strategies or to develop two? Why?","How does a wind farm actually change a country's exposure to an unreliable supplier?","What does using less energy do to the amount of supply a country must find?","What does the top level ask for that a list of strategies cannot give?"]},{"title":"Planning a six-mark 'Assess' on fossil fuel extraction","setup":"The question: Assess the advantages and disadvantages of the extraction of a fossil fuel. Use a named example in your answer. [6 marks] You have studied the Camisea natural gas project in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru. Six marks are levels-marked from Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) to Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6), and you have about six to seven minutes.","steps":["Decode the command word before planning anything: assess means weigh the parts and reach an informed overall judgement, so two lists with a shrug at the end cannot reach the top level.","Prove the example in the first sentence with location and date, because that is where the knowledge marks start: a gas field in the Lower Urubamba valley of the Cusco region, in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru, in production since 2004.","Sort your facts into two columns before writing, and take only the strongest two or three on each side, because six marks will not hold more: national gains (a domestic fuel in place of imported oil, gas-fired electricity, export earnings through the plant at Pampa Melchorita, royalties reaching the Cusco region) against local costs (a reserve for indigenous groups in voluntary isolation beside the field, clearings for well pads and the pipeline route, spills from the liquids pipeline).","Run one evaluation thread through both columns so the judgement is built rather than announced at the end: who gains, who loses, at what scale, and for how long.","Write the verdict you are aiming at before you start, then point both paragraphs at it: the gains are national, thinly spread and temporary, while the costs are local, concentrated and permanent."],"cues":["What does this command word demand that 'describe' would not?","What two details must appear early if the examiner is to believe you know the example?","Which of your Camisea facts belong on the advantages side, which on the disadvantages side, and how many of each will fit?","What single question, asked of both columns, would turn two lists into an argument?","If the verdict is written first, what does that change about the paragraphs?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What does energy security mean?","a":"The extent to which an affordable, reliable and stable energy supply can be achieved. Energy insecurity is what a country has when any one of those three fails."},{"q":"What three reasons does the specification give for energy consumption rising?","a":"Economic development, rising population and technology."},{"q":"What four kinds of factor affect energy supply?","a":"Physical factors, the cost of exploitation and production, technology, and political factors."},{"q":"Why does a fall in the world's energy supply push up the price of food?","a":"Nitrogen fertiliser is manufactured from natural gas, and irrigation pumps, tractors, food processing and refrigerated transport all use fuel, so higher energy prices feed straight through into food prices."},{"q":"Why is run-of-river micro-hydro well suited to a remote mountain village in an LIC or NEE?","a":"A steep, fast stream gives continuous power without a dam or reservoir, so no land is flooded and nobody is moved; there is no fuel to buy or carry in; and it costs far less than extending the national grid across mountains. Local people can be trained to maintain it."}]},"room":{"facts":["Energy security is the extent to which an affordable, reliable and stable energy supply can be achieved; energy insecurity is what a country has when any one of those three fails.","A country with an energy surplus produces more than it consumes and can export; a country in energy deficit consumes more than it produces and must import.","Consumption is rising for three reasons: economic development, rising population and technology.","Four kinds of factor affect supply: physical factors, the cost of exploitation and production, technology, and political factors.","Fossil fuels still supply about 80% of the world's primary energy, with oil the largest single source; roughly 700 to 800 million people have no electricity supply at all.","The renewables named by the specification are biomass, wind, hydro, tidal, geothermal, wave and solar; the non-renewables are fossil fuels and nuclear power.","The fossil fuel example: Camisea, a natural gas field in the Lower Urubamba valley of the Cusco region in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru, in production since 2004, with two pipelines running roughly 700 km over the Andes to the coast.","The local renewable example: the Chambamontera micro-hydro scheme in the mountains of the Jaen province, Cajamarca region, northern Peru — a run-of-river scheme built with help from the NGO Practical Action, with no dam and no reservoir.","Micro-hydro schemes generate up to about 100 kW: enough for lighting, radio, refrigeration and small tools, but not for heavy industry.","A modern combined cycle gas turbine converts around 60% of the energy in its fuel into electricity, where an older coal-fired station managed under 40%; burning gas releases roughly half the carbon dioxide of coal for the same electricity.","A carbon footprint is the total greenhouse gas emissions caused by a person, product or organisation, usually expressed as carbon dioxide equivalent."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following is a non-renewable source of energy? Shade one circle only.\nA  Geothermal\nB  Natural gas\nC  Tidal\nD  Wave\n[1 mark]","a":"One mark for B. No credit if two or more answers are shaded. AO1 = 1 mark.","sol":"B, natural gas. Natural gas formed from organic matter buried and heated over millions of years, so once a field is emptied it is gone on any human timescale. Geothermal heat, tides and waves all keep being renewed, so they are renewable.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The word doing the work in this question is 'non-renewable'. Make sure you can say what it means before you read the four options.","Ask of each option whether using it today leaves less of it for tomorrow.","Work out which option had to be formed underground over millions of years."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, calculate the percentage of the world's primary energy that came from fossil fuels in 2022. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for 82% (oil 32% + coal 27% + natural gas 23%). AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"The fossil fuels in the table are oil, coal and natural gas. Adding them: 32 + 27 + 23 = 82%. As a check, the rows left out (hydro, other renewables and nuclear) add to 18%, and 82 + 18 = 100%.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Only some of the six rows are fuels that were formed underground from buried organic matter.","Pick out those rows, then do one addition with them.","Write down which three rows you are using before you add anything."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, describe the change in coal's share of the electricity generated in the United Kingdom between 2012 and 2025. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic description of the change shown, eg coal's share fell steeply. Second mark for developing it with detail from the table, eg from about 40% in 2012 to none at all by 2025, with most of the fall over by 2019. No credit for explaining why the share fell. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Coal's share of UK electricity generation fell steeply and continuously across the period, from about 40% in 2012 to none at all by 2025. Most of that fall had already happened by 2019, when only about 2% was left, so the steepest decline came in the first years of the period rather than at the end.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Read down the column and decide on the overall direction before you write anything.","One mark is for stating the change, the other for developing it with figures taken from the table.","Quote the two ends of the table first, then look for where the change was fastest."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why global energy consumption is rising. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each valid reason, eg economic development and industrialisation; rising incomes bringing cars, appliances and air conditioning; population growth; urbanisation; new technologies such as data centres and electric vehicles. Credit two reasons only. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"Economic development: as countries such as China and India industrialise, factories, offices, construction and freight all draw power, and as incomes rise people buy fridges, televisions, air conditioning and cars, so energy use per person climbs. Rising population: world population passed 8 billion in November 2022, and almost all further growth is in countries where energy use per person is also rising.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification gives three reasons for this. You only need two of them.","Each reason has to be a cause of rising consumption rather than a sign of it, so test anything you think of by asking whether it makes the world use more energy than it used before.","The first move is to ask what happens to a country's energy use as it industrialises."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"State two causes of energy insecurity. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each cause stated, physical or human, eg the unequal distribution of fossil fuel reserves; depletion of reserves; volatile oil and gas prices; political instability or conflict involving producer countries; the cost of building new plant; rising consumption. Credit two only. AO1 = 2 marks.","sol":"First, fossil fuel reserves are unevenly distributed — around half of proven oil reserves lie around the Persian Gulf — so most countries must import from a few producers. Second, prices are volatile and supply can be cut for political reasons, as European gas supplies were after Russia invaded Ukraine in February 2022.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Causes can be physical, such as where the fuel happens to be, or human, such as who controls it.","You are asked to state, so two short sentences are enough; no explanation is needed.","Think first about the gap between where energy is used and where it is found."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, suggest two economic benefits of the Camisea gas project for Peru. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Credit interpretation of the extract — royalties shared with the Cusco region, a domestic fuel replacing imported oil, cheaper fuel for converted taxis, exports through the LNG plant — each developed once. No credit for environmental or social effects unless they are given an economic consequence.","sol":"Figure 3 says that royalties from the field are shared with the Cusco region through a government fund that pays for roads, schools and water supplies. That is income the region would not otherwise have, and better roads and schools raise incomes and opportunities well away from the wells themselves, so the benefit is spread across the region rather than staying with the company. Secondly, the extract says Peru now burns its own gas instead of imported oil, and has exported gas since the plant at Pampa Melchorita opened in 2010. Money that used to leave the country to pay for imported fuel now stays inside it, and the exports earn foreign currency, so the balance of trade improves and the government has more to spend. Cheaper fuel also lowers costs for the thousands of Lima taxi drivers who converted their vehicles to natural gas, which raises their take-home earnings.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The question says economic, so go through the extract looking only for sentences about money.","Two developed points score better here than four bare ones: take each benefit one step further than the extract does.","Ask who, apart from the gas company, ends up with more money because of the project."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, suggest how the scheme shown could improve the quality of life of people in the village. [4 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks. Credit interpretation of the photograph — the cable to the houses, the school and shop, the absence of a reservoir, the farmland the penstock crosses — developed into effects on living conditions, eg electric light replacing kerosene, refrigeration, evening study, small businesses. Generic answers that ignore the photograph are capped at Level 1.","sol":"Figure 4 shows a single cable running from the powerhouse to a scattered group of houses with tin roofs, so the village has electric light for the first time. That matters more than it sounds. Children can read and do schoolwork after dark, where before the only light came from kerosene lamps or candles, which are dim, costly and a fire risk, and whose smoke irritates eyes and lungs. The photograph also shows a school and a small shop at the road end, and electricity lets the shop run a fridge and a freezer, so food and medicines keep, and a clinic could store vaccines safely. Because the scheme is run-of-river, with no dam and no reservoir anywhere in the photograph, nobody has had to move off the terraced fields of maize and coffee the penstock crosses, so this improvement in living conditions has cost the village none of its farmland.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Everything you need is in the photograph: follow the cable and see where it ends up.","Turn each feature you can see into something a person in the village can now do that they could not do before.","Ask what the household used for light before that cable arrived, and what was wrong with it."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, suggest why the world's supply of energy is insecure. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks. Credit analysis of the table — the dominance of the three fossil fuel rows, the small renewable and nuclear shares — applied to insecurity: finite reserves, uneven distribution, dependence on imports and on a few producers, price volatility, vulnerable transport routes. Answers that ignore Figure 1 are capped at Level 1.","sol":"Figure 1 shows that oil, coal and natural gas together supplied 82% of the world's primary energy in 2022, with oil alone at 32%. Insecurity begins there, because all three are finite: every barrel and every cubic metre burnt is one that cannot be burnt again, so reserves are being drawn down while demand rises. The second problem is distribution. These fuels only occur where the geology put them, and reserves are heavily concentrated — around half of proven oil reserves lie around the Persian Gulf — so most countries in the table's 82% are importing from a small number of producers, and a supply that depends on someone else is neither reliable nor stable. That makes prices volatile and leaves consumers exposed to decisions taken elsewhere, as Europe found when Russia invaded Ukraine in February 2022 and gas prices spiked. Transport adds a third weakness, since roughly a fifth of the world's oil is shipped through the Strait of Hormuz. By contrast the rows that do not depend on imports at all, hydro and other renewables, supplied only 7% each, so the shift to domestic, renewable supply is real but is still far too small to make the world's supply secure.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Add the top three rows of Figure 1 before you write, and ask what those three fuels have in common.","Insecurity has three parts in the mark scheme's definition: affordable, reliable, stable. Test the table against each.","Begin with what 'finite' means for a resource that most of the world depends on."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)"]},{"q":"Explain how physical factors and the cost of production affect the supply of energy. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Credit a chain of reasoning: geology and climate decide where a resource exists (fossil fuel basins, hot rock, steep relief and rainfall, sunshine, wind), while the cost of extracting it decides whether it is worth taking (deepwater, Arctic, tar sands, falling solar costs). Max Level 1 for a list of factors with no development.","sol":"Physical factors decide what exists in the first place. Coal, oil and gas are found only where ancient swamps and seas were buried and heated, which is why the North Sea and the Persian Gulf have them and most countries do not. Geothermal power needs hot rock near the surface, so Iceland can use it and the United Kingdom largely cannot; hydro-electricity needs steep relief and reliable rainfall, which is why it suits Peru, Norway and Nepal; solar output is highest in low latitudes with clear skies; wind needs exposed coasts and shallow seas. Cost then decides how much of what exists is actually supplied. A resource is only produced if it can be sold for more than it costs to extract, so deepwater oil, Arctic drilling and tar sands are developed when prices are high and shut in when prices fall, while easily reached fields keep producing throughout. Costs also change over time, and that changes supply: the cost of electricity from large solar farms has fallen by roughly 90% since 2010, so sunshine that was always there has only recently become worth using, and solar capacity has grown accordingly.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two different questions are being asked here: what is there, and whether it is worth getting out.","Take one energy source at a time and say what physical conditions it needs, then what it costs to use.","Start with why fossil fuels are found in some countries and not others."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)"]},{"q":"Explain how energy insecurity can affect food production and industrial output. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Credit developed chains: gas as the feedstock for nitrogen fertiliser, fuel for irrigation pumps, machinery, processing and refrigerated transport; power cuts and higher costs closing factories and cutting output and jobs. Named support (the 2021-22 gas price rise, South Africa's power cuts) is credited. Max Level 1 for a list of effects with no chain.","sol":"Farming is far more dependent on energy than it looks. Nitrogen fertiliser is manufactured from natural gas, so when gas prices rose steeply in 2021 and 2022 European fertiliser plants cut production or shut down altogether, fertiliser became expensive and scarce, and farmers who used less of it harvested less from the same land. Energy is also needed to run irrigation pumps, tractors and combines, to process and package food, and to move it in refrigerated lorries, so every stage of the chain gets dearer and food prices rise, which hits the poorest consumers hardest. Industry is affected in two ways. Where supply is simply unreliable, production stops: South Africa's scheduled power cuts, at their worst in 2023, have repeatedly shut factories, smelters and mines for hours at a time, so output falls, orders are lost and firms must buy diesel generators to keep going. Where supply is available but expensive, energy-intensive industries such as steel, cement, glass and chemicals become uncompetitive, so plants cut shifts or close and jobs are lost, which is why energy insecurity quickly becomes an economic problem rather than only a technical one.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Farming uses energy long before the tractor starts. Ask what fertiliser is actually made from.","Two separate chains are wanted: one ending in less food, one ending in less output. Keep them apart.","Begin with the link between natural gas and nitrogen fertiliser."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)"]},{"q":"Explain how energy security can be improved. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Energy security is the extent to which an affordable, reliable and stable supply can be achieved. Credit developed strategies at different scales: renewable capacity, diversifying the mix and suppliers, domestic fossil fuels and nuclear, storage and interconnectors, efficiency and demand reduction. Expect at least one strategy developed rather than a list.","sol":"Energy security means an affordable, reliable and stable supply, so improvements have to be judged against all three. The first strategy is to increase renewable supply, because wind, solar, hydro and geothermal use resources a country already has and cannot be cut off by another government. A wind or solar farm needs no imported fuel once built, so every unit it generates is a unit of gas that does not have to be bought at whatever price the market sets, and Iceland shows the end point of this, heating about nine homes in ten with its own geothermal water. The weakness is that wind and solar are intermittent, so they have to be backed by storage, interconnectors or gas plant if the supply is to stay reliable. The second strategy is to cut the demand that has to be met at all, which is quicker and cheaper than building anything: insulation, LED lighting and combined heat and power reduce waste, and a modern combined cycle gas turbine converts around 60% of its fuel into electricity where an older coal station managed under 40%, so the same fuel goes much further. Diversifying suppliers and fuels matters too, since a country buying from several sources is far less exposed than one buying from a single pipeline.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The examiner wants to see that you know what the word security means here before you name any strategy.","Two strategies developed properly beat five named in passing; choose ones that work in different ways.","Ask what a wind farm changes about a country's need to buy fuel from abroad."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)","3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"Explain how energy conservation in homes and in transport can reduce the demand for energy. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks. Credit developed explanation of design and behaviour: insulation, glazing, draught-proofing, passive solar gain, smart meters and LED lighting; public transport, cycling, congestion charging, electric and hybrid vehicles. Named support such as BedZED is credited. Max Level 1 for a list of measures with no link to reduced demand.","sol":"Most of the energy a home uses goes on heating, and most of that heat escapes through the walls, roof, windows and gaps around doors. Insulating a loft and cavity walls, fitting double or triple glazing and draught-proofing slow that loss down, so the boiler runs for fewer hours to hold the same temperature and the house draws less gas or electricity for the rest of its life. Design can go further: at BedZED in Sutton in south London, about a hundred homes completed in 2002 were built with super-insulation, south-facing windows that trap solar gain and wind cowls that ventilate the rooms while recovering the heat from the air leaving them, so the demand for heating was cut rather than met. Inside the house, LED bulbs and smart meters cut the rest by using less power for the same light and by showing people what their appliances actually cost. Transport works the same way. A bus or a tram carries dozens of people on the fuel several cars would burn, so investment in public transport, cycle lanes and congestion charging replaces many separate journeys with few, and an electric vehicle converts far more of its energy into movement than a petrol engine, which wastes most of it as heat.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Ask first where the energy a house uses actually goes, then what stops it going there.","Every measure you name has to be chained to less energy being used, not just listed.","Begin with heat escaping from a house, and what insulation does to that."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)"]},{"q":"Assess the advantages and disadvantages of the extraction of a fossil fuel. Use a named example in your answer. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks, marked on the Levels 1–3 grid the energy topic uses at this tariff (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). 'Assess' requires an informed overall judgement, so credit a named extraction example with location and date, economic advantages set against environmental and social disadvantages, and a judgement that weighs scale, beneficiaries and timescale. No credit for a general account of fossil fuels with no example.","sol":"Extracting a fossil fuel always trades benefits against damage, and which side wins depends on who is counting and over how long. The Camisea gas field lies in the Lower Urubamba valley of the Cusco region, in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru, and has produced gas since 2004. The advantages are national and economic: Peru had been importing oil and now burns its own gas, so gas-fired stations are, with hydropower, one of the two main sources of the country's electricity, thousands of Lima taxis run on domestic gas, and exports have earned foreign currency since the liquefied natural gas plant at Pampa Melchorita opened in 2010. Royalties reach the Cusco region and pay for roads, schools and water supplies, and burning gas releases roughly half the carbon dioxide of coal for the same electricity. The disadvantages are local and environmental: well pads and the pipeline route cut clearings through primary rainforest, there were several spills from the natural gas liquids pipeline in the first years after 2004, and the field lies beside a reserve for indigenous groups living in voluntary isolation, for whom contact brings disease. Overall the gains are real but thinly spread across a whole country and last only as long as the gas does, while the losses fall on a few thousand people and on the forest, and some of them are permanent.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'Assess' is not two lists side by side. Decide what your overall verdict is before you write, then aim every sentence at it.","Sort your example's facts by who gains and who loses, then by how long each effect lasts.","The first move is to fix the example in place: where exactly is it, and when did production start?"],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)"]},{"q":"'A local renewable energy scheme is the best way of providing a sustainable supply of energy in an LIC or NEE.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use a named example in your answer. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks, marked on the Levels 1–3 grid the energy topic uses at this tariff (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). 'To what extent' requires an appraisal of how far the claim holds, not a yes or no. Credit a named local scheme with its workings and its benefits, set against its limitations and against the alternatives at national scale, and a conclusion that qualifies the claim by scale and by site.","sol":"The statement holds at the scale of a village and fails at the scale of a country. Chambamontera, a farming community in the mountains of the Jaen province, in the Cajamarca region of northern Peru, sits in steep terrain far from the national grid, where extending grid lines would cost far more than the community could ever repay. Its micro-hydro scheme takes part of the flow of a fast stream behind a small intake weir, leads it along a channel to a settling tank and then down a penstock to a turbine and generator, before returning the water to the river. Because it is run-of-river there is no dam and no reservoir, so no farmland is flooded and nobody is displaced. The fuel is free, local people are trained to maintain it, and electric light replaces kerosene, so children can study after dark and a shop can run a fridge: sustainable environmentally, economically and socially at once. The limits are real, though. Micro-hydro generates up to about 100 kW, enough for lights and small tools but not for industry, the initial cost needed help from the NGO Practical Action, output falls in the dry season, and only steep, wet sites suit it. So I agree to a considerable extent for remote rural communities, but the cities and industry of an NEE still need large-scale supply.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'To what extent' is not a yes or no. Find the condition under which the statement is true and the one under which it fails.","Scale is the key that unlocks this question: judge the claim for a village, then judge it again for a whole country.","The first move is to work out why the national grid never reached a community like this one."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)","3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, discuss the view that rising demand for energy will lead to conflict between countries. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks. 'Discuss' requires a balanced treatment with developed ideas. Credit analysis of the table — the 82% of primary energy still coming from oil, coal and gas, the small renewable shares — applied to the case for conflict (uneven reserves, import dependence, supply used as leverage, vulnerable shipping routes) and to the case against (trade and interdependence, domestic renewables, efficiency), with a substantiated judgement. Answers that ignore Figure 1 are capped at Level 1.","sol":"Figure 1 shows that oil, coal and natural gas together supplied 82% of the world's primary energy in 2022, and that is where the risk begins, because those three fuels exist only where the geology put them. Around half of proven oil reserves lie around the Persian Gulf, so the countries that consume the most are rarely the countries that hold the most, and that mismatch turns supply into a political question. Energy can then be used as leverage, as Europe discovered when Russia invaded Ukraine in February 2022 and gas prices spiked, and transport concentrates the risk further, since roughly a fifth of the world's oil passes through the Strait of Hormuz. Against that, exporters need customers as much as importers need fuel, so most disputes end in renegotiated contracts rather than in fighting. Figure 1 also shows hydro and other renewables at 7% each, and although those shares are small they are growing: electricity from large solar farms has fallen in cost by roughly 90% since 2010, and a country that generates its own power cannot be blockaded. Overall, rising demand makes tension very likely but conflict far from inevitable, and the risk is greatest where supply is held by few producers and has to pass through a chokepoint.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'Discuss' means two developed sides, not one side and a sentence of doubt at the end.","Use Figure 1 to size the problem first: how much of the world's energy still has to be dug or pumped out of somewhere in particular?","The first move is to ask why the countries that use the most energy are rarely the ones that produce it."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)"]},{"q":"Evaluate the contribution that energy conservation can make to a sustainable energy future. [6 marks]","a":"AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 4 marks, marked on the Levels 1–3 grid the energy topic uses at this tariff (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6). 'Evaluate' requires judgement supported by evidence on both sides. Credit sustainable home, workplace and transport design, demand reduction, efficiency in the use of fossil fuels and individual carbon footprints, each judged rather than described, set against the limits of conservation, with an overall verdict.","sol":"Energy that is never used needs no power station, no fuel and no import, so conservation improves affordability, reliability and stability at once, which is why it is the cheapest contribution available. Most of a home's energy goes on heating and most of that heat escapes through walls, roof and windows, so insulation, triple glazing and draught-proofing cut the loss for the life of the building; BedZED in Sutton in south London, about a hundred homes completed in 2002, added super-insulation, south-facing windows for passive solar gain and wind cowls that recover heat from the air leaving the rooms. Workplaces use sensors, LED lighting and combined heat and power; transport design replaces car journeys with public transport and cycle networks; and technology stretches the fuel we still burn, since a modern combined cycle gas turbine converts around 60% of its fuel into electricity where an older coal station managed under 40%. The limits are just as clear. Conservation reduces demand but supplies nothing, retrofitting existing homes is slow and expensive, and demand in LICs and NEEs is rightly rising, since roughly 700 to 800 million people still have no electricity at all. My judgement is that conservation is necessary but not sufficient: the partner of renewable supply rather than a substitute for it.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'Evaluate' needs a verdict supported from both sides, so plan the case against conservation as carefully as the case for it.","Judge each measure as you name it: how much does it actually save, and what does it cost to put in?","The first move is to work out where the energy used in a house actually goes."],"codes":["3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)","3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does energy security mean?","a":"The extent to which an affordable, reliable and stable energy supply can be achieved."},{"q":"In which year did the Camisea gas field in Peru begin production?","a":"2004."},{"q":"In which country is the Chambamontera micro-hydro scheme?","a":"Peru (in the mountains of the Jaen province, Cajamarca region, in the north of the country)."},{"q":"What is a carbon footprint?","a":"The total greenhouse gas emissions caused by a person, product or organisation, usually expressed as carbon dioxide equivalent."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Energy security","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"The extent to which an affordable, reliable and stable energy supply can be achieved. Energy insecurity is what a country has when any one of those three fails."},{"front":"Energy surplus and energy deficit","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Surplus: a country produces more energy than it consumes, so it can export — Qatar, Norway, Saudi Arabia. Deficit: it consumes more than it produces and must import — Japan, and the UK since 2004."},{"front":"Renewable and non-renewable sources of energy","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"Renewable: biomass, wind, hydro, tidal, geothermal, wave and solar, all replenished naturally. Non-renewable: fossil fuels (coal, oil, natural gas) and nuclear fuel, which are finite."},{"front":"Why world energy consumption is rising","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Economic development, so industry and richer households use more; rising population, which passed 8 billion in November 2022; and technology, which creates new demands such as vehicles, cooling and data centres."},{"front":"The factors that affect energy supply","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Physical factors (geology, relief, climate); the cost of exploitation and production; technology, such as fracking and larger turbines; and political factors, such as sanctions, war and nuclear policy."},{"front":"The global pattern of energy consumption","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (surplus and deficit)","open":false,"back":"Highest per person in North America, the Gulf states, Europe, Australia and increasingly China; lowest across sub-Saharan Africa, where most of the 700 to 800 million people with no electricity live."},{"front":"Impacts of energy insecurity","code":"3.2.3.4 — Demand for energy resources is rising globally but supply can be insecure, which may lead to conflict (impacts of insecurity)","open":false,"back":"Exploration of difficult and sensitive areas; economic and environmental costs; less food, since fertiliser is made from gas; lower industrial output where power cuts happen; potential conflict where demand exceeds supply."},{"front":"Fracking (hydraulic fracturing)","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"Pumping water, sand and chemicals at high pressure into shale to crack it and release gas or oil. It unlocked United States shale gas; England halted it in November 2019 after tremors in Lancashire."},{"front":"The Camisea gas project — what and where","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"A natural gas field in the Lower Urubamba valley of the Cusco region, in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru. In production since 2004; two pipelines run roughly 700 km over the Andes to the coast."},{"front":"Camisea — the advantages","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"A domestic fuel in place of imported oil; gas-fired electricity; Lima taxis converted to gas; export earnings since the LNG plant at Pampa Melchorita opened in 2010; royalties shared with the Cusco region."},{"front":"Camisea — the disadvantages","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (increasing supply)","open":false,"back":"Well pads and pipeline clearings in primary rainforest; a reserve for indigenous groups in voluntary isolation lies beside the field, and contact brings disease; several liquids-pipeline spills after 2004."},{"front":"Micro-hydro","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)","open":false,"back":"Small-scale hydro-electricity, up to about 100 kW: intake weir, channel, settling tank, penstock, turbine and generator. Run-of-river, so no dam, no reservoir and no land flooded."},{"front":"The Chambamontera micro-hydro scheme","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)","open":false,"back":"A run-of-river scheme for a farming community in the Jaen province, Cajamarca, northern Peru, built with the NGO Practical Action. Electric light replaces kerosene, and no grid connection is needed."},{"front":"Carbon footprint","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)","open":false,"back":"The total greenhouse gas emissions caused by a person, product or organisation, usually given as carbon dioxide equivalent. Cut it by insulating, wasting less power, using public transport and flying less."},{"front":"Local renewable energy schemes in LICs and NEEs — examples to name","code":"3.2.3.4 — Different strategies can be used to increase energy supply (sustainable resource future)","open":true,"back":"Chambamontera micro-hydro in northern Peru; household biogas digesters fed with cattle dung in Nepal; solar home systems in Bangladesh."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Table: where the world's primary energy came from in 2022 (written for this room; the shares are real and are given as whole per cents and adjusted so that they total 100)\n\nSource | Share of the world's primary energy, 2022\nOil | 32%\nCoal | 27%\nNatural gas | 23%\nHydro-electricity | 7%\nOther renewables (wind, solar, biomass, geothermal) | 7%\nNuclear | 4%","Figure 2 — Table: coal's share of the electricity generated in the United Kingdom (written for this room; the shares are real and are rounded)\n\nYear | Coal's share of UK electricity generation\n2012 | about 40%\n2015 | about 22%\n2019 | about 2%\n2025 | none\n\nNote printed beneath the table: the United Kingdom's last coal-fired power station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar in Nottinghamshire, closed on 30 September 2024, ending more than 140 years of coal-fired electricity in the country.","Figure 3 — Text extract: the Camisea natural gas project, Peru (written for this room in our own words; the project and its effects are real)\n\nCamisea is a natural gas field in the Lower Urubamba valley of the Cusco region, in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru. Gas was found there by Shell in the 1980s; Shell later withdrew, and a consortium led by the Argentine company Pluspetrol brought the field into production in 2004. No road was built to the gas field: workers, pipe and equipment arrive by air and by river, and the wells sit on cleared pads in primary rainforest. Two pipelines run roughly 700 km from the forest and over the Andes at more than 4,000 m: one carries natural gas to Lima, on the Pacific coast, and the other carries natural gas liquids to the coast at Pisco. Peru now burns its own gas instead of imported oil: gas-fired stations are, with hydropower, one of the two main sources of the country's electricity, thousands of taxis in Lima have been converted to run on natural gas, and the country has exported gas since the liquefied natural gas plant at Pampa Melchorita opened in 2010. Royalties from the field are shared with the Cusco region through a government fund that pays for roads, schools and water supplies. The field also lies beside a reserve set aside for indigenous groups living in voluntary isolation, for whom contact with outsiders brings diseases they have no resistance to, and there were several spills from the pipeline carrying natural gas liquids in the first years after production began.","Figure 4 — Photograph, described for this room: a village micro-hydro scheme in the Peruvian Andes, of the kind installed at Chambamontera (the scheme at Chambamontera is real, and every feature named is a real part of a micro-hydro scheme; the scene is described for this room rather than printed, and the sizes and distances in it are illustrative)\n\nThe photograph looks down a steep, green valley side. In the top left a fast, shallow stream runs over rock, and a low concrete weir about waist high turns part of the flow sideways into a narrow lined channel. The channel contours along the slope for a few hundred metres to a small square concrete tank, where leaves and grit settle out. From the tank a black steel pipe, the penstock, runs straight down the hillside between terraced fields of maize and coffee bushes. At the bottom of the slope the pipe enters a small brick powerhouse with a corrugated roof, no taller than a garage, and a thin stream of water runs back out of it into the river below. A single cable on wooden poles leads away from the powerhouse towards a scattered group of houses with tin roofs, where a school and a small shop stand at the road end. There is no dam, no reservoir and no flooded land anywhere in the photograph, and no pylons or grid lines reach the valley from outside."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) on the six-mark 'Assess' question below","items":["Question: Assess the advantages and disadvantages of the extraction of a fossil fuel. Use a named example in your answer. [6 marks]","Point: the advantages of extracting gas at Camisea are national and economic, and they are genuinely large — but they are thinly spread and temporary, which is what decides the judgement.","Named evidence: the Camisea natural gas project — the Lower Urubamba valley, Cusco region, in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru; gas found by Shell in the 1980s and brought into production in 2004; two pipelines running roughly 700 km over the Andes at more than 4,000 m, one carrying natural gas to Lima and one carrying natural gas liquids to the coast at Pisco; the liquefied natural gas plant at Pampa Melchorita, exporting since 2010; royalties reaching the Cusco region.","The developed paragraph: \"Camisea's advantages are national and economic, and they are substantial. The field lies in the Lower Urubamba valley of the Cusco region, in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru, and has produced gas since 2004. Peru had been buying oil abroad at whatever price the market set; now gas-fired stations are, with hydropower, one of the two main sources of the country's electricity, thousands of Lima taxis run on domestic gas, and exports have earned foreign currency since the liquefied natural gas plant at Pampa Melchorita opened in 2010, while royalties reach the Cusco region and pay for roads, schools and water supplies. Set against that, these gains are spread thinly across a country of more than 30 million people and last only as long as the field does, whereas the forest cleared for the well pads and for the pipeline route will not grow back in a lifetime.\"","Link to the question: the paragraph does not simply list advantages. It sizes them — who receives them, how widely, and for how long — which is what 'assess' asks for, and it sets up the comparison the conclusion will complete.","Mini-judgement: at national scale the advantages are decisive; at local scale they are not, because the costs are concentrated on the few thousand people who live in the forest and on the forest itself.","Why an examiner would place this in Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): the only six-mark grid the energy topic has is the one AQA prints for Paper 2 question 06.4, Question 6 Energy, marked AO1 = 2 marks and AO2 = 4 marks over Levels 1–3. Its Level 3 asks for 'thorough understanding of the interrelationships between environments and processes' in the energy context, demonstrated 'in detail', and its Level 2 supplies the knowledge strand, 'specific and accurate knowledge'. This paragraph meets both: the valley, the region, the date and the export plant are specific and accurate, and it links extraction in the Amazon to electricity and taxis in Lima and to money spent in Cusco instead of listing facts. Those descriptors are written for a question about improving energy security, so for the 'assess the advantages and disadvantages' focus here AQA's nearest wording is Paper 2 question 01.8 — 'a detailed and balanced appreciation of its advantages and disadvantages' — which is exactly what the last sentence supplies."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["Question: the same. Assess the advantages and disadvantages of the extraction of a fossil fuel. Use a named example in your answer. [6 marks]","The weaker paragraph: \"Camisea is a gas project in Peru which is good for the economy because it makes a lot of money and creates jobs for local people. Gas is also better than coal because it is cleaner. But it is bad as well because it is in the rainforest, so lots of trees get cut down and animals lose their habitats. Also there are tribes who live there and they get ill from the workers. So there are advantages and disadvantages of extracting fossil fuels and it just depends on your opinion.\"","Which descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) of the six-mark grid AQA prints for Paper 2 question 06.4. AO1 is 'limited knowledge': the country is right and nothing else is specific — no region, no valley, no date, no pipeline, no figure. AO2 shows no more than 'simple understanding of the interrelationships between environments and processes', because 'makes a lot of money' is never connected to anything a Peruvian would notice.","Which descriptor it misses, and exactly how: Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) wants thorough understanding demonstrated in detail, and on an 'assess' focus AQA's nearest wording, from Paper 2 question 01.8, is 'a detailed and balanced appreciation of its advantages and disadvantages'. The last sentence — 'it just depends on your opinion' — is the opposite of that, because it refuses to weigh the two sides at all. Nothing in the paragraph compares the size, the reach or the duration of a benefit against a cost.","Two further faults an examiner would mark: 'creates jobs for local people' is the vaguest possible economic claim and is not developed into who, how many or doing what; and 'gas is cleaner than coal' is true but is left as an assertion, where 'roughly half the carbon dioxide of coal for the same electricity' would have earned credit.","What one change would move it up a level: attach real detail to each side and then compare them. Replacing 'makes a lot of money' with 'royalties shared with the Cusco region through a government fund pay for roads, schools and water supplies' supplies the AO1 detail, and adding 'but those gains are spread across a country of more than 30 million people while the damage falls on a few thousand' turns two lists into the assessment the command word demands.","On vocabulary: 'good', 'bad', 'lots of' and 'tribes' are not specialist terms, and a Section C energy answer carries no separate marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar, so precise language pays here only by making the geography precise. Swap in the terms the grid rewards — non-renewable, royalties, primary rainforest, indigenous, voluntary isolation, carbon dioxide, energy security — and the same sentences begin to show the understanding the marks are actually for."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the Camisea natural gas project, Peru (the fossil fuel extraction example)","items":["Where: the Lower Urubamba valley of the Cusco region, in the Amazon rainforest of south-east Peru; the main gas field is in the block known as Block 88.","Discovery and ownership: gas was found by Shell in the 1980s; Shell withdrew in 1998, and a consortium led by the Argentine company Pluspetrol, with partners including Hunt Oil of the United States, developed the field.","Production: gas has flowed since 2004.","Access: no road was built to the gas field. Workers, pipe and equipment arrive by air and by river, and the wells sit on cleared pads surrounded by forest — a design intended to stop the settlement and clearance that roads normally bring.","The pipelines: two lines run roughly 700 km from the rainforest, over the Andes at more than 4,000 m, to the Pacific coast, one carrying natural gas, which continues to Lima, and one carrying natural gas liquids to the coast at Pisco.","Exports: the liquefied natural gas plant at Pampa Melchorita, on the coast south of Lima, opened in 2010 and made Peru a gas exporter.","What it did for Peru's energy mix: Peru had been importing oil; gas-fired power stations are now, with hydropower, one of the two main sources of the country's electricity, and large numbers of taxis and buses in Lima have been converted to run on natural gas.","The money: the field pays royalties to the Peruvian state, and part of the take is returned to the regions. Cusco, the region the field itself lies in, receives its share through the gas canon (canon gasífero), the portion of the royalty and tax take returned to the producing region, which pays for roads, schools and water supplies. A separate national fund, FOCAM (the Fondo de Desarrollo Socioeconómico de Camisea), pays the regions the pipelines cross — Ayacucho, Huancavelica, Ica and Lima — and not Cusco.","The people: the field lies beside the Kugapakori–Nahua–Nanti Reserve, set aside for indigenous groups living in voluntary isolation or in initial contact, for whom contact with outsiders brings diseases they have little resistance to.","Environmental costs: clearings for well pads and for the pipeline route through primary rainforest; erosion and landslides on the steep pipeline route; increased river traffic disturbing fishing; and several spills from the natural gas liquids pipeline in the first years after production began in 2004.","Carbon: burning gas releases roughly half the carbon dioxide of coal for the same electricity, so the project cut emissions compared with coal or oil — but it is still a fossil fuel, and the field exists to produce more of it.","The exam point this example proves: the advantages of extraction are mostly national, economic and temporary, while the disadvantages are mostly local, environmental, social and long-lasting — which is why 'assess' questions on extraction turn on scale and timescale."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the Chambamontera micro-hydro scheme, Peru (the local renewable scheme in an LIC or NEE)","items":["Where: Chambamontera, a small farming community in the mountains of the Jaen province, in the Cajamarca region of northern Peru.","The problem it solves: the community is remote, scattered and in steep terrain, so extending the national grid to it would cost far more than the households could repay; before the scheme, light came from kerosene lamps, candles and batteries, and cooking from firewood.","What it is: a micro-hydro scheme. Micro-hydro generates up to about 100 kW — enough for lighting, radio, television, refrigeration and small tools, but not for heavy industry.","Design: run-of-river, so there is no dam and no reservoir, no land is flooded and nobody is resettled — the opposite of a scheme like the Three Gorges Dam in China, for which around 1.3 million people had to move.","How it works, in order: a low intake weir diverts part of a fast mountain stream into a lined channel; the channel contours along the slope to a settling tank where grit and leaves drop out; a steel penstock pipe then falls steeply down the hillside; the water's fall spins a turbine coupled to a generator in a small powerhouse; the water returns to the river, and a cable carries the electricity to the houses.","Who built it: the community, with help from the international NGO Practical Action, contributing labour and a share of the cost; local people were trained to operate and maintain the plant, and a village committee runs it and collects small payments for the electricity.","Social benefits: clean electric light instead of kerosene, so children can study after dark and there is less indoor smoke and less fire risk; radio and television; refrigeration for food, medicines and vaccines.","Economic benefits: no fuel to buy or carry in, so running costs are low; power for grain mills, workshops, coffee processing and small businesses, which raises incomes; the payments and the maintenance work keep money and skills inside the village.","Environmental benefits: the energy is renewable and produces no greenhouse gases in operation; no land is flooded; less kerosene is burnt and less firewood is cut, which slows local deforestation.","Limitations: the initial cost was high enough to need outside funding; output falls when stream flow drops in the dry season; the design only suits places with steep slopes and reliable rainfall; the intake has to be cleared of debris and the plant maintained; and the output is far too small for industry.","Why it counts as sustainable: it meets the community's needs now, from a resource that renews itself, without damaging the environment or the ability of future generations to meet their own needs — and because it is locally owned and locally maintained, it keeps working after the NGO has gone.","The exam point this example proves: small, local, renewable schemes can transform quality of life in rural areas of an LIC or NEE at a cost the community can sustain, but they supply villages, not cities or industry."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the terms, figures and places this room is examined on","items":["Energy security: the extent to which an affordable, reliable and stable energy supply can be achieved. Test every strategy you name against all three words.","Surplus and deficit: a surplus country produces more than it consumes and exports the difference (Qatar, Norway, Saudi Arabia, Canada); a deficit country imports (Japan, most of the European Union, and the United Kingdom, which became a net importer of natural gas in 2004 after North Sea output passed its peak around 1999).","The global picture: fossil fuels still supply about 80% of the world's primary energy, oil being the largest single source; roughly 700 to 800 million people have no electricity supply at all, and more than two billion still cook on wood, charcoal or dung.","Why consumption rises: economic development (industry, transport, appliances, air conditioning), rising population (the world passed 8 billion in November 2022) and technology, which both creates demand and saves it.","Factors affecting supply: physical (geology for fossil fuels, hot rock for geothermal, steep relief and rainfall for hydro, sunshine for solar, exposure for wind); cost of exploitation and production (deepwater, Arctic and tar sands only pay at high prices, while the cost of electricity from large solar farms has fallen by roughly 90% since 2010); technology (hydraulic fracturing with horizontal drilling made the United States the world's largest natural gas producer; larger turbines opened deeper water); and political factors (sanctions, war, producer decisions, nuclear policy).","Political shocks worth naming: Russia's invasion of Ukraine in February 2022 and the European gas price spike that followed; the Fukushima nuclear accident of March 2011 and the retreat from nuclear power it caused, with Germany closing its last reactors in April 2023; England's halt to fracking in November 2019 after tremors, the largest of magnitude 2.9, at the Preston New Road site in Lancashire.","Impacts of insecurity: exploration of difficult and environmentally sensitive areas (the Deepwater Horizon rig exploded in the Gulf of Mexico in April 2010, killing 11 workers and releasing millions of barrels of oil over nearly three months); economic and environmental costs; food production, because nitrogen fertiliser is made from natural gas and every stage of farming and food transport uses fuel; industrial output, cut by power failures such as South Africa's scheduled power cuts, at their worst in 2023; and the potential for conflict where demand exceeds supply.","Chokepoints and reserves: around half of proven oil reserves lie around the Persian Gulf, and roughly a fifth of the world's oil is shipped through the Strait of Hormuz.","The renewable options and their weaknesses: wind and solar are cheap and quick to build but intermittent; hydro is reliable and long-lived but can flood land and displace people; tidal is completely predictable but needs a large tidal range and a suitable estuary; wave power is still small-scale; geothermal is limited to volcanic areas — about nine Icelandic homes in ten are heated with geothermal water; biomass is renewable only if what is burnt is regrown.","The non-renewable options: fossil fuels are reliable and transportable but finite and the main cause of climate change, with gas releasing roughly half the carbon dioxide of coal for the same electricity; nuclear power is large, steady and low-carbon — about two-thirds of France's electricity — but expensive to build and leaves radioactive waste.","Scale of large hydro: the Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze in China, fully operational in 2012, has an installed capacity of 22,500 MW, and around 1.3 million people were moved for it.","The United Kingdom's shift: coal supplied about 40% of UK electricity in 2012 and about 2% in 2019, and the last coal-fired power station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar in Nottinghamshire, closed on 30 September 2024; wind is now the largest single renewable source, supplying roughly 30% of the country's electricity.","Energy conservation: insulation, double or triple glazing, draught-proofing, passive solar gain and smart meters in homes — BedZED in Sutton, south London, about a hundred homes completed in 2002, combined super-insulation with wind cowls that ventilate rooms while recovering heat; sensors, LED lighting and combined heat and power in workplaces; public transport, cycle networks, congestion charging and electric vehicles in transport.","Efficiency in the use of fossil fuels: a modern combined cycle gas turbine converts around 60% of the energy in its fuel into electricity, where an older coal-fired station managed under 40%; combined heat and power uses the waste heat as well.","Other local renewable schemes worth a sentence: household biogas digesters in Nepal, fed with cattle dung and water to make methane for cooking and lighting and a slurry that fertilises fields, installed in their hundreds of thousands since the national programme began in 1992; and solar home systems in Bangladesh, a panel, battery and controller giving a household light and phone charging off-grid."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan for the six-mark 'to what extent' question — 'A local renewable energy scheme is the best way of providing a sustainable supply of energy in an LIC or NEE.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use a named example in your answer. [6 marks]","items":["Decode the command phrase: 'to what extent' asks how far the claim holds, not whether you like it. Flat agreement and flat disagreement score the same. The examiner wants an appraisal of the degree to which the statement is true, so you need a variable that makes it true in one case and false in another — here that variable is scale, with site conditions second.","Decode the statement itself: two words do the work. 'Best' invites a comparison, so the alternatives have to appear — grid extension, a diesel generator, a large dam, solar home systems, biogas. 'Sustainable' has three strands you can score on: environmental (renewable, no flooded land, no emissions), economic (affordable to run and to repair) and social (it keeps working, locally, after the NGO leaves).","Six marks is two developed paragraphs and a verdict, so plan three moves and no more, and cut the introduction altogether: state your line and name your example in the first sentence. Agree strongly for scattered rural communities and disagree for cities and industry, then name Chambamontera, a farming community in the mountains of the Jaen province, in the Cajamarca region of northern Peru.","Paragraph 1 — the scheme and what it delivers, in that order so the process is shown and not just asserted: an intake weir on a fast stream, a channel to a settling tank, a penstock down the hillside, a turbine and generator in a powerhouse, the water returned to the river. Run-of-river, so no dam, no reservoir, no flooded fields and nobody displaced. Then the effects: clean light instead of kerosene, evening study, refrigeration for food and vaccines, no fuel to buy, local people trained to maintain it.","Mini-judgement to close paragraph 1: the realistic comparison is not with a national grid but with no electricity at all, because extending grid lines across that terrain would cost far more than the community could repay — and a scheme that is sustainable environmentally, economically and socially at once is rare.","Paragraph 2 — the limits, because 'to what extent' is not satisfied by praise: micro-hydro generates up to about 100 kW, so it cannot run industry; the initial cost needed outside funding from Practical Action; output falls when the stream drops in the dry season; and it only suits steep, wet terrain, where a flat, dry village needs solar home systems or household biogas digesters like Nepal's instead. Add the scale counter-case in one sentence rather than a paragraph of its own: the cities and factories of an NEE are supplied by large power stations, and in Peru that means hydropower dams and the Camisea gas field. Mini-judgement: 'best' is conditional on the site and on the scale.","The evaluation thread running through both paragraphs: sustainability has to be judged against the realistic alternative for that place and that scale. Say it at the end of each paragraph rather than saving it all for the verdict, because the understanding marks are awarded across the whole answer.","The verdict — it must answer, not summarise: agree to a considerable extent for remote rural communities in an LIC or NEE, where a local renewable scheme is affordable, sustainable and locally controlled in a way nothing else is; disagree for the national scale, where large supply is still needed. Name the word that fails: 'best' is only true once you have said 'best for whom, and at what scale'.","Timing and the vocabulary that lifts the level: about a minute a mark, so roughly six to seven minutes — one minute planning the three moves, five writing and the rest checking. There are no separate marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar on a Section C energy question, so specialist terms earn their place by making the geography precise: run-of-river, penstock, turbine, intake weir, renewable, sustainable, energy security, kerosene, grid extension."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:17:18.789664Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file itself after the fix pass, and every fault the first verdict raised is cleared: there is no mention of SPaG anywhere in the kit, the only tariffs in it are 1, 2, 4 and 6 marks, and every AO split now matches a real one — AO1 = 1 on the multiple choice, AO4 = 1 on the calculation, AO4 = 2 on the describe-from-Figure-2 question (Paper 2 06.2), AO1 = 2 on the two-mark state and give questions (06.3), AO1 = 2 + AO2 = 4 on the six-mark knowledge questions (06.4) and AO2 = 2 + AO3 = 4 on both resource six-markers (03.3), including question 7, which was the wrong key last time. The model paragraphs now cite 06.4's own Level 1–3 wording with an honest note that AQA's nearest 'assess' phrasing comes from 01.8; FOCAM is correctly distinguished from Cusco's canon gasifero; Figure 3's two pipelines are separated; Figure 2 is a clean data column with the Ratcliffe-on-Soar note printed beneath it; and the give-away hints on questions 0 and 3 are replaced with method nudges. I rechecked every date, place and figure in the rewritten solutions, paragraphs, plan and worked example — Camisea from 2004, Pampa Melchorita from 2010, the 700 km pipelines, Chambamontera in Jaen province, Practical Action, 100 kW, the 82% fossil share and 7% each for hydro and other renewables in Figure 1, half of proven oil reserves around the Persian Gulf, a fifth of world oil through Hormuz, February 2022, BedZED's hundred homes from 2002, 60% against under 40%, 700–800 million without electricity, the 90% solar cost fall since 2010 — and every one is true and recounts correctly against the figures. Publishable as it stands; what is left is presentational."}}},"3.3.1":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.3.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Issue evaluation is the part of the course where geography stops being a set of separate topics and becomes a decision. You are given a booklet of resources about one real, contemporary issue — maps, graphs, tables, photographs and the words of the people involved — and asked to work out what is going on, whose interests collide, which of the options on the table is worth the money, and why. Nothing you have learned is off limits: river processes, extreme weather, climate change, urban change and the economy can all be needed on the same page. The marks are not for knowing things. They are for using the resources, weighing the alternatives and reaching a decision you can defend in terms of its effect on people and on the physical environment. This room gives you a full set of resources on one issue — flooding in the upper Calder Valley in West Yorkshire — and takes you from the one-mark reading questions to the extended judgement.","idea":[{"h":"What an issue evaluation actually asks of you","t":"An issue evaluation is critical thinking and problem solving done with geography. You are handed secondary sources about a problem that has no tidy answer, and you have to think your way through it: read the evidence, work out the cause, list the options, test each one and choose. That is a different job from an essay. In an essay you supply the material; here the material is supplied and your value is what you do with it. Nothing is credited for knowledge you simply unload — on this section there are no marks for AO1 at all on the final decision, so a rehearsed case study written out for its own sake earns nothing. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it treats every figure as evidence for or against an option, and every sentence it writes either interprets a resource or moves the judgement forward.","code":"3.3.1 — This section contributes a critical thinking and problem-solving element to the assessment structure"},{"h":"Where the issue comes from, and why two topics are better than one","t":"The issue can come from any compulsory part of the course, and the resources may take it somewhere you have never studied. That is deliberate: this section is synoptic, so you are expected to bring more than one theme from the physical units and the human units to bear on the same problem. Flooding in a Pennine valley, for example, is a river landscapes question (drainage basins, discharge, hydrographs, hard and soft engineering), a weather hazards and climate change question (warmer, wetter winters and more intense rainfall), and a human geography question all at once, because the towns at risk are former mill towns whose shops, cafes and converted mills sit on the floodplain. The scale matters too: what happens on the moor decides what happens in the town, and what the town does decides what arrives downstream. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it names the link between themes out loud rather than leaving the examiner to spot it.","code":"3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content"},{"h":"Twelve weeks with the resources, then a clean copy","t":"The resources are released twelve weeks before the exam so that nobody meets them cold. They can be maps at different scales, diagrams, graphs, statistics, photographs, satellite images, sketches, extracts from published material and quotations from interest groups. You may work through them, annotate them and research the place as much as you like beforehand, but the original booklet stays behind and you are given a clean copy in the exam. That means the value of the twelve weeks is in your head, not in your margins: know which figure holds which evidence, what the largest and smallest values are, where two resources disagree, and which piece of the course each figure connects to. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it cites figures by number — Figure 2 shows, Figure 4 says — so the examiner can see the evidence being used rather than remembered.","code":"3.3.1 — A resource booklet will be available twelve weeks before the date of the exam"},{"h":"From small questions to one big judgement","t":"The questions come in a deliberate order. The short ones put the issue in context and test geographical skills: label a place, shade a class on a choropleth, read a value off a graph, calculate a difference or a percentage. The middle ones ask you to interpret and analyse — explain a pattern, evaluate the extent of something, examine the conflicting viewpoints. Then everything turns into a single extended piece of writing in which you make an evaluative judgement about what should be done. Each earlier question is a brick in that wall, so the evidence you work out in the short answers is evidence you should reuse at the end. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it arrives at the extended question already knowing the numbers, because it worked them out on the way through.","code":"3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"},{"h":"Stakeholders, alternatives and a decision you can defend","t":"A critical perspective means taking the issue apart: who wants what, who pays, who gains, who loses, and what each option would actually do. Every option has advantages and disadvantages, and the alternatives have to be evaluated, not dismissed. The decision itself is judged on its justification — the evidence behind it and the reasoning that links the evidence to the choice — and the justification has to cover impacts on both people and the physical environment, because the interrelationship between the two is the geography of the issue. A cheap scheme that wrecks a habitat and a green scheme that leaves people flooded are both failures. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it says why the rejected options were rejected, names the group that loses under its own choice, and still defends the choice.","code":"3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading the cause of the issue out of a map (Figure 1)","setup":"Figure 1, written for this room, prints a sketch map of the upper Calder Valley above the valley's flood record. It shows moorland above about 400 m on both sides of the valley, with reservoirs, blanket peat, old drainage ditches called grips, sheep grazing and grouse moor. Four steep side valleys, or cloughs, feed the River Calder: Walsden Water at Todmorden, Colden Water and Hebden Water at Hebden Bridge, and Cragg Brook at Mytholmroyd. The valley floor is flat and narrow, in places less than 300 m wide, and carries the River Calder, the Rochdale Canal, the A646 road and the Manchester to Leeds railway line in the same strip. Four towns stand on that floor: Todmorden, Hebden Bridge, Mytholmroyd and Sowerby Bridge, with nineteenth-century mills now used as shops, cafes, flats and workshops beside the river. The question set is: Using Figure 1, suggest one reason why the towns in the upper Calder Valley flood easily. [2 marks]","steps":["Choose one condition on the map that puts water into the river quickly: the four cloughs are short and steep, so rain that falls on the moor is in the Calder within hours rather than days.","Develop that condition into its effect, which is where the second mark sits: a large volume arrives at the same time from four tributaries, so discharge rises faster than the channel through the towns can carry it.","Tie the effect to what is on the ground in the map, so the answer stays with Figure 1: the water spills onto a floor less than 300 m wide, which is exactly the strip occupied by the mills, shops and flats of Hebden Bridge and Mytholmroyd.","Check the reason against the rest of the map before you stop: that floor under 300 m wide is shared with the Rochdale Canal, the main valley road and the Manchester to Leeds railway, so water that has left the channel has nowhere to spread sideways and rises in depth through the towns instead."],"cues":["Which single feature on this map gets water to the river fastest?","That is one mark. What has to happen to the river for the second?","How do you show the examiner you are still reading Figure 1 and not a textbook?","The valley floor carries more than the river. What does that do to water that has already left the channel?"]},{"title":"Turning a hydrograph into evidence (Figure 2)","setup":"Figure 2 gives two storm hydrographs for the same tributary catchment above Mytholmroyd, drawn for the same storm. Rainfall: 18 mm fell in hour 2, 12 mm in hour 3, 6 mm in hour 4 and nothing after hour 5, so peak rainfall is in hour 2. Catchment as it is now, with drained peat and few trees: discharge 8 cubic metres per second at hour 0, 20 at hour 4, 52 at hour 6, 68 at hour 8, 40 at hour 12, 20 at hour 18 and 12 at hour 24. Catchment after upstream land management, with blocked drains, re-wetted peat and new woodland: 8 at hour 0, 14 at hour 4, 28 at hour 6, 40 at hour 8, 51 at hour 12, 36 at hour 18 and 22 at hour 24. The question set is: Using Figure 2, calculate the percentage fall in peak discharge between the catchment as it is now and the catchment after land management. Show your working. [2 marks] One mark is for the answer and one for the working, and nothing on this tariff is credited for saying what the change means. The work below goes past the question at the end on purpose, because the same two rows also carry the change in lag time, and those are the figures the 6- and 9-mark questions in this room expect you to quote.","steps":["Find the two peaks before doing any arithmetic: the highest figure in the first series is 68 at hour 8, and the highest in the second is 51 at hour 12.","Subtract to get the fall, then divide by the original peak and multiply by 100: the fall is 17, and 17 divided by 68 is 0.25, so the peak drops by 25 per cent.","Read the lag change off the same two rows, because it is free evidence: peak rainfall is in hour 2, so the lag time grows from six hours to ten hours.","Say what both numbers do for the towns: a quarter less water per second is more likely to stay inside the channel, and four extra hours is time for a warning to reach people and for shops to move stock upstairs."],"cues":["Which value in each series is the peak?","Percentage change of what, divided by what?","The same two rows hold a second piece of evidence. What is it?","Why would a resident of Mytholmroyd care about either number?"]},{"title":"Testing three options for value for money (Figure 3)","setup":"Figure 3 sets out three options for the valley. Option A, walls and channel works through Hebden Bridge and Mytholmroyd, costs 45 million pounds, better protects 900 homes and businesses and takes 4 years. Option B, natural flood management across the upper catchment, costs 12 million pounds, better protects 500 and takes 10 years. Option C, a flood storage area upstream of Mytholmroyd with flood doors and raised electrics in 700 properties and a faster warning system, costs 28 million pounds, better protects 1,100 and takes 5 years. A note under the table says that about 30 million pounds is available for the valley over the next five years. The question set is: work out the cost per property better protected for each option, and decide what that test can and cannot tell you.","steps":["Divide cost by properties for each option in turn: A is 45,000,000 divided by 900, which is 50,000 pounds each.","Do the same for the other two: B is 12,000,000 divided by 500, which is 24,000 pounds each, and C is 28,000,000 divided by 1,100, which is just over 25,000 pounds each.","Rank them and check the ranking against the budget: B is the cheapest per property and C is close behind, while A costs twice as much per property and its total is more than the money available in any case.","Name what the test misses, which is what lifts the point from arithmetic to geography: it counts nothing for the carbon stored in re-wetted peat under B, nothing for the ten years B takes, and nothing for the cost A passes downstream to Brighouse."],"cues":["What calculation turns a total cost into a fair comparison?","Do the same for B and C — which is cheaper?","Which option is ruled out before the ranking even matters?","What does this test fail to count?"]},{"title":"Sorting the stakeholders and finding the real conflict (Figure 4)","setup":"Figure 4 gives five views on the valley. A shop owner in Hebden Bridge has been flooded three times since 2012 and wants walls built now. A hill farmer above Cragg Vale says leaky dams and new trees take grazing land and that a storage area would flood his best fields, and asks who will pay him. An Environment Agency engineer says walls are the quickest to deliver, at four years, and protect the town centres that flood first, though they are not the option that protects the most property and they move water downstream faster, and that natural flood management works well in small and medium storms. A volunteer with a local river group says the drained and burnt moor sends water down in hours and should be fixed first, and that dams and trees store carbon and bring back wildlife. A resident of Brighouse, downstream, says that whatever is done upstream, the water must not be sent to them any faster. The question set is: work out which option each speaker supports and where the real conflict lies.","steps":["Attach each speaker to an option: the shop owner and, on speed alone, the engineer lean to Option A, whose four years is the shortest delivery time in Figure 3 even though its 900 properties are fewer than Option C's 1,100; the volunteer argues for Option B; the farmer opposes B and C because both take his land; the Brighouse resident rules out A.","Look for the axis they disagree along, rather than listing them: the argument is about where the money is spent, upstream on the land or downstream in the town, and therefore about who is protected first.","Add the second axis, which is who pays: the farmer carries the cost of an upstream solution in lost grazing, while the Brighouse resident carries the cost of a downstream one in a faster peak arriving at their own doorstep.","Find the point of agreement, because that is where a defensible decision starts: nobody argues for doing nothing, and nobody defends sending more water down the valley faster than it goes now."],"cues":["Which option is each speaker really asking for?","What single question are they disagreeing about?","Under each solution, who ends up paying?","Is there anything all five would accept?"]},{"title":"Building the decision paragraph that earns the top level","setup":"You have Figures 1 to 4 and this question: choose the option in Figure 3 that you think should be funded, and justify your choice using evidence from the resources and your own understanding. You know that about 30 million pounds is available, that Option A costs 45 million and protects 900, Option B costs 12 million and protects 500 over 10 years, and Option C costs 28 million and protects 1,100 over 5 years. You know from Figure 2 that the peak falls from 68 to 51 cubic metres per second and the lag grows from six to ten hours if the upper catchment is managed, and from Figure 1 that 400 properties flooded in June 2012, 1,500 in Storm Eva on 26 December 2015 and 800 in Storm Ciara on 9 February 2020.","steps":["Rank the options against the money before you write a word: set the three costs beside the roughly £30 million available, and Option A at £45 million does not fit, so the live choice is between Option B and Option C on what they protect and how soon.","State the decision in the first sentence and attach the strongest number to it: fund Option C, which fits inside the budget and better protects 1,100 properties, more than either alternative.","Give each alternative its due, then say why it loses: Option B treats the cause, lowering the peak by a quarter and lengthening the lag by four hours, but it protects only 500 properties and takes ten years, and the valley has flooded three times in eight years; Option A goes in a single evidenced clause, £45 million against the £30 million available, with Figure 4's Brighouse resident naming the flaw in walls — they pass the problem on.","Close with the people-and-environment judgement and the qualifier that shows the decision is informed, not rehearsed: C protects the shops and flats that flood first, but it floods farmland deliberately and leaves the drained moor untreated, so the moorland work must follow as winters turn wetter."],"cues":["Which option can you remove immediately, and on what evidence?","What must the first sentence of a decision answer contain?","How do you show the alternative has been evaluated rather than ignored?","What two things must the judgement weigh before it ends?"]}],"check":[{"q":"In Figure 3, which options could be funded on their own from the 30 million pounds available, and which could not?","a":"Option B at 12 million and Option C at 28 million each fit. Option A at 45 million does not, and B and C together at 40 million do not either."},{"q":"Figure 2 shows peak discharge falling from 68 to 51 cubic metres per second and the peak arriving in hour 12 instead of hour 8. Which two words name those two changes?","a":"Peak discharge, which falls by 25 per cent, and lag time, which grows from six hours to ten hours."},{"q":"Why does an answer that describes all three options carefully but never chooses one score badly?","a":"The marks are for applying the evidence to make and justify a decision. A description without a choice, and without reasons for rejecting the others, has not done the task."},{"q":"Name the two things a justification has to weigh before the answer ends.","a":"The likely impact on people and the likely impact on the physical environment."}]},"room":{"facts":["Lag time is the gap between peak rainfall and peak discharge. Lengthening the lag and lowering the peak are the two things any flood scheme is trying to achieve.","Cost per property protected = total cost of the scheme divided by the number of properties it better protects. In Figure 3 that is 50,000 pounds for Option A, 24,000 for Option B and just over 25,000 for Option C.","Four tests to run on every option in an issue evaluation: does it fit the money available, how many people and properties does it protect, what does it do to the physical environment, and who gains and who loses?","Hard engineering keeps water in the channel and moves it on faster, so it protects one place and can worsen the next one downstream. Natural flood management holds water back across the catchment, so it lowers the peak everywhere but stores a limited volume and takes years to work.","A decision is justified by three moves in order: choose an option, evaluate it against the alternatives using named evidence from the resources, and judge its impact on both people and the physical environment.","The flood record printed under the map in Figure 1 gives three floods in eight years in the same valley: 400 properties in June 2012, 1,500 in Storm Eva on 26 December 2015 and 800 in Storm Ciara on 9 February 2020.","Section A of Paper 3, the issue evaluation, is worth 37 of the paper's 76 marks. Split the 90 minutes between the sections in proportion to their marks, not at a minute a mark: 37 of 76 marks is about 44 minutes, so give Section A about 45 minutes — about half the paper — and Section B, the fieldwork and its 39 marks, the rest. A minute a mark is the rule for pacing one question, but across the section it accounts for only 37 of the 90 minutes; the rest is reading the resources, planning and checking.","The Section A stems run in one order: 1- and 2-mark point-marked questions on a named figure (State, Give, Complete, Calculate, and 'Using Figure 1' questions, including reading a photograph), then 6-mark level-of-response questions (Describe, Explain, Suggest, Compare, Analyse, Evaluate, and 'Using Figure X and your own understanding'), then the 9-mark decision question that closes the section. The specimen Section A has ten parts worth 1, 1, 6, 1, 1, 1, 6, 2, 6 and 9 marks — 34 raw marks, 37 with the 3 SPaG marks on the decision question — so Section A sets no 4-mark level-of-response question; Paper 3's only 4-mark level question, marked Levels 1 to 2, is in Section B.","The 9-mark decision question is marked AO3 = 6 and AO4 = 3, with no AO1 at all, across Levels 1 to 3: Basic 1-3, Clear 4-6, Detailed 7-9.","Paper 3 carries 6 SPaG marks in total and only two questions carry them: 3 on the Section A decision question and 3 on the Section B question about your own fieldwork enquiry. No other question on the paper is marked for SPaG."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1, which of the three floods shown flooded the greatest number of homes and businesses in the upper Calder Valley? Shade one oval. A 22 June 2012   B 26 December 2015   C 9 February 2020 [1 mark]","a":"One mark for the correct answer: B 26 December 2015. No credit if two or more answers are shaded. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"B, 26 December 2015. Read down the column headed 'Homes and businesses flooded in the valley' and compare the three values printed there: 400 for 22 June 2012, 1,500 for 26 December 2015 and 800 for 9 February 2020. The largest of the three is 1,500, so the answer is the Storm Eva flood on Boxing Day 2015. Only one oval may be shaded, so resist the temptation to hedge between two.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Only one column in Figure 1 counts properties; the rainfall and river level columns answer a different question.","Compare the three values in that column, then read across the row to find the date that goes with the largest.","Commit to one of the three; hedging between two is not an answer here."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, calculate the difference between the number of homes and businesses flooded on 26 December 2015 and the number flooded on 9 February 2020. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for the correct answer: 700. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"700. Figure 1 gives 1,500 properties flooded on 26 December 2015 and 800 on 9 February 2020, and 1,500 minus 800 is 700. Write the number on its own; a difference needs no units here because the column is already a count of properties, and there is no second mark for explaining the sum on a one-mark calculation.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The word difference means one value taken away from another, not a percentage or a ratio.","Take the two values out of the same column of Figure 1, one from each of the two rows named in the question.","One subtraction, one number written down, nothing else."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, state which one of the three options could not be paid for from the money available to the valley. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for the correct answer: Option A. Credit an answer that names the walls and channel works scheme. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"Option A. The note printed under the table in Figure 3 says that about 30 million pounds is available for the valley over the next five years. Option A, the walls and channel works through Hebden Bridge and Mytholmroyd, costs 45 million pounds, which is more than the money available. Option B at 12 million and Option C at 28 million both fit inside it, although not together, because 12 and 28 come to 40.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The figure you need is not in the table itself but in the note printed underneath it.","Compare each option's cost with the money available and find the one that is larger.","Name the option; you are not asked for the reasoning here."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1, suggest one reason why the towns in the upper Calder Valley flood easily. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for identifying a condition shown on the map, eg four steep cloughs feed the Calder (1); the valley floor is under 300 m wide in places (1). Second mark for developing it into the effect on flood risk, eg so run-off arrives quickly and together, and the water spreads over the strip the towns occupy (2). AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Figure 1 shows four short, steep side valleys feeding the River Calder — Walsden Water, Colden Water, Hebden Water and Cragg Brook — so rain falling on the moorland above is in the main river within hours, and the discharge from all four arrives at much the same time. That is the first mark. The second comes from what happens next: the valley floor is flat and in places less than 300 m wide, and the river shares that strip with the canal, the A646 and the railway, so when the channel fills there is nowhere for the water to spread except across the four towns built on the same floor. The mills, shops and flats beside the river are therefore the first things the water reaches.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The command is suggest, so the reason has to come out of what is drawn on the map rather than out of memory.","Pick one feature that speeds water into the river, or one that stops it spreading out safely, and follow it through to the towns.","Two marks means one reason taken two steps: the condition on the map, then what it does to the water and the buildings."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, calculate the percentage fall in peak discharge between the catchment as it is now and the catchment after upstream land management. Show your working. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer: 25 per cent. One mark for an indication of the calculation, eg peak falls from 68 to 51, so 17 divided by 68, multiplied by 100. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"25 per cent. The peak of the first hydrograph in Figure 2 is 68 cubic metres per second, reached in hour 8; the peak of the managed catchment is 51 cubic metres per second, reached in hour 12. The fall is 68 minus 51, which is 17. As a percentage of the original peak that is 17 divided by 68, which is 0.25, multiplied by 100, so 25 per cent. Write the subtraction and the division down: half the marks on a calculation of this kind are for showing where the figure came from.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A percentage change is always measured against the value you started with, not the one you ended at.","Find the highest value in each of the two series first, then work with just those two numbers.","Two marks, so the working has to appear on the page as well as the answer."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and Figure 4, suggest one reason why the resident of Brighouse is worried about Option A. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for identifying the concern, eg walls and a deepened channel move water out of the towns faster (1). Second mark for developing it to the effect downstream, eg so a higher peak reaches Brighouse sooner and the flood risk is transferred rather than removed (2). AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Brighouse lies downstream of the mapped valley, and Option A works by keeping the river inside a walled, deepened and widened channel through Hebden Bridge and Mytholmroyd. That is the identification mark: the scheme speeds the water on rather than storing it. The development is what this means for the resident. Water that is no longer allowed to spill in the upper towns arrives downstream in a shorter time and in a larger volume, so the peak at Brighouse is higher and comes sooner, and the people who gain nothing from the walls carry part of their cost. The Environment Agency engineer in the same figure makes the same point, which is why the objection is a geographical one rather than simply local feeling.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look at where Brighouse sits in relation to the two towns Option A would protect.","Ask what a smooth, walled channel does to the speed and timing of the flow that leaves those towns.","One concern, developed one step to its effect further down the river."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and your own understanding, explain how managing the land in the upper catchment changes the way a storm reaches the towns downstream. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks. Credit a logical chain from land cover to infiltration, storage and run-off, quantified from the hydrographs (peak 68 to 51, lag 6 hours to 10, higher flow still at hour 24). Max Level 1 for description of the graph alone with no mechanism.","sol":"As the catchment stands, Figure 2 shows a flashy response. Drained, burnt and bare blanket peat has little capacity to absorb, the grips carry water straight off the moor, and there are few trees to intercept the rain, so most of the 18 mm that falls in hour 2 travels as overland flow down the steep cloughs. Discharge climbs from 8 cubic metres per second to 52 by hour 6 and peaks at 68 in hour 8, only six hours after the heaviest rain. After management the same storm behaves differently. Leaky timber dams hold water in the cloughs and release it slowly, new woodland intercepts rain on its leaves while its roots open the soil so more water infiltrates, and re-wetted sphagnum peat acts as a sponge. Water therefore reaches the channel by slower routes, so the peak falls to 51, a quarter lower, and does not arrive until hour 12, making the lag time ten hours. For the towns that is the whole point: a lower peak is more likely to stay inside the channel, and four extra hours is time for a warning to work. The limit is visible in the same graph — the water is delayed, not removed, so the managed catchment is still running at 22 cubic metres per second at hour 24 against 12, and in a truly extreme storm the upstream stores fill and the benefit shrinks.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The graph shows what changes; the marks are for why it changes, so think about where the rain goes before it reaches the river.","Work through interception, infiltration and storage in turn, and give each one a route that ends at the channel.","Anchor every stage in a number from Figure 2, and finish with what the change is worth to people in the towns."],"codes":["3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, evaluate the relative importance of the valley's physical geography and its weather in causing the floods shown. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, AO4 = 1 mark. Credit evaluation that weighs the permanent conditions on the map in Figure 1 against the storm rainfall in the flood record printed under it, supported with evidence from both, and reaches a supported view. Max Level 1 for description of one part of the figure in isolation.","sol":"The physical geography decides that the valley will flood; the weather decides when and how badly. Figure 1 shows conditions that never change: moorland catchments of blanket peat that shed water quickly, four short steep cloughs delivering that water almost simultaneously, and a floor less than 300 m wide shared by the river, the canal, the A646, the railway and four towns built on the only flat land there is. Given those conditions, any intense rainfall produces a flood, which is why 60 mm of summer thunderstorm rain on 22 June 2012 still put 400 properties under water. The weather, though, controls the scale. The two worst events in Figure 1 were named winter storms: Storm Eva on 26 December 2015, with 90 mm in 24 hours and a peak level of 4.6 m, flooded 1,500 properties, and Storm Ciara on 9 February 2020, with 80 mm, flooded 800. Half again as much rain produced almost four times the damage, so the relationship is not a simple one. On balance the physical setting is the more important cause, because it is the reason an ordinary storm becomes a flood at all and it cannot be changed, while the weather is the trigger whose severity sets the size of the loss.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["One figure, two kinds of cause: the map holds the permanent conditions, the flood record under it holds the events.","Ask what each cause explains that the other cannot — why here, and why this size.","Evaluate means the answer must come down somewhere, with evidence from both halves of Figure 1 behind the verdict."],"codes":["3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3 and your own understanding, evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of Option A for the people and the environment of the upper Calder Valley. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks. Credit balanced evaluation of the walls and channel works using the table's own evidence (45 million pounds, 900 properties, 4 years, loss of river frontage, faster flow downstream) and a supported overall view. No credit for simply describing the option.","sol":"Option A has the strongest short-term case for people. It better protects 900 homes and businesses and it is the quickest of the three at four years, which matters because the properties that flood first are the ground-floor shops, cafes and flats in the converted mills beside the river, and those are the businesses the towns now live on. Walls up to 1.5 m, a deepened and widened channel and three rebuilt bridges with wider arches also raise the capacity of the channel exactly where the risk is concentrated, so the protection is immediate rather than gradual. The disadvantages are serious. At 45 million pounds it costs more than the roughly 30 million available, so funding it means abandoning everything else. It works by moving water on, so the flow leaves the towns faster and reaches Sowerby Bridge and Brighouse sooner, transferring risk to people who gain nothing from the scheme. Environmentally it does nothing for the moorland that generates the flood, and it cuts off views of the river and part of the riverside path, weakening the very riverside character that draws visitors. Overall, Option A buys the most protection soonest for the people inside the walls, but it is unaffordable, it treats the symptom rather than the cause, and it exports part of the problem downstream.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Advantages and disadvantages both have to be there, and each one needs a figure or a phrase from the table behind it.","Sort your points into what the option does for people and what it does to the physical environment, then look at the cost against the money available.","Finish with one sentence that says which side of the balance is heavier and why."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied","3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4 and your own understanding, analyse the conflicting views of the stakeholders about how the valley's flood risk should be managed. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 3 marks, AO4 = 1 mark. Credit analysis that groups the five speakers, finds the axes of disagreement (upstream against downstream spending, who bears the cost) and uses their own words as evidence. Max Level 1 for listing the views one after another.","sol":"The five speakers divide along one main line: whether the money should be spent on the land above the valley or on the towns within it. The shop owner, flooded three times since 2012, wants walls and wants them now, and the Environment Agency engineer concedes that walls are the quickest to deliver, four years against five for Option C and ten for Option B, and that they protect the town centres that flood first — while stopping short of claiming they protect the most property, because Figure 3 gives Option A 900 against Option C's 1,100. Against them, the volunteer argues that the drained and burnt moor is what sends water down in hours, so the cause should be treated first, and points to gains the engineer does not price at all, carbon storage and returning wildlife. Underneath that argument runs a second one about who pays. The hill farmer loses grazing under natural flood management and his best fields under a storage area, and asks the question nobody in the figure answers: who compensates him? The Brighouse resident carries the opposite cost, because a walled, faster channel upstream delivers a higher peak sooner to a town that gets no protection in return. The conflict is therefore not simply between people and the environment but between places and between generations, since the upstream work is slow. What they share is small but real: nobody argues for doing nothing, and nobody defends sending water down the valley faster than it already goes.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Analyse means find the connections between the views, not report them one by one.","Try to group the speakers by what they want, then look for the question they are actually disagreeing about.","Ask who bears the cost of each solution, and finish by saying whether there is any common ground."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and your own understanding, evaluate the extent to which a changing UK climate makes schemes like those in Figure 3 more urgent. [6 marks]","a":"AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 4 marks. Credit understanding that UK winters are expected to become warmer and wetter with more intense rainfall, applied to the evidence in Figure 1, and balanced by the limits of three events and by non-climatic causes. Max Level 1 for generic climate change knowledge with no use of the resource.","sol":"The case for urgency is real but must be made carefully. UK winters are expected to become warmer and wetter, and warmer air holds more moisture, so the intense rainfall that produces flash flooding in steep catchments is expected to become more frequent. Figure 1 fits that expectation: two of the three floods listed were named winter storms, Eva on 26 December 2015 and Ciara on 9 February 2020, and the valley flooded three times in the eight years from 2012 to 2020, with the largest event putting 1,500 properties under water. If events of that size become ordinary, a scheme designed for today's peak discharge is already out of date, which strengthens the case for the options in Figure 3 that can keep growing, such as the catchment work in Option B, over a wall of fixed height. Against that, three events are far too few to prove a trend, and each was a single storm that cannot on its own be attributed to a changing climate. Other causes matter too: drained peat, burnt moor and buildings on the floodplain would produce floods here whatever the climate did. Overall, climate change raises the urgency and should change which option is chosen rather than whether to act, because the land use of the catchment is a cause that can be fixed now.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["This question reaches outside the resources into what you know about the UK's changing climate; both halves are needed.","Use Figure 1 as the test: does what it shows match what a wetter, stormier winter climate would produce?","To what extent means saying how far, so weigh the evidence against the limits of three events before you judge."],"codes":["3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied"]},{"q":"Choose the option in Figure 3 that you think should be funded for the upper Calder Valley. Use evidence from Figures 1 to 4 and your own understanding to justify your choice. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO3 = 6 marks, AO4 = 3 marks. Credit a decision based on a wide range of evidence from the full set of resources, evaluating the chosen option in terms of socio-economic and environmental benefits and against the rejected alternatives, with links between different parts of the course. Any of the three options can reach Level 3 if substantiated, but an answer choosing Option A must confront the funding gap — 45 million pounds against the roughly 30 million Figure 3 says is available — for its choice to count as substantiated.","sol":"Option C should be funded. It fits the roughly 30 million pounds available, at 28 million, where Option A at 45 million does not, and it better protects 1,100 homes and businesses, more than the 900 of Option A or the 500 of Option B. That matters because the problem is repeated and, in the worst events, growing: Figure 1 records 400 properties flooded in the summer storm of June 2012 but 1,500 in Storm Eva on Boxing Day 2015, and the map printed above that record shows why. Four towns are squeezed onto a valley floor in places less than 300 m wide, sharing that strip with the river, the canal, the A646 and the railway, and fed by four steep cloughs. There is nowhere for the water to go and nowhere for the towns to move.\n\nThe socio-economic case is the stronger half of Option C. An embankment holding back up to 1.2 million cubic metres of water on 40 hectares of farmland takes the top off the peak before it reaches Mytholmroyd, while flood doors and raised electrics in 700 properties mean that water which does get in causes days of cleaning rather than months of rebuilding, and sirens and text alerts buy the hours in which stock can be moved upstairs. The shop owner in Figure 4 has been flooded three times since 2012, and it is the ground-floor shops, cafes and flats in the converted mills that flood first, so keeping them trading protects the visitor and small-business economy on which these former mill towns now depend.\n\nEnvironmentally, Option C is the weakest of the three, and that has to be conceded. It leaves untouched the drained peat and burnt moorland that Figure 2 identifies as the root of the problem: as the catchment stands the peak reaches 68 cubic metres per second six hours after the heaviest rain, while a managed catchment peaks at 51, a quarter lower, and ten hours after. It also floods 40 hectares of farmland deliberately, which is precisely the hill farmer's objection, and compensation for him would have to be part of the scheme.\n\nOption A is ruled out twice over, by a cost beyond the budget and by the flaw the engineer and the Brighouse resident both name: walls hand a faster, higher peak to the towns downstream. Option B is the better long-term answer but protects the fewest people and takes ten years. Since warmer, wetter winters are expected to make storms like Eva more common, the decision should be to fund Option C now for the people most exposed, and to commit to the catchment work of Option B as soon as money allows.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["There is no credit here for knowledge you have not used, so plan around the evidence in the figures rather than around a case study.","Test each option against the same three questions: can it be paid for, how many people does it protect, and what does it do to the physical environment?","Decide in the first sentence, then spend the rest of the answer proving it and saying why the other two were rejected."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied","3.3.1 — This section contributes a critical thinking and problem-solving element to the assessment structure","3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]},{"q":"'The money would do more good spent on the moors above the valley than on walls in the towns.' Using the resources and your own understanding, evaluate this statement and reach a decision. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO3 = 6 marks, AO4 = 3 marks. Credit evaluation of upstream catchment management against downstream hard engineering, quantified from Figures 2 and 3 and supported by the stakeholder views, ending in a substantiated decision. Credit agreement, disagreement or a qualified position.","sol":"The statement is right in the long run, and Figure 2 makes its case better than any argument. With the catchment as it is, drained peat and few trees, discharge peaks at 68 cubic metres per second only six hours after the heaviest rain; the same storm on a managed catchment peaks at 51, a quarter lower, and four hours later, in hour 12. Lowering the peak and lengthening the lag is the only intervention that treats the cause rather than the symptom, because Figure 1 shows the water arriving down four steep cloughs — Walsden Water, Colden Water, Hebden Water and Cragg Brook — onto a floor less than 300 m wide that the towns, the canal, the A646 and the railway already share. Option B costs 12 million pounds against the roughly 30 million available, and it earns a second dividend that no wall can: 900 hectares of re-wetted blanket peat is a carbon store and a habitat as well as a sponge.\n\nWalls, the other half of the statement, are the weaker proposition. Option A costs 45 million and therefore cannot be funded at all from the money available. It works by moving water on faster, which is exactly what the Brighouse resident fears and what the Environment Agency engineer concedes, so part of its cost is paid by people downstream who receive no protection. It also cuts off views of the river and part of the riverside path, weakening the riverside character that the shops and cafes trade on.\n\nThe statement still has to face the volunteer's overreach. Natural flood management better protects only 500 properties against 900 for walls, and it takes ten years to work as trees grow and peat re-wets. Figure 1 shows the valley flooding three times in eight years, and the shop owner flooded three times since 2012 cannot wait a decade. The engineer's warning is the sharpest point against the statement: upstream stores hold a limited volume, so in the largest storms, of which Storm Eva on 26 December 2015 is the local example with 1,500 properties flooded, they fill and the peak comes through anyway.\n\nThe decision is to agree, with one condition. Fund Option B at 12 million and hold the remaining 18 million for Option C, not walls, so people are protected before the woodland grows. Figure 3 prices Option C whole, at 28 million, so the last 10 million must still be found. Since UK winters are expected to become warmer and wetter, money spent on the moor is money spent on a risk that keeps rising, while a wall of fixed height is overtaken by the first storm larger than the one it was designed for.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The statement names two places to spend money, so the answer has to test both rather than praise one.","The hydrographs and the cost table between them give you numbers for effectiveness, cost and time; use all three measures.","A decision is still required, and a qualified yes is a decision if you say exactly what the qualification is."],"codes":["3.3.1 — This section contributes a critical thinking and problem-solving element to the assessment structure","3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied"]},{"q":"Choose the option in Figure 3 that you think does most for both the people of the upper Calder Valley and its physical environment. Justify your choice using evidence from the resources and your own understanding. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO3 = 6 marks, AO4 = 3 marks. Credit a decision justified in terms of impact on people and on the physical environment together, with evidence drawn from across the resources and the alternatives evaluated. Generic statements about advantages and disadvantages without a decision stay at Level 1.","sol":"Option B does most for both, because it is the only one of the three whose benefit to people and its benefit to the physical environment come from the same action. Blocking the moorland drains, re-wetting 900 hectares of blanket peat, planting 600 hectares of new woodland in the cloughs and building 1,200 leaky timber dams all work by slowing water down. Figure 2 measures the result: the peak falls from 68 to 51 cubic metres per second, a quarter lower, and the lag time grows from six hours to ten. For people that means a flow more likely to stay inside the channel through Hebden Bridge and Mytholmroyd, and four extra hours in which a warning can reach a shop owner who has been flooded three times since 2012. For the physical environment the same works rebuild a habitat: wet sphagnum peat stores carbon instead of releasing it, woodland in the cloughs adds shelter and shade, and the river keeps a natural channel rather than being deepened, widened and walled.\n\nThe alternatives each fail one half of the test. Option A better protects 900 properties in only four years, but at 45 million pounds it exceeds the roughly 30 million available, it gives the physical environment nothing at all, and it moves a faster, higher peak down to Sowerby Bridge and Brighouse, which the Environment Agency engineer concedes and the Brighouse resident refuses. Option C protects the most people, 1,100, and fits the budget at 28 million, but it deliberately floods 40 hectares of farmland behind an earth embankment, and its flood doors and raised electrics in 700 properties are an admission that water will still get in; it manages the consequences rather than the cause.\n\nOption B's weaknesses are real and have to be said. It better protects only 500 properties, it takes ten years, and the engineer is right that upstream storage is least effective in the very largest storms, of which Storm Eva on 26 December 2015 and its 1,500 flooded properties is the local example. The hill farmer also loses grazing, so the scheme needs payments to the people whose land does the work.\n\nWeighing those, Option B still wins on the question asked. It is the cheapest at 12 million, it treats the cause rather than the symptom, and it is the only option that leaves the valley's physical environment better than it found it. With warmer, wetter winters expected, a catchment that absorbs more water every year as trees and peat mature is worth more than a wall built to yesterday's peak.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The question sets the criterion for you: nothing counts here unless it is weighed for people and for the physical environment.","Run all three options through that double test before choosing, so the rejected two are evaluated rather than dismissed.","Name the losers under your own choice and answer them; a justification that hides its weaknesses is not a justification."],"codes":["3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied","3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content"]},{"q":"Calderdale Council must show that public money is well spent. Using Figures 3 and 4 and your own understanding, decide which option gives the best value for the money available and justify your decision. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","a":"AO3 = 6 marks, AO4 = 3 marks. Credit a decision built on comparison of cost against benefit, including cost per property protected (50,000, 24,000 and just over 25,000 pounds), time to complete and costs the table does not price, with a substantiated conclusion.","sol":"Value for money is not the same as cheapness, and the arithmetic has to come first. Dividing the cost of each option by the number of properties it better protects gives 45 million pounds over 900 for Option A, which is 50,000 pounds each; 12 million over 500 for Option B, which is 24,000 each; and 28 million over 1,100 for Option C, which is just over 25,000 each. On that test Option A is about twice as expensive per property as either alternative, and at 45 million it is beyond the roughly 30 million available in any case, so the real choice is between B and C.\n\nOption C is the better value of the two on everything the council can count. It better protects 1,100 properties, more than twice Option B's 500, for just over 25,000 pounds each, it fits inside the budget with about 2 million pounds to spare, and it delivers in five years rather than ten. Its three parts reinforce one another: the storage area lowers the peak reaching Mytholmroyd, the flood doors and raised electrics in 700 properties cut the cost of the water that still gets in, and the sirens and text alerts turn Figure 2's longer lag into usable warning time. Against the losses in Figure 1 — 1,500 properties flooded by Storm Eva in December 2015 and 800 by Storm Ciara in February 2020 — a scheme that spares most of that damage repays itself over a handful of events.\n\nThe honest qualification is that the cheapest per property is Option B, and that the table does not price everything. Option B's 900 hectares of re-wetted peat stores carbon and returns habitat, benefits that never appear in a cost per property; Option A's real bill includes the higher peak it hands to Brighouse, which the resident in Figure 4 names and the Environment Agency engineer accepts; and Option C's storage area takes 40 hectares of a farmer's land, so compensation belongs in its cost too.\n\nThe decision is Option C. It protects the most property of any option that fits the money and the time the council actually has, and Figure 3 completes it in five years against the ten years Option B needs, so the protection arrives twice as quickly for the properties that flood most often. The council should, however, treat the roughly 2 million pounds left over as the start of the catchment work in Option B, because in the long run slowing the water on the moor is what keeps the cost of every future scheme down.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["Value for money needs a calculation before it needs an opinion; the table gives you both numbers you need.","Compare the options on cost per property, on how long each takes and on how much of the loss in the flood record each would prevent.","Before concluding, ask what the cost column leaves out on each side — the environment and the people downstream both belong in the sum."],"codes":["3.3.1 — This section contributes a critical thinking and problem-solving element to the assessment structure","3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied","3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In Figure 3, which option is the cheapest and how much does it cost?","a":"Option B, natural flood management across the upper catchment, at 12 million pounds."},{"q":"In Figure 2, what is the lag time of the catchment as it is now, and what does it become after upstream land management?","a":"Six hours now, ten hours after management, because peak rainfall is in hour 2 and the peak discharge moves from hour 8 to hour 12."},{"q":"A justified decision has to weigh the likely impact on which two things?","a":"People and the physical environment."},{"q":"Name the approach that blocks moorland drains, re-wets peat, plants woodland and builds leaky dams to lower the flood peak.","a":"Natural flood management, also called slowing the flow or catchment-based soft engineering."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Issue evaluation","back":"Working through secondary resources on one contemporary geographical issue, weighing the options and the people affected, and deciding what should be done, using learning from several parts of the course at once.","code":"3.3.1 — This section contributes a critical thinking and problem-solving element to the assessment structure","open":false},{"front":"Stakeholder","back":"Any person, group or organisation with an interest in an issue and something to gain or lose from the decision: residents, businesses, farmers, landowners, councils, the Environment Agency and wildlife groups.","code":"3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied","open":false},{"front":"One piece of evidence from the resources against Option A, the walls","back":"Its £45 million cost is beyond the roughly £30 million available; it better protects 900 properties against Option C's 1,100; it moves water on faster, so Brighouse downstream receives a higher peak sooner.","code":"3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied","open":true},{"front":"Option appraisal","back":"Judging every option against the same tests: cost against the money available, people and property protected, effect on the physical environment, time to complete, and who gains and who loses.","code":"3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied","open":false},{"front":"A justified decision","back":"A choice that names its option, cites the resource evidence behind it, says what it does for people and for the physical environment, and explains why the alternatives were rejected.","code":"3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied","open":false},{"front":"Storm hydrograph","back":"A graph of river discharge against time after a storm, with rainfall drawn as bars. Its shape gives peak discharge, lag time and the steepness of the rising and falling limbs.","code":"3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)","open":false},{"front":"Lag time","back":"The gap between peak rainfall and peak discharge. A longer lag means water reaches the river by slower routes, so the peak is lower and a warning has time to work.","code":"3.3.1 — Assessment will consist of a series of questions related to a contemporary geographical issue(s)","open":false},{"front":"Natural flood management","back":"Slowing run-off across a whole catchment: leaky dams in steep side valleys, new woodland, re-wetted peat, blocked moorland drains and rivers allowed to spill onto floodplain land.","code":"3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","open":false},{"front":"Hard engineering on a river","back":"Walls, embankments, deepened or widened channels and relief channels that keep water in the channel and move it on quickly, protecting the built-up area but passing the flow downstream faster.","code":"3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","open":false},{"front":"Flood storage area","back":"An embanked basin, usually farmland, deliberately flooded during a storm to hold water back and cut the peak that reaches the settlements downstream.","code":"3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","open":false},{"front":"Property-level resilience","back":"Flood doors, air-brick covers, raised sockets and tiled floors that limit damage when water gets into a building. It shortens the recovery; it does not stop the flood.","code":"3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","open":false},{"front":"Blanket peat and grips","back":"Deep upland peat, drained in the past by ditches called grips and often burnt. Drained, bare peat sheds water fast; re-wetted sphagnum peat holds water back and stores carbon.","code":"3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","open":false},{"front":"Synoptic link","back":"Bringing two or more parts of the course to bear on one issue, such as river processes, extreme weather and climate change, and the economy of a former mill town on a floodplain.","code":"3.3.1 — The issue(s) will arise from any aspect of the compulsory sections of the subject content","open":false},{"front":"The pre-release resources booklet","back":"The maps, graphs, tables, photographs, extracts and quoted viewpoints on the issue, released twelve weeks ahead to be worked through, then met again as a clean copy in the exam.","code":"3.3.1 — A resource booklet will be available twelve weeks before the date of the exam","open":false},{"front":"Cost per property protected","back":"The cost of a scheme divided by the number of properties it better protects: a quick test of value for money that counts nothing for the environment, the time taken or fairness.","code":"3.3.1 — Students will develop a critical perspective on the issue(s) studied","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — written for this room. The issue: flooding in the upper Calder Valley, West Yorkshire — the valley and its flood record. A sketch map of the valley and its catchment, covering roughly 15 km of valley from Todmorden in the west to Sowerby Bridge in the east, with the record of its recent floods printed underneath it.\nShown on the map: moorland on both sides above about 400 m, with reservoirs, blanket peat, old drainage ditches called grips, sheep grazing and grouse moor; four steep side valleys, called cloughs, feeding the River Calder — Walsden Water at Todmorden, Colden Water and Hebden Water at Hebden Bridge, and Cragg Brook at Mytholmroyd; a flat valley floor, in places less than 300 m wide, carrying the River Calder, the Rochdale Canal, the A646 road and the Manchester to Leeds railway line in the same strip; four towns strung west to east along that floor — Todmorden, Hebden Bridge, Mytholmroyd and Sowerby Bridge — with nineteenth-century mills, now shops, cafes, flats and small workshops, standing beside the river in each; and an arrow off the eastern edge showing the Calder continuing through Brighouse, Dewsbury and Wakefield before it joins the River Aire.\nReal: the towns, the rivers, the canal, the railway, the road and the shape of the valley. Simplified and rounded for this room: the heights, the widths and the distances.\nThe flood record, printed under the map. The dates and the storm names are real; the rainfall totals, river levels and property counts are illustrative.\nDate | Event | Rainfall in 24 hours (mm) | Peak river level at Mytholmroyd (m) | Homes and businesses flooded in the valley | Season\n22 June 2012 | Summer thunderstorms | 60 | 3.4 | 400 | Summer\n26 December 2015 | Storm Eva | 90 | 4.6 | 1500 | Winter\n9 February 2020 | Storm Ciara | 80 | 4.2 | 800 | Winter\nNote printed under the table: two of the three floods listed came from named winter storms, and the valley flooded three times in the eight years from 2012 to 2020.","Figure 2 — written for this room; figures illustrative. Two storm hydrographs for the same tributary catchment above Mytholmroyd, drawn for the same storm: one for the catchment as it is now (drained and burnt blanket peat, few trees), one modelled for the catchment after upstream land management (drains blocked, peat re-wetted, new woodland in the cloughs, leaky dams).\nHorizontal axis: hours from the start of the storm, 0 to 24, marked every 2 hours. Left vertical axis: discharge in cubic metres per second, 0 to 80, marked every 10. Right vertical axis: rainfall in mm per hour, 0 to 20, drawn as bars.\nRainfall bars: 18 mm in hour 2, 12 mm in hour 3, 6 mm in hour 4, nothing after hour 5. Peak rainfall is in hour 2.\nCatchment as it is now (hours, discharge): 0, 8 | 4, 20 | 6, 52 | 8, 68 | 12, 40 | 18, 20 | 24, 12\nCatchment after land management (hours, discharge): 0, 8 | 4, 14 | 6, 28 | 8, 40 | 12, 51 | 18, 36 | 24, 22\nNote printed under the graph: peak discharge falls from 68 to 51 cubic metres per second, and the peak arrives in hour 12 instead of hour 8, so the lag time grows from six hours to ten.","Figure 3 — written for this room; costs and figures illustrative. Three options for reducing flood risk in the upper Calder Valley.\nOption | What it involves | Cost (£ million) | Homes and businesses better protected | Time to complete | Main effects\nA Walls and channel works in the towns | Stone-faced flood walls up to 1.5 m high along both banks through Hebden Bridge and Mytholmroyd, the channel deepened and widened, and three old bridges rebuilt with wider arches | 45 | 900 | 4 years | Protects the ground-floor shops and flats that flood first; walls cut off views of the river and part of the riverside path; water leaves the towns faster and reaches Sowerby Bridge and Brighouse sooner\nB Natural flood management across the upper catchment | 1200 leaky timber dams in the cloughs, 600 hectares of new woodland, 900 hectares of blanket peat re-wetted, moorland drains blocked and burning ended | 12 | 500 | 10 years | Slows run-off and lowers the peak; stores carbon and improves habitat; the benefit is smallest in the very largest storms; some hill farmers lose grazing land\nC Flood storage area, resilient buildings and better warnings | An earth embankment in a side valley holding back up to 1.2 million cubic metres of water on 40 hectares of farmland, flood doors and raised electrics fitted to 700 properties, and sirens and text alerts across the valley | 28 | 1100 | 5 years | Takes the top off the peak before it reaches Mytholmroyd and gives people time to act; the storage area floods deliberately, so farmland is under water for days; resilience limits damage but does not keep water out\nNote printed under the table: about £30 million is available for the valley over the next five years, so the three options cannot all be funded.","Figure 4 — written for this room; the speakers are invented but the arguments are typical of real schemes. Five views on what should be done in the upper Calder Valley.\nShop owner, Hebden Bridge town centre: 'I have been flooded three times since 2012. Each time it is six weeks before I can trade again and the insurance costs more afterwards. Build the walls, and build them now — four years already feels like a long wait.'\nHill farmer, above Cragg Vale: 'Leaky dams and new trees take grazing off me, and the storage area would put my best fields under water on purpose. I am not against the towns being protected. I am against paying for it out of my own farm with nobody answering the question of who compensates me.'\nEnvironment Agency engineer: 'Walls are the quickest to deliver — four years — and they protect the town centres that flood first, though they are not the option that protects the most property. They also move water on, and somebody downstream receives it sooner. Work in the catchment lowers the peak in small and medium storms; in the very largest the upstream stores fill and the flood comes through anyway. Every scheme has to show that its benefits outweigh its cost.'\nVolunteer, local river group: 'The moor is the problem. Drained and burnt peat sends water into the cloughs in hours instead of days. Block the grips, re-wet the peat and plant the cloughs, and you cut the peak for every town in the valley — and you store carbon and bring back wildlife while you are at it. Walls just hand the problem to the next town.'\nResident of Brighouse, downstream: 'Nobody up there asks us. Whatever is done in Hebden Bridge and Mytholmroyd, the one thing we cannot accept is water arriving here faster and higher than it does now.'"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 7-9) on the decision question about the upper Calder Valley","items":["The question it belongs to: Choose the option in Figure 3 that you think should be funded for the upper Calder Valley. Use evidence from Figures 1 to 4 and your own understanding to justify your choice. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks] (AO3 = 6 marks, AO4 = 3 marks)","The paragraph: Option C should be funded, and the budget makes the first part of the case. About £30 million is available, so Option A at £45 million is not a real choice, however attractive its four-year delivery looks to a shop owner who has been flooded three times since 2012. Within the money that exists, Option C better protects 1,100 homes and businesses against Option B's 500, and it does so in five years rather than ten. The mechanism matters as much as the number: an embankment storing up to 1.2 million cubic metres on 40 hectares of farmland takes the top off the peak before it reaches Mytholmroyd, while flood doors and raised electrics in 700 properties turn a month of rebuilding into days of cleaning, and text alerts convert the longer lag time shown in Figure 2 into warning that people can act on. That combination protects exactly what Figure 1 shows to be exposed — the ground-floor shops, cafes and converted mills packed onto a valley floor less than 300 m wide alongside the river, the canal, the A646 and the railway. The environmental price is real and has to be admitted: 40 hectares of a hill farmer's land is flooded deliberately, and the drained peat that Figure 2 identifies as the cause is left untreated. On the evidence available, though, protecting the most people inside the money and the time the valley actually has is the stronger claim, provided the catchment work of Option B follows as soon as funds allow.","Why it reaches Level 3 (Detailed, 7-9): the application is thorough because the chosen project is evaluated in both socio-economic and environmental terms rather than praised; the decision rests on a wide range of supportive evidence, with figures pulled from Figure 1, Figure 2, Figure 3 and Figure 4 in a single argument; detailed links are made between different areas of the course, since flood hydrology, land management and the economy of a former mill town all appear in the same reasoning; and the findings are communicated with clarity, in ordered sentences using specialist terms — discharge, lag time, peat, resilience, storage — which is what carries the High performance SPaG marks for a wide range of specialist terminology."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1-3) on the same question, visibly weaker","items":["The question it belongs to: Choose the option in Figure 3 that you think should be funded for the upper Calder Valley. Use evidence from Figures 1 to 4 and your own understanding to justify your choice. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks] (AO3 = 6 marks, AO4 = 3 marks)","The paragraph: I would choose option A because walls are the best way of stopping a flood. Flooding is a big problem in this area and lots of houses and shops got flooded which is really bad for the people who live there and for businesses. Walls would keep the water in the river so the town stays dry, and it would be finished quicker than the other ones. Option B would take too long and option C floods a farm which is not fair on the farmer. Flooding is getting worse because of climate change so something needs to be done soon. Overall option A is the best choice because it protects the most people.","What it meets and what it misses: it meets Level 1 (Basic, 1-3) because it applies knowledge and understanding to make a decision based on a limited range of supportive evidence and selects information with some links to the judgement — a choice is made and two options are dismissed. It misses Level 2 and Level 3 on every count. The evidence it does use is real but unquantified, which is what a limited range of supportive evidence looks like: it knows that Option A finishes soonest and that Option C floods a farm, and that homes and businesses have been flooded, but it gives no cost, no number of properties and no figure by name. Because nothing is quantified, the claim that Option A protects the most people goes unchecked, and it is simply wrong, as Figure 3 gives Option A 900 against Option C's 1,100 — Figure 4's engineer claims only that walls are the quickest to deliver, not that they protect the most property. The claim that Option A can be afforded is wrong too, since £45 million exceeds the £30 million available. Nothing is evaluated in socio-economic or environmental terms, no link is made to any other part of the course, and the conclusion repeats the opening sentence instead of weighing anything. The writing would also cost SPaG marks: it is loose and repetitive, with no specialist vocabulary beyond the word flooding."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the terms and the moves of an issue evaluation","items":["Issue: a contemporary geographical problem with no agreed answer, where people disagree about what should be done and any solution costs somebody something.","Stakeholder: a person, group or organisation with an interest in the issue and something to gain or lose. Ask of every one of them what they want, what they fear and what power they have.","Conflict: incompatible wants over the same place or the same money. Locate the axis of the conflict — usually where the money is spent, or who bears the cost — rather than listing opinions.","Option appraisal: running every option through the same tests. Cost against the money available; number of people and properties protected; effect on the physical environment; time to deliver; who gains and who loses.","Cost-benefit thinking: a scheme is normally expected to show that its benefits outweigh its costs. Cost per property protected is a quick version of that test, but it prices nothing for habitat, carbon or fairness.","Trade-off: the thing you give up by choosing. A decision that admits its trade-off and defends it is worth far more than one that pretends there is none.","Sustainability: whether the option still works, and still pays, in fifty years. Fixed defences are overtaken by a rising risk; catchment work grows more effective as trees and peat mature.","Justification: the decision plus the reasoning and the evidence. It must say what the option does for people, what it does for the physical environment, and why each alternative was rejected.","Synoptic reach: the issue will touch more than one theme from the physical and human units, and saying the link out loud is what turns clear application into detailed application.","Section A of Paper 3: 37 marks of the paper's 76, sat in 1 hour 30 minutes with a clean copy of the resources booklet released twelve weeks earlier. Share the 90 minutes between the sections by marks, not at a minute a mark: 37 of 76 is about 44, so about 45 minutes, about half the paper, and the rest for Section B.","The stems: multiple choice and 1- and 2-mark data and photograph questions set on a named figure; 6-mark level-of-response questions in the board's command words; one 9-mark decision question at the end. The specimen's ten parts are worth 1, 1, 6, 1, 1, 1, 6, 2, 6 and 9 — 34 raw marks, 37 with SPaG — so no 4-mark level-of-response question appears in Section A.","The decision question: 9 marks, AO3 = 6 and AO4 = 3 with no AO1, plus 3 SPaG marks (High performance 3, Intermediate 2, Threshold 1); Levels Basic 1-3, Clear 4-6, Detailed 7-9."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the upper Calder Valley issue, as this room's resources print it","items":["The place (Figure 1): four towns — Todmorden, Hebden Bridge, Mytholmroyd and Sowerby Bridge — on a valley floor in places less than 300 m wide, shared with the River Calder, the Rochdale Canal, the A646 and the Manchester to Leeds railway. Downstream the Calder runs on to Brighouse, Dewsbury and Wakefield before joining the Aire.","The catchment (Figure 1): moorland above about 400 m of blanket peat, drained by old ditches called grips, with four steep cloughs feeding the river — Walsden Water, Colden Water, Hebden Water and Cragg Brook.","The flood record (Figure 1): 22 June 2012, summer thunderstorms, 60 mm in 24 hours, 400 properties flooded; 26 December 2015, Storm Eva, 90 mm, peak level 4.6 m, 1,500 properties; 9 February 2020, Storm Ciara, 80 mm, 800 properties. Three floods in eight years, two of them in winter storms.","The hydrographs (Figure 2): rainfall peaks in hour 2. As the catchment stands, discharge peaks at 68 cubic metres per second in hour 8, a lag of six hours. After upstream land management it peaks at 51 in hour 12, a lag of ten hours — a fall of 25 per cent in the peak, and still 22 rather than 12 at hour 24.","Option A (Figure 3): walls up to 1.5 m, deepened and widened channel, three rebuilt bridges. £45 million, 900 properties better protected, 4 years. Fastest, but unaffordable, passes flow downstream faster and cuts the towns off from the river.","Option B (Figure 3): 1,200 leaky dams, 600 hectares of new woodland, 900 hectares of peat re-wetted, drains blocked, burning ended. £12 million, 500 properties, 10 years. Cheapest and treats the cause; slow, and weakest in the largest storms.","Option C (Figure 3): storage area holding up to 1.2 million cubic metres on 40 hectares of farmland, resilience measures in 700 properties, sirens and text alerts. £28 million, 1,100 properties, 5 years. Protects the most people inside the budget; floods farmland deliberately and leaves the moor untreated.","The money (Figure 3): about £30 million over five years. A at £45 million is out; B and C together at £40 million are out; B alone leaves about £18 million and C alone leaves about £2 million.","Cost per property better protected: Option A £50,000, Option B £24,000, Option C just over £25,000.","The stakeholders (Figure 4): a flooded shop owner wanting walls now; a hill farmer losing grazing under B and fields under C and asking about compensation; an Environment Agency engineer weighing speed against transferred risk and warning that upstream storage fills in extreme storms; a river-group volunteer arguing that the drained moor is the cause; a Brighouse resident downstream refusing a faster, higher peak."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — real UK flood-management examples worth knowing behind this issue","items":["Boxing Day, 26 December 2015 (Storm Eva): severe flooding across northern England, including Calderdale towns such as Hebden Bridge, Mytholmroyd and Todmorden, the Kirkstall area of Leeds, and parts of York where the Foss meets the Ouse. Earlier the same month Storm Desmond flooded Carlisle and Kendal in Cumbria.","Storm Ciara, 9 February 2020: flooding again in the Calder Valley; a week later Storm Dennis brought severe flooding to the Severn and Wye valleys.","Slowing the Flow at Pickering, North Yorkshire: leaky woody dams on Pickering Beck, new planting, moorland drain blocking and a flood storage bund completed in 2015; the town escaped flooding in the Boxing Day 2015 storms that hit much of northern England.","Somerset Levels, winter 2013-14: an exceptionally wet winter left low-lying farmland under water for weeks and flooded several hundred homes. Afterwards the Rivers Tone and Parrett were dredged and a long-term flood action plan was agreed for the area.","Jubilee River, Thames: a flood relief channel about 11 km long around Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton, opened in 2002. It protects those towns but has been criticised for passing risk downstream, which is the classic objection to hard engineering.","Who does what in England: the Environment Agency manages flood risk from main rivers and the sea, issues flood alerts and flood warnings, and appraises schemes; local councils handle surface water and local drainage; landowners and farmers control much of the land where run-off begins.","Hard engineering on rivers: dams and reservoirs, channel straightening, deepening and widening, embankments and flood relief channels. Soft or natural approaches: afforestation, wetland and floodplain restoration, river restoration, leaky dams and peatland re-wetting.","The climate context, in the specification's own terms: UK winters are expected to become warmer and wetter and heavy rainfall events more frequent, so a defence designed for today's peak discharge may be undersized within its own lifetime."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — 'Choose the option in Figure 3 that you think should be funded for the upper Calder Valley. Use evidence from Figures 1 to 4 and your own understanding to justify your choice.' [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","items":["Decode the command first. Choose and justify means the answer must name one option, defend it with evidence, and say why the other two were rejected. There is no AO1 here at all, so a rehearsed case study written out for its own sake earns nothing; every sentence has to use a resource or move the judgement.","Know where the marks sit: AO3 = 6 for applying the evidence, evaluating the chosen project in socio-economic and environmental terms and linking parts of the course; AO4 = 3 for selecting the right information and communicating the findings clearly; 3 more for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","Two minutes before writing: rank the options. Write the three costs against the roughly £30 million available and cross out anything that does not fit, then note the properties protected and the time to complete beside each. That single line of workings decides the answer.","Opening, two sentences. Name the option and give the strongest number for it straight away — Option C, £28 million, 1,100 properties better protected — and say what criterion the decision is being made on.","Paragraph 1, the socio-economic case, using Figure 3 and Figure 4. What the option physically does: 1.2 million cubic metres held back on 40 hectares, resilience measures in 700 properties, warnings that turn Figure 2's longer lag into usable time. Who benefits: the ground-floor shops, cafes and flats in the converted mills that Figure 1 shows packed onto a floor under 300 m wide, and the shop owner flooded three times since 2012.","Paragraph 2, the environmental case, honestly, using Figure 2. Concede that the drained and burnt peat that turns 18 mm of rain into a peak of 68 cubic metres per second in six hours is untouched, and that 40 hectares of farmland floods on purpose. Set against it what the option does not do: no walling of the channel, no faster flow handed downstream.","Paragraph 3, the alternatives evaluated rather than dismissed. Option A: fastest at four years and 900 protected, but £45 million exceeds the money, and both the engineer and the Brighouse resident name the flaw — faster water downstream. Option B: cheapest at £12 million, treats the cause and lowers the peak by a quarter, but protects only 500 and takes ten years, and the engineer warns it is weakest in the very largest storms.","The evaluation thread that holds it together: one criterion applied to all three options — how much of the harm recorded in Figure 1 does this option prevent, for whom, at what cost to the physical environment, and how soon? Asking the same four things three times is what makes a conclusion follow rather than arrive.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly ten minutes of writing and a minute at the end reading it back, because three further marks ride on spelling, punctuation, grammar and the specialist terms — discharge, lag time, peak, catchment, blanket peat, resilience, storage, hard engineering.","What the conclusion must say: not that all three options have advantages and disadvantages. It must restate the choice, name the trade-off it is accepting — deliberate flooding of farmland and an untreated moor — and say what would change the answer, namely that the catchment work of Option B should follow as soon as money allows, since warmer, wetter winters will keep raising the peak that any fixed defence was built for."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:28:14Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"The engineer's quotation, the single capping fault last time, is properly fixed and the fix is carried consistently into the Q9 model answer, the stakeholder worked example and the Level 1 commentary; the merge from five resources to four is clean, with every figure reference renumbered and every countable claim still recomputing correctly, so the room is publishable, but two new exam-mechanics items overstate the paper by putting 4-mark level-of-response questions in Section A when the specimen puts the only one in Section B."}}},"3.3.2":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.3.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Fieldwork is the only part of this course you write about from your own experience. Paper 3 Section B is worth 39 of the paper's 76 marks and asks about fieldwork twice over: once on materials from a place you have never been, where every mark comes from reading the resource and thinking like a fieldworker, and once on the two enquiries you actually carried out, whose titles you have to be able to give. Almost every mark in Section B is AO3 — application, interpretation and judgement — so nothing is earned by describing what you did. Marks come from justifying a method, explaining an anomaly, assessing a graph part by part, and judging how far results you know to be imperfect still support a conclusion. This room teaches the enquiry process as a chain of six strands, from choosing a question to evaluating the answer, and shows you how to write about a real set of results honestly enough to reach the top level.","idea":[{"h":"Two enquiries, two contrasting environments, one of them where people meet physical processes","t":"The specification is exact about what you must have done. Two geographical enquiries, each using primary data you collected yourself as part of a fieldwork exercise, with a clear link to the content of units 3.1 and 3.2. The fieldwork must take place outside the classroom and the school grounds on at least two occasions, in contrasting environments, and between them the two enquiries must show an understanding of both physical and human geography. In at least one of them you are expected to show an understanding of the interaction between physical and human geography. The two enquiries in this room are built to that rule: a coastal enquiry into beach sediment either side of the rock groynes at Mappleton on the Holderness coast, which is physical but turns on a human structure interrupting longshore drift, and an urban enquiry into environmental quality along a transect east from Broadmead in central Bristol. A top-level answer uses that pairing deliberately: when the question asks about your physical enquiry it names the physical process and the human structure acting on it, because the interaction is where the geography is.","code":"3.3.2 — Students need to undertake two geographical enquiries, each of which must include the use of primary data"},{"h":"Two kinds of fieldwork question: one about a place you have never seen, one about your own data","t":"Your understanding of the enquiry process is assessed in two ways. First, questions based on fieldwork materials from an unfamiliar context: a sketch map, a photograph, a table of somebody else's counts, a dispersion graph. You are not expected to know the place; you are expected to read the resource, judge the method behind it and suggest improvements, so every answer must quote something from the resource. Second, questions based on your own individual enquiry work, for which you must be able to identify the titles of your enquiries. In those questions the examiner cannot check your data, so credit comes entirely from how you reason about it: the justification, the analysis, the evaluation, the judgement. In both kinds you are expected to apply knowledge and understanding to interpret, analyse and evaluate information and issues related to geographical enquiry, and to select, adapt and use skills and techniques to investigate questions and communicate findings. A top-level answer therefore never recites a method; it argues from the data, whether the data are on the page in front of it or in its own results table.","code":"3.3.2 — Students’ understanding of the enquiry process will be assessed in the following two ways"},{"h":"Strand 1: a question you can actually answer, resting on a theory, in a place you can safely reach","t":"A workable enquiry question passes three tests. It names variables you can measure — sediment long axis north of a groyne against sediment long axis south of it, environmental quality score against distance from the centre — so that the answer can be yes, no or partly rather than a matter of opinion. It rests on a geographical theory or concept you can state before you go: longshore drift and attrition behind the beach enquiry, the changing character of land use away from a city centre behind the urban one. And it fits a place and a scale you can reach and work in safely in a day, with sources of primary evidence at that place and secondary evidence such as Ordnance Survey maps, Environment Agency records or Index of Multiple Deprivation scores to set it in context. Risk belongs to this strand too: physical fieldwork carries tides, slippery rock armour and unstable boulder-clay cliffs, human fieldwork carries traffic, strangers and getting separated in a busy street, and each risk must be met by a specific control, not by 'being careful'. A top-level answer justifies the question and the site in the same breath: the site was chosen because it lets this particular theory be tested.","code":"3.3.2 — Suitable question for geographical enquiry"},{"h":"Strand 2: primary and secondary data, and the sampling strategy that decides what your numbers are worth","t":"Primary data is collected first hand by you for your own enquiry; secondary data already exists because somebody else gathered it, usually for another purpose. Both are legitimate and a strong enquiry uses each for what it is good at. Having chosen the physical or human data you need, you must decide how to select it, because you cannot measure everything. Random sampling gives every item an equal chance and removes bias but can leave gaps. Systematic sampling — every metre along a tape, every tenth person, every third building — is quick, repeatable and evenly spread, but can fall into step with a repeating pattern on the ground. Stratified sampling divides the area or population into groups and samples each in proportion, which is the only way to guarantee that a small but important zone is represented. Recording matters as much: a prepared recording sheet, consistent units, the same observer for judgement-based scores. The mark scheme is blunt that naming a method earns nothing, because naming involves no justification. A top-level answer links the method to the theory being tested and the sampling strategy to the quality of the data, and names the bias it accepted.","code":"3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry"},{"h":"Strand 3: choosing a presentation technique that shows what the data actually do","t":"A range of visual, graphical and cartographic methods is available and each suits a particular kind of data. Continuous data measured along a transect suit a line graph; discrete categories suit bar charts; parts of a whole suit divided bars or pie charts; the spread of many readings at a few sites suits a dispersion graph; a relationship between two variables suits a scatter graph with a line of best fit. Where the data have a location, the technique should keep it: located bars, located proportional symbols, proportional flow lines, choropleth shading or an isoline map all fix the value to the place, which an ordinary bar chart throws away. Techniques can also be adapted — proportional circles drawn onto a base map, bipolar scores plotted as a radar or divided bar, photographs annotated with the process they show. Accuracy of axes, scale, class intervals and key is part of the technique, not decoration. A top-level answer can take its own graph apart component by component — axes, scale, categories, proportionality, what the plot lets a reader see at a glance — and say what each part does well and badly, because that is exactly what the six-mark 'assess' question rewards.","code":"3.3.2 — Selecting appropriate ways of processing and presenting fieldwork data"},{"h":"Strand 4: describe the pattern, find the anomaly, then explain both","t":"Analysis has a fixed order. Describe first: the direction of the trend, its size in the data's own units, where it is steepest. Then use statistics to sharpen it — the mean and the median as measures of central tendency, the range and the interquartile range as measures of spread, and for ordered data the quartiles read straight off the list or the dispersion graph. With eleven ordered values the median is the sixth, the lower quartile the third and the upper quartile the ninth, and the interquartile range is the upper quartile minus the lower. Then look for links between data sets: does the environmental quality score fall where land use changes, does sediment size fall where the groyne interrupts the drift. Then find the anomalies — the values that do not fit — and explain them from the conditions at that site rather than deleting them, because an anomaly explained is worth more than a tidy graph. A top-level answer never stops at the description: every figure it quotes is followed by the process, structure or land use that produced it.","code":"3.3.2 — Describing, analysing and explaining fieldwork data"},{"h":"Strand 5: a conclusion answers the question you asked, in the evidence you collected","t":"A conclusion is not a summary of what you did and it is not a general statement about coasts or cities. It is a direct answer to the title of the enquiry, drawn from the results and quoting them. If the enquiry asked whether beach sediment becomes smaller south of the groynes, the conclusion says whether it does, by how much, and on what evidence — a median long axis twenty-five millimetres smaller at the southern site, with a narrower interquartile range. If the hypothesis is only partly supported, the conclusion says which part holds and which does not, and where the exception lies. Good conclusions also return to the theory: the direction of change fits attrition during southward longshore drift, and the size of the step fits a groyne trapping the coarsest material. A top-level answer keeps the conclusion tied to the original aims and never claims more than the data can carry, because the reliability of that claim is what the next strand is about.","code":"3.3.2 — Reaching conclusions"},{"h":"Strand 6: evaluation is three moves, and the third one is where the marks are","t":"Evaluation means identifying the problems of your data collection methods, the limitations of the data you collected, the other data that might have helped, and the extent to which your conclusions were reliable. On the nine-mark question the mark scheme sets out three moves in order and rewards all three. First evaluate the results themselves: accuracy of the instrument and the reading, sample size, sampling strategy, and the variables that interfered on the day — the tide, the weather, a single observer's judgement, one morning in June. Second evaluate what those results contributed to the conclusion: which results carried the conclusion, which added nothing, whether the pattern was strong enough to build on. Third judge how far the conclusion is therefore reliable, and say in which respect — often the direction of the finding is secure while its size is not. Suggesting further data belongs here too: more sites, more repeats, a different season, a second observer, a matching secondary source. A top-level answer is honest and specific, because a generic list of problems with no judgement is exactly what the bottom level describes.","code":"3.3.2 — Evaluation of geographical enquiry"}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning a topic into a question you can actually answer","setup":"A class is going to the Holderness coast for a day. Four titles are suggested for the physical enquiry. (a) 'Holderness.' (b) 'Is the Holderness coast interesting?' (c) 'Is beach sediment smaller south of the rock groynes at Mappleton than north of them?' (d) 'Why does Britain need sea defences?' Decide which title is workable and say what is wrong with each of the others. A workable title must name variables that can be measured, rest on a geographical theory you can state beforehand, and fit a place and a scale you can reach and work in safely in one day.","steps":["Put the three tests to (a): it names no variables and asks nothing, so there is no answer it could be right or wrong about — it is a place name, not an enquiry question.","Put them to (b): 'interesting' cannot be measured with a tape, a ruler or a counter, so two students could collect the same data and reach opposite answers — the variable fails the first test.","Put them to (d): the scale is the whole country and the evidence would have to come from records rather than a beach, so no primary data could be collected on the day — it fails the third test.","Check (c) against all three: the measured variables are pebble long axis in millimetres at two named sites, one north of the groyne and one south of it; the theory is longshore drift moving material southwards with attrition reducing it, and a groyne interrupting that drift; and both sites lie on one beach that can be worked on a falling tide in an afternoon.","Write the reasoning as the justification you will be asked for: the title was chosen because it turns a named process into two things that can be measured at a site the class can reach safely."],"cues":["What would an answer to title (a) even look like?","How would two students each measure whether a coast is interesting?","Where would the data for title (d) have to come from?","What does title (c) ask you to measure, in what two places, and what process links them?","How would you say all of that in one sentence to an examiner?"]},{"title":"Choosing a sampling strategy for the beach transect","setup":"For Enquiry A you have one falling tide and about forty minutes at each of two beach sites 500 m apart, and both sites must be treated identically so that they can be compared. Four strategies are offered. (i) Pick up the pebbles that look typical of the beach. (ii) Lay a 10 m tape along the strandline and take the pebble lying at every 1 m mark, without looking. (iii) Use random numbers to choose coordinates inside a 10 m by 10 m quadrat and take the pebble at each. (iv) Divide the beach into three zones — strandline, mid-beach, upper beach — and sample each zone in proportion to its width. Choose one strategy and justify it, and say what each of the others would cost you.","steps":["Rule out (i) immediately: 'typical' is your own judgement, so you would be choosing the pebbles that fit what you expect, and that bias cannot be corrected afterwards or even measured.","Weigh (iii): random sampling gives every pebble an equal chance and removes bias entirely, but reading coordinates and finding each point is slow, and a small random set can leave whole stretches of the site unsampled.","Weigh (iv): stratified sampling is the strongest choice if the beach really does have distinct zones, because it guarantees each is represented, but the zones have to be identified and measured first and the time is not there for two sites.","Weigh (ii): taking the pebble at every 1 m mark from 0 m to 10 m gives eleven pebbles per site, spreads them evenly across the same length of beach at both sites, and can be repeated by anyone with the same tape, so like is compared with like.","Choose (ii) and name the bias you accepted: systematic sampling could fall into step with a repeating pattern such as regular cusps, so the justification says why even coverage and exact repeatability across two sites mattered more on this day than that risk."],"cues":["Which strategy lets your expectations decide which pebbles are measured?","What does removing bias cost you in time at a 500 m spacing?","What must you already know about the beach before you can use strategy (iv)?","How many pebbles does a mark at every metre of a 10 m tape give you?","What weakness does your chosen strategy still carry, and why accept it?"]},{"title":"Finding the anomaly in the pedestrian counts and explaining it","setup":"Figure 2 gives pedestrian counts taken for ten minutes at six sites down a high street, at 100 m intervals from the market square: Site 1 (0 m) 148, Site 2 (100 m) 121, Site 3 (200 m) 96, Site 4 (300 m) 103, Site 5 (400 m) 54, Site 6 (500 m) 31. Figure 1 shows that the street between Site 1 and Site 3 is pedestrianised and lined with chain shops and cafes, that a bus station with six stands opens onto the street beside Site 4, that three of the seven shop units between Site 4 and Site 6 are vacant, and that a supermarket car park lies behind Site 5. Identify the anomaly and explain it.","steps":["Work out the step between each pair of neighbouring sites: a fall of 27, then a fall of 25, then a rise of 7, then a fall of 49, then a fall of 23.","Every step is downward except one, so the rise is the value that does not fit the trend: the count at Site 4 is higher than the count at the site nearer the square.","Go to Figure 1 for the cause rather than inventing one: the bus station opens onto the street beside Site 4, so people arriving and leaving buses are counted there even though the site is well out from the square.","Check the explanation against the rest of the map so that it does not contradict the trend: the vacant units and the through-traffic beyond Site 4 explain why the fall resumes so steeply immediately afterwards, the largest single step in the whole transect.","State the rule an examiner is looking for: an anomaly is a value that breaks the pattern, and in fieldwork it is explained from the conditions at that site, not deleted to tidy the graph."],"cues":["What is the difference between each site and the next?","Which of those steps is the odd one out?","What does the map show at the site where the odd step occurs?","Why does the count then collapse so sharply beyond that site?","What should a fieldworker do with a value like this?"]},{"title":"From a list of pebble sizes to a median, quartiles and a meaning","setup":"Table 4a gives the long axis of eleven pebbles at each of two sites, in millimetres, already in order of size. Site 1, 50 m north of the southern rock groyne at Mappleton: 22, 28, 34, 41, 45, 52, 58, 63, 70, 76, 88. Site 2, 500 m south of the groyne: 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 31, 35, 38, 44, 51. Find the median, the quartiles and the interquartile range at each site, and say what the comparison shows about sediment moving along this coast.","steps":["With eleven ordered values the middle one is the sixth, so the median long axis is 52 mm at Site 1 and 27 mm at Site 2.","The quartiles sit at the third and ninth values: at Site 1 the lower quartile is 34 mm and the upper is 70 mm, so the interquartile range is 36 mm; at Site 2 they are 18 mm and 38 mm, so the interquartile range is 20 mm.","Compare the two summaries rather than the two lists: the median falls by 25 mm between the sites and the middle half of the measurements occupies a band only a little over half as wide at the southern site.","Read that against the theory: material carried south by longshore drift is worn down by attrition as it travels, and a groyne holds the coarsest material on its northern side, so both a smaller median and a tighter spread are what the theory predicts.","Say what the statistic cannot do, because the next question will ask: two sites on one falling tide cannot show a gradient along the coast, and a tighter spread could also mean the southern site simply had less variety of material delivered to it."],"cues":["Which value is the middle one when there are eleven of them?","Which positions in an ordered list of eleven give the two quartiles?","What has happened to the typical size and to the spread between the sites?","Which process would produce exactly that pair of changes?","What could these two sites not tell you, however clean the numbers look?"]},{"title":"Writing the conclusion, then evaluating it honestly","setup":"Enquiry A asked: 'Is beach sediment smaller south of the rock groynes at Mappleton than north of them, on the Holderness coast?' The results are the two sets of eleven measurements in Table 4a, summarised as a median of 52 mm and an interquartile range of 36 mm at Site 1, against a median of 27 mm and an interquartile range of 20 mm at Site 2. The conditions were: eleven pebbles per site, two sites 500 m apart, one falling tide on one day in June, pebbles taken at 1 m intervals along a 10 m tape without looking, long axis measured with a ruler to the nearest millimetre, and rock groynes in place at the site since 1991. Write the conclusion and then evaluate how far it can be trusted.","steps":["Write the conclusion as a direct answer to the title, carrying a figure: sediment is smaller at the southern site, the typical long axis being about half that north of the groyne, and it is also more uniform in size.","Evaluate the results first: the ruler reading is precise but the long axis of an irregular pebble is partly a judgement, the sample is small, and one falling tide in one month cannot represent a beach that is reworked by every storm.","Evaluate what those results contributed: the size difference between the sites is far larger than the measurement error, so the results carried the conclusion's direction securely, while the spread figures added a second, weaker line of support.","Say what other data would have helped: sites every 100 m over a kilometre rather than two sites, a repeat on a different tide and in winter, a roundness index alongside size to test attrition directly, and secondary records of cliff retreat either side of the defences.","Give the judgement in the form the mark scheme wants: the direction of the finding is reliable because the gap is too large to be an artefact of the method, but its size and its cause are not, because two sites on one day cannot separate attrition during drift from the groyne holding back the coarsest material."],"cues":["What did the enquiry ask, and what is the one-sentence answer with a number in it?","Which part of the method is a judgement rather than a measurement?","Was the difference between the sites bigger or smaller than the error in the method?","What would you add if you were sent back for a second day?","Which part of your conclusion is safe, and which part is not?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A student writes the title 'Is the beach at Mappleton nice?' What is wrong with it, in the language of strand 1?","a":"It names no measurable variable. 'Nice' is an opinion, so two students could collect the same data and reach opposite answers, and no geographical theory sits behind it. A workable title names quantities that can be measured and a process that links them."},{"q":"In Table 4b the total environmental quality score falls from +6 at Site A to -4 at Site D. Which single category is negative at every site, and what does that tell you?","a":"Noise, scoring -1, -1, -2 and -2. It tells you that one variable is poor along the whole transect rather than only at the far end. Noise does still slip by one point between Site A and Site D, but most of the ten-point fall comes from the other categories: litter and the condition of the buildings three points each, pedestrian safety two, greenery one."},{"q":"Why does the mark scheme give no credit for simply naming the data collection method in a 'justify' question?","a":"Because naming involves no justification. Credit comes from linking the method to the theory being tested, from explaining how the sampling strategy protects the quality of the data, or from explaining why the measurement or the survey criteria were appropriate."},{"q":"Name the three moves, in order, that the nine-mark fieldwork evaluation question rewards.","a":"Evaluate the results themselves; evaluate the contribution those results made to the conclusion; judge how far the conclusion is therefore reliable."}]},"room":{"facts":["The six strands of the enquiry process, in order: a suitable question for enquiry; selecting, measuring and recording data; processing and presenting the data; describing, analysing and explaining it; reaching conclusions; evaluating the enquiry.","Primary data is collected first hand by you for your own enquiry; secondary data already exists because somebody else collected it, usually for a different purpose — census figures, Ordnance Survey maps, Environment Agency records, Index of Multiple Deprivation scores.","Median and quartiles from an ordered list of eleven values: the median is the sixth value, the lower quartile the third and the upper quartile the ninth, and the interquartile range is the upper quartile minus the lower quartile.","Fieldwork is examined in two ways: on materials from an unfamiliar context, where every point must be anchored in the resource, and on your own two enquiries, whose titles you must be able to give.","The fieldwork evaluation runs in three moves: evaluate the results (accuracy, sample size, sampling strategy, the variables that interfered), evaluate what those results contributed to the conclusion, then judge how reliable the conclusion therefore is.","Naming a method is not justifying it. A justification links the method to the theory being tested, links the sampling strategy to the quality of the data, or explains why the criteria or questions used were appropriate."],"questions":[{"q":"State one difference between primary data and secondary data. [1 mark]","a":"AO1 = 1 mark. One mark for a difference that turns on who collected the data and for what purpose, eg primary data is collected first hand by the student for their own enquiry whereas secondary data was collected by somebody else, usually for another purpose. No credit for listing examples alone.","sol":"Primary data is data you collect yourself, for your own enquiry: the pebbles you measured on the beach, the pedestrians you counted on the high street, the answers people gave to your questionnaire. Secondary data already exists because somebody else gathered it, normally for some other purpose entirely: census figures, an Ordnance Survey map, Environment Agency records of cliff retreat, the Index of Multiple Deprivation scores for a neighbourhood. The difference is not how numerical the data are, nor whether they are on paper or on a screen; it is who collected them and for whom. The same rainfall total is primary if you read it from your own rain gauge and secondary if you take it from a weather station's published record.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The difference has nothing to do with the type of number or the format it arrives in.","Ask who was holding the tape measure, the clipboard or the counter, and who they were collecting it for.","One sentence with two halves: what you did, set against what somebody else had already done."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1 and Figure 2, give two techniques that could be used to collect primary data about how the high street shown is used. [2 marks]","a":"AO4 = 2 marks. One mark for each technique that could reasonably be used in the area shown, eg pedestrian counts, traffic counts, land-use mapping, an environmental quality survey, a questionnaire to shoppers, a count of vacant units, annotated photographs or field sketches. No credit for a technique that could not be carried out in the area shown, such as measuring river velocity.","sol":"Land-use mapping: walk the 500 m of street with a base map and record the use of every ground-floor unit — chain shop, cafe, vacant, bus station — which would show directly how the character of the street changes away from the market square. An environmental quality survey: score the same set of criteria at each of the six sites on a bipolar scale, giving a number for the condition of the street that can be compared with the pedestrian counts already collected. Both techniques fit the place Figure 1 shows: a street of shops, vacant units, a bus station and a car park invites counts, mapping and surveys of people and buildings, and both would be collected first hand by the students at the six sites already marked in Figure 2.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what Figure 1 actually contains — shops, units, buses, people — and ask what each of those could be counted or scored.","A technique only earns the mark if it could be carried out standing at one of the six sites on that street.","Two short phrases, each naming the technique and what it would record."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, describe the pattern of pedestrian flow along the high street. [2 marks]","a":"AO4 = 2 marks. One mark for a basic description of the pattern using the table, eg pedestrian numbers fall with distance from the market square. Second mark for a developed point using detail from the table, eg from 148 at Site 1 to 31 at Site 6, with the fall interrupted by a rise at Site 4. No credit for simply listing or comparing the individual counts.","sol":"Pedestrian numbers fall overall with distance from the market square. The highest count is 148 at Site 1, next to the square itself, and the lowest is 31 at Site 6, 500 m away, a fall of 117 people in ten minutes across the transect. The decline is not perfectly even: counts drop by 27 and then 25 over the first 200 m, rise slightly to 103 at Site 4, and then fall away most sharply of all between Site 4 and Site 5, a drop of 49. In shape, then, the pattern is a concentration of pedestrians at the square end of the street, a gentle decline through the pedestrianised stretch, one local peak, and a collapse to low numbers over the outer 200 m.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Describe the shape of the whole set of counts, not the individual sites one after another.","A description earns its second mark from detail taken out of the table — where the numbers are highest, lowest, and where they change fastest.","Two sentences: the overall trend with its end values, then how evenly it happens."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Describing, analysing and explaining fieldwork data"]},{"q":"Suggest one alternative technique for presenting the data in Figure 2 so that variation across the area shown in Figure 1 is displayed. [1 mark]","a":"AO4 = 1 mark. Only one alternative is required. Accept any technique that keeps the spatial dimension, eg located proportional symbols, located bars, proportional flow lines, or an isoline map of pedestrian numbers. No credit for a bar chart or pie chart alone, because neither shows where on the street the counts were taken.","sol":"Located proportional symbols: draw a circle at each of the six sites on the sketch map in Figure 1, with the area of each circle proportional to the count at that site. The technique keeps the geography that a table throws away — the reader sees at a glance that the largest circle sits at the market square end, that the circles shrink down the street, and that the circle at Site 4 is slightly larger than the one above it, next to the bus station that explains it. Located bars or proportional flow lines along the street would do the same job. An ordinary bar chart would show the same six numbers but would lose the one thing the enquiry is about, which is where the pedestrians were.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The question tells you what the technique must do: show variation across an area.","Ask what a plain bar chart of the six counts would throw away that Figure 1 supplies.","Name the technique in a phrase and say what it would be drawn on."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Selecting appropriate ways of processing and presenting fieldwork data"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, calculate the interquartile range of the pebble long-axis measurements at Site 1. Show your working. [2 marks]","a":"AO4 = 2 marks. One mark for the correct interquartile range: 36 mm (only possible answer). One mark for an indication of the calculation, eg upper quartile 70 mm, lower quartile 34 mm.","sol":"Table 4a lists eleven measurements for Site 1, already in order: 22, 28, 34, 41, 45, 52, 58, 63, 70, 76, 88. With eleven ordered values the quartiles are read at the third and the ninth positions, so the lower quartile is 34 mm and the upper quartile is 70 mm. The interquartile range is the upper quartile minus the lower quartile: 70 - 34 = 36 mm. The units matter, because the measurements are millimetres. It is worth noting what this figure does that the range does not: the full range at Site 1 is 88 - 22 = 66 mm, but the interquartile range describes only the middle half of the pebbles and so is not dragged about by the single largest or smallest stone in the sample.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The interquartile range is about the middle of a set of data, not about its two extremes.","Count how many measurements there are, then work out which positions in the ordered list mark off the first and third quarters.","Two lines of working: the two quartile values with their units, then the subtraction."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Describing, analysing and explaining fieldwork data"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, give one developed reason why the two beach locations used in Enquiry A were chosen. [2 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks. One mark for stating an advantage of the locations, second mark for developing it, eg the two sites lie on either side of a rock groyne (1) so that any difference in sediment size can be linked to the groyne interrupting southward longshore drift (2). Other creditworthy reasons include both sites lying on the same beach so conditions are otherwise comparable, or both being reachable on one falling tide. Allow one mark for an undeveloped reason.","sol":"The two sites were chosen because one lies 50 m north of the southern rock groyne and the other 500 m south of it, so the structure that the enquiry is really about sits between them. Longshore drift on the Holderness coast carries material southwards, and a groyne interrupts that movement and holds the coarser material on its updrift side; by measuring on both sides of it the student can attribute any difference in sediment size to the groyne and to the attrition that continues south of it, rather than to some unrelated change in the beach. The choice is also practical and fair: both sites are on the same stretch of beach, exposed to the same waves and worked on the same falling tide, so the conditions the enquiry is not testing are held as constant as fieldwork allows.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look at where the two sites sit in relation to the structure named in the enquiry title.","A developed reason says what the choice of site allows the student to conclude that another choice would not.","One sentence stating the advantage, one sentence saying what it makes possible."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Suitable question for geographical enquiry"]},{"q":"Explain one problem with the accuracy of the pedestrian counts described in Figure 1. [2 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks. One developed comment is required, because the stem asks for one problem: one mark for identifying a problem that relates to accuracy and a second for developing it, eg people were missed or counted twice in a busy two-way flow (1) so the recorded total is not the true number passing the site (2). A second, separate problem earns nothing, so two undeveloped problems score 1 mark. Other creditworthy problems include the ten minutes not being timed identically at every site, provided the consequence for the figure recorded is given. No credit for comments that do not relate to accuracy of the data collected, such as the time of day, the day of the week or the weather.","sol":"The counts cannot be exactly accurate because of how people were counted. Figure 1 says two students stood at each site, one counting those walking north and one those walking south, for ten minutes. At Site 1, where 148 people passed, that is roughly one person every four seconds arriving from two directions at once, so in a group it is very easy to miss somebody or to count the same person twice if they double back past the site. The recorded figure is therefore an approximation rather than a true count, and the scope for that error grows with the flow, so it is largest at exactly the sites where the counts are highest. That matters for the enquiry rather than only for the tally: if the busiest sites are the least accurately counted, the size of the fall from Site 1 to Site 6 is the part of the table least to be trusted, even though its direction is unaffected.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The question is only about accuracy — whether the number written down is the true number — not about whether the day was representative.","Picture two students at the busiest site with people passing in both directions and think about what goes wrong with the tally itself.","State the one problem in a clause, then spend the second half of the answer on what it does to the figure that ends up in the table — the development, not a second problem, earns the second mark."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Evaluation of geographical enquiry","3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2 and Figure 1, suggest one reason for the anomalous pedestrian count at Site 4. [2 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks. One mark for recognising that Site 4 breaks the downward trend, eg 103 pedestrians against 96 at the site nearer the square. Second mark for a reason drawn from Figure 1, eg the bus station opens directly onto the street beside Site 4, so people arriving on and leaving buses are counted there even though it is 300 m from the market square. Answers must be based on an interpretation of the information provided.","sol":"Every other step down the street is a fall, but the count rises from 96 at Site 3 to 103 at Site 4, so Site 4 is the anomaly. The reason is on the map rather than in the table: Figure 1 shows a bus station with six stands opening directly onto the street beside Site 4. Pedestrians there are not only people who have walked out from the market square but also passengers arriving on and leaving buses, who join the street at that point and were therefore caught by the ten-minute count. The bus station acts as a second generator of pedestrian flow part-way along the transect, which lifts the count locally without changing the overall pattern — the fall resumes immediately afterwards, and the steepest drop of all, 49 people, comes between Site 4 and Site 5.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["First be sure which value is the anomalous one, and say in what way it fails to fit.","The explanation has to come out of the map, not out of general knowledge about high streets.","One clause identifying the odd value, one naming what stands at that site and what it does to the count."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Describing, analysing and explaining fieldwork data"]},{"q":"Suggest one risk that students would face while collecting the data for Enquiry A in Figure 3, and explain how that risk could be reduced. [2 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks. One mark for a risk that genuinely arises on the beach described, eg being cut off or caught by the rising tide, slipping on wet rock armour, or being struck by material falling or slumping from the boulder-clay cliff. Second mark for a control that matches the risk, eg checking the tide tables and working only on a falling tide, keeping off the rock groyne itself, or staying well out from the cliff foot. A generic answer such as 'be careful' earns no second mark.","sol":"The clearest risk is the tide. Both sites are on an open beach and the students are working along the strandline with tapes and rulers, so a rising tide could cut them off from the access point or force them against the cliff. The control is specific: check the tide tables before the visit and work only on a falling tide, as Figure 3 says the class did, with a fixed time at which everyone leaves the beach regardless of how much data is left to collect. A second, equally real risk is the cliff itself. Holderness cliffs are boulder clay, which slumps when it is wet, so material can come down without warning; the control is to lay the tapes well out from the cliff foot and never to shelter under it.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about where the students are physically standing for the whole afternoon, and what is above them and beside them.","A risk is only worth writing if you can name the thing that would make it less likely, rather than telling people to take care.","One clause for the hazard, one for the control that answers it directly."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Suitable question for geographical enquiry"]},{"q":"Justify one of the data collection methods used in Enquiry A in Figure 3. [3 marks]","a":"AO3 = 3 marks. Identifying the method is not itself credited, because naming it involves no justification. Credit justification: linking the method to the concept or theory being tested (sediment size linked to longshore drift and attrition); explaining how the sampling strategy protects the quality or comparability of the data; explaining why the particular measurement or criteria chosen were appropriate. One detailed justification can earn maximum marks; one developed point with one brief point can also earn maximum marks; three brief statements are creditworthy provided each carries an element of justification.","sol":"Taking the pebble lying at every 1 m mark along a 10 m tape, without looking, is justified on three grounds. First, it tests the theory directly: the enquiry rests on longshore drift carrying material south and attrition reducing its size, and the long axis of a pebble in millimetres is the simplest measure of that reduction, so the method produces exactly the variable the concept predicts will change. Second, the sampling strategy protects the data. Choosing without looking removes the temptation to pick pebbles that look typical or interesting, which would build the student's expectations into the results; and because the same 10 m tape and the same 1 m interval were used at both sites, the two samples are directly comparable rather than being collected in two different ways. Third, it is honest about what one tide allows: eleven pebbles per site is a small sample, but it can be completed identically at both sites on a single falling tide, so the comparison between sites is not contaminated by different weather, wave conditions or tidal states.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Naming the method is free; the marks are all in the reasons, so plan the reasons before you write the method down.","There are three different kinds of reason available: the theory the method tests, the sampling strategy behind it, and the comparability of the two sites.","Write in 'because' clauses, one for each reason, each finishing with what it does for the quality of the data."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry"]},{"q":"Using Figure 5 and your own understanding, describe and interpret the spread of the pebble long-axis measurements shown for the two sites. [4 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks. Levels 1-2. The answer must apply an understanding of a dispersion graph and must use the data plotted on it. Credit reference to the range, the median or other measure of central tendency, the quartiles and interquartile range, the degree of clustering around the middle, and to what the difference between the sites means. Level 2 (Clear, 3-4): clear application of knowledge and understanding of dispersion graphs in describing and interpreting the spread of pebble sizes, with clear reference to the data shown on the dispersion graph. Level 1 (Basic, 1-2): limited application, with basic use of the data shown on the dispersion graph.","sol":"On Figure 5 the Site 1 crosses are spread up almost the whole scale, from 22 mm to 88 mm, a range of 66 mm; the sixth cross up gives a median of 52 mm, and the third and the ninth give an interquartile range of 36 mm. The Site 2 crosses are bunched into the lower third of the same scale, from 12 mm to 51 mm, a range of 39 mm, with a median of 27 mm and an interquartile range of 20 mm; nine of the eleven lie at or below 38 mm. Both the typical size and the spread are therefore much smaller south of the groyne: the median falls by 25 mm, and the middle half of the pebbles occupies a band a little over half as wide. Interpreting that, the southern site has sediment that is not only finer but more uniform, which is what would be expected if material carried south by longshore drift has been worn down by attrition on the way, while the coarsest material has been trapped on the northern side of the groyne. The two columns barely overlap in their middle halves, so the difference is a clear one rather than a matter of a few unusual stones.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Spread is a separate idea from average: two sites can differ in the typical pebble, in how varied the pebbles are, or in both.","Read a number for the spread out of each column before you write anything — a dispersion graph gives you the highest and lowest crosses directly, and with eleven crosses the median is the sixth from the bottom and the quartiles the third and the ninth.","Describe both sites in the same terms, then spend the second half of the answer saying what the difference means about the coast."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Describing, analysing and explaining fieldwork data"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, analyse how far the pedestrian counts support the hypothesis that pedestrian numbers fall with distance from the market square. [4 marks]","a":"AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks. Levels 1-2. Responses should analyse the table, find the connections in it and use the evidence to support a view on how far the hypothesis holds. Credit the overall downward trend quantified, the site that breaks it and its explanation, and the uneven rate of decline. Level 2 (Clear, 3-4): clear application of knowledge and understanding in analysing how far the counts support the hypothesis, with clear reference to the data shown in the table. Level 1 (Basic, 1-2): limited application, with basic use of the data shown. Max Level 1 for description of the table with no analysis.","sol":"The data support the hypothesis in direction. Counts fall from 148 at Site 1, beside the market square, to 31 at Site 6, 500 m away — a fall of 117, or about four fifths of the opening count — and every site except Site 4 records fewer pedestrians than the site before it. The support is not uniform, however. The decline is gentle across the pedestrianised stretch, 27 people over the first 100 m and 25 over the second, and then collapses over the outer half of the street, the largest single step being the drop of 49 between Site 4 and Site 5. That suggests a step between two different kinds of street rather than a smooth decay with distance. At one point the hypothesis fails outright: the count rises from 96 to 103 at Site 4. Since the rest of the pattern is so consistent, this is best treated as an anomaly produced by something at that site rather than as evidence against the trend. Overall the counts support the hypothesis as a general statement, but distance is standing in for the real cause, which is the change in land use and access along the street.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["'How far' means the answer cannot simply be yes: the marks come from where the data fit and where they do not.","Quantify the trend first, then look for the places where the table behaves differently from what the hypothesis would predict.","Three moves: the supporting evidence with figures, the evidence that qualifies it, then a short verdict on how far the hypothesis holds."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Describing, analysing and explaining fieldwork data","3.3.2 — Reaching conclusions"]},{"q":"Assess the effectiveness of the presentation technique(s) you used in your human geography enquiry. [6 marks] (In the exam you write about your own enquiry; answer here on Enquiry B in Figure 3 and Table 4b.)","a":"AO3 = 6 marks. Levels 1-3. The command word is 'assess', so an informed overall judgement on the effectiveness of the named technique(s) is required, reached by examining their constituent parts — axes, scales, categories, proportionality, what the plot allows a reader to see. Evaluative comments leading to that judgement are credited at Levels 1 and 2. Level 3 (Detailed, 5-6): a developed and balanced appreciation of the effectiveness of the components making up the technique, and a substantiated overall assessment. Level 1: evaluation generic or incomplete, with the technique possibly not named.","sol":"The environmental quality scores from Enquiry B were presented in two ways: a compound bar for each of the four sites, divided into the five survey categories, and a line graph of the total score against distance east of Broadmead. The compound bars work well in one respect and badly in another. Because every bar is built from the same five categories on the same scale from -2 to +2, the reader can see which component drives the change: buildings and litter swing from positive at Site A to negative at Site D, while greenery hardly moves. But the scale crosses zero, so negative categories are drawn below the axis and the bar splits in two, which makes the total hard to read. The line graph handles the total far better. With distance on a continuous horizontal axis at 500 m intervals and total score on a vertical axis from +6 to -4, it shows a near-straight decline of ten points over 1.5 km, and the even spacing of the sites means the gradient can be trusted. Its weakness is that it hides which category is responsible, and with only four plotted points its straightness may be an illusion. Overall the pair is effective: the line graph carries the finding and the bars explain it, and neither would have been sufficient alone. The compound bar is the weaker component and would have been better replaced by a radar chart, which handles bipolar categories without crossing an axis.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'Assess' means taking the technique apart before judging it: axes, scale, categories, what a reader can and cannot see at a glance.","Bipolar data that run from negative to positive create a particular difficulty for some graph types — work out which part of your graph that difficulty lands on.","Judge each component in turn, then finish with one overall verdict that says which component was weakest and what would replace it."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Selecting appropriate ways of processing and presenting fieldwork data"]},{"q":"Evaluate the extent to which the results of your physical geography enquiry allowed you to reach a reliable conclusion. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks] (In the exam you write about your own enquiry; answer here on Enquiry A in Figure 3 and Table 4a.)","a":"AO3 = 9 marks. Levels 1-3, plus 3 marks for SPaG. Answers must evaluate the results themselves (accuracy, sample size, sampling strategy, and the variables that affected the fieldwork), evaluate the contribution those results made to the conclusion, and then give a judgement linking the results to the reliability of the conclusion. Level 3 (Detailed, 7-9): detailed evaluation of results; detailed evaluation of their contribution to the conclusion; an informed judgement as to the extent to which the results contributed to reaching a reliable conclusion. Level 1: basic evaluation with a weak and generic judgement.","sol":"Enquiry A asked whether beach sediment south of the rock groynes at Mappleton is smaller than sediment north of them, and concluded that it is: the median long axis in Table 4a falls from 52 mm at the site 50 m north of the groyne to 27 mm at the site 500 m south of it, and the interquartile range falls from 36 mm to 20 mm, so the southern material is both finer and more uniform.\nThe results themselves are of mixed quality. The measurement is sound in principle — a ruler reading to the nearest millimetre is precise enough for pebbles of this size — but the long axis of an irregular stone is partly a judgement about how it is held, so two students would not always record the same figure. The sampling strategy is a genuine strength: taking the pebble at every metre along a 10 m tape without looking removed the temptation to choose pebbles that looked typical, and using the identical tape and interval at both sites meant like was compared with like. Sample size is the clear weakness. Eleven pebbles at each of two sites is a very thin sample of a beach that stretches for kilometres, and one falling tide on one day in June cannot represent a shoreline that is reworked by every storm; the material lying at the strandline in June may have arrived in a single event.\nThose results nevertheless carried the conclusion a long way. The gap between the sites is large — the medians differ by 25 mm and the middle halves of the two samples barely overlap — so it is far too big to be explained by a millimetre or two of measurement error, and the direction of the finding is secure. What the results could not do is explain the finding. Two sites cannot show whether size falls gradually southwards, as attrition during longshore drift would predict, or drops in one step at the groyne, which is what a structure trapping the coarsest material would produce; both processes predict the same pair of numbers.\nMy judgement is therefore that the results supported a reliable conclusion about direction but not about cause or magnitude. Had I sampled at 100 m intervals over a kilometre, repeated the transect on a second tide and in winter, and recorded pebble roundness alongside size, the same data collection would have distinguished attrition from the effect of the groyne. As it stands the conclusion should be stated narrowly: sediment is smaller south of the defences on this date, for reasons the enquiry identified but did not separate.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["The question is not asking what went wrong; it is asking what your results were worth, which is a different and more demanding thing.","Work through the results in the examiner's order — how good were they, what did they contribute, how far can the conclusion be trusted — and give each move real space.","Aim at a judgement that separates the part of your conclusion that is safe from the part that is not, and say what extra data would have secured the second part."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Evaluation of geographical enquiry","3.3.2 — Reaching conclusions"]},{"q":"To what extent did the results of your human geography enquiry contribute to the conclusion you reached? [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks] (In the exam you write about your own enquiry; answer here on Enquiry B in Figure 3 and Table 4b.)","a":"AO3 = 9 marks. Levels 1-3, plus 3 marks for SPaG. Credit an evaluation of the results (subjectivity of a bipolar survey, single observer, sample size, the day and time, the other data sets collected), an evaluation of how far those results carried the conclusion reached, and an informed judgement on the extent of that contribution and on the reliability of the conclusion. Level 3 (Detailed, 7-9): detailed evaluation of results and of their contribution, with an informed judgement. Level 1: basic evaluation and a weak, generic judgement.","sol":"Enquiry B asked how environmental quality changes along a 1.5 km transect east from Broadmead in central Bristol, and concluded that it declines steadily with distance from the regenerated centre: the total bipolar score in Table 4b falls from +6 at Site A to -4 at Site D, a ten-point fall across four sites, dropping by four points and then by three at each of the last two steps.\nThe results have one large weakness and several strengths. The weakness is that a bipolar environmental quality survey measures a judgement, not a quantity. Deciding that litter deserves +1 rather than 0 is an opinion, and although a single observer at all four sites at least kept the standard consistent, one person's assumptions about what an inner-city street should look like are built into every figure. Four sites is a small sample, and all were scored on one morning, so a street that happened to have been swept that day scored well for reasons that have nothing to do with its normal condition. Against that, the survey used the same five criteria in the same order at every site, which makes the sites comparable even if the absolute scores are not objective, and the land-use mapping and the ten short interviews at each site gave two independent lines of evidence to set beside the scores.\nThe contribution of the results to the conclusion was uneven. The total scores carried the conclusion almost entirely, and carried it well: the fall is large, consistent and in one direction, with no site breaking the sequence. The category breakdown contributed more than the totals to understanding why: litter and building condition each fall three points from Site A to Site D, pedestrian safety two, and noise and greenery one each. Noise is negative at all four sites, but it too slips a point, so it accounts for one of the ten, not none. The interviews contributed least: ten answers per site is too few to be more than illustrative, and they did not enter the conclusion.\nTo a considerable extent, then, the results did contribute to the conclusion, but they support a narrower conclusion than the one I wrote. What the data show reliably is that my assessment of environmental quality fell along this transect on this morning, driven chiefly by building condition and litter. What they cannot show is that environmental quality itself falls with distance from the centre in Bristol generally, because that would need several transects in different directions, more than one observer scoring independently, repeat visits on different days, and secondary data such as Index of Multiple Deprivation scores to test the subjective scores against an external measure.","m":9,"d":3,"hints":["'To what extent' asks how far the results carried the conclusion, so some results will have contributed more than others — sort them before you write.","A survey based on your own scoring has a particular kind of weakness; name it precisely rather than calling the enquiry biased.","End by rewriting your own conclusion in the narrower form the evidence actually supports, and say what would be needed to justify the wider one."],"codes":["3.3.2 — Evaluation of geographical enquiry","3.3.2 — Reaching conclusions"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many geographical enquiries must you have carried out, and what must each one include?","a":"Two, in contrasting environments; each must include primary data you collected yourself during fieldwork outside the classroom and school grounds."},{"q":"For these eleven ordered pebble measurements in millimetres — 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 31, 35, 38, 44, 51 — what is the interquartile range?","a":"20 mm: the upper quartile is the ninth value, 38 mm, and the lower quartile is the third value, 18 mm."},{"q":"What is an anomaly in fieldwork data, and what should be done with one?","a":"A value that does not fit the pattern of the rest. It should be identified and explained from the conditions at that site, not deleted."},{"q":"Name the three things a fieldwork evaluation must do, in order.","a":"Evaluate the results; evaluate the contribution the results made to the conclusion; judge how far the conclusion is therefore reliable."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Primary data","back":"Data you collect yourself for your own enquiry: pebbles measured, pedestrians counted, questionnaire answers recorded. It fits your question exactly, but one day limits the sample size.","code":"3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry","open":false},{"front":"Secondary data","back":"Data somebody else collected, usually for another purpose: census figures, Ordnance Survey maps, Environment Agency records, Index of Multiple Deprivation scores. Large and long-run, but never shaped to your exact question.","code":"3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry","open":false},{"front":"Systematic sampling","back":"Taking data at a regular interval: every metre along a tape, every tenth person, every third building. Quick, evenly spread and repeatable, but can fall into step with a repeating pattern on the ground.","code":"3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry","open":false},{"front":"Random sampling","back":"Every item in the area or population has an equal chance of being selected, usually by random numbers. It removes the collector's bias entirely, but is slow and can leave parts of the site unsampled.","code":"3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry","open":false},{"front":"Stratified sampling","back":"Dividing the area or population into groups and sampling each in proportion to its size. The only strategy that guarantees a small but important zone is represented; it needs the groups identified first.","code":"3.3.2 — Selecting, measuring and recording data appropriate to the chosen enquiry","open":false},{"front":"Risk assessment in fieldwork","back":"The hazards of the site listed before going, each paired with a named control: tides met by working a falling tide; unstable boulder-clay cliffs met by keeping out from the cliff foot; traffic met by counting from the pavement.","code":"3.3.2 — Suitable question for geographical enquiry","open":false},{"front":"The three tests of a workable enquiry question","back":"It names variables that can be measured; it rests on a geographical theory or concept you can state beforehand; and it fits a place and a scale you can reach and work in safely.","code":"3.3.2 — Suitable question for geographical enquiry","open":false},{"front":"Contrasting environments: the fieldwork requirement","back":"The two enquiries must be in contrasting environments, between them covering physical and human geography, each using primary data collected outside the classroom and school grounds; at least one must show the interaction between physical and human geography.","code":"3.3.2 — Students need to undertake two geographical enquiries, each of which must include the use of primary data","open":false},{"front":"The six strands of a geographical enquiry, in order","back":"A suitable question for enquiry; selecting, measuring and recording data; processing and presenting data; describing, analysing and explaining data; reaching conclusions; evaluating the enquiry.","code":"3.3.2 — Students’ understanding of the enquiry process will be assessed in the following two ways","open":false},{"front":"Anomaly","back":"A value that does not fit the pattern of the rest of the data, such as a pedestrian count that rises where every other count falls. Explain it from the conditions at that site rather than removing it.","code":"3.3.2 — Describing, analysing and explaining fieldwork data","open":false},{"front":"Interquartile range","back":"The upper quartile minus the lower quartile: the spread of the middle half of the data. In an ordered list of eleven values the quartiles are the third and the ninth. Unlike the range, it ignores extremes.","code":"3.3.2 — Describing, analysing and explaining fieldwork data","open":false},{"front":"Located proportional symbols","back":"Circles or squares drawn at the place each reading was taken, sized in proportion to the value. Keeps the geography a bar chart throws away, so distribution and magnitude are read together.","code":"3.3.2 — Selecting appropriate ways of processing and presenting fieldwork data","open":false},{"front":"Scatter graph","back":"Two measured variables plotted against each other, one per axis, with a line of best fit. It shows whether a relationship exists, its direction and its strength, and it makes anomalies stand out.","code":"3.3.2 — Selecting appropriate ways of processing and presenting fieldwork data","open":false},{"front":"An evidenced conclusion","back":"A direct answer to the title of the enquiry, drawn from the results and quoting them, saying which part of the hypothesis holds and which does not. It is not a summary of what you did.","code":"3.3.2 — Reaching conclusions","open":false},{"front":"The three moves of a fieldwork evaluation","back":"Evaluate the results (accuracy, sample size, sampling strategy, variables that interfered); evaluate what those results contributed to the conclusion; judge how far the conclusion is therefore reliable.","code":"3.3.2 — Evaluation of geographical enquiry","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 (written for this room) — A sketch map of an unfamiliar fieldwork site, described in words. The town is not named and the site is illustrative; nothing in it is offered as a real place.\nThe map shows the high street of an English market town. The street runs roughly north to south for 500 m. At the north end is the market square, and Site 1 stands at its southern corner. Sites 2 to 6 are marked at 100 m intervals down the street: Site 2 at 100 m, Site 3 at 200 m, Site 4 at 300 m, Site 5 at 400 m and Site 6 at 500 m from the square. The stretch from Site 1 to Site 3 is pedestrianised and lined with chain shops and cafes. A bus station with six stands opens directly onto the street beside Site 4. Beyond Site 4 the street is open to traffic; the buildings are two-storey terraces, three of the seven shop units are vacant, and a supermarket car park lies behind Site 5. A note on the map records the method: students counted pedestrians at each site for ten minutes, working from Site 1 down to Site 6 one site after another on a Tuesday morning in June, with two students at each site, one counting people walking north and one counting people walking south.","Figure 2 (written for this room) — Pedestrian counts on the high street shown in Figure 1: a data table. Figures illustrative.\nSite | Distance from the market square (m) | Pedestrians counted in 10 minutes\nSite 1 | 0 | 148\nSite 2 | 100 | 121\nSite 3 | 200 | 96\nSite 4 | 300 | 103\nSite 5 | 400 | 54\nSite 6 | 500 | 31","Figure 3 (written for this room) — The two individual enquiries this room answers on, written as a real GCSE pair must be: contrasting environments, one physical and one human, with the physical enquiry also examining a human structure acting on a physical process. The coastal setting, the cliff material, the direction of drift and the date of the Mappleton defences are real; the class, the sites and all of the data are illustrative.\nEnquiry A (physical). Title: 'Is beach sediment smaller south of the rock groynes at Mappleton than north of them, on the Holderness coast?'\nTheory behind it: the Holderness cliffs are boulder clay left by ice, and the coast is one of the fastest eroding in Europe; longshore drift carries eroded material southwards towards Spurn Point, and attrition rounds and reduces it as it travels. Two rock groynes and a rock revetment were built at Mappleton in 1991 to protect the village and the coast road, and a groyne interrupts drift and holds coarser material on its updrift side.\nLocations: Site 1 on the beach 50 m north of the southern groyne; Site 2 on the beach 500 m south of it.\nMethod: at each site a 10 m tape was laid along the strandline and the pebble lying at every 1 m mark was picked up without looking (systematic sampling, eleven pebbles per site) and its long axis measured in millimetres with a ruler. Both sites were worked on the same falling tide on the same day in June.\nSecondary data: Ordnance Survey maps and Environment Agency records showing the position of the cliff line in different years.\nEnquiry B (human). Title: 'How does environmental quality change along a 1.5 km transect east from Broadmead in central Bristol?'\nTheory behind it: land use and environmental quality often change with distance from a city centre, because the centre attracts investment and regeneration while inner areas carry older housing, heavier through-traffic and less open space.\nLocations: four sites on a straight transect east from the Broadmead shopping area — Site A at 0 m, Site B at 500 m, Site C at 1000 m, Site D at 1500 m.\nMethod: at each site the same student scored five categories on a bipolar environmental quality survey from -2 (worst) to +2 (best): litter and cleanliness, noise, greenery and trees, condition of buildings, and safety for pedestrians. The use of every building within 100 m either side of the site was mapped, and ten pedestrians at each site were asked one question about the street as a place to live.\nSecondary data: the Index of Multiple Deprivation scores published for the neighbourhoods the transect crosses.","Figure 4 (written for this room) — The results of the two enquiries in Figure 3. All figures are illustrative, but every countable claim the questions make about them is true of the numbers printed here.\nTable 4a — Enquiry A: pebble long axis in millimetres, eleven pebbles per site, listed in order of size.\nSite 1, 50 m north of the groyne | 22 | 28 | 34 | 41 | 45 | 52 | 58 | 63 | 70 | 76 | 88\nSite 2, 500 m south of the groyne | 12 | 15 | 18 | 21 | 24 | 27 | 31 | 35 | 38 | 44 | 51\nTable 4b — Enquiry B: environmental quality survey scores, each category scored from -2 to +2.\nSite | Distance east of Broadmead (m) | Litter | Noise | Greenery | Buildings | Pedestrian safety | Total\nA | 0 | +2 | -1 | +1 | +2 | +2 | +6\nB | 500 | +1 | -1 | 0 | +1 | +1 | +2\nC | 1000 | 0 | -2 | +1 | -1 | +1 | -1\nD | 1500 | -1 | -2 | 0 | -1 | 0 | -4","Figure 5 (written for this room) — A dispersion graph of the Enquiry A pebble measurements, described by its axes and its plotted points. It plots the same twenty-two measurements printed as Table 4a; figures illustrative.\nThe vertical axis is pebble long axis in millimetres, running from 0 to 90 mm and labelled every 10 mm. The horizontal axis carries no scale, only two columns side by side: the left column is Site 1 (50 m north of the southern groyne) and the right column is Site 2 (500 m south of it). Every pebble measured is plotted as one cross at its own height in its site's column, so each column holds eleven crosses.\nSite 1 column — crosses at 22, 28, 34, 41, 45, 52, 58, 63, 70, 76 and 88 mm.\nSite 2 column — crosses at 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27, 31, 35, 38, 44 and 51 mm.\nRead off the plot: the Site 1 crosses are spread up almost the whole scale, the highest at 88 mm and only one of them below 25 mm; the Site 2 crosses are bunched in the lower half, nine of the eleven at or below 38 mm and none above 51 mm."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Paper 3 Section B, the 9-mark fieldwork evaluation on the physical enquiry, 9 marks + 3 SPaG)","items":["Question: Evaluate the extent to which the results of your physical geography enquiry allowed you to reach a reliable conclusion. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks] — answered here on Enquiry A in Figure 3 and Table 4a.","Point: the results were strong enough to establish the direction of the finding but not its cause, so the conclusion is reliable only in a narrow form.","Named evidence: in Table 4a the median long axis falls from 52 mm at the site 50 m north of the southern groyne at Mappleton to 27 mm 500 m south of it, and the interquartile range falls from 36 mm to 20 mm, from a sample of eleven pebbles per site taken at 1 m intervals along a 10 m tape on one falling tide in June.","Developed explanation — evaluating the results: the ruler reads to the nearest millimetre, but the long axis of an irregular pebble is partly a judgement about how the stone is held, so the individual readings carry a small error; the sampling strategy is a real strength, because choosing without looking removed the temptation to pick pebbles that looked typical and the identical tape and interval at both sites made the two samples directly comparable; the weakness is sample size, since eleven pebbles at two sites on one tide cannot represent a beach that is reworked by every storm.","Developed explanation — evaluating the contribution to the conclusion: the difference between the sites is far larger than the measurement error, the medians being 25 mm apart with the middle halves of the two samples barely overlapping, so the results carried the direction of the conclusion securely; what they could not do is explain it, because two sites cannot distinguish a gradual reduction by attrition during southward drift from a single step caused by the groyne holding back the coarsest material, and both processes predict exactly this pair of numbers.","Link to the question: the extent to which the results allowed a reliable conclusion therefore depends on which part of the conclusion is being tested — the observation is secure, the explanation is not.","Mini-judgement: the conclusion should be stated narrowly, that sediment was smaller south of the defences on this date; sampling every 100 m over a kilometre, repeating on a second tide and in winter, and recording roundness alongside size would have made the wider claim defensible.","Why this is Level 3 (Detailed, 7-9) on the 9-mark grid: it provides a detailed evaluation of the results themselves, evaluates in detail the contribution those results made to the conclusion, and gives an informed judgement as to the extent to which they allowed a reliable conclusion — the three AO3 strands the grid names. Its spelling and punctuation are consistently accurate and it uses a wide range of specialist terms — longshore drift, attrition, groyne, updrift, median, interquartile range, systematic sampling — which is High performance for the 3 SPaG marks."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (the same 9-mark question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: the same — Evaluate the extent to which the results of your physical geography enquiry allowed you to reach a reliable conclusion. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","The answer as a weak candidate writes it: 'We went to the beach at Mappleton and measured pebbles with a ruler. We found that the pebbles were smaller further south, which is what we thought would happen. There were some problems with our fieldwork. We did not measure very many pebbles and it was quite windy, and some people in the group did not measure properly so our results might not be right. If we did it again we would measure more pebbles and be more careful. Overall our conclusion was quite reliable because we got the answer we expected.'","The descriptor it meets — Level 1 (Basic, 1-3): 'Provides a basic evaluation of results' is satisfied, just. The answer does name two genuine limitations, the small sample and inconsistent measuring, so there is something evaluative on the page.","The descriptor it misses — the second AO3 strand: it never evaluates the contribution the results made to the conclusion. It says the pebbles were smaller but gives no figure, so it cannot argue that the difference is bigger than the error, and it never asks which of the results carried the conclusion and which added nothing.","The descriptor it misses — the third AO3 strand: the grid says that at Level 1 'any judgement as to the extent to which the results contributed to reaching a reliable conclusion will be weak and generic', which is exactly what the last sentence is. Worse, it treats getting the expected answer as evidence of reliability, which reverses the reasoning the question is testing.","What would lift it two levels: put the medians and the interquartile ranges from Table 4a into the second sentence; tie each limitation to what it does to the conclusion rather than listing it; drop the weather, which affected comfort rather than accuracy; and end by separating the part of the conclusion the data can support from the part they cannot.","Its SPaG: the spelling and punctuation are reasonable and the grammar is under control, but almost no specialist terms appear — no drift, no attrition, no median, no sampling strategy — which is Threshold rather than High performance."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (the 6-mark 'Assess the effectiveness of the presentation technique(s) you used in your human geography enquiry')","items":["Question: Assess the effectiveness of the presentation technique(s) you used in your human geography enquiry. [6 marks] — answered here on Enquiry B in Figure 3 and Table 4b.","Point: two techniques were used, a compound bar for each site and a line graph of the totals, and they are effective together because each covers the other's blind spot.","Named evidence: the compound bars divide each site's score into the five survey categories on a common scale from -2 to +2; the line graph plots the total score against distance east of Broadmead at 0 m, 500 m, 1000 m and 1500 m, falling from +6 to -4.","Component-by-component evaluation, which is what 'assess' demands: the bars' shared categories and shared scale let a reader see which component drives the change — buildings and litter swing from positive to negative while greenery barely moves — but because the scale crosses zero the bar has to be drawn in both directions from the axis, which makes the total hard to read off; the line graph's continuous, evenly spaced distance axis means its gradient can be trusted and its steady ten-point decline is visible at a glance, but it hides the categories entirely and rests on only four plotted points.","Link to the question: effectiveness here is not a property of either graph alone but of what the pair allows a reader to do, which is to see the trend and then see what causes it.","Substantiated overall assessment: the pair is effective, with the line graph carrying the finding and the bars explaining it; the compound bar is the weaker component because bipolar data crossing an axis do not suit it, and a radar chart of the five categories would present the same information without splitting the plot.","Why this is Level 3 (Detailed, 5-6) on the 6-mark grid: it shows a developed and balanced appreciation of the effectiveness of the components making up the named techniques — axes, scale, categories, number of plots — and finishes with a substantiated overall assessment that names the weakest component and its replacement, which is exactly what the top level requires."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the enquiry process, the terms and the techniques this room is marked on","items":["Geographical enquiry: a sequence of six strands — a suitable question; selecting, measuring and recording data; processing and presenting data; describing, analysing and explaining data; reaching conclusions; evaluating the enquiry.","Hypothesis: a testable statement the enquiry sets out to support or reject, such as 'beach sediment is coarser on the north side of the groyne than on the south side', which can be answered yes, no or partly by measurement.","Primary data: collected first hand by the student for this enquiry, during fieldwork outside the classroom and school grounds.","Secondary data: already in existence, collected by somebody else and usually for another purpose — census returns, Ordnance Survey maps, Environment Agency records, Index of Multiple Deprivation scores.","Quantitative data: measured as numbers, such as pebble long axis in millimetres or a pedestrian count. Qualitative data: recorded as description or opinion, such as an interview answer or an annotated photograph.","Continuous data: can take any value on a scale, such as river velocity, and suit line graphs. Discrete data: fall into separate categories or whole counts, such as land-use types, and suit bar charts.","Random sampling: every item has an equal chance of selection, usually chosen by random numbers; removes collector bias but is slow and can leave gaps.","Systematic sampling: data taken at a fixed interval — every metre, every tenth person, every third building; even, quick and repeatable, but can coincide with a repeating pattern on the ground.","Stratified sampling: the area or population is divided into groups and each is sampled in proportion to its size; the only way to guarantee that a small but important zone is represented.","Pilot study: a small trial run of the method before the real fieldwork, used to check that the recording sheet, the criteria and the timings work.","Bipolar environmental quality survey: the same criteria scored at each site on a scale running from a negative extreme to a positive one, for example -2 to +2; comparable between sites but subjective, so one observer should score them all.","Measures of central tendency: the mean (total divided by the number of values), the median (the middle value of an ordered list) and the mode (the most frequent value).","Measures of spread: the range (largest minus smallest) and the interquartile range (upper quartile minus lower quartile), which describes the middle half and ignores extremes.","Quartiles from an ordered list of eleven values: the lower quartile is the third value, the median the sixth and the upper quartile the ninth.","Dispersion graph: every individual reading plotted as a point on one vertical scale for each site, so the spread, the clustering and the outliers are all visible at once.","Presentation techniques that keep the location: located bars, located proportional symbols, proportional flow lines, choropleth shading and isoline maps. A plain bar or pie chart loses the location entirely.","Scatter graph and line of best fit: used to test a relationship between two measured variables, showing its direction, its strength and any points that do not fit.","Anomaly: a value that breaks the pattern of the rest, to be identified and explained from the conditions at that site rather than removed.","Accuracy: how close a recorded figure is to the true value — a question about the instrument and the reading. Reliability: whether repeating the fieldwork would give the same result.","Risk assessment: the hazards of the site listed in advance, each paired with a named control — tide tables and a falling tide on a beach, working out from an unstable cliff foot, counting from the pavement on a busy street."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the two enquiries this room answers on (the specification's required pair)","items":["The requirement: two geographical enquiries, each including primary data collected as part of a fieldwork exercise, in contrasting environments, between them showing an understanding of both physical and human geography, with at least one showing the interaction between physical and human geography.","Fieldwork must take place outside the classroom and school grounds on at least two occasions, and the enquiries may be based on any part of the content of units 3.1 and 3.2.","Enquiry A, the physical enquiry: 'Is beach sediment smaller south of the rock groynes at Mappleton than north of them, on the Holderness coast?'","Holderness, East Yorkshire: a coastline of roughly 60 km from Flamborough Head in the north to Spurn Point in the south, and one of the fastest-eroding coastlines in Europe, retreating on average of the order of one to two metres a year.","The cliffs are boulder clay, or glacial till, left behind by the ice sheets of the last glaciation: soft, unconsolidated and prone to slumping when saturated, which is why the retreat is so rapid.","Longshore drift on this coast moves material southwards, and the eroded till feeds the spit at Spurn Point at the mouth of the Humber.","Mappleton: two rock groynes and a rock revetment were built in 1991 to protect the village and the coast road; a groyne interrupts longshore drift and traps coarser material on its updrift, northern side, and the scheme has been criticised for starving beaches further south of sediment.","The physical-human interaction this enquiry rests on: a hard-engineering structure built by people changes the movement of sediment produced by a physical process, which is what makes it a legitimate physical enquiry that also meets the interaction requirement.","Enquiry A method: a 10 m tape along the strandline at each site, the pebble at every 1 m mark taken without looking (systematic sampling, eleven per site), long axis measured in millimetres with a ruler, both sites worked on one falling tide in June.","Enquiry A results (illustrative, printed as Table 4a): Site 1, 50 m north of the groyne — median 52 mm, lower quartile 34 mm, upper quartile 70 mm, interquartile range 36 mm, range 22 to 88 mm. Site 2, 500 m south — median 27 mm, lower quartile 18 mm, upper quartile 38 mm, interquartile range 20 mm, range 12 to 51 mm.","Enquiry B, the human enquiry: 'How does environmental quality change along a 1.5 km transect east from Broadmead in central Bristol?' — an urban environment contrasting with the coast, as the specification requires.","Enquiry B method: the same observer scoring five bipolar categories from -2 to +2 at four sites 500 m apart (litter, noise, greenery, buildings, pedestrian safety), plus land-use mapping within 100 m of each site and ten short interviews per site.","Enquiry B results (illustrative, printed as Table 4b): total scores +6 at 0 m, +2 at 500 m, -1 at 1000 m and -4 at 1500 m, a ten-point fall across the transect; of those ten points litter accounts for three and the condition of the buildings for three, pedestrian safety for two, and noise and greenery for one each. Noise is the only category negative at all four sites, falling from -1 to -2.","Secondary data used: Ordnance Survey maps and Environment Agency cliff-line records for Enquiry A; published Index of Multiple Deprivation scores for the neighbourhoods on the Bristol transect for Enquiry B."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Paper 3 Section B: what the fieldwork questions ask, and how long to spend","items":["Fieldwork is assessed in two ways: questions on fieldwork materials from an unfamiliar context, and questions on the student's own individual enquiry work, for which the titles of both enquiries must be given.","Section B of Paper 3 is worth 39 marks of the paper's 76, and almost all of it is AO3 — applying knowledge and understanding to interpret, analyse and evaluate — with AO4 marks on the map, graph and calculation tasks.","The unfamiliar-context questions are short and resource-bound: name techniques that could be used at the site shown (AO4), complete or plot on a graph or map (AO4), describe a pattern from the resource (AO4), suggest an alternative presentation method (AO4), calculate a statistic such as the interquartile range and show the working (AO4), and explain a problem with the accuracy of the data (AO3).","The unfamiliar-context section also carries a 4-mark level-of-response question, marked at Levels 1-2 only (Basic 1-2, Clear 3-4), typically on reading and interpreting a graph such as a dispersion graph, although any resource in the section can carry it; the split is AO3 2 marks, AO4 2 marks.","On the student's own enquiry, a 2-mark question asks for a developed reason why the location was selected, and a 3-mark question asks for a justification of a data collection method — where naming the method earns nothing because naming involves no justification.","The 6-mark question on the student's own enquiry is marked at Levels 1-3 (Basic 1-2, Clear 3-4, Detailed 5-6), AO3 = 6 marks, and asks the student to assess the effectiveness of their presentation technique(s) component by component before giving one overall verdict.","The 9-mark question on the student's own enquiry is marked at Levels 1-3 (Basic 1-3, Clear 4-6, Detailed 7-9), AO3 = 9 marks, with 3 further marks for spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology.","SPaG on that question: High performance 3 marks (consistently accurate spelling and punctuation, effective control of grammar, a wide range of specialist terms), Intermediate 2, Threshold 1, and 0 where errors severely hinder meaning.","Timing: about a minute a mark. Roughly ten to eleven minutes on the 9-mark evaluation, seven on the 6-mark assessment, four or five on the 4-mark level question, and no more than a minute each on the point-marked skills questions.","Marking is best fit: examiners start at the lowest level and work up, and a response that is predominantly Level 2 with a little Level 3 material stays in Level 2 but is marked near the top of it. Indicative content is not exhaustive and other valid points are credited."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Evaluate the extent to which the results of your physical geography enquiry allowed you to reach a reliable conclusion. [9 marks] [+3 SPaG marks]","items":["Decoding the command word: 'evaluate' means judging how well something worked with the evidence on both sides, and 'the extent to which' means the answer cannot be a straight yes or no. There is no AO1 and no AO2 in this question at all — the whole nine marks are AO3 — so nothing is earned for describing what you did or for explaining coastal processes in general. Every sentence has to be doing one of three jobs.","Opening move (two sentences, no introduction): give the title of the enquiry and the conclusion you reached, with one figure from your results inside it. For Enquiry A: the title, then 'I concluded that sediment is smaller south of the groynes, the median long axis falling from 52 mm at the northern site to 27 mm at the southern one.' The examiner now knows what is being evaluated.","Paragraph 1 — evaluate the results themselves, which is the first AO3 strand. Take accuracy, sampling strategy and sample size in turn and say something true about each: the ruler reads to the nearest millimetre but the long axis of an irregular pebble is a judgement; taking the pebble at every 1 m mark without looking removed selection bias and made the two sites directly comparable; eleven pebbles per site on one falling tide in June is a very small sample of a beach reworked by every storm. Balance matters — name at least one strength, or the paragraph becomes a list of complaints.","Paragraph 2 — evaluate what those results contributed to the conclusion, which is the second AO3 strand and the one weak answers skip. Argue it with numbers: the medians are 25 mm apart and the middle halves of the two samples barely overlap, so the difference is far larger than the measurement error and the direction of the conclusion is carried securely; the interquartile ranges add a second, weaker line of support; the secondary map data contributed nothing to this particular conclusion.","Paragraph 3 — the limit of what the results could do, which sets up the judgement. Two sites cannot separate a gradual reduction by attrition during southward longshore drift from a single step caused by the groyne trapping the coarsest material, because both processes predict exactly the pair of numbers observed. This is where the answer stops being a list of problems and becomes geography.","The evaluation thread that runs through every paragraph: each time you state a limitation, say in the same sentence what it does to the conclusion. 'The sample was small' earns little; 'the sample was small, so the size of the difference is insecure even though its direction is not' earns a great deal.","Timing: about a minute a mark plus the SPaG, so roughly eleven minutes — two on the plan and the opening, three on each of the two main paragraphs, two on the judgement, and the last minute reading it back for spelling and for the specialist terms that carry the 3 SPaG marks: longshore drift, attrition, groyne, updrift, strandline, median, interquartile range, systematic sampling, anomaly, reliability.","What the conclusion must say: not 'quite reliable' but reliable in which respect and unreliable in which. Reliable in direction, because the difference is too large to be an artefact of the method; unreliable in magnitude and in cause, because two sites on one tide cannot establish either. Then name the change that would fix it — sampling every 100 m over a kilometre, a repeat on a different tide and season, and a roundness index alongside size — so that the judgement is substantiated rather than announced."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:24:16Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Every one of the eight faults flagged in the previous verdict has been fixed and each fix recounts correctly against the printed figures — the Table 4b breakdown now accounts for all ten points in three separate places, Q11 sits on the question 04.9 grid at AO3 2 / AO4 2 Levels 1-2, Q6 demands one developed problem, Enquiry A's title matches its two sites, and the new Figure 5 dispersion graph gives the AO4 half of Q10 something real to read; all that remains is the phrase 'the lower third' in Figure 5 and in Q10's solution, which is untrue of five of the eleven Site 2 crosses and should be 'the lower half' before this goes to students."}}},"3.4":{"id":"AQA-8035","topic":"3.4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Skills are not a topic you revise last: they are the marks that appear on every paper. AO4 is worth a quarter of the whole GCSE and AO3 another 35 per cent, and almost all of it is earned on a figure — a map extract, a table, a graph, a photograph. Every paper opens with short skills questions worth one or two marks each that are pure technique: a six-figure grid reference, a distance from the scale bar, a median, an interquartile range, a percentage change. They are the cheapest marks on the paper and the ones most often thrown away, because a student who has never counted the tenths inside a grid square guesses, and a guess scores nothing. The longer questions then say 'Using Figure 1…', and the mark scheme caps an answer that ignores the figure. This room teaches the skills on resources written for it, so you can practise every move on figures you can see in words.","idea":[{"h":"What the skills requirement actually is, and where it bites","t":"The specification asks you to develop and demonstrate cartographic, graphical, numerical and statistical skills throughout the course, and says plainly that they are assessed in all three written exams. Ordnance Survey maps or other map extracts may appear on any of the three papers, so you cannot decide that maps 'belong' to the physical paper. In practice the skills show up in two shapes. First, short questions that are pure technique and are point-marked: give a grid reference, measure a distance, calculate a range. Second, longer questions that begin 'Using Figure…' and are levels-marked, where the figure is the evidence and knowledge from your head is only what you use to interpret it. A top-level answer never treats the figure as decoration: it quotes a value, a place, a grid reference or a shape from the resource in almost every sentence, and it uses the technique the question names rather than talking around it.","code":"3.4 — Students are required to develop and demonstrate a range of geographical skills"},{"h":"Cartographic skills: grid references, scale, direction, gradient and cross-sections","t":"On an Ordnance Survey map the grid lines are one kilometre apart. Read eastings first, along the bottom of the map, then northings up the side — along the corridor, then up the stairs. Four figures name the square (6444 is the square whose south-west corner is easting 64, northing 44); six figures divide that square into tenths and pin a point to about 100 m (645 441). Scale converts paper to ground: at 1:25 000 one centimetre is 250 m, so 4 cm is a kilometre and every grid square is drawn 4 cm across; at 1:50 000 one centimetre is 500 m, so 2 cm is a kilometre. Straight-line distance is measured with a ruler, curved-line distance with string or the edge of a piece of paper turned at each bend. Direction is taken from the first place named to the second, using north up the map. Contours give relief: close together is steep, far apart is gentle, and a gradient is the vertical difference divided by the horizontal distance, written as 1 in n. A cross-section is that relief drawn side-on along a line, so you can see the shape of a valley. A top-level answer reads the map rather than describing it in general terms, and quotes grid references, heights and distances as its evidence.","code":"3.4.1 — Cartographic skills"},{"h":"Graphical skills: choosing the right graph, completing it and reading it","t":"You need to be able to choose a graph as well as read one. Line charts show change over time; bar charts compare separate categories; divided bars and pie charts show proportions of a whole; histograms with equal class intervals show frequency; scattergraphs show the relationship between two variables; population pyramids show age and sex structure; dispersion graphs plot every value on one scale so the spread is visible. Maps carry data too: choropleth maps shade areas by value, isoline maps join points of equal value, dot maps, proportional symbols, desire lines and flow lines all show movement or quantity in place. The exam may ask you to complete a graph or map where the axes and scale are already provided, and the mark is for accuracy — a bar drawn to the right height, an isoline that passes on the correct side of every plotted number, a point plotted at exactly the right value. When reading a graph, give the overall trend first, then support it with figures, then name any anomaly. A top-level answer describes the pattern and quotes numbers from the axes rather than listing the plotted values one after another.","code":"3.4.2 — Graphical skills"},{"h":"Numerical skills: number, scale, sampling and drawing conclusions from figures","t":"Numerical skill starts with understanding what a number stands for: the units, the area it covers, and how one unit relates to another — centimetres to kilometres on a map scale, thousands of visitors on a graph axis, people per square kilometre on a density map. It includes designing a data collection sheet and collecting data with an eye on accuracy, sample size, procedure, control groups and reliability: a sample of ten pebbles chosen because they caught your eye is biased towards large ones, while a systematic sample taken at a fixed interval, or a random sample chosen with random numbers, can be defended. It also includes proportion and ratio, and magnitude and frequency — big events happen rarely, small ones often. Finally it asks you to draw an informed conclusion from figures rather than from an impression. A top-level answer states what the numbers show, says how confident that makes it, and names the weakness in the data that limits the conclusion.","code":"3.4.3 — Numerical skills"},{"h":"Statistical skills: central tendency, spread, percentage change and the trend line","t":"Three averages measure the centre of a set of figures. The mean is the total divided by the number of values, and is pulled about by extremes. The median is the middle value of the ranked list — for n values it is the (n + 1) ÷ 2 th — and ignores extremes. The mode is the value that occurs most often. Two measures describe the spread: the range, largest minus smallest, which one freak value can wreck, and the interquartile range, upper quartile minus lower quartile, which covers the middle half of the data and is therefore much steadier. For a ranked set of n values, the lower quartile is the (n + 1) ÷ 4 th value and the upper quartile the 3(n + 1) ÷ 4 th. Percentage change is (new value − original value) ÷ original value × 100, and a negative answer is a decrease. On a scattergraph you sketch a line of best fit through the middle of the points, read values inside the data by interpolating and beyond it by extrapolating, and say how strong the relationship looks. The last statistical skill is the most valuable: spotting when a statistic has been chosen to flatter, such as a mean quoted without its spread. A top-level answer names the measure it uses, quotes the figure it produces, and says what that measure hides.","code":"3.4.4 — Statistical skills"},{"h":"Using qualitative and quantitative data from primary and secondary sources","t":"Geographers work with two kinds of evidence. Quantitative data is counted or measured — pebble lengths, pedestrian counts, census figures — and can be averaged, graphed and compared; qualitative data is described rather than measured — a photograph, an interview, a field sketch, a written account — and captures how a place looks and feels. Primary data is what you collect yourself in the field; secondary data comes from somebody else, such as a census, a published map, satellite imagery or a layer in a geographical information system. Each kind answers a question the other cannot: a table of counts tells you how many cars used the car park, a photograph tells you that the verge beside it has been churned to mud. The strongest work triangulates, using several sources that point the same way. A top-level answer chooses the source that fits the question, says what it shows, and then says honestly what that source cannot show.","code":"3.4.5 — Use of qualitative and quantitative data"},{"h":"Formulating an enquiry and building an argument","t":"An enquiry is a sequence, not a heap of results. It begins with a question or hypothesis that is answerable with the data you can actually collect, grounded in a geographical idea — pebbles become rounder downstream because of attrition, land values fall away from the city centre. Then you select, measure and record the data; choose how to process and present it; describe, analyse and explain what it shows, including the anomalies; reach an evidenced conclusion in relation to the original question; and evaluate the whole thing. Writing it up is an argument: each paragraph makes a point, supports it with named evidence, explains why the evidence means what you say it means, and reaches a small judgement, and the conclusion answers the question rather than repeating it. A top-level answer keeps the question in view in every paragraph and finishes with a conclusion that is qualified — it says how far the evidence supports the claim, not simply that it does.","code":"3.4.6 — Formulate enquiry and argument"},{"h":"Literacy: writing for the reader and using the specialist words","t":"Almost everything you are marked on is written, so the words carry the marks. Two habits matter. The first is specialist terminology: say attrition rather than 'the stones bash together', say interquartile range rather than 'the middle bit', say interlocking spurs, choropleth, gradient, anomaly. The examiner is looking for a wide range of specialist terms used correctly, and an everyday paraphrase of a technical idea is worth less than the term itself. The second is control: clear sentences, accurate spelling, punctuation that separates one idea from the next, and paragraphs that each do one job. You should also be able to change register for the audience a question names — a report for a council, an explanation for a resident. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology are assessed directly on the indicated extended questions, so a strong argument written carelessly loses marks that have nothing to do with geography. A top-level answer reads as though it was planned: a first sentence that answers the question, developed paragraphs, and a conclusion that does not contradict them.","code":"3.4.7 — Literacy"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading a grid reference off Figure 1a","setup":"Figure 1a is an Ordnance Survey map extract at a scale of 1:25 000, with grid lines drawn every kilometre. The youth hostel symbol stands inside the square whose western grid line is the easting numbered 64 and whose southern grid line is the northing numbered 44. Inside that square the hostel lies halfway across from west to east, and one tenth of the way up from south to north. Give the four-figure reference of the square, and the six-figure reference of the hostel itself.","steps":["Take the eastings first, because a reference always runs along the corridor before it goes up the stairs: the western line is 64, so any reference in this square opens 64.","Now add the northings: the southern line is 44, so the four-figure reference of the whole square is 6444, and it names one square kilometre of ground.","Split the square into ten equal strips from west to east. Halfway across is five strips, so the easting gains a third figure and becomes 645.","Do the same from south to north. One tenth up is one strip, so the northing becomes 441 and the full six-figure reference is 645 441, fixing the hostel to about 100 m."],"cues":["Which pair of grid lines do you read before the other?","Which four figures name the square itself?","What do you divide the square into to get a third figure?","What does 'one tenth of the way up' do to the northing?"]},{"title":"Straight-line and curved-line distance from the scale","setup":"Figure 1a is drawn at 1:25 000. A student measures the footpath between Coldbeck Youth Hostel and the triangulation pillar on Craig Wen by laying a piece of string along every bend of the path: the string measures 6.0 cm. She then measures the straight line joining the same two points with a ruler: 4.0 cm. Work out both distances on the ground in kilometres, and explain why the two answers are different.","steps":["Turn the scale into something usable: 1:25 000 means one centimetre of paper stands for 25 000 cm of ground, and 25 000 cm is 250 m, so every centimetre on this map is a quarter of a kilometre.","Curved-line distance first: 6.0 cm of string × 0.25 km = 1.5 km, so walking the footpath covers 1.5 km.","Straight-line distance next: 4.0 cm × 0.25 km = 1.0 km, the distance as the crow flies.","The path is longer because it zigzags across the steep face below the summit rather than climbing straight up it, so the walk is half a kilometre further than the direct line."],"cues":["How many metres does one centimetre stand for here?","Which measurement belongs to the path itself?","Which measurement belongs to the ruler line?","Why would a path avoid the direct route up a crag?"]},{"title":"Calculating a gradient and writing it as 1 in n","setup":"On Figure 1a, Coldbeck Youth Hostel at grid reference 645 441 stands at 200 m above sea level, and the triangulation pillar on Craig Wen at grid reference 645 451 is marked 400 m. The two points lie on the same easting and the straight-line distance between them is 1.0 km. Calculate the average gradient of the slope between the hostel and the pillar, and express your answer in the form 1 in n.","steps":["Start with the vertical difference, because gradient compares rise with run: 400 m − 200 m = 200 m of climb.","Put the horizontal distance into the same unit as the climb: 1.0 km = 1000 m of ground.","Gradient is rise over run, so the slope is 200 m of climb for every 1000 m travelled.","To say that as 1 in n, divide the run by the rise: 1000 ÷ 200 = 5, so the average gradient is 1 in 5."],"cues":["Which two heights do you need first?","Why can you not divide metres by kilometres?","What is the rise for every metre of ground?","How do you turn that ratio into 1 in n?"]},{"title":"Median, quartiles and interquartile range from Figure 2","setup":"Figure 2 gives the long axis, in centimetres, of eleven pebbles measured at Site 1 on Coldbeck Water, listed in the order they were collected: 5, 20, 3, 8, 12, 2, 16, 5, 7, 4, 6. Calculate the median, the lower quartile, the upper quartile and the interquartile range of these measurements, showing how you found each one.","steps":["Nothing works until the values are in order, so rank them smallest to largest: 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 12, 16, 20.","The median is the middle of the ranked list. With eleven values, (11 + 1) ÷ 2 = 6, so it is the sixth value: 6 cm.","The lower quartile sits a quarter of the way along: (11 + 1) ÷ 4 = 3, so it is the third value: 4 cm.","The upper quartile sits three quarters of the way along: 3 × (11 + 1) ÷ 4 = 9, so it is the ninth value: 12 cm.","The interquartile range is the gap between the quartiles, 12 − 4 = 8 cm, and that is the spread of the middle half of the pebbles."],"cues":["What has to happen to the list before any of this works?","Which position in the list holds the median?","Which position holds the lower quartile?","Which position holds the upper quartile?","What do you do with the two quartiles?"]},{"title":"Percentage change from a line graph","setup":"Figure 3 is a line graph of the number of visitors to Coldbeck each year. Reading from the vertical axis, the point plotted for 2014 stands at 60 thousand visitors and the point plotted for 2024 stands at 165 thousand visitors. Calculate the percentage change in visitor numbers between 2014 and 2024, and say in words what your answer means.","steps":["Decide which value is the base, because percentage change is always measured from the earlier figure: the base is 60 thousand.","Find the actual change: 165 − 60 = 105 thousand extra visitors a year.","Now scale that against the base: divide the change by the original and multiply by one hundred, (105 ÷ 60) × 100.","105 ÷ 60 = 1.75, and 1.75 × 100 = 175, so visitor numbers rose by 175 per cent — they have very nearly trebled in ten years."],"cues":["Which of the two readings is the starting point?","What is the size of the rise itself?","What do you divide the rise by?","Is the final figure a rise or a fall, and how big?"]},{"title":"Suggesting environmental pressures from a photograph","setup":"Figure 4 is a ground photograph taken from the fellside south of Coldbeck, looking north across the valley. In the foreground a drystone wall encloses rough grazing with about twenty sheep. In the middle distance lies the village: a church with a square tower, roughly forty grey stone houses along one street, a full car park holding about twenty-five cars, and a stone bridge over a shallow, stony river. On the eastern edge of the village about fifteen tents stand on riverside grass. Behind, the fellside rises through a dark block of conifers to bare crags and a rounded summit, with a pale zigzag scar where a footpath crosses the open fell. A four-mark question asks you to suggest the pressures that tourism is placing on the environment shown.","steps":["Sort the features into visitor pressure and ordinary farming, because only the first kind can be credited: the full car park, the tents and the pale path scar come from visitors, while the wall and the sheep do not.","Develop the car park: it is already full, so cars will spill onto the verges of the single street, compacting the soil, killing the grass and adding fumes and noise to a quiet valley.","Develop the path scar: walkers have worn the turf off the fell in a zigzag line, so rain now runs down bare ground instead of soaking into it, gullying the scar, while walkers stepping onto the grass at the edges widen it further.","Develop the tents and keep the point environmental: pitched on grass beside the water, they trample riverbank vegetation and risk litter and waste water reaching the shallow channel the bridge crosses."],"cues":["Which things in the picture are there because of visitors?","What happens when a car park is already full?","Why does bare ground on a slope get worse rather than recover?","What is under the tents, and what does that threaten?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which figures of a grid reference do you read first, and why is the order easy to remember?","a":"The eastings, read along the bottom of the map, before the northings read up the side — along the corridor, then up the stairs."},{"q":"On a 1:25 000 map, how far on the ground is 1 cm, and how wide is each grid square on the paper?","a":"1 cm stands for 250 m, so a 1 km grid square is drawn 4 cm across."},{"q":"Why is the interquartile range often a fairer measure of spread than the range?","a":"The range uses only the largest and smallest values, so a single freak value distorts it; the interquartile range covers the middle half of the ranked data and ignores the extremes."},{"q":"A value falls from 125 to 30. Write down the calculation for the percentage change and the answer.","a":"(30 − 125) ÷ 125 × 100 = −76%, that is, a fall of 76 per cent."}]},"room":{"facts":["The three papers: Paper 1 Living with the physical environment, 1 hour 30 minutes, 88 marks including 3 for SPaG, 35 per cent of the GCSE; Paper 2 Challenges in the human environment, 1 hour 30 minutes, 88 marks including 3 for SPaG, 35 per cent; Paper 3 Geographical applications, 1 hour 30 minutes, 76 marks including 6 for SPaG, 30 per cent.","Skills are assessed in all three written exams, and an Ordnance Survey map or another map extract may be used on any of them.","Timing: allow roughly a minute a mark and leave reading time for every figure. Papers 1 and 2 give 90 minutes for 88 marks and Paper 3 gives 90 minutes for 76 marks, with Paper 3's resources booklet released twelve weeks before the exam.","The assessment objectives: AO1 knowledge 15 per cent; AO2 understanding 25 per cent; AO3 applying knowledge and understanding to interpret, analyse, evaluate and judge 35 per cent, 10 per cent of it applied to fieldwork; AO4 selecting, adapting and using skills and communicating findings 25 per cent, 5 per cent of it used on fieldwork data and contexts.","The skills tariffs: 1-, 2-, 3- and 4-mark questions are point-marked; a levels-marked 4-mark question has Levels 1–2 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4); a 6-mark question has Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6); a 9-mark question has Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–3, Clear 4–6, Detailed 7–9). No paper carries a 5-, 8-, 10- or 12-mark question, and there is no Level 4.","The stems: Give, State, Complete and Calculate carry 1 or 2 marks; Describe, Suggest, Outline and Compare carry 2 to 4; 'Explain how…' and 'Suggest why…' carry 4 or 6; Assess, Evaluate, 'To what extent…' and Discuss carry 6 or 9. 'Using Figure…' is a prefix, not a tariff: it appears on questions worth anything from 1 to 9 marks and means the resource must be quoted. 'Justify' carries 3 marks on a sampling or method question (specimen Paper 3 05.2) and 9 on Paper 3's decision question.","SPaG is assessed on the indicated 9-mark questions only — 3 marks on Paper 1 question 01.9, 3 on Paper 2 question 01.8 and 6 on Paper 3, 3 each on questions 03.3 and 05.4 — and scored High performance 3, Intermediate 2, Threshold 1.","How the short questions are marked: on a calculation one mark is for the answer and one for an indication of the working; on 'Complete', 'Shade' or 'Draw' the mark is for accuracy alone; and on a 'Using Figure…' question an answer that ignores the figure is capped, because the figure is the evidence."],"questions":[{"q":"Using Figure 1a, give the six-figure grid reference of the church with a tower in Coldbeck village. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for 651 438. No credit for a four-figure reference or for the figures given in the wrong order. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"651 438. The church stands in the square whose western easting is 65 and whose southern northing is 43, so the reference begins 65 and 43. Inside that square the church is one tenth of the way east and eight tenths of the way north, which gives the third figures 1 and 8, and therefore 651 438.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The numbers along the bottom of the map do a different job from the numbers up the side.","Find the square the symbol sits in first, then work out where inside that square it sits.","Write six figures with the first three coming from one axis and the last three from the other."],"codes":["3.4.1 — Cartographic skills"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1a, state the compass direction of the triangulation pillar on Craig Wen (645 451) from Coldbeck Youth Hostel (645 441). [1 mark]","a":"One mark for north (accept due north). AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"North. The two points share the same easting, 645, so neither lies further east or west than the other; the pillar has the higher northing, 451 against 441, and northings increase up the map, so the pillar lies due north of the hostel.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Direction is always taken from the first place named towards the second.","Compare the two references figure by figure and see which pair is identical.","Decide what it means when the easting does not change at all but the northing rises."],"codes":["3.4.1 — Cartographic skills"]},{"q":"The footpath between Coldbeck Youth Hostel and the triangulation pillar on Craig Wen measures 6.0 cm when a piece of string is laid along it on Figure 1a. Using the scale of the map, calculate the real length of the footpath. Give your answer in kilometres. [1 mark]","a":"One mark for 1.5 km. Accept 1500 m. AO4 = 1 mark.","sol":"1.5 km. At 1:25 000 one centimetre on the map stands for 25 000 cm on the ground, which is 250 m or 0.25 km. The string measures 6.0 cm, so the path is 6.0 × 0.25 = 1.5 km long on the ground.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The scale printed on the map tells you what one centimetre of paper is worth outside.","Convert that value into kilometres before you multiply anything.","One multiplication is all this needs, using the string measurement."],"codes":["3.4.1 — Cartographic skills"]},{"q":"Outline two ways a student writing up a geographical enquiry can make the findings clear to a reader who has not seen the data. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each way that genuinely aids communication, eg naming the figure the reader should look at (1); quoting a value with its units rather than a vague comparison (1); using the specialist term for the process or technique instead of an everyday word (1); ending with a conclusion that answers the enquiry question directly (1). No credit for neat handwriting, colour or presentation alone. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"First, name the figure and quote its numbers. A reader who has not seen the data cannot follow 'the pebbles got smaller', but can follow 'in Figure 2 the mean long axis falls from 8.0 cm at Site 1 to 6.0 cm at Site 2, a fall of 25 per cent'. Second, use the specialist terms rather than everyday ones: attrition, long axis, mean, interquartile range, anomaly, rather than 'wearing away' and 'the odd one out'. Using a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate is one of the three things the SPaG criteria reward, alongside accurate spelling and punctuation and control of grammar, so the habit earns marks twice over — once for clarity and once on the indicated nine-mark questions.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Write for someone holding the paper but not your notebook.","Two different ways are wanted, so make one about evidence and one about vocabulary.","Start by asking what a reader needs in order to check a claim you make."],"codes":["3.4.7 — Literacy"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, calculate the median long axis of the pebbles measured at Site 2. Show your working. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct median, 5 cm; one mark for an indication of the method, eg values ranked and the sixth of eleven identified. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Ranked from smallest to largest, the Site 2 measurements are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 16. With eleven values the median is the (11 + 1) ÷ 2 = 6th value in the ranked list. Counting in, the sixth value is 5, so the median long axis at Site 2 is 5 cm.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The median is about position in a list, not about totals.","The figures are printed in the order they were collected, which is not the order you need.","Work out which position in eleven values is the middle one before you look up any number."],"codes":["3.4.4 — Statistical skills"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, calculate the interquartile range of the pebble long axes measured at Site 1. Show your working. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct interquartile range, 8 cm; one mark for an indication of the calculation, eg upper quartile 12, lower quartile 4. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Ranked, the Site 1 measurements are 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 12, 16, 20. With eleven values the lower quartile is the (11 + 1) ÷ 4 = 3rd value, which is 4 cm, and the upper quartile is the 3 × (11 + 1) ÷ 4 = 9th value, which is 12 cm. The interquartile range is the upper quartile minus the lower quartile: 12 − 4 = 8 cm.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This measure describes the middle half of the data, so the extremes are not part of it.","You need two positions in the ranked list, one a quarter along and one three quarters along.","Rank the eleven figures first and decide which numbered values you are hunting for."],"codes":["3.4.4 — Statistical skills"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, calculate the percentage change in the number of visitors to Coldbeck between 2014 and 2024. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for the correct answer, an increase of 175%; one mark for an indication of the calculation, eg 105 ÷ 60 × 100. Accept 175% increase or +175%. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The graph is plotted at 60 thousand visitors in 2014 and 165 thousand in 2024. The change is 165 − 60 = 105 thousand. Percentage change is the change divided by the original value and multiplied by one hundred: (105 ÷ 60) × 100 = 1.75 × 100 = 175. Visitor numbers therefore increased by 175 per cent over the ten years.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Percentage change is always measured against the earlier of the two figures.","Find the size of the change first and keep it separate from the starting value.","Read both values off the vertical axis before you divide anything."],"codes":["3.4.4 — Statistical skills"]},{"q":"Using Figure 3, describe the trend in the number of visitors to Coldbeck between 2014 and 2024. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for a basic statement of the overall trend, eg visitor numbers rose. Second mark for development using data from the graph or for identifying the 2020 anomaly with figures. No credit for simply listing the value for each year. AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"Visitor numbers rose overall, from 60 thousand in 2014 to 165 thousand in 2024, an increase of 105 thousand. The rise was steady at roughly 13 thousand a year up to 2019, when it reached 125 thousand. There is a sharp anomaly in 2020, when the figure collapsed to 30 thousand, the lowest value on the graph; numbers recovered to 85 thousand in 2021 and passed the 2019 figure in 2022, after which the line climbs more steeply than before.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Say what the line does as a whole before you say anything about individual years.","One year breaks the pattern; a description that ignores it is incomplete.","Support the shape you describe with figures taken from the vertical axis."],"codes":["3.4.2 — Graphical skills"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, suggest a suitable enquiry question that the pebble data could be used to answer, and justify why the data collected would allow you to answer it. [3 marks]","a":"Credit any question or hypothesis the two sites and the measurements can actually test, eg 'Does the long axis of pebbles in Coldbeck Water decrease with distance downstream?'. The marks are for the justification, not the question: the two sites differ in distance downstream, so there is something to compare (1); the long axis in centimetres is quantitative, so the sets can be processed and compared (1); eleven readings at each site are enough to calculate a mean, a median and an interquartile range rather than relying on one pebble (1). AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"A suitable question is: 'Does the long axis of pebbles in Coldbeck Water decrease with distance downstream?' Figure 2 can answer it because the only thing that differs between the two sets is distance downstream — Site 1 is one kilometre below the source and Site 2 two kilometres below it — so any difference in size can be linked to the distance travelled and to attrition along the way. The data are quantitative and measured in the same unit at both sites, so they can be processed rather than merely described: the mean falls from 8.0 cm to 6.0 cm, a fall of 25 per cent, and the median from 6 cm to 5 cm. Eleven readings at each site are enough to rank the values and take an interquartile range, 8 cm at Site 1 against 5 cm at Site 2, which shows the spread narrowing as well as the size falling. Eleven is still a small sample gathered at only two points, so the question can be answered but the conclusion would need more sites and more pebbles before it could be trusted.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A good enquiry question names the thing you measured and the thing you think it varies with.","The marks are for the justification, so ask what it is about these particular measurements that makes them usable.","Begin by writing the question out in full, as a question."],"codes":["3.4.6 — Formulate enquiry and argument"]},{"q":"The pebbles in Figure 2 were collected by picking up whichever stones caught the student's eye at each site. Justify a sampling method that would have produced more reliable data. [3 marks]","a":"The method must be named, but naming alone earns nothing; credit comes from the justification. One detailed justification can earn full marks; three brief justified statements are also creditworthy. Expect reference to bias, sample size and repeatability. AO3 = 3 marks.","sol":"A systematic sample would be better: lay a tape across the channel and measure the long axis of the pebble nearest each 50 cm mark. This removes the student's own bias, because the decision about which pebble to measure is made by the tape and not by which stones look interesting; picking by eye favours large, pale or unusual pebbles and so inflates the mean. It also fixes the procedure, so a second student repeating the work at the same site would collect a comparable set of measurements, which makes the comparison between Site 1 and Site 2 fair. Increasing the sample from eleven to, say, thirty pebbles at each site would further reduce the effect of a single very large stone, such as the 20 cm pebble at Site 1, on the averages.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The mark scheme gives nothing for naming a method, so plan to spend your words on the reasons.","Think about what is wrong with letting the collector decide which stone to pick up.","Consider both how the pebbles are chosen and how many of them there are."],"codes":["3.4.3 — Numerical skills"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1b, describe the shape of the Coldbeck valley shown by the cross-section along the line A–B. [4 marks]","a":"Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear description of the asymmetry, the narrow floor and the relief, using heights and distances from the section. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): simple statements such as 'it is a valley' with little or no data. AO3 = 2 marks, AO4 = 2 marks.","sol":"The section shows a narrow, asymmetrical valley. From A the land falls steeply from 340 m to the lowest point on the section, 195 m, where Coldbeck Water crosses the line, a drop of 145 m in only 0.55 km. The floor is very narrow: the 210 m level is reached at about 0.47 km from A on the northern side and at 0.70 km on the southern side, so between them the floor is only about 0.23 km wide, and the youth hostel stands on it at 200 m. South of the river the ground rises again, but far more gently, reaching only 245 m at B, a rise of 50 m over 0.45 km. The two sides are therefore quite unlike each other: the northern slope is roughly 1 in 4 while the southern slope is closer to 1 in 9, so the section is lop-sided rather than a symmetrical V. The total relief shown along the kilometre is 145 m.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A cross-section is read from one end to the other, like a journey.","Compare the two sides of the valley against each other rather than describing them one after the other.","Every statement about steepness should carry a height and a distance from the section."],"codes":["3.4.1 — Cartographic skills"]},{"q":"Using Figure 4, suggest the pressures that tourism is placing on the environment shown in the photograph. [4 marks]","a":"Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear interpretation of the photograph with pressures developed through to an environmental consequence. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): features identified with limited interpretation. No credit for features not observed in the photograph, or for effects on the local economy or community. AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"The car park in the middle of the picture is full, with about twenty-five cars, so further visitors will park on the verges of the single street; that compacts the soil, kills the grass and leaves bare, muddy ground beside the road. On the fell behind the village a pale zigzag scar shows where walkers have worn the turf off the slope. Because the vegetation has gone, rainfall now runs across bare ground instead of soaking in, so the scar is being gullied and widened, and walkers avoiding the loose surface trample the grass on either side, spreading the damage. The fifteen tents are pitched on grass beside the river, where trampling wears away the bank vegetation and litter or waste water can reach the shallow, stony channel crossed by the bridge, harming water quality and the plants and animals in it.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Only what you can actually see in the picture can be credited.","Separate the things that are there because of visitors from the things that are there because of farming.","Each point needs to be followed through to what it does to soil, vegetation or water."],"codes":["3.4.5 — Use of qualitative and quantitative data"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1a and your own understanding, suggest how relief and drainage have influenced the pattern of settlement and communications in the Coldbeck valley. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): thorough analysis of the map, with a logical chain of reasoning linking relief and drainage to where building and routes are and are not, supported by grid references, heights and distances. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): accurate map evidence but limited development. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): description of the map without inference. AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks.","sol":"Settlement in the valley is confined to the flat ground. Coldbeck village lies strung along a single street on the valley floor around 651 438, where the contours are widely spaced, so building land is level and easy to work; the church tower at 651 438 and the inn at 652 438 stand 100 m apart, a compact, nucleated settlement rather than a scattered one. The village is also at the lowest bridging point over Coldbeck Water, the stone bridge at 650 438 beside which the map prints a spot height of 190 m, which explains why routes converge there. Relief has pushed communications the same way: the B road follows the valley floor from the western edge of the extract, through the village and along the northern shore of the reservoir, because climbing out of the valley would mean crossing the face below Craig Wen, where the section along A–B falls 145 m in 0.55 km. Where relief is hostile, building stops: nothing stands above the 300 m contour, the ground near the source at 638 448 is marshy, and the only building far up the valley is the youth hostel at 645 441, alone on the valley floor at 200 m and placed for walkers where the footpath to the summit meets the floor. Drainage removes land too — the reservoir floods the low ground east of the village, so apart from a car park and picnic site at 664 439 there is no settlement in squares 6643 and 6743.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The question names two physical controls; plan a point about each rather than writing about the map in general.","Look at where people have not built as well as where they have.","Anchor every claim with something you could point to on the map — a grid reference, a height or a distance."],"codes":["3.4.1 — Cartographic skills"]},{"q":"Using Figure 2, assess how useful the mean is as a measure of the pebble sizes at Site 1. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): balanced assessment weighing what the mean does well against the distortion caused by the largest pebbles, using calculated values, and reaching a substantiated overall judgement. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear statements of strengths and weaknesses with some data. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): generic comments about averages with little reference to the table. AO3 = 6 marks.","sol":"The mean at Site 1 is 8.0 cm, because the eleven measurements total 88 cm. Its strength is that it uses every measurement, so no reading is ignored, and it gives a single figure that can be compared straight away with the 6.0 cm mean at Site 2 — a fall of 25 per cent downstream, which is exactly the kind of comparison the enquiry needs. Its weakness at this site is serious. The ranked values are 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 12, 16, 20, so seven of the eleven pebbles are smaller than the mean of 8.0 cm and only three are larger than it. Two large pebbles, 16 cm and 20 cm, drag it upwards, and because the sample is only eleven strong a single boulder has a disproportionate effect. The median, 6 cm, and the mode, 5 cm, both sit much closer to the typical pebble on this bed, and the interquartile range of 8 cm shows how widely spread the middle half of the data is. Overall the mean is useful but not sufficient: it is a fair way of comparing two sites quickly, yet quoted on its own it overstates the size of a typical pebble at Site 1, so it should be reported alongside the median and a measure of spread.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Assess means weigh it up, so you will need something on each side before you judge.","Work out the mean and at least one other average so you have something to compare it with.","Look at how many of the eleven values actually lie below the mean, and ask why."],"codes":["3.4.4 — Statistical skills"]},{"q":"Assess the effectiveness of the line graph in Figure 3 as a way of presenting the visitor data for Coldbeck. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): developed and balanced appreciation of the components of the technique — axes, scale, the continuous line, the anomaly — with a substantiated overall assessment. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): specific components evaluated with clear statements about effectiveness. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): generic or incomplete evaluation. AO3 = 6 marks.","sol":"A line graph suits this data well, because the years are continuous and the reader wants the shape of the change rather than eleven separate totals. The horizontal axis is at even one-year intervals, so the slope of the line is an honest picture of the rate of change: the gentle climb from 60 thousand in 2014 to 125 thousand in 2019 is visibly less steep than the recovery from 30 thousand in 2020 to 165 thousand in 2024. The vertical scale starts at zero and is marked every 20 thousand, which matters, because a truncated axis would have exaggerated the rise; but the 20 thousand interval also means values must be estimated between gridlines, so a reading such as 95 thousand for 2017 cannot be taken as exact. The single line makes the 2020 anomaly unmissable, which is the graph's greatest strength, although joining 2019 to 2020 with a straight line implies a steady decline through the year when the collapse was in fact abrupt. The technique also shows only totals: it says nothing about who the visitors were or where they went, so it cannot on its own support conclusions about pressure on the footpaths. Overall the line graph is an effective choice for showing the trend and the anomaly, and its weaknesses are limits on precision and on the questions it can answer rather than faults in the technique itself.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Take the graph apart into its components before you judge any of them.","Ask what this kind of graph shows that a bar chart of the same figures would not.","Finish with one overall verdict rather than leaving a list of good and bad points."],"codes":["3.4.2 — Graphical skills"]},{"q":"Using Figure 1a and Figure 4, assess how far the photograph adds to what the map extract shows about the Coldbeck valley. [6 marks]","a":"Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): balanced assessment of what each source contributes, with evidence drawn from both and a substantiated judgement about qualitative and quantitative sources together. Level 2 (Clear, 3–4): clear statements about each source using some evidence. Level 1 (Basic, 1–2): the sources described separately. No credit for describing one source in isolation. AO3 = 6 marks.","sol":"The photograph adds condition, which the map cannot carry. The map marks a car park at 650 439 and a footpath running north from 645 441, but only the photograph shows that the car park is full and that the path has worn into a pale scar across the open fell; the map records that features exist, the photograph records how they are being used and how damaged they are. It also shows character — grey stone houses along one street, a square church tower, a shallow stony river — which turns the symbols at 651 438 into a recognisable place. Against this, the map does things the photograph cannot. It gives position and quantity: the summit of Craig Wen is fixed at 645 451 and measured at 400 m, and the reservoir begins 0.9 km east of the village and runs a further 1.3 km to its dam, entirely out of the picture. The photograph shows a single view from one spot at one moment, so it cannot tell you how steep the fell actually is, how far away anything is, or what lies beyond the skyline, and a different season or a wet afternoon would give a different impression. Overall the photograph adds a great deal, but only because the map is there to locate it: the qualitative source supplies evidence of use and condition that measurement misses, while the quantitative source supplies the position, height and distance that the picture cannot. Used together they are far stronger than either alone.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Both sources are named in the question, so an answer that leans on one of them is capped.","Think about the difference between evidence you can measure and evidence you can only see.","Ask what each source cannot show, and let that lead you to the judgement."],"codes":["3.4.5 — Use of qualitative and quantitative data"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many centimetres on a 1:25 000 map stand for one kilometre on the ground?","a":"4 cm, because 1 cm stands for 250 m."},{"q":"In a six-figure grid reference, which three figures are written first?","a":"The eastings — the numbers read along the bottom of the map."},{"q":"Write the formula for percentage change.","a":"(new value − original value) ÷ original value × 100."},{"q":"In a ranked list of 11 values, which value is the upper quartile?","a":"The ninth, because 3 × (11 + 1) ÷ 4 = 9."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Four-figure grid reference","back":"Names one square kilometre on an OS map. Read the easting along the bottom first, then the northing up the side: 6444 is the square whose south-west corner is easting 64, northing 44.","code":"3.4.1 — Cartographic skills","open":false},{"front":"Six-figure grid reference","back":"Divides a grid square into tenths to fix a point to about 100 m. Eastings still come first: 645 441 is five tenths east and one tenth north inside square 6444.","code":"3.4.1 — Cartographic skills","open":false},{"front":"The scales 1:25 000 and 1:50 000","back":"At 1:25 000 one centimetre stands for 250 m, so 4 cm is a kilometre and each grid square is drawn 4 cm across. At 1:50 000 one centimetre stands for 500 m, so 2 cm is a kilometre.","code":"3.4.1 — Cartographic skills","open":false},{"front":"Straight-line and curved-line distance","back":"Straight-line distance is measured with a ruler between two points. Curved-line distance follows a road, path or river using string or the edge of paper turned at each bend, and is always the longer of the two.","code":"3.4.1 — Cartographic skills","open":false},{"front":"Contour interval","back":"The height difference between one contour and the next: 5 m on a 1:25 000 map, or 10 m in mountain areas and on 1:50 000 maps. Contours close together mean a steep slope; widely spaced, gentle ground.","code":"3.4.1 — Cartographic skills","open":false},{"front":"Gradient","back":"Vertical difference divided by horizontal distance, in the same unit. To write it as 1 in n, divide distance by height change: 200 m of rise in 1000 m is 1 in 5. Larger n, gentler slope.","code":"3.4.1 — Cartographic skills","open":false},{"front":"Cross-section","back":"A side view of the land along a line drawn on a map. Heights are read where each contour crosses the line and plotted against distance. The vertical scale is usually stretched, so slopes look steeper than they really are.","code":"3.4.1 — Cartographic skills","open":false},{"front":"Choropleth and isoline maps","back":"A choropleth shades whole areas by value, darker for higher, so it hides variation inside each area. An isoline joins places of equal value and is drawn between the plotted numbers, higher values one side, lower the other.","code":"3.4.2 — Graphical skills","open":false},{"front":"Dispersion graph and line of best fit","back":"A dispersion graph plots every value as a dot against one scale, so the range, median and interquartile range can be read off it. A line of best fit runs through the middle of a scattergraph to show the trend.","code":"3.4.2 — Graphical skills","open":false},{"front":"Interquartile range and percentage change","back":"Interquartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile, the spread of the middle half, so one freak value cannot distort it. Percentage change = (new − original) ÷ original × 100; a negative answer is a decrease.","code":"3.4.4 — Statistical skills","open":false},{"front":"Sampling: random, systematic and stratified","back":"Random: every point has an equal chance, chosen with random numbers. Systematic: measured at a fixed interval, such as every 50 cm. Stratified: the sample is shared between groups in proportion to their size.","code":"3.4.3 — Numerical skills","open":false},{"front":"The command words and the marks they carry","back":"1–2 marks: Give, State, Complete, Calculate. 2–4: Describe, Outline, Suggest, Compare. 4 or 6: Explain how, Suggest why. 6 or 9: Assess, Evaluate, To what extent, Discuss. Using Figure prefixes any tariff; Justify is 3 (method) or 9 (decision).","open":false,"code":"3.4 — Students are required to develop and demonstrate a range of geographical skills"},{"front":"The tariffs and their level grids","back":"1 to 4 marks are point-marked. A levels-marked 4-mark question has Levels 1–2, Basic 1–2 and Clear 3–4. A 6-mark question runs Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6. A 9-mark question runs Basic 1–3, Clear 4–6, Detailed 7–9.","open":false,"code":"3.4 — Students are required to develop and demonstrate a range of geographical skills"},{"front":"Give one way the Detailed descriptor asks for more than the Basic one","back":"Basic says limited and basic, Detailed says thorough and detailed; Basic describes, Detailed analyses, and Level 3 asks for full analysis of the resource, using evidence to support response; Basic asserts, Detailed reaches a substantiated judgement.","open":true,"code":"3.4.6 — Formulate enquiry and argument"},{"front":"Give one way to earn the SPaG marks on a nine-mark question","back":"Spell and punctuate with consistent accuracy; use the rules of grammar with effective control of meaning overall; use a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate. High performance is 3 marks, Intermediate 2, Threshold 1.","open":true,"code":"3.4.7 — Literacy"}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The resources written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Figure 1 — Ordnance Survey map extract and cross-section: the Coldbeck valley. Written for this room. Coldbeck, Craig Wen and Coldbeck Water are invented names and the extract is illustrative, drawn in words rather than copied from any published map; the map conventions used — grid squares, scale, contours and symbols — are the real Ordnance Survey ones.\nFigure 1a — the map extract, scale 1:25 000. The extract covers eastings 63 to 68 and northings 43 to 48, a square 5 km by 5 km. Grid lines are 1 km apart and, at this scale, each grid square is drawn 4 cm across. The contour interval is 10 m, with thicker labelled contours every 50 m.\nRelief: the high ground is in the north-west. The highest point, Craig Wen, carries a triangulation pillar marked 400 m at 645 451. Crags are marked on its steep southern face around 646 445, where the ground drops from 340 m to 200 m in half a kilometre. The land falls south from the summit to a valley floor at about 195 m. No land in the extract rises above 400 m.\nDrainage: Coldbeck Water rises in marshy ground at about 380 m at 638 448 and flows south-east down the valley, passing 50 m south of the youth hostel, then east through Coldbeck village and on into Coldbeck Reservoir. The reservoir stretches east from 660 437 to its dam at 673 438, about 1.3 km long and up to 0.4 km wide, filling the low ground in grid squares 6643 and 6743.\nSettlement and communications: Coldbeck village lies on the valley floor around 651 438, about forty buildings strung along a single street. A church with a tower is marked at 651 438, an inn at 652 438 and a car park at 650 439; a spot height of 190 m is printed beside the stone bridge at 650 438, the lowest crossing of the beck. Coldbeck Youth Hostel stands alone on the valley floor at 645 441, at 200 m. A camp site is marked at 653 437 and a second car park with a picnic site at 664 439 on the northern shore of the reservoir. A B road runs along the valley floor from the western edge of the extract, through the village, and along the northern shore of the reservoir to the eastern edge; no road climbs out of the valley within the extract. A public footpath links the youth hostel at 645 441 with the triangulation pillar at 645 451, zigzagging up the crags between them: measured along its length with string it is 6.0 cm on the map, while the straight line between the two points measures 4.0 cm.\nLand use: a block of coniferous woodland covers the lower northern valley side in grid square 6544. Field boundaries and rough grazing cover the southern valley side below 300 m; above that the fell is open, with no walls and no buildings. The line A–B is drawn on the extract as a straight line running due south from A at 645 446, through the youth hostel at 645 441, to B at 645 436.\nFigure 1b — cross-section drawn along the line A–B. Horizontal scale 1:25 000 (1 cm = 250 m); vertical scale 1 cm = 50 m, so the vertical scale is five times the horizontal one and the slopes appear steeper on the section than they are on the ground. The plotted heights, given as distance from A:\nDistance from A | Height\n0.00 km (A, 645 446) | 340 m\n0.20 km | 290 m\n0.40 km | 230 m\n0.50 km (the youth hostel) | 200 m\n0.55 km (Coldbeck Water) | 195 m\n0.70 km | 210 m\n0.85 km | 225 m\n1.00 km (B, 645 436) | 245 m","Figure 2 — Data table: the long axis of pebbles measured on Coldbeck Water. Written for this room; the figures are illustrative fieldwork data, not measurements taken from a real river. Eleven pebbles were measured at each of two sites on Coldbeck Water — Site 1 one kilometre below the source, on the reach near the youth hostel at 645 441, and Site 2 two kilometres below the source, on the gravel bed just downstream of the stone bridge at 650 438 and still well above the head of Coldbeck Reservoir at 660 437 — and the long axis of each is given in centimetres in the order the pebbles were collected.\nPebble | Site 1 long axis (cm) | Site 2 long axis (cm)\n1 | 5 | 9\n2 | 20 | 4\n3 | 3 | 16\n4 | 8 | 5\n5 | 12 | 1\n6 | 2 | 7\n7 | 16 | 3\n8 | 5 | 8\n9 | 7 | 5\n10 | 4 | 6\n11 | 6 | 2\nTotal | 88 | 66\nMean | 8.0 | 6.0","Figure 3 — Line graph: visitors to Coldbeck, 2014 to 2024. Written for this room; the visitor figures are illustrative, although the collapse in 2020 reflects the real closure of visitor attractions in the United Kingdom during the Covid-19 pandemic. The horizontal axis is labelled Year and runs from 2014 to 2024, one division per year. The vertical axis is labelled Visitors (thousands), runs from 0 to 200 and is marked every 20. A single line joins the plotted points listed below; no other data is shown.\nYear | Visitors (thousands)\n2014 | 60\n2015 | 70\n2016 | 80\n2017 | 95\n2018 | 110\n2019 | 125\n2020 | 30\n2021 | 85\n2022 | 130\n2023 | 150\n2024 | 165","Figure 4 — Photograph: the Coldbeck valley from the fellside south of the village, looking north. Written for this room; the scene is illustrative and nothing in it is measured data. In the foreground a drystone wall runs across the slope, enclosing rough grazing where about twenty sheep are feeding. Beyond it, on the valley floor, lies the village: a church with a square stone tower, roughly forty grey stone houses along a single street, and a low stone bridge over a shallow river running over pale stones. Beside the street a tarmac car park is full, holding about twenty-five cars, and three more cars stand with two wheels on the grass verge, which has been churned to mud. At the eastern edge of the village, on flat grass beside the river, about fifteen tents are pitched. Behind the village the far valley side rises steeply: a dark block of conifers covers its lower slope, bare grey crags break through above the trees, and higher still the open fell is crossed by a pale zigzag strip several paces wide where the turf has been worn off a footpath, with a shallow channel of loose gravel running down its centre. At the top left of the picture the fell ends in a rounded summit with a small pillar standing on it. The only road visible is the village street; the reservoir lies out of the picture to the east."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the cartographic and graphical skills the specification names","items":["Atlas maps — latitude and longitude: latitude is measured north or south of the Equator, longitude east or west of the Prime Meridian; a place is given as latitude first, then longitude.","Atlas maps — distribution and pattern: describe where things are and are not, using compass directions, named regions and the key, rather than listing country names, which earns nothing on its own.","Atlas maps — association: look for two thematic maps that shade the same areas, and say that the patterns coincide before you suggest why.","OS maps — scale: 1:25 000 means 1 cm = 250 m, so 4 cm = 1 km; 1:50 000 means 1 cm = 500 m, so 2 cm = 1 km. The grid squares are 1 km either way.","OS maps — grid references: eastings before northings; four figures name a 1 km square, six figures divide it into tenths and fix a point to about 100 m.","OS maps — distance: straight-line with a ruler, curved-line with string or the edge of paper turned at each bend, then converted with the scale.","OS maps — direction: taken from the first place named towards the second, using the eight compass points with north up the map.","OS maps — relief: contours close together mean steep, far apart mean gentle; spot heights and triangulation pillars give exact heights between contours; gradient is vertical difference over horizontal distance, written 1 in n.","OS maps — cross-sections and transects: a side view along a line on the map, plotting the height at each contour crossing against distance, used to show the shape of a valley or a ridge.","OS maps — inference: settlement on flat, low, well-drained land and at bridging points; roads along valleys; land use changing with altitude; tourism inferred from car parks, camp sites, youth hostels, viewpoints and footpaths.","Maps with photographs: match the photograph to the map by finding two or three features in both, then work out the direction the camera is pointing.","Sketch maps and annotated photographs: draw the main lines only, then label what a feature is and annotate why it matters.","Graphs to construct: line charts, bar charts, pie charts, pictograms, histograms with equal class intervals, divided bars, scattergraphs and population pyramids.","Maps to complete: choropleth, isoline, dot maps, desire lines, proportional symbols and flow lines — the marks are for accuracy, so a bar must be the right height and an isoline must pass on the correct side of every plotted value.","Choosing a technique: continuous change over time takes a line graph; separate categories take a bar chart; parts of a whole take a pie chart or divided bar; a relationship between two variables takes a scattergraph; anything with a spatial dimension needs a map-based method, not a bar chart."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the numerical and statistical methods, worked on this room's figures","items":["Mean = total ÷ number of values. Site 1 in Figure 2: 88 ÷ 11 = 8.0 cm. It uses every value but is dragged by extremes.","Median = the (n + 1) ÷ 2 th value of the ranked list. Site 1: the 6th of 11 ranked values = 6 cm. It ignores extremes.","Mode = the value that occurs most often. Site 1: 5 cm, the only long axis recorded twice. A data set can have no mode or several.","Range = largest − smallest. Site 1: 20 − 2 = 18 cm. One freak value can wreck it.","Lower quartile = the (n + 1) ÷ 4 th ranked value. Site 1: the 3rd of 11 = 4 cm.","Upper quartile = the 3(n + 1) ÷ 4 th ranked value. Site 1: the 9th of 11 = 12 cm.","Interquartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. Site 1: 12 − 4 = 8 cm, the spread of the middle half of the data.","Percentage change = (new − original) ÷ original × 100. Figure 3, 2014 to 2024: (105 ÷ 60) × 100 = 175 per cent increase.","Percentage decrease works the same way and comes out negative. Figure 3, 2019 to 2020: (−95 ÷ 125) × 100 = −76 per cent.","Comparing two means with a percentage: Figure 2, Site 1 to Site 2, (6.0 − 8.0) ÷ 8.0 × 100 = −25 per cent, a quarter smaller downstream.","Gradient = vertical difference ÷ horizontal distance in the same unit; write it as 1 in n by dividing distance by height change. Youth hostel to summit: 200 m in 1000 m = 1 in 5.","Sample size and bias: eleven pebbles chosen by eye is a small, biased sample; a systematic sample at a fixed interval, or a random sample chosen with random numbers, removes the collector's preference.","Anomaly: a value that breaks the pattern, such as the 30 thousand visitors in 2020 on Figure 3. Name it, quote it, and suggest a cause — do not quietly ignore it.","Interpolation reads a value inside the range of the data; extrapolation continues a trend beyond it, and is far less safe because the trend may not hold.","Weakness in selective presentation: a mean quoted with no measure of spread, an axis that does not start at zero, a percentage with no base figure, or a trend drawn from too few years."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: how geographical skills are examined, and the six stages of an enquiry","items":["Skills are assessed in all three written exams, and OS maps or other map extracts may be used on any of them.","AO4 — select, adapt and use skills and techniques — is worth 25 per cent of the qualification, with 5 per cent of it used on fieldwork data and contexts.","AO3 — apply knowledge and understanding to interpret, analyse and evaluate information and make judgements — is worth 35 per cent, with 10 per cent applied to fieldwork.","Short skills questions are point-marked: one mark for the correct answer on multiple choice, with no credit if two or more answers are shaded; on a calculation, one mark for the answer and one for an indication of the working.","A 'Complete', 'Shade' or 'Draw' question is marked on accuracy alone: a bar at exactly the right height, a plot at exactly the right value, an isoline on the correct side of every number.","A 'Calculate' question can require units, and often allows a small range of answers where a value has to be measured from a figure.","'Using Figure…' means the figure is the evidence: an answer that ignores it is capped, and features not visible in the resource earn nothing.","Longer questions are levels-marked at three tariffs: 4 marks with Levels 1–2 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4), 6 marks with Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–2, Clear 3–4, Detailed 5–6) and 9 marks with Levels 1–3 (Basic 1–3, Clear 4–6, Detailed 7–9).","Marking is best fit: examiners start at the lowest level and climb, and indicative content is a guide, not a checklist, so other valid points are credited.","Allow roughly a minute a mark: Paper 1 and Paper 2 give 90 minutes for 88 marks each, and Paper 3 gives 90 minutes for 76 marks.","Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terminology are assessed on the indicated extended questions — three marks on Paper 1, three on Paper 2 and six on Paper 3 — scored as High 3, Intermediate 2, Threshold 1.","Enquiry stage 1 — a suitable question or hypothesis: the geographical theory behind it, suitable primary and secondary sources and fieldwork locations, and the risks and how to reduce them.","Enquiry stage 2 — selecting, measuring and recording data: primary against secondary, choosing the physical and human data to collect, sampling methods, and justifying the collection method.","Enquiry stage 3 — processing and presenting the data: knowing the range of visual, graphical and cartographic methods available, selecting and using them accurately, and adapting them.","Enquiry stage 4 — describing, analysing and explaining the data: linking data sets, using appropriate statistical techniques, and identifying anomalies.","Enquiry stage 5 — reaching conclusions: drawing evidenced conclusions that answer the original aims of the enquiry.","Enquiry stage 6 — evaluation: the problems with the collection methods, the limitations of the data, other data that would have helped, and how far the conclusions can be trusted."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) on the six-mark question 'Using Figure 1a and your own understanding, suggest how relief and drainage have influenced the pattern of settlement and communications in the Coldbeck valley'","items":["The paragraph: Relief has confined settlement in the Coldbeck valley to the floor. The village lies strung along one street around 651 438, where the spot height beside the bridge is 190 m and the 10 m contours are so widely spaced that the ground is effectively level, so there is flat, buildable, well-drained land here and almost nowhere else on the extract. Drainage then decided the exact spot: the bridge at 650 438 is the lowest crossing of Coldbeck Water, and the church at 651 438 and the inn at 652 438 stand within two hundred metres of it, which is the classic pattern of a settlement growing at a bridging point rather than scattering along the valley. The same two controls have shaped the routes. The B road follows the valley floor from the western edge of the extract, through the village and along the northern shore of the reservoir, and no road climbs out of the valley at all, because the only way north is across the face below Craig Wen, where the section along A–B falls 145 m in 0.55 km — a gradient of roughly 1 in 4, far too steep for a road to be worth building. Where relief and drainage are hostile, people have simply not built: nothing stands above the 300 m contour, the ground around the source of the beck at 638 448 is marked as marshy, and the reservoir has drowned the low ground in squares 6643 and 6743, so the eastern third of the extract holds no settlement at all. The one exception proves the rule — the youth hostel at 645 441 is isolated at 200 m where the footpath to the summit meets the valley floor, and it is there for walkers, not for farming.","Why this reaches Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6): the descriptor asks for thorough application of knowledge and understanding to analyse the resource, with a logical chain of reasoning and full use of the evidence. Every claim is anchored in the map — grid references, a spot height, a contour value, a measured gradient — and each one is carried through from the physical control to the human consequence.","The point: relief confines settlement to the valley floor.","The named evidence: the village at 651 438, the spot height of 190 m beside the bridge at 650 438, the B road, the 145 m fall in 0.55 km along A–B, the marsh at 638 448, the reservoir in squares 6643 and 6743, the youth hostel at 645 441.","The developed explanation: flat, well-drained land allows building; the lowest bridging point concentrates routes; gradients of about 1 in 4 rule out a road; marsh and open water remove land from use altogether.","The link to the question: each paragraph names relief or drainage as the cause and settlement or communications as the effect, which is exactly what the question asks to be suggested.","The mini-judgement: relief sets the limits and drainage chooses the site within them — and the isolated youth hostel shows that where a building does break the rule, it is there for a reason relief itself created."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (Basic, 1–2) on the same six-mark question, visibly weaker","items":["The paragraph: The village is in the valley where it is flat. There are hills on the map and they are steep so nobody lives on them. The road goes along the bottom of the valley because that is easier than going over the hill. There is a river running through the village and a reservoir at the other end of the map. People build near rivers because they need water. The youth hostel is on its own away from the village.","The descriptor it meets: Level 1 (Basic, 1–2). The grid used here is AQA's six-mark resource-analysis grid printed for Paper 1, question 04.7, River landscapes in the UK (6 marks, AO1 = 2 marks, AO2 = 2 marks, AO3 = 2 marks), whose Level 1 reads 'AO1 Demonstrates some knowledge of river processes and environments. AO2 Shows limited geographical understanding of the interrelationships between environments and processes.' That grid is written for explaining the formation of river landforms, so it is being read across to a map-inference question: 'river processes and environments' stands here for the valley's relief and drainage. The answer does show that flat land attracts settlement and that roads follow valleys, so it is not worthless, but the understanding of how relief and drainage work together is exactly as limited as the descriptor says.","The descriptor it misses: Level 3 (Detailed, 5–6) of the same grid asks for 'thorough application of knowledge and understanding to analyse geographical information' and 'full analysis of the resource, using evidence to support response'. There is not one grid reference, height, contour value or distance in the whole paragraph, so nothing is analysed — the map could be any valley in Britain.","What is missing, specifically: the bridging point at 650 438 that explains where the village actually is; the 190 m spot height and the widely spaced contours that explain why the floor is buildable; the 145 m fall in 0.55 km that explains why no road climbs out; the marsh at 638 448 and the drowned squares 6643 and 6743 that explain the gaps in settlement.","The reasoning that is missing: 'it is flat so people live there' is an assertion, not a chain. A Level 3 answer says flat land is buildable and well drained, that a river must be crossed somewhere and the crossing draws routes together, and that routes then draw buildings to the crossing.","The judgement that is missing: nothing weighs relief against drainage, and the youth hostel is noticed but not explained, so the last sentence adds no marks at all."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan: 'Assess the effectiveness of the line graph in Figure 3 as a way of presenting the visitor data for Coldbeck. [6 marks]'","items":["The command word decoded: 'assess' means weigh the parts and give an informed overall judgement. The mark scheme for this kind of question wants the constituent components of the technique examined one at a time, and then a substantiated overall assessment; evaluative comments without that final verdict are held at the lower levels.","What counts as a component here: the choice of a line rather than bars, the horizontal axis and its intervals, the vertical axis and where it starts, the interval between gridlines, the way the line treats the gap between plotted years, and the limits of what a single total per year can show.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly seven minutes — one minute to note the components down the margin, five to write, one to check the verdict actually answers the question. There are no SPaG marks on a six-mark question, but the specialist terms still have to be right.","Paragraph 1 — the choice of technique, with its evidence: the years are continuous, so a line is the right form; it shows rate of change through slope, and the climb from 60 thousand in 2014 to 125 thousand in 2019 is visibly gentler than the recovery from 30 thousand in 2020 to 165 thousand in 2024. Mini-judgement: effective, because the reader sees the shape of the change and not just eleven totals.","Paragraph 2 — the axes, with their evidence: the horizontal axis is at even one-year intervals, so the slope is honest; the vertical axis starts at zero and is marked every 20 thousand, so the rise is not exaggerated the way a truncated axis would exaggerate it. Mini-judgement: the scale is fair, but values between gridlines such as 95 thousand in 2017 must be estimated, so precision is limited.","Paragraph 3 — the anomaly and what the technique does with it: the single line makes the collapse to 30 thousand in 2020 impossible to miss, which is the graph's greatest strength; against that, joining 2019 to 2020 with a straight line implies a steady decline across the year when the fall was abrupt. Mini-judgement: excellent at flagging the anomaly, slightly misleading about its shape.","Paragraph 4 — the limits of the data the technique carries: only annual totals are plotted, so the graph says nothing about who came, when in the year they came, or where in the valley they went, and it cannot on its own support a conclusion about pressure on the footpaths. Mini-judgement: a limit on the questions it can answer rather than a fault in the technique.","The evaluation thread that runs through all four paragraphs: does each component help the reader see the trend and the anomaly honestly? Keep returning to that question so the paragraphs argue rather than list.","What the conclusion must say: an overall verdict, not a summary. Something like — the line graph is an effective choice for this data because its form matches continuous annual change and its honest axes let both the long rise and the 2020 collapse be read at a glance; its weaknesses are limits on precision and on what a yearly total can reveal, so it should be supported by a second technique rather than replaced.","The trap to avoid: describing what the graph shows instead of assessing how well it shows it. A paragraph of trend description earns nothing here, because the question is about the technique."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:41:00Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"The cap is lifted: the two stem-tariff lines are now correct and agree with the specimen mark schemes and with the room's own questions, and nothing else in the kit moved. Every printed figure recomputes — Figure 2's totals 88 and 66, means 8.0 and 6.0, medians 6 and 5, quartiles 4/12 and 3/8, IQRs 8 and 5, mode 5, range 18; Figure 3's 175% rise, -76% collapse and 13-thousand-a-year trend to 2019; Figure 1a's grid references, the 6.0 cm and 4.0 cm string and ruler distances at 1:25 000, the 1 in 5 hostel-to-summit gradient, the 1.3 km reservoir and the 0.9 km to its head; Figure 1b's 145 m relief with 1 in 3.8 and 1 in 9 sides and the 0.23 km floor. Tariffs are 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6 only, with no 9-mark question and no SPaG marks on any stem; every AO split and level label is one the three specimen mark schemes and levels.md print; all sixteen answer keys re-solve; and all seven key ideas are examined. Publish it. The four remaining problems are polish — tighten the two-mark literacy model and let Q8 concede the uncontrolled variables."}}}} };
